window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 22-year-old sexually active woman presents with two days of migratory pain in the knees and wrists, painful finger swelling along tendon sheaths, and several small pustules on the distal extremities. She has low-grade fever. Synovial fluid from one knee is only mildly inflammatory. Which organism most likely explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Staphylococcus aureus seeding a single joint hematogenously from a distant skin focus in adults", "B": "Borrelia burgdorferi causing early disseminated Lyme disease after a tick bite in an endemic region", "C": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae causing disseminated infection with the classic triad of tenosynovitis, dermatitis and arthritis", "D": "Streptococcus pyogenes producing a post-infectious migratory polyarthritis after untreated pharyngitis, as in rheumatic fever", "E": "Neisseria meningitidis causing meningococcemia with petechial rash and immune complex arthritis after close contact exposure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Neisseria gonorrhoeae causing disseminated infection with the classic triad of tenosynovitis, dermatitis and arthritis): Disseminated gonococcal infection in a sexually active young adult produces migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis and pustular or vesicular skin lesions, often with a mildly inflammatory effusion. Staphylococcus aureus typically causes a single, markedly purulent joint. Rheumatic fever and Lyme disease lack the tendon sheath and pustular findings described."}, {"id": 2, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 20-year-old man develops rising end-tidal carbon dioxide, masseter rigidity, and a temperature of 40 C minutes after induction with succinylcholine and sevoflurane for an appendectomy. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of the drug used to treat this emergency?", "options": {"A": "Stimulation of calcium uptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum by activating SERCA pump activity", "B": "Inhibition of calcium release through the ryanodine receptor of skeletal muscle sarcoplasmic reticulum", "C": "Blockade of the dihydropyridine voltage sensor in the T tubule that triggers calcium release", "D": "Activation of GABA-A receptors in the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center to lower the set point", "E": "Blockade of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, preventing depolarization"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inhibition of calcium release through the ryanodine receptor of skeletal muscle sarcoplasmic reticulum): This is malignant hyperthermia from a volatile anesthetic and succinylcholine, treated with dantrolene, which blocks the ryanodine receptor and stops uncontrolled calcium release in skeletal muscle. Blocking acetylcholine receptors would not stop calcium release inside the muscle fiber, and the hyperthermia is peripheral rather than a raised hypothalamic set point."}, {"id": 3, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 48-year-old man with work stress has a resting heart rate of 104 beats per minute and blood pressure of 148/92 mmHg on repeated visits. Examination shows tender, ropy, hypertonic paraspinal muscles with restricted segmental motion at T1 to T5. Thyroid studies are normal. Which osteopathic explanation best links these findings?", "options": {"A": "Facilitated segments at T10 to L1 raise renal sympathetic tone, activating the renin angiotensin system", "B": "Segmental facilitation at these levels sustains sympathetic outflow, raising heart rate and vascular tone", "C": "Facilitated segments at T5 to T9 raise foregut sympathetic outflow, producing tachycardia and hypertension", "D": "Restriction at the thoracic inlet obstructs lymphatic drainage, expanding plasma volume and raising pressure", "E": "Reduced vagal outflow from cranial base restriction removes parasympathetic braking on heart rate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Segmental facilitation at these levels sustains sympathetic outflow, raising heart rate and vascular tone): Sympathetic fibers to the heart and vessels arise from T1 to T5, and a chronically facilitated segment can lower the threshold for sympathetic discharge, which fits the tachycardia, raised pressure, and ropy tissue of long-standing dysfunction. Reduced vagal outflow would be suggested by cranial base findings, which are not present here."}, {"id": 4, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 28-year-old man is found unresponsive with a respiratory rate of 5 per minute and pinpoint pupils. After intravenous naloxone he wakes, speaks and refuses transport. Forty minutes later he is again drowsy with slow, shallow breathing. Paramedics learn he had taken a long-acting opioid. Which explanation best accounts for the recurrent sedation?", "options": {"A": "Naloxone partially agonizes kappa receptors, causing sedation as its effect on mu receptors faded", "B": "The opioid's volume of distribution rose after naloxone, increasing free drug concentration in the brain", "C": "Naloxone was metabolized into an active agonist, which reactivated opioid receptors in the brainstem", "D": "Naloxone upregulated mu receptors within minutes, so the circulating opioid produced a greater effect", "E": "Naloxone has a shorter duration of action than the opioid, so receptor blockade wore off"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Naloxone has a shorter duration of action than the opioid, so receptor blockade wore off): Naloxone is a competitive antagonist whose clinical effect lasts a short time, whereas long-acting opioids such as methadone persist far longer. As naloxone is cleared, the remaining opioid again occupies mu receptors and depresses respiration, so patients need observation or repeat dosing. It is not an agonist and does not upregulate receptors."}, {"id": 5, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has burning, electric-shock pain in both feet that worsens at night. Monofilament testing is reduced over the soles. His clinician starts pregabalin and explains it works differently from opioids and NSAIDs. At which molecular target does pregabalin act to reduce this pain?", "options": {"A": "Serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake transporters in descending pathways, enhancing inhibition of dorsal horn transmission", "B": "Alpha-2-delta subunit of presynaptic calcium channels, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release in the dorsal horn", "C": "Mu receptors in the dorsal horn, hyperpolarizing postsynaptic neurons and reducing substance P release", "D": "Cyclooxygenase enzymes in peripheral tissue, reducing prostaglandin synthesis and nociceptor sensitization", "E": "Voltage-gated sodium channels in peripheral axons, slowing recovery from inactivation and reducing ectopic firing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alpha-2-delta subunit of presynaptic calcium channels, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release in the dorsal horn): Pregabalin binds the alpha-2-delta subunit of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, decreasing release of glutamate, substance P, and norepinephrine in the dorsal horn. Duloxetine acts at monoamine transporters, carbamazepine at sodium channels, opioids at mu receptors, and NSAIDs at cyclooxygenase, so each is a tempting but different mechanism."}, {"id": 6, "domain": "community_health", "question": "Investigators design a trial to compare two antibiotics for otitis media, setting alpha at 0.05 and planned power at 80 percent. Slow recruitment leaves far fewer participants than planned, and the final analysis finds no significant difference. Which consequence of the smaller sample is most important when interpreting this result?", "options": {"A": "Selection bias is introduced because a smaller sample produces nonrepresentative treatment groups of participants", "B": "Power is unchanged because it is fixed by alpha, so only the confidence interval widens", "C": "The null hypothesis is proven true, because a smaller sample cannot generate a significant difference", "D": "Statistical power falls, raising the chance of a type II error beyond the intended level", "E": "The type I error rate rises above 0.05, so a false positive result becomes more likely"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Statistical power falls, raising the chance of a type II error beyond the intended level): Fewer participants than planned reduces statistical power, which raises the probability of a type II error, so a real difference may have been missed. Alpha is set in advance, so the type I error rate is unchanged. A nonsignificant result does not prove the null hypothesis, and sample size itself does not create selection bias."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 72-year-old man notes that his hats no longer fit and has progressive hearing loss. Serum calcium and phosphate are normal, alkaline phosphatase is markedly elevated, and gamma-glutamyl transferase is normal. A radiograph shows a thickened, cotton wool skull. Which malignant complication is most closely associated with this disease?", "options": {"A": "Ewing sarcoma arising from the diaphysis with onion skin periosteal reaction and a characteristic translocation", "B": "Multiple myeloma with lytic lesions and monoclonal protein production in the marrow of axial bones", "C": "Giant cell tumor of bone arising in the epiphysis with multinucleated osteoclast like cells", "D": "Osteosarcoma or other sarcoma arising from the abnormal bone, presenting as new focal pain and swelling", "E": "Metastatic prostate carcinoma producing osteoblastic lesions through hematogenous spread, especially in the lumbar spine and pelvis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Osteosarcoma or other sarcoma arising from the abnormal bone, presenting as new focal pain and swelling): Normal calcium, phosphate, and gamma-glutamyl transferase with a high alkaline phosphatase of bone origin and a cotton wool skull indicate Paget disease of bone. Sarcomatous transformation, usually osteosarcoma, is the feared complication in long standing disease. Myeloma and prostate metastases can mimic the biochemical picture but are not complications of Paget disease."}, {"id": 8, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 45-year-old man who received a kidney transplant eight years ago and takes tacrolimus and mycophenolate presents with a firm nodule with a central keratin-filled crater on the dorsum of his hand. It enlarged over six weeks. He has had sun exposure as a landscaper. Which diagnosis and behavior is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Amelanotic melanoma, a pigment-poor tumor arising from melanocytes at the dermal-epidermal junction", "B": "Squamous cell carcinoma, with a higher rate of recurrence and metastasis in transplant recipients", "C": "Basal cell carcinoma, which is the most common skin cancer overall and in transplant recipients", "D": "Keratoacanthoma, a benign crateriform lesion that involutes without treatment even in transplant recipients", "E": "Merkel cell carcinoma, a neuroendocrine tumor linked to polyomavirus with a violaceous nodule"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Squamous cell carcinoma, with a higher rate of recurrence and metastasis in transplant recipients): Chronic immunosuppression and sun exposure markedly raise squamous cell carcinoma risk, which outnumbers basal cell carcinoma in transplant recipients and behaves more aggressively. A rapidly growing keratin-filled crater is the classic appearance. Keratoacanthoma-like lesions in this setting are treated as squamous carcinoma, and Merkel cell tumors are usually smooth violaceous nodules."}, {"id": 9, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 45-year-old painter has 3 months of right shoulder pain with overhead work. Pain occurs at 70 to 120 degrees of abduction and is reproduced when the examiner passively flexes the arm to 90 degrees and internally rotates it. Strength is full. Which structure is most likely being compressed beneath the coracoacromial arch?", "options": {"A": "Suprascapular nerve at the spinoglenoid notch beneath the inferior transverse scapular ligament", "B": "Glenoid labrum between the glenoid rim and humeral head during throwing motions", "C": "Supraspinatus tendon and subacromial bursa pinched between the humeral head and acromion", "D": "Long head of the biceps tendon within the bicipital groove beneath the transverse ligament", "E": "Subscapularis tendon at its insertion on the lesser tuberosity during forced external rotation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Supraspinatus tendon and subacromial bursa pinched between the humeral head and acromion): A painful arc between 60 and 120 degrees and a positive Hawkins test indicate subacromial impingement, in which the supraspinatus tendon and bursa are compressed under the acromial arch. Full strength argues against a significant rotator cuff tear. Biceps, labral, subscapularis, and nerve lesions each have a different provocation pattern."}, {"id": 10, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 4-year-old girl has had three months of a swollen, warm right knee and mild limp that is worse in the morning, without fever or rash. A second joint, the left ankle, became swollen last month. Antinuclear antibody is positive and rheumatoid factor is negative. Which ocular complication requires scheduled slit-lamp screening in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Optic neuritis with painful loss of vision from demyelination of the optic nerve", "B": "Chronic anterior uveitis, which is often painless and silent until synechiae or cataract develop", "C": "Scleritis with severe boring pain, typically seen in adults with longstanding rheumatoid arthritis", "D": "Proliferative retinopathy from retinal ischemia with neovascularization after years of uncontrolled hyperglycemia", "E": "Keratoconjunctivitis sicca from lacrimal gland infiltration, causing dry eyes and gritty discomfort"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Chronic anterior uveitis, which is often painless and silent until synechiae or cataract develop): Oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis with positive antinuclear antibody carries the highest risk of chronic anterior uveitis, which is usually asymptomatic and can lead to synechiae, band keratopathy and cataract. This is why guideline based slit-lamp screening is done at scheduled intervals even when the eyes look normal. Scleritis is a rheumatoid arthritis complication in adults."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A tall, thin 19-year-old man develops sudden right pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea while resting at school. Examination shows hyperresonance to percussion, absent breath sounds, and decreased tactile fremitus on the right. Blood pressure and heart rate are normal. Which change in intrapleural mechanics explains the collapse of his right lung?", "options": {"A": "Pleural fluid accumulation raises hydrostatic pressure and compresses the lung from its basal surface", "B": "Surfactant loss raises alveolar surface tension, so the lung collapses despite normal pleural pressure", "C": "Pleural pressure equalizes with atmospheric, so unopposed elastic recoil of the lung pulls it inward", "D": "Pleural pressure becomes more negative, so the chest wall recoils inward and compresses the lung", "E": "Airway obstruction by mucus traps gas distally so absorption atelectasis shrinks the affected lung"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pleural pressure equalizes with atmospheric, so unopposed elastic recoil of the lung pulls it inward): Normally the lung recoils inward while the chest wall recoils outward, which holds intrapleural pressure below atmospheric. A ruptured subpleural bleb lets air enter the pleural space, so the pressure equalizes and the lung collapses. Hyperresonance with absent breath sounds distinguishes this from an effusion or mucus-plug atelectasis."}, {"id": 12, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 78-year-old man with advanced heart failure becomes obtunded and cannot communicate. His daughter, whom he named as health care proxy, is asked about intubation. He never discussed ventilators with her, but she knows his values and lifelong attitudes well. Which decision standard should she apply first?", "options": {"A": "Best interests standard, weighing the burdens and benefits of ventilation as a reasonable person would", "B": "Her own preference, choosing the course she personally would want if she were in his position", "C": "Clinical futility standard, withholding intubation because the physician judges the intervention medically inappropriate for him", "D": "Substituted judgment, choosing what he would decide given his known values and past statements", "E": "Implied consent standard, proceeding with intubation because most patients in emergencies would accept life-saving treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Substituted judgment, choosing what he would decide given his known values and past statements): The surrogate hierarchy starts with substituted judgment, reconstructing what the patient himself would choose from his values, and the proxy knows them well. The objective benefit and burden standard applies only when his wishes cannot be inferred. Her own preference is irrelevant to the decision."}, {"id": 13, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 74-year-old man has had progressive gait difficulty over eight months, with small shuffling steps, feet that seem stuck to the floor, and two falls. His wife notes urinary urgency with occasional incontinence and slowed thinking. Head CT shows enlarged ventricles out of proportion to sulcal atrophy. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Vascular dementia with stepwise decline and focal neurologic findings following multiple small cerebral infarcts", "B": "Parkinson disease with resting tremor, bradykinesia, and sustained good response to levodopa therapy", "C": "Normal pressure hydrocephalus with impaired cerebrospinal fluid absorption producing the gait, urinary, and cognitive triad", "D": "Alzheimer disease with prominent early memory loss and relatively preserved gait until the late stages", "E": "Dementia with Lewy bodies with fluctuating cognition and well-formed visual hallucinations alongside mild parkinsonism"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Normal pressure hydrocephalus with impaired cerebrospinal fluid absorption producing the gait, urinary, and cognitive triad): The triad of magnetic gait, urinary urgency, and cognitive slowing with ventriculomegaly out of proportion to atrophy identifies normal pressure hydrocephalus, which may improve after cerebrospinal fluid drainage. Alzheimer disease usually preserves gait until late, and Parkinson disease would show tremor and respond to levodopa."}, {"id": 14, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 52-year-old man with acute myeloid leukemia has had prolonged neutropenia after induction chemotherapy. On day 14 he develops persistent fever despite broad-spectrum antibiotics, pleuritic chest pain and cough with hemoptysis. CT shows multiple nodules, one with a surrounding ground-glass halo. Serum galactomannan is positive. Which organism is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Rhizopus species, a mold with broad nonseptate hyphae branching at right angles that invades vessels", "B": "Candida albicans, a dimorphic yeast forming pseudohyphae and budding yeast within necrotic lung tissue", "C": "Pneumocystis jirovecii, an atypical fungus forming cysts in alveoli that produces diffuse ground-glass infiltrates", "D": "Cryptococcus neoformans, an encapsulated yeast with narrow-based buds that spreads from lung to meninges", "E": "Aspergillus fumigatus, a mold with septate hyphae branching at acute angles that invades pulmonary vessels"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Aspergillus fumigatus, a mold with septate hyphae branching at acute angles that invades pulmonary vessels): Prolonged neutropenia is the major risk factor for invasive aspergillosis, which causes angioinvasive nodules with a halo sign and releases galactomannan detectable in serum. Septate hyphae with acute-angle branching distinguish it from Rhizopus, which has broad nonseptate hyphae and is typical in diabetic ketoacidosis. Yeasts and Pneumocystis do not produce this angioinvasive pattern with a positive galactomannan."}, {"id": 15, "domain": "community_health", "question": "An 85-year-old nursing home resident with end-stage Alzheimer dementia is bedbound, nonverbal and has had two aspiration pneumonias this year. She now eats poorly. Her son and surrogate asks for a feeding tube to prevent aspiration and prolong her life. She left no written directive. Which recommendation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Percutaneous gastrostomy placement, because bypassing swallowing removes the aspiration risk and improves nutritional status", "B": "Short trial of nasogastric feeding, followed by gastrostomy if she regains weight and tolerates it well", "C": "Defer to the facility's standard protocol of providing artificial nutrition since no written directive exists", "D": "Comfort-focused hand feeding, since tube feeding has not been shown to prolong survival in advanced dementia", "E": "Parenteral nutrition through a central line, because enteral feeding carries aspiration risk in this setting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Comfort-focused hand feeding, since tube feeding has not been shown to prolong survival in advanced dementia): In advanced dementia, feeding tubes do not reduce aspiration, prevent pressure injury or prolong life, and they add risks such as restraint use and infection. Careful hand feeding preserves comfort and contact and fits goals of care discussed with the surrogate. Absence of a directive does not oblige artificial nutrition."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 52-year-old man has fatigue and left upper quadrant fullness. Examination shows splenomegaly. Laboratory studies show a white blood cell count of 140,000 per microliter with myelocytes, metamyelocytes, and basophils, and a low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score. Cytogenetics reveal a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22. What is the functional consequence of this translocation?", "options": {"A": "Fusion of the retinoic acid receptor gene blocking promyelocyte maturation at a single stage", "B": "Activating mutation of the thrombopoietin receptor gene (MPL) driving platelet overproduction in marrow megakaryocytes", "C": "Juxtaposition of an antiapoptotic gene with the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus blocking cell death", "D": "Overexpression of cyclin D1 from placement beside an immunoglobulin enhancer driving G1 progression", "E": "A fusion protein with constitutive tyrosine kinase activity driving unchecked proliferation of myeloid precursors"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A fusion protein with constitutive tyrosine kinase activity driving unchecked proliferation of myeloid precursors): The Philadelphia chromosome, t(9;22), joins BCR to ABL1 and produces a fusion tyrosine kinase that is constitutively active, causing chronic myeloid leukemia with marked leukocytosis and a low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score. This is the target of imatinib. The 14;18 and 11;14 translocations belong to lymphomas, and 15;17 belongs to acute promyelocytic leukemia."}, {"id": 17, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 35-year-old woman is brought in after ingesting a large quantity of amitriptyline. She is confused with dilated pupils, dry skin and a heart rate of 128. ECG shows a QRS duration of 140 milliseconds and a terminal R wave in aVR. Blood pressure is 82/50 mm Hg. Which treatment is most appropriate first?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous sodium bicarbonate, which raises serum pH and sodium load to relieve sodium channel blockade", "B": "Intravenous flumazenil, which antagonizes the sedative effect and restores alertness and blood pressure", "C": "Oral activated charcoal followed by observation, since the tachycardia and wide QRS usually resolve alone", "D": "Intravenous procainamide, a class IA antiarrhythmic that suppresses the ventricular conduction abnormality and tachycardia", "E": "Intravenous physostigmine, which reverses the anticholinergic delirium and tachycardia and narrows the QRS complex"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous sodium bicarbonate, which raises serum pH and sodium load to relieve sodium channel blockade): A wide QRS and terminal R wave in aVR show sodium channel blockade from a tricyclic, which causes arrhythmias and hypotension. Sodium bicarbonate gives sodium and alkalinizes the blood, reducing drug binding to the channel. Physostigmine and class IA antiarrhythmics are contraindicated, and charcoal alone is inadequate for a toxic patient."}, {"id": 18, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with heart failure develops crampy left-sided abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea one day after an episode of hypotension from gastrointestinal bleeding. She has no fever. Colonoscopy shows pale, edematous mucosa with a sharp segmental demarcation at the splenic flexure, sparing the rectum. Which mechanism best explains this distribution of injury?", "options": {"A": "Atherosclerotic stenosis at the celiac trunk origin limiting perfusion of the foregut and proximal duodenum", "B": "Thrombosis of the inferior mesenteric vein producing venous congestion of the rectosigmoid colon", "C": "Thromboembolic occlusion of the superior mesenteric artery trunk beyond the middle colic branch", "D": "Low-flow injury at the watershed between superior and inferior mesenteric artery territories", "E": "Compression of the superior mesenteric artery between the aorta and the duodenum"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low-flow injury at the watershed between superior and inferior mesenteric artery territories): The splenic flexure lies at the watershed between the superior mesenteric artery and the inferior mesenteric artery, so systemic hypotension produces segmental ischemic colitis there while the rectum is spared by its dual internal iliac supply. An acute proximal superior mesenteric embolus would infarct small bowel as well."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 55-year-old woman has violaceous discoloration and edema of both upper eyelids, scaly erythematous papules over the knuckles, and several months of difficulty rising from a chair. Creatine kinase is markedly elevated. If a muscle biopsy is performed, which finding is most characteristic of her condition?", "options": {"A": "Perifascicular fiber atrophy with inflammatory infiltrates concentrated around perimysial vessels and connective tissue septa", "B": "Rimmed vacuoles and cytoplasmic inclusions with endomysial inflammation in older patients", "C": "Ragged red fibers from subsarcolemmal accumulation of abnormal mitochondria on trichrome stain", "D": "Endomysial CD8 positive T cells surrounding and invading nonnecrotic muscle fibers", "E": "Scattered necrotic fibers with sparse inflammation, seen after prolonged statin exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perifascicular fiber atrophy with inflammatory infiltrates concentrated around perimysial vessels and connective tissue septa): Heliotrope eyelids, Gottron papules and proximal weakness indicate dermatomyositis, a humoral microangiopathy. Biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy with perimysial and perivascular inflammation, unlike polymyositis, where CD8 cells invade fibers within the endomysium."}, {"id": 20, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 22-year-old recruit collapses after prolonged exertion in hot weather. Urine is dark brown, and dipstick is strongly positive for blood, but microscopy shows no red blood cells. Serum potassium is 6.1 mEq/L and calcium is 7.4 mg/dL. Which mechanism best explains the renal injury he is at risk for?", "options": {"A": "Uric acid crystal precipitation in collecting ducts from rapid tumor cell turnover", "B": "Tubular obstruction and oxidant injury from filtered myoglobin, worsened by volume depletion", "C": "Acute interstitial nephritis from drug hypersensitivity with eosinophils in the urine", "D": "Light chain cast nephropathy from monoclonal immunoglobulin fragments precipitating with Tamm-Horsfall protein", "E": "Hemoglobin cast nephropathy from intravascular hemolysis releasing free hemoglobin into the filtrate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tubular obstruction and oxidant injury from filtered myoglobin, worsened by volume depletion): Dark urine that is heme positive on dipstick without red cells points to myoglobinuria from rhabdomyolysis. Myoglobin obstructs tubules, generates ferryl heme oxidant injury and constricts afferent vessels, especially with volume depletion and acidic urine. Hemolysis would also give heme pigment but does not fit the exertional setting with muscle breakdown, hyperkalemia and hypocalcemia."}, {"id": 21, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 29-year-old runner has right-sided mid-back pain. On motion testing, a thoracic segment rotates normally to the left, but rotation to the right stops well short of the normal physiologic limit, with a firm, abnormal resistance felt before the usual end of active range. Which term best describes the point where the segment stops?", "options": {"A": "Restrictive barrier, a pathologic limit that reduces motion inside the usual normal range", "B": "Physiologic barrier, the normal limit of active motion before passive elastic resistance begins", "C": "Anatomic barrier, the bony or ligamentous limit beyond which injury to tissue occurs", "D": "Neutral point, the midrange position where tissues are most relaxed in all planes", "E": "Elastic barrier, the normal end range of passive motion beyond the active limit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Restrictive barrier, a pathologic limit that reduces motion inside the usual normal range): A restrictive barrier is an abnormal limit that shortens motion within the normal range, which is what distinguishes it from the physiologic barrier that ends normal active motion. The anatomic barrier lies beyond all of these and is the limit at which tissue damage begins, so it cannot describe an early firm stop."}, {"id": 22, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 28-year-old woman has three weeks of dry cough, tender red nodules on both shins, and bilateral ankle swelling. The chest radiograph is shown. Serum calcium is 11.4 mg/dL with low parathyroid hormone. Biopsy of a hilar node shows noncaseating granulomas. Which mechanism best accounts for her hypercalcemia?", "options": {"A": "Granuloma macrophages express 1-alpha-hydroxylase and convert vitamin D to calcitriol without feedback regulation", "B": "Primary parathyroid adenoma with autonomous parathyroid hormone release and increased bone resorption", "C": "Excess dietary vitamin D intake producing intestinal calcium hyperabsorption with suppressed parathyroid hormone secretion", "D": "Tumor secretion of parathyroid hormone-related peptide acting on bone and renal tubules", "E": "Osteolytic metastases releasing calcium directly from bone through local cytokine-mediated osteoclast activation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Granuloma macrophages express 1-alpha-hydroxylase and convert vitamin D to calcitriol without feedback regulation): Erythema nodosum, hilar adenopathy and noncaseating granulomas indicate sarcoidosis. Activated macrophages in the granulomas express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, producing calcitriol outside the kidney without the normal feedback control. This raises calcium and suppresses parathyroid hormone, unlike primary hyperparathyroidism.", "image": "question_images/q_22.png"}, {"id": 23, "domain": "community_health", "question": "In an animal laboratory exercise, a student adds a fixed concentration of an unknown compound to tissue exposed to increasing concentrations of acetylcholine. The dose-response curve shifts to the right in a parallel fashion, and the maximal contraction is unchanged. Which mechanism best explains the compound's action?", "options": {"A": "Physiologic antagonism, with the compound activating an opposing receptor pathway in the same tissue", "B": "Reversible competitive antagonism at the same binding site, overcome by higher agonist concentration", "C": "Irreversible noncompetitive antagonism, lowering efficacy because functional receptors are removed from the pool", "D": "Allosteric inhibition, binding a distinct site to lower both agonist affinity and maximal response", "E": "Partial agonism, with the compound competing for receptors but yielding a lower maximal response"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reversible competitive antagonism at the same binding site, overcome by higher agonist concentration): A parallel rightward shift with an unchanged maximum indicates that the compound competes with the agonist for the same site, so more agonist restores the full response and potency falls. Noncompetitive or irreversible antagonists lower the maximum because functional receptors are lost. A partial agonist would also lower the ceiling."}, {"id": 24, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 55-year-old man with right low back pain has a tender point at the posterior iliac crest and is treated with counterstrain. The physician holds the position of ease for about ninety seconds, then rapidly stands him upright. On retesting, tenderness has returned fully. The patient has hypertension. Which error most likely explains the result?", "options": {"A": "Holding the position beyond thirty seconds overstimulated the muscle spindle and worsened tenderness", "B": "His hypertension prevented the proprioceptive reset, so treatment could not work", "C": "Passive shortening was replaced by rapid compression directly over the tender point", "D": "The position of ease was too close to the restrictive barrier of motion", "E": "Returning him to neutral too abruptly restarted the abnormal proprioceptive reflex activity"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Returning him to neutral too abruptly restarted the abnormal proprioceptive reflex activity): A quick return from the position of ease can restimulate the spindle and bring the tenderness back, so the patient should be returned to neutral slowly and passively. The hold of about ninety seconds was adequate. Hypertension does not block the effect, and the technique does not use compression."}, {"id": 25, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 25-year-old man treated with dapsone for dermatitis herpetiformis develops headache, dyspnea, and cyanosis. Pulse oximetry reads 85 percent on room air and does not improve with supplemental oxygen. Arterial blood appears chocolate brown, and PaO2 is normal. Which treatment best addresses the underlying problem?", "options": {"A": "Methylene blue to reduce ferric iron in hemoglobin back to the ferrous state", "B": "Sodium thiosulfate to donate sulfur for conversion of cyanide into thiocyanate for excretion", "C": "Folinic acid to bypass dihydrofolate reductase inhibition and restore red cell maturation", "D": "Hydroxocobalamin to bind cyanide and restore cytochrome oxidase function in mitochondria", "E": "Hyperbaric oxygen to displace carbon monoxide from hemoglobin and increase dissolved oxygen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Methylene blue to reduce ferric iron in hemoglobin back to the ferrous state): Dapsone oxidizes hemoglobin iron to the ferric state, forming methemoglobin that cannot carry oxygen and gives chocolate brown blood with a normal PaO2. Methylene blue is reduced by NADPH and then reduces methemoglobin back to functional hemoglobin. Cyanide antidotes target cytochrome oxidase poisoning, a different mechanism."}, {"id": 26, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 36-week newborn is delivered after severe oligohydramnios and has anuria, pulmonary hypoplasia, a flattened face and poorly ossified calvarium. The mother has chronic hypertension and took several daily medications throughout pregnancy without supervision. Which drug and mechanism best explain this newborn's findings?", "options": {"A": "Atenolol, which causes fetal growth restriction through reduced uteroplacental perfusion and beta blockade", "B": "Hydrochlorothiazide, which depletes fetal volume and electrolytes and causes neonatal thrombocytopenia with hyponatremia", "C": "Lisinopril, which lowers fetal angiotensin II and impairs glomerular filtration and urine production", "D": "Lithium, which alters fetal cardiac development and produces Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve", "E": "Warfarin, which blocks vitamin K dependent proteins and causes nasal hypoplasia with stippled epiphyses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lisinopril, which lowers fetal angiotensin II and impairs glomerular filtration and urine production): ACE inhibitors cross the placenta in the second and third trimesters and lower fetal angiotensin II, reducing glomerular filtration. The resulting oligohydramnios causes pulmonary hypoplasia, Potter-like facies and calvarial hypoplasia. Atenolol can limit fetal growth but does not abolish urine output, and warfarin produces bony and nasal malformations from early exposure."}, {"id": 27, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 30-year-old woman has chronic headaches after a motor vehicle collision two years ago. Cranial examination shows the sphenoid's right greater wing rotated cephalad and the left greater wing caudad, while the occiput rotates in the opposite direction around an anteroposterior axis. Which strain pattern is present?", "options": {"A": "Right sidebending rotation, named for the side of cranial convexity around vertical axes", "B": "Left torsion, named for the side of the inferior greater wing of the sphenoid", "C": "Right lateral strain, named for the side toward which the sphenoid base translates", "D": "Right torsion, named for the side of the superior greater wing of the sphenoid", "E": "Superior vertical strain, named for the cephalad position of the sphenoid base"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Right torsion, named for the side of the superior greater wing of the sphenoid): Torsion is named for the side of the higher greater wing of the sphenoid, here the right, with the occiput rotating the opposite way around the anteroposterior axis. Sidebending rotation is named for the side of convexity and occurs around vertical axes. Lateral and vertical strains involve translation of the sphenoid base rather than opposite rotation of the greater wings."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 29-year-old man involved in a minor car collision requests narcotic pain medication and a disability letter, reporting severe back pain. Examination is inconsistent, with normal gait when distracted and no objective deficit. His lawyer scheduled this visit and he seems reluctant to cooperate. Which statement best explains his behavior?", "options": {"A": "Deliberate feigning of symptoms driven by an external goal such as drugs or compensation", "B": "Chronic pain from a missed lumbar injury that produces variable findings on repeated examination", "C": "Unconscious production of motor symptoms to resolve an internal psychological conflict about the collision", "D": "Excessive anxiety about pain that is disproportionate and persistent despite normal examination findings", "E": "Deliberate symptom fabrication whose only goal is assuming the sick role and receiving medical attention"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Deliberate feigning of symptoms driven by an external goal such as drugs or compensation): Conscious fabrication or exaggeration for a clear external reward, here opioids and disability benefits with legal involvement, defines malingering, which is not a psychiatric disorder. Factitious disorder is also deliberate but lacks external incentive, while conversion and somatic symptom disorder are not consciously produced."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 66-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis takes ibuprofen daily and has hypertension controlled with lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide. After a week of vomiting and poor oral intake, he presents with rising creatinine and reduced urine output. Which mechanism most directly explains the kidney injury caused by his analgesic in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Loss of prostaglandin mediated afferent arteriolar dilation, lowering glomerular filtration during volume depletion", "B": "Thromboxane mediated renal vasoconstriction from unopposed platelet activation after cyclooxygenase inhibition", "C": "Immune mediated interstitial nephritis from drug induced T cell hypersensitivity within tubulointerstitium", "D": "Blockade of efferent arteriolar constriction by the drug, lowering intraglomerular filtration pressure", "E": "Direct tubular toxicity from drug crystals precipitating in the collecting duct lumen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Loss of prostaglandin mediated afferent arteriolar dilation, lowering glomerular filtration during volume depletion): In a volume depleted patient, afferent arteriolar dilation by prostaglandins preserves glomerular filtration, and cyclooxygenase inhibition removes it. Combined with an ACE inhibitor that dilates the efferent arteriole and a diuretic, this produces the so called triple whammy of prerenal injury. Interstitial nephritis is possible but does not fit this hemodynamic setting best."}, {"id": 30, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 32-year-old woman in her third trimester is awakened at night by tingling in her right hand that improves when she shakes it. Tapping over the volar wrist reproduces the symptoms. Which sensory finding is most consistent with the compressed nerve at this site?", "options": {"A": "Numbness of the ring and little fingers with normal sensation over the dorsal hand", "B": "Numbness of the thumb, index and middle fingers including the skin over the thenar eminence", "C": "Numbness over the dorsal first web space and the posterior forearm with normal fingers", "D": "Numbness of the lateral forearm and thumb base with normal sensation of the fingers", "E": "Numbness of the thumb, index and middle fingers with normal sensation over the thenar eminence"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Numbness of the thumb, index and middle fingers with normal sensation over the thenar eminence): Compression beneath the flexor retinaculum affects the median nerve's digital branches to the lateral three and a half digits, but the palmar cutaneous branch arises proximally and passes superficial to the retinaculum, so thenar skin stays normal. Loss of thenar skin sensation would suggest a lesion proximal to the wrist."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 62-year-old man has chronic left supraclavicular fullness and tight fascia at the thoracic inlet. His physician plans myofascial release at the inlet to improve lymphatic return. Lymph from the entire lower body and left upper extremity re-enters the venous system at which site that this treatment is meant to unburden?", "options": {"A": "Arch of the azygos vein where it empties into the superior vena cava", "B": "Junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins posterior to the clavicle", "C": "Junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins near the right clavicle", "D": "Cisterna chyli anterior to the first two lumbar vertebral bodies beside the aorta", "E": "Right atrium at the superior vena cava junction behind the third costal cartilage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins posterior to the clavicle): The thoracic duct drains the lower body and the left upper body and empties at the left venous angle, so fascial restriction at the thoracic inlet can impede its outflow. The cisterna chyli is the origin of the duct rather than its termination, which makes it a tempting but incorrect site here."}, {"id": 32, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A male newborn is cyanotic within hours of birth, and oxygen saturation remains at 70 percent despite 100 percent oxygen. Chest radiograph shows a narrow mediastinum with increased pulmonary vascular markings. Cyanosis improves transiently when prostaglandin E1 is given. Failure of which embryologic process most likely caused this defect?", "options": {"A": "Persistent single truncal vessel from absent division of the truncus arteriosus, overriding both ventricles", "B": "Failure of the ductus arteriosus to constrict after birth, leaving a left to right shunt", "C": "Anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular septum causing right ventricular outflow obstruction", "D": "Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to spiral during division of the truncus arteriosus", "E": "Abnormal fusion of the endocardial cushions leaving a common atrioventricular canal defect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to spiral during division of the truncus arteriosus): Transposition of the great arteries arises when the aorticopulmonary septum fails to spiral, creating parallel circulations that need a mixing shunt such as a patent duct to survive. Prostaglandin E1 keeps the ductus open and briefly improves saturation. Tetralogy of Fallot also causes cyanosis but shows decreased pulmonary markings and a boot-shaped heart."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old man has itching after hot showers and episodes of burning pain in his fingertips. Examination shows facial plethora and a palpable spleen tip. Hematocrit is 59 percent, platelets are elevated, arterial oxygen saturation is 98 percent, and serum erythropoietin is low. Which mechanism most likely explains the erythrocytosis?", "options": {"A": "Activating mutation of a cytoplasmic kinase causing growth factor independent signaling in marrow progenitors", "B": "Relative erythrocytosis from diuretic use and contracted plasma volume with normal red cell mass", "C": "Chronic nocturnal hypoxemia from obstructive sleep apnea stimulating renal erythropoietin release and marrow expansion", "D": "Ectopic erythropoietin secretion by a renal tumor raising red cell mass despite normal oxygenation", "E": "Reciprocal translocation producing a constitutive fusion kinase and expansion of the granulocyte lineage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Activating mutation of a cytoplasmic kinase causing growth factor independent signaling in marrow progenitors): A high hematocrit with a low erythropoietin level, splenomegaly, thrombocytosis, aquagenic pruritus and erythromelalgia indicates polycythemia vera, driven by a JAK2 V617F mutation that makes progenitors independent of erythropoietin. Secondary causes would show high or inappropriately normal erythropoietin. Normal oxygen saturation also argues against hypoxic drive."}, {"id": 34, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with a long-standing lumbar curve has L2 through L4 held in neutral. On examination, the group is freely sidebent to the right and freely rotated to the left, while flexion and extension are normal. How should this dysfunction be written?", "options": {"A": "L2 to L4 neutral, rotated right, sidebent left, a type I group dysfunction", "B": "L2 to L4 neutral, rotated right, sidebent right, a type I group dysfunction", "C": "L3 extended, rotated left, sidebent left, a type II single segment dysfunction", "D": "L2 to L4 flexed, rotated left, sidebent right, a type II group dysfunction", "E": "L2 to L4 neutral, rotated left, sidebent right, a type I group dysfunction"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (L2 to L4 neutral, rotated left, sidebent right, a type I group dysfunction): A neutral group curve follows type I mechanics, with sidebending and rotation to opposite sides, and the dysfunction is named for the directions of freedom. Free right sidebending with free left rotation therefore gives neutral, rotated left, sidebent right. A neutral group is not a single flexed or extended segment, so the type II designations do not fit."}, {"id": 35, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 34-year-old woman is found unresponsive with a respiratory rate of 6 per minute and pinpoint pupils. Her breathing improves after an intravenous antagonist, but 90 minutes later she becomes sedated again with a rate of 8. She had ingested methadone. Which of the following best explains the recurrence?", "options": {"A": "Rebound hypoventilation from antagonist withdrawal, as occurs after abrupt cessation of chronic opioids", "B": "Pharmacodynamic tolerance to the reversal drug develops within an hour, reducing its receptor occupancy", "C": "Accumulation of an active opioid metabolite from acute renal failure, overwhelming the single reversal dose", "D": "Short duration of the reversal drug relative to the opioid, permitting renewed mu receptor activation", "E": "Delayed absorption of a second sedative, since the antagonist blocks only opioid receptors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Short duration of the reversal drug relative to the opioid, permitting renewed mu receptor activation): Naloxone is a competitive antagonist with a short duration of action, while methadone acts for a day or longer, so receptors are re-occupied as the antagonist wears off. This is why patients need observation or an infusion, and the recurrence of the same opioid picture argues against a new sedative."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 26-year-old woman has had persistent auditory hallucinations, paranoid delusions, and disorganized speech for three months, with a clear decline in her job performance. Mood symptoms were never prominent, urine toxicology is negative, and a thyroid panel is normal. She was well before onset. Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Brief psychotic disorder, since psychosis lasts at least one day but under one month", "B": "Schizophrenia, since characteristic psychotic symptoms have persisted with functional decline for at least six months", "C": "Schizophreniform disorder, since psychotic symptoms have lasted over one month but under six months", "D": "Delusional disorder, since fixed nonbizarre beliefs persist for a month or longer without prominent hallucinations", "E": "Schizoaffective disorder, since delusions or hallucinations persist for two weeks or more without a mood episode"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Schizophreniform disorder, since psychotic symptoms have lasted over one month but under six months): The symptoms meet the criteria for schizophrenia but have lasted three months, so schizophreniform disorder is the diagnosis until six months have passed. Brief psychotic disorder ends within a month, and schizoaffective or delusional disorders do not fit absent mood episodes and with prominent hallucinations."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with a catecholamine-secreting adrenal tumor is prepared for surgery with phenoxybenzamine. During preoperative testing, phenylephrine infusion raises her blood pressure far less than expected, even at the highest dose. Which pharmacodynamic mechanism explains why higher phenylephrine doses cannot restore the full response?", "options": {"A": "Partial agonist activity of phenoxybenzamine that caps vascular tone below the full agonist response", "B": "Physiologic antagonism by circulating catecholamines acting at beta-two receptors to dilate vessels", "C": "Reversible competitive blockade that raises the dose needed but preserves the maximal vascular response", "D": "Covalent alpha-receptor blockade that lowers the maximal response by reducing the functional receptor pool", "E": "Tachyphylaxis from depletion of norepinephrine stores that normally mediate indirect sympathomimetic effects"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Covalent alpha-receptor blockade that lowers the maximal response by reducing the functional receptor pool): Phenoxybenzamine binds alpha receptors covalently, so receptors are removed from the available pool and the maximal response falls; extra agonist cannot overcome this. A reversible competitive blocker would only shift the curve rightward. Phenylephrine acts directly, so depletion of stored norepinephrine would not blunt it."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 31-year-old woman at 36 weeks of gestation presents with severe abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding. The uterus is rigid and tender. She then oozes blood from her intravenous site. Fibrinogen is 90 mg/dL, D-dimer is markedly elevated, and PT and aPTT are prolonged. Which process best explains these coagulation findings?", "options": {"A": "Placental thromboplastin entering the circulation, driving systemic thrombin generation and factor consumption", "B": "Immune destruction of platelets by antiplatelet antibodies produced during the late third trimester", "C": "Circulating lupus anticoagulant interfering with phospholipid-dependent clotting assays and causing falsely prolonged aPTT", "D": "Hepatic synthetic failure from fatty liver of pregnancy reducing clotting factor and fibrinogen production", "E": "Vitamin K deficiency from malabsorption reducing carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX and X"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Placental thromboplastin entering the circulation, driving systemic thrombin generation and factor consumption): Placental abruption releases tissue factor-rich material into the maternal circulation, causing disseminated intravascular coagulation with consumption of fibrinogen and factors, prolonged PT and aPTT, and a high D-dimer from secondary fibrinolysis. Hepatic failure would not raise D-dimer so markedly, and a lupus anticoagulant causes thrombosis rather than oozing."}, {"id": 39, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A male infant born at 29 weeks gestation to a mother with poorly controlled diabetes has Apgar scores of 7 and 8. Within two hours he develops grunting, nasal flaring, and intercostal retractions. The chest radiograph shown is obtained. Which pathophysiologic process best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Group B streptococcal pneumonia with early-onset sepsis and neutrophilic alveolar infiltration", "B": "Pulmonary hypoplasia from prolonged oligohydramnios limiting alveolar development and gas exchange surface", "C": "Meconium aspiration producing airway obstruction, chemical pneumonitis and patchy air trapping", "D": "Insufficient surfactant from immature type II pneumocytes causing high alveolar surface tension and atelectasis", "E": "Retained fetal lung fluid from delayed clearance across pulmonary lymphatics and veins"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Insufficient surfactant from immature type II pneumocytes causing high alveolar surface tension and atelectasis): Prematurity and maternal diabetes (fetal hyperinsulinism delays surfactant synthesis) predispose to neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. Without surfactant from type II cells, surface tension collapses alveoli, giving atelectasis, a ground-glass pattern with air bronchograms and low lung volumes. Transient tachypnea and meconium aspiration are disorders of term infants.", "image": "question_images/q_39.png"}, {"id": 40, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 2-year-old boy has three days of rhinorrhea followed by a barking cough and hoarse voice that worsen at night. He has inspiratory stridor at rest, mild retractions, a temperature of 37.9 C, and is drinking fluids. Which organism most likely caused this illness?", "options": {"A": "Haemophilus influenzae type b causing supraglottic cellulitis with drooling and rapid airway obstruction", "B": "Bordetella pertussis causing paroxysmal coughing with an inspiratory whoop after a catarrhal stage", "C": "Corynebacterium diphtheriae producing a toxin-mediated pseudomembrane over the pharynx and larynx", "D": "Parainfluenza virus causing subglottic edema and a narrowed airway below the vocal cords", "E": "Respiratory syncytial virus causing necrosis of small airway epithelium with wheezing and air trapping"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Parainfluenza virus causing subglottic edema and a narrowed airway below the vocal cords): Barking cough, hoarseness and inspiratory stridor after a cold in a toddler describe croup, caused most often by parainfluenza virus infecting the subglottic region. Epiglottitis causes drooling and toxicity, RSV causes lower airway wheezing, and diphtheria and pertussis have distinctive membranes or whooping paroxysms."}, {"id": 41, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 66-year-old man with severe emphysema is seen in clinic. Arterial blood gas at baseline shows pH 7.36, PaCO2 68 mm Hg and bicarbonate 37 mEq/L. A resident asks why the pH is nearly normal despite chronic carbon dioxide retention. Which renal response is responsible for this degree of compensation?", "options": {"A": "Immediate buffering by intracellular proteins and hemoglobin that raises plasma bicarbonate within minutes", "B": "Increased tubular hydrogen secretion and ammonium excretion, with new bicarbonate generation over several days", "C": "Hepatic conversion of lactate to bicarbonate over several days, which buffers the rising carbon dioxide level", "D": "Reduced ammonium production in the proximal tubule, limiting hydrogen loss and preserving the plasma bicarbonate concentration", "E": "Increased bicarbonate excretion by the proximal tubule to offset the acidemia produced by retained carbon dioxide"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increased tubular hydrogen secretion and ammonium excretion, with new bicarbonate generation over several days): Over three to five days, proximal tubule ammoniagenesis and distal hydrogen secretion rise, generating new bicarbonate that returns the pH close to normal in chronic respiratory acidosis. Cell buffering raises bicarbonate only slightly and within minutes, so it cannot account for a bicarbonate of 37 mEq/L."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 67-year-old man on postoperative day 5 after total knee arthroplasty develops sudden dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. Heart rate is 112/min and respiratory rate is 28/min. His arterial blood gas on room air is shown in the table. Chest radiograph is clear. Which interpretation best explains the blood gas?", "options": {"A": "Acute respiratory alkalosis with a normal alveolar-arterial gradient from central hyperventilation driven by postoperative pain", "B": "Acute respiratory acidosis from opioid induced hypoventilation, with hypoxemia and a normal alveolar-arterial gradient", "C": "Mixed respiratory alkalosis and anion gap acidosis from salicylate toxicity after postoperative analgesic use", "D": "Metabolic alkalosis from volume contraction after perioperative diuretics with hypoxemia from compensatory hypoventilation", "E": "Acute respiratory alkalosis with a widened alveolar-arterial gradient from perfusion mismatch and increased dead space"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Acute respiratory alkalosis with a widened alveolar-arterial gradient from perfusion mismatch and increased dead space): The pH is high with a low carbon dioxide and normal bicarbonate, an acute respiratory alkalosis. Estimating the alveolar oxygen at roughly 112 mm Hg and subtracting the measured 62 gives a gradient near 50, which is wide for his age and fits embolic perfusion mismatch rather than hyperventilation alone. Pain driven hyperventilation would keep the gradient normal.", "image": "question_images/q_42.png"}, {"id": 43, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has a proximal colon adenocarcinoma. Her mother had endometrial cancer at 45 and a maternal uncle had colon cancer at 41. Immunohistochemistry of her tumor shows absent MSH2 and MSH6 nuclear staining. Which mechanism best explains tumor development in this family?", "options": {"A": "Germline STK11 mutation producing hamartomatous polyps and an increased risk of breast and pancreatic cancer", "B": "Sequential KRAS then p53 mutation in a sporadic adenoma carcinoma sequence of the left colon", "C": "Loss of APC function producing unchecked beta catenin signaling and hundreds of adenomatous polyps", "D": "Defective DNA mismatch repair allowing microsatellite instability and rapid accumulation of mutations", "E": "Biallelic MUTYH loss impairing base excision repair and producing characteristic G:C to T:A transversions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Defective DNA mismatch repair allowing microsatellite instability and rapid accumulation of mutations): Early proximal colon cancer with endometrial cancer in relatives and loss of MSH2 and MSH6 staining indicates Lynch syndrome from defective mismatch repair, which causes microsatellite instability. APC loss causes florid polyposis, and the sporadic KRAS and p53 sequence does not explain loss of repair protein staining."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 48-year-old man with chronic low back pain presents to the emergency department with six hours of new bilateral leg weakness, difficulty starting urination, and numbness around the buttocks and genitals. Rectal tone is decreased and ankle reflexes are absent. Both legs have severe radiating pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Guillain-Barre syndrome with ascending symmetric weakness and areflexia after a recent diarrheal illness", "B": "Cauda equina syndrome from a large central disc herniation compressing the lumbosacral nerve roots", "C": "Conus medullaris syndrome from a cord tip lesion with early symmetric sphincter and saddle deficits", "D": "Bilateral L5 radiculopathy from lateral recess stenosis causing foot drop and a painful limp", "E": "Acute transverse myelitis with a sensory level on the trunk and spastic paraparesis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cauda equina syndrome from a large central disc herniation compressing the lumbosacral nerve roots): Severe bilateral radicular pain, saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, reduced rectal tone and absent ankle reflexes indicate compression of many lumbosacral nerve roots, a surgical emergency. Conus lesions tend to cause less radicular pain and more symmetric early sphincter loss, and myelitis would produce a trunk sensory level and spasticity."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 62-year-old man who smokes returns from a cruise with high fever, watery diarrhea, headache, and confusion. Sodium is 126 mEq/L. Sputum Gram stain shows many neutrophils but no organisms, and chest radiograph shows patchy infiltrates in both lungs. He is allergic to sulfa drugs. Which test is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Serum galactomannan assay detecting Aspergillus cell wall polysaccharide released into the blood", "B": "Sputum culture on standard sheep blood agar incubated overnight for common bacterial respiratory pathogens", "C": "Urinary antigen assay detecting Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1 lipopolysaccharide shed into the urine", "D": "Urinary antigen assay detecting pneumococcal C polysaccharide that is shed into the urine", "E": "Serum cold agglutinin titer measured against type O red blood cells at refrigerator temperature"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Urinary antigen assay detecting Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1 lipopolysaccharide shed into the urine): Pneumonia with diarrhea, headache, confusion, and hyponatremia after exposure to aerosolized water points to Legionella, which stains poorly and does not grow on routine blood agar. The urinary antigen detects serogroup 1, the most common cause, and is the quick confirmatory test. The pneumococcal urinary antigen is tempting because the format is the same, but it would not identify this organism."}, {"id": 46, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A screening test for a viral infection is being evaluated in a clinic. The test correctly identifies 90 percent of persons who truly have the infection and 80 percent of those without it. A physician asks which term describes the proportion of infected persons who test positive.", "options": {"A": "Sensitivity, the true positive rate among all persons who actually carry the disease", "B": "Accuracy, the proportion of all test results that match true disease status", "C": "Negative predictive value, the health probability among all persons who test negative", "D": "Positive predictive value, the disease probability among all persons who test positive", "E": "Specificity, the true negative rate among all persons who are actually free of disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sensitivity, the true positive rate among all persons who actually carry the disease): Sensitivity is the proportion of truly diseased persons who test positive, so it equals the true positive rate. Specificity describes the healthy group instead, and predictive values depend on the tested population's prevalence, so neither answers a question conditioned on disease status."}, {"id": 47, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "An 82-year-old man in a nursing home eats mostly tea and toast and refuses fruits and vegetables. He has bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages with coiled hairs on the legs, and a slow-healing pressure ulcer. He takes lisinopril and acetaminophen. Prothrombin time and platelet count are normal. Which deficiency is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Phylloquinone deficiency, impairing gamma carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X in the liver", "B": "Thiamine deficiency, impairing pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase activity with peripheral neuropathy and heart failure", "C": "Zinc deficiency, impairing metalloenzyme function with delayed wound healing, hair loss, and impaired taste sensation", "D": "Niacin deficiency, impairing NAD synthesis with dermatitis in sun exposed skin, diarrhea, and dementia", "E": "Ascorbic acid deficiency, impairing hydroxylation of proline and lysine needed for stable collagen cross-linking"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ascorbic acid deficiency, impairing hydroxylation of proline and lysine needed for stable collagen cross-linking): Bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, and impaired wound healing in a man without fruit or vegetables indicate scurvy. Vitamin C is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase, and its lack weakens collagen. A normal prothrombin time and platelet count argue against a vitamin K deficiency, and zinc deficiency does not cause these follicular findings."}, {"id": 48, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old man presents for evaluation of palpitations. His ECG shows a QRS duration of 140 ms with an rSR' pattern in lead V1 and a wide slurred S wave in leads I and V6. Which statement best describes the ventricular activation sequence producing this tracing?", "options": {"A": "The right ventricle depolarizes first, and the left ventricle follows late by slow spread", "B": "An accessory pathway preexcites part of the ventricle, producing a short PR and delta wave", "C": "The posterior fascicle conducts first, then the anterior fascicle fires late after the septal impulse", "D": "The left ventricle depolarizes first, and the right ventricle follows late by slow spread", "E": "Both ventricles depolarize together, but the septum activates right to left producing terminal slurring"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The left ventricle depolarizes first, and the right ventricle follows late by slow spread): In right bundle branch block the septum and left ventricle activate normally, giving the initial small r in V1, and the right ventricle depolarizes late by cell-to-cell spread, causing the terminal R' in V1 and the wide S in lateral leads. Left bundle branch block reverses the sequence and the septal vector, producing broad R waves in the lateral leads."}, {"id": 49, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 31-year-old woman has a Pap test showing a high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion, and cervical biopsy confirms severe dysplasia with koilocytic change. HPV genotyping is positive for type 16. Which viral mechanism most directly contributes to progression toward invasive carcinoma in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Viral E6 binding retinoblastoma protein to release E2F while E7 promotes degradation of p53", "B": "Viral activation of RAS through point mutation, driving constitutive MAPK signaling and uncontrolled proliferation", "C": "Viral inhibition of mismatch repair proteins producing microsatellite instability similar to hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer", "D": "Viral E6 promoting degradation of p53 while E7 binds retinoblastoma protein to release E2F", "E": "Insertion of viral DNA next to c-MYC, driving promoter-mediated overexpression as seen with retroviruses"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Viral E6 promoting degradation of p53 while E7 binds retinoblastoma protein to release E2F): High-risk HPV types express E6, which targets p53 for ubiquitin mediated degradation, and E7, which binds the retinoblastoma protein and frees E2F to drive the cell cycle. Together they remove two key checkpoints. Reversing the targets of the two proteins is the most tempting error."}, {"id": 50, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 9-month-old boy is brought for a well visit. His mother reports that when he is startled by a loud noise, he extends and abducts both arms, then brings them together and cries. He sits without support, babbles, and feeds well, and his growth is normal. Which interpretation of this reflex finding is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "An expected finding reflecting ongoing vestibular maturation in breastfed infants at this age", "B": "A normal variant, since this startle reflex ordinarily persists until the first birthday", "C": "Persistence past six months reflects delayed cortical inhibition of a brainstem reflex and needs workup", "D": "Persistent asymmetric tonic neck reflex, which normally fades by twelve months and needs no assessment", "E": "The parachute reflex, which normally emerges around this age and persists throughout later life"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Persistence past six months reflects delayed cortical inhibition of a brainstem reflex and needs workup): The Moro reflex is a brainstem-mediated primitive reflex that normally disappears by about four to six months as cortical control matures. Persistence at nine months suggests delayed inhibition from cerebral injury or dysfunction. The parachute reflex is easily confused with it, but that reflex appears later and persists for life, and it involves protective forward arm extension when the infant is tipped."}, {"id": 51, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 19-year-old man has tea-colored urine, facial puffiness, and blood pressure of 155/98 mm Hg 2 weeks after a sore throat treated with antibiotics. Urinalysis shows protein 2+ and a sediment finding in the microscopy shown. Serum creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL. Which additional finding is most likely in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Normal serum complement with mesangial IgA deposits on immunofluorescence microscopy", "B": "Low serum C3 with tram-track basement membrane splitting and subendothelial deposits", "C": "Positive anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies with pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis", "D": "Linear IgG deposition along the glomerular basement membrane on immunofluorescence microscopy", "E": "Low serum C3 with subepithelial immune complex humps on electron microscopy"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Low serum C3 with subepithelial immune complex humps on electron microscopy): Nephritic syndrome with red cell casts two weeks after pharyngitis fits poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, which shows low C3 and subepithelial humps from immune complexes. IgA nephropathy follows an infection within a day or two and keeps complement normal. Membranoproliferative disease also lowers C3 but lacks the clear infectious trigger and humps.", "image": "question_images/q_51.png"}, {"id": 52, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has headaches and difficulty seeing cars approaching from the sides while driving. Visual field testing shows loss of both outer visual fields. MRI shows a 2 cm suprasellar extension of a pituitary macroadenoma. Her other pituitary hormones are normal. Which fibers are most likely compressed by this mass?", "options": {"A": "Optic tract fibers posterior to the chiasm, producing a contralateral homonymous hemianopia", "B": "Crossing axons from nasal retinal ganglion cells decussating within the optic chiasm", "C": "Optic radiations carrying superior visual field fibers through Meyer loop in the temporal lobe", "D": "Uncrossed axons from temporal retinal ganglion cells running in the lateral chiasm", "E": "Ipsilateral optic nerve axons anterior to the chiasm, producing monocular loss of vision"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Crossing axons from nasal retinal ganglion cells decussating within the optic chiasm): Nasal retinal fibers carry the temporal visual field and cross in the chiasm, which lies directly above the pituitary. A suprasellar mass compresses these crossing fibers and produces bitemporal hemianopia. Lateral compression, such as from a carotid aneurysm, affects uncrossed temporal retinal fibers instead."}, {"id": 53, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 26-year-old woman with no medical history plans to conceive in the next few months. She asks which daily vitamin supplement she should start now to lower the risk of her baby having a spinal cord closure defect. She follows a mixed diet and takes no medications. Which supplement is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin D, which supports fetal skeletal mineralization and maternal calcium absorption throughout pregnancy", "B": "Vitamin A at high doses, which supports epithelial differentiation and fetal growth during the first trimester", "C": "Folic acid, which supports DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing embryonic cells during early gestation", "D": "Iron, which supports expanded maternal red cell mass and fetal hemoglobin synthesis during the second trimester", "E": "Cobalamin alone, which serves as the methyl donor cofactor for homocysteine remethylation in embryonic cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Folic acid, which supports DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing embryonic cells during early gestation): Folic acid begun before conception and continued into early pregnancy reduces the risk of neural tube defects by supporting thymidylate and purine synthesis in rapidly dividing cells. High dose vitamin A is teratogenic. Iron and vitamin D are useful in pregnancy but do not prevent failure of neural tube closure."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 55-year-old woman has a screening mammogram showing subtle architectural distortion without a discrete mass or calcifications. Core biopsy shows small, uniform, discohesive cells infiltrating the stroma in single files around benign ducts in a targetoid pattern. Which molecular abnormality most directly explains this growth pattern?", "options": {"A": "Loss of E-cadherin expression weakening epithelial cell to cell adhesion and permitting dispersed stromal spread", "B": "Loss of BRCA1 DNA repair function producing high grade medullary sheets with lymphocytic infiltrate", "C": "Amplification of HER2 producing overexpressed growth factor receptor signaling and high grade comedo growth", "D": "Overexpression of BCL2 blocking apoptosis in estrogen driven glandular epithelium forming cohesive nests", "E": "Inactivating mutation of TP53 causing genomic instability and rapidly proliferating pleomorphic cells in solid sheets"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Loss of E-cadherin expression weakening epithelial cell to cell adhesion and permitting dispersed stromal spread): Invasive lobular carcinoma characteristically loses E-cadherin, so tumor cells lose cell to cell cohesion and infiltrate as single files without forming a mass. This explains the subtle distortion on imaging. HER2 amplification and TP53 loss are associated with ductal tumors that form cohesive masses."}, {"id": 55, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 79-year-old man with osteoarthritis, hypertension, and insomnia is evaluated after two falls in three months. His medications are lisinopril, atorvastatin, acetaminophen, amlodipine, and nightly diphenhydramine. He has dry mouth, constipation, and mild morning confusion. Which medication is the best candidate for discontinuation based on Beers criteria?", "options": {"A": "Amlodipine, because calcium channel blockers cause edema and dizziness that most likely explain his falls", "B": "Atorvastatin, because statins cause myopathy and weakness that predominantly explain recurrent falls at this age", "C": "Lisinopril, because angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors commonly cause orthostatic hypotension and falls in older adults", "D": "Diphenhydramine, a sedating anticholinergic antihistamine that raises delirium, constipation, and fall risk in older adults", "E": "Acetaminophen, because liver toxicity at routine doses outweighs analgesic benefit for osteoarthritis in older patients"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diphenhydramine, a sedating anticholinergic antihistamine that raises delirium, constipation, and fall risk in older adults): First-generation antihistamines such as diphenhydramine are on the Beers list because anticholinergic and sedative effects cause confusion, constipation, dry mouth, and falls. His symptoms match these effects. The other drugs are reasonable in this setting, and sleep hygiene is the safer approach to insomnia."}, {"id": 56, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 55-year-old woman has chronic constipation. Osteopathic examination shows restricted atlanto-occipital motion with suboccipital tissue tension. The physician explains that treating this region may influence the autonomic supply of the gut. Which parasympathetic pathway from the brainstem supplies the small bowel and the proximal portion of the large bowel?", "options": {"A": "Pelvic splanchnic nerves from S2 to S4 synapsing in ganglia of the gut wall", "B": "Cranial nerve VII preganglionic fibers from the pons synapsing in the pterygopalatine ganglion", "C": "Cranial nerve IX preganglionic fibers from the medulla synapsing in the otic ganglion", "D": "Greater splanchnic nerve fibers from T5 to T9 synapsing in the celiac ganglion", "E": "Cranial nerve X preganglionic fibers from the medulla oblongata synapsing in the bowel wall"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cranial nerve X preganglionic fibers from the medulla oblongata synapsing in the bowel wall): The vagus nerve carries cranial parasympathetic outflow to the gut through about the splenic flexure, which is why suboccipital and upper cervical work is used for upper and midgut function. Sacral outflow is also parasympathetic but supplies the distal colon. Cranial nerves VII and IX target head glands, not the bowel."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis presents with asterixis and day-night sleep reversal after a gastrointestinal bleed. He is started on an oral nonabsorbable disaccharide and titrated to two to three soft stools daily. Which mechanism best explains the benefit of this drug in his encephalopathy?", "options": {"A": "Stimulation of hepatic urea cycle enzymes, increasing conversion of circulating ammonia into urea", "B": "Selective inhibition of intestinal urease producing bacteria, directly reducing ammonia generation from dietary protein", "C": "Blockade of central GABA-A receptors, reversing neuroinhibition from elevated endogenous benzodiazepine-like ligands", "D": "Colonic bacterial breakdown acidifies stool, trapping ammonia as ammonium and accelerating its fecal elimination", "E": "Branched chain amino acid supplementation that competes with aromatic amino acids for blood brain barrier transport"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Colonic bacterial breakdown acidifies stool, trapping ammonia as ammonium and accelerating its fecal elimination): Lactulose is broken down by colonic bacteria into acids, lowering stool pH so that ammonia is converted to ammonium, which cannot diffuse back into the blood and is excreted. The laxative effect also shortens transit. Rifaximin works by reducing ammonia producing bacteria, which is the tempting wrong mechanism here."}, {"id": 58, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 52-year-old man with BMI 38 snores loudly, wakes unrefreshed and dozes at traffic lights. His wife witnesses breathing pauses. Polysomnography shows an apnea-hypopnea index of 34 events per hour with oxygen nadir 82 percent. He is hypertensive. Which is the most appropriate first-line therapy?", "options": {"A": "Modafinil each morning, which promotes wakefulness and corrects the daytime sleepiness and oxygen desaturation", "B": "Zolpidem at bedtime, a hypnotic that consolidates sleep and reduces the number of breathing pauses", "C": "Mandibular advancement device as sole therapy, which is preferred over pressure therapy in severe disease", "D": "Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty as the initial procedure, which removes redundant pharyngeal tissue and cures severe disease", "E": "Continuous positive airway pressure at night, which splints the collapsing upper airway during sleep"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Continuous positive airway pressure at night, which splints the collapsing upper airway during sleep): Moderate to severe obstructive sleep apnea is treated first with continuous positive airway pressure, which keeps the pharyngeal airway open, and with weight loss, which lowers collapsibility. Wakefulness agents only treat sleepiness. Sedative hypnotics can worsen obstruction, and oral appliances are mainly for mild to moderate disease or intolerance of pressure therapy."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 45-year-old man attends a banquet where beef stew was prepared in bulk and left on the counter at room temperature for hours. Ten hours later, he develops watery diarrhea and crampy abdominal pain without fever or significant vomiting. Symptoms resolve within 24 hours. Other attendees are similarly affected. Which mechanism most likely explains this illness?", "options": {"A": "Mucosal invasion of the distal ileum by an organism causing fever and inflammatory diarrhea", "B": "Spores surviving cooking germinate in slowly cooled food and release enterotoxin in the gut", "C": "Heat-labile toxin elaborated by colonizing organisms that raises cyclic AMP in small bowel enterocytes", "D": "Preformed heat-stable toxin ingested with the food, acting on vagal afferents to trigger vomiting", "E": "Preformed emetic toxin from germinated spores in reheated rice causing vomiting within hours"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Spores surviving cooking germinate in slowly cooled food and release enterotoxin in the gut): Clostridium perfringens spores survive cooking and germinate when large batches cool slowly, and the ingested cells sporulate in the gut and release an enterotoxin that causes watery diarrhea after about 8 to 16 hours. Staphylococcal toxin is preformed, so illness starts within a few hours with prominent vomiting. Salmonella causes fever and a longer incubation."}, {"id": 60, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 24-year-old man is found minimally responsive with a respiratory rate of 6 breaths per minute and pinpoint pupils. Arterial blood gas on room air is in the panel shown. Using an inspired oxygen fraction of 0.21, sea level, and a respiratory quotient of 0.8, which mechanism best accounts for his hypoxemia?", "options": {"A": "Low inspired oxygen tension, because ambient oxygen content is reduced as at high altitude", "B": "Diffusion limitation, because a thickened alveolar membrane slows oxygen equilibration and widens the gradient", "C": "Hypoventilation, because retained carbon dioxide displaces alveolar oxygen and the gradient stays normal", "D": "Ventilation-perfusion mismatch, because low-ratio units add venous admixture and widen the gradient", "E": "Right-to-left shunt, because deoxygenated blood bypasses ventilated alveoli and widens the gradient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hypoventilation, because retained carbon dioxide displaces alveolar oxygen and the gradient stays normal): Alveolar oxygen is about 0.21 times 713 minus PaCO2 divided by 0.8, which is roughly 150 minus 88, or 62 mm Hg. Subtracting the measured PaO2 of 55 gives a gradient near 7 mm Hg, which is normal. Hypoxemia with hypercapnia and a normal gradient therefore reflects hypoventilation from opioid-induced respiratory depression.", "image": "question_images/q_60.png"}, {"id": 61, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 28-year-old woman with bulimia presents with weakness after several weeks of self-induced vomiting. Blood pressure falls from 118/72 supine to 96/60 standing. Serum bicarbonate is 36 mEq/L, potassium is 2.9 mEq/L, and urine chloride is 8 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains why isotonic saline should correct her alkalosis?", "options": {"A": "Sodium load suppresses proximal hydrogen secretion by inhibiting the sodium hydrogen exchanger, reducing bicarbonate reabsorption", "B": "Volume repletion lowers angiotensin II and aldosterone, allowing bicarbonate excretion with chloride delivery distally", "C": "Saline supplies potassium that corrects intracellular acidosis in tubular cells and halts renal bicarbonate generation", "D": "Infused volume dilutes serum bicarbonate and lowers the ratio to carbon dioxide, normalizing the arterial pH", "E": "Saline suppresses antidiuretic hormone, increasing free water excretion and lowering the concentration of serum bicarbonate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Volume repletion lowers angiotensin II and aldosterone, allowing bicarbonate excretion with chloride delivery distally): Vomiting loses gastric acid and volume, and the volume contraction raises angiotensin II and aldosterone, which keep reabsorbing bicarbonate and secreting hydrogen despite the alkalosis. A low urine chloride marks this saline-responsive state. Isotonic saline restores volume and chloride delivery so the kidney can excrete bicarbonate; potassium repletion helps but is not supplied by saline."}, {"id": 62, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 17-year-old girl presents with abdominal distension and a palpable pelvic mass. Imaging shows a 15 cm solid right ovarian mass. Serum alpha-fetoprotein and human chorionic gonadotropin are normal, but lactate dehydrogenase is elevated. Histology shows sheets of large uniform cells with clear cytoplasm separated by fibrous septa containing lymphocytes. Which tumor is most consistent with these findings?", "options": {"A": "Immature teratoma with primitive neuroepithelial rosettes, graded by the amount of immature neural tissue", "B": "Dysgerminoma, the ovarian counterpart of testicular seminoma, usually sensitive to radiation and platinum therapy", "C": "Granulosa cell tumor with Call-Exner bodies, elevated inhibin, and estrogen excess causing endometrial hyperplasia", "D": "Choriocarcinoma with cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast, hemorrhage, and markedly elevated human chorionic gonadotropin levels", "E": "Yolk sac tumor with Schiller-Duval bodies, markedly elevated alpha-fetoprotein, and aggressive behavior in young patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dysgerminoma, the ovarian counterpart of testicular seminoma, usually sensitive to radiation and platinum therapy): Sheets of large clear cells with fibrous septa and lymphocytes, with raised lactate dehydrogenase and normal alpha-fetoprotein and gonadotropin, identify a dysgerminoma. It is the ovarian equivalent of seminoma and is radiosensitive. A yolk sac tumor would be recognized by raised alpha-fetoprotein and Schiller-Duval bodies."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 20-year-old soccer player felt his knee give way after planting and pivoting, followed by rapid swelling. With the knee flexed to 30 degrees and the femur stabilized, the examiner pulls the proximal tibia forward and feels increased excursion with a soft endpoint. Which structure is the primary restraint being tested?", "options": {"A": "Anterior cruciate ligament, which limits the shin sliding in front of the thigh bone", "B": "Posterior cruciate ligament, which limits posterior tibial translation and is injured by a dashboard impact", "C": "Lateral collateral ligament, which resists varus stress and is tested with the knee near 30 degrees", "D": "Medial collateral ligament, which resists valgus stress and is injured by a lateral blow", "E": "Posterolateral corner complex, which limits tibial external rotation and is assessed by the dial test"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Anterior cruciate ligament, which limits the shin sliding in front of the thigh bone): The Lachman test, performed at about 20 to 30 degrees of flexion, assesses anterior tibial translation, which the anterior cruciate ligament chiefly restrains. A soft endpoint and a pivoting mechanism with rapid hemarthrosis support a tear. The posterior cruciate ligament limits posterior translation, and the collaterals are tested with varus and valgus stress."}, {"id": 64, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history is admitted with confusion. Blood pressure is normal, mucous membranes are moist, and there is no edema. Serum sodium is 121 mEq/L, serum osmolality 255 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg, and urine sodium 45 mEq/L. A central lung mass is seen on imaging. Which mechanism explains his hyponatremia?", "options": {"A": "Absent antidiuretic hormone secretion causing dilute urine output and free water loss with hypernatremia risk", "B": "Hypervolemic dilution from reduced cardiac output with avid renal sodium retention and elevated aldosterone", "C": "Cortisol deficiency reducing free water clearance and impairing sodium conservation in the distal nephron", "D": "Aldosterone deficiency causing renal sodium wasting with volume depletion and compensatory thirst-driven water intake", "E": "Excess antidiuretic hormone increasing free water reabsorption in collecting ducts with persistent urinary sodium loss"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Excess antidiuretic hormone increasing free water reabsorption in collecting ducts with persistent urinary sodium loss): Euvolemic hyponatremia with concentrated urine and urine sodium above 40 mEq/L in a smoker with a central mass points to ectopic ADH from small cell carcinoma. Water is retained, which dilutes serum sodium, while natriuresis persists. Adrenal insufficiency can look similar but requires a different clinical context, and heart failure would show edema with low urine sodium."}, {"id": 65, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A trial of a new analgesic for osteoarthritis uses a patient-reported pain scale as its primary outcome. Participants and treating clinicians know who receives the drug, and the placebo is an inactive pill that is visibly different. Patients assigned to the new drug report greater improvement. Which design change would most directly reduce the bias affecting this outcome?", "options": {"A": "Switching to an intention-to-treat analysis so that every randomized participant stays in the original group", "B": "Restricting enrollment to participants with severe osteoarthritis to increase external validity of the findings", "C": "Adding a washout period between treatment arms so carryover effects disappear before the next phase", "D": "Masking treatment assignment from participants and investigators using a look-alike inactive tablet", "E": "Enrolling more participants so that random error decreases and the effect estimate becomes more precise"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Masking treatment assignment from participants and investigators using a look-alike inactive tablet): A subjective outcome in an unmasked trial is vulnerable to expectation effects in both patients and clinicians. Double blinding with an indistinguishable placebo directly targets this problem. A larger sample improves precision but not bias, and intention-to-treat analysis protects against attrition and crossover rather than perception bias."}, {"id": 66, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 3-year-old boy has had six days of fever to 39.5 C that has not responded to acetaminophen. He has bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, cracked red lips, a strawberry tongue, a swollen left cervical lymph node, and red swollen hands with peeling at the fingertips. Which complication is the main reason for early echocardiography?", "options": {"A": "Dilated cardiomyopathy from viral myocarditis, presenting with early systolic dysfunction and ventricular enlargement", "B": "Coronary artery aneurysm from medium vessel vasculitis, with a risk of thrombosis or infarction", "C": "Acute pericardial tamponade from purulent pericarditis, causing hypotension and muffled distant heart sounds", "D": "Infective endocarditis of the aortic valve from bacteremia, with vegetations and embolic phenomena", "E": "Rheumatic mitral stenosis from immune mimicry after pharyngeal streptococcal infection with valve scarring"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Coronary artery aneurysm from medium vessel vasculitis, with a risk of thrombosis or infarction): Kawasaki disease is a medium vessel vasculitis, and the coronary arteries are the most important target, so echocardiography at diagnosis and in follow-up detects aneurysms that can thrombose. Rheumatic mitral disease follows streptococcal infection and develops over years rather than in the acute febrile illness."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 30-year-old woman has had fever, cough, and nasal congestion for 3 days. She is otherwise healthy, and examination shows no sign of bacterial infection. Her osteopathic physician recommends fluids, rest, and a treatment aimed at lymphatic drainage to support her recovery. Which tenet best explains this approach to her illness?", "options": {"A": "The body can regulate itself and has an inherent capacity to heal when supported", "B": "Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered anatomy changes physiology and the reverse", "C": "The body is a unit, and each person is a combination of body, mind, and spirit", "D": "Health maintenance should be prioritized through screening, immunization, and reduction of modifiable risk factors", "E": "Rational treatment rests on understanding body unity, self regulation, and the relationship between form and activity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The body can regulate itself and has an inherent capacity to heal when supported): Supporting hydration, rest, and drainage while the body recovers reflects the tenet of self regulation and self healing. The fourth tenet is a summary of the other three and the preventive principle is worthwhile but is not a tenet."}, {"id": 68, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 44-year-old woman notes a painless, slowly enlarging, firm, mobile 3 cm mass near the angle of the jaw over two years. Facial nerve function is intact. Fine needle aspiration shows epithelial cells and myoepithelial cells within a myxoid and chondroid stroma. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Mucoepidermoid carcinoma, the most common salivary malignancy, composed of mucinous, squamous, and intermediate cells", "B": "Warthin tumor, a benign papillary cystic lesion with lymphoid stroma, often bilateral in older male smokers", "C": "Adenoid cystic carcinoma, a slow growing malignancy with a cribriform pattern and marked perineural invasion", "D": "Acinic cell carcinoma, a low grade tumor of serous acinar differentiation with basophilic granular cytoplasm", "E": "Pleomorphic adenoma, the most common benign salivary neoplasm, which carries recurrence risk if enucleated"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Pleomorphic adenoma, the most common benign salivary neoplasm, which carries recurrence risk if enucleated): A painless, slowly growing, mobile parotid mass with a mixture of epithelial and myoepithelial cells in a myxochondroid stroma is a pleomorphic adenoma. Simple enucleation can leave tumor pseudopodia and lead to recurrence. Warthin tumor has lymphoid stroma, and the carcinomas lack the chondroid stroma and often cause nerve findings."}, {"id": 69, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old carpenter hurt his mid-back lifting while twisting. T6 moves symmetrically in neutral, but when the physician sidebends the segment to the right, rotation to the left and flexion are then restricted, and bending in any other plane likewise changes available motion. He also has mild right knee crepitus. Which Fryette principle explains this?", "options": {"A": "Type I mechanics, where a neutral group curve couples sidebending and rotation to opposite sides", "B": "Fryette's first law applied to a group curve that remains symmetrical in neutral posture", "C": "Type II mechanics, where a flexed or extended single segment couples sidebending and rotation same side", "D": "Type III mechanics, where initiating movement in one plane alters the range available in the others", "E": "Fryette's second law applied to a non-neutral lone segment sidebending and rotating to the same side"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Type III mechanics, where initiating movement in one plane alters the range available in the others): Type III describes how motion introduced in one plane modifies the available motion in the remaining planes, which matches the finding that sidebending first changes rotation and flexion. The symmetric neutral testing argues against a type I group pattern, and a type II pattern requires a segment held in flexion or extension."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 61-year-old man, nine years after a kidney transplant on tacrolimus and mycophenolate, has scaly keratotic papules on the dorsal hands and a firm, crusted nodule on the left ear helix. He farmed for decades. A biopsy of the nodule is planned. Which cutaneous malignancy is most strongly increased in transplant recipients compared with the general population?", "options": {"A": "Basal cell carcinoma, the most common skin cancer in people with normal immune function", "B": "Squamous cell carcinoma, promoted by impaired immune surveillance of ultraviolet damaged keratinocytes in sun exposed skin", "C": "Kaposi sarcoma, a human herpesvirus 8 related vascular tumor that rises with immunosuppression", "D": "Melanoma, arising from melanocytes with a modest rise in risk after solid organ transplantation", "E": "Merkel cell carcinoma, a polyomavirus associated neuroendocrine tumor of sun exposed skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Squamous cell carcinoma, promoted by impaired immune surveillance of ultraviolet damaged keratinocytes in sun exposed skin): Chronic immunosuppression and ultraviolet damage greatly increase squamous cell carcinoma, which outnumbers basal cell carcinoma in transplant recipients, the reverse of the general population. Kaposi sarcoma and Merkel carcinoma are also increased but are much rarer, which is why sun protection and regular skin examination are emphasized."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 49-year-old man with chronic heavy alcohol use is brought in confused and unable to walk steadily. Examination shows horizontal nystagmus, bilateral lateral rectus weakness, and a wide-based gait. Glucose is 64 mg/dL, and an intravenous dextrose infusion is available. What is the best next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Give intravenous folate and cyanocobalamin to treat the alcohol related neuropathy before further testing", "B": "Give naloxone to reverse possible opioid intoxication presenting with confusion and ataxia", "C": "Give parenteral thiamine first, since carbohydrate metabolism consumes the cofactor and can worsen neuronal injury", "D": "Give intravenous magnesium sulfate to treat hypomagnesemia causing cerebellar dysfunction and refractory nystagmus", "E": "Give dextrose now to correct hypoglycemia, and add thiamine after the glucose level normalizes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give parenteral thiamine first, since carbohydrate metabolism consumes the cofactor and can worsen neuronal injury): This is Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia) from thiamine deficiency in alcohol use disorder. Thiamine is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase, so a glucose load given first can exhaust remaining stores and worsen mammillary body injury. Hypoglycemia still needs treatment, but thiamine goes before or with it."}, {"id": 72, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 4-year-old unvaccinated girl presents with a fever of 39.8 C that began abruptly six hours ago, along with muffled voice, drooling, and refusal to lie flat. She sits leaning forward with her chin extended and has no cough. Which is the most appropriate immediate step?", "options": {"A": "Start oral dexamethasone and discharge home with return precautions after a short observation", "B": "Give nebulized racemic epinephrine and observe for improvement in the emergency department", "C": "Secure the airway in the operating room with anesthesia and otolaryngology present", "D": "Obtain a lateral neck radiograph in the radiology suite to confirm a thumb sign", "E": "Examine the oropharynx with a tongue depressor to visualize the swollen epiglottis directly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Secure the airway in the operating room with anesthesia and otolaryngology present): Abrupt toxic fever, drooling, muffled voice and the tripod posture in an unvaccinated child indicate epiglottitis, a rapidly obstructing airway emergency. The airway should be secured under controlled conditions by experts, and any agitation, throat examination or transport for imaging risks sudden obstruction. Racemic epinephrine and steroids treat croup, which has a barking cough instead."}, {"id": 73, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 22-year-old woman is brought from an all-night dance event with confusion, jaw clenching and a seizure. Friends say she drank several liters of water and felt intensely close to everyone earlier. Temperature is 39.6 C, pulse 130 per minute, pupils are dilated, serum sodium is 124 mEq/L. Which substance most likely caused this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Phencyclidine, an NMDA receptor antagonist causing agitation, nystagmus, hyperthermia and violent behavior with seizures", "B": "MDMA, a serotonin releasing amphetamine that causes hyperthermia, bruxism and excess antidiuretic hormone release", "C": "Cocaine, a monoamine reuptake blocker that causes vasospasm, tachycardia, hypertension and hyperthermia with seizures", "D": "Lysergic acid diethylamide, a serotonin receptor agonist producing perceptual distortion, mydriasis and mild tachycardia", "E": "Synthetic cannabinoid, a full CB1 agonist producing agitation, tachycardia, vomiting and occasional seizures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (MDMA, a serotonin releasing amphetamine that causes hyperthermia, bruxism and excess antidiuretic hormone release): Intense emotional closeness, bruxism, hyperthermia, seizure and hyponatremia point to MDMA, which releases serotonin and promotes antidiuretic hormone secretion, and the large water intake worsens dilution. Cocaine causes hyperthermia and seizures but not an empathogenic state or hyponatremia. Phencyclidine typically produces nystagmus and violence rather than dilutional sodium loss."}, {"id": 74, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 68-year-old man has 3 months of low back pain and fatigue. Calcium is 12.1 mg/dL, hemoglobin is 9.2 g/dL, and creatinine is 3.4 mg/dL. Urine dipstick is negative for protein, but sulfosalicylic acid testing is strongly positive. Skeletal survey shows punched-out lytic lesions. What best explains his kidney injury?", "options": {"A": "Immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane activating complement and producing proliferative glomerulonephritis", "B": "Glomerular deposition of amyloid fibrils derived from light chains causing heavy albuminuria and nephrotic syndrome", "C": "Monoclonal light chains precipitating with Tamm-Horsfall protein to form obstructing casts in distal tubules", "D": "Uric acid crystal precipitation in collecting ducts after rapid tumor cell turnover and lysis", "E": "Hypercalcemia causing renal vasoconstriction and volume depletion, producing prerenal azotemia from impaired concentrating ability"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Monoclonal light chains precipitating with Tamm-Horsfall protein to form obstructing casts in distal tubules): Multiple myeloma produces free light chains that dipsticks, which detect albumin, miss, while sulfosalicylic acid detects them. These light chains bind Tamm-Horsfall protein and form obstructing casts in distal tubules, causing cast nephropathy. Hypercalcemia can contribute but does not explain the Bence Jones proteinuria, and amyloid would give heavy albuminuria."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 30-year-old man has a cutaneous abscess that grows gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase positive and coagulase positive. Susceptibility testing shows resistance to oxacillin and cefazolin. He recently completed a hospital stay for a joint replacement and has no allergies. Which alteration most directly explains this resistance pattern?", "options": {"A": "Mutation of the ribosomal binding site that prevents macrolide attachment to the bacterial ribosome", "B": "Production of a plasmid encoded penicillinase that hydrolyzes the beta lactam ring of penicillin", "C": "Loss of outer membrane porin channels that limits beta lactam entry into the periplasmic space", "D": "Acquisition of mecA encoding an altered penicillin binding protein with low affinity for beta lactams", "E": "Alteration of the D alanine D alanine terminus to D lactate that blocks vancomycin binding"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acquisition of mecA encoding an altered penicillin binding protein with low affinity for beta lactams): Methicillin resistant Staphylococcus aureus carries mecA on the SCCmec cassette, producing PBP2a, which has low affinity for nearly all beta lactams including oxacillin and cefazolin. Plasmid penicillinase explains penicillin resistance only, since oxacillin is penicillinase stable."}, {"id": 76, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 7-year-old girl has developed axillary odor and sparse pubic hair over six months. Examination shows Tanner stage 1 breasts, no recent growth acceleration, and a bone age within one year of chronologic age. Her mother reports no exposure to topical hormone creams. Which explanation best accounts for her findings?", "options": {"A": "Central precocious puberty from early pulsatile gonadotropin-releasing hormone secretion driving estrogen-mediated breast development", "B": "Early adrenal androgen secretion without activation of the gonadal axis, known as premature adrenarche", "C": "Exogenous androgen exposure from a topical product used by a household adult causing virilization", "D": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia from 21-hydroxylase deficiency with rapid growth and markedly advanced bone age", "E": "An estrogen-secreting ovarian granulosa cell tumor causing rapid breast development and vaginal bleeding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Early adrenal androgen secretion without activation of the gonadal axis, known as premature adrenarche): Adrenarche is the rise of adrenal androgens that produces body odor and pubic or axillary hair without breast development. Normal growth velocity and near-normal bone age argue against central precocious puberty and hyperplasia. The pattern is benign, though it warrants follow-up for later progression."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has 3 months of weight loss, heat intolerance, palpitations, and tremor. Pulse is 112/min. Examination shows diffuse nontender thyroid enlargement, a bruit over the gland, and the eye findings in the photograph shown. Which mechanism best explains her thyroid hormone excess?", "options": {"A": "Pituitary thyrotroph adenoma secreting excess TSH that drives follicular hormone synthesis through normal receptors", "B": "Viral infection causing granulomatous inflammation and leakage of stored T3 and T4 from damaged follicles", "C": "Autonomous follicular adenoma producing excess T3 and T4 independent of TSH receptor stimulation", "D": "Antithyroid peroxidase antibodies destroying follicles with release of preformed T3 and T4 into circulation", "E": "Autoantibodies stimulating the TSH receptor, driving unregulated synthesis and release of T3 and T4"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Autoantibodies stimulating the TSH receptor, driving unregulated synthesis and release of T3 and T4): Diffuse enlargement with a bruit and infiltrative eye disease is typical of Graves disease, caused by IgG antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor. Hormone output is unregulated and diffusely increased. A toxic adenoma and thyroiditis do not cause ophthalmopathy.", "image": "question_images/q_77.png"}, {"id": 78, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 28-year-old man visits his primary care physician after four episodes of sudden chest tightness, palpitations, sweating and fear of dying that peaked within ten minutes. Cardiac workup and thyroid studies are normal. He now avoids driving on highways. Which is the most appropriate long-term pharmacologic treatment?", "options": {"A": "An SSRI such as sertraline, started low and titrated, for sustained reduction of attack frequency", "B": "Bupropion extended release, to raise dopamine and norepinephrine and reduce anticipatory avoidance behavior", "C": "Propranolol taken before episodes, to block peripheral adrenergic symptoms and prevent attacks reliably", "D": "Scheduled alprazolam three times daily, for rapid control with durable benefit and low dependence risk", "E": "Buspirone titrated daily, a partial serotonin agonist with proven efficacy for recurrent panic attacks"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An SSRI such as sertraline, started low and titrated, for sustained reduction of attack frequency): Panic disorder is treated long term with an SSRI or SNRI plus cognitive behavioral therapy, starting at a low dose because early jitteriness can aggravate attacks. A benzodiazepine can bridge the first weeks but is not sound maintenance therapy because of tolerance, withdrawal and misuse risk. Buspirone helps generalized anxiety but is not effective for panic."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 28-year-old man has tendon xanthomas over both Achilles tendons and a corneal arcus. His father had a myocardial infarction at age 42. The lipid panel shown reveals markedly elevated LDL cholesterol with normal triglycerides, and he has no secondary cause of dyslipidemia. Which defect most likely accounts for these findings?", "options": {"A": "Apolipoprotein C-II deficiency, impairing lipoprotein lipase activation and clearance of chylomicrons and VLDL remnants", "B": "Overproduction of hepatic VLDL from insulin resistance, raising triglycerides and small dense LDL particles", "C": "Defective LDL receptor function, reducing hepatic clearance of LDL particles from plasma by receptor-mediated endocytosis", "D": "Absent microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, preventing apolipoprotein B particle assembly in liver and intestine", "E": "Apolipoprotein E2 homozygosity, impairing hepatic remnant uptake and raising both cholesterol and triglycerides"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defective LDL receptor function, reducing hepatic clearance of LDL particles from plasma by receptor-mediated endocytosis): Tendon xanthomas, corneal arcus, premature coronary disease in a relative and isolated LDL elevation indicate familial hypercholesterolemia from defective LDL receptors, so LDL clearance falls. Normal triglycerides argue against remnant disorders, lipoprotein lipase problems and insulin-resistant VLDL overproduction. Abetalipoproteinemia gives the opposite pattern with very low LDL.", "image": "question_images/q_79.png"}, {"id": 80, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has intensely itchy, flat-topped, violaceous polygonal papules on her wrists and shins. Examination of her buccal mucosa shows a lacy white reticular pattern. She reports her symptoms began during a stressful period caring for a relative. Which infection should be specifically screened for?", "options": {"A": "Hepatitis C virus, since chronic infection is associated with oral and cutaneous lichen planus", "B": "Sarcoptes scabiei, since burrows and intensely itchy papules appear on the wrists and finger webs", "C": "Group A streptococcus, since pharyngeal infection precedes eruptive guttate papules on the trunk", "D": "Treponema pallidum, since secondary disease produces papulosquamous eruptions on the palms and soles", "E": "Human immunodeficiency virus, since acute seroconversion produces a diffuse pruritic papular eruption"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hepatitis C virus, since chronic infection is associated with oral and cutaneous lichen planus): The flat-topped violaceous papules with Wickham striae in the mouth are lichen planus, which has a recognized association with chronic hepatitis C infection. Secondary syphilis, guttate psoriasis and scabies can cause itchy or scaly eruptions but do not give polygonal violaceous papules with lacy oral changes."}, {"id": 81, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 45-year-old man arrives in the emergency department after a motorcycle crash with hypotension and ongoing hemorrhage. His blood type is unknown and no records are available. The trauma team starts a massive transfusion protocol that includes plasma. Which plasma type can be given safely to a recipient of unknown ABO type?", "options": {"A": "Group O negative plasma, because it lacks D, A and B antigens on donor cells", "B": "Group A plasma, because it lacks anti-A antibodies and carries only mild anti-B titers", "C": "Group O plasma, because it lacks both A and B antigens on donor cells", "D": "Group B plasma, because it lacks anti-B antibodies and avoids the highest hemolysis risk", "E": "Group AB plasma, because it lacks both anti-A and anti-B antibodies that attack recipient cells"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Group AB plasma, because it lacks both anti-A and anti-B antibodies that attack recipient cells): Plasma carries the donor's antibodies, so the safest plasma for an unknown recipient is the one with no anti-A or anti-B, which is group AB. Group O plasma contains both anti-A and anti-B and would hemolyze group A, B or AB recipient cells. This is the reverse of the red cell rule, where group O is the universal donor."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 61-year-old man receiving cisplatin develops severe nausea and is given a drug that blocks serotonin-gated ion channels on vagal afferents and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone. He also takes sotalol for atrial fibrillation. Which adverse effect is of greatest concern with this combination?", "options": {"A": "Tardive dyskinesia from chronic blockade of nigrostriatal dopamine receptors", "B": "QT interval prolongation with risk of torsades de pointes on electrocardiogram", "C": "Respiratory depression from activation of central mu opioid receptors", "D": "Hyperprolactinemia with galactorrhea from tuberoinfundibular dopamine receptor blockade", "E": "Urinary retention and dry mouth from muscarinic receptor blockade"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (QT interval prolongation with risk of torsades de pointes on electrocardiogram): Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist that can prolong the QT interval, and sotalol adds additional QT prolongation through potassium channel blockade, raising the risk of torsades. The other effects belong to dopamine antagonists, antimuscarinics or opioids."}, {"id": 83, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 27-year-old woman had painful monocular vision loss that resolved over a month. Now she has four days of leg numbness and weakness, and the left eye fails to adduct on rightward gaze with nystagmus of the abducting eye. Symptoms worsen after a hot shower. MRI shows periventricular ovoid lesions. Which cerebrospinal fluid finding is most supportive?", "options": {"A": "Xanthochromia with erythrocytes persisting equally in the first and final collection tubes", "B": "Oligoclonal immunoglobulin G bands in spinal fluid, with increased intrathecal antibody synthesis", "C": "Elevated 14-3-3 protein with a normal cell count in rapidly progressive dementia", "D": "Neutrophilic pleocytosis with markedly reduced glucose and elevated protein concentration", "E": "Albuminocytologic dissociation with markedly elevated protein and a nearly normal cell count"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Oligoclonal immunoglobulin G bands in spinal fluid, with increased intrathecal antibody synthesis): Optic neuritis, internuclear ophthalmoplegia, symptoms worsening with heat, and periventricular lesions disseminated in time and space indicate multiple sclerosis. Oligoclonal IgG bands in spinal fluid but not matched in serum support the diagnosis. Albuminocytologic dissociation points to Guillain-Barre syndrome instead."}, {"id": 84, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 45-year-old man has had pneumonia for 8 days and remains febrile despite appropriate antibiotics. Chest radiograph shows a moderate right pleural effusion. Thoracentesis yields turbid fluid with pH 6.9, glucose 28 mg/dL, markedly elevated lactate dehydrogenase, and many neutrophils. He uses an albuterol inhaler. Which is the best next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Repeat thoracentesis in 24 hours while continuing the current antibiotics and observing the fluid", "B": "Start high dose systemic corticosteroids to reduce inflammation of the pleural surfaces and fever", "C": "Perform chemical pleurodesis with talc to prevent recurrent accumulation of fluid within the pleura", "D": "Place a chest tube for continuous drainage, since this fluid is a complicated parapneumonic effusion", "E": "Obtain a closed pleural biopsy to exclude tuberculous pleuritis before changing any therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Place a chest tube for continuous drainage, since this fluid is a complicated parapneumonic effusion): A pleural pH below 7.2, low glucose, and high lactate dehydrogenase indicate a complicated parapneumonic effusion approaching empyema, which requires drainage with a chest tube because antibiotics alone fail. Repeat sampling delays the necessary drainage. Pleurodesis is for recurrent malignant effusions, and steroids do not clear loculated infected fluid."}, {"id": 85, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 24-year-old woman has progressive bilateral hearing loss and tinnitus. Her father had similar hearing loss and a spinal tumor removed at a young age. MRI shows bilateral cerebellopontine angle masses. A mutation in which gene on which chromosome best explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "NF2 on chromosome 22 encoding merlin, a membrane cytoskeletal tumor suppressor protein", "B": "TSC2 on chromosome 16 encoding tuberin, which inhibits mTOR signaling", "C": "NF1 on chromosome 17 encoding neurofibromin, a negative regulator of RAS signaling", "D": "TP53 on chromosome 17 encoding a tumor suppressor that arrests the cell cycle", "E": "VHL on chromosome 3 encoding a protein that degrades hypoxia inducible factor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (NF2 on chromosome 22 encoding merlin, a membrane cytoskeletal tumor suppressor protein): Bilateral vestibular schwannomas with an affected parent and spinal tumors define neurofibromatosis type 2, caused by loss of the NF2 gene on chromosome 22. Neurofibromatosis type 1 is on chromosome 17 and causes neurofibromas, café-au-lait spots, and optic gliomas, not bilateral eighth nerve tumors."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 26-year-old woman with generalized epilepsy has been seizure-free for three years on valproate. She is not using contraception and asks about becoming pregnant next year. Her neurologic examination and laboratory values are normal. Which counseling plan best reduces the risk of neural tube defects in a future pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Stop valproate once pregnancy is confirmed and rely on folic acid supplements to prevent defects", "B": "Stop all antiseizure medication before conception since three seizure free years makes recurrence unlikely", "C": "Transition to a safer antiseizure drug before conception and begin higher dose folic acid supplementation", "D": "Change antiseizure drugs in the second trimester, after the neural tube has already closed", "E": "Continue valproate at the lowest effective dose and start prenatal vitamins after a missed period"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transition to a safer antiseizure drug before conception and begin higher dose folic acid supplementation): Valproate carries the highest neural tube defect risk among common antiseizure drugs, and the neural tube closes around day 28, often before pregnancy is recognized. The risk is reduced by changing to a lower risk agent and supplementing folic acid before conception. Switching in the second trimester arrives long after the vulnerable period has passed, and stopping all medication risks uncontrolled seizures."}, {"id": 87, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 58-year-old man has lost 8 kg over three months with early satiety and postprandial fullness. He mentions a hard, painless 2 cm lump above the left clavicle, medial to the sternocleidomastoid. He drinks one glass of wine weekly. Hemoglobin is 10 g/dL with microcytosis. Which primary site is the most likely source of this node?", "options": {"A": "Left lung apex, because apical tumors spread to ipsilateral scalene and supraclavicular nodes", "B": "Thyroid gland, because its lymph drains to central compartment and lower deep cervical nodes", "C": "Nasopharynx, because its lymph drains to retropharyngeal and upper deep cervical nodes along the jugular chain", "D": "Stomach, because abdominal lymph drains upward via the thoracic duct into the left venous angle", "E": "Floor of mouth, because its lymph drains to submental and submandibular nodes then deep cervical nodes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stomach, because abdominal lymph drains upward via the thoracic duct into the left venous angle): A hard left supraclavicular node, known as Virchow node, drains lymph from the thoracic duct, which collects abdominal lymph. With early satiety, weight loss, and iron deficiency anemia, an occult gastric adenocarcinoma is most likely. Head and neck primaries drain to higher cervical levels, and the symptoms point away from a lung or thyroid source."}, {"id": 88, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has several months of difficulty climbing stairs and combing her hair. She has a violaceous eyelid rash and scaly papules over the knuckles. Creatine kinase is elevated. Muscle strength testing shows symmetric proximal weakness without sensory loss. Which finding is expected on muscle biopsy?", "options": {"A": "Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation composed of B cells and CD4 T cells", "B": "Endomysial infiltrate of CD8 T cells invading nonnecrotic fibers expressing MHC class I", "C": "Necrotizing fibers with scant inflammation and macrophage clearance, as seen with statin therapy", "D": "Type 2 fiber atrophy without inflammation, as seen with chronic glucocorticoid excess", "E": "Rimmed vacuoles and eosinophilic inclusions within fibers with endomysial inflammation in older men"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perifascicular atrophy with perivascular inflammation composed of B cells and CD4 T cells): Heliotrope rash and Gottron papules identify dermatomyositis, a complement mediated microangiopathy of the perimysial vessels that leaves perifascicular atrophy and perivascular inflammation by B and CD4 T cells. The endomysial CD8 infiltrate is characteristic of polymyositis, which lacks skin findings. Rimmed vacuoles occur in inclusion body myositis in older patients."}, {"id": 89, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with long-standing heart failure takes furosemide and lisinopril. She reports mild exertional dyspnea but no chest pain at rest. On examination, the paraspinal tissue from T1 to T5 on the left is cool, dry, thickened, and stringy, with little tenderness. Which interpretation best explains these tissue findings?", "options": {"A": "Persistent sympathetic facilitation from cardiac disease, producing fibrotic texture in the upper thoracic segments", "B": "Parasympathetic overactivity from vagal tone, producing cool dry tissue over the same thoracic segments", "C": "Primary rib dysfunction of the second rib, producing localized fibrosis independent of the heart", "D": "Acute facilitation from a new myocardial infarction, producing warm moist tissue at those segments", "E": "Diuretic induced dehydration of the skin, producing thickened dry tissue without any visceral reflex contribution"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Persistent sympathetic facilitation from cardiac disease, producing fibrotic texture in the upper thoracic segments): Chronic visceral disease leads to long-standing facilitation that evolves from warm, boggy tissue to cool, dry, fibrotic, ropy tissue at the cardiac segments, T1 to T5. Acute infarction would give warm, moist, tender tissue, which is the tempting mistake. Tissue texture, rather than diuretic use or a single rib, reflects the duration and visceral source of the reflex."}, {"id": 90, "domain": "community_health", "question": "During a measles outbreak in a town of 50,000 people, 200 residents develop the disease and 4 die. Officials want a measure that reflects the proportion of affected patients who die from the illness, rather than the burden of death across the whole town. Which measure should they report, and what is its value?", "options": {"A": "Attack rate, 0.4 percent, from cases divided by the total town population", "B": "Case fatality, 2 percent, from deaths among cases divided by the number of cases", "C": "Case fatality, 0.008 percent, from deaths divided by the total town population", "D": "Survival proportion, 98 percent, from patients who lived divided by the number who became ill", "E": "Mortality rate, 8 per 100,000, from deaths divided by the total town population"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Case fatality, 2 percent, from deaths among cases divided by the number of cases): Case fatality uses only affected patients as the denominator: 4 deaths among 200 cases is 2 percent. The mortality rate of 8 per 100,000 divides the same deaths by the whole town, and the attack rate describes how many became ill. A survival proportion of 98 percent is the complement, not the requested measure."}, {"id": 91, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has 3 months of progressive difficulty climbing stairs and combing her hair. Examination shows a violaceous periorbital rash and scaly erythematous papules over the knuckles. Creatine kinase is markedly elevated. Her physician is concerned about an associated condition and plans age appropriate screening. Which finding on muscle biopsy is most characteristic of her disease?", "options": {"A": "Endomysial infiltrate of CD8 positive T cells surrounding and invading non necrotic muscle fibers", "B": "Ragged red fibers with abnormal mitochondrial accumulation beneath the sarcolemma on trichrome stain", "C": "Necrotizing fibers with sparse inflammation and antibody directed against signal recognition particle", "D": "Rimmed vacuoles with inclusions and endomysial inflammation in fibers of the quadriceps", "E": "Perifascicular atrophy with perimysial inflammatory infiltrate of CD4 positive T cells and B cells"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Perifascicular atrophy with perimysial inflammatory infiltrate of CD4 positive T cells and B cells): The heliotrope rash and Gottron papules identify dermatomyositis, a complement mediated microangiopathy of muscle. Biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy with perimysial and perivascular inflammation. The endomysial CD8 pattern marks polymyositis, and rimmed vacuoles mark inclusion body myositis."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 55-year-old man who worked 25 years as a sandblaster has slowly progressive dyspnea. Chest radiograph shows multiple small upper lobe nodules and thin calcification along the rims of enlarged hilar lymph nodes. He smokes half a pack daily. He asks which complication his occupational lung disease makes more likely. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Pleural plaques with calcification, because silica deposits preferentially on the parietal pleura", "B": "Tuberculosis, since silica uptake injures alveolar macrophages and weakens their control of inhaled mycobacteria", "C": "Granulomatous sarcoid-like disease, because silica triggers a delayed cell-mediated reaction like beryllium", "D": "Malignant mesothelioma, because silica fibers migrate to the pleura and transform mesothelial cells", "E": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis, because silica acts as an inhaled antigen provoking a type III reaction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tuberculosis, since silica uptake injures alveolar macrophages and weakens their control of inhaled mycobacteria): Silicosis causes upper lobe nodules and eggshell calcification of hilar nodes. Silica is engulfed by alveolar macrophages and damages them, which impairs killing of mycobacteria and raises the risk of tuberculosis. Mesothelioma and pleural plaques are linked to asbestos."}, {"id": 93, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 29-year-old man arrives after a stab wound to the back with an entry wound left of the midline at T10. Examination reveals left leg weakness and a Babinski sign on the left. Vibration and position sense are absent in the left leg. What finding is expected in the right leg below the lesion?", "options": {"A": "Loss of pain and temperature, since spinothalamic fibers cross close to their level of entry", "B": "Normal sensation of every modality, since the lesion damaged only the left half of the cord", "C": "Saddle anesthesia with loss of anal tone, because sacral roots are compressed at the conus", "D": "Loss of fine touch and proprioception, because dorsal column fibers ascend on the same side", "E": "Spastic weakness with Babinski sign, because corticospinal fibers cross in the medulla before descending"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Loss of pain and temperature, since spinothalamic fibers cross close to their level of entry): This is Brown-Sequard syndrome from a left hemisection. Motor and dorsal column loss are ipsilateral, while spinothalamic fibers cross within a segment or two of entry, so the opposite leg loses pain and temperature below the level. Corticospinal fibers cross in the medulla, so weakness is on the side of the lesion."}, {"id": 94, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and obesity is examined by an osteopathic physician who palpates small, firm, tender, pea-sized tissue changes in the deep fascia over the anterior chest wall and corresponding posterior paraspinal areas. Which description best fits these palpatory findings?", "options": {"A": "Segmental facilitation, a lowered spinal cord threshold causing paraspinal hypertonicity at the affected level", "B": "Myofascial trigger points, taut muscle bands producing referred pain on compression and a local twitch response", "C": "Counterstrain tender points, muscle and tendon sites treated by passive positioning into the position of ease", "D": "Chapman reflex points, neurolymphatic tissue changes that reflect visceral dysfunction and guide diagnosis and treatment", "E": "Acupuncture points, meridian sites that regulate qi flow and are needled to treat distant disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chapman reflex points, neurolymphatic tissue changes that reflect visceral dysfunction and guide diagnosis and treatment): Chapman reflex points are small, firm, pea-sized neurolymphatic tissue changes in deep fascia with paired anterior and posterior locations, and they reflect visceral dysfunction. Trigger points and counterstrain points sit in muscle and tendon, facilitation describes a spinal mechanism rather than the palpable nodule, and acupuncture points belong to a different system."}, {"id": 95, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A newborn is jaundiced at 20 hours of age. The mother's and infant's blood bank results are shown in the panel. The mother had a previous uncomplicated delivery with no prenatal care during that pregnancy. Which earlier intervention would most likely have prevented this infant's condition?", "options": {"A": "Antenatal corticosteroids given to accelerate fetal hepatic bilirubin conjugation before the prior delivery", "B": "Maternal plasmapheresis combined with intravenous immunoglobulin throughout the earlier pregnancy and delivery", "C": "Anti-D immune globulin at 28 weeks gestation and within 72 hours of the first delivery", "D": "Intrauterine transfusion of the fetus during the second trimester of the earlier pregnancy", "E": "Delayed umbilical cord clamping at the earlier delivery to reduce maternal-fetal red cell mixing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anti-D immune globulin at 28 weeks gestation and within 72 hours of the first delivery): An Rh-negative mother with anti-D antibodies and a positive direct antiglobulin test in an Rh-positive infant, with matching ABO types, indicates Rh hemolytic disease from sensitization during the first Rh-positive pregnancy. Anti-D immune globulin at 28 weeks and within 72 hours after delivery clears fetal red cells before the mother mounts an immune response. Treatments for fetal anemia occur only after sensitization.", "image": "question_images/q_95.png"}, {"id": 96, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has acute hepatitis B. A liver biopsy shows scattered shrunken, intensely eosinophilic hepatocytes with fragmented nuclei, lying in sinusoids as round bodies, with no surrounding inflammatory infiltrate. These hepatocytes are being killed by cytotoxic T cells. Which molecule directly performs the final proteolytic dismantling of cellular proteins in these hepatocytes?", "options": {"A": "Bcl-2, a mitochondrial membrane protein that blocks cytochrome c release and prevents cell death", "B": "Calpain, a calcium dependent protease that degrades cytoskeletal proteins during irreversible membrane injury", "C": "Granzyme B, a serine protease delivered by cytotoxic T cells that cleaves and activates procaspases", "D": "Phospholipase A2, an enzyme that hydrolyzes membrane phospholipids and releases arachidonic acid during necrosis", "E": "Caspase-3, an executioner protease activated by cleavage that digests nuclear and cytoskeletal proteins"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Caspase-3, an executioner protease activated by cleavage that digests nuclear and cytoskeletal proteins): Councilman bodies are apoptotic hepatocytes: shrunken, eosinophilic, with fragmented nuclei and no inflammation. Caspase-3 is the executioner caspase that cleaves cellular proteins and activates DNases. Granzyme B from the T cell starts the cascade by activating procaspases, but it is not the final effector, and Bcl-2 would oppose death."}, {"id": 97, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 74-year-old woman taking omeprazole for ten years presents with muscle cramps, tremor, and perioral tingling. Her calcium remains low despite adequate vitamin D and oral supplements, and her potassium is also low. She had a recent vertebral compression fracture. Which explanation best accounts for her refractory electrolyte abnormalities?", "options": {"A": "Hypomagnesemia from reduced intestinal absorption, impairing parathyroid hormone secretion and tissue response", "B": "Calcium carbonate malabsorption from raised gastric pH, correctable with citrate salts alone", "C": "Chronic kidney disease with phosphate retention producing secondary hypocalcemia over years", "D": "Vitamin B12 malabsorption from reduced acid causing neuropathy and secondary hypocalcemia", "E": "Autoimmune hypoparathyroidism with low parathyroid hormone from glandular destruction by antibodies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hypomagnesemia from reduced intestinal absorption, impairing parathyroid hormone secretion and tissue response): Long-term acid suppression is associated with hypomagnesemia, which blunts parathyroid hormone release and action and causes hypocalcemia and hypokalemia that resist replacement until magnesium is corrected. Calcium carbonate absorption does fall with higher gastric pH, but that would not explain the low potassium or tetany. Autoimmune and renal causes lack supporting findings."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "An 8-month-old boy has an intensely itchy, weeping, erythematous rash on his cheeks and extensor limbs, sparing the diaper area. His mother has asthma and his father has seasonal allergic rhinitis. Which underlying abnormality best explains the skin barrier defect?", "options": {"A": "Inflammatory response to Malassezia yeast overgrowth within sebum-rich areas of the scalp", "B": "Deficiency of transglutaminase 1 impairing cornified envelope cross-linking in lamellar ichthyosis", "C": "Loss-of-function mutation of filaggrin, impairing keratin filament aggregation and epidermal water retention", "D": "Defective keratin 5 or 14 producing basal keratinocyte fragility and blistering after friction", "E": "Autoantibodies against desmoglein 3 disrupting adhesion between suprabasal keratinocytes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Loss-of-function mutation of filaggrin, impairing keratin filament aggregation and epidermal water retention): Infantile atopic dermatitis with a personal and family atopic history is linked to filaggrin loss-of-function mutations, which weaken the stratum corneum and let water escape and allergens enter. Pemphigus, ichthyosis and epidermolysis bullosa involve different proteins, and Malassezia drives seborrheic dermatitis rather than extensor eczema."}, {"id": 99, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 6-year-old boy has recurrent painful knee swelling after minor play and bled for hours after a dental extraction. His maternal uncle had similar joint problems. His parents report that he also has seasonal allergies. The coagulation panel shown is obtained. Which deficiency best explains the pattern of results?", "options": {"A": "Factor VIII deficiency, reducing assembly of the intrinsic tenase complex on activated platelets", "B": "Vitamin K deficiency, reducing gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX and X", "C": "von Willebrand factor deficiency, reducing platelet adhesion to exposed subendothelial collagen at injury", "D": "Factor XI deficiency, reducing thrombin-driven amplification of the intrinsic pathway on platelet surfaces", "E": "Factor IX deficiency, reducing activation of factor X by the intrinsic pathway complex"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Factor VIII deficiency, reducing assembly of the intrinsic tenase complex on activated platelets): An isolated prolonged aPTT that corrects on mixing, with a normal PT, normal platelet count, normal bleeding time and a markedly low factor VIII activity, in a boy with hemarthroses and an affected maternal uncle, indicates X-linked hemophilia A. Factor IX would give the same clinical picture but its activity is normal here. Von Willebrand disease would prolong bleeding time and rarely causes joint bleeds.", "image": "question_images/q_99.png"}, {"id": 100, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A test for a disease has sensitivity and specificity of 95 percent. It is first used in a specialty clinic where prevalence is 40 percent, then offered to a general population screening program where prevalence is 0.1 percent. What happens to the test's predictive values in the general program compared with the clinic?", "options": {"A": "Positive predictive value rises sharply and negative predictive value falls slightly as prevalence drops", "B": "Positive predictive value falls sharply and negative predictive value rises slightly as prevalence drops", "C": "Both predictive values fall because lower prevalence reduces the test's ability to detect disease", "D": "Sensitivity falls sharply and specificity rises slightly as the disease becomes less common in the population", "E": "Both predictive values stay the same because sensitivity and specificity are fixed properties of the test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Positive predictive value falls sharply and negative predictive value rises slightly as prevalence drops): Positive and negative predictive values depend on prevalence even when sensitivity and specificity are fixed. At very low prevalence most positive results are false positives, so positive predictive value collapses, while nearly everyone is truly healthy so negative predictive value approaches its maximum."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 45-year-old woman develops severe hypoxemia 24 hours after admission for gastric acid aspiration pneumonia. Chest imaging shows bilateral diffuse opacities, there is no evidence of left atrial hypertension, and the PaO2 to FiO2 ratio is 120 despite PEEP of 10. Which ventilator strategy has demonstrated reduced mortality in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Minimal PEEP of 0 to 3 cmH2O to avoid impairing venous return", "B": "High-frequency oscillatory ventilation used routinely to lower mortality compared with conventional ventilation", "C": "Inhaled nitric oxide as standard therapy to improve survival in moderate to severe disease", "D": "Tidal volume near 6 mL/kg predicted body weight with plateau pressure below 30 cmH2O", "E": "Tidal volume near 12 mL/kg ideal body weight to maximize alveolar recruitment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tidal volume near 6 mL/kg predicted body weight with plateau pressure below 30 cmH2O): The patient meets criteria for moderate to severe ARDS, and lung-protective ventilation with low tidal volumes and limited plateau pressure lowers mortality by reducing ventilator-induced lung injury. Nitric oxide improves oxygenation briefly but not survival, and oscillatory ventilation did not help in trials. Higher PEEP is generally used rather than minimal PEEP."}, {"id": 102, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A nurse practitioner at a rural clinic reviews the immunization record of a healthy 14-month-old at a well-child visit. The child received the primary series of inactivated vaccines earlier but has not yet received any vaccine containing weakened live organisms. Which vaccine given at this visit is a live attenuated product?", "options": {"A": "Diphtheria, tetanus and acellular pertussis vaccine, which contains toxoids and purified bacterial antigens", "B": "Measles, mumps and rubella vaccine, which contains replicating weakened viruses that induce durable immunity", "C": "Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, which links capsular polysaccharide to a carrier protein for T-cell response", "D": "Hepatitis A vaccine, which contains inactivated whole virus given as two doses six months apart", "E": "Inactivated poliovirus vaccine, which contains formalin-killed virus delivered by intramuscular injection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Measles, mumps and rubella vaccine, which contains replicating weakened viruses that induce durable immunity): Measles, mumps and rubella vaccine is a live attenuated combination given at 12 to 15 months. Hepatitis A, pneumococcal conjugate, acellular pertussis with toxoids and injected poliovirus vaccines are all inactivated or subunit products that cannot replicate."}, {"id": 103, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 62-year-old man with stable angina takes sublingual nitroglycerin before climbing stairs and reports relief of chest tightness within minutes, along with a mild throbbing headache. Which mechanism best explains the reduction in myocardial oxygen demand at the doses used for exertional angina?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of angiotensin converting enzyme, reducing afterload and aldosterone mediated sodium retention", "B": "Opening of ATP-sensitive potassium channels, hyperpolarizing arteriolar smooth muscle cells to lower vascular resistance", "C": "Nitric oxide release raising cGMP, relaxing venous smooth muscle, and lowering ventricular preload", "D": "Blockade of beta-1 receptors in the myocardium, decreasing contractility and heart rate", "E": "Blockade of L-type calcium channels in the sinoatrial node, slowing the heart rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nitric oxide release raising cGMP, relaxing venous smooth muscle, and lowering ventricular preload): Nitrates are converted to nitric oxide, which activates guanylate cyclase and raises cGMP, causing smooth muscle relaxation. At anginal doses the venous effect dominates, reducing preload and wall tension and therefore oxygen demand. The headache comes from dilation of meningeal vessels."}, {"id": 104, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "An 8-year-old girl has a sharply demarcated, round patch of complete hair loss on the scalp with smooth skin, no scale, and no erythema. Short broken hairs that taper toward the scalp are seen at the margin. Her pediatrician notes small pits in several fingernails. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Traction alopecia, mechanical damage from tight hairstyles that produces marginal hair loss and eventual scarring", "B": "Tinea capitis, a dermatophyte infection of the hair shaft that causes scaling and broken hairs", "C": "Telogen effluvium, diffuse shedding after a physiologic stressor pushes many follicles into the resting phase", "D": "Alopecia areata, a T cell mediated autoimmune attack on actively growing hair bulbs", "E": "Trichotillomania, compulsive hair pulling producing irregular patches containing hairs of differing lengths"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Alopecia areata, a T cell mediated autoimmune attack on actively growing hair bulbs): A smooth, sharply demarcated round patch with tapering broken hairs and nail pitting points to alopecia areata, in which T cells attack anagen bulbs but spare stem cells so regrowth remains possible. Tinea capitis is the tempting alternative in a child but produces scale and inflammation."}, {"id": 105, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 72-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction presents with orthopnea and dyspnea at night. His chest radiograph shows an enlarged cardiac silhouette, upper lobe pulmonary vessel diversion, and thin horizontal lines extending to the pleural surface at the lung bases. What do these horizontal basal lines represent?", "options": {"A": "Calcified diaphragmatic pleural plaques left by prior asbestos exposure along the lower lung zones", "B": "Interstitial edema thickening the interlobular septa along the peripheral lung adjacent to pleura", "C": "Dilated bronchi seen in cross section with thickened walls forming parallel tram-track lines", "D": "Fluid tracking within the minor fissure producing a thin linear density between lung lobes", "E": "Plate-like subsegmental atelectasis from hypoventilation of the dependent portions of the lower lobes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interstitial edema thickening the interlobular septa along the peripheral lung adjacent to pleura): These are Kerley B lines, produced by edema fluid distending the interlobular septa at the lung bases, and together with cardiomegaly and cephalization they indicate cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Pleural plaques from asbestos are calcified, irregular and usually located on the diaphragm or lateral chest wall, and they do not accompany orthopnea and cardiac enlargement."}, {"id": 106, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with epilepsy started lamotrigine two weeks ago. She now has fever, burning eyes, painful erosions on the lips and mouth, and dusky target-like macules on the trunk that are coalescing, with sloughing over about 8 percent of her body surface. Gentle lateral pressure detaches the epidermis. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms with facial edema, lymphadenopathy and organ involvement", "B": "Morbilliform drug eruption with blanching erythematous macules and papules and no mucosal involvement", "C": "Stevens-Johnson syndrome with widespread keratinocyte apoptosis from a delayed T cell mediated drug hypersensitivity", "D": "Erythema multiforme minor following herpes simplex infection, with typical target lesions on the extremities", "E": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome with toxin mediated splitting of the superficial granular layer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stevens-Johnson syndrome with widespread keratinocyte apoptosis from a delayed T cell mediated drug hypersensitivity): Mucosal erosions, fever, dusky targetoid lesions and under 10 percent detachment after starting an aromatic antiseizure drug indicate Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Scalded skin syndrome spares the mucosa and affects mostly children, while DRESS usually appears two to eight weeks later with eosinophilia and organ involvement."}, {"id": 107, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 68-year-old man who has lived alone for years and eats mostly tea and toast takes no supplements. He presents with bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages with corkscrew hairs, and poor wound healing. Which post-translational step in collagen synthesis is most likely impaired?", "options": {"A": "Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues in the procollagen alpha chains", "B": "Oxidative deamination of lysine by lysyl oxidase to form cross-links", "C": "Glycosylation of selected hydroxylysine residues within the rough endoplasmic reticulum", "D": "Cleavage of the propeptide ends by procollagen peptidases outside the cell", "E": "Assembly of the triple helix through disulfide bonds at the carboxyl termini"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues in the procollagen alpha chains): Vitamin C is the cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases, so scurvy produces under-hydroxylated procollagen that cannot form a stable triple helix, causing bleeding gums and poor healing. Propeptide cleavage and glycosylation do not require vitamin C. Lysyl oxidase needs copper, as seen in Menkes disease."}, {"id": 108, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 55-year-old man with metastatic colon cancer has a do-not-resuscitate order recorded during a recent admission. He is now scheduled for a palliative bowel diversion under general anesthesia for obstruction. He is alert, oriented and fully able to decide. His wife is at bedside and says the order should simply remain in effect. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Follow his wife's wish and keep the order unchanged, since she is his closest surrogate", "B": "Cancel the operation because a do-not-resuscitate order cannot be reconciled with general anesthesia and surgery", "C": "Ask the hospital ethics committee to decide on the order before the patient is consulted again", "D": "Suspend the order automatically for surgery, since anesthesia involves resuscitative measures as routine care", "E": "Review the order with him preoperatively and agree whether to suspend, modify or maintain it"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Review the order with him preoperatively and agree whether to suspend, modify or maintain it): Perioperative do-not-resuscitate orders call for a preoperative discussion: a capable patient decides which resuscitative measures he accepts during and after surgery. Automatic suspension and automatic continuation both ignore his autonomy, and his wife acts as surrogate only if he lacks capacity. Cancelling surgery is unnecessary."}, {"id": 109, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 19-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has vomiting, abdominal pain, and deep rapid breathing after running out of insulin. Glucose is 510 mg/dL, sodium 134, potassium 2.9, chloride 96, bicarbonate 9 mEq/L, and venous pH is 7.08. Isotonic saline has been started. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Start the insulin infusion immediately and add potassium once levels have normalized on repeat testing", "B": "Give intravenous potassium chloride first and delay insulin until the serum level rises above 3.3 mEq/L", "C": "Switch to hypotonic half-normal saline to correct the free water deficit before starting the insulin infusion", "D": "Give intravenous sodium bicarbonate to correct the severe acidemia before any insulin is started", "E": "Give intravenous phosphate replacement first to restore intracellular ATP stores before starting any insulin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give intravenous potassium chloride first and delay insulin until the serum level rises above 3.3 mEq/L): The anion gap is 29, confirming diabetic ketoacidosis, but total body potassium is depleted and the serum level here is low. Insulin shifts potassium into cells and could cause fatal arrhythmia, so potassium is replaced first until it exceeds 3.3 mEq/L. Bicarbonate is not indicated at this pH, and phosphate is replaced only in selected cases."}, {"id": 110, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 30-year-old woman at 30 weeks gestation asks for lymphatic osteopathic treatment for leg swelling. Over three days her left calf became painful, warm and swollen compared with the right, while both ankles show mild pitting edema. Heart rate is 96/min and oxygen saturation is 98 percent. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Thoracic inlet myofascial release alone, then reassurance that dependent edema is expected in pregnancy", "B": "Compression duplex ultrasonography of the left leg before any lower extremity manipulation is performed", "C": "Counterstrain to the gastrocnemius tender point with the calf held in a position of ease", "D": "Pedal pump technique to mobilize lower extremity lymph, followed by reassessment of the swelling", "E": "Deep soft tissue massage of the calf to move edema fluid toward the thigh"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Compression duplex ultrasonography of the left leg before any lower extremity manipulation is performed): Pregnancy is hypercoagulable, and unilateral, warm, tender calf swelling suggests deep vein thrombosis rather than benign dependent edema. Imaging comes first, because pumping, massaging or mobilizing the limb risks embolization. Treatment of the lymphatic system can resume once thrombosis is excluded."}, {"id": 111, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 60-year-old man who smokes has progressive proximal leg weakness, dry mouth, and erectile dysfunction. Reflexes are diminished, but strength improves briefly after sustained muscle contraction. Eye movements are normal. Chest imaging shows a central lung mass. Which underlying mechanism best accounts for his weakness?", "options": {"A": "Immune demyelination of peripheral nerves after a diarrheal illness, with ascending areflexic paralysis", "B": "Autoantibodies against muscle-specific kinase producing prominent bulbar and respiratory weakness in young women", "C": "Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated P/Q-type calcium channels, a paraneoplastic effect of small cell carcinoma", "D": "Autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors, typically associated with thymic hyperplasia or thymoma", "E": "Inflammatory muscle fiber necrosis with elevated creatine kinase and perifascicular atrophy on biopsy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated P/Q-type calcium channels, a paraneoplastic effect of small cell carcinoma): Proximal weakness that transiently improves with exertion, hyporeflexia, autonomic symptoms such as dry mouth, and a lung mass indicate Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome. Antibodies against presynaptic calcium channels reduce acetylcholine release, and small cell carcinoma is the usual paraneoplastic source. Myasthenia gravis has ocular fatigable weakness and postsynaptic antibodies."}, {"id": 112, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 66-year-old long-term smoker with diffuse atherosclerosis and resistant hypertension is started on an ACE inhibitor. One week later creatinine rises from 1.1 to 2.4 mg/dL, and he develops flash pulmonary edema. Duplex ultrasonography shows high grade stenosis of both renal arteries. Which mechanism most directly explains the acute drop in filtration?", "options": {"A": "Immune mediated interstitial inflammation from drug hypersensitivity impairs tubular function and filtration", "B": "Loss of angiotensin II mediated efferent arteriolar constriction lowers glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure", "C": "Direct tubular toxicity from the drug causes acute tubular necrosis with sloughed epithelial casts", "D": "Reduced aldosterone effect causes volume depletion and prerenal azotemia from excess sodium loss", "E": "Bradykinin mediated afferent arteriolar constriction lowers renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loss of angiotensin II mediated efferent arteriolar constriction lowers glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure): With bilateral renal artery stenosis, glomerular filtration depends on angiotensin II constricting the efferent arteriole to maintain pressure. An ACE inhibitor removes that constriction and filtration pressure collapses, producing acute kidney injury and, with sodium retention, pulmonary edema. Interstitial nephritis and tubular necrosis do not fit this timing and setting."}, {"id": 113, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 29-year-old man eats a large meal, and acidic chyme lowers his duodenal luminal pH to 3.0. He has no history of ulcer disease, and his pancreatic and biliary function are normal. Which hormone is released by this stimulus, and what is its principal action?", "options": {"A": "Gastric inhibitory peptide from K cells, stimulating insulin release and inhibiting gastric acid output", "B": "Secretin from duodenal S cells, stimulating ductal bicarbonate secretion and reducing gastric acid output", "C": "Motilin from M cells, initiating migrating motor complexes during fasting and increasing gastric emptying", "D": "Gastrin from antral G cells, stimulating parietal cell acid secretion and pancreatic enzyme release", "E": "Cholecystokinin from duodenal I cells, stimulating acinar enzyme secretion and gallbladder contraction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Secretin from duodenal S cells, stimulating ductal bicarbonate secretion and reducing gastric acid output): Duodenal acid triggers S cells to release secretin, which neutralizes acid by driving bicarbonate secretion from pancreatic ductal cells and also decreases gastric acid secretion. Cholecystokinin is released by fatty acids and amino acids rather than by acid."}, {"id": 114, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man in a clinic has a pretest probability of pulmonary embolism of about 50 percent. A D-dimer assay with a negative likelihood ratio of 0.1 returns negative. The patient also had knee surgery years ago and takes lisinopril. Which interpretation of the result is best?", "options": {"A": "Posttest probability is about 0.1 percent, since the ratio is read directly as a percent", "B": "Posttest probability is about 5 percent, found by multiplying the pretest probability by the ratio", "C": "Posttest probability is about 9 percent, found by converting to odds before applying ratio", "D": "Posttest probability is about 50 percent, since a negative test does not alter pretest estimate", "E": "Posttest probability is about 90 percent, found by subtracting the ratio from one"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Posttest probability is about 9 percent, found by converting to odds before applying ratio): Convert the pretest probability of 50 percent to odds of 1, multiply by the negative likelihood ratio of 0.1 to get odds of 0.1, then convert back to a probability of about 9 percent. Multiplying probabilities directly gives 5 percent, which is wrong because likelihood ratios apply to odds. The surgery history and lisinopril do not change the calculation."}, {"id": 115, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension and diabetes develops weakness of the left face, arm and leg over several hours. She takes lisinopril and metformin. Sensation, vision, language and cognition are normal, and there is no neglect. CT shows no hemorrhage. Which site of infarction is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Left posterior limb of the internal capsule from lenticulostriate branch occlusion", "B": "Right posterior limb of the internal capsule from lenticulostriate branch occlusion", "C": "Right anterior cerebral artery infarction of the paracentral lobule and medial frontal cortex", "D": "Right cortical branch occlusion of the middle cerebral artery causing hemispheric infarction", "E": "Right medullary pyramid from anterior spinal artery branch occlusion causing hemiparesis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right posterior limb of the internal capsule from lenticulostriate branch occlusion): A pure motor hemiparesis involving face, arm and leg equally, without sensory, visual or cortical signs, is the classic lacunar syndrome of the posterior limb of the internal capsule, where corticospinal fibers lie close together. Lenticulostriate branches are small penetrators vulnerable to hypertensive and diabetic vessel disease. Cortical or medullary lesions would add other deficits or spare the face."}, {"id": 116, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 2-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department after coughing and choking while playing with small toys. He was lying supine on a couch when the episode began. He now has unilateral wheezing, and a radiograph confirms a retained foreign body. Which location in the tracheobronchial tree is the most likely site where the object lodged?", "options": {"A": "Posterior basal segment of the right lower lobe, the most dependent route when upright", "B": "Left main bronchus, because it is wider and runs more vertically than the right", "C": "Right middle lobe bronchus, because its narrow opening traps aspirated material easily", "D": "Superior segment of the right lower lobe bronchus, which is most gravity dependent when recumbent", "E": "Left lower lobe anterior basal segment, the most dependent route when supine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Superior segment of the right lower lobe bronchus, which is most gravity dependent when recumbent): In a recumbent child the superior segment of the right lower lobe bronchus is the most dependent and posterior branch, so aspirated material tends to enter it. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left, which is why aspiration favors the right side overall. The posterior basal segment is the usual site when the child is upright, so it fits this history less well."}, {"id": 117, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 29-year-old woman at 28 weeks gestation reports mild breathlessness climbing stairs, with no cough, chest pain or leg swelling. Heart rate is 88/min, oxygen saturation is 99% on room air, and the chest is clear. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.44, PaCO2 30 mm Hg, PaO2 104 mm Hg and bicarbonate 20 mEq/L. Which explanation is best?", "options": {"A": "Early diabetic ketoacidosis causing a primary metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation and low bicarbonate", "B": "Anxiety related hyperventilation causing an acute respiratory alkalosis without renal compensation or bicarbonate change", "C": "Pulmonary embolism causing hypoxemia, tachypnea and an elevated alveolar to arterial oxygen gradient", "D": "Progesterone driven rise in minute ventilation with renal bicarbonate compensation, a normal pregnancy adaptation", "E": "Peripartum cardiomyopathy with pulmonary edema causing hypoxemia and hyperventilation from stimulated pulmonary receptors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progesterone driven rise in minute ventilation with renal bicarbonate compensation, a normal pregnancy adaptation): Progesterone increases respiratory center sensitivity so minute ventilation rises, lowering PaCO2 to about 30 mm Hg, and the kidneys lower bicarbonate to about 20 mEq/L to hold pH near 7.44. The normal oxygen saturation, PaO2 and heart rate argue against embolism or heart failure. Acute hyperventilation would not yet show a lowered bicarbonate."}, {"id": 118, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A randomized trial of a new diabetes education program enrolls only highly motivated adults aged 30 to 50 from a single academic center. Randomization, blinded outcome assessment and complete follow-up were maintained, and the program significantly lowered hemoglobin A1c. A rural clinic serving older, less motivated patients asks whether results apply. Which limitation is most relevant?", "options": {"A": "Publication bias, because trials with positive results are more likely to be reported in journals", "B": "Measurement bias, because hemoglobin A1c is an unreliable marker of long-term glycemic control", "C": "Low statistical power, because participants were limited to a narrow age range", "D": "Weak internal validity, because enrolling only motivated volunteers allowed selection confounding between treatment groups", "E": "Limited external validity, because the study population differs from the clinic's patients in key characteristics"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Limited external validity, because the study population differs from the clinic's patients in key characteristics): Randomization, blinding and full follow-up protect internal validity, so the result is probably valid for the people studied. The clinic's concern is whether it generalizes to older and less motivated patients, which is external validity. Restricting enrollment narrows the population to which the findings reliably apply."}, {"id": 119, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old man hospitalized after hip replacement receives unfractionated heparin for prophylaxis. On day 7 his platelet count falls from 230,000 to 98,000 per microliter and he develops painful swelling of his left calf with a new deep vein thrombosis on ultrasound. He has no rash or bleeding. What is the best next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Continue the current heparin dose and transfuse platelets to correct the falling platelet count", "B": "Discontinue the offending anticoagulant and begin a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban", "C": "Give protamine sulfate to reverse the anticoagulant and recheck the platelet count tomorrow", "D": "Switch to low molecular weight heparin to reduce antibody binding while continuing anticoagulation", "E": "Begin warfarin at a full loading dose while heparin continues until the INR is therapeutic"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Discontinue the offending anticoagulant and begin a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban): A platelet fall of more than half between days 5 and 10 of heparin exposure with a new thrombosis is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, an antibody-mediated prothrombotic state. All heparin must stop and a non-heparin direct thrombin inhibitor started. Warfarin alone can worsen thrombosis through early protein C depletion, and low molecular weight heparin cross-reacts with the antibodies."}, {"id": 120, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A resident notices that a senior surgeon smells of alcohol on two consecutive mornings and has slurred speech and a mild hand tremor. A patient is already anesthetized for an elective cholecystectomy that he is about to begin. The resident is worried about retaliation. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Contact the state medical board anonymously and leave the operating room team to proceed as scheduled", "B": "Speak with the surgeon privately first and ask him to hand off the case voluntarily", "C": "Alert the department chair immediately so the surgeon does not operate until he is evaluated", "D": "Keep a private log of observations and wait to see whether a pattern emerges over weeks", "E": "Assist with the operation and report the concern to the program director once it is finished"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Alert the department chair immediately so the surgeon does not operate until he is evaluated): A physician's duty to protect patients requires immediate action when a colleague may be impaired, and the first step is to remove the patient from risk by alerting the chair or chief of staff so the surgeon does not operate. Private conversation or delayed reporting leaves the patient exposed. Board reporting may follow through institutional channels but does not stop this case."}, {"id": 121, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 66-year-old man recovering from lobar pneumonia has shallow breathing, with ultrasound showing reduced right hemidiaphragm excursion and no structural nerve lesion. The physician suspects somatic dysfunction affecting neural supply to the diaphragm and wishes to treat the vertebral levels from which its motor supply arises. Which levels should be evaluated?", "options": {"A": "C3 to C5, the cervical segments contributing the motor fibers to the diaphragm", "B": "T5 to T9, the thoracic segments associated with sympathetic supply to the stomach", "C": "T1 to T4, the thoracic segments supplying sympathetic fibers to the head and neck", "D": "L1 to L3, the lumbar vertebral bodies where the diaphragmatic crura attach", "E": "C1 and C2, the upper cervical segments adjacent to the jugular foramen transmitting the vagus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (C3 to C5, the cervical segments contributing the motor fibers to the diaphragm): The phrenic nerve, the sole motor supply to the diaphragm, arises from C3 to C5, so dysfunction there can reduce excursion. The thoracic sympathetic levels, the upper cervical vagal region, and the lumbar crural attachments are real structures but do not provide the diaphragm's motor innervation."}, {"id": 122, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 41-year-old woman develops perioral numbness and carpal spasm 36 hours after total thyroidectomy for papillary carcinoma. Tapping the facial nerve anterior to the ear causes twitching of the facial muscles. Her ECG shows a prolonged QT interval. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous calcium gluconate, which rapidly raises ionized calcium and reduces neuromuscular excitability after parathyroid injury", "B": "Oral calcitriol, which increases intestinal calcium absorption and corrects the hormone deficiency after parathyroid injury", "C": "Intravenous bisphosphonate, which lowers bone turnover and prevents further calcium loss from the skeleton", "D": "Intravenous magnesium sulfate, which relaxes smooth muscle and corrects the neuromuscular irritability of hypomagnesemia", "E": "Subcutaneous teriparatide, which replaces parathyroid hormone and raises serum calcium over the following several days"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous calcium gluconate, which rapidly raises ionized calcium and reduces neuromuscular excitability after parathyroid injury): Perioral paresthesias, carpal spasm, a Chvostek sign, and QT prolongation after neck surgery indicate symptomatic hypocalcemia from parathyroid devascularization or removal. Intravenous calcium gluconate corrects it within minutes and protects against arrhythmia. Calcitriol and parathyroid hormone replacement are useful for maintenance but do not act quickly enough, and a bisphosphonate would lower calcium further."}, {"id": 123, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 68-year-old man with long-standing emphysema arrives in the emergency department with three days of increased dyspnea and purulent sputum. He is alert, speaking in short phrases, and using accessory muscles. Arterial blood gas findings are in the panel shown. Pulse oximetry is 86% on room air. Which intervention is most appropriate next?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous aminophylline loading dose to improve diaphragmatic contractility and add bronchodilation", "B": "Noninvasive bilevel positive pressure ventilation to rest fatigued respiratory muscles and reduce hypercapnia", "C": "High-flow oxygen by nonrebreather mask to restore saturation to the normal range quickly", "D": "Intravenous sodium bicarbonate infusion to correct the acidemia and improve ventilatory drive", "E": "Immediate endotracheal intubation with sedation and mechanical ventilation to secure the airway"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Noninvasive bilevel positive pressure ventilation to rest fatigued respiratory muscles and reduce hypercapnia): An alert, cooperative patient with a COPD exacerbation and acute hypercapnic acidosis (pH 7.28, PaCO2 68) benefits from bilevel noninvasive ventilation, which lowers the work of breathing, reduces intubation and improves survival. Uncontrolled high-flow oxygen can worsen carbon dioxide retention, and intubation is reserved for failure of noninvasive support. Bicarbonate does not fix the underlying ventilatory failure.", "image": "question_images/q_123.png"}, {"id": 124, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 4-month-old infant has rapidly increasing head circumference, a bulging anterior fontanelle, and downward deviation of the eyes. The pregnancy was uncomplicated. Imaging shows dilated lateral and third ventricles with a normal-sized fourth ventricle. Which of the following is the most likely site of obstruction?", "options": {"A": "Arachnoid granulations, impairing CSF absorption into the superior sagittal venous sinus", "B": "Cerebral aqueduct, causing noncommunicating obstruction between the third and fourth ventricles", "C": "Choroid plexus, producing excess CSF from a papilloma in the lateral ventricle", "D": "Foramina of Luschka and Magendie, blocking outflow from the fourth ventricle to the subarachnoid space", "E": "Interventricular foramina of Monro, blocking drainage of one lateral ventricle into the third"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cerebral aqueduct, causing noncommunicating obstruction between the third and fourth ventricles): Enlarged lateral and third ventricles with a normal fourth ventricle place the block at the aqueduct, and downward gaze deviation (sunsetting) reflects pressure on the midbrain tectum. Blockage at the fourth ventricle outlets or failed absorption would also enlarge the fourth ventricle."}, {"id": 125, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 52-year-old obese man with hypertension is referred because his wife observes loud snoring and pauses in breathing at night. He falls asleep while driving. During polysomnography, apneic episodes show continued thoracoabdominal effort with absent airflow, and oxygen saturation falls to 82% during events. What is the most likely mechanism of these apneas?", "options": {"A": "Transient absence of central respiratory drive with cyclic crescendo-decrescendo breathing from prolonged circulation time", "B": "Laryngospasm triggered by nocturnal gastroesophageal reflux with intermittent closure of the vocal cords", "C": "Blunted medullary chemoreceptor response to carbon dioxide from chronic obesity-related hypoventilation while awake", "D": "Collapse of the pharyngeal airway from reduced upper airway muscle tone during sleep", "E": "Failed diaphragmatic contraction from phrenic nerve dysfunction that eliminates inspiratory effort during sleep"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Collapse of the pharyngeal airway from reduced upper airway muscle tone during sleep): Obstructive apnea is defined by absent airflow despite persistent respiratory effort, which points to upper airway collapse as pharyngeal muscle tone falls during sleep; obesity and hypertension are typical associations. Central apnea would show no effort at all, and a Cheyne-Stokes pattern is seen in heart failure or after stroke. Obesity hypoventilation causes sustained daytime hypercapnia rather than discrete apneas."}, {"id": 126, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 19-year-old man develops masseter rigidity, tachycardia, rising end-tidal carbon dioxide and a temperature of 39.8 C within minutes of induction with sevoflurane and succinylcholine. His father had an unexplained reaction to anesthesia during surgery. Dark urine is later noted. Which drug directly interrupts the underlying mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Rocuronium, which competes with acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors to relax rigid muscle", "B": "Dantrolene, which blocks ryanodine receptor release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum", "C": "Neostigmine, which inhibits acetylcholinesterase to restore neuromuscular transmission and muscle relaxation", "D": "Cyproheptadine, which blocks serotonin receptors to reduce neuromuscular hyperactivity and hyperthermia", "E": "Bromocriptine, which activates dopamine receptors to relieve central rigidity and heat production"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dantrolene, which blocks ryanodine receptor release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum): Malignant hyperthermia results from uncontrolled calcium release through ryanodine receptor type 1 after exposure to volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine, causing sustained contraction, heat production and rhabdomyolysis. Dantrolene binds the receptor and reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Bromocriptine is used in neuroleptic malignant syndrome and cyproheptadine in serotonin syndrome, which have different triggers."}, {"id": 127, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 60-year-old man has chronic constipation with firm stool in the descending and sigmoid colon. Examination shows a restricted sacrum with tender posterior tissues over the lumbosacral junction, and the physician wants to consider the autonomic effects of treating this region. Which source supplies the preganglionic parasympathetic innervation to the distal colon?", "options": {"A": "Vagus nerve fibers traveling with the superior mesenteric artery beyond the splenic flexure", "B": "Spinal cord segments S2 to S4 through the pelvic splanchnic nerves reaching the hindgut", "C": "Lumbar splanchnic nerves from L1 to L2 synapsing in the inferior mesenteric ganglion", "D": "Glossopharyngeal nerve fibers relaying in the otic ganglion before reaching the abdominal viscera", "E": "Greater splanchnic nerve fibers from T5 to T9 synapsing in the celiac ganglion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Spinal cord segments S2 to S4 through the pelvic splanchnic nerves reaching the hindgut): The hindgut distal to the splenic flexure receives parasympathetic supply from S2 to S4 through the pelvic splanchnic nerves, whereas the vagus supplies the gut only to about the splenic flexure. Lumbar splanchnic fibers are sympathetic and slow motility instead. The greater splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibers to the celiac ganglion."}, {"id": 128, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A community clinic serves a neighborhood with a life expectancy 12 years lower than a wealthier area 5 miles away. Residents face high rates of unstable housing, limited grocery access and unreliable transport. A physician wants to address the root of this gap. Which approach best targets the upstream cause?", "options": {"A": "Start a screening program for hypertension and diabetes to detect disease earlier in this neighborhood", "B": "Recruit more specialists to the clinic so residents can access referral care without traveling far", "C": "Provide patient education on diet and exercise at each visit to improve individual health behaviors", "D": "Extend clinic hours to treat illnesses sooner among residents with unstable housing and limited grocery access", "E": "Partner with local agencies on shelter, nutrition and public transit to change conditions that shape health"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Partner with local agencies on shelter, nutrition and public transit to change conditions that shape health): Upstream, or primary prevention, interventions change social determinants such as housing, food access and transportation, which drive much of the gap in life expectancy. Screening and extended hours are secondary or tertiary measures that address disease after it appears. Patient education targets individual behavior without changing the conditions that limit it."}, {"id": 129, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 59-year-old man with heart failure and an ejection fraction of 28 percent develops lip and tongue swelling three weeks after starting lisinopril. The airway is protected after treatment. He remains symptomatic with blood pressure 134/80 mm Hg and normal potassium. Which replacement regimen best continues renin angiotensin system inhibition?", "options": {"A": "Aliskiren, a direct renin inhibitor that blocks the first step of angiotensin formation", "B": "Spironolactone alone, an aldosterone antagonist that replaces the angiotensin converting enzyme blockade", "C": "Sacubitril valsartan, an angiotensin receptor neprilysin inhibitor that raises natriuretic peptides and lowers afterload", "D": "Valsartan, an angiotensin receptor blocker that avoids ACE inhibition and bradykinin accumulation, with close monitoring", "E": "Captopril, a short acting ACE inhibitor restarted at a reduced dose with careful observation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Valsartan, an angiotensin receptor blocker that avoids ACE inhibition and bradykinin accumulation, with close monitoring): ACE inhibitor angioedema is bradykinin mediated, so an angiotensin receptor blocker is the usual replacement, used with caution. Sacubitril valsartan is contraindicated after angioedema because neprilysin inhibition also raises bradykinin. Another ACE inhibitor repeats the mechanism, and aliskiren and spironolactone do not replace the proven therapy."}, {"id": 130, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 12-year-old boy is evaluated for constipation. He has long limbs, thickened lips, and the tongue findings shown in the photograph. His mother died of thyroid cancer at age 30, and his maternal uncle had adrenal surgery. Which syndrome best explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "MEN type 2B from RET mutation, with mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus, and early medullary thyroid carcinoma", "B": "MEN type 2A from RET mutation, with medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and primary hyperparathyroidism", "C": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 from neurofibromin loss, with cutaneous neurofibromas, Lisch nodules, and occasional pheochromocytoma", "D": "MEN type 1 from menin loss, with parathyroid hyperplasia, pituitary adenoma, and pancreatic islet tumors", "E": "Marfan syndrome from fibrillin 1 mutation, with lens subluxation and risk of aortic root dilation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (MEN type 2B from RET mutation, with mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus, and early medullary thyroid carcinoma): Mucosal neuromas of the lips and tongue, intestinal ganglioneuromatosis causing constipation, marfanoid habitus, and a family history of thyroid cancer and adrenal tumors point to multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2B. Type 2A shares the RET mutation and the tumors but does not produce mucosal neuromas or marfanoid habitus. Marfan syndrome has no endocrine tumors.", "image": "question_images/q_130.png"}, {"id": 131, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 3-year-old boy living in an older home with peeling paint has constipation, abdominal pain, and irritability. Hemoglobin is 8.9 g/dL with microcytosis, ferritin is normal, and the peripheral smear shows coarse basophilic stippling. A radiograph of the knees shows dense metaphyseal bands. Which pair of enzymes is most directly inhibited?", "options": {"A": "Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase and coproporphyrinogen oxidase, blocking decarboxylation and oxidation of intermediates", "B": "Uroporphyrinogen synthase and ALA synthase, blocking ring closure and mitochondrial heme initiation", "C": "Heme oxygenase and biliverdin reductase, blocking heme catabolism and conversion to unconjugated bilirubin", "D": "Ferrochelatase and ALA dehydratase, blocking iron insertion into protoporphyrin and porphobilinogen formation", "E": "ALA synthase and porphobilinogen deaminase, blocking the first step and linear tetrapyrrole assembly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ferrochelatase and ALA dehydratase, blocking iron insertion into protoporphyrin and porphobilinogen formation): Pica or paint exposure, microcytic anemia with normal ferritin, basophilic stippling and lead lines on long bone radiographs indicate lead poisoning. Lead inhibits ferrochelatase and ALA dehydratase, so protoporphyrin and aminolevulinic acid accumulate. The normal ferritin separates this from iron deficiency, and stippling reflects ribosomal RNA retained by inhibited pyrimidine 5-nucleotidase."}, {"id": 132, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 36-year-old warehouse worker has sharp pain radiating down the back of the left leg to the heel after lifting a pallet. MRI shows a posterolateral disc herniation at L5 to S1 compressing the traversing nerve root. Which examination finding is most expected?", "options": {"A": "Weak great toe dorsiflexion with numbness over the dorsum of the foot and normal reflexes", "B": "Reduced ankle jerk with weak plantar flexion and numbness along the lateral foot", "C": "Reduced knee jerk with weak knee extension and numbness over the medial leg", "D": "Weak hip flexion with numbness over the anterior thigh and reduced knee jerk", "E": "Saddle numbness with urinary retention and loss of anal tone from bilateral root compression"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced ankle jerk with weak plantar flexion and numbness along the lateral foot): A posterolateral L5 to S1 herniation compresses the traversing S1 root, which supplies plantar flexion, the ankle jerk and the lateral foot. Weak great toe dorsiflexion would indicate L5 root compression from the L4 to L5 level, and saddle numbness suggests a large central herniation affecting several roots."}, {"id": 133, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with atopic dermatitis of the eyelids and neck has developed skin thinning and telangiectasias after repeated topical steroid use. Her dermatologist prescribes a nonsteroidal ointment that works by blocking T cell activation. Which mechanism describes how this drug works?", "options": {"A": "Inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase, which limits thymidylate and purine synthesis in rapidly dividing cells", "B": "Binding FKBP12 and inhibiting calcineurin, which prevents NFAT dependent cytokine transcription within activated T cells", "C": "Inhibiting inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, which depletes guanosine nucleotides needed for lymphocyte proliferation", "D": "Binding cyclophilin and inhibiting calcineurin, which prevents NFAT dependent interleukin 2 transcription in T cells", "E": "Binding FKBP12 and inhibiting mTOR, which blocks interleukin 2 driven T cell cycle progression"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Binding FKBP12 and inhibiting calcineurin, which prevents NFAT dependent cytokine transcription within activated T cells): Topical tacrolimus binds FKBP12, and the complex inhibits calcineurin so NFAT cannot move to the nucleus to drive cytokine genes, giving steroid-sparing anti-inflammatory action without skin atrophy. Cyclosporine uses cyclophilin and sirolimus acts on mTOR, and neither is used as a topical dermatitis ointment."}, {"id": 134, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 22-year-old man who smokes cigarettes presents with hemoptysis and dark urine after a recent influenza-like illness. Creatinine is 3.4 mg/dL and urinalysis shows red cell casts. Immunofluorescence of a kidney biopsy shows smooth linear staining along the glomerular capillary walls. Which antigen is the target of the pathogenic antibodies?", "options": {"A": "The noncollagenous domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in basement membranes", "B": "Myeloperoxidase within neutrophil lysosomes producing pauci-immune crescentic glomerulonephritis without immunoglobulin deposition along capillary walls", "C": "Phospholipase A2 receptor expressed on the subepithelial surface of podocyte foot processes in glomeruli", "D": "Double stranded DNA released from apoptotic nuclei forming circulating complexes that deposit in glomeruli", "E": "Proteinase 3 stored in azurophilic granules of neutrophils, leading to small vessel necrotizing vasculitis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The noncollagenous domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in basement membranes): Pulmonary hemorrhage with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and linear IgG staining is anti-glomerular basement membrane disease, caused by antibodies to type IV collagen. Smoking and infection expose the alveolar antigen. ANCA vasculitis also causes a pulmonary-renal syndrome but shows little or no immunoglobulin on staining."}, {"id": 135, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man undergoes repair of a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm with prolonged aortic clamping. On waking he has paralysis of both legs, loss of pain and temperature below the umbilicus, and urinary retention. Vibration and joint position sensation remain normal in both feet. Which cord territory was infarcted?", "options": {"A": "Both lateral corticospinal tracts alone from an isolated upper motor neuron lesion", "B": "Anterior two thirds of the thoracic cord supplied by the anterior spinal artery", "C": "Posterior third of the cord supplied by the paired posterior spinal arteries", "D": "Central gray matter around the canal causing suspended sensory loss in the arms", "E": "Right lateral half of the cord from occlusion of a segmental radicular artery"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Anterior two thirds of the thoracic cord supplied by the anterior spinal artery): Aortic clamping reduces flow through the artery of Adamkiewicz into the anterior spinal artery, which supplies the anterior two thirds of the cord, including the corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts and anterior horns. Motor paralysis, pain and temperature loss and bladder dysfunction follow, while the posterior columns, fed by paired posterior spinal arteries, preserve vibration and position sense."}, {"id": 136, "domain": "community_health", "question": "After a patient receives a tenfold overdose of heparin because two vials with similar labels were stored side by side, the hospital convenes a team to review the event retrospectively. Their main goal is to identify underlying process failures rather than to assign blame. Which method is this team using?", "options": {"A": "Statistical process control, a method that charts routine process variation over time to detect drift", "B": "Peer review, a confidential evaluation of an individual physician's clinical judgment and standard of care", "C": "Sentinel surveillance, a reporting network that collects selected event data from a few designated reporting sites", "D": "Root cause analysis, a retrospective systems review that traces contributing latent factors behind an adverse event", "E": "Failure mode and effects analysis, a prospective review that ranks possible process failures before harm occurs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Root cause analysis, a retrospective systems review that traces contributing latent factors behind an adverse event): Root cause analysis is a retrospective, systems-focused review of an adverse event, here the look-alike vials that made the error likely. Failure mode and effects analysis looks similar but is prospective, used before harm occurs. Peer review targets an individual clinician's performance, not the process."}, {"id": 137, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 6-year-old boy had bloody diarrhea for four days after visiting a petting farm. He is now pale and lethargic with decreased urine output. Hemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL, platelets are 40,000/mm3, creatinine is rising, and the smear shows schistocytes. Which is the most appropriate management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Supportive care with cautious fluid management and renal monitoring, and dialysis only if indicated", "B": "Prophylactic platelet transfusion given to raise the count above a safe threshold", "C": "Urgent plasma exchange to remove autoantibodies against ADAMTS13 as in thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "D": "Ciprofloxacin to eradicate the organism and shorten the duration of toxin release", "E": "Loperamide to reduce stool frequency and limit fluid loss from colonic inflammation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supportive care with cautious fluid management and renal monitoring, and dialysis only if indicated): Shiga toxin producing E. coli O157:H7 causes hemolytic uremic syndrome, and management is supportive because antibiotics may increase toxin release and antimotility agents prolong exposure. Platelet transfusion can feed microthrombi and is reserved for active bleeding or procedures. Plasma exchange targets TTP, which lacks the diarrheal prodrome and renal predominance seen here."}, {"id": 138, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 24-year-old woman with a BMI of 33 has had irregular menses every 2 to 4 months, facial hirsutism, and acne. She does not desire pregnancy and uses no contraception. Testosterone is mildly elevated, TSH and prolactin are normal, and ultrasound shows enlarged ovaries with multiple small peripheral follicles. Which treatment best addresses both her bleeding pattern and androgen excess?", "options": {"A": "Clomiphene citrate stimulating ovulation through hypothalamic estrogen receptor blockade and raising follicular recruitment", "B": "Combined oral contraceptive therapy suppressing ovarian androgen output while giving regular endometrial shedding", "C": "Spironolactone monotherapy blocking androgen receptors in hair follicles and reducing sebum production in skin", "D": "Cyclic medroxyprogesterone acetate every 3 months producing withdrawal bleeding and protecting the endometrium from unopposed estrogen", "E": "Metformin monotherapy improving insulin sensitivity and modestly reducing ovarian androgen production and cycle irregularity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Combined oral contraceptive therapy suppressing ovarian androgen output while giving regular endometrial shedding): In polycystic ovary syndrome without a wish for pregnancy, combined oral contraceptives give predictable withdrawal bleeding, protect the endometrium, and lower ovarian androgen production and free testosterone. Progestin alone covers the endometrium but not the hirsutism. Spironolactone helps hair growth but needs contraception and does not regulate cycles."}, {"id": 139, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 10-year-old boy falls from his bicycle onto an outstretched hand and has pain and swelling at the wrist. A radiograph shows a fracture line through the physis of the distal radius that exits through a triangular metaphyseal fragment. Neurovascular examination is normal. Which classification best describes this injury and its usual growth risk?", "options": {"A": "Salter Harris type V, a physeal crush injury with a high risk of premature closure", "B": "Salter Harris type I, with fracture confined to the physis and a good prognosis", "C": "Salter Harris type II, which carries a favorable outlook after anatomic reduction", "D": "Salter Harris type IV, with a fracture crossing metaphysis, physis and epiphysis, risking growth arrest", "E": "Salter Harris type III, with intra-articular epiphyseal extension and risk of growth disturbance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Salter Harris type II, which carries a favorable outlook after anatomic reduction): A fracture through the physis that exits through the metaphysis, leaving a triangular Thurstan Holland fragment, is Salter Harris type II, the most common type. It does not cross the epiphysis, so the germinal layer is usually spared and prognosis is good. Types III to V involve the epiphysis or crush the growth plate and carry higher risk."}, {"id": 140, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "An 8-year-old girl is referred because teachers report brief staring episodes many times each day, lasting about ten seconds, with immediate return to activity and no memory of the events. An episode is provoked by hyperventilation in clinic. EEG shows generalized 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharges. Which mechanism of action explains the drug of choice?", "options": {"A": "Binding to synaptic vesicle protein 2A to modulate presynaptic neurotransmitter release in cortical terminals", "B": "Irreversible inhibition of GABA transaminase raising the synaptic concentration of the inhibitory transmitter", "C": "Prolonged inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels in rapidly firing cortical pyramidal neurons", "D": "Increased frequency of chloride channel opening at the benzodiazepine site of postsynaptic GABA-A receptors", "E": "Blockade of low-threshold T-type calcium channels in thalamic relay neurons that drive oscillating cortical rhythm"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Blockade of low-threshold T-type calcium channels in thalamic relay neurons that drive oscillating cortical rhythm): Staring spells with generalized 3 Hz discharges indicate childhood absence epilepsy, for which ethosuximide is the drug of choice. It acts by blocking T-type calcium currents in thalamic neurons that generate the oscillation. Sodium channel blockers such as carbamazepine and phenytoin can aggravate absence seizures."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with right shoulder pain after a fall from a ladder has an acutely tender, small point in the tissue near the coracoid process. The physician plans counterstrain and wants to explain the proposed mechanism and treatment time. Which explanation is consistent with Jones' description?", "options": {"A": "Active isometric contraction resets the spindle gain, held about five seconds", "B": "Passive stretching of the tissue fatigues the spindle, held about three minutes", "C": "Compression at the point increases tissue tension, held about thirty seconds", "D": "Passive shortening of the tissue reduces abnormal proprioceptor input, held about ninety seconds", "E": "A thrust through the barrier resets the spindle, delivered in under one second"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Passive shortening of the tissue reduces abnormal proprioceptor input, held about ninety seconds): Jones proposed that a strained muscle's spindle sends persistent abnormal signals, and passively shortening the tissue for about ninety seconds quiets them. Stretching, active contraction and thrust belong to other techniques. Counterstrain is gentle, so compression or a thrust at the tender point would defeat its purpose."}, {"id": 142, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A county health department surveys a random sample of 2,000 adolescents on a single day, recording current vaping and current wheezing, and finds wheezing in 18 percent of vapers versus 9 percent of non-vapers. A commissioner concludes that vaping caused wheezing. What is the main limitation of this design?", "options": {"A": "Exposure and outcome are measured together, so which came first cannot be determined here", "B": "Random sampling of adolescents prevents any confounding between vaping and other causes of wheezing", "C": "Sample size is too small to estimate prevalence among adolescents in the county reliably", "D": "Loss to follow-up over time biases the comparison between the vaping and nonvaping groups", "E": "Incidence cannot be calculated, yet temporality is clear since vaping is recorded before wheezing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Exposure and outcome are measured together, so which came first cannot be determined here): A cross-sectional survey measures exposure and outcome at the same moment, so it cannot show that vaping came before wheezing. Reverse causation or shared causes remain possible. Random sampling helps generalizability but does not remove confounding, and no follow-up exists, so loss to follow-up is not a concern."}, {"id": 143, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has persistent bleeding from the uterus and surgical wound after cesarean delivery complicated by placental abruption. She has already received red cells and plasma. Fibrinogen remains 70 mg/dL, platelets are 110,000 per microliter, and INR is 1.4. Which blood product best corrects the most significant deficit?", "options": {"A": "Additional fresh frozen plasma, which supplies every coagulation factor in physiologic proportions and volume", "B": "Cryoprecipitate, a pooled product rich in factor I, factor VIII, and factor XIII", "C": "Apheresis platelet concentrate, which raises the platelet count and delivers some factor V", "D": "Packed red cells, which restore oxygen carrying capacity and carry residual plasma proteins", "E": "Prothrombin complex concentrate, which supplies vitamin K dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cryoprecipitate, a pooled product rich in factor I, factor VIII, and factor XIII): After massive obstetric bleeding the critical deficit is fibrinogen, which is only mildly supplied by plasma. Cryoprecipitate concentrates fibrinogen, factor VIII, factor XIII and von Willebrand factor in a small volume and raises fibrinogen quickly. The platelet count and INR are only mildly abnormal and do not explain the ongoing bleeding."}, {"id": 144, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 46-year-old woman reports episodic facial flushing with spicy food and wine. Her central facial skin in the photograph shown has persistent erythema with scattered papules and pustules. Examination reveals no comedones, and she reports no joint pain or oral ulcers. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Systemic lupus erythematosus, an autoimmune disease causing photosensitive malar erythema with immune complex deposition", "B": "Rosacea, a chronic inflammatory disease with vascular hyperreactivity and papulopustules centered on convex facial areas", "C": "Seborrheic dermatitis, an inflammatory response to Malassezia yeast affecting the nasolabial folds and eyebrows", "D": "Acne vulgaris, a follicular disease driven by sebum, keratin plugging, and Cutibacterium acnes proliferation", "E": "Perioral dermatitis, a papulopustular eruption concentrated around the mouth and chin after topical steroid use"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rosacea, a chronic inflammatory disease with vascular hyperreactivity and papulopustules centered on convex facial areas): Episodic flushing with identifiable triggers, persistent central erythema, and papulopustules without comedones define papulopustular rosacea. Lupus produces a fixed photosensitive malar rash without pustules, and acne vulgaris would show comedones.", "image": "question_images/q_144.png"}, {"id": 145, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 48-year-old office worker who types nine hours daily has chronic neck pain. Palpation reveals an exquisitely tender point on the posterior aspect of the C5 spinous process. The physician plans counterstrain. Which starting position is most consistent with treating this point?", "options": {"A": "Cervical extension with small sidebending and rotation adjustments until tenderness decreases", "B": "Cervical flexion with small sidebending and rotation adjustments away from the tender side until tenderness decreases", "C": "Cervical traction in neutral with firm sustained pressure applied directly over the point", "D": "Extension of the neck with a thrust delivered through the tender point", "E": "Rotation of the neck to the restrictive barrier held for ninety seconds"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cervical extension with small sidebending and rotation adjustments until tenderness decreases): Posterior tender points are treated with extension, with small sidebending and rotation adjustments to find the position of greatest comfort. Flexion is used for anterior points. Traction, a barrier-directed hold and a thrust are not counterstrain."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 67-year-old man with a 45 pack-year smoking history reports progressive exertional dyspnea and minimal cough. He retired from a plumbing career last year. His FEV1/FVC is 0.52, total lung capacity is increased, and diffusing capacity is reduced. The expiratory flow-volume loop is shown. Which diagnosis best fits the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Chronic bronchitis, with mucous gland hypertrophy narrowing airways while diffusing capacity remains preserved", "B": "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, with reduced compliance and a proportionally preserved FEV1 to FVC ratio", "C": "Bronchiectasis, with permanent airway dilation causing productive cough and a normal total lung capacity", "D": "Asthma, with reversible bronchoconstriction and a normal or raised diffusing capacity between attacks", "E": "Emphysema, with parenchymal destruction reducing elastic recoil and alveolar surface for gas transfer"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Emphysema, with parenchymal destruction reducing elastic recoil and alveolar surface for gas transfer): An FEV1/FVC of 0.52 with increased total lung capacity and a scooped expiratory limb shows obstruction with hyperinflation. The reduced diffusing capacity points to loss of alveolar-capillary surface, which is characteristic of emphysema. Chronic bronchitis causes obstruction with a preserved diffusing capacity and prominent sputum production.", "image": "question_images/q_146.png"}, {"id": 147, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 38-year-old man in the clinic becomes loud and angry after waiting 90 minutes for an appointment about back pain, saying doctors care only about money. He is not threatening. The physician has a full afternoon schedule. Which response is most appropriate first?", "options": {"A": "Explain that scheduling delays result from a heavy patient volume and that his anger is unhelpful", "B": "Acknowledge his frustration, apologize for the delay, and invite him to explain what concerns him most", "C": "Move straight to examining his back pain to finish the visit and restore the schedule", "D": "Warn him the visit will end if he stays loud and security may be called", "E": "Defend the practice, explaining that fees come from insurers and the physician is salaried"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acknowledge his frustration, apologize for the delay, and invite him to explain what concerns him most): With a loud but non-threatening patient, the first step is empathic acknowledgment and an apology for the delay, which de-escalates and uncovers the real concern. Explaining, defending or shortening the visit escalates conflict. Threatening to call security is for genuine safety risk, and the examination follows once he feels heard."}, {"id": 148, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man with uncontrolled hypertension misses visits because he distrusts the health system, lacks transportation and cannot manage insurance renewal forms. His regimen is appropriate and well tolerated. The clinic wants a team member who shares his language and neighborhood and can visit him at home to connect him with services. Which role best fits?", "options": {"A": "Community health worker, a trusted lay liaison who provides outreach, navigation and culturally concordant support", "B": "Nurse care manager, who triages telephone calls, follows clinical protocols and coordinates specialist referrals", "C": "Medical interpreter, who provides accurate language interpretation during clinical encounters and in written materials for patients", "D": "Clinical pharmacist, who reconciles medications, adjusts doses under protocol and counsels on adherence strategies", "E": "Physician assistant, who extends clinic visit capacity by diagnosing and prescribing under physician supervision"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Community health worker, a trusted lay liaison who provides outreach, navigation and culturally concordant support): Community health workers come from the community they serve, build trust and address nonclinical needs such as transportation, insurance paperwork and home follow-up, which are this patient's barriers. A pharmacist or nurse care manager would address clinical management that is already adequate. An interpreter solves only language during encounters."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 55-year-old man taking warfarin presents to the clinic two days after falling from a ladder and striking his head. He reports a worsening headache, vomiting, and drowsiness. Cranial examination finds a restricted occipitomastoid suture and tender occiput. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Perform venous sinus drainage first, then image only if the headache persists after treatment", "B": "Apply frontal lift to improve venous return and recheck neurologic status after treatment", "C": "Withhold osteopathic manipulation and obtain urgent noncontrast head CT for possible intracranial hemorrhage", "D": "Treat the occipitomastoid restriction gently and schedule outpatient imaging for the following week", "E": "Perform compression of the fourth ventricle to lower intracranial pressure before arranging transport"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold osteopathic manipulation and obtain urgent noncontrast head CT for possible intracranial hemorrhage): Headache, vomiting, and drowsiness after head trauma in an anticoagulated patient suggest intracranial hemorrhage, so urgent noncontrast CT is the priority. Cranial osteopathic treatment is contraindicated in acute intracranial bleeding and raised intracranial pressure. Treating first or imaging later delays diagnosis and could worsen the outcome."}, {"id": 150, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has a 2.2 cm solitary thyroid nodule found on neck palpation. She has no radiation exposure or family history of thyroid cancer. Ultrasound shows a solid hypoechoic nodule with microcalcifications. Serum TSH is 1.8 mIU/L. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Radioactive iodine uptake scan to determine whether the nodule is functioning or cold", "B": "Levothyroxine suppression therapy for 6 months to test whether the nodule shrinks", "C": "Repeat ultrasound in 12 months to monitor growth, since most solid nodules are benign", "D": "Immediate lobectomy based on sonographic features, given the suspicious microcalcifications seen on imaging", "E": "Ultrasound-guided fine needle aspiration of the nodule, with cytology to classify malignancy risk"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ultrasound-guided fine needle aspiration of the nodule, with cytology to classify malignancy risk): With a normal TSH and a suspicious sonographic appearance in a nodule over 1 cm, fine needle aspiration is the next step. A scan is reserved for a suppressed TSH where a hot nodule is possible. Surgery follows cytology, and suppression therapy has little role."}, {"id": 151, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 47-year-old man with obesity starts a medication that prevents absorption of about a third of ingested fat. After a high-fat dinner, he reports oily rectal spotting, urgency, and flatulence. His physician recommends taking a daily multivitamin at a different time. Which mechanism of the drug accounts for these effects?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of brush border alpha glucosidase, leaving complex carbohydrates undigested to ferment in the colon", "B": "Irreversible inhibition of gastric and pancreatic lipases, so undigested triglycerides pass into the colon", "C": "Inhibition of microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, preventing chylomicron assembly and causing enterocyte lipid accumulation", "D": "Blockade of cholecystokinin release from duodenal cells, reducing pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction", "E": "Binding of bile acids in the intestinal lumen, interrupting enterohepatic recirculation and fat micelle formation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Irreversible inhibition of gastric and pancreatic lipases, so undigested triglycerides pass into the colon): Orlistat covalently inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipases, so about 30 percent of dietary triglyceride is not hydrolyzed and passes to the colon, causing oily spotting and flatulence. The same steatorrhea reduces absorption of fat soluble vitamins, which is why a multivitamin is separated from the dose. Acarbose acts on carbohydrates and would not cause oily stool."}, {"id": 152, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 27-year-old woman has thin translucent skin with visible veins, easy bruising, and a history of spontaneous colon perforation. Her joints are mildly hypermobile. Her father died of an arterial rupture at age forty. She is 160 cm tall with normal arm span and normal eyes. Which defect is most likely?", "options": {"A": "A COL5A1 mutation disrupting type V collagen fibril assembly in classic skin tissue", "B": "A PLOD1 mutation reducing lysyl hydroxylase activity and cross-link formation", "C": "A COL3A1 mutation producing structurally abnormal type III collagen in vessel walls", "D": "An FBN1 mutation weakening fibrillin-rich microfibrils in the aortic media", "E": "A COL1A1 mutation lowering type I collagen synthesis in bone and sclerae"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A COL3A1 mutation producing structurally abnormal type III collagen in vessel walls): Translucent skin, spontaneous bowel perforation and early arterial rupture indicate vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome from COL3A1 mutations. Classic type shows hyperextensible skin and atrophic scars with COL5A1 defects but rarely arterial rupture. Normal height, arm span and eyes argue against Marfan syndrome, and fractures would point to type I collagen."}, {"id": 153, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 24-year-old woman improves after 4 days of influenza, then develops high fever, hemoptysis, and dyspnea. Chest radiograph shows patchy infiltrates with small cavities, and sputum Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. She works at a daycare center. The isolate is resistant to nafcillin. Which mechanism most likely explains the resistance?", "options": {"A": "Production of plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase that hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring of penicillin molecules", "B": "Loss of outer membrane porin channels that limits drug entry into the periplasmic space", "C": "Substitution of D-alanyl-D-lactate for the peptidoglycan terminus, which reduces glycopeptide binding", "D": "Methylation of 23S ribosomal RNA at the 50S subunit, which reduces macrolide binding", "E": "Acquisition of mecA encoding an altered penicillin-binding protein with reduced affinity for beta-lactam drugs"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Acquisition of mecA encoding an altered penicillin-binding protein with reduced affinity for beta-lactam drugs): Post-influenza necrotizing pneumonia with clustered gram-positive cocci is Staphylococcus aureus, and nafcillin resistance defines methicillin resistance. The mecA gene encodes penicillin-binding protein 2a, which beta-lactams bind poorly, so the cell wall still forms. Plasmid beta-lactamase explains penicillin resistance, but nafcillin is designed to resist that enzyme."}, {"id": 154, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 27-year-old woman has eight months of crampy lower abdominal pain that improves after defecation, with alternating loose and hard stools. Weight, hemoglobin, C-reactive protein, fecal calprotectin, and celiac serology are normal. She has no nocturnal symptoms or blood in stool, and her mother has hypothyroidism. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Initiation of an anti tumor necrosis factor agent for presumed early inflammatory bowel disease", "B": "Empiric course of oral antibiotics directed at suspected bacterial overgrowth as first line therapy", "C": "Colonoscopy with random mucosal biopsies to exclude microscopic colitis before any treatment", "D": "CT of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast to exclude occult structural disease", "E": "Reassurance with dietary modification and soluble fiber, then symptom targeted therapy for the predominant complaint"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Reassurance with dietary modification and soluble fiber, then symptom targeted therapy for the predominant complaint): Pain relieved by defecation with altered stool form, normal inflammatory markers, and no alarm features meets the criteria for irritable bowel syndrome, which is managed with reassurance, diet and fiber, and symptom directed drugs. Further testing is reserved for alarm features, older age, or failure of therapy."}, {"id": 155, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 66-year-old strict vegan has fatigue, numbness in his feet, and an unsteady gait. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL with an MCV of 118 fL. Serum methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are both elevated. A physician plans to give folic acid alone. Which biochemical defect explains his neurologic findings?", "options": {"A": "Impaired pyridoxal phosphate dependent decarboxylation reducing GABA and other neurotransmitter synthesis in peripheral nerves", "B": "Impaired mutase conversion of an odd chain fatty acid metabolite, yielding abnormal myelin lipids", "C": "Impaired thiamine dependent pyruvate dehydrogenase activity reducing aerobic ATP production in axons", "D": "Failure of homocysteine remethylation causing direct endothelial toxicity and cerebral small vessel disease", "E": "Impaired thymidylate synthesis from reduced tetrahydrofolate, causing defective DNA replication in neurons"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Impaired mutase conversion of an odd chain fatty acid metabolite, yielding abnormal myelin lipids): Cobalamin is a cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase and for methionine synthase, so deficiency raises both methylmalonic acid and homocysteine. Folate deficiency raises homocysteine only, and the neurologic injury of cobalamin deficiency is linked to the mutase defect and abnormal myelin lipids. Giving folic acid alone can correct the anemia while allowing neurologic damage to progress."}, {"id": 156, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A newborn has bilateral microtia with absent ear canals, a cleft palate, a conotruncal cardiac defect and thymic hypoplasia. The mother, age 24, was treated for severe nodulocystic acne early in the first trimester, and no pregnancy test was done before treatment. Which mechanism best explains this pattern of anomalies?", "options": {"A": "Folate antagonism that impairs thymidine synthesis and prevents closure of the neural tube", "B": "Excess retinoid signaling that disrupts cranial neural crest cell migration and pharyngeal arch patterning", "C": "Fetal renin angiotensin suppression that reduces renal perfusion and causes skull ossification defects", "D": "Inhibition of vitamin K dependent carboxylation that causes nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses", "E": "Cereblon mediated protein degradation that suppresses limb bud outgrowth and causes phocomelia with ear anomalies"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excess retinoid signaling that disrupts cranial neural crest cell migration and pharyngeal arch patterning): Isotretinoin is a vitamin A derivative whose excess retinoic acid signaling disrupts cranial neural crest cells, producing the microtia, cleft palate, conotruncal and thymic defects of retinoid embryopathy. Thalidomide also affects the ear but acts through cereblon to cause limb reduction, which is absent here. Folate antagonists chiefly cause neural tube defects."}, {"id": 157, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with long-standing type 1 diabetes has early satiety and postprandial vomiting, and a gastric emptying study shows delayed emptying. After starting a prokinetic she develops restlessness, tremor and bradykinesia. Blockade of which receptor in which pathway best explains these adverse effects?", "options": {"A": "Dopamine D2 receptors in the tuberoinfundibular pathway, increasing prolactin secretion from pituitary lactotrophs", "B": "Muscarinic M1 receptors in the basal ganglia, producing anticholinergic tremor with dry mouth and urinary retention", "C": "Histamine H1 receptors in the vestibular nuclei, producing sedation and impaired motor coordination", "D": "Serotonin 5-HT3 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, reducing vagal afferent emetic signaling", "E": "Dopamine D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway, reducing inhibitory dopaminergic tone on striatal neurons"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Dopamine D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway, reducing inhibitory dopaminergic tone on striatal neurons): Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist and 5-HT4 agonist that speeds gastric emptying, but D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway causes acute dystonia, akathisia and parkinsonism. D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway causes hyperprolactinemia, which is a different adverse effect."}, {"id": 158, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 6-year-old girl has chronic mild jaundice, splenomegaly, and pigmented gallstones. Her father had a splenectomy. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL, mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is elevated, and the direct antiglobulin test is negative. The smear shows dense red cells lacking central pallor. Which defect best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Mutation of the beta globin gene producing unstable, polymerizing hemoglobin molecules in red cells", "B": "Absence of glycosylphosphatidylinositol anchored proteins increasing complement mediated lysis of red cells", "C": "Deficiency of ankyrin or spectrin linking the membrane skeleton to the lipid bilayer", "D": "Deficiency of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase impairing reduced glutathione regeneration under oxidant stress in red cells", "E": "Autoantibody coating of red cells promoting phagocytosis by splenic macrophages in the spleen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deficiency of ankyrin or spectrin linking the membrane skeleton to the lipid bilayer): Spherocytes with high mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration, a family history, and a negative antiglobulin test indicate hereditary spherocytosis, caused by defects in ankyrin, spectrin, band 3, or protein 4.2. Loss of membrane surface makes the cells spherical and vulnerable to splenic removal. Splenectomy reduces hemolysis."}, {"id": 159, "domain": "community_health", "question": "Annual lung cancer incidence is 200 per 100,000 among smokers and 20 per 100,000 among nonsmokers in a large registry. A health officer planning cessation services wants to know how many cases per 100,000 smokers each year could be attributed to smoking, noting the relative risk is 10. Which value best answers her question?", "options": {"A": "220 per 100,000 per year, found by adding the exposed rate to the unexposed rate", "B": "90 percent, found by dividing the excess rate by the rate among exposed persons", "C": "180 per 100,000 per year, the exposed rate minus the unexposed rate of disease", "D": "A value of 10, found by dividing the exposed rate by the unexposed rate", "E": "20 per 100,000 per year, the baseline rate that occurs without the exposure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (180 per 100,000 per year, the exposed rate minus the unexposed rate of disease): Attributable risk is the incidence in exposed persons minus the incidence in unexposed persons: 200 minus 20, or 180 per 100,000 per year, which counts preventable cases. The relative risk of 10 describes strength of association, not case numbers. The attributable proportion of 90 percent is a percentage and does not give cases per 100,000 smokers."}, {"id": 160, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has fatigue, weight loss, salt craving, and darkening of skin creases and buccal mucosa for 6 months. Blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg. Sodium is 128 mEq/L, potassium is 5.8 mEq/L, and glucose is 62 mg/dL. Which pathophysiologic process explains the hyperkalemia and hyperpigmentation together?", "options": {"A": "Isolated aldosterone synthase deficiency with preserved cortisol, causing hyperkalemia and raised ACTH from mild feedback", "B": "Hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism from diabetic kidney damage with preserved cortisol and acanthosis-related skin darkening", "C": "Adrenal medullary destruction removing catecholamines, causing hypotension with compensatory melanocyte stimulating hormone release", "D": "Cortical destruction removing aldosterone and cortisol, with lost feedback raising pituitary POMC-derived melanocyte-stimulating peptides", "E": "Pituitary destruction removing ACTH, with preserved aldosterone and cortisol deficiency causing hypotension and hypoglycemia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cortical destruction removing aldosterone and cortisol, with lost feedback raising pituitary POMC-derived melanocyte-stimulating peptides): Primary adrenal failure removes cortisol and aldosterone, causing hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and hypoglycemia. Falling cortisol removes negative feedback, so ACTH and its POMC cleavage products, including MSH, rise and darken the skin. Secondary insufficiency spares aldosterone and does not pigment."}, {"id": 161, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with major depression with psychotic features has refused food for a week and has voiced intent to end her life. Two antidepressant trials failed. Her psychiatrist recommends the most rapidly effective treatment. Which of the following is the most common adverse effect of this treatment?", "options": {"A": "Aspiration pneumonia from loss of airway reflexes during the paralysis required for the procedure", "B": "Seizures that recur spontaneously after the course ends, requiring long-term anticonvulsant therapy", "C": "Cardiac arrhythmia and ischemia, the leading cause of treatment discontinuation in older patients", "D": "Structural brain injury visible as cortical atrophy on follow-up neuroimaging months later", "E": "Short-term memory loss, including gaps for events near the time of the procedure"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Short-term memory loss, including gaps for events near the time of the procedure): Psychotic depression with refusal of food and suicidal intent in an older adult is a strong indication for electroconvulsive therapy. Its most common adverse effect is transient confusion and memory impairment around the treatments, and it does not cause detectable structural brain damage or epilepsy."}, {"id": 162, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 4-year-old girl was hypotonic with a weak suck as an infant. She now has an insatiable appetite, central obesity, short stature, small hands and feet, and mild intellectual disability. She is not ataxic and her speech is delayed but she laughs appropriately. Which genetic mechanism most likely explains her condition?", "options": {"A": "Loss of the paternally derived 15q11 to 13 region with the maternal copy silenced by imprinting", "B": "Expanded CGG repeat in FMR1 with hypermethylation of the promoter and silenced gene expression", "C": "Loss of the maternally derived 15q11 to 13 region with the paternal copy silenced by imprinting", "D": "Deletion of chromosome 22q11 affecting pharyngeal arch development and thymic and parathyroid formation", "E": "Maternal UBE3A point mutation causing absent expression of the ubiquitin ligase in neurons"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Loss of the paternally derived 15q11 to 13 region with the maternal copy silenced by imprinting): Hypotonia in infancy followed by hyperphagia, obesity, small hands and feet and mild intellectual disability is Prader-Willi syndrome. It arises from loss of the paternal 15q11 to 13 region, because the maternal genes are imprinted and silent. Angelman syndrome involves loss of the maternal copy and gives ataxia and inappropriate laughter."}, {"id": 163, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 59-year-old man with end-stage kidney disease missed his last two dialysis sessions and now has generalized weakness. Potassium is 7.4 mEq/L, and the electrocardiogram tracing shown was obtained. He is hemodynamically stable. Which intervention should be given first to reduce his immediate risk of arrhythmia?", "options": {"A": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate binding colonic potassium in exchange for sodium to lower total body stores", "B": "Intravenous calcium gluconate to raise the threshold for cardiac myocyte depolarization and stabilize the membrane", "C": "Nebulized albuterol stimulating beta two receptors to move potassium into cells via the sodium potassium pump", "D": "Intravenous regular insulin with glucose to shift potassium into skeletal muscle and cardiac cells within minutes", "E": "Intravenous furosemide to increase urinary potassium excretion at the thick ascending limb and distal nephron"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous calcium gluconate to raise the threshold for cardiac myocyte depolarization and stabilize the membrane): Peaked T waves, a widened QRS or flattened P waves signal hyperkalemic cardiac toxicity, and calcium gluconate antagonizes the resting membrane effect within minutes without lowering potassium. Insulin with glucose and albuterol then shift potassium into cells, and dialysis definitively removes it. A binder or diuretic is too slow or unreliable in a dialysis patient.", "image": "question_images/q_163.png"}, {"id": 164, "domain": "community_health", "question": "County A has a crude death rate of 12 per 1,000, higher than County B at 8 per 1,000. County A has a much older population, while County B is a college community. Age-specific death rates are nearly identical in the two counties. Which method would best allow a fair comparison of mortality between the counties?", "options": {"A": "Matching the counties on median income, which equalizes the age distribution between populations", "B": "Reporting the crude death rate ratio, since crude rates already reflect the true mortality risk", "C": "Using the proportional mortality ratio, comparing deaths from each cause to total deaths in each county", "D": "Direct standardization, weighting each county's stratum rates by a common standard population structure", "E": "Calculating the case fatality rate in each county, which removes the effect of population age"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Direct standardization, weighting each county's stratum rates by a common standard population structure): The crude gap reflects age structure, not different risk at each age. Direct standardization applies each county's rates to one shared reference population, removing the confounding effect of age. Case fatality and proportional mortality describe different quantities and do not adjust the population-wide rate."}, {"id": 165, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes for 18 years and proliferative retinopathy develops nephrotic range proteinuria with a urine protein excretion of 5 g daily. Blood pressure is 148/92 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 1.9 mg/dL. Kidney biopsy is performed. Which histologic finding is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Cellular crescents filling Bowman space with fibrin and rupture of the glomerular basement membrane", "B": "Nodular mesangial expansion with hyalinized arterioles and diffusely thickened glomerular basement membranes", "C": "Extracellular amorphous deposits showing apple green birefringence after Congo red staining under polarized light", "D": "Hypercellular glomeruli with neutrophils and subepithelial humps on electron microscopy after streptococcal infection", "E": "Diffuse capillary wall thickening with subepithelial spikes seen on silver stain and granular IgG"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Nodular mesangial expansion with hyalinized arterioles and diffusely thickened glomerular basement membranes): Long standing diabetes with retinopathy and nephrotic proteinuria produces Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules, hyalinosis of both afferent and efferent arterioles, and thick basement membranes. Amyloid is a reasonable alternative cause of nephrotic syndrome, but it would stain with Congo red and is not tied to retinopathy. Crescents and humps indicate nephritic syndromes."}, {"id": 166, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 76-year-old man with advanced dementia is nonverbal and grimaces when the physician palpates his left lumbar paraspinal muscles. Nursing home staff report he guards when turned in bed. The tissue feels warm and boggy. The physician wants an indirect treatment. Which technique is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain, since a sustained position of ease is held for about ninety seconds", "B": "Muscle energy, since a graded isometric contraction restores the restricted motion", "C": "Articulatory technique, since repetitive springing gradually mobilizes the restricted lumbar tissues", "D": "High velocity low amplitude, since a thrust directly corrects the restriction", "E": "Functional technique, since the endpoint is judged by palpated tissue response"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Functional technique, since the endpoint is judged by palpated tissue response): A nonverbal patient cannot give the tenderness ratings that counterstrain depends on, so a technique guided by the physician's palpation is more reliable. Functional technique is indirect and follows tissue response. Muscle energy, articulatory and high velocity low amplitude techniques are direct or active and do not meet the request for an indirect method, and counterstrain, although indirect, needs patient feedback that cannot be obtained here."}, {"id": 167, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 6-year-old girl cries every time she enters the pediatrician's office, though she was initially calm. She received painful injections at three prior visits, and the waiting room now elicits crying even before any injection. In classical conditioning terms, what is the waiting room?", "options": {"A": "Conditioned stimulus, a formerly neutral cue that now predicts the pain and triggers a learned response", "B": "Unconditioned stimulus, the painful injection that reflexively produces fear and crying without prior learning", "C": "Discriminative stimulus, a cue signaling that a particular voluntary behavior will be followed by reinforcement", "D": "Negative reinforcer, a stimulus whose removal increases the likelihood of her avoidance behavior in future", "E": "Unconditioned response, the reflexive crying that occurs after the painful injection is delivered to her"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Conditioned stimulus, a formerly neutral cue that now predicts the pain and triggers a learned response): The injection is the unconditioned stimulus and the crying to pain is the unconditioned response. Repeated pairing made the formerly neutral waiting room a conditioned stimulus that elicits conditioned crying. Operant concepts such as reinforcers and discriminative stimuli concern voluntary behavior shaped by consequences, not this reflexive association."}, {"id": 168, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 44-year-old woman has chronic neck pain and a sense of tightness at the top of the chest. Examination shows a restricted first rib on the right and a taut fascial sheet crossing the thoracic inlet that anchors the dome over the lung apex to the lowest cervical transverse process. Which tissue is most likely involved in this restriction?", "options": {"A": "Prevertebral fascia, covering the longus muscles and cervical vertebral bodies behind the pharynx", "B": "Pretracheal fascia, enclosing the thyroid gland, trachea, and esophagus within the anterior neck", "C": "Clavipectoral fascia, investing the pectoralis minor and spanning from the clavicle to the coracoid", "D": "Investing layer of deep cervical fascia, surrounding the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles", "E": "Sibson fascia, also called the suprapleural membrane, suspending the cupola from the neck"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Sibson fascia, also called the suprapleural membrane, suspending the cupola from the neck): Sibson fascia is the suprapleural membrane, which suspends the cupola from the lowest cervical transverse process and the first rib, so tension there restricts the first rib and the thoracic inlet. The prevertebral layer lies nearby but does not cap the lung apex."}, {"id": 169, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 66-year-old man underwent right hemicolectomy for stage III colon adenocarcinoma and completed adjuvant chemotherapy. His oncologist plans surveillance visits every three months with colonoscopy at one year. Which serum test is most useful at these visits to detect recurrence of this tumor?", "options": {"A": "Alpha-fetoprotein, an oncofetal glycoprotein elevated in hepatocellular carcinoma and nonseminomatous germ cell tumors alike", "B": "Cancer antigen 125, a glycoprotein used to follow epithelial ovarian cancer and some peritoneal disease", "C": "Chromogranin A, a secretory granule protein elevated in neuroendocrine tumors such as small bowel carcinoid", "D": "Carcinoembryonic antigen, a glycoprotein elevated in many bowel cancers and useful for tracking relapse after resection", "E": "Carbohydrate antigen 19-9, a sialylated Lewis antigen mainly used to follow pancreatic and biliary adenocarcinoma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Carcinoembryonic antigen, a glycoprotein elevated in many bowel cancers and useful for tracking relapse after resection): Carcinoembryonic antigen is the marker followed after resection of colorectal adenocarcinoma, because a rising level after normalization suggests recurrence. It is not a screening test, but it is the appropriate surveillance marker here. The other markers track tumors from different tissues and are not expected to rise with colonic adenocarcinoma."}, {"id": 170, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A research physician measures renal blood flow in a healthy volunteer using a compound that is freely filtered and avidly secreted by tubular epithelium, so almost none remains in the renal vein. The urine and plasma concentrations are measured, and the hematocrit is 45 percent. Which substance was most likely infused?", "options": {"A": "Glucose, which is filtered and fully reabsorbed by sodium-linked transporters at normal plasma concentrations", "B": "Creatinine, which is filtered and slightly secreted by tubules, giving a mild overestimate of filtration rate", "C": "Para-aminohippurate, which the proximal tubule extracts almost completely from blood during a single circulation", "D": "Inulin, which is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so clearance equals filtration rate", "E": "Urea, which is filtered and partly reabsorbed along the nephron, with recycling in medullary collecting duct"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Para-aminohippurate, which the proximal tubule extracts almost completely from blood during a single circulation): Para-aminohippurate is filtered and then secreted by organic anion transporters, so about 90 percent is cleared in one pass and its clearance approximates effective renal plasma flow. Dividing by one minus the hematocrit converts plasma flow to blood flow. Inulin measures filtration only because it is not secreted."}, {"id": 171, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 55-year-old man with 15 years of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes has bilateral cataracts, burning pain in both feet, and loss of vibration sense. Analysis of an excised lens shows intracellular accumulation of a sugar alcohol. Which biochemical process most directly produces this accumulation?", "options": {"A": "Aldose reductase converts excess glucose to sorbitol, which accumulates and draws water into susceptible cells", "B": "Sorbitol dehydrogenase converts glucose directly to fructose, which depletes ATP through unregulated phosphorylation in the liver", "C": "Galactokinase converts excess galactose to galactitol, which accumulates in cells and causes osmotic injury", "D": "Nonenzymatic glycation of long lived proteins forming advanced glycation end products that cross link collagen", "E": "Protein kinase C activation by diacylglycerol increases vascular permeability and VEGF expression in the capillary endothelium"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Aldose reductase converts excess glucose to sorbitol, which accumulates and draws water into susceptible cells): In hyperglycemia the polyol pathway shunts excess glucose through aldose reductase to sorbitol. Lens and nerve cells have little sorbitol dehydrogenase, so sorbitol builds up, draws water in, and contributes to cataracts and neuropathy. Advanced glycation and protein kinase C activation also contribute to complications but do not produce a sugar alcohol."}, {"id": 172, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 10-year-old boy falls from a bicycle onto an outstretched hand and has tenderness over the distal radius. Radiography shows a fracture line running across the growth plate and then exiting toward the shaft, producing a triangular fragment of shaft bone that stays attached to the epiphysis. Which Salter-Harris classification applies?", "options": {"A": "Type II, a fracture traversing the physis and metaphysis with the joint surface spared", "B": "Type IV, a vertical fracture crossing metaphysis, physis and epiphysis, entering the joint space", "C": "Type I, a pure separation through the physis without any bony fragment, joint spared", "D": "Type III, a fracture through the physis then exiting through the epiphysis into the joint", "E": "Type V, a crush injury of the physis that is often missed on initial films"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Type II, a fracture traversing the physis and metaphysis with the joint surface spared): The triangular metaphyseal fragment is the Thurston-Holland fragment of a type II injury, the most common Salter-Harris pattern. The epiphysis stays attached to the physis and the joint surface is intact, which separates it from type III and type IV injuries."}, {"id": 173, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has a mother and maternal uncle with colon cancer diagnosed in their forties and an aunt with endometrial cancer. Colonoscopy reveals an adenocarcinoma in the cecum. Immunohistochemistry of the tumor shows loss of MLH1 and PMS2 staining, and BRAF V600E mutation is absent. Which underlying defect best explains her cancer?", "options": {"A": "Biallelic MUTYH mutation impairing base excision repair of oxidative damage, producing dozens of adenomas", "B": "Sporadic MLH1 promoter hypermethylation with somatic BRAF mutation, arising through the serrated polyp pathway", "C": "Germline mutation in a DNA mismatch repair gene, causing microsatellite instability and hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer", "D": "Germline STK11 mutation producing hamartomatous polyps with perioral pigmentation and elevated risk of several cancers", "E": "Germline APC mutation producing hundreds of adenomatous polyps beginning in the teenage years with eventual carcinoma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Germline mutation in a DNA mismatch repair gene, causing microsatellite instability and hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer): Early onset right sided colon cancer with endometrial cancer in relatives and loss of MLH1 and PMS2 in a tumor without BRAF mutation points to Lynch syndrome, a germline defect in mismatch repair that causes microsatellite instability. Sporadic MLH1 methylation also silences MLH1 but is accompanied by BRAF V600E and lacks this family pattern."}, {"id": 174, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 43-year-old woman has recurrent epigastric pain and ulcers in the distal duodenum despite eradication of Helicobacter pylori and no NSAID use. She has chronic watery diarrhea that improves with a proton pump inhibitor. Fasting gastrin is modestly elevated and gastric pH is 1.5. Which result would most strongly support the suspected diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Rise in serum gastrin after a protein meal exceeding that seen in normal antral stimulation", "B": "Elevated fasting gastrin with gastric pH above 6, reflecting loss of parietal cell acid secretion", "C": "Paradoxical rise in serum gastrin after intravenous secretin, reflecting autonomous release from tumor cells", "D": "Fall in serum gastrin after intravenous secretin, reflecting normal antral G cell suppression", "E": "Elevated fasting gastrin that normalizes once acid suppression is stopped for several weeks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Paradoxical rise in serum gastrin after intravenous secretin, reflecting autonomous release from tumor cells): Ulcers in unusual locations with diarrhea, gastric acid hypersecretion and elevated gastrin suggest Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. Gastrinoma cells respond paradoxically to secretin with a rise in gastrin, whereas normal G cells are suppressed. A high pH with high gastrin indicates achlorhydria, as in atrophic gastritis."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 54-year-old man with a bicuspid aortic valve reports progressive exertional dyspnea. Pulse pressure is 90 mm Hg with bounding pulses and head bobbing. Examination reveals an early diastolic blowing murmur at the left sternal border and a mid to late diastolic rumble at the apex without an opening snap. Which condition best explains the apical rumble?", "options": {"A": "Left atrial myxoma obstructing mitral inflow with a tumor plop heard in early diastole", "B": "Tricuspid stenosis with right atrial enlargement and a prominent a wave in the jugular pulse", "C": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with dynamic outflow obstruction and systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve", "D": "Rheumatic mitral stenosis with a thickened and fused valve restricting diastolic inflow to the ventricle", "E": "Severe aortic regurgitation with the regurgitant jet impinging on the anterior mitral leaflet"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Severe aortic regurgitation with the regurgitant jet impinging on the anterior mitral leaflet): Wide pulse pressure, bounding pulses, and an early diastolic murmur establish severe aortic regurgitation. The regurgitant jet pushes the anterior mitral leaflet and partly closes it, making a functional apical rumble called the Austin Flint murmur. True mitral stenosis would produce an opening snap and does not explain the peripheral signs."}, {"id": 176, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A cohort study of 6,000 adults estimates the association between regular heavy alcohol use and gallstone disease, reporting a relative risk of 1.4 with a 95 percent confidence interval of 0.9 to 2.1. Which conclusion is best supported by these results?", "options": {"A": "The result shows no association exists because the interval includes the null value of one", "B": "The p value is below 0.05 because the lower limit of the interval nears one", "C": "Heavy alcohol use raises gallstone risk by 40 percent because the point estimate lies above one", "D": "The data are compatible with no association, so the result is not statistically significant", "E": "The wide interval shows the study was underpowered so a true effect exists but was missed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The data are compatible with no association, so the result is not statistically significant): The confidence interval includes 1.0, so the data are compatible with no association and the result is not statistically significant at the 0.05 level. This does not prove absence of association, because the interval is wide and also compatible with a 2-fold increase. The point estimate alone would not justify claiming a 40 percent increase."}, {"id": 177, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 9-year-old child develops fever, sore mouth, and painful target-like lesions with dusky centers on the trunk and palms nine days after starting an antibiotic. Mucosal erosions involve the lips and conjunctivae. Skin detachment totals six percent of body surface area. Which diagnosis is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, a toxin mediated superficial exfoliation sparing the mucous membranes", "B": "Stevens-Johnson syndrome, a drug reaction with mucosal erosions and under ten percent epidermal loss", "C": "DRESS syndrome, a delayed drug reaction with eosinophilia, facial edema, and visceral organ involvement", "D": "Erythema multiforme major, typically postinfectious with raised typical target lesions on extremities and mucosal involvement", "E": "Toxic epidermal necrolysis, a drug reaction with mucosal erosions and more than thirty percent epidermal loss"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stevens-Johnson syndrome, a drug reaction with mucosal erosions and under ten percent epidermal loss): Detachment under 10 percent of body surface with mucosal erosions and atypical targets after a new drug defines Stevens-Johnson syndrome, whereas detachment above 30 percent would be toxic epidermal necrolysis. Erythema multiforme major is usually herpes related with raised typical targets, and scalded skin spares the mucosa."}, {"id": 178, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with a levonorgestrel intrauterine device has light monthly bleeding. A mid-luteal serum progesterone ordered for an unrelated fertility study confirms that she ovulates regularly. She asks how the device is preventing pregnancy. Which mechanism is the primary contraceptive effect in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Blockade of tubal ciliary motion, preventing the fertilized ovum from reaching the uterine cavity", "B": "Local progestin effect thickening cervical mucus and thinning the endometrium, blocking sperm passage and implantation", "C": "Release of copper ions that are toxic to sperm and ova within the uterine cavity", "D": "Inhibition of follicle stimulating hormone that prevents follicle recruitment and creates an anovulatory state", "E": "Systemic suppression of the luteinizing hormone surge by negative feedback, preventing follicular rupture each cycle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Local progestin effect thickening cervical mucus and thinning the endometrium, blocking sperm passage and implantation): The levonorgestrel intrauterine system acts mainly locally, thickening cervical mucus to impede sperm and thinning the endometrium. Systemic levels are low enough that many users continue to ovulate, as the luteal progesterone here shows. Ovulation suppression is the dominant mechanism of combined hormonal pills, and copper release belongs to the copper device."}, {"id": 179, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 34-year-old woman develops sudden left hemiparesis after a long flight and has a swollen right calf. Examination reveals no murmur. Echocardiography shows a tunnel-like slit between two overlapping atrial septa, and bubble contrast crosses to the left atrium only after Valsalva release. Which developmental failure best explains the stroke mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Failure of the septum primum to fuse with the septum secundum after birth", "B": "Incomplete closure of the membranous interventricular septum allowing a left to right ventricular shunt", "C": "Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to spiral, leaving a communication between great vessels", "D": "Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse, leaving a low atrial septal defect", "E": "Excessive resorption of the septum primum producing a large central atrial defect"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Failure of the septum primum to fuse with the septum secundum after birth): A patent foramen ovale results when the flap of septum primum never seals against the septum secundum, leaving a slit that opens only when right atrial pressure rises, such as after Valsalva release. A venous clot can then cross to the left side and embolize to the brain. A fixed secundum defect would show a persistent shunt and usually a flow murmur."}, {"id": 180, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 41-year-old woman with severe anorexia nervosa and a BMI of 13 kg/m2 is admitted and started on full-calorie enteral feeding. On day 3 she develops generalized weakness, confusion, and a prolonged QT interval on telemetry. Serum phosphate is 1.2 mg/dL, with low potassium and magnesium. Which mechanism most likely explains the phosphate depletion?", "options": {"A": "Increased renal phosphate wasting from secondary hyperparathyroidism caused by chronic vitamin D deficiency", "B": "Gastrointestinal phosphate loss from purging with vomiting and diarrhea in the days before admission", "C": "Insulin surge after carbohydrate delivery pushes phosphate intracellularly for glycolytic intermediates and ATP synthesis", "D": "Impaired intestinal phosphate absorption from mucosal atrophy that develops during prolonged starvation", "E": "Binding of phosphate to calcium in soft tissues after an acute rise in serum calcium"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Insulin surge after carbohydrate delivery pushes phosphate intracellularly for glycolytic intermediates and ATP synthesis): In chronic starvation, total body phosphate is depleted despite a normal serum level. When carbohydrate is reintroduced, insulin stimulates cellular uptake of phosphate, potassium, and magnesium and drives phosphorylation reactions, causing the profound drop that precipitates arrhythmia and weakness. Poor absorption or purging would lower stores earlier, but the day 3 timing points to this intracellular shift."}, {"id": 181, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old retired coal miner with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis presents for an evaluation of cough. Chest radiograph shows several well-defined, rounded nodules 0.5 to 5 cm in diameter scattered in the periphery of both lungs, on a background of small upper lobe opacities. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Caplan syndrome, with necrobiotic nodules developing in lungs already affected by dust-related pneumoconiosis", "B": "Granulomatosis with polyangiitis, with necrotizing granulomas and cavitating nodules from small vessel vasculitis", "C": "Reactivation tuberculosis, with multiple caseating granulomas forming peripheral nodules in a patient on immunosuppression", "D": "Metastatic adenocarcinoma from an occult primary seeding the lung periphery with multiple cannonball lesions", "E": "Progressive massive fibrosis, with coalescence of simple nodules into large upper lobe conglomerate masses"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Caplan syndrome, with necrobiotic nodules developing in lungs already affected by dust-related pneumoconiosis): Caplan syndrome is the combination of rheumatoid arthritis and pneumoconiosis, here from coal dust, producing multiple well-defined peripheral necrobiotic nodules. Progressive massive fibrosis forms large conglomerate masses in the upper lobes rather than discrete peripheral nodules. Metastases and granulomatous disease are possible but are less likely with this background."}, {"id": 182, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a persistent dry cough from a lung tumor requests a cough suppressant. He takes fluoxetine daily for depression, has opioid use disorder in sustained remission, and has no history of seizures or allergies. Which agent is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Pseudoephedrine, an alpha-adrenergic agonist that shrinks nasal mucosa to reduce postnasal drip", "B": "Codeine, a mu opioid agonist that suppresses the medullary cough center and provides mild analgesia", "C": "Dextromethorphan, an NMDA receptor antagonist with sigma agonist activity at the medullary cough center", "D": "Benzonatate, a local anesthetic analog that blunts the afferent limb of the cough reflex", "E": "Guaifenesin, an expectorant that thins bronchial secretions to ease their clearance by coughing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Benzonatate, a local anesthetic analog that blunts the afferent limb of the cough reflex): Benzonatate anesthetizes stretch receptors in the lungs and pleura, suppressing cough without opioid or serotonergic activity. Dextromethorphan can precipitate serotonin syndrome with an SSRI, and fluoxetine inhibits its metabolism. Codeine is a poor choice after opioid use disorder, and expectorants and decongestants do not treat a dry cough."}, {"id": 183, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 15-year-old gymnast reports 2 months of low back pain that worsens with back extension and landing from vaults. Pain is reproduced when she stands on one leg and leans backward. Neurologic examination and plain radiographs are normal. Which condition is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Scheuermann kyphosis with anterior vertebral wedging of three consecutive thoracic vertebral bodies", "B": "Pars interarticularis stress reaction, a precursor to a fracture of the posterior vertebral arch", "C": "Sacroiliac joint dysfunction from asymmetric loading, with pain over the posterior superior iliac spine", "D": "Idiopathic adolescent scoliosis with muscle strain from compensatory asymmetric paraspinal loading", "E": "Lumbar disc herniation with nerve root irritation producing pain aggravated by forward flexion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pars interarticularis stress reaction, a precursor to a fracture of the posterior vertebral arch): Repetitive hyperextension in a young athlete causes pars interarticularis stress injury, producing back pain on extension and a positive single leg hyperextension test. Plain films are often normal early, so advanced imaging may be needed. Disc herniation worsens with flexion, and adolescent scoliosis is usually painless."}, {"id": 184, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 24-year-old man is stabbed in the back and, on examination, has absent pinprick sensation below a horizontal line across his belly button, with normal sensation above it. His upper extremity function and breathing are normal. Which dermatome level corresponds to this sensory boundary?", "options": {"A": "T4 dermatome, which wraps around the chest at the level of the nipples", "B": "T12 dermatome, which covers the lower abdomen at the level of the suprapubic region", "C": "T10 dermatome, which wraps around the abdomen at the level of the umbilicus", "D": "T7 dermatome, which wraps around the lower chest at the level of the xiphoid", "E": "L1 dermatome, which covers the groin at the level of the inguinal ligament"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (T10 dermatome, which wraps around the abdomen at the level of the umbilicus): The T10 dermatome supplies the skin around the umbilicus, so loss below that line places the sensory level at T10. T4 is the nipple line, T7 the xiphoid, T12 the suprapubic area, and L1 the groin. Normal arm function and breathing fit a thoracolumbar level rather than a cervical injury."}, {"id": 185, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 35-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum removed for Crohn disease. He now has fatigue, numbness in both feet, a hemoglobin of 9.4 g/dL with a mean corpuscular volume of 112 fL, and watery diarrhea after fatty meals. Which pair of substances is malabsorbed because of the resection?", "options": {"A": "Glucose and galactose, which enter through sodium coupled transporters in the proximal jejunum", "B": "Lactose and sucrose, which are hydrolyzed by brush border enzymes in the upper small bowel", "C": "Iron and folate, which are absorbed mainly in the duodenum and proximal jejunum", "D": "Calcium and magnesium, which rely on vitamin D dependent transport in the duodenum", "E": "Cobalamin bound to intrinsic factor and conjugated bile acids absorbed in the distal ileum"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cobalamin bound to intrinsic factor and conjugated bile acids absorbed in the distal ileum): The terminal ileum is the only site of absorption for the intrinsic factor and cobalamin complex and for reuptake of conjugated bile acids, so resection causes macrocytic anemia with neuropathy and bile acid diarrhea. Iron, folate, calcium, and sugars are absorbed in the proximal small bowel and are spared."}, {"id": 186, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 6-year-old boy has had 4 months of painless limp and intermittent right knee discomfort. Examination shows limited hip abduction and internal rotation, and a Trendelenburg gait. He is afebrile with a normal white cell count. Plain film shows a small, dense, flattened femoral epiphysis. Which process explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Bacterial seeding of metaphyseal vessels with pus formation and secondary joint space infection", "B": "Weakening of the proximal physis from obesity related shear leading to epiphyseal displacement", "C": "Self limited synovial inflammation after a viral illness producing an irritable hip with effusion", "D": "Inherited epiphyseal dysplasia with symmetric flattening of several epiphyses from defective cartilage matrix proteins", "E": "Idiopathic interruption of femoral head blood supply followed by bone death and gradual revascularization"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Idiopathic interruption of femoral head blood supply followed by bone death and gradual revascularization): Legg Calve Perthes disease is idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head in children aged 4 to 8, producing a painless limp, restricted abduction and rotation, and a dense flattened epiphysis on radiograph. The slipped epiphysis of adolescence and infection have different ages and findings. Synovitis does not change bone density."}, {"id": 187, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with known cirrhosis is admitted for confusion. The team wants a single laboratory test that reflects her current hepatic synthetic capacity better than albumin, which has a half-life of about three weeks and is also affected by nutrition and capillary leak. Which test best serves this purpose?", "options": {"A": "Prothrombin time with INR, reflecting rapid turnover of vitamin K dependent factor VII", "B": "Serum alanine aminotransferase, reflecting the quantity of hepatocytes currently releasing cytosolic enzyme into blood", "C": "Serum alkaline phosphatase, reflecting the integrity of canalicular bile flow and biliary epithelium", "D": "Gamma-glutamyl transferase, reflecting induction of microsomal enzymes by alcohol and certain drugs", "E": "Total bilirubin, reflecting hepatic conjugation capacity independent of hemolysis or biliary flow"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Prothrombin time with INR, reflecting rapid turnover of vitamin K dependent factor VII): Factor VII has a half-life of only a few hours, so the INR falls out of range quickly when synthesis fails and tracks function in real time once vitamin K is replete. Aminotransferases report hepatocyte injury, not capacity. Albumin lags by weeks and is confounded by nutrition and third spacing."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 34-year-old man with chronic low back pain is examined by an osteopathic student. The student records tenderness, tissue texture change, and asymmetry at L3 and is told one criterion remains to complete the diagnostic definition of somatic dysfunction. Which finding completes the standard four criteria?", "options": {"A": "Positive straight leg raise reproducing radicular symptoms on the affected side", "B": "Elevated serum inflammatory markers confirming local soft tissue injury at the involved level", "C": "Radiographic osteophyte formation at the facet joints of the symptomatic spinal level", "D": "Restricted range of motion on palpatory testing at the involved segment or region", "E": "Pain radiating into the lower extremity below the knee along a dermatome"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Restricted range of motion on palpatory testing at the involved segment or region): TART stands for tissue texture change, asymmetry, restriction of motion, and tenderness, so the missing element is restriction of motion found on motion testing. Serum markers, osteophytes, and radicular signs may accompany back pain but are not part of the somatic dysfunction definition."}, {"id": 189, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 12-year-old boy with mild persistent asthma and allergic rhinitis takes a once-daily oral tablet to reduce symptoms. His mother recently reports nightmares, irritability, and episodes of low mood since starting it. He has had no other medication changes. Which drug is he most likely taking?", "options": {"A": "Montelukast, a cysteinyl leukotriene receptor antagonist carrying a boxed warning for neuropsychiatric events", "B": "Zafirlukast, a leukotriene receptor antagonist taken twice daily on an empty stomach", "C": "Oral albuterol, a short-acting beta agonist producing tremor and tachycardia at standard doses", "D": "Zileuton, a 5-lipoxygenase inhibitor that mainly causes hepatotoxicity and requires liver enzyme monitoring", "E": "Theophylline, a methylxanthine whose toxicity causes tremor, tachyarrhythmia and seizures at high serum levels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Montelukast, a cysteinyl leukotriene receptor antagonist carrying a boxed warning for neuropsychiatric events): Montelukast is the once-daily leukotriene receptor antagonist used for mild persistent asthma and allergic rhinitis, and it carries a boxed warning for neuropsychiatric effects such as nightmares, irritability and depression. Zafirlukast is dosed twice daily, and zileuton is dosed more often and mainly threatens the liver. Theophylline toxicity is arrhythmic and convulsive."}, {"id": 190, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder and malnutrition is admitted with confusion and a serum sodium of 106 mEq/L. Treatment with hypertonic saline raises the sodium to 125 mEq/L over the first 12 hours. Four days later he develops dysarthria, dysphagia and flaccid quadriparesis, with preserved vertical eye movements and awareness. Which brain structure is most likely injured?", "options": {"A": "Lateral medulla, where infarction of the posterior inferior cerebellar territory causes dysphagia and crossed sensory loss", "B": "Dorsal midbrain tegmentum, where compression impairs upgaze and produces light near dissociation of the pupils", "C": "Basis pontis, where rapid osmotic shifts cause demyelination of descending corticospinal and corticobulbar motor fibers", "D": "Mammillary bodies and medial thalamus, where thiamine deficiency produces confusion, ataxia and amnesia in malnourished drinkers", "E": "Cerebral cortex diffusely, where cerebral edema from rapid sodium correction causes seizures and altered consciousness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Basis pontis, where rapid osmotic shifts cause demyelination of descending corticospinal and corticobulbar motor fibers): Raising the sodium by more than about 8 to 10 mEq/L in 24 hours in a chronically hyponatremic, malnourished patient dehydrates brain cells and injures oligodendrocytes, classically in the basis pontis. Corticospinal and corticobulbar damage gives quadriparesis and bulbar palsy with preserved vertical eye movements, the locked-in picture. Thiamine deficiency produces a different, earlier syndrome."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 40-year-old woman develops sudden dyspnea and hypoxemia 90 minutes after receiving fresh frozen plasma for a coagulopathy. Temperature is 38.4 C, blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg, and jugular veins are flat. Chest radiograph shows new bilateral infiltrates, and brain natriuretic peptide is normal. What most likely caused this reaction?", "options": {"A": "Recipient antibodies against donor red cell antigens causing intravascular complement mediated hemolysis", "B": "Circulatory overload from rapid volume expansion raising pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure in a failing heart", "C": "Donor antibodies to leukocyte antigens activating recipient neutrophils within the pulmonary capillary bed", "D": "Bacterial endotoxin from a contaminated unit triggering systemic cytokine release and septic shock", "E": "Recipient anti-IgA antibodies binding donor immunoglobulin and triggering mast cell mediator release"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Donor antibodies to leukocyte antigens activating recipient neutrophils within the pulmonary capillary bed): Acute noncardiogenic pulmonary edema within six hours of a plasma-containing product, with fever, hypotension, a normal natriuretic peptide and flat neck veins, is transfusion-related acute lung injury. Donor anti-HLA or anti-neutrophil antibodies activate recipient neutrophils in the lung and increase capillary permeability. Circulatory overload would show raised venous pressure and a high natriuretic peptide."}, {"id": 192, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 17-year-old boy has painful nodules and cysts across the face and upper back with early pitted scarring. Six months of topical tretinoin plus oral doxycycline produced no improvement. He is otherwise healthy and takes no other medications. Which treatment is most appropriate next?", "options": {"A": "Oral spironolactone, which blocks androgen receptors and lowers sebum production in affected skin", "B": "Topical benzoyl peroxide, which kills Cutibacterium acnes without promoting bacterial resistance to therapy", "C": "Intralesional triamcinolone to every nodule, which reduces inflammation and prevents formation of new comedones", "D": "Oral isotretinoin, which shrinks sebaceous glands and reduces Cutibacterium acnes colonization and inflammation", "E": "Oral minocycline substituted for doxycycline, which gives stronger anti-inflammatory action against the same organism"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Oral isotretinoin, which shrinks sebaceous glands and reduces Cutibacterium acnes colonization and inflammation): Severe nodulocystic acne with scarring that has failed a topical retinoid plus an oral antibiotic is the classic indication for isotretinoin, the only agent that durably reduces sebum output. Spironolactone is used in women with hormonal acne and does not suit a boy, while topical or intralesional measures cannot control widespread nodules."}, {"id": 193, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 58-year-old accountant has two years of inappropriate jokes, shoplifting, compulsive eating of sweets, and loss of empathy. His wife says he no longer cares about family events. Memory and navigation are relatively preserved, and neurologic examination is otherwise normal. Which imaging finding is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Prominent bilateral hippocampal and posterior parietal atrophy with relative preservation of frontal lobes", "B": "Asymmetric atrophy of frontal and anterior temporal lobes with relative sparing of the hippocampus", "C": "Cortical ribboning on diffusion imaging with rapidly progressive involvement of the deep gray nuclei", "D": "Confluent periventricular white matter hyperintensities with scattered lacunar infarcts in the basal ganglia", "E": "Atrophy of the caudate nucleus with enlargement of the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Asymmetric atrophy of frontal and anterior temporal lobes with relative sparing of the hippocampus): Early personality change, disinhibition, hyperorality, and apathy with preserved memory describe behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia. Imaging shows frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, and the hippocampus is relatively spared early. Posterior and hippocampal atrophy characterizes Alzheimer disease."}, {"id": 194, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A medical student examines a histology slide of spleen from a 35-year-old trauma donor. A small artery runs through a cuff of densely packed small lymphocytes within the white pulp. Immunostaining of this cuff is positive for CD3. Which structure is she looking at?", "options": {"A": "Marginal zone, the region between red and white pulp that traps blood borne encapsulated bacteria", "B": "Lymphoid follicle with a germinal center, the B cell zone where somatic hypermutation occurs", "C": "Cords of Billroth, the reticular meshwork where macrophages remove aged and damaged red cells", "D": "Splenic sinusoids, the discontinuous endothelial channels that filter red cells through narrow slits", "E": "Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath, the T cell compartment that surrounds each central arteriole"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Periarteriolar lymphoid sheath, the T cell compartment that surrounds each central arteriole): The periarteriolar lymphoid sheath is the T cell zone of the splenic white pulp that surrounds the central arteriole, and it stains for T cell markers. B cells occupy the adjacent follicles, and the marginal zone lies at the outer edge of the white pulp. Cords of Billroth and sinusoids belong to the red pulp."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 15-year-old girl has a fixed thoracic curve noted at a school physical. In the standing radiograph shown, the thoracic spine bows to the patient's right over several adjacent segments. The physician classifies the dysfunction using Fryette mechanics. Which description best fits the curve?", "options": {"A": "Type I group curve, sidebent right with vertebral bodies rotated left toward the concavity", "B": "Type II single segment, sidebent left and rotated left, producing a leftward concavity", "C": "Type III motion, in which initial rotation of the curve then modifies sidebending and flexion", "D": "Type II single segment, sidebent right and rotated right, producing a rightward bow", "E": "Type I group curve, sidebent left with vertebral bodies rotated right toward the convexity"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Type I group curve, sidebent left with vertebral bodies rotated right toward the convexity): A curve bowing to the right is concave left, so the group is sidebent left, and a neutral group curve follows type I mechanics with rotation to the opposite side, which here is right toward the convexity. Type II coupling applies to single non-neutral segments rather than a multi-segment curve.", "image": "question_images/q_195.png"}, {"id": 196, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 28-year-old primigravid woman at 34 weeks gestation has right upper quadrant pain and a headache. Blood pressure is 158/104 mm Hg on two measurements four hours apart, and urine protein is 3+. Platelet count is 82,000/mm3, aspartate aminotransferase is elevated, and the smear shows schistocytes. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Acute fatty liver of pregnancy with hypoglycemia, coagulopathy and prominent hepatic microvesicular steatosis", "B": "Gestational hypertension without proteinuria, which resolves postpartum and spares platelet and hepatic function", "C": "Preeclampsia with HELLP syndrome from endothelial injury causing microangiopathic hemolysis and hepatic ischemia", "D": "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura from ADAMTS13 deficiency with fever, renal failure and neurologic findings", "E": "Gestational thrombocytopenia from hemodilution with mild platelet reduction and no hypertension or hemolysis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Preeclampsia with HELLP syndrome from endothelial injury causing microangiopathic hemolysis and hepatic ischemia): Hypertension with proteinuria after 20 weeks defines preeclampsia, and hemolysis with elevated liver enzymes and low platelets identifies the HELLP variant. Schistocytes reflect microangiopathic hemolysis from the endothelial injury. Acute fatty liver of pregnancy tends to cause hypoglycemia and coagulopathy rather than severe hypertension, and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura would not improve with delivery."}, {"id": 197, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 50-year-old man has C4 diagnosed as extended, rotated left, sidebent left after a rear-end collision weeks ago, with no neurologic deficit or instability on imaging. The physician then chooses muscle energy treatment. Which sequence correctly applies the technique to this segment?", "options": {"A": "Engage extension, left rotation, and left sidebending, then hold passively about ninety seconds", "B": "Engage flexion, right rotation, and right sidebending, then have the patient push toward freedom against counterforce", "C": "Engage extension, right rotation, and left sidebending, then have the patient push against counterforce briefly", "D": "Engage flexion, right rotation, and right sidebending, then have the patient push toward that barrier", "E": "Engage flexion, right rotation, and right sidebending, then apply a high-velocity low-amplitude thrust through the barrier"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Engage flexion, right rotation, and right sidebending, then have the patient push toward freedom against counterforce): Muscle energy is a direct technique, so the physician engages the restrictive barrier, here flexion, right rotation, and right sidebending, and the patient pushes isometrically toward the direction of freedom against a counterforce. Holding the position of ease describes an indirect method, and a thrust belongs to a different technique."}, {"id": 198, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 41-year-old firefighter is seen six weeks after a building collapse in which a colleague died. He has recurrent nightmares, avoids the fire station, startles at sirens and feels detached from family. He sleeps four hours nightly and drinks two beers to settle. Symptoms began the week of the event. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Generalized anxiety disorder, because tension and sleep disturbance dominate and have lasted several weeks", "B": "Adjustment disorder with anxiety, because distress followed an identifiable stressor and impaired his work and sleep", "C": "Alcohol use disorder, because nightly drinking explains the insomnia, irritability and exaggerated startle he describes", "D": "Posttraumatic stress disorder, because intrusion, avoidance and arousal symptoms persist beyond one month after the trauma", "E": "Acute stress disorder, because symptoms began immediately after a life-threatening event with intrusion and avoidance features"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Posttraumatic stress disorder, because intrusion, avoidance and arousal symptoms persist beyond one month after the trauma): Reexperiencing, avoidance, negative mood changes and hyperarousal after a traumatic exposure make this a trauma disorder, and duration beyond one month separates posttraumatic stress disorder from acute stress disorder, which lasts three days to one month. Adjustment disorder is reserved for presentations that do not meet full criteria for another disorder."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 38-year-old man has 3 weeks of low back pain radiating down the posterior thigh and lateral calf to the dorsum of the foot after lifting. He has difficulty walking on his heels, great toe extension is weak, and the ankle reflex is normal. Which nerve root is most likely compressed?", "options": {"A": "L5 root, typically compressed by a posterolateral L4 to L5 disc herniation", "B": "L3 root, typically compressed by a posterolateral L2 to L3 disc herniation", "C": "L4 root, typically compressed by a posterolateral L3 to L4 disc herniation", "D": "S2 root, typically compressed by a central disc herniation at the lumbosacral junction", "E": "S1 root, typically compressed by a posterolateral L5 to S1 disc herniation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (L5 root, typically compressed by a posterolateral L4 to L5 disc herniation): L5 radiculopathy produces weakness of great toe extension and ankle dorsiflexion with numbness on the dorsum of the foot and a preserved ankle reflex. A posterolateral herniation at L4 to L5 compresses the traversing L5 root. S1 radiculopathy instead weakens plantar flexion and abolishes the ankle jerk."}, {"id": 200, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 63-year-old man with chronic kidney disease who takes low dose aspirin has sudden severe pain and swelling of the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Aspiration yields turbid fluid with 55,000 white cells per microliter. Which finding is expected on polarized light microscopy when a crystal's long axis lies parallel to the compensator axis?", "options": {"A": "Needle shaped crystals that appear yellow, reflecting negative birefringence of monosodium urate", "B": "Envelope shaped crystals that appear colorless, reflecting the absent birefringence of calcium oxalate", "C": "Rhomboid shaped crystals that appear yellow, reflecting negative birefringence of calcium pyrophosphate", "D": "Needle shaped crystals that appear blue, reflecting positive birefringence of monosodium urate", "E": "Rhomboid shaped crystals that appear blue, reflecting positive birefringence of calcium pyrophosphate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Needle shaped crystals that appear yellow, reflecting negative birefringence of monosodium urate): Acute podagra in a patient with kidney disease and aspirin use points to gout, whose monosodium urate crystals are needle shaped and negatively birefringent, appearing yellow when parallel to the compensator axis. Calcium pyrophosphate crystals are rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent and blue when parallel, which makes them the tempting mix up."}, {"id": 201, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has two months of drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen late in the day and improve after resting her eyes. Pupils are normal, reflexes are intact, and sensation is normal. Ice applied over a ptotic lid improves it briefly. Which pathogenic mechanism is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels reducing acetylcholine release at nerve terminals", "B": "Loss of anterior horn motor neurons producing fasciculations with mixed upper motor signs", "C": "Immune attack on peripheral nerve myelin producing segmental conduction block and diffuse areflexia", "D": "Autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors reducing endplate potentials at the neuromuscular junction", "E": "Botulinum toxin cleavage of SNARE proteins preventing acetylcholine vesicle fusion at nerve endings"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors reducing endplate potentials at the neuromuscular junction): Fatigable ptosis and diplopia with normal pupils and reflexes, improving with rest or cold, are typical of myasthenia gravis. The antibodies target postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors, reducing receptor number and endplate potentials. Lambert-Eaton syndrome involves presynaptic calcium channels and strength that improves with repeated effort."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 31-year-old man who injects heroin has five days of fever, pleuritic chest pain, and cough. Temperature is 39.2 C. A new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border increases with inspiration. Chest radiography shows multiple peripheral nodular opacities. Blood cultures are drawn. Which organism most likely causes his valvular infection?", "options": {"A": "Streptococcus gallolyticus, which reaches the valves from colonic neoplasia with transient bacteremia", "B": "Staphylococcus epidermidis, which forms biofilm on prosthetic valve material after cardiac surgery", "C": "Enterococcus faecalis, which seeds valves after genitourinary or lower gastrointestinal instrumentation and bacteremia", "D": "Viridans group streptococci, which colonize valves previously damaged by rheumatic or congenital disease", "E": "Staphylococcus aureus, which adheres to normal valve endothelium through fibronectin binding surface proteins"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Staphylococcus aureus, which adheres to normal valve endothelium through fibronectin binding surface proteins): Injection drug use with a tricuspid murmur that grows with inspiration and septic pulmonary emboli points to right sided endocarditis, most often from Staphylococcus aureus. Viridans streptococci infect damaged left sided valves subacutely. Enterococcus, coagulase negative staphylococci, and Streptococcus gallolyticus have different exposures."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "community_health", "question": "The dose-response curves shown compare two analgesics, drug X and drug Y, acting at the same receptor in a pain model. Both curves plateau at the same maximal relief, but drug X also causes more sedation per milligram. Which drug is more potent, and which is more efficacious?", "options": {"A": "Drug X is more efficacious because sedation shows a greater biologic effect at each dose", "B": "Drug X is more potent, and the two drugs are equally efficacious at the same receptor", "C": "Drug Y is more potent because its higher dose produces a more complete relief of pain", "D": "Neither drug can be ranked until their dose-response curves are compared at equal sedation", "E": "Drug X is both more potent and more efficacious because it needs a lower dose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Drug X is more potent, and the two drugs are equally efficacious at the same receptor): Potency is the dose needed for a given effect and is read from the horizontal position of the curve, so the curve to the left belongs to the more potent drug. Efficacy is the maximal effect, which is identical because both curves reach the same plateau. Sedation is a separate effect and does not change either property for analgesia.", "image": "question_images/q_203.png"}, {"id": 204, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man has sudden vertigo, hoarseness, difficulty swallowing, and unsteady gait. Examination shows left-sided facial pain and temperature loss, right-sided body pain and temperature loss, a left Horner syndrome, and left limb ataxia. Strength is normal. Occlusion of which vessel most likely accounts for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplying the lateral caudal pons, inner ear, and facial nucleus", "B": "Superior cerebellar artery supplying the rostral lateral pons and the superior cerebellar peduncle", "C": "Basilar artery paramedian perforators supplying the basis pontis and descending corticospinal fibers", "D": "Anterior spinal artery branches supplying the medial medulla and the pyramidal decussation tracts", "E": "Left posterior inferior cerebellar artery or its parent vertebral artery supplying the lateral medulla"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Left posterior inferior cerebellar artery or its parent vertebral artery supplying the lateral medulla): Crossed sensory loss, dysphagia and hoarseness from nucleus ambiguus involvement, ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and ataxia define the lateral medullary syndrome. It results from occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery or the vertebral artery. The anterior inferior cerebellar artery lesion adds facial paralysis and hearing loss, and medial medullary infarcts cause weakness."}, {"id": 205, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 44-year-old woman has resistant hypertension despite three antihypertensive drugs, along with muscle weakness and nocturia. Blood pressure is 168/102 mm Hg without edema. Her brother also has hypertension, and she takes no diuretic or oral contraceptive. Her laboratory panel is shown. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Apparent mineralocorticoid excess from deficient 11 beta hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase activity and low aldosterone", "B": "Primary aldosteronism from autonomous adrenal mineralocorticoid production suppressing plasma renin activity", "C": "Renal artery stenosis with renin dependent angiotensin II driven hypertension and secondary aldosterone excess", "D": "Cushing syndrome with cortisol excess saturating renal mineralocorticoid receptor protection and low aldosterone", "E": "Liddle syndrome with constitutive collecting duct sodium channel activity and suppressed aldosterone production"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Primary aldosteronism from autonomous adrenal mineralocorticoid production suppressing plasma renin activity): Hypertension with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, suppressed renin, and a high aldosterone level indicates primary aldosteronism. Renal artery stenosis would raise renin. Liddle syndrome, apparent mineralocorticoid excess, and Cushing syndrome mimic the electrolyte picture but have low aldosterone.", "image": "question_images/q_205.png"}, {"id": 206, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 44-year-old man is found wandering in a distant city, unable to recall his name, family or job. He is calm and oriented to place. Neurologic examination, head CT, toxicology and EEG are normal. His wife reports that he lost his business and his brother died the week before he left. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Depersonalization disorder, with persistent detachment from one's own body while memory remains fully intact", "B": "Transient global amnesia, with abrupt anterograde memory loss lasting hours and preserved personal identity", "C": "Dissociative identity disorder, with two or more distinct personality states taking recurrent control of behavior", "D": "Dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, triggered by severe psychological stress with intact basic cognition", "E": "Wernicke Korsakoff syndrome, with thiamine deficiency causing confabulation and anterograde amnesia in alcohol use"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, triggered by severe psychological stress with intact basic cognition): Sudden inability to recall personal information after severe stress, with purposeful travel and a normal medical workup, is dissociative amnesia with fugue. Transient global amnesia keeps identity intact and is anterograde, and Korsakoff syndrome follows chronic thiamine deficiency with confabulation. Dissociative identity disorder needs distinct personality states."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "Before performing lymphatic techniques on a patient with chronic lower extremity edema, a medical student reviews the thoracic duct. The duct begins as a dilated lymphatic sac that collects lymph from the lumbar and intestinal trunks. At which location does this sac lie?", "options": {"A": "Anterior to the L1 and L2 vertebral bodies, to the right of the aorta", "B": "Posterior to the left clavicle, at the junction of the subclavian and jugular veins", "C": "Anterior to the T12 vertebral body, between the crura at the aortic hiatus", "D": "Anterior to the T4 vertebral body, posterior to the arch of the aorta", "E": "Anterior to the sacral promontory, at the bifurcation of the common iliac vessels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Anterior to the L1 and L2 vertebral bodies, to the right of the aorta): The cisterna chyli lies anterior to L1 and L2 and gathers lymph from the lumbar and intestinal trunks before the duct ascends. The duct passes through the aortic hiatus at T12, a common error because the sac and the hiatus are close but distinct."}, {"id": 208, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 3-day-old full term boy becomes lethargic and tachypneic after feeding, with vomiting and a respiratory alkalosis. Plasma ammonia is 420 micromol/L. Urine organic acid analysis shows markedly elevated orotic acid, and plasma citrulline is low. A maternal uncle died in infancy of an unexplained illness. Which enzyme is most likely deficient?", "options": {"A": "N acetylglutamate synthase, which produces the allosteric activator required by the first urea cycle enzyme", "B": "Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I, the rate limiting mitochondrial enzyme that needs N acetylglutamate as an activator", "C": "Argininosuccinate synthetase, which condenses citrulline with aspartate and whose loss causes marked citrullinemia", "D": "Arginase, the final cytosolic enzyme that cleaves arginine into urea and ornithine, causing spastic diplegia", "E": "Ornithine transcarbamylase, an X linked defect that diverts mitochondrial carbamoyl phosphate into pyrimidine synthesis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ornithine transcarbamylase, an X linked defect that diverts mitochondrial carbamoyl phosphate into pyrimidine synthesis): Hyperammonemia with high orotic acid and low citrulline in a male infant with an affected maternal uncle indicates ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency. Carbamoyl phosphate accumulates, leaves the mitochondrion, and enters the pyrimidine pathway, raising orotic acid. Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I deficiency is similar but gives normal orotic acid."}, {"id": 209, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A researcher plots sensitivity against 1 minus specificity for every possible cutoff of a new biomarker for pancreatic cancer, using samples from 150 patients with disease and 150 without. Which feature of the resulting curve summarizes the overall discriminating ability of the test?", "options": {"A": "The predictive value at the midpoint, where a higher value means fewer false negatives", "B": "The cutoff value at the far right end, where specificity is at its highest", "C": "The sensitivity at the leftmost point, where a higher value means better overall accuracy", "D": "The area beneath the plotted line, where 1.0 is perfect and 0.5 is chance", "E": "The slope at the curve's steepest point, where a steeper rise means higher prevalence"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The area beneath the plotted line, where 1.0 is perfect and 0.5 is chance): The area under the ROC curve summarizes discrimination across all cutoffs: 1.0 means perfect separation and 0.5 means performance no better than chance. Individual points on the curve show the trade-off at one cutoff, and the far right end reflects high sensitivity with low specificity, not good overall performance."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 26-year-old woman, six days postpartum, breastfeeds her newborn. She notes that milk begins to drip from both breasts when she hears her infant cry, and that suckling triggers uterine cramps. She is afebrile and her breasts are soft without erythema or focal tenderness. Which hormonal pathway explains the milk drip?", "options": {"A": "Neural reflex releasing oxytocin from the posterior pituitary to contract myoepithelial cells around alveoli", "B": "Falling estrogen and progesterone after placental delivery causing smooth muscle contraction around milk ducts", "C": "Dopamine release from the hypothalamus increasing prolactin output and ejecting milk through ductal smooth muscle", "D": "Suckling-induced prolactin release from the anterior pituitary directly squeezing milk from the alveolar lumen", "E": "Human placental lactogen persisting in the circulation and stimulating milk secretion from alveolar cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Neural reflex releasing oxytocin from the posterior pituitary to contract myoepithelial cells around alveoli): Suckling and conditioned cues like an infant's cry trigger a neural reflex that releases oxytocin from the posterior pituitary, contracting myoepithelial cells and ejecting milk. The same hormone contracts the uterus, explaining the cramps. Prolactin drives milk synthesis, not ejection, and dopamine inhibits it."}, {"id": 211, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 68-year-old man with a 20-year history of poorly controlled diabetes and chronic kidney disease is examined for recurrent flank discomfort. The osteopathic physician finds paraspinal tissue at T10 to L1 that is cool, dry, ropy and fibrotic with little tenderness. Which interpretation best fits these findings in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Age-related connective tissue degeneration of the paraspinal muscles, with no reflex contribution from the kidneys", "B": "Acute viscerosomatic reflex from renal colic through parasympathetic vagal outflow producing warm, boggy paraspinal tissue", "C": "Acute somatic dysfunction from recent strain, with edema and erythema expected in the involved paraspinal tissue", "D": "Long-standing somatic dysfunction maintained by a viscerosomatic reflex from renal disease acting through sympathetic outflow", "E": "Local quadratus lumborum strain from overuse, producing tender hypertonic muscle with acute inflammatory features"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Long-standing somatic dysfunction maintained by a viscerosomatic reflex from renal disease acting through sympathetic outflow): Cool, dry, ropy, fibrotic and minimally tender tissue is the chronic texture pattern, and T10 to L1 matches the sympathetic viscerosomatic level of the kidney. Years of renal disease can sustain this reflex. Warm, moist, boggy findings would indicate an acute process, and the vagus does not produce paraspinal texture change at these levels."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 30-year-old nurse starting a new job receives an intradermal tuberculin test. No change is seen at 30 minutes, but 48 hours later there is a firm raised area of induration 14 mm across with little redness. She is asymptomatic. Which immunologic mechanism best explains the skin reaction?", "options": {"A": "Antibodies bind antigens on cell surfaces and activate complement, causing local cell destruction", "B": "Direct activation of the alternative complement pathway by tuberculin protein, causing local edema and swelling", "C": "Preformed IgE on mast cells cross-links with allergen, releasing histamine within minutes of re-exposure", "D": "Sensitized CD4 T cells release cytokines and recruit macrophages hours after allergen re-exposure", "E": "Soluble antigen-antibody complexes deposit in dermal vessel walls, activating complement and neutrophil recruitment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sensitized CD4 T cells release cytokines and recruit macrophages hours after allergen re-exposure): Induration at 48 hours without an early wheal marks a delayed type hypersensitivity response: memory CD4 T cells recognize antigen presented on macrophages and release interferon gamma and other cytokines that recruit and activate macrophages. Immediate IgE reactions peak within minutes, and immune complex reactions in the Arthus pattern peak within hours with vascular damage."}, {"id": 213, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man with long-standing poorly controlled hypertension and diabetes develops sudden weakness of the left face, arm, and leg over minutes. Sensation, vision, language, and cognition are intact on examination. MRI shows a 1 cm infarct in the posterior limb of the right internal capsule. Which underlying vessel pathology is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Thrombosis of basilar artery perforators supplying the ventral pons and descending corticospinal fibers", "B": "Lipohyalinosis of small penetrating lenticulostriate arteries damaged by chronic hypertension and diabetes", "C": "Atherosclerotic carotid stenosis causing artery-to-artery embolism into cortical branch territories", "D": "Watershed hypoperfusion between anterior and middle cerebral artery territories during systemic hypotension", "E": "Cardiac embolus from atrial fibrillation occluding the proximal middle cerebral artery trunk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lipohyalinosis of small penetrating lenticulostriate arteries damaged by chronic hypertension and diabetes): A pure motor hemiparesis with no cortical signs and a small deep capsular infarct defines a lacunar stroke. The mechanism is lipohyalinosis of small penetrating arteries driven by hypertension and diabetes. Embolic and large artery mechanisms usually produce cortical deficits such as aphasia, neglect, or visual field loss."}, {"id": 214, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 72-year-old man is found supine and unresponsive after a seizure and vomiting on a hospital ward. Over the next day he develops fever, cough, and hypoxemia. He remained supine during the event. A chest radiograph is obtained. In which segment is the infiltrate most likely to appear?", "options": {"A": "Basal segments of the right lower lobe, which are most dependent in the upright position", "B": "Anterior segment of the right upper lobe, which is most dependent in supine position", "C": "Apical segment of the left upper lobe, which is most dependent during head-down positioning", "D": "Superior segment of the right lower lobe, the most dependent bronchial opening in supine position", "E": "Lingular segments of the left upper lobe, which are most dependent in the left lateral position"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Superior segment of the right lower lobe, the most dependent bronchial opening in supine position): In the supine position the superior segment of the lower lobe, together with the posterior segment of the upper lobe, is the most dependent, and the right bronchus is wider and straighter, so the right superior segment is a typical site. Upright aspiration favors the basal segments, and the other choices fit different body positions."}, {"id": 215, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 38-year-old woman with bipolar disorder has taken lithium for eleven years and now reports drinking and urinating large volumes. Serum sodium is 147 mEq/L, and urine osmolality is 190 mOsm/kg and does not rise after intranasal desmopressin. Lithium level is high-normal and she wishes to continue it. Which drug would best reduce her polyuria?", "options": {"A": "Demeclocycline, which antagonizes vasopressin action in the collecting duct and is used to treat chronic hyponatremia", "B": "Spironolactone, which blocks aldosterone receptors and restores aquaporin expression that chronic lithium exposure has suppressed", "C": "Higher dose desmopressin, which activates V2 receptors to insert aquaporin channels in collecting duct cells", "D": "Hydrochlorothiazide, which induces mild volume depletion and proximal reabsorption, lowering urine volume in nephrogenic diabetes insipidus", "E": "Amiloride, which blocks the epithelial sodium channel that carries lithium into collecting duct principal cells"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Amiloride, which blocks the epithelial sodium channel that carries lithium into collecting duct principal cells): Lithium enters principal cells through the epithelial sodium channel and impairs aquaporin 2 expression and signaling, causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus that does not respond to desmopressin. Amiloride blocks that entry and is first-line when lithium must continue. Thiazides also reduce urine volume but raise lithium levels, which is hazardous when the level is already high-normal."}, {"id": 216, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 22-year-old snowboarder falls on an outstretched hand and has tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox with normal initial wrist radiographs. Cast immobilization is advised because the injured bone carries a risk of nonunion and avascular necrosis. Which anatomical feature best explains that risk?", "options": {"A": "The dorsal intercarpal arch supplies the proximal pole directly, protecting it from fracture ischemia", "B": "The bone lacks tendon insertions, so fragments rotate apart and cannot heal together", "C": "Articular cartilage covers the whole surface, leaving no entry points for nutrient vessels", "D": "Blood enters through the distal pole, so a waist fracture strands the proximal fragment", "E": "Blood enters only through the proximal pole, so a distal fracture strands the tip"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Blood enters through the distal pole, so a waist fracture strands the proximal fragment): The scaphoid receives most of its blood from radial artery branches that enter near the distal pole and run retrograde, so a waist or proximal fracture can leave the proximal fragment ischemic. This is why snuffbox tenderness is treated as a fracture despite normal early films."}, {"id": 217, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 4-month-old girl has poor feeding, hypotonia and developmental delay. Laboratory testing shows persistent lactic acidosis with elevated plasma alanine and a normal lactate to pyruvate ratio. Symptoms worsen after high-carbohydrate feedings, and a ketogenic diet with added lysine and leucine is planned. Which enzyme is most likely deficient?", "options": {"A": "Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA for entry into the citric acid cycle", "B": "Pyruvate kinase, catalyzing the last substrate-level phosphorylation of glycolysis and sustaining red cell ATP production", "C": "Pyruvate carboxylase, converting pyruvate to oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis and replenishing citric acid cycle intermediates in mitochondria", "D": "Cytochrome c oxidase, transferring electrons to oxygen at the end of the respiratory chain in mitochondria", "E": "Lactate dehydrogenase, interconverting pyruvate and lactate while regenerating NAD+ so anaerobic glycolysis can continue in tissues"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, converting pyruvate to acetyl-CoA for entry into the citric acid cycle): A block in pyruvate dehydrogenase sends pyruvate toward lactate and alanine while the cytosolic redox state, and so the lactate to pyruvate ratio, stays normal. Carbohydrate loading worsens acidosis, and the purely ketogenic amino acids lysine and leucine provide acetyl-CoA without needing the complex. Respiratory chain and pyruvate carboxylase defects would raise the ratio."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 41-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension has an ECG during a clinic visit. The QRS complex is predominantly negative in lead I and predominantly positive in lead aVF, and the rhythm is sinus at 78 beats per minute. Which description of the mean frontal QRS axis and its likely cause is correct?", "options": {"A": "Left axis deviation, reflecting left anterior fascicular block from conduction system disease", "B": "Normal axis, because the net QRS deflection in the inferior leads is positive", "C": "Right axis deviation, reflecting a lateral wall myocardial infarction that removed left-sided forces", "D": "Left axis deviation, reflecting left ventricular hypertrophy from long-standing systemic hypertension", "E": "Right axis deviation, reflecting right ventricular hypertrophy from chronic pressure overload"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Right axis deviation, reflecting right ventricular hypertrophy from chronic pressure overload): A negative QRS in lead I with a positive aVF places the axis between plus 90 and plus 180 degrees, which is right axis deviation. In pulmonary hypertension the right ventricle hypertrophies and shifts the mean vector rightward. Lateral infarction can cause right axis deviation but does not fit a chronic pulmonary vascular disease."}, {"id": 219, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 57-year-old woman has right leg lymphedema after pelvic surgery and type 2 diabetes. Her physician plans a full lymphatic sequence using the principle that fluid must have a clear path central to the area being treated. Which sequence best follows this principle?", "options": {"A": "Pelvic diaphragm, thoracic inlet, respiratory diaphragm, inguinal region, then the thigh and leg together", "B": "Foot and leg first, then thigh, inguinal region, pelvic diaphragm, respiratory diaphragm, and thoracic inlet last", "C": "Inguinal region first, then thoracic inlet, thigh, pelvic diaphragm, respiratory diaphragm, and the leg last", "D": "Respiratory diaphragm, thigh, inguinal region, thoracic inlet, pelvic diaphragm, and finally the foot and leg", "E": "Thoracic inlet, respiratory diaphragm, then pelvic diaphragm, inguinal region, thigh, and finally the leg"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Thoracic inlet, respiratory diaphragm, then pelvic diaphragm, inguinal region, thigh, and finally the leg): Lymph from the leg must travel through the inguinal region, pelvic diaphragm, and respiratory diaphragm before it reaches the thoracic duct terminus, so the central sites are opened first and the periphery is treated last. Starting at the foot pushes fluid into restricted channels that cannot yet accept it."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve has had a stable INR on warfarin for two years. He is started on a drug for pulmonary tuberculosis, and over the next three weeks his INR falls to 1.2. His diet and adherence are unchanged. Which mechanism most likely explains the subtherapeutic INR?", "options": {"A": "Induction of hepatic CYP enzymes accelerating clearance of the anticoagulant after several weeks of exposure", "B": "Displacement of warfarin from albumin binding sites, reducing the free fraction available", "C": "Reduced gastrointestinal absorption of warfarin because the new drug binds it in the intestinal lumen", "D": "Inhibition of CYP2C9 by the new drug, reducing formation of the active warfarin metabolite", "E": "Decreased renal tubular reabsorption of warfarin increasing urinary clearance of the unchanged drug"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Induction of hepatic CYP enzymes accelerating clearance of the anticoagulant after several weeks of exposure): Rifampin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes through nuclear receptor activation, which takes days to weeks and speeds metabolism of warfarin, lowering the INR. Displacement from albumin would raise free drug, not lower the effect. Warfarin is cleared by hepatic metabolism, so renal handling does not explain the change."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with decompensated cirrhosis and tense ascites is admitted with creatinine rising from 1.0 to 2.8 mg/dL over five days. Urinalysis shows no protein or blood, urine sodium is 6 mEq/L, and creatinine is unchanged after two days of albumin infusion with diuretics held. Which mechanism best explains her renal failure?", "options": {"A": "Acute tubular necrosis from ischemic injury, expected to produce muddy brown casts and high urine sodium", "B": "Prerenal azotemia from simple volume depletion, expected to correct promptly with albumin and diuretic withdrawal", "C": "Intense renal vasoconstriction caused by splanchnic arterial vasodilation and reduced effective circulating volume", "D": "Obstructive uropathy from ascites compressing the ureters, expected to cause hydronephrosis on imaging", "E": "Immune complex glomerulonephritis from viral infection, expected to produce hematuria and red cell casts"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intense renal vasoconstriction caused by splanchnic arterial vasodilation and reduced effective circulating volume): Low urine sodium, a bland sediment and no response to albumin challenge fit hepatorenal syndrome, in which splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume and drives severe renal vasoconstriction. Acute tubular necrosis would have urine sodium above 40 and muddy brown casts. Prerenal azotemia from volume loss would correct with the albumin."}, {"id": 222, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 32-year-old woman presents for a routine skin check. She has a firm, 6-mm, brown papule on her lower leg that appeared a few months after an insect bite and dimples inward when squeezed from the sides. It is asymptomatic. Which histologic finding would most likely be seen on biopsy?", "options": {"A": "Nests of melanocytes at the dermal-epidermal junction with maturation and descent into the dermis", "B": "Proliferating blood vessels lined by plump endothelium within lobules of the superficial dermis", "C": "Dermal proliferation of spindle cells with collagen trapping at the lesion periphery and overlying epidermal hyperplasia", "D": "Atypical keratinocytes throughout the epidermis with full-thickness loss of normal maturation", "E": "Basaloid keratinocyte proliferation containing keratin-filled horn cysts and pseudohorn cysts within the epidermis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dermal proliferation of spindle cells with collagen trapping at the lesion periphery and overlying epidermal hyperplasia): A firm brown papule on the leg that dimples when pinched is a dermatofibroma, a benign fibrohistiocytic proliferation in the dermis. Histology shows spindle cells entrapping collagen bundles at the edge with overlying epidermal hyperplasia. Nevus nests, horn cysts and in situ carcinoma do not produce the dimple sign."}, {"id": 223, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 63-year-old woman on postoperative day one after open cholecystectomy has a temperature of 38.1 C, mild tachypnea, and diminished breath sounds with crackles at both lung bases. Oxygen saturation is 92 percent on room air and the chest radiograph shows linear opacities at the bases. Which process is the most likely cause of her findings?", "options": {"A": "Aspiration pneumonitis from gastric contents entering the airway during induction of anesthesia", "B": "Alveolar collapse from reduced lung volumes and shallow breathing after upper abdominal surgery", "C": "Pulmonary embolism from venous stasis in the deep veins of the lower extremities", "D": "Hospital-acquired pneumonia from bacterial colonization of the lower airways after surgery", "E": "Cardiogenic pulmonary edema from volume overload and impaired left ventricular filling after surgery"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alveolar collapse from reduced lung volumes and shallow breathing after upper abdominal surgery): Fever, bibasilar crackles, and linear basal opacities on the first postoperative day are the classic picture of atelectasis, caused by shallow breathing and reduced lung volumes after upper abdominal surgery. Pneumonia typically appears later with focal consolidation, and pulmonary embolism, edema, and aspiration have different timing and findings."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 35-year-old man with epilepsy has taken phenytoin for years with a stable therapeutic level. His physician raises the daily dose by a small amount, and within two weeks he develops nystagmus, ataxia, and slurred speech. The serum level is now more than double the previous value. What best explains this disproportionate rise?", "options": {"A": "Increased intestinal absorption from a change in gastric pH after the dose adjustment", "B": "Saturation of hepatic metabolism so elimination shifts from first-order toward zero-order kinetics", "C": "Displacement from albumin by a coadministered drug raising only the free fraction of phenytoin", "D": "Reduced renal tubular secretion causing accumulation of unchanged drug in the plasma compartment", "E": "Induction of cytochrome P450 enzymes accelerating clearance of the parent drug over several weeks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Saturation of hepatic metabolism so elimination shifts from first-order toward zero-order kinetics): Phenytoin metabolism by hepatic enzymes saturates within the therapeutic range, so small dose increases cause large, nonlinear rises in concentration. Toxicity here shows as nystagmus, ataxia, and dysarthria. Enzyme induction would lower the level, and the drug is not meaningfully cleared by the kidney."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 72-year-old woman has knee osteoarthritis pain rated 6 of 10 that limits walking. She has chronic kidney disease stage 3, takes warfarin, and was hospitalized last year for a bleeding duodenal ulcer. She lives alone and enjoys gardening. Which analgesic is the best initial oral choice?", "options": {"A": "Acetaminophen at limited daily doses, avoiding prostaglandin inhibition in the stomach and kidneys", "B": "Ibuprofen taken with food, since meals protect the gastric mucosa from prostaglandin inhibition", "C": "Celecoxib, because selective cyclooxygenase-2 inhibition spares renal prostaglandins and gastric mucosa", "D": "Tramadol, because weak opioid activity avoids gastrointestinal and renal toxicity in older adults", "E": "Naproxen with omeprazole, since acid suppression offsets the bleeding tendency on warfarin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acetaminophen at limited daily doses, avoiding prostaglandin inhibition in the stomach and kidneys): Acetaminophen is the preferred first oral agent for osteoarthritis when NSAIDs are unsafe, and this woman's kidney disease, warfarin, and prior ulcer bleed all argue against an NSAID. Celecoxib spares gastric mucosa somewhat but still impairs renal prostaglandins. Taking an NSAID with food or acid suppression only partly lowers bleeding risk, and tramadol adds fall and serotonergic risks."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 13-year-old boy with obesity has had several weeks of left knee pain and a limp. Examination shows the left hip externally rotates as it is flexed, with limited internal rotation, and he is afebrile with normal inflammatory markers. The radiograph shown is of the pelvis. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Slipped capital femoral epiphysis, posteroinferior displacement of the femoral head through the growth plate", "B": "Femoral neck stress fracture, repetitive loading in an athlete with a cortical lucent line", "C": "Septic arthritis, bacterial seeding of the hip joint causing fever and severe pain", "D": "Transient synovitis, a self-limited inflammatory effusion of the hip following a viral illness", "E": "Legg-Calve-Perthes disease, avascular collapse of the femoral head in a child"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slipped capital femoral epiphysis, posteroinferior displacement of the femoral head through the growth plate): Referred knee pain, obligate external rotation on hip flexion and obesity at the age of 13 are classic for slipped capital femoral epiphysis, in which the epiphysis slips posteriorly and inferiorly off the neck through the weak physis. Perthes disease occurs in younger children, and normal inflammatory markers argue against septic arthritis.", "image": "question_images/q_226.png"}, {"id": 227, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has progressive exertional dyspnea and near-syncope. Examination shows a loud pulmonic second heart sound and a right ventricular heave. Right heart catheterization shows mean pulmonary artery pressure of 40 mm Hg, wedge pressure 8 mm Hg, and no vasoreactivity. She starts an oral vasodilator that prolongs nitric oxide signaling. Which drug is this?", "options": {"A": "Montelukast, a leukotriene receptor antagonist that relaxes bronchial and pulmonary vascular smooth muscle", "B": "Sildenafil, which inhibits phosphodiesterase type 5 and raises cyclic guanosine monophosphate in vascular smooth muscle", "C": "Bosentan, which blocks endothelin receptors on vascular smooth muscle and reduces vasoconstriction and proliferation", "D": "Treprostinil, a prostacyclin analog that raises cyclic adenosine monophosphate in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle", "E": "Nifedipine, a dihydropyridine channel blocker whose benefit is limited to vasoreactive disease on testing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sildenafil, which inhibits phosphodiesterase type 5 and raises cyclic guanosine monophosphate in vascular smooth muscle): Pulmonary arterial hypertension is defined by a high mean pressure with a normal wedge pressure. Sildenafil blocks degradation of cyclic guanosine monophosphate, amplifying nitric oxide mediated relaxation. Bosentan is also an oral therapy but acts on endothelin, and a calcium channel blocker is reserved for patients who respond to acute vasodilator testing."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 29-year-old nulligravid woman has 3 years of pelvic pain that worsens just before and during menses, deep dyspareunia, and 18 months of infertility. Pelvic examination shows a fixed retroverted uterus and tender uterosacral nodularity. Ultrasound shows a 4 cm unilocular left ovarian cyst with homogeneous ground-glass echoes. Which finding is expected on pathologic examination of the cyst?", "options": {"A": "Endometrial glands and stroma with hemosiderin-laden macrophages lining a cavity filled with old blood", "B": "Luteinized granulosa and theca cells lining a hemorrhagic cyst that collapses with a scalloped wall", "C": "Ciliated tubal type epithelium lining a thin walled serous cyst with papillary projections inside", "D": "Single layer of tall columnar mucinous cells lining a multiloculated cyst filled with sticky fluid", "E": "Mature skin, hair follicles, and sebaceous glands lining a cyst containing sebum and hair"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Endometrial glands and stroma with hemosiderin-laden macrophages lining a cavity filled with old blood): Cyclic pelvic pain, dyspareunia, infertility, uterosacral nodularity, and a ground-glass unilocular ovarian cyst describe an endometrioma. Histology shows endometrial glands and stroma with hemosiderin-laden macrophages and old blood. A corpus luteum cyst can hemorrhage but is functional and does not explain the chronic pain and infertility."}, {"id": 229, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 22-year-old man with type 1 diabetes stops taking insulin for 3 days and presents with nausea, abdominal pain, and a fruity odor on his breath. Serum beta-hydroxybutyrate is markedly elevated. Which hepatic biochemical change most directly increases ketone body synthesis in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Increased pyruvate dehydrogenase activity floods the citric acid cycle with acetyl CoA, saturating oxaloacetate in hepatocytes", "B": "Activation of acetyl CoA carboxylase raises malonyl CoA, driving fatty acid synthesis and ketone production", "C": "Falling malonyl CoA levels relieve inhibition of carnitine acyltransferase I, increasing fatty acid entry into mitochondria", "D": "Rising glucose 6 phosphatase activity increases hepatic glucose output, which is converted directly into acetoacetate", "E": "Decreased hormone sensitive lipase activity in adipose tissue increases free fatty acid delivery to the liver"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Falling malonyl CoA levels relieve inhibition of carnitine acyltransferase I, increasing fatty acid entry into mitochondria): Without insulin, acetyl CoA carboxylase activity falls, malonyl CoA drops, and carnitine acyltransferase I is released from inhibition, so fatty acids enter mitochondria for beta oxidation. The resulting acetyl CoA exceeds citric acid cycle capacity and is converted to ketone bodies. Insulin deficiency raises hormone sensitive lipase activity, not lowers it."}, {"id": 230, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with a new pulmonary embolism is started on warfarin without a parenteral anticoagulant. On day four she develops painful, well demarcated skin lesions on her thighs that progress to hemorrhagic necrosis. Platelet count is normal. Which mechanism most likely explains this complication?", "options": {"A": "Direct cytotoxic injury to keratinocytes by a warfarin metabolite causing epidermal necrosis and bleeding", "B": "Antibodies against platelet factor 4 and heparin complexes triggering platelet activation and widespread thrombosis", "C": "Cholesterol crystal embolization from atheromatous plaques causing livedo and ischemic digital lesions", "D": "Early fall in protein C creating a transient hypercoagulable state with dermal microvascular thrombosis", "E": "Rapid decline of factor VII impairing hemostasis and causing hemorrhage into the dermal vessels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Early fall in protein C creating a transient hypercoagulable state with dermal microvascular thrombosis): Protein C has a short half-life, so its level falls before the vitamin K dependent procoagulant factors, briefly tipping the balance toward thrombosis in the skin microvasculature. This causes warfarin induced skin necrosis, usually on days three to five, especially when no bridging anticoagulant is given. Heparin induced thrombocytopenia does not fit normal platelets without heparin."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 22-year-old man of Mediterranean descent develops dark urine, back pain, and jaundice two days after eating fava beans. Hemoglobin falls to 8.4 g/dL. A peripheral smear shows bite cells and supravital staining shows Heinz bodies. Which biochemical deficiency most directly allows oxidant injury to hemoglobin in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Reduced catalase activity from hereditary acatalasemia, limiting hydrogen peroxide breakdown in cells", "B": "Reduced 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate synthesis from the Rapoport Luebering shunt, limiting oxygen release to tissues", "C": "Reduced ATP generation from pyruvate kinase failure, limiting membrane sodium potassium pump function", "D": "Reduced NADPH generation from the pentose phosphate pathway, limiting regeneration of reduced glutathione", "E": "Reduced NADH generation from glycolysis, limiting methemoglobin reductase activity in red cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced NADPH generation from the pentose phosphate pathway, limiting regeneration of reduced glutathione): Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is the first enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway and supplies NADPH, which regenerates reduced glutathione to neutralize hydrogen peroxide. Without it, oxidants denature hemoglobin into Heinz bodies, and splenic macrophages remove them as bite cells. Pyruvate kinase deficiency impairs ATP, not oxidant defense."}, {"id": 232, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 30-year-old man with HIV infection plans an international trip and has no documentation of measles immunity. He takes antiretroviral therapy, has no opportunistic infections, and has a CD4 count of 150 cells per microliter that is rising. He asks for vaccination before departure in three weeks. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Give a half dose of the live vaccine today and complete the series after travel", "B": "Give immune globulin before travel and omit active vaccination since HIV prevents durable immunity", "C": "Defer the live combined vaccine until CD4 count recovers above 200 cells per microliter", "D": "Give the live combined vaccine today since CD4 counts above 100 are acceptable", "E": "Check measles IgG first and give the vaccine today if the titer is low"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer the live combined vaccine until CD4 count recovers above 200 cells per microliter): Live measles-containing vaccine is contraindicated in adults with HIV and a CD4 count below 200 because vaccine virus can disseminate. With antiretroviral therapy his count is rising, so vaccination should wait until it exceeds that threshold. A low titer does not change the contraindication, and fractional doses are not recognized."}, {"id": 233, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 14-month-old girl in a pediatric clinic had three days of fever to 104 F with irritability but no cough, diarrhea or focal findings. The fever stopped abruptly this morning, and within hours a blanching pink macular eruption appeared on her trunk and neck. She is now playful and feeding well. Which agent most likely caused this illness?", "options": {"A": "Rubella virus infection with tender postauricular nodes and a rash spreading from face to feet", "B": "Human herpesvirus 6 infection with a blanching macular rash appearing as fever breaks in a toddler", "C": "Measles virus infection with cough, coryza, conjunctivitis and a rash beginning at the hairline", "D": "Coxsackievirus infection with fever followed by oral ulcers and vesicles on the palms and soles", "E": "Parvovirus B19 infection with bright facial erythema and a lacy reticular rash on the limbs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Human herpesvirus 6 infection with a blanching macular rash appearing as fever breaks in a toddler): Roseola infantum from human herpesvirus 6 gives several days of high fever in an otherwise well toddler, then a rash as the fever breaks. Parvovirus B19 produces a slapped-cheek pattern without a preceding high fever, and measles brings a prominent respiratory prodrome that this child lacked."}, {"id": 234, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 26-year-old woman at 10 weeks gestation has severe nausea with vomiting and mild palpitations. Thyroid examination shows no goiter or eye findings. Thyroid stimulating hormone is 0.08 mIU/L and free thyroxine is mildly elevated. Her symptoms began only after conception. Which mechanism best explains these laboratory findings?", "options": {"A": "Excess iodine from prenatal vitamins causing destructive thyroiditis with release of stored thyroid hormone", "B": "Stimulating autoantibodies against the thyroid stimulating hormone receptor cause autonomous thyroid hormone excess and goiter", "C": "Autonomous hyperfunctioning thyroid nodule producing thyroxine independent of pituitary thyroid stimulating hormone signals", "D": "High placental gonadotropin concentrations weakly activate the thyrotropin receptor because of shared structural homology", "E": "Increased estrogen induced thyroxine binding globulin raising free thyroxine and suppressing thyroid stimulating hormone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (High placental gonadotropin concentrations weakly activate the thyrotropin receptor because of shared structural homology): Human chorionic gonadotropin peaks at roughly 8 to 12 weeks and shares an alpha subunit and similar beta structure with thyrotropin, so it weakly stimulates the thyrotropin receptor and transiently suppresses thyroid stimulating hormone. This gestational transient thyrotoxicosis often accompanies hyperemesis and resolves as hCG falls. Graves disease would typically show a goiter, eye findings and persistence. Estrogen raises binding globulin and total, not free, thyroxine."}, {"id": 235, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 20-year-old woman with normal body weight reports eating large amounts of food in two hours, followed by self-induced vomiting several times weekly. Examination shows enlarged parotid glands and scarring over the knuckles. She feels ashamed and hides the behavior from her roommates. Which laboratory pattern is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Hypokalemic non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with hypochloremia from chronic laxative abuse and diarrhea", "B": "Hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with elevated serum amylase from salivary gland stimulation", "C": "Hypophosphatemia with elevated transaminases from refeeding and starvation, typical of low body weight", "D": "Hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis with elevated lipase from pancreatic injury caused by repeated vomiting", "E": "Hyponatremia with low serum osmolality from excessive water intake, typical of restricting anorexia nervosa"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with elevated serum amylase from salivary gland stimulation): Repeated vomiting loses hydrogen, chloride and potassium, producing hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis, and parotid enlargement raises salivary amylase. Laxative abuse causes a non-anion-gap acidosis, and the starvation findings belong to low weight anorexia, not a normal weight patient with binge and purge behavior."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 74-year-old man has eight months of slow shuffling gait with feet that seem stuck to the floor, urinary urgency with occasional incontinence, and slowed thinking. CT shows enlarged lateral ventricles without prominent sulcal widening. Gait improves markedly after removal of 40 mL of cerebrospinal fluid by lumbar puncture. What is the underlying mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Overproduction of cerebrospinal fluid by a choroid plexus papilloma in the lateral ventricle", "B": "Impaired cerebrospinal fluid absorption at arachnoid granulations producing communicating hydrocephalus with normal pressure", "C": "Thrombosis of the superior sagittal sinus raising venous pressure and intracranial pressure over weeks", "D": "Ventricular enlargement secondary to diffuse cortical neuronal loss after a neurodegenerative process", "E": "Obstruction of the cerebral aqueduct blocking flow from the third to the fourth ventricle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Impaired cerebrospinal fluid absorption at arachnoid granulations producing communicating hydrocephalus with normal pressure): The triad of gait disturbance, urinary urgency, and cognitive slowing with ventriculomegaly that improves after lumbar puncture indicates normal pressure hydrocephalus. The proposed mechanism is reduced absorption of cerebrospinal fluid over the convexities. In ex vacuo enlargement sulci widen, and drainage would not improve gait."}, {"id": 237, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A new blood test detects a certain cancer two years earlier than symptoms would appear, but no treatment has improved outcomes. Screened patients show a five-year survival of 80 percent compared with 60 percent in unscreened patients. Which bias most likely explains the apparent survival benefit?", "options": {"A": "Confounding by age, because younger patients are screened more often and survive longer", "B": "Selection bias, because screened participants differ systematically from unscreened ones in baseline health", "C": "Length-time bias, because slowly progressive tumors are preferentially detected by periodic screening", "D": "Recall bias, because screened patients remember earlier symptoms better than unscreened patients", "E": "Lead-time bias, because diagnosis moves earlier without delaying the actual time of death"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Lead-time bias, because diagnosis moves earlier without delaying the actual time of death): When screening finds disease earlier but does not change the date of death, survival measured from diagnosis looks longer even though patients live no longer. That is lead-time bias. Length-time bias would instead arise from slow-growing tumors being overrepresented among screen-detected cases."}, {"id": 238, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 72-year-old man presents with suprapubic discomfort and decreased urine output over 2 days. He reports weak stream and nocturia for years. Serum creatinine is 3.1 mg/dL, up from 1.1 last year. Renal ultrasonography shows bilateral hydronephrosis. Bladder scan shows 650 mL of urine. Which step is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Begin urgent hemodialysis for kidney failure because the creatinine has tripled within the past year", "B": "Administer a rapid intravenous crystalloid bolus to correct presumed prerenal volume depletion and restore perfusion", "C": "Arrange percutaneous renal biopsy to distinguish acute tubular necrosis from interstitial nephritis before treatment", "D": "Place a urethral catheter to relieve the outlet obstruction and monitor closely for postobstructive diuresis", "E": "Start intravenous furosemide to convert oliguria to nonoliguric flow and flush the obstructed collecting system"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Place a urethral catheter to relieve the outlet obstruction and monitor closely for postobstructive diuresis): A large residual bladder volume with bilateral hydronephrosis in a man with prostatic symptoms points to a postrenal cause, and decompression with a bladder catheter usually restores function. Urine output may rise sharply afterward, so fluids and electrolytes need monitoring. Fluids, diuretics, and biopsy do not address the blockage."}, {"id": 239, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 14-year-old boy has three months of thigh pain, night fever and a tender mass. Radiography shows a destructive lesion in the femoral diaphysis with layered periosteal reaction. Biopsy shows sheets of small round blue cells that are periodic acid-Schiff positive. Which cytogenetic finding is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Translocation of chromosomes 12 and 22 creating EWSR1 and ATF1 fusion in clear cell sarcoma", "B": "Translocation between chromosomes X and 18 creating SS18 and SSX fusion in synovial sarcoma", "C": "Loss of RB1 and TP53 function with complex karyotype typical of osteosarcoma", "D": "Translocation between chromosomes 11 and 22 creating an EWSR1 and FLI1 fusion transcription factor", "E": "Translocation between chromosomes 2 and 13 creating PAX3 and FOXO1 fusion in alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Translocation between chromosomes 11 and 22 creating an EWSR1 and FLI1 fusion transcription factor): Small round blue cells with PAS-positive glycogen, onion-skin periosteal reaction and a diaphyseal lesion in an adolescent with systemic symptoms indicate Ewing sarcoma, characterized by t(11;22) producing the EWSR1 and FLI1 fusion. Osteosarcoma more often arises in the metaphysis and carries RB1 and TP53 loss, which is the tempting alternative."}, {"id": 240, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 24-year-old man presents with a single painless, firm ulcer on the shaft of the penis that appeared two weeks ago, with nontender bilateral inguinal lymphadenopathy. Darkfield microscopy of the ulcer exudate shows motile spirochetes. He has no drug allergies. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Oral acyclovir for ten days to suppress an active genital herpes simplex outbreak in young adults", "B": "Ceftriaxone intramuscularly plus oral azithromycin, the regimen for gonococcal and chlamydial urethritis in young men", "C": "A single intramuscular dose of benzathine penicillin G, given after confirming he has no allergy", "D": "Oral doxycycline for twenty-one days, the regimen for lymphogranuloma venereum with tender inguinal buboes", "E": "A single oral azithromycin dose, the regimen for chancroid with painful ragged genital ulcers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A single intramuscular dose of benzathine penicillin G, given after confirming he has no allergy): A painless indurated ulcer with motile spirochetes on darkfield is primary syphilis, which is treated with a single intramuscular dose of benzathine penicillin G in patients without allergy. Herpes and chancroid cause painful ulcers, and the gonorrhea regimen does not cover Treponema pallidum."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 12-year-old boy with obesity has had 6 weeks of left knee pain and a limp. Knee examination is normal, but the left thigh rotates outward when the hip is flexed. Temperature and inflammatory markers are normal. Which diagnosis best explains the radiograph shown?", "options": {"A": "Transient synovitis of the hip following a viral illness with joint effusion", "B": "Posterior and inferior displacement of the femoral epiphysis through the physeal hypertrophic zone", "C": "Septic arthritis of the hip from hematogenous seeding with purulent joint fluid", "D": "Avascular necrosis of the femoral epiphysis with subchondral collapse, crescent sign and increased bone density", "E": "Osgood Schlatter disease from repetitive quadriceps traction at the tibial tubercle apophysis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Posterior and inferior displacement of the femoral epiphysis through the physeal hypertrophic zone): Obesity, adolescent age, knee pain, and obligate external rotation on hip flexion indicate slipped capital femoral epiphysis, where the epiphysis displaces posteriorly and inferiorly through the hypertrophic zone of the physis. Knee pain is referred through the obturator nerve, so hip examination is essential. Normal markers argue against infection.", "image": "question_images/q_241.png"}, {"id": 242, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 70-year-old man with COPD presents with three days of increased dyspnea, sputum volume, and sputum purulence. He is alert and cooperative. Arterial blood gas on supplemental oxygen shows pH 7.28, PaCO2 62 mm Hg, and PaO2 58 mm Hg. He reports a penicillin rash years ago. Which intervention is the best next step in respiratory support?", "options": {"A": "High-flow oxygen by non-rebreather mask to normalize saturation to 100 percent before further assessment", "B": "Oral corticosteroids and antibiotics alone, with nasal cannula oxygen and a repeat gas in hours", "C": "Immediate endotracheal intubation with mechanical ventilation, because pH below 7.30 mandates invasive support", "D": "Intravenous aminophylline infusion as the main therapy to improve ventilation and reduce carbon dioxide", "E": "Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation by tight face mask, with controlled low-flow oxygen titrated to saturation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation by tight face mask, with controlled low-flow oxygen titrated to saturation): Acute hypercapnic respiratory acidosis with a pH below 7.35 in an alert patient with COPD is an indication for noninvasive positive pressure ventilation, which lowers intubation rates and mortality. Oxygen should be titrated to a saturation of about 88 to 92 percent to avoid worsening carbon dioxide retention. Intubation is reserved for those who fail it or cannot protect the airway."}, {"id": 243, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 31-year-old woman with obesity at 27 weeks gestation has a positive glucose challenge test and a diagnostic oral glucose tolerance test confirming gestational diabetes. She asks why this developed only now. Which placental product most directly contributes to the progressive insulin resistance of late pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Placental relaxin, which loosens the pubic symphysis and reduces glucose transporter expression in muscle", "B": "Placental corticotropin releasing hormone, which raises cortisol and directly inhibits pancreatic beta cell secretion", "C": "Placental progesterone, which relaxes smooth muscle and lowers insulin receptor number in skeletal muscle", "D": "Placental estriol, which stimulates hepatic gluconeogenesis and blocks peripheral glucose uptake in adipose tissue", "E": "Human chorionic somatomammotropin, which antagonizes insulin and promotes lipolysis to spare glucose for the fetus"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Human chorionic somatomammotropin, which antagonizes insulin and promotes lipolysis to spare glucose for the fetus): Human placental lactogen, also called chorionic somatomammotropin, rises in proportion to placental mass and is a major diabetogenic placental hormone, antagonizing insulin in maternal tissues so more glucose reaches the fetus. Progesterone, cortisol and other hormones contribute modestly. Relaxin acts mainly on ligaments and does not drive glucose intolerance."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A test with sensitivity 95 percent and specificity 95 percent is used to screen a population in which disease prevalence is 1 percent, and the same test is later used in a referral clinic where prevalence is 40 percent. How will the positive predictive value differ between the two settings?", "options": {"A": "Lower in the screening population because most positive results there come from false positives", "B": "Lower in the referral clinic because the larger diseased group dilutes true positive results", "C": "Identical in both settings because predictive value depends only on sensitivity and specificity values", "D": "Higher in the screening population because specificity rises when prevalence of disease falls", "E": "Higher in the screening population because fewer diseased persons produce fewer false negative results"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower in the screening population because most positive results there come from false positives): Predictive values depend on prevalence. At 1 percent prevalence with 95 percent specificity, false positives from the large healthy group outnumber true positives, giving a positive predictive value near 16 percent, whereas at 40 percent prevalence it exceeds 90 percent. Sensitivity and specificity stay fixed, but they do not alone determine predictive value."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 36-year-old man with a BMI of 37 kg/m2 lost 10 percent of his weight with a calorie restricted diet but reports constant hunger and has regained half of it. Hormone levels are measured after the weight loss. Which pattern of appetite hormones is expected compared with before the diet?", "options": {"A": "Lower leptin from reduced fat mass and higher ghrelin from the stomach, both increasing hunger", "B": "Higher leptin from adipocytes and higher ghrelin from the stomach, with unchanged hunger signals", "C": "Lower leptin from reduced fat mass and lower ghrelin from the stomach, with unchanged hunger", "D": "Higher leptin from adipocytes and lower ghrelin from the stomach, both lowering hunger signals", "E": "Higher peptide YY and cholecystokinin from the distal intestine, both increasing hunger between meals"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower leptin from reduced fat mass and higher ghrelin from the stomach, both increasing hunger): Weight loss reduces adipose mass, so circulating leptin falls, removing a satiety signal to the hypothalamus, while ghrelin rises, promoting hunger. This adaptive response, together with lower energy expenditure, promotes weight regain. Peptide YY and cholecystokinin are satiety signals that tend to fall or stay unchanged, not hunger signals."}, {"id": 246, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 23-year-old woman is treated for pelvic inflammatory disease after presenting with fever, lower abdominal pain, mucopurulent cervical discharge, and cervical motion tenderness. One week later she develops sharp right upper quadrant pain that worsens with inspiration. Liver enzymes are normal, Murphy sign is negative, and gallbladder ultrasound is unremarkable. Which diagnosis best explains the new pain?", "options": {"A": "Pyogenic hepatic abscess from portal bacteremia with fever, tender hepatomegaly, and elevated alkaline phosphatase", "B": "Acute cholecystitis from cystic duct obstruction by a stone with gallbladder wall thickening", "C": "Right subphrenic abscess from pelvic fluid collections tracking along the paracolic gutter after treatment", "D": "Perihepatitis with inflammation of the liver capsule from ascending pelvic infection, producing adhesions", "E": "Right lower lobe pneumonia with pleuritic pain referred to the upper abdomen and productive cough"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perihepatitis with inflammation of the liver capsule from ascending pelvic infection, producing adhesions): Pleuritic right upper quadrant pain after pelvic inflammatory disease with normal liver tests and a normal gallbladder is Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome, an infection of the liver capsule that leaves violin-string adhesions. A subphrenic abscess would usually bring ongoing fever and a visible collection. The negative Murphy sign argues against cholecystitis."}, {"id": 247, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 9-year-old boy with sickle cell disease has fever and five days of severe pain in the right tibia. Temperature is 39.1 C, and the area is warm and tender. Blood cultures are pending, and radiography shows only soft tissue swelling. Which organism is disproportionately associated with this complication in his underlying condition?", "options": {"A": "Salmonella species that translocate from the gut and seed poorly perfused infarcted bone", "B": "Pasteurella multocida, introduced by an animal bite and spreading to adjacent bone", "C": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa, typical after a nail puncture through the sole of a sneaker", "D": "Staphylococcus aureus, the most frequent pathogen of hematogenous bone infection in all children", "E": "Streptococcus pneumoniae, an encapsulated organism that causes overwhelming sepsis after functional asplenia in childhood"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Salmonella species that translocate from the gut and seed poorly perfused infarcted bone): Sickle cell disease causes bone infarcts and gut barrier changes, so Salmonella species are disproportionately recovered from osteomyelitis in these patients. Staphylococcus aureus remains the most common single cause overall and must still be covered empirically, which is why it is tempting here. Pseudomonas follows puncture wounds and Pasteurella follows animal bites."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 29-year-old woman at 30 weeks of gestation reports daily burning retrosternal pain after meals that worsens when she lies down. She has had no dysphagia, vomiting, or weight loss, and vital signs and examination are normal apart from the gravid uterus. Which physiologic change best explains her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Progesterone relaxing lower esophageal sphincter smooth muscle, lowering barrier pressure during gestation", "B": "Human placental lactogen impairing vagal tone and slowing esophageal body peristalsis", "C": "Placental gastrin release producing G-cell driven acid hypersecretion and delayed sphincter closure", "D": "Oxytocin contracting the gastric fundus to raise intragastric pressure above sphincter resistance", "E": "Estrogen stimulating parietal cells to raise gastric hydrochloric acid output during the third trimester"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progesterone relaxing lower esophageal sphincter smooth muscle, lowering barrier pressure during gestation): Rising progesterone relaxes smooth muscle, including the lower esophageal sphincter, and the enlarging uterus adds intra-abdominal pressure, so reflux is common in late pregnancy. Gastric acid secretion actually falls in pregnancy, so an acid hypersecretion mechanism does not explain the symptoms."}, {"id": 249, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 68-year-old woman fractured her wrist after falling from standing height. DXA shows a femoral neck T-score of minus 2.9, and secondary causes are excluded. She is started on a first-line oral agent that she must take fasting with a full glass of water while staying upright. Which mechanism of action explains the drug's benefit?", "options": {"A": "Activates the calcitonin receptor on osteoclasts, inhibiting their ruffled border activity and reducing resorption", "B": "Activates the parathyroid hormone receptor intermittently, stimulating osteoblast mediated bone formation more than osteoclast mediated resorption", "C": "Binds RANKL and prevents its interaction with RANK, reducing osteoclast formation, function, and survival in bone", "D": "Binds hydroxyapatite and induces osteoclast apoptosis by inhibiting farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway", "E": "Binds the estrogen receptor, acting as an agonist in bone and an antagonist in breast tissue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Binds hydroxyapatite and induces osteoclast apoptosis by inhibiting farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway): The fasting, upright, full glass of water instructions describe an oral nitrogen containing bisphosphonate such as alendronate. It concentrates in bone at sites of remodeling and inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, which triggers osteoclast apoptosis and reduces fracture risk. RANKL antibodies and teriparatide are injected, and raloxifene and calcitonin are not first-line choices."}, {"id": 250, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 24-year-old man arrives after a stab wound to the left chest. He is hypotensive and tachycardic with distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. Breath sounds are clear and equal bilaterally, and the trachea is midline. A bedside ultrasound machine is unavailable. Which intervention is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Rapid infusion of two units of whole blood to correct the estimated hemorrhage", "B": "Intravenous dobutamine to support failing contractility and raise cardiac output until cardiac imaging is obtained", "C": "Chest tube placement to drain blood collecting in the left pleural cavity", "D": "Needle decompression of the left pleural space to release trapped air under tension", "E": "Pericardial drainage to relieve impaired diastolic filling from fluid surrounding the heart"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Pericardial drainage to relieve impaired diastolic filling from fluid surrounding the heart): Hypotension, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds after penetrating chest trauma form Beck triad, indicating cardiac tamponade, an obstructive shock. Removing the pericardial fluid restores ventricular filling. Normal, equal breath sounds and a midline trachea argue against tension pneumothorax or hemothorax, and volume or inotropes do not relieve compression."}, {"id": 251, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 9-month-old boy is brought by his father for a swollen right thigh. The father says the infant rolled off a couch onto a carpeted floor earlier today. The infant cannot yet crawl. Radiography shows a spiral femoral shaft fracture. Growth is normal and there is no family history of fractures. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Splint the leg and arrange orthopedic follow-up, accepting the described fall as a plausible cause", "B": "Obtain vitamin D, calcium and alkaline phosphatase levels to look for rickets as the cause", "C": "Admit for observation and ask the father to describe the event again to resolve inconsistencies", "D": "Order a skeletal survey and report suspected abuse to child protective services today", "E": "Send collagen gene testing for osteogenesis imperfecta and defer any report until the results return"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Order a skeletal survey and report suspected abuse to child protective services today): A spiral long bone fracture in a nonambulatory infant with a mechanism that does not fit is a red flag for inflicted injury. Physicians must report reasonable suspicion, not proof, and a skeletal survey looks for other occult fractures. Metabolic or genetic workups can follow but must not delay protection of the child."}, {"id": 252, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 24-year-old woman arrives six hours after an intentional ingestion of about 25 grams of acetaminophen. Her serum level plots above the treatment line on the nomogram, and she is given intravenous N-acetylcysteine. Which mechanism best explains how this agent protects her liver from further injury?", "options": {"A": "Inhibiting cytochrome P450 2E1 so less reactive metabolite forms from the ingested drug", "B": "Competitively displacing the parent drug from hepatocyte receptors, as naloxone does for opioid binding sites", "C": "Restoring glutathione stores so the toxic reactive metabolite is conjugated and detoxified before binding hepatocyte proteins", "D": "Binding the unabsorbed drug within the gastrointestinal lumen and preventing further systemic absorption", "E": "Enhancing renal tubular secretion of the parent compound so less drug reaches hepatic enzymes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Restoring glutathione stores so the toxic reactive metabolite is conjugated and detoxified before binding hepatocyte proteins): At toxic doses the sulfation and glucuronidation pathways saturate and more drug is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI, which depletes glutathione. N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione so NAPQI is conjugated. Activated charcoal limits absorption but does not neutralize metabolite already formed."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 55-year-old woman reports gritty eyes and difficulty swallowing dry crackers for 2 years. Examination shows bilateral parotid enlargement and dental caries. Schirmer test is abnormal. She is concerned about long term complications of her condition. Which complication is she at greatest increased risk of developing compared with the general population?", "options": {"A": "Squamous cell carcinoma of the oral cavity from chronic mucosal irritation and dryness", "B": "Hodgkin lymphoma with Reed Sternberg cells infiltrating cervical lymph nodes and salivary tissue", "C": "B cell lymphoma, most often marginal zone type arising within the salivary glands", "D": "Interstitial pulmonary fibrosis progressing to restrictive lung disease and respiratory failure", "E": "Pleomorphic adenoma of the parotid gland from autoimmune destruction of acinar tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (B cell lymphoma, most often marginal zone type arising within the salivary glands): Sjogren syndrome carries a markedly increased risk of non-Hodgkin B cell lymphoma, particularly marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa associated tissue, driven by chronic B cell stimulation. Interstitial lung disease can occur but is not the greatest relative risk. Hodgkin lymphoma and oral squamous carcinoma are not the characteristic malignant associations."}, {"id": 254, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 71-year-old man has sudden painless passage of a large volume of maroon blood per rectum. He takes aspirin daily for coronary disease. Blood pressure is stable and he has no abdominal tenderness. Colonoscopy shows multiple sigmoid outpouchings, with active bleeding from the neck of one. Which mechanism most likely explains the bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Erosion of ectatic submucosal vessels in the cecum from chronic muscular contraction", "B": "Ischemic mucosal necrosis at the splenic flexure watershed after a hypotensive episode", "C": "Surface ulceration of a villous adenoma with friable epithelium and fragile vasculature", "D": "Rupture of dilated rectal submucosal veins draining into the portal system under high pressure", "E": "Rupture of a vasa recta artery stretched across the neck of a diverticulum"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rupture of a vasa recta artery stretched across the neck of a diverticulum): Diverticula form where the vasa recta penetrate the circular muscle, so the artery runs over the dome or neck and can rupture, causing painless brisk bleeding. Angiodysplasia is a cecal lesion without diverticula, and ischemic colitis and varices have distinct histories absent here."}, {"id": 255, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 33-year-old woman has gluteal pain while walking. A medical student explains that the sacrum rotates during gait about an axis running diagonally from one superior sacroiliac joint region to the opposite inferior pole. Which axis is being described, and which motion occurs about it?", "options": {"A": "Longitudinal axis through the coccyx, with sacral side-to-side translation as the pelvis shifts weight", "B": "Superior transverse axis, with respiratory sacral motion accompanying the cranial rhythmic impulse during inhalation", "C": "Oblique axis, with sacral torsion occurring as the lumbar spine rotates during ambulation", "D": "Inferior transverse axis, with iliosacral motion occurring as the ilium rotates posteriorly during sitting", "E": "Middle transverse axis, with postural flexion and extension of the sacrum as the trunk bends"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Oblique axis, with sacral torsion occurring as the lumbar spine rotates during ambulation): The sacrum twists about oblique axes during walking, producing torsion as the lumbar spine rotates. The superior transverse axis is associated with respiratory motion and the middle transverse axis with postural flexion and extension, so neither describes a diagonal gait axis."}, {"id": 256, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A girl born at 29 weeks gestation is now 6 weeks old, pale, and feeding poorly, with mild tachycardia. Her hemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL with a low reticulocyte count, normal MCV, and a normal bilirubin. Frequent blood sampling occurred in the neonatal unit. Which mechanism best explains her anemia?", "options": {"A": "Iron deficiency from depleted maternal stores producing microcytic hypochromic cells and a compensatory reticulocytosis", "B": "Vitamin B12 deficiency from poor intake causing megaloblastic changes with an elevated mean corpuscular volume", "C": "Blunted erythropoietin response as oxygen sensing shifts from liver to kidney, plus phlebotomy losses", "D": "Hemolysis from G6PD deficiency with oxidant exposure causing reticulocytosis and jaundice in the first weeks", "E": "Parvovirus B19 infection of erythroid progenitors causing transient aplastic crisis with marked reticulocytopenia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blunted erythropoietin response as oxygen sensing shifts from liver to kidney, plus phlebotomy losses): Preterm infants have an exaggerated physiologic nadir because erythropoietin production is low as the oxygen sensing site moves from the liver to the kidney, and repeated blood draws add to the loss. A normal MCV, low reticulocyte count, and normal bilirubin argue against iron deficiency, hemolysis, and B12 deficiency."}, {"id": 257, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 16-year-old football player has a closed both-bone forearm fracture treated with casting. Twelve hours later he has worsening pain that opioids do not relieve, a tense swollen forearm, and a palpable radial pulse with brisk capillary refill. Which finding is the earliest and most reliable sign of the evolving complication?", "options": {"A": "Loss of the radial pulse from arterial occlusion beneath the swollen forearm", "B": "Severe discomfort with passive stretch of the muscles in the affected compartment", "C": "Bruising and warmth over the fracture site from bleeding into soft tissue", "D": "Pallor and a cool hand from reduced distal blood flow in the limb", "E": "Flaccid paralysis of finger movement from established nerve and muscle ischemia after prolonged compartment pressure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Severe discomfort with passive stretch of the muscles in the affected compartment): Pain out of proportion to the injury, made worse by passive stretching of the involved muscles, is the earliest reliable clinical sign. Pulses and capillary refill usually stay normal because compartment pressure rises above venous and capillary pressure long before it exceeds arterial pressure. Pallor and paralysis are late findings that mean irreversible injury is under way."}, {"id": 258, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A male infant born at 29 weeks' gestation develops grunting, nasal flaring, and retractions within hours of birth. Chest radiograph shows diffuse ground-glass opacities with air bronchograms. Alveoli of different sizes coexist in his lungs. Which mechanism best explains why his smaller alveoli collapse preferentially into larger ones?", "options": {"A": "Absent surfactant leaves tension fixed, so tiny alveoli generate greater collapsing pressure per Laplace's law", "B": "Hyaline membrane deposition stiffens the larger alveoli, redirecting gas into compliant smaller units", "C": "Thickened alveolar walls reduce diffusion capacity and cause progressive loss of the smaller units", "D": "Poiseuille's law, since narrower terminal airways raise resistance and divert gas toward wider alveolar ducts", "E": "Interdependence among alveoli fails because the pores of Kohn are absent in preterm lungs"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Absent surfactant leaves tension fixed, so tiny alveoli generate greater collapsing pressure per Laplace's law): Collapsing pressure in a sphere equals twice the surface tension divided by the radius. Surfactant normally lowers tension more as alveoli shrink, which stabilizes them, but this preterm infant lacks it, so small alveoli empty into larger neighbors. Poiseuille's law describes airway resistance and does not explain this alveolar instability."}, {"id": 259, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 39-year-old man received a deceased donor kidney four months ago and takes tacrolimus, mycophenolate and prednisone. Creatinine has risen from 1.2 to 2.1 mg/dL over a week without fever or graft tenderness. Tacrolimus trough is in the target range. Biopsy shows interstitial lymphocytic infiltrate with tubulitis; C4d staining is negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Hyperacute rejection from preformed antibodies, causing immediate thrombosis of graft vessels within minutes to hours", "B": "Calcineurin inhibitor nephrotoxicity from afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction, causing isometric tubular vacuolization and arteriolar hyalinosis", "C": "Chronic allograft nephropathy with interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy and vascular intimal thickening over many months", "D": "Acute cellular rejection from recipient T cells reacting against donor MHC molecules on tubular epithelium", "E": "Acute antibody mediated rejection from donor specific antibodies with capillaritis, C4d deposition and glomerulitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute cellular rejection from recipient T cells reacting against donor MHC molecules on tubular epithelium): A rapid rise in creatinine months after transplant with a lymphocytic interstitial infiltrate and tubulitis, but negative C4d, defines acute cellular rejection driven by recipient T cells. A therapeutic tacrolimus level and the inflammatory infiltrate argue against drug toxicity, which shows vacuolization without infiltrate. Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours and chronic rejection shows fibrosis."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 31-year-old man has failed to conceive with his partner for two years. Semen analysis shows severe oligospermia. Serum testosterone and LH are normal, FSH is elevated, and inhibin B is low. Testicular biopsy is planned. Impaired function of which cell type best explains the hormone pattern?", "options": {"A": "Leydig cells synthesizing testosterone from cholesterol in response to luteinizing hormone stimulation in the interstitium", "B": "Spermatogonia undergoing mitotic divisions to renew the stem cell pool and produce primary spermatocytes", "C": "Epididymal principal cells absorbing testicular fluid and secreting factors needed for sperm maturation", "D": "Sertoli cells supporting germ cells and restraining pituitary gonadotropin secretion through a peptide hormone", "E": "Peritubular myoid cells generating the contractions that propel sperm through the seminiferous tubules"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sertoli cells supporting germ cells and restraining pituitary gonadotropin secretion through a peptide hormone): Sertoli cells nurture developing germ cells and secrete the peptide hormone that inhibits pituitary FSH. Damage to the seminiferous epithelium lowers that peptide, so FSH rises while Leydig cell testosterone and LH stay normal. Germ cell loss itself does not provide the feedback signal."}, {"id": 261, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 33-year-old man had gastroenteritis two weeks ago. For three days he has had ascending leg weakness and tingling in his fingers. Examination shows symmetric leg weakness, absent knee and ankle reflexes, and normal bladder function. Forced vital capacity has fallen from 3.9 to 2.4 liters since admission. What is the best next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Admit for serial respiratory monitoring and begin intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange", "B": "Discharge with outpatient neurology follow-up and nerve conduction studies next week", "C": "Obtain urgent spinal MRI and proceed to surgical decompression of the lumbar canal", "D": "Perform edrophonium testing to confirm neuromuscular junction dysfunction before further treatment", "E": "Start high-dose oral prednisone to suppress the autoimmune attack on peripheral nerve myelin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Admit for serial respiratory monitoring and begin intravenous immunoglobulin or plasma exchange): Ascending symmetric weakness with areflexia after a diarrheal illness, likely from Campylobacter molecular mimicry, indicates Guillain-Barre syndrome. A declining vital capacity signals risk of respiratory failure, so monitoring and immunotherapy are required. Steroids give no benefit, and cord compression would usually cause bladder dysfunction and a sensory level."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 45-year-old man with no transfusion history has fatigue, erectile dysfunction, new diabetes and pain in the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints. Ferritin is elevated and transferrin saturation exceeds 60 percent. A liver biopsy stained with Prussian blue is shown. Which mechanism most directly explains this disease?", "options": {"A": "Impaired hepatic copper excretion into bile from ATP7B mutation, causing toxic copper accumulation in hepatocytes", "B": "Excess hepcidin from chronic inflammation trapping iron within macrophages and limiting enterocyte absorption", "C": "Inappropriately low hepcidin from HFE dysfunction, increasing ferroportin mediated intestinal iron absorption and macrophage release", "D": "Misfolded alpha-1 antitrypsin retained within the endoplasmic reticulum, producing periodic acid Schiff positive hepatocyte globules", "E": "Repeated red cell transfusions delivering excess iron beyond the capacity of macrophage and transferrin binding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inappropriately low hepcidin from HFE dysfunction, increasing ferroportin mediated intestinal iron absorption and macrophage release): Blue granular hepatocyte iron on Prussian blue with high transferrin saturation fits hereditary hemochromatosis, where HFE dysfunction lowers hepcidin, so ferroportin stays active and the gut absorbs too much iron. High hepcidin in inflammation does the reverse and traps iron in macrophages. The history excludes transfusional overload.", "image": "question_images/q_262.png"}, {"id": 263, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 38-year-old man with HIV infection, off antiretroviral therapy, has had two weeks of worsening headache and low-grade fever without neck stiffness. CD4 count is 40 cells per microliter. Lumbar puncture opening pressure is 38 cm H2O, with 15 lymphocytes per microliter. A cerebrospinal fluid antigen test for an encapsulated yeast is positive. Which initial treatment is best?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous amphotericin B with oral flucytosine, followed by serial therapeutic lumbar punctures for raised pressure", "B": "Intravenous pyrimethamine with sulfadiazine for presumed Toxoplasma encephalitis with ring-enhancing brain lesions", "C": "Intravenous ceftriaxone, vancomycin, and ampicillin for empiric treatment of acute bacterial meningitis", "D": "Oral fluconazole alone at standard dose for outpatient treatment of fungal meningitis in this setting", "E": "Intravenous acyclovir for presumed herpes simplex encephalitis involving the medial temporal lobes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous amphotericin B with oral flucytosine, followed by serial therapeutic lumbar punctures for raised pressure): A CD4 count below 100, subacute headache, high opening pressure, and a positive cryptococcal antigen indicate cryptococcal meningitis. Induction is amphotericin B plus flucytosine, and elevated pressure is managed with repeated lumbar puncture. Fluconazole alone is used later for consolidation or maintenance, not for induction of severe disease."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 45-year-old woman reports bright red blood on toilet paper and in the bowl after hard stools, without pain. Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions that prolapse during straining and reduce spontaneously, located above the dentate line. Which anatomic feature best explains the absence of pain with this condition?", "options": {"A": "External sphincter relaxation during straining lowers tension on the submucosal vascular plexus", "B": "Visceral innervation of the mucosa above the pectinate line lacks somatic pain fibers", "C": "Blood supply from the superior rectal artery creates arteriovenous channels that blunt nerve stimulation", "D": "Portal venous drainage through the superior rectal vein relieves congestion and pain", "E": "Inferior rectal nerve branches supply the vascular cushions but carry only autonomic fibers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Visceral innervation of the mucosa above the pectinate line lacks somatic pain fibers): Internal hemorrhoids arise above the dentate line, where mucosa is supplied by visceral afferents that sense stretch but not sharp pain. External hemorrhoids and fissures lie below it and are supplied by the somatic inferior rectal nerve, which is why they hurt. Bleeding is bright red because it comes from arteriovenous cushions."}, {"id": 265, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 28-year-old hiker develops foul-smelling, greasy, floating stools, bloating, and flatulence two weeks after drinking untreated stream water. He reports low-grade malaise but no fever or blood in the stool. Stool antigen testing is positive. Which pathologic process best explains his steatorrhea?", "options": {"A": "Enterotoxin driven chloride secretion from crypt cells, overwhelming the colon's absorptive capacity", "B": "Trophozoites coating the duodenal brush border, blunting villi and reducing absorptive surface area", "C": "Bile acid deconjugation by bacterial overgrowth in the proximal small bowel, impairing micelle formation", "D": "Pancreatic enzyme insufficiency from ductal obstruction by parasites, reducing intraluminal fat digestion", "E": "Villous atrophy driven by gluten-triggered T cell injury with crypt hyperplasia in the duodenum"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Trophozoites coating the duodenal brush border, blunting villi and reducing absorptive surface area): Giardia trophozoites adhere to the duodenal and jejunal brush border without invading, causing villous blunting, disaccharidase loss, and fat malabsorption that produce greasy, foul stools. Bacterial overgrowth can also impair fat absorption, but the positive stool antigen after stream water exposure points to the protozoan. Celiac disease gives a similar biopsy but does not follow a two-week exposure history."}, {"id": 266, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension and diffuse atherosclerosis begins an ACE inhibitor. Ten days later her creatinine rises from 1.1 to 2.4 mg/dL, and a bruit is heard over both flanks. Potassium is 5.4 mEq/L. Which change in glomerular hemodynamics best explains the fall in filtration?", "options": {"A": "Afferent arteriolar constriction from loss of prostaglandin synthesis, lowering glomerular capillary pressure and renal plasma flow", "B": "Reduced filtration coefficient from podocyte foot process effacement, lowering capillary permeability to water and solutes", "C": "Increased hydrostatic pressure in Bowman space from tubular obstruction by crystals, reducing net filtration pressure", "D": "Increased oncotic pressure in the glomerular capillary from hemoconcentration, opposing filtration along the capillary length", "E": "Efferent arteriolar dilation that lowers glomerular capillary pressure when inflow through narrowed renal arteries drops"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Efferent arteriolar dilation that lowers glomerular capillary pressure when inflow through narrowed renal arteries drops): Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole and so maintains glomerular capillary pressure when renal perfusion is low. Blocking it in bilateral renal artery stenosis drops filtration and raises creatinine and potassium. Prostaglandin loss from NSAIDs produces a similar picture but acts on the afferent arteriole instead."}, {"id": 267, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 56-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and a prior ischemic stroke begins high-intensity atorvastatin. At follow-up six weeks later, her LDL cholesterol has fallen by 45 percent and triglycerides by 20 percent, and she reports mild muscle aches with a normal creatine kinase. Which hepatocyte change most directly explains the lower plasma LDL?", "options": {"A": "Blocked intestinal absorption through NPC1L1, reducing chylomicron remnant delivery of cholesterol to the liver", "B": "Reduced intracellular cholesterol synthesis triggers SREBP-mediated increase in surface LDL receptors that clear circulating particles", "C": "Inhibition of hepatic lipase, lowering conversion of VLDL to intermediate-density particles and downstream LDL formation", "D": "Enhanced PPAR-alpha transcription, increasing lipoprotein lipase expression and fatty acid oxidation in liver and muscle", "E": "Increased fecal bile acid loss, upregulating 7-alpha-hydroxylase and depleting the hepatic cholesterol pool"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced intracellular cholesterol synthesis triggers SREBP-mediated increase in surface LDL receptors that clear circulating particles): Atorvastatin inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, lowering hepatocyte cholesterol, which activates SREBP-2 and raises LDL receptor expression so more LDL is cleared from plasma. NPC1L1 blockade is ezetimibe, PPAR-alpha activation is fibrates, and increased bile acid loss is bile acid resins. Those mechanisms are real but do not account for a statin response."}, {"id": 268, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 52-year-old woman has a primary melanoma excised from her calf. Pathology reports a Breslow thickness of 2.4 mm, no ulceration, and a mitotic rate of 1 per square millimeter. Sentinel lymph node biopsy is negative. Using the current American Joint Committee on Cancer staging system, which primary tumor category applies?", "options": {"A": "T2a, because thickness exceeds 1.0 mm but not 2.0 mm and ulceration is absent", "B": "T1b, because thickness exceeds 0.8 mm but not 1.0 mm and ulceration is absent", "C": "T4a, because thickness exceeds 4.0 mm and ulceration is absent from the lesion", "D": "T3a, because thickness exceeds 2.0 mm but not 4.0 mm and ulceration is absent", "E": "T3b, because thickness exceeds 2.0 mm but not 4.0 mm and ulceration is present"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (T3a, because thickness exceeds 2.0 mm but not 4.0 mm and ulceration is absent): T category for melanoma depends on Breslow thickness: up to 1.0 mm is T1, over 1.0 to 2.0 mm is T2, over 2.0 to 4.0 mm is T3, and over 4.0 mm is T4. The letter a or b reflects absence or presence of ulceration. A thickness of 2.4 mm without ulceration is T3a, and the negative sentinel node does not alter T category."}, {"id": 269, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 15-year-old high school student has had 12 hours of vague discomfort around the umbilicus with nausea and loss of appetite. The pain has now shifted to the right lower quadrant and is sharper with movement. Which pathway best explains why the early pain was felt near the umbilicus?", "options": {"A": "Somatic afferents from the parietal peritoneum carried by the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves", "B": "Foregut visceral afferents traveling with sympathetic nerves and entering the cord near T6", "C": "Midgut visceral afferents traveling with sympathetic nerves and entering the cord near T10", "D": "Hindgut visceral afferents traveling with sympathetic nerves and entering the cord near L1", "E": "Parasympathetic afferents carried in the vagus nerve to the nucleus of the solitary tract"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Midgut visceral afferents traveling with sympathetic nerves and entering the cord near T10): The appendix is a midgut derivative, so early inflammation stimulates visceral afferents that accompany sympathetic fibers to the T10 segment, perceived as poorly localized pain at that dermatome around the navel. When inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, somatic nerves localize the pain to the right lower quadrant. Foregut and hindgut afferents enter at different levels and map to the epigastrium and suprapubic region."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 19-year-old student performs a sustained handgrip exercise during a physiology laboratory while forearm electromyography is recorded. Action potentials spread down the T tubules and trigger calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Which event directly allows myosin heads to bind actin and begin cross-bridge cycling in the sarcomere?", "options": {"A": "Calcium binding calmodulin, which activates myosin light chain kinase and phosphorylates the myosin head", "B": "Hydrolysis of ATP by the myosin head, which cocks it into the high energy position", "C": "Binding of fresh ATP to the myosin head, which releases it from the thin filament", "D": "Dihydropyridine receptor conformational change that mechanically opens the ryanodine receptor channel in the terminal cisterna", "E": "Calcium binding troponin C, which draws tropomyosin off the thin filament binding sites"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Calcium binding troponin C, which draws tropomyosin off the thin filament binding sites): In skeletal muscle, released calcium binds troponin C, which pulls tropomyosin off the binding sites on the thin filament so the myosin head can attach. Calmodulin and myosin light chain kinase operate in smooth muscle. ATP binding releases a bound head and ATP hydrolysis re-cocks it, so neither is the event that permits attachment."}, {"id": 271, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 38-year-old man with ulcerative colitis develops a rapidly enlarging, exquisitely painful ulcer on his left shin that began as a pustule after a minor bump. The ulcer has a violaceous, undermined border and a purulent base. Wound cultures are negative and biopsy shows a dense neutrophilic infiltrate. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Begin prolonged intravenous vancomycin for resistant Staphylococcus aureus soft tissue infection", "B": "Apply multilayer compression bandaging to treat the venous hypertension of a chronic leg ulcer", "C": "Start systemic corticosteroids to suppress the neutrophil driven inflammatory process within the ulcer", "D": "Start therapeutic anticoagulation for deep venous thrombosis of the calf causing skin necrosis", "E": "Perform wide surgical debridement of the necrotic edges to promote granulation and closure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start systemic corticosteroids to suppress the neutrophil driven inflammatory process within the ulcer): A painful ulcer with a violaceous undermined border, pathergy and negative cultures in a patient with inflammatory bowel disease is pyoderma gangrenosum, a sterile neutrophilic dermatosis. Immunosuppression is the treatment, whereas debridement provokes new lesions at the injury site."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 34-year-old man has well-demarcated erythematous plaques with thick silvery scale over both elbows and knees. Removing a scale reveals pinpoint bleeding. He reports occasional morning stiffness in his fingers. A skin biopsy would most likely show which combination of findings?", "options": {"A": "Orthokeratosis, wedge-shaped hypergranulosis, and a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermal-epidermal junction", "B": "Hyperkeratosis with follicular plugging, basal vacuolar change, and mucin deposits within the dermis", "C": "Parakeratosis, neutrophils in the stratum corneum, thinned granular layer, and elongated rete ridges", "D": "Spongiosis with intercellular edema, eosinophils, and a sparse perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate", "E": "Suprabasal acantholysis with a tombstone row of basal cells along the blister floor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Parakeratosis, neutrophils in the stratum corneum, thinned granular layer, and elongated rete ridges): Silvery scale on extensor surfaces with the Auspitz sign points to plaque psoriasis, whose biopsy shows parakeratosis, Munro microabscesses, a thin or absent granular layer and regular acanthosis with elongated rete ridges. Wedge hypergranulosis with a band-like infiltrate indicates lichen planus, and spongiosis suggests eczema."}, {"id": 273, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A randomized trial of a new antihypertensive agent in 400 adults reports a reduction in systolic blood pressure of 8 mm Hg compared with placebo, with p = 0.03. A medical student asks what the p value represents. Which is the best definition?", "options": {"A": "The probability of a difference at least this large if the true effect were zero", "B": "The probability that the reduction was caused by chance rather than by the agent", "C": "The probability that the observed 8 mm Hg reduction will be reproduced in a second trial", "D": "The probability that the new agent has no true effect on pressure given these results", "E": "The probability of failing to detect a real reduction in pressure when one truly exists"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The probability of a difference at least this large if the true effect were zero): A p value is the probability of observing a difference at least as large as the one found if the null hypothesis (no true effect) were correct. It is not the probability that the null is true or that chance caused the result, and it does not describe replication. Failing to detect a real effect is a type II error, a different quantity."}, {"id": 274, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 62-year-old woman has neck pain and has recently noticed brief dizziness and double vision when turning her head to the right. Vertebrobasilar insufficiency is suspected and she is awaiting vascular imaging. She asks for osteopathic care today. Which approach is most important for her physician to avoid until the evaluation is complete?", "options": {"A": "Myofascial release of the suboccipital tissues using sustained gentle traction and an indirect approach", "B": "Functional technique of the cervical segments using light palpation and guidance toward dynamic neutral", "C": "Counterstrain of posterior cervical tender points with passive positioning toward ease and a brief hold", "D": "Cervical thrust technique with rotation and extension, which stresses the vertebral arteries near the atlas", "E": "Light cervical muscle energy using minimal patient effort and short isometric holds at the barrier"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cervical thrust technique with rotation and extension, which stresses the vertebral arteries near the atlas): Symptoms with head rotation point to vertebrobasilar insufficiency, an absolute contraindication to cervical HVLA because rotation and extension stress the vertebral arteries. Gentle indirect and low force methods can be used while the imaging is pending."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 46-year-old man has fatigue, arthralgias of the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints, and new diabetes mellitus. Skin appears bronze. Serum ferritin is 1,900 ng/mL and transferrin saturation is 88 percent. HFE testing shows C282Y homozygosity, and hemoglobin is 15.2 g/dL. Which therapy is the best initial approach to reduce his iron burden?", "options": {"A": "Subcutaneous deferoxamine infusion, binding circulating and stored iron for excretion in urine and stool", "B": "Oral deferasirox, binding iron in the gut and bloodstream for biliary excretion in the stool", "C": "Early liver transplantation to replace the organ responsible for hepcidin production and iron regulation", "D": "Scheduled therapeutic phlebotomy, mobilizing stored iron to make new red cells until stores normalize", "E": "Strict dietary iron restriction and elimination of vitamin C, relying on reduced intestinal absorption alone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Scheduled therapeutic phlebotomy, mobilizing stored iron to make new red cells until stores normalize): Bronze skin, diabetes, arthropathy, high ferritin and transferrin saturation with HFE C282Y homozygosity indicate hereditary hemochromatosis. Because hemoglobin is normal, phlebotomy is the safest, cheapest and most effective way to deplete iron, as the marrow uses stored iron to rebuild red cells. Chelators are reserved for patients who cannot tolerate phlebotomy or have anemia."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A term newborn girl is noticeably jaundiced at 14 hours of age, is feeding well, and has no dysmorphic features. Her mother is blood type O positive and the infant is type A positive. The laboratory panel shown was obtained. Which diagnosis best explains the jaundice?", "options": {"A": "Crigler Najjar syndrome type I with absent UDP glucuronosyltransferase causing severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "B": "Physiologic jaundice from immature hepatic conjugation, which typically appears after the first day of life", "C": "ABO incompatibility with maternal anti-A antibodies causing early extravascular hemolysis of infant red cells", "D": "Breast milk jaundice from beta glucuronidase in milk increasing intestinal reabsorption of unconjugated bilirubin", "E": "Biliary atresia with obstructed extrahepatic ducts causing conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, pale stools, and dark urine"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (ABO incompatibility with maternal anti-A antibodies causing early extravascular hemolysis of infant red cells): Jaundice in the first 24 hours is pathologic, and the positive direct antiglobulin test, reticulocytosis, low hemoglobin, and spherocytes with an O mother and an A infant show immune hemolysis from ABO incompatibility. The unconjugated pattern also excludes biliary atresia, and the enzyme deficiency would not cause hemolysis.", "image": "question_images/q_276.png"}, {"id": 277, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with alcohol related cirrhosis develops confusion and a flapping tremor two days after a gastrointestinal bleed. Serum ammonia is elevated. Her physician starts lactulose and titrates it to two or three soft stools daily. Which mechanism best explains how this drug lowers circulating ammonia?", "options": {"A": "Bacterial fermentation acidifies colonic contents, converting ammonia to poorly absorbed ammonium that is trapped and excreted", "B": "Activation of hepatic carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I, increasing urea synthesis from ammonia in surviving hepatocytes", "C": "Supply of branched chain amino acids competing with aromatic amino acids for blood brain barrier transport", "D": "Inhibition of intestinal glutaminase in enterocytes, decreasing ammonia released from glutamine metabolism in the small bowel", "E": "Direct inhibition of urease producing colonic bacteria, reducing the bacterial load that generates ammonia from urea"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bacterial fermentation acidifies colonic contents, converting ammonia to poorly absorbed ammonium that is trapped and excreted): Lactulose is a nonabsorbable disaccharide that colonic bacteria ferment to organic acids. The lower luminal pH converts diffusible ammonia to ammonium, which is trapped and eliminated, and the laxative effect speeds excretion. Rifaximin reduces urease producing bacteria, but that is a different drug mechanism."}, {"id": 278, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 40-year-old man with HIV infection and a CD4 count of 45 cells per microliter presents with fever, weight loss, and cough after cleaning out an abandoned chicken coop in the Ohio River valley. He has hepatosplenomegaly and pancytopenia. Bone marrow biopsy shows small oval yeast forms inside macrophages. Which organism is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Cryptococcus neoformans, an encapsulated yeast with narrow-based budding seen outside host cells in tissue", "B": "Pneumocystis jirovecii, an opportunistic fungus forming cysts that stain with silver in alveolar spaces", "C": "Histoplasma capsulatum, a dimorphic fungus acquired from soil enriched with bird or bat droppings", "D": "Coccidioides immitis, a dimorphic fungus forming spherules filled with endospores in tissue of desert dwellers", "E": "Blastomyces dermatitidis, a dimorphic fungus with broad-based budding found near waterways in the Great Lakes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Histoplasma capsulatum, a dimorphic fungus acquired from soil enriched with bird or bat droppings): Small yeast inside macrophages in a patient with advanced HIV who has exposure to bird droppings in the Ohio and Mississippi river valleys is disseminated histoplasmosis. Blastomyces shares the region but shows broad-based budding yeast, and Cryptococcus is extracellular with a capsule. Coccidioides forms spherules instead of yeast in tissue."}, {"id": 279, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 24-year-old woman using urinary ovulation predictor kits gets a positive result on day 13 of a regular 28-day cycle and asks what caused it. Ultrasonography the same day shows a 20 mm dominant follicle. Her endometrial lining is thick and proliferative. Which physiologic event directly triggers the hormone surge detected by the kit?", "options": {"A": "Falling inhibin B from granulosa cells removes negative feedback and releases a surge at midcycle", "B": "Sustained high estradiol from the mature follicle exerts positive feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary", "C": "Rising progesterone from the corpus luteum suppresses the pituitary and permits a rebound surge", "D": "A midcycle fall in follicle stimulating hormone lifts inhibition of the gonadotropin releasing hormone neurons", "E": "Decreasing dopamine from the arcuate nucleus lifts inhibition on pituitary lactotrophs and gonadotrophs at midcycle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sustained high estradiol from the mature follicle exerts positive feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary): Estradiol above a threshold concentration for about two days switches from negative to positive feedback, causing a GnRH driven luteinizing hormone surge that triggers ovulation roughly 36 hours later. Progesterone from a corpus luteum is not yet present and suppresses gonadotropins after ovulation. Inhibin B and follicle stimulating hormone changes modulate follicle selection but do not generate the surge."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 28-year-old man presents with more than 50 moles, several larger than 6 mm with irregular borders and variable brown pigmentation. His father died of melanoma at age 45. None of the lesions has changed recently. He works indoors as an accountant. Which is the most appropriate management plan?", "options": {"A": "Prophylactic excision of every atypical-appearing mole to eliminate the future risk of melanoma", "B": "Reassurance and routine skin checks only if new symptoms such as bleeding or itching develop", "C": "Periodic full-body skin examinations with biopsy of any lesion that changes or looks atypical", "D": "Cryotherapy of all lesions over 6 millimeters followed by daily topical imiquimod cream", "E": "Shave biopsy of the single largest mole with clinic follow-up scheduled in five years"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Periodic full-body skin examinations with biopsy of any lesion that changes or looks atypical): Numerous atypical nevi with a family history of melanoma place him at high risk, and the standard approach is lifelong surveillance with total-body examination and biopsy of changing or suspicious lesions. Prophylactic excision of all nevi is impractical and does not eliminate risk since most melanomas arise de novo. Destructive treatment would prevent histologic assessment."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 55-year-old man in the intensive care unit requires a rapidly achieved therapeutic plasma concentration of an intravenous drug. The drug has a volume of distribution of 0.5 L/kg, and the target plasma concentration is 10 mg/L. He weighs 80 kg, and the half-life is 12 hours. What loading dose should be given?", "options": {"A": "33 mg, calculated by dividing the total drug amount needed by the half-life in hours", "B": "40 mg, calculated from the volume of distribution per kilogram times body weight", "C": "400 mg, calculated from the total volume of distribution times the target concentration", "D": "800 mg, calculated from the target concentration times a doubled volume for tissue binding", "E": "100 mg, calculated by using only a plasma volume of about 10 liters"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (400 mg, calculated from the total volume of distribution times the target concentration): The loading dose equals the volume of distribution times the desired concentration. The volume is 0.5 L/kg times 80 kg, or 40 L, and multiplying by 10 mg/L gives 400 mg. Half-life determines the maintenance schedule and does not enter the loading dose."}, {"id": 282, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 68-year-old man with severe emphysema and chronic hypercapnia is brought in with dyspnea and a respiratory rate of 28/min. Arterial oxygen saturation is 78%. He is given high-flow oxygen and soon becomes drowsy; repeat blood gas shows PaCO2 rising from 62 to 84 mm Hg. Which mechanism contributes most to this worsening hypercapnia?", "options": {"A": "Renal bicarbonate retention raising arterial pH enough to suppress central chemoreceptor stimulation within minutes", "B": "Release of hypoxic vasoconstriction increasing dead space, plus Haldane displacement of CO2 from hemoglobin", "C": "Respiratory muscle fatigue from sudden increases in work of breathing caused by oxygen administration itself", "D": "Suppressed hypoxic drive at the carotid bodies as the dominant cause of reduced minute ventilation", "E": "Oxygen-induced bronchoconstriction raising airway resistance and trapping carbon dioxide in the distal airspaces"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Release of hypoxic vasoconstriction increasing dead space, plus Haldane displacement of CO2 from hemoglobin): Oxygen relieves hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction in poorly ventilated alveoli, so perfusion shifts toward units with high dead space, and oxygenated hemoglobin binds less CO2 (the Haldane effect). Loss of hypoxic drive contributes, but the change in minute ventilation is usually modest. Bicarbonate retention occurs over days, not minutes, and does not explain this rapid change."}, {"id": 283, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A physician treating a tender point on the anterior chest wall places the patient in a position that shortens the tender tissue until the tenderness decreases substantially. The patient is held there for about 90 seconds and then slowly returned to neutral. The physician applies no thrust or patient effort. Which technique is being used?", "options": {"A": "Articulatory technique, a direct method using repetitive springing through the restricted range of the joint", "B": "Counterstrain, an indirect passive method that moves tissue toward its position of ease and holds it", "C": "High velocity low amplitude, a direct method using a thrust delivered at the restrictive barrier", "D": "Facilitated oscillatory release, a method that sums fast vibratory pulses applied over a tender muscle", "E": "Muscle energy, a direct method using patient isometric contraction against a physician applied counterforce"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Counterstrain, an indirect passive method that moves tissue toward its position of ease and holds it): Positioning a tender point toward ease and holding about 90 seconds without force describes counterstrain. Muscle energy needs a patient contraction and HVLA needs a thrust, and both are direct methods."}, {"id": 284, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "An anesthesiologist performs a lumbar puncture at the L4 to L5 interspace in an adult. After the needle passes through the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, a sudden loss of resistance is felt as it traverses the firm elastic structure joining adjacent laminae just before the epidural space. Which structure is this?", "options": {"A": "Ligamentum flavum, composed of elastin and spanning neighboring vertebral arches within the canal", "B": "Posterior longitudinal ligament, a narrow band running along the back of the vertebral bodies", "C": "Nuchal ligament, the thickened midline extension of the supraspinous ligament in the neck", "D": "Ligamentum denticulatum, the lateral pial extension that anchors the spinal cord to dura", "E": "Anterior longitudinal ligament, a broad band covering the front of the vertebral bodies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ligamentum flavum, composed of elastin and spanning neighboring vertebral arches within the canal): The ligamentum flavum is rich in elastin and joins the laminae of adjacent vertebrae, so it gives the characteristic loss of resistance just before the epidural space. The longitudinal ligaments run along the vertebral bodies and are not crossed by a posterior midline needle."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 27-year-old woman has asthma with daytime symptoms most days and nocturnal awakenings weekly despite daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, good adherence, and correct inhaler technique. Triggers are controlled, and she has no GERD or rhinitis. Spirometry shows FEV1 72% of predicted with bronchodilator reversibility. Which change is the most appropriate next step in her step-up therapy?", "options": {"A": "Add scheduled oral theophylline as the first add-on, with serum drug levels monitored regularly", "B": "Replace the inhaled corticosteroid with a long-acting beta agonist used as sole maintenance therapy", "C": "Combine a long-acting beta agonist with the inhaled corticosteroid in a single maintenance inhaler", "D": "Add a scheduled short-acting beta agonist four times daily alongside the current corticosteroid dose", "E": "Start daily oral prednisone for long-term control, tapering only after nocturnal awakenings resolve"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Combine a long-acting beta agonist with the inhaled corticosteroid in a single maintenance inhaler): Persistent symptoms on a low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, after adherence, technique and triggers are checked, call for stepping up with an inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting beta agonist combination. A long-acting beta agonist must not be used without the corticosteroid because of increased risk of severe exacerbations. Theophylline and scheduled short-acting agents are not preferred add-ons."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with a severe asthma exacerbation is intubated and ventilated at a rate of 20 breaths per minute. Fifteen minutes later she becomes hypotensive with rising peak pressures. Breath sounds are diminished bilaterally, the trachea is midline, and the expiratory flow tracing does not return to zero before the next breath. Which action is most appropriate first?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a portable chest radiograph and wait for the result before changing settings", "B": "Increase the delivered tidal volume to overcome the rising airway resistance and hypoxemia", "C": "Place an emergent needle decompression in the second intercostal space at the midclavicular line", "D": "Disconnect the ventilator briefly and then lower the respiratory rate to lengthen expiratory time", "E": "Raise external PEEP to 15 cmH2O to splint the narrowed airways open"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Disconnect the ventilator briefly and then lower the respiratory rate to lengthen expiratory time): Failure of expiratory flow to reach zero indicates auto-PEEP from dynamic hyperinflation, which raises intrathoracic pressure, reduces venous return and causes hypotension. Briefly disconnecting the circuit lets trapped gas escape, and a lower rate with a longer expiratory time prevents recurrence. A midline trachea and the flow tracing argue against tension pneumothorax."}, {"id": 287, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 34-year-old office worker lies supine at the table edge and pulls his left knee to his chest. The right leg hangs with the knee flexed, and the right thigh rises off the table. When the examiner passively extends the right knee, the thigh drops flat. Which interpretation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Hamstring shortening, because the resting leg cannot lie flat and the pelvis tilts posteriorly during testing", "B": "Sacroiliac joint dysfunction, because asymmetric pelvic motion tilts the thigh upward during the maneuver", "C": "Iliopsoas contracture, because the thigh would stay raised whichever way the knee is positioned during testing", "D": "Tensor fasciae latae shortening, because the thigh would drift into abduction and external rotation while rising", "E": "Rectus femoris shortening, since this two-joint hip flexor is slackened once the knee is straightened"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rectus femoris shortening, since this two-joint hip flexor is slackened once the knee is straightened): In the modified Thomas test, a thigh that rises with the knee flexed but drops when the knee is extended implicates the rectus femoris, because knee extension slackens that two-joint muscle. A tight iliopsoas would keep the thigh raised whatever the knee position. Abduction during the test points to the tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band."}, {"id": 288, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 25-year-old woman has her second pulmonary embolism, which occurred 2 months after stopping oral contraceptives. Her father and an aunt each had an unprovoked deep venous thrombosis before age 40. Antithrombin, protein C, and protein S activities are normal. Which inherited defect is the most likely underlying cause?", "options": {"A": "Point mutation in factor V that makes it resistant to inactivation by activated protein C", "B": "Deficiency of antithrombin, which reduces inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa in the circulation", "C": "Deficiency of protein S, the cofactor that enhances activated protein C inactivation of factor Va", "D": "Point mutation in the prothrombin gene noncoding region that raises plasma prothrombin concentration", "E": "Cystathionine beta synthase deficiency causing hyperhomocysteinemia with endothelial injury and venous thrombosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Point mutation in factor V that makes it resistant to inactivation by activated protein C): Factor V Leiden is the most common inherited thrombophilia, and a family history of unprovoked venous clots with normal antithrombin, protein C, and protein S fits it. The altered factor V cannot be cleaved by activated protein C, so clotting continues unchecked. The prothrombin gene variant is less common, and the deficiencies were excluded by testing."}, {"id": 289, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 72-year-old woman has 3 weeks of new temporal headache, jaw pain while chewing, and shoulder and hip stiffness each morning. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 98 mm/hr. She reports transient blurring of vision in the left eye yesterday. Her blood pressure is normal. What is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Obtain temporal artery biopsy first and begin glucocorticoids only after pathology confirms the diagnosis", "B": "Start low dose prednisone for polymyalgia rheumatica and reassess symptoms at the next visit", "C": "Begin high dose glucocorticoids at once and pursue biopsy confirmation of the artery afterward", "D": "Refer for urgent ophthalmology examination and start topical corticosteroid drops for presumed uveitis", "E": "Order carotid duplex ultrasound and begin aspirin therapy for presumed transient ischemic attack"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin high dose glucocorticoids at once and pursue biopsy confirmation of the artery afterward): Jaw claudication, visual symptoms, and a very high sedimentation rate indicate giant cell arteritis, where amaurosis signals impending permanent blindness. High dose glucocorticoids should start immediately because biopsy remains positive for days after treatment begins. Low dose steroids are insufficient, and eye drops or antiplatelet therapy do not treat the arteritis."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 62-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with an inferior wall myocardial infarction and new complete heart block. Angiography shows a right-dominant circulation with occlusion of the mid right coronary artery. Ischemia of which structure best explains the conduction abnormality in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Sinoatrial node, perfused by the left circumflex artery in most individuals", "B": "Left posterior fascicle, perfused by the left circumflex near the obtuse marginal branch", "C": "Atrioventricular node, perfused by a branch arising where the posterior descending artery originates", "D": "Bundle of His distal portion, perfused chiefly by the left anterior descending septal perforators", "E": "Anterior papillary muscle of the left ventricle, perfused by the left anterior descending artery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Atrioventricular node, perfused by a branch arising where the posterior descending artery originates): In right-dominant circulation, the right coronary artery gives off the posterior descending artery and the AV nodal artery at the crux of the heart. Inferior infarction from right coronary occlusion therefore commonly produces AV block. Septal perforators from the left anterior descending mainly supply the bundle branches, so they do not explain an inferior infarct."}, {"id": 291, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 44-year-old woman has progressive left hearing loss from a cerebellopontine angle mass. On corneal testing, touching either cornea with cotton produces blinking of the right eye only, and the left eye stays open. She reports feeling the touch on both corneas. Which nerve is damaged?", "options": {"A": "Right facial nerve carrying the efferent limb to orbicularis oculi", "B": "Left oculomotor nerve carrying efferent fibers to the levator palpebrae superioris", "C": "Pontine reticular formation interneurons linking the trigeminal and facial nuclei bilaterally", "D": "Left trigeminal ophthalmic division carrying the afferent limb from the cornea", "E": "Left facial nerve carrying the efferent limb to orbicularis oculi"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Left facial nerve carrying the efferent limb to orbicularis oculi): The corneal reflex has an afferent limb in the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve and an efferent limb in the facial nerve to orbicularis oculi. Because sensation is felt on both sides and the right eye blinks to either stimulus, the afferent and central pathways work and only the left efferent limb fails. Oculomotor damage would cause ptosis rather than failure to close."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 40-year-old office worker has had neck pain for two weeks. Palpation shows a posteriorly prominent right C5 articular pillar, reduced left sidebending of the segment, point tenderness over the right pillar, and warm, boggy paraspinal muscle. Neurologic examination is normal. Which term best describes this clinical entity in osteopathic medicine?", "options": {"A": "Facet arthropathy, degenerative joint disease with morning stiffness and bony crepitus on motion testing", "B": "Myofascial trigger point, a taut band with referred pain pattern and a local twitch response", "C": "Fibromyalgia, chronic widespread pain with multiple symmetric tender points and nonrestorative sleep in adults", "D": "Cervical radiculopathy, nerve root compression causing dermatomal pain, sensory loss, and reflex change", "E": "Somatic dysfunction, impaired function of body framework elements and their associated neurovascular structures"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Somatic dysfunction, impaired function of body framework elements and their associated neurovascular structures): Asymmetry, restricted motion, tenderness, and tissue texture change at one segment are the diagnostic criteria for somatic dysfunction, here acute given the warm, boggy tissue. Radiculopathy would cause neurologic findings, and fibromyalgia is widespread and not segmental."}, {"id": 293, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 15-year-old boy has had four months of aching left thigh pain that is worse at night and wakes him from sleep. Ibuprofen relieves it within minutes. He has no fever or weight loss. Radiograph shows a small 8 mm lucent focus with a dense surrounding rim of cortical sclerosis in the femoral diaphysis. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Brodie abscess, a subacute pyogenic infection forming a sclerotic rimmed lucent cavity in metaphyseal bone", "B": "Osteosarcoma, a malignant bone-forming tumor with periosteal sunburst reaction in the distal femoral metaphysis", "C": "Osteoid osteoma, a benign osteoblastic tumor whose small nidus produces prostaglandins that drive nocturnal pain", "D": "Stress fracture of the femoral shaft, caused by repetitive loading with periosteal thickening and activity-related pain", "E": "Osteoblastoma, a larger osteoblastic lesion over 2 cm with pain poorly responsive to anti-inflammatory drugs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Osteoid osteoma, a benign osteoblastic tumor whose small nidus produces prostaglandins that drive nocturnal pain): Night pain relieved quickly by anti-inflammatory drugs with a small lucent nidus surrounded by reactive sclerosis is classic for osteoid osteoma. Osteoblastoma shares the histology but is larger than 2 cm and responds poorly to NSAIDs. A Brodie abscess can look similar but would be expected to cause infectious features and a metaphyseal location."}, {"id": 294, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 34-year-old male smoker has nightly attacks of severe stabbing pain behind the right eye lasting about forty-five minutes. During attacks, the right eye tears, the nostril runs, and the eyelid droops. He paces the room because lying still worsens the pain. Attacks have occurred nightly for three weeks. Which medication best prevents further attacks?", "options": {"A": "Topiramate, an anticonvulsant used for migraine prevention through sodium channel and glutamate effects", "B": "Propranolol, a nonselective beta blocker used for migraine prevention in patients with frequent attacks", "C": "Ibuprofen, a nonsteroidal drug taken nightly for relief of tension-type headache pain", "D": "Amitriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant used for tension headache and migraine prevention at bedtime", "E": "Verapamil, a calcium channel blocker used as first-line preventive therapy for this disorder"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Verapamil, a calcium channel blocker used as first-line preventive therapy for this disorder): Unilateral periorbital pain with ipsilateral tearing, rhinorrhea, ptosis, and restlessness in a circadian pattern is cluster headache. Verapamil is the preferred preventive, and oxygen or triptans treat acute attacks. Migraine preventives such as propranolol and topiramate are not first-line for this disorder."}, {"id": 295, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 27-year-old woman has several months of knee pain and swelling. Radiography shows an eccentric, lytic, expansile lesion with a soap-bubble appearance in the epiphysis of the distal femur, extending to the subchondral bone. Biopsy shows many multinucleated cells among sheets of mononuclear stromal cells. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Chondroblastoma, an epiphyseal cartilage tumor of adolescents with chicken-wire calcification and grooved nuclei", "B": "Giant cell tumor of bone, whose neoplastic stromal cells recruit osteoclast precursors via RANKL", "C": "Osteosarcoma, a malignant osteoid producing tumor with sunburst periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle", "D": "Aneurysmal bone cyst, a blood filled cystic lesion with fluid levels and stromal septa", "E": "Brown tumor of hyperparathyroidism, with osteoclast-rich lesions and hemosiderin in a cortical lytic defect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Giant cell tumor of bone, whose neoplastic stromal cells recruit osteoclast precursors via RANKL): An eccentric lytic epiphyseal lesion in a skeletally mature young adult with multinucleated cells among mononuclear stromal cells is a giant cell tumor, whose neoplastic stromal cells express RANKL. Chondroblastoma also involves the epiphysis but occurs in adolescents with open growth plates and shows chondroid matrix and grooved nuclei. Brown tumor needs high serum calcium and parathyroid hormone."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with osteoporosis and a known L2 compression fracture seen on recent imaging has urinary urgency and lumbar pain. Osteopathic examination finds tender, tight paraspinal muscles and restricted lumbar motion at L1 and L2 with a tender point on the anterior abdominal wall. She is anxious about forceful treatment. Which technique is the most appropriate choice?", "options": {"A": "Soft tissue technique with deep kneading and stretching of the paraspinal muscles at both levels", "B": "Counterstrain, passively holding the region in its position of ease for about ninety seconds", "C": "Muscle energy with maximal isometric contraction by the patient against firm counterforce at the barrier", "D": "Articulatory technique using repetitive springing and leverage to carry the lumbar segments through restriction", "E": "High velocity low amplitude thrust applied to the L2 segment at its restrictive barrier"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Counterstrain, passively holding the region in its position of ease for about ninety seconds): Counterstrain is an indirect, passive technique that moves the tissues toward ease and avoids loading the fractured vertebra. High velocity thrust is contraindicated at a fracture, and strong leverage, springing, or maximal contraction add risk in osteoporotic bone. Gentle indirect treatment also suits her anxiety about forceful methods."}, {"id": 297, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 52-year-old man with Graves disease is started on methimazole. He asks how the medication reduces hormone production in his thyroid gland. He has a normal complete blood count and normal liver tests at baseline. Which step in thyroid hormone synthesis is directly inhibited by this drug?", "options": {"A": "Deiodination of T4 to T3 by type 1 deiodinase in peripheral tissues", "B": "TSH receptor binding and cyclic AMP signaling on the follicular cell membrane", "C": "Proteolysis of thyroglobulin by lysosomal enzymes to liberate stored T3 and T4", "D": "Sodium-iodide symporter uptake of iodide across the basolateral membrane of follicular cells", "E": "Peroxidase-mediated iodide oxidation and iodination of tyrosyl residues on thyroglobulin within the colloid"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Peroxidase-mediated iodide oxidation and iodination of tyrosyl residues on thyroglobulin within the colloid): Thionamides inhibit thyroid peroxidase, which oxidizes iodide and attaches it to tyrosine residues, blocking organification and coupling. Methimazole does not block peripheral T4 to T3 conversion, unlike propylthiouracil. Iodide uptake itself is the target of perchlorate."}, {"id": 298, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old man has acute left knee pain and warmth without trauma or fever. Arthrocentesis yields cloudy fluid, and the synovial fluid analysis is shown. Radiographs show linear calcification within the menisci. He also reports fatigue, arthralgia of the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints, and bronze-tinged skin. Which additional test is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "HLA-B27 testing and sacroiliac imaging to evaluate for an axial spondyloarthropathy", "B": "Antinuclear antibody and anti-double-stranded DNA titers to evaluate for systemic lupus erythematosus", "C": "Serum uric acid and urine urate excretion to evaluate for purine overproduction", "D": "Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone levels to evaluate for primary hyperparathyroidism", "E": "Serum ferritin and transferrin saturation to evaluate for systemic iron overload disease"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Serum ferritin and transferrin saturation to evaluate for systemic iron overload disease): Rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent crystals with chondrocalcinosis indicate calcium pyrophosphate deposition, and a patient under 60 should be screened for an underlying cause. Bronze skin, fatigue and second and third metacarpophalangeal arthropathy point to hemochromatosis, so ferritin and transferrin saturation come first. Hyperparathyroidism also predisposes but lacks these distinguishing features.", "image": "question_images/q_298.png"}, {"id": 299, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 35-year-old woman with three weeks of fatigue and occasional chest discomfort has small, firm, tender tissue texture changes in the third and fourth intercostal spaces adjacent to the sternum. These findings are consistent with a Chapman reflex point. Which organ is associated with this anterior point?", "options": {"A": "Heart, with a corresponding posterior point near the transverse process of T2", "B": "Lung, with a corresponding posterior point near the transverse processes of T3 and T4", "C": "Liver and gallbladder, with a corresponding posterior point near the transverse processes of T5 and T6", "D": "Bronchi, with a corresponding posterior point near the transverse process of T2", "E": "Stomach, with a corresponding posterior point near the transverse processes of T5 and T6"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lung, with a corresponding posterior point near the transverse processes of T3 and T4): The anterior Chapman point for the lung lies in the third and fourth intercostal spaces near the sternum, with a posterior counterpart near the transverse processes of T3 and T4. The bronchial point sits a space higher, and the heart, stomach, and liver and gallbladder points correspond to other locations and levels."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 57-year-old man with short bowel syndrome has received home parenteral nutrition containing dextrose and amino acids, without lipid emulsion, for 10 weeks. He develops a dry, scaly dermatitis, sparse hair, delayed wound healing, and a platelet count of 82,000/mm3. Serum zinc is normal. Which deficiency most likely explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Biotin deficiency reducing carboxylase activity needed for fatty acid synthesis and amino acid catabolism", "B": "Vitamin A deficiency impairing epithelial differentiation and keratinization of skin and mucosal surfaces", "C": "Linoleic acid deficiency limiting arachidonic acid synthesis needed for membrane integrity and eicosanoid production", "D": "Niacin deficiency reducing NAD synthesis needed for oxidative metabolism in skin and gut epithelium", "E": "Zinc deficiency impairing metalloenzyme activity needed for epithelial repair and keratinocyte differentiation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Linoleic acid deficiency limiting arachidonic acid synthesis needed for membrane integrity and eicosanoid production): Essential fatty acid deficiency develops within weeks when lipid is omitted from parenteral nutrition, because humans cannot synthesize linoleic acid, the precursor of arachidonic acid. It causes scaly dermatitis, alopecia, poor healing, and thrombocytopenia. Biotin and niacin deficiencies produce different skin and neurologic syndromes, and zinc deficiency gives a periorificial rash with a low zinc level."}, {"id": 301, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has painful oral erosions for two months, followed by flaccid blisters that rupture easily on the chest and back. Gentle lateral pressure on normal-appearing skin causes the epidermis to slide off. A lesional biopsy, shown in the micrograph, reveals a split just above the basal layer. What does direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin most likely show?", "options": {"A": "Linear IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone beneath the epidermis", "B": "Granular IgA deposits clustered at the tips of the dermal papillae in uninvolved skin", "C": "Perivascular IgG and C3 complement deposits within small dermal vessel walls with leukocytoclasis", "D": "Netlike intercellular IgG and C3 deposition around keratinocytes throughout the lower epidermis", "E": "Granular IgG, IgM and C3 band along the dermoepidermal junction of sun exposed skin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Netlike intercellular IgG and C3 deposition around keratinocytes throughout the lower epidermis): Pemphigus vulgaris causes acantholysis just above the basal layer, and IgG against desmoglein 3 produces a netlike chicken-wire pattern around keratinocytes. Linear basement membrane staining belongs to pemphigoid, where the blister forms below the epidermis.", "image": "question_images/q_301.png"}, {"id": 302, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 29-year-old nurse had a needlestick injury three weeks ago and now has fatigue, mild jaundice and an ALT of 900 U/L. Serology shows HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, anti-HBc IgM positive and anti-HBc IgG negative. Which interpretation of these results is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Precore mutant chronic hepatitis B, in which e antigen is absent despite ongoing viral replication", "B": "Chronic hepatitis B with low viral replication, reflecting persistent infection and a failed immune response", "C": "Acute hepatitis C infection, since core antibody is detectable early after percutaneous blood exposure", "D": "Resolved hepatitis B infection with natural immunity, reflected by core antibody and absent surface antigen", "E": "Acute hepatitis B in the window period, after surface antigen clearance but before antibody appears"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Acute hepatitis B in the window period, after surface antigen clearance but before antibody appears): During the window period, surface antigen has cleared and surface antibody has not yet risen, so only IgM core antibody marks acute hepatitis B. Chronic infection would keep surface antigen positive beyond six months. Resolved infection would show surface antibody and IgG core antibody."}, {"id": 303, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 2-year-old boy has a painless scrotal swelling. Ultrasound shows a solid testicular mass. Serum alpha-fetoprotein is markedly elevated and beta-hCG is normal. Histology shows glomerulus-like structures with a central capillary surrounded by a space lined by tumor cells. Which tumor is most likely present in this child?", "options": {"A": "Leydig cell tumor with Reinke crystals causing androgen overproduction and early virilization in boys", "B": "Embryonal carcinoma with pleomorphic cells in glandular papillary sheets and frequent areas of hemorrhage", "C": "Seminoma composed of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm and a prominent lymphocytic infiltrate in stroma", "D": "Endodermal sinus tumor arising from primitive yolk sac elements in the developing testis tissue", "E": "Choriocarcinoma with syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblast producing very high levels of beta-hCG in blood"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Endodermal sinus tumor arising from primitive yolk sac elements in the developing testis tissue): Yolk sac tumor is the most common testicular tumor in young boys and produces alpha-fetoprotein. Its Schiller-Duval bodies resemble glomeruli. Seminoma occurs in adults and does not make alpha-fetoprotein, and choriocarcinoma would raise beta-hCG."}, {"id": 304, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with long-standing type 2 diabetes and gastroparesis has taken metoclopramide for two years. She now has lip smacking, tongue protrusion, and repetitive chewing movements that she cannot suppress. Which mechanism of the drug best explains this adverse effect?", "options": {"A": "Motilin receptor agonism producing excessive excitation of striatal cholinergic interneurons in the brain", "B": "Peripheral serotonin 5-HT3 receptor blockade causing disinhibited firing of cortical motor neurons", "C": "Central dopamine D2 receptor blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway with later receptor upregulation", "D": "Serotonin 5-HT4 receptor agonism causing diffuse serotonergic overstimulation of the motor cortex", "E": "Muscarinic receptor antagonism in the basal ganglia causing relative dopaminergic excess and chorea"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Central dopamine D2 receptor blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway with later receptor upregulation): Metoclopramide blocks D2 receptors and crosses the blood brain barrier, so prolonged use can cause tardive dyskinesia through nigrostriatal D2 blockade and upregulation. Its prokinetic effect comes partly from 5-HT4 agonism and cholinergic enhancement, which do not explain these movements. Long-term use in the elderly and in diabetic patients needs close monitoring."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 31-year-old woman reports burning her hands on a stove without feeling pain. Examination shows loss of pain and temperature sensation over both shoulders and arms in a cape distribution, with preserved light touch, vibration and strength. Which structure is interrupted?", "options": {"A": "Decussating second-order spinothalamic axons in the anterior white commissure at cervical levels", "B": "Dorsal root ganglion neurons carrying pain input into the tract of Lissauer", "C": "Ascending fibers of the fasciculus cuneatus in the dorsal columns of the cervical cord", "D": "Descending lateral corticospinal tract fibers in the lateral funiculus of the cervical cord", "E": "Ascending spinothalamic tract fibers in the contralateral anterolateral funiculus of the thoracic cord"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decussating second-order spinothalamic axons in the anterior white commissure at cervical levels): A syrinx expands around the central canal and interrupts second-order pain and temperature axons as they cross in the anterior white commissure, producing a bilateral cape-like dissociated sensory loss. Dorsal columns carry touch, vibration and position sense and lie posteriorly, so they are spared. Spinothalamic tract lesions after crossing cause contralateral loss below the level instead."}, {"id": 306, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A community clinic launches a program to measure blood pressure in all adults attending a church health fair, with the goal of finding asymptomatic hypertension before it damages the kidneys or heart. Which level of prevention does this activity represent?", "options": {"A": "Primary prevention, which averts the onset of disease in people without any current pathology", "B": "Quaternary prevention, which protects patients from unnecessary medical interventions and overdiagnosis by clinicians", "C": "Tertiary prevention, which reduces disability and complications in patients with established clinical disease", "D": "Secondary prevention, which detects disease early in its preclinical stage to limit progression", "E": "Primordial prevention, which targets population risk factors before they become established in a community"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Secondary prevention, which detects disease early in its preclinical stage to limit progression): Screening asymptomatic adults finds disease in a latent or preclinical stage, which is the definition of secondary prevention. Primary prevention would aim to prevent hypertension from developing, and tertiary prevention would manage complications in people already diagnosed."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "community_health", "question": "During a summer in a wooded region, health officials follow 500 hikers, half of whom use tick repellent by their own choice, and record how many develop the expanding skin lesion shown on the forearm of one hiker. They want to compare the incidence of the lesion between the groups. Which study design is this?", "options": {"A": "Case series, in which a group of affected hikers is described without any comparison group", "B": "Randomized trial, in which the investigators assign repellent use to hikers and compare incidence", "C": "Prospective cohort study, in which exposure groups are observed forward in time for new disease", "D": "Cross-sectional study, in which exposure and lesion presence are recorded together at a single time", "E": "Case-control study, in which affected hikers are matched with unaffected hikers and exposure is compared"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prospective cohort study, in which exposure groups are observed forward in time for new disease): The investigators identified hikers by repellent use and followed them forward to count new lesions, which defines a prospective cohort study. Because hikers chose repellent themselves, it is not a randomized trial. A case-control design would start from affected hikers, and a case series lacks a comparison group.", "image": "question_images/q_307.png"}, {"id": 308, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 28-year-old man is brought in with decreased need for sleep, racing speech, and spending his savings for five days after starting fluoxetine for depression. Two years earlier he had a week of elevated energy. Urine drug screen is negative. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Add a benzodiazepine alone and observe for resolution of agitation over several days", "B": "Increase the fluoxetine dose to treat the underlying depression driving the behavioral change", "C": "Switch to a tricyclic antidepressant with stronger efficacy for severe bipolar depression", "D": "Discontinue the antidepressant and begin a mood stabilizer such as lithium or valproate", "E": "Start a stimulant to improve concentration and treat possible comorbid adult attention deficit disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discontinue the antidepressant and begin a mood stabilizer such as lithium or valproate): Manic symptoms after starting an antidepressant, with a prior period of elevated energy, indicate bipolar disorder with antidepressant-associated mania. The antidepressant should be stopped and a mood stabilizer started, whereas more serotonergic or stimulant drugs would worsen the episode."}, {"id": 309, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with long standing rheumatoid arthritis reports new neck pain radiating to the occiput and brief paresthesias with neck flexion. A lateral cervical radiograph in flexion shows an increased gap between the dens and the anterior arch of the atlas. Which structure was most likely compromised by synovitis?", "options": {"A": "Transverse band of the cruciate ligament, restraining forward translation of C1 on C2 in flexion", "B": "Tectorial membrane, the continuation of the posterior longitudinal ligament covering the dens within the canal", "C": "Alar ligaments, which restrain excessive rotation and sidebending of the skull on C2", "D": "Apical ligament of the dens, a vestigial band joining the dens tip to the occiput", "E": "Ligamentum nuchae, the midline fibroelastic septum attached to the spinous processes of the cervical vertebrae"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Transverse band of the cruciate ligament, restraining forward translation of C1 on C2 in flexion): Pannus erodes the transverse band of the cruciate ligament, which normally holds the dens against the anterior arch and restrains forward translation of C1 on C2, producing atlantoaxial subluxation, seen as a widened atlantodental interval in flexion. Alar ligaments limit rotation and sidebending of the skull, so their failure would not widen this gap in flexion. Cord compression is the danger, so flexion films are obtained before intubation."}, {"id": 310, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 68-year-old man with atrial fibrillation is brought to the emergency department after sudden right-sided weakness. Examination shows right lower limb weakness greater than upper limb weakness, right sensory loss in the leg, abulia, and urinary incontinence. Speech is preserved. Which vascular territory is infarcted?", "options": {"A": "Left posterior cerebral artery supplying the medial occipital and inferior temporal cortex", "B": "Left middle cerebral artery supplying the lateral frontal and parietal convexity", "C": "Right anterior cerebral artery supplying the medial frontal and parietal cortex", "D": "Left anterior cerebral artery supplying the medial frontal and parietal cortex", "E": "Left anterior choroidal artery supplying the posterior limb of the internal capsule"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left anterior cerebral artery supplying the medial frontal and parietal cortex): The anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes, where the leg and foot representation of the motor and sensory homunculus lies. Leg predominant contralateral deficits with abulia and incontinence from paracentral and medial frontal involvement localize here. Middle cerebral infarcts affect the face and arm more than the leg and add aphasia when the dominant hemisphere is involved."}, {"id": 311, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 26-year-old man is stabbed in the back, and imaging shows a complete right-sided hemisection of the spinal cord at T8. He has no other injuries and is hemodynamically stable. Which pattern of sensory loss below the level of the lesion is expected?", "options": {"A": "Bilateral loss of vibration and proprioception with preserved pain and temperature sensation on both sides", "B": "Right loss of pain and temperature sensation with preserved vibration and proprioception on both sides", "C": "Bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation with preserved vibration and proprioception on both sides", "D": "Right loss of vibration and proprioception with left loss of pain and temperature sensation", "E": "Left loss of vibration and proprioception with right loss of pain and temperature sensation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Right loss of vibration and proprioception with left loss of pain and temperature sensation): Dorsal column fibers ascend uncrossed, so vibration and proprioception are lost on the same side as the hemisection. Spinothalamic fibers cross near their level of entry, so pain and temperature loss appears on the opposite side beginning a few levels below the lesion. This is Brown-Sequard syndrome; bilateral patterns would suggest central or anterior cord or posterior column disease."}, {"id": 312, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 19-year-old man reports painful muscle cramps and dark urine after sprinting, though he tolerates mild walking. During an ischemic forearm exercise test his ammonia rises normally but venous lactate does not rise. Serum creatine kinase is elevated after exercise, and a muscle biopsy shows subsarcolemmal glycogen accumulation. Which enzyme is most likely deficient in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II, impairing long-chain fatty acid oxidation and causing rhabdomyolysis after prolonged endurance activity", "B": "Mitochondrial complex I deficiency causing early lactic acidosis with minimal exertion and ragged red fibers", "C": "Myophosphorylase, which normally liberates glucose-1-phosphate from stored polysaccharide to supply glycolysis during intense exercise", "D": "Acid alpha-glucosidase, causing lysosomal glycogen storage with progressive proximal weakness and cardiomyopathy in affected patients", "E": "Muscle phosphofructokinase-1, blocking conversion of fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate and producing hemolysis during intense exercise"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Myophosphorylase, which normally liberates glucose-1-phosphate from stored polysaccharide to supply glycolysis during intense exercise): Without myophosphorylase, muscle cannot mobilize glycogen, so intense exercise causes cramps and myoglobinuria with glycogen buildup, and lactate stays flat because glycolysis is not fed. The normal ammonia rise proves effort was adequate. Tarui disease can look similar but adds hemolysis, and mitochondrial disease would raise lactate early."}, {"id": 313, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A pharmaceutical trial of a new migraine drug fails its primary endpoint, but investigators test 20 secondary outcomes using alpha of 0.05 each and report that sleep quality improved significantly with p = 0.04. The company proposes advertising the drug for improving sleep. Which statement best describes the main problem?", "options": {"A": "A p value of 0.04 gives a 4 percent probability that the sleep finding is false", "B": "Type II error is the main concern since twenty outcomes demand a larger sample", "C": "Publication bias explains the finding, since negative primary results are less likely to be published", "D": "The primary endpoint failure proves the drug has no effect so secondary findings are invalid", "E": "Multiple comparisons inflate the overall type I error, so one nominal hit may be chance"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Multiple comparisons inflate the overall type I error, so one nominal hit may be chance): Testing 20 outcomes at 0.05 each gives roughly a 64 percent chance of at least one false positive, so a single p value of 0.04 among many tests is weak evidence. A p value is not the probability that a result is false, and a failed primary endpoint does not make secondary findings invalid, only exploratory. Publication bias and type II error do not explain a single significant finding."}, {"id": 314, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 36-year-old man, three months after a below-knee amputation for trauma, reports sharp electric pain at the end of the residual limb when his prosthesis is worn. Tapping a small tender nodule on the distal stump reproduces the pain locally. He also describes an occasional cramping sensation in the absent foot. Which diagnosis explains the stump pain?", "options": {"A": "Heterotopic ossification, formation of mature lamellar bone in soft tissue around the distal residual limb", "B": "Phantom limb pain, perception of pain in the missing limb from cortical and spinal reorganization", "C": "Complex regional pain syndrome of the stump, a burning disorder with diffuse allodynia and edema", "D": "Residual limb osteomyelitis, a chronic bone infection with sinus drainage and fever near the cut bone", "E": "Traumatic neuroma, disorganized regenerating axons and Schwann cells at the cut end of a nerve"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Traumatic neuroma, disorganized regenerating axons and Schwann cells at the cut end of a nerve): A tender nodule that produces local electric pain on tapping (a Tinel sign at the stump) is a traumatic neuroma of the transected nerve. The cramping felt in the absent foot is phantom sensation, a separate phenomenon from the stump pain. Heterotopic ossification and infection would show different local features."}, {"id": 315, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old man undergoes thoracic aortic surgery, and the surgeon identifies a fibrous cord running from the aortic arch to the left pulmonary artery, adjacent to the left recurrent laryngeal nerve. During the operation, the cord is carefully preserved. This structure represents the postnatal remnant of which fetal structure?", "options": {"A": "Foramen ovale, a flap shunt from right atrium to left atrium", "B": "Ductus venosus, a shunt from umbilical vein to inferior vena cava", "C": "Ductus arteriosus, a shunt from pulmonary trunk to descending aorta", "D": "Umbilical artery, carrying deoxygenated blood from internal iliac to placenta", "E": "Umbilical vein, carrying oxygenated blood from placenta to the fetal liver"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ductus arteriosus, a shunt from pulmonary trunk to descending aorta): The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrosed ductus arteriosus, derived from the left sixth aortic arch, and it lies beside the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which hooks around it. The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum in the liver, a tempting remnant that is anatomically distant from the aortic arch."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old cyclist has right lower chest pain after a fall onto the handlebars. On palpation, the lateral shaft of right rib 9 remains prominent and wide during exhalation and its excursion is smaller than on the left. Which motion type is involved and which dysfunction is present?", "options": {"A": "Bucket-handle motion of rib 9, with an inhalation dysfunction because the rib stays up", "B": "Bucket-handle motion of rib 9, with an exhalation dysfunction because the rib stays down", "C": "Pump-handle motion of rib 9, with an inhalation dysfunction because the rib stays up", "D": "Caliper motion of rib 9, with an exhalation dysfunction because the rib stays down", "E": "Pump-handle motion of rib 9, with an exhalation dysfunction because the rib stays down"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bucket-handle motion of rib 9, with an inhalation dysfunction because the rib stays up): Ribs 6 to 10 move mainly in bucket-handle fashion, widening the lateral chest, so a rib 9 that stays prominent laterally and moves less is stuck in inhalation. Pump-handle motion belongs to ribs 1 to 5, and an exhalation dysfunction would show the rib held low rather than prominent."}, {"id": 317, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 62-year-old man who smokes presents with gross hematuria and a dull right flank ache. Examination shows a palpable flank mass. Hemoglobin is 18.4 g/dL. CT shows a heterogeneous right renal mass invading the renal vein. Biopsy reveals polygonal cells with clear cytoplasm. Which molecular alteration is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Loss of function of TSC1 or TSC2 with mTOR activation, producing fat containing angiomyolipomas", "B": "Biallelic loss of the VHL gene on chromosome 3p, causing HIF accumulation and erythropoietin production", "C": "Activating mutation of the MET proto-oncogene on chromosome 7, driving papillary tumor architecture", "D": "Deletion of the WT1 gene on chromosome 11p, impairing nephron differentiation in the embryonic kidney tissue", "E": "Mutation in the BHD gene causing oncocytoma and chromophobe tumors with eosinophilic granular cytoplasm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Biallelic loss of the VHL gene on chromosome 3p, causing HIF accumulation and erythropoietin production): Clear cell renal carcinoma arises from proximal tubule cells and usually carries VHL loss on 3p, which stabilizes HIF and raises erythropoietin, explaining the polycythemia. Papillary carcinoma is linked to MET, and Wilms tumor occurs in children. Hematuria, a flank mass and renal vein invasion are classic features of renal cell carcinoma."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 14-year-old boy is concerned that he is shorter than classmates. His height is at the 3rd percentile and has tracked there since age 10. His testes measure 3 mL, bone age is 12 years, and growth velocity is normal. His father reached final height at age 19. Which explanation best fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Growth hormone deficiency, causing subnormal growth velocity with delayed bone age and midline facial features", "B": "Familial short stature, in which bone age equals chronologic age and puberty begins on time", "C": "Kallmann syndrome with isolated gonadotropin deficiency, anosmia, and failure of spontaneous pubertal progression", "D": "Klinefelter syndrome with small firm testes, tall stature, and elevated gonadotropin levels after expected pubertal age", "E": "Constitutional delay of growth and puberty, with catch-up growth expected as skeletal maturation advances"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Constitutional delay of growth and puberty, with catch-up growth expected as skeletal maturation advances): A short boy with delayed bone age, normal growth velocity, early prepubertal testes, and a father who matured late has constitutional delay, a normal variant. Familial short stature would show a bone age equal to chronologic age. Growth hormone deficiency would reduce growth velocity, and Klinefelter syndrome is associated with tall stature."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 5-year-old boy has had four long-bone fractures after minor falls, and radiographs show generalized osteopenia. His sclerae appear blue, he has mild conductive hearing loss, and his parents report no bruising. His teeth are normal. Which molecular defect best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Mutated type II collagen disrupting hyaline cartilage matrix and the vitreous humor", "B": "Deficient lysyl hydroxylase causing under-hydroxylated lysine and poor collagen cross-linking", "C": "Reduced production of structurally normal type I collagen from a COL1A1 null allele", "D": "Defective fibrillin-1 that impairs microfibril assembly within elastic connective tissue", "E": "Abnormal type III collagen that weakens the walls of vessels and hollow organs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduced production of structurally normal type I collagen from a COL1A1 null allele): Blue sclerae, fractures with minimal trauma and hearing loss with normal teeth fit osteogenesis imperfecta type I, caused by a null COL1A1 allele that reduces the amount of normal type I collagen. Fibrillin, type III and type II collagen defects produce Marfan syndrome, vascular Ehlers-Danlos and chondrodysplasias. Under-hydroxylated lysine appears in kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos."}, {"id": 320, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A health department screens asymptomatic adults at a community fair with a sensitive, inexpensive rapid assay. A healthy 29-year-old with no risk factors tests positive, and local prevalence of the infection is 0.1 percent. The patient is anxious and asks whether to start treatment today. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Arrange a more specific confirmatory assay, since most positives at this prevalence are false", "B": "Repeat the same rapid assay in one week, since a second positive confirms infection", "C": "Reassure the patient the result is a false positive and recommend no further evaluation", "D": "Order a more sensitive assay, since false negatives are the main concern at this prevalence", "E": "Start treatment today, since the highly sensitive rapid assay is reliable when it is positive"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Arrange a more specific confirmatory assay, since most positives at this prevalence are false): At a prevalence of 0.1 percent, even a sensitive assay with moderate specificity produces mostly false positives, so a single positive result has low positive predictive value. A more specific confirmatory assay separates true from false positives. Treating immediately ignores base rates, and repeating the same assay can reproduce the same cross-reacting false positive."}, {"id": 321, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with giant cell arteritis has taken prednisone 40 mg daily for 5 months. She now notes difficulty rising from a chair without muscle pain. Creatine kinase and aldolase are normal, electromyography is normal, and thigh muscle biopsy shows type II fiber atrophy. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "Myasthenia gravis from autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction", "B": "Glucocorticoid induced myopathy from enhanced protein catabolism and reduced synthesis in fast twitch fibers", "C": "Polymyositis from CD8 positive T cell attack on muscle fibers expressing class I antigens", "D": "Vasculitic neuropathy from arteritis involving vasa nervorum supplying the proximal peripheral nerves", "E": "Hypothyroid myopathy from impaired glycogen mobilization and slowed relaxation in skeletal muscle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Glucocorticoid induced myopathy from enhanced protein catabolism and reduced synthesis in fast twitch fibers): Chronic glucocorticoid exposure causes painless proximal weakness with normal muscle enzymes and electromyography, and biopsy shows type II fiber atrophy. Inflammatory myopathy would raise creatine kinase and show inflammation. The clinical context of prolonged high dose steroid therapy makes the drug effect the best explanation."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A case-control study finds that heavy coffee drinkers have a threefold higher odds of lung cancer. Further analysis shows that coffee drinkers are much more likely to smoke, and smoking independently raises lung cancer risk. Smoking is not a consequence of coffee drinking. Which approach best addresses this problem in the data analysis?", "options": {"A": "Stratify by tobacco exposure or fit a multivariable model that adjusts for that exposure", "B": "Blind the interviewers to case status when asking about coffee drinking habits", "C": "Match controls to cases on coffee intake to remove the coffee effect", "D": "Calculate relative risk instead of odds ratio, since odds ratios are biased by smoking", "E": "Increase the sample size so random error shrinks and the coffee association weakens"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stratify by tobacco exposure or fit a multivariable model that adjusts for that exposure): Smoking is associated with coffee use, raises cancer risk independently and is not on the causal path, so it is a classic confounder. Stratification or multivariable adjustment controls it in the analysis. A larger sample reduces random error but leaves confounding intact, and relative risk cannot be obtained directly from a case-control design."}, {"id": 323, "domain": "community_health", "question": "Two candidate troponin assays are evaluated for detecting myocardial infarction in 400 emergency department patients, and the receiver operating characteristic curves are shown. Assay X has an area under the curve of 0.95 and assay Y has 0.70. Which statement best interprets these findings across all possible cutoffs?", "options": {"A": "Assay Y has higher specificity than assay X at each threshold since its curve lies lower", "B": "Assay X has 95 percent sensitivity and 95 percent specificity at its optimal cutoff point", "C": "Assay Y is preferable for ruling out disease because its curve passes nearer the diagonal", "D": "Assay X is more likely to rank a diseased patient above a nondiseased patient", "E": "Assay X has a positive predictive value of 95 percent in this emergency department population"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assay X is more likely to rank a diseased patient above a nondiseased patient): The area under the ROC curve equals the probability that a randomly chosen diseased patient receives a higher result than a randomly chosen nondiseased patient, so 0.95 outperforms 0.70. It does not give sensitivity, specificity or predictive value at any single cutoff. Predictive value also depends on prevalence, which a ROC curve does not contain.", "image": "question_images/q_323.png"}, {"id": 324, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A study reports a Pearson correlation coefficient of negative 0.82 between hours of weekly exercise and resting heart rate among 200 adults. Participants were volunteers recruited from a gym and a walking club. A student concludes that exercise causes the lower heart rate. Which statement best describes what the coefficient shows?", "options": {"A": "A strong inverse association explaining about eighty-two percent of the variation in heart rate", "B": "A weak inverse association, because the coefficient is negative and below one", "C": "A moderate inverse association, since the slope of the regression line equals negative 0.82", "D": "A strong inverse association proving that exercise lowers resting heart rate in individuals", "E": "A strong inverse linear association explaining about two thirds of variation, without proving causation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A strong inverse linear association explaining about two thirds of variation, without proving causation): A coefficient of negative 0.82 indicates a strong inverse linear relationship, and squaring it gives about 0.67, so roughly two thirds of the variability is shared. Correlation in a volunteer sample cannot establish that exercise lowers heart rate, since fitter people may simply choose to exercise more. The coefficient is not the regression slope."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A pediatric clinic wants to know whether the proportion of children who develop otitis media differs between those attending daycare and those cared for at home. Each child is classified as having or not having the outcome. Sample sizes are adequate in every cell. Which test best compares these two groups?", "options": {"A": "Chi-square test of independence, which evaluates counts arranged in a two by two table", "B": "Paired t test, which compares measurements taken twice on the same children", "C": "Analysis of variance, which compares means of a continuous outcome among several groups", "D": "Pearson correlation coefficient, which measures the strength of linear association between two continuous variables", "E": "Unpaired t test, which compares the means of a continuous outcome between two groups"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chi-square test of independence, which evaluates counts arranged in a two by two table): Both exposure (daycare versus home) and outcome (otitis media present or absent) are categorical, so counts in a two by two table are compared with a chi-square test. The t tests and analysis of variance need a continuous outcome summarized as a mean. A Fisher exact test would replace chi-square only if expected cell counts were small."}, {"id": 326, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 30-year-old runner is receiving treatment for a somatic dysfunction. The physician positions her at the restrictive barrier and asks her to push gently against a precisely applied counterforce for several seconds, then relaxes and repositions to the new barrier. Which technique is being described, and how is it classified?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain, an indirect technique holding a position of ease against a counterforce", "B": "Myofascial release, an indirect technique applying sustained pressure to fascial tension", "C": "Functional technique, an indirect method following tissue feedback toward the barrier", "D": "Muscle energy, a direct technique driven by the patient's own isometric contraction", "E": "High velocity low amplitude, a direct technique delivering a thrust through the barrier"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Muscle energy, a direct technique driven by the patient's own isometric contraction): Patient-activated isometric contraction against a counterforce from the physician, followed by repositioning to the new barrier, defines muscle energy, a direct technique. Counterstrain and functional technique move toward ease rather than the barrier. High velocity low amplitude uses a thrust rather than a sustained contraction."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 14-year-old girl is diagnosed with osteosarcoma. Her mother had breast cancer at age 32, a maternal uncle had an adrenocortical carcinoma, and a maternal grandmother had a brain tumor in her forties. Genetic testing reveals a germline mutation in a gene whose protein normally acts after DNA damage. Which cellular function is lost?", "options": {"A": "Halting the cell cycle at G1 and initiating apoptosis when DNA repair fails", "B": "Correcting nucleotide mismatches that arise during replication of repetitive microsatellite sequences in dividing cells", "C": "Sequestering transcription factors on a hypophosphorylated protein until cyclin dependent kinases phosphorylate it", "D": "Hydrolyzing GTP to switch off a membrane signaling protein after growth factor binding occurs", "E": "Linking cadherins to the actin cytoskeleton to maintain adhesion between neighboring epithelial cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Halting the cell cycle at G1 and initiating apoptosis when DNA repair fails): The pattern of sarcoma, early breast cancer, adrenocortical carcinoma, and brain tumors in one family fits Li-Fraumeni syndrome from germline TP53 mutation. The p53 protein stops the cycle and triggers apoptosis after unrepaired DNA damage. Mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, which has a different tumor spectrum."}, {"id": 328, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 52-year-old man slipped on ice and landed hard on his right buttock yesterday. The right leg appears shorter, the standing flexion test is positive on the right, and both the right ASIS and right PSIS sit higher than the left. The pubic tubercle is also higher. He takes metformin. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Right anterior innominate rotation with an accompanying right inferior pubic shear", "B": "Right unilateral sacral extension with an accompanying right sacroiliac joint restriction", "C": "Right outflare of the innominate with accompanying compression at the pubic symphysis", "D": "Right superior innominate shear with an accompanying right superior pubic shear", "E": "Right posterior innominate rotation with an accompanying right superior pubic shear"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Right superior innominate shear with an accompanying right superior pubic shear): Both landmarks rising with a short leg and a higher pubic tubercle after a fall onto the buttock indicates a superior innominate shear with a superior pubic shear. Posterior rotation also shortens the leg but raises the ASIS while lowering the PSIS. Anterior rotation would lengthen the leg."}, {"id": 329, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 24-year-old man is found unresponsive after an opioid overdose, breathing six times per minute with pinpoint pupils. Lungs are clear on auscultation and the chest radiograph is normal. On room air at sea level his PaCO2 is 80 mmHg and PaO2 is 50 mmHg. Using a respiratory quotient of 0.8, which interpretation of his hypoxemia is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Hypoxemia reflects a diffusion limitation with a widened alveolar to arterial oxygen gradient", "B": "Hypoxemia reflects intrapulmonary shunt with a markedly widened alveolar to arterial oxygen gradient", "C": "Hypoxemia reflects ventilation-perfusion mismatch with a mildly widened alveolar to arterial gradient", "D": "Hypoxemia reflects right-to-left cardiac shunt with a widened gradient that persists on supplemental oxygen", "E": "Hypoxemia is fully explained by alveolar hypoventilation with a normal alveolar to arterial gradient"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Hypoxemia is fully explained by alveolar hypoventilation with a normal alveolar to arterial gradient): Alveolar PO2 is about 150 minus 80 divided by 0.8, which is 50 mmHg, so the alveolar to arterial gradient is near zero. A normal gradient with high PaCO2 means the hypoxemia comes from hypoventilation, and it will correct with ventilation and oxygen. Shunt, V/Q mismatch and diffusion defects all widen the gradient."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 22-year-old woman with primary dysmenorrhea asks her osteopathic physician whether her back findings relate to her cramping. The physician palpates the paravertebral tissues during an acute episode and expects reflex changes from sympathetic input to the uterus. At which spinal segmental levels is the viscerosomatic reflex most likely to be found?", "options": {"A": "S2 to S4 sacral region, matching parasympathetic outflow through the pelvic splanchnic nerves", "B": "T5 to T9 paraspinal region, matching sympathetic outflow to the stomach and upper gastrointestinal tract", "C": "T10 to L2 paraspinal region, matching preganglionic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column", "D": "T8 to T10 paraspinal region, matching sympathetic outflow to the small intestine and mesentery", "E": "T1 to T4 paraspinal region, matching sympathetic outflow to the head and neck"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (T10 to L2 paraspinal region, matching preganglionic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column): Sympathetic viscerosomatic reflexes from the uterus and prostate map to roughly T10 to L2, so acute paraspinal tissue changes appear there. The sacral levels carry parasympathetic outflow and would not be the sympathetic reflex site. The small bowel is served higher, at about T8 to T10."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 40-year-old man has had thick, dry, fissured plaques on both palms for years, and over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream has not helped. Examination shows lichenified, scaly skin without signs of infection, and a KOH preparation is negative. Which topical therapy is most appropriate to start for this thick-skinned area?", "options": {"A": "Clobetasol propionate ointment, a superpotent steroid able to penetrate the thick palmar stratum corneum", "B": "Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1 percent, a medium potency steroid often inadequate on palmar and plantar skin", "C": "Desonide 0.05 percent, a low potency steroid chosen to limit atrophy in sensitive areas", "D": "Hydrocortisone 1 percent, a low potency steroid suited to thin skin of the face", "E": "Tacrolimus 0.1 percent ointment, a calcineurin inhibitor used where steroids cause skin atrophy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clobetasol propionate ointment, a superpotent steroid able to penetrate the thick palmar stratum corneum): Palmar and plantar skin has a thick stratum corneum that limits absorption, so hyperkeratotic dermatitis needs a high or superpotent steroid for a limited course. Low potency steroids are reserved for the face and skin folds, where atrophy is a greater risk and absorption is higher."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with schizophrenia treated with risperidone develops breast tenderness, galactorrhea and amenorrhea over four months. Her pregnancy test is negative, serum prolactin is elevated and her symptoms improve with aripiprazole substitution. Which dopamine pathway blockade best explains the endocrine findings?", "options": {"A": "Tuberoinfundibular pathway from hypothalamus to anterior pituitary, where dopamine tonically inhibits lactotroph hormone secretion", "B": "Mesolimbic pathway to the nucleus accumbens, where blockade relieves positive symptoms and also raises prolactin", "C": "Hypothalamic histaminergic pathway to the cortex, where blockade increases appetite and prolactin secretion", "D": "Nigrostriatal pathway to the dorsal striatum, where blockade causes parkinsonism and elevated prolactin together", "E": "Mesocortical pathway to the prefrontal cortex, where blockade worsens negative symptoms and raises prolactin secretion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tuberoinfundibular pathway from hypothalamus to anterior pituitary, where dopamine tonically inhibits lactotroph hormone secretion): Dopamine released from tuberoinfundibular neurons acts on pituitary D2 receptors to suppress prolactin, so D2 blockade by risperidone removes the inhibition and causes hyperprolactinemia. Mesolimbic blockade produces the antipsychotic benefit and nigrostriatal blockade produces parkinsonism, but neither controls lactotroph output."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 45-year-old man has recurrent episodes of fever, chills, cough, and dyspnea beginning four to six hours after cleaning his pigeon loft. Symptoms resolve within two days and are absent on vacations. High-resolution CT shows centrilobular ground-glass nodules. Biopsy shows poorly formed granulomas and interstitial lymphocytes. Which immunologic mechanism best explains his disease?", "options": {"A": "Type I IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation producing immediate bronchoconstriction after antigen inhalation", "B": "Type II antibody binding to alveolar basement membrane antigens with complement-mediated injury", "C": "Direct toxic injury from inhaled silica particles engulfed by alveolar macrophages with fibrogenic cytokine release", "D": "Type IV reaction with CD8 T cells killing alveolar epithelial cells infected by an inhaled virus", "E": "Type III immune complex reaction with a type IV T-cell response to inhaled antigens"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Type III immune complex reaction with a type IV T-cell response to inhaled antigens): Symptoms starting hours after exposure to avian antigens, resolving away from exposure, with ground-glass nodules and poorly formed granulomas, describe hypersensitivity pneumonitis. The acute phase involves immune complex deposition, and the granulomatous chronic phase reflects a T-cell mediated reaction. The delay excludes an IgE-mediated type I process."}, {"id": 334, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 71-year-old woman in a nursing home has recurrent atelectasis and a weak cough. The physician finds restricted rib cage motion on inhalation and treats the thoracic cage with soft tissue and rib raising, reasoning that altered chest wall mechanics impairs lung ventilation and that restoring mechanics will improve ventilatory efficiency. Which tenet most directly supports this reasoning?", "options": {"A": "Rational treatment is based on understanding the body unit, self-regulation, and structure-function principles", "B": "Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered anatomy changes physiologic performance", "C": "The rule of the artery is supreme, so free vascular supply governs all tissue health", "D": "The body possesses self-regulatory mechanisms and an inherent capacity for self-healing and health maintenance", "E": "The body is a unit, with person made of body, mind, and spirit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered anatomy changes physiologic performance): The physician connects a mechanical restriction of the rib cage to altered lung function and expects treatment of structure to improve function, which is the reciprocal relationship tenet. The treatment-planning tenet is a reasonable general statement but is the less specific match to the stated reasoning."}, {"id": 335, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 63-year-old man who had tuberculosis in his youth reports fatigue, abdominal swelling, and leg edema. Jugular venous pressure is elevated and rises further during inspiration. A high pitched early diastolic sound is heard at the apex. Chest radiography shows pericardial calcification. Which mechanism best explains the inspiratory rise in jugular venous pressure?", "options": {"A": "Restrictive infiltration stiffens the myocardium so diastolic compliance falls and filling pressures stay fixed", "B": "Tricuspid regurgitation produces retrograde systolic flow that is accentuated when the right ventricle fills", "C": "A rigid pericardium blocks transmission of negative thoracic pressure so venous return cannot increase", "D": "Right ventricular failure from pulmonary hypertension reduces forward flow and raises systemic venous pressure", "E": "Pericardial effusion compresses the right atrium and impairs early diastolic filling during inspiration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A rigid pericardium blocks transmission of negative thoracic pressure so venous return cannot increase): In constrictive pericarditis the thick calcified pericardium isolates the heart from respiratory pressure changes, so inspiration cannot draw more blood into the right heart and venous pressure paradoxically rises, which is Kussmaul sign. The pericardial knock and calcification confirm constriction. Effusion and myocardial restriction are different structural problems."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 41-year-old woman with Sjogren syndrome has recurrent flank pain with passed calcium phosphate stones and generalized fatigue. Serum bicarbonate is 15 mEq/L, potassium 3.0 mEq/L, and chloride 113 mEq/L, with an anion gap of 10. Urine pH is 6.5 despite systemic acidemia. Which renal tubular disorder best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Type 4 acidosis from aldosterone deficiency or resistance, with hyperkalemia and reduced ammonium excretion", "B": "Chronic laxative use causing bicarbonate loss with a normal gap, acidic urine and appropriate ammonium excretion", "C": "Advanced chronic kidney disease with reduced ammonium production and retained acids, causing high anion gap acidosis", "D": "Proximal type 2 acidosis from reduced bicarbonate reabsorption, with Fanconi syndrome and acidic urine", "E": "Distal type 1 acidosis from impaired intercalated cell hydrogen secretion, with hypokalemia and stone formation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Distal type 1 acidosis from impaired intercalated cell hydrogen secretion, with hypokalemia and stone formation): Autoimmune injury to alpha intercalated cells in Sjogren syndrome prevents distal hydrogen secretion, so urine cannot acidify below 5.5 despite acidemia, producing a normal gap acidosis with hypokalemia, hypocitraturia and calcium phosphate stones. Proximal type 2 and gut losses acidify the urine, and type 4 raises potassium."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with obesity, prediabetes and poor sleep meets her osteopathic physician. In addition to glucose and lipid management, the physician explores her work stress, family support and spiritual outlook, and plans lifestyle changes alongside manipulation. Which osteopathic tenet is most directly reflected by this comprehensive evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Rational treatment is founded on understanding the other three principles of body unity, self-regulation and structure-function", "B": "Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered structure changes function and altered function changes structure", "C": "Preventive medicine principle: early screening and risk-factor modification reduce disease burden at the population level", "D": "The body is a unit, and the person comprises body, mind and spirit", "E": "The body is capable of self-regulation, self-healing and health maintenance when given supportive conditions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The body is a unit, and the person comprises body, mind and spirit): Assessing stress, relationships and spirituality alongside metabolic numbers treats the patient as a person of body, mind and spirit, which is the unity tenet. Self-regulation and structure-function are separate tenets, rational treatment applies them to care, and population screening is a public health principle rather than a tenet."}, {"id": 338, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 9-year-old boy develops hives, wheezing, lip swelling, and a blood pressure of 70/40 mm Hg ten minutes after eating a cookie at school. He has a known peanut allergy and oxygen saturation is 91 percent. Emergency services have been called. Which medication should be given first?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous diphenhydramine to block histamine receptors and halt further mediator effects", "B": "Intravenous methylprednisolone to suppress the inflammatory cascade and prevent biphasic relapse", "C": "Intramuscular epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh to reverse vasodilation and bronchospasm", "D": "Nebulized albuterol to relieve bronchospasm as the main cause of the hypoxemia", "E": "Intravenous norepinephrine infusion to restore vascular tone in distributive shock"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intramuscular epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh to reverse vasodilation and bronchospasm): Anaphylaxis with airway and circulatory compromise is treated first with intramuscular epinephrine, which acts on alpha and beta receptors to raise vascular tone and relax bronchial muscle. Antihistamines, steroids, and albuterol are adjuncts that do not reverse shock. A vasopressor infusion is reserved for refractory cases after epinephrine and fluids."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 31-year-old ballet dancer has lateral midfoot pain after landing from a jump on an inverted foot. Plain films are normal. There is tenderness over the lateral plantar midfoot, pain with push-off, and an antalgic gait. Peroneal tendon strength is preserved. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Peroneus longus tendon subluxation behind the lateral malleolus causing snapping with push-off pain", "B": "Lateral ankle sprain with anterior talofibular ligament tearing and midfoot pain", "C": "Stress fracture of the fifth metatarsal base despite normal plain films", "D": "Plantar cuboid somatic dysfunction with altered gliding at the calcaneocuboid joint", "E": "Navicular dysfunction with posterior tibial tendon overload and medial arch pain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Plantar cuboid somatic dysfunction with altered gliding at the calcaneocuboid joint): Lateral midfoot pain after inversion with plantar tenderness, normal plain films and preserved peroneal strength fits plantar cuboid dysfunction. A lateral ankle sprain would localize to the ligament below the malleolus, a fifth metatarsal stress fracture would produce bony tenderness at the base of the metatarsal, and peroneal subluxation would weaken eversion."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 4-month-old boy has an intermittent bulge in the right groin that enlarges with crying and reduces when he is calm. At surgery, the hernia sac is found lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels and travels through the deep inguinal ring into the spermatic cord. What is the underlying anatomic defect?", "options": {"A": "Persistent patency of the processus vaginalis extending through the deep inguinal ring", "B": "Persistent umbilical ring defect permitting protrusion of bowel through the linea alba", "C": "Attenuation of the transversalis fascia within the medial inguinal triangle of Hesselbach", "D": "Spigelian fascia weakness lateral to the rectus sheath at the semilunar line", "E": "Herniation through the femoral canal beneath the inguinal ligament and medial to the femoral vein"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Persistent patency of the processus vaginalis extending through the deep inguinal ring): A sac lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels that passes through the deep ring is an indirect inguinal hernia, which in infants reflects a patent processus vaginalis. Direct hernias pass medial to those vessels through Hesselbach triangle and represent acquired weakness of the transversalis fascia, typically in older men."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 19-year-old college student has had ten days of fatigue, fever, exudative pharyngitis, and posterior cervical adenopathy. Examination shows a palpable spleen tip, and a heterophile antibody test is positive. He asks for osteopathic treatment to speed his recovery. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Splenic pump with rhythmic compression over the left lower ribs to boost antibody release", "B": "Abdominal diaphragm release with deep sustained pressure beneath the left costal margin", "C": "Liver pump with rhythmic compression of the right lower ribs to enhance hepatic lymph flow", "D": "Mesenteric lifting with deep sustained bimanual lifting of the abdominal contents toward the umbilicus", "E": "Gentle cervical and thoracic inlet myofascial release, avoiding splenic pump over the left lower ribs"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Gentle cervical and thoracic inlet myofascial release, avoiding splenic pump over the left lower ribs): Infectious mononucleosis causes splenomegaly, and compression over the spleen or deep abdominal pressure risks splenic rupture, so the splenic pump is contraindicated. Gentle techniques directed to the cervical chain and thoracic inlet can support lymphatic drainage without loading the spleen."}, {"id": 342, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 69-year-old man recovers from a stroke that initially caused numbness of the entire left side of his body. Three weeks later he develops severe burning pain over the left face, arm and leg, triggered by light touch or a cool breeze. Strength is normal, and he takes aspirin and atorvastatin. Which site was infarcted?", "options": {"A": "Right trigeminal spinal nucleus in the lateral medulla supplied by vertebral artery branches", "B": "Right postcentral gyrus supplied by cortical branches of the middle cerebral artery", "C": "Right ventral posterolateral and posteromedial thalamic nuclei supplied by thalamogeniculate branches", "D": "Right posterior limb of the internal capsule supplied by lenticulostriate arteries", "E": "Left ventral posterolateral and posteromedial thalamic nuclei supplied by thalamogeniculate branches of the posterior cerebral artery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right ventral posterolateral and posteromedial thalamic nuclei supplied by thalamogeniculate branches): Thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy) follows infarction of the ventral posterior nuclei of the thalamus, which relay sensation from the contralateral face and body. After initial hemisensory loss, central sensitization produces burning pain and allodynia on the opposite side, so a left-sided syndrome localizes to the right thalamus, usually from thalamogeniculate branches of the posterior cerebral artery. Medullary lesions give crossed face and body findings."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 70-year-old man with decompensated heart failure has orthopnea, bibasilar crackles, and bilateral pitting leg edema. He has right-sided rib dysfunction and thoracic paraspinal tension, and he becomes short of breath when lying flat. Which osteopathic approach is most appropriate for this visit?", "options": {"A": "Supine HVLA to the thoracic spine with the patient flat for the best segmental leverage", "B": "Gentle seated soft tissue and low-effort muscle energy to the ribs, keeping the trunk upright", "C": "Supine thoracic lymphatic pump with rapid chest compression to mobilize pulmonary edema fluid quickly", "D": "Prolonged supine myofascial release of the thoracic inlet with the table flat for twenty minutes", "E": "Prone counterstrain at posterior thoracic tender points held for ninety seconds with the table flat"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gentle seated soft tissue and low-effort muscle energy to the ribs, keeping the trunk upright): A patient with orthopnea tolerates only upright or semi-upright positioning, so gentle low-effort techniques in the seated position treat the rib dysfunction without worsening dyspnea. Flat supine or prone positioning raises venous return and pulmonary congestion, which makes the other plans poorly tolerated even when the technique itself is reasonable."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 79-year-old woman hospitalized for community-acquired pneumonia fell yesterday and now has focal tenderness and crepitus over the left seventh rib. A radiograph confirms a nondisplaced fracture. The physician wants a lymphatic technique to support recovery. Which technique should be avoided in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Suboccipital release applying gentle traction at the occiput to improve cranial lymphatic and vagal flow", "B": "Cervical fascial release at the thoracic inlet using gentle soft tissue tension with the patient supine", "C": "Popliteal fossa release using sustained gentle fascial tension behind both knees with the patient supine", "D": "Pedal lymphatic pump using rhythmic dorsiflexion and plantarflexion of the feet with the patient supine", "E": "Thoracic lymphatic pump using rhythmic compression and release of the anterior and lateral rib cage"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Thoracic lymphatic pump using rhythmic compression and release of the anterior and lateral rib cage): Rhythmic compression of the rib cage would load a fractured rib, so the thoracic pump is contraindicated here. The pedal pump and the head, neck, and popliteal releases act at regions away from the fracture and apply no chest wall compression, so they remain acceptable ways to support lymph flow."}, {"id": 345, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 22-year-old woman has had recurrent mild jaundice since adolescence, worsening during pregnancy and with oral contraceptive use. Total bilirubin is 4.1 mg/dL, with 70 percent conjugated. AST, ALT, and alkaline phosphatase are normal. Liver biopsy shows a grossly dark organ with coarse pigment granules in hepatocytes. Which condition explains her findings?", "options": {"A": "Benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis, caused by a defective bile salt transporter with episodic cholestasis and pruritus", "B": "Dubin Johnson syndrome, caused by defective MRP2 mediated canalicular secretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile", "C": "Wilson disease, caused by defective ATP7B copper excretion with hepatic copper accumulation and low ceruloplasmin", "D": "Gilbert syndrome, caused by reduced UGT1A1 expression producing mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia during fasting or stress", "E": "Rotor syndrome, caused by defective hepatocyte uptake and storage of conjugated bilirubin, with normal liver color"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dubin Johnson syndrome, caused by defective MRP2 mediated canalicular secretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile): Chronic fluctuating conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal enzymes and a pigmented black liver is characteristic of Dubin Johnson syndrome, a defect of canalicular export through MRP2 that estrogen can worsen. Rotor syndrome looks similar biochemically but the liver is not pigmented, and Gilbert syndrome is predominantly unconjugated."}, {"id": 346, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 33-year-old man with intermittent urinary hesitancy has lumbar findings on osteopathic examination. With the spine in neutral, L1 through L4 are sidebent left and rotated right as a group. The physician notes that motion testing in flexion and extension does not change the pattern. Which classification and mechanics best describe this finding?", "options": {"A": "Type III dysfunction in which motion in one plane first modifies the other planes", "B": "Type I neutral group curve with sidebending and rotation occurring toward opposite sides", "C": "Type I neutral group curve with sidebending and rotation occurring toward the same side", "D": "Type II non-neutral single segment with sidebending and rotation occurring toward the same side", "E": "Type II non-neutral group curve with sidebending and rotation occurring toward opposite sides"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Type I neutral group curve with sidebending and rotation occurring toward opposite sides): A group of segments in neutral that are sidebent one way and rotated the other is a Fryette type I dysfunction. Type I is typically found in the thoracic and lumbar spine and does not change with flexion or extension. Type II involves one non-neutral segment with sidebending and rotation to the same side."}, {"id": 347, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 72-year-old man has fluctuating confusion over one year with well-formed visual hallucinations of small children. His wife reports he shouts and acts out dreams at night. Mild rigidity is present. After haloperidol is given for agitation, he becomes severely rigid and unresponsive. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Parkinson disease dementia, in which cognitive decline follows years of established motor disease", "B": "Alzheimer disease with psychosis, where early hallucinations and rigidity are unusual features", "C": "Frontotemporal dementia with early disinhibition, hyperorality, and relative sparing of visuospatial skills", "D": "Dementia with Lewy bodies with severe neuroleptic sensitivity to dopamine blockade", "E": "Vascular dementia with stepwise decline, focal deficits, and prominent cardiovascular risk factors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dementia with Lewy bodies with severe neuroleptic sensitivity to dopamine blockade): Fluctuating cognition, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behavior disorder, and parkinsonism define dementia with Lewy bodies. Severe reactions to neuroleptics are characteristic. In Parkinson disease dementia, motor symptoms precede dementia by more than a year, which is not the case here."}, {"id": 348, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 38-year-old woman reports 10 days of severe anterior neck pain radiating to the jaw, beginning 3 weeks after an upper respiratory infection. She has palpitations and low-grade fever. The thyroid is exquisitely tender and firm. ESR is 84 mm/h, TSH is suppressed, and radioactive iodine uptake is 2 percent. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug for pain with propranolol for adrenergic symptoms while the process resolves", "B": "Methimazole titrated to normalize hormone levels, with radioactive iodine therapy if symptoms persist", "C": "Levothyroxine replacement to suppress TSH and shrink the inflamed gland over several weeks", "D": "Intravenous antibiotics with ultrasound-guided drainage of a suspected suppurative focus within the gland", "E": "Total thyroidectomy after antithyroid preparation to remove the inflamed and overactive gland"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug for pain with propranolol for adrenergic symptoms while the process resolves): A painful tender gland after a viral illness with a high ESR and very low uptake is subacute granulomatous thyroiditis. The thyrotoxic phase reflects hormone leakage, so drugs that block synthesis are ineffective. Pain and adrenergic symptoms are treated supportively, and the course usually resolves over weeks to months."}, {"id": 349, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 41-year-old man presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back after a night of heavy drinking. Lipase is 1,450 U/L and he is tachycardic with a heart rate of 112 per minute. He has no signs of cholangitis. Which initial management is best supported for his first 24 hours?", "options": {"A": "Urgent ERCP with sphincterotomy within the first twenty four hours to relieve ductal pressure", "B": "Goal directed isotonic crystalloid resuscitation, analgesia and early oral feeding as tolerated", "C": "Prophylactic broad spectrum antibiotics to prevent infection of pancreatic necrosis, with strict fasting for a week", "D": "Total parenteral nutrition begun immediately to rest the pancreas, with restricted intravenous fluids", "E": "Octreotide infusion to suppress pancreatic secretion and avoidance of opioid analgesia during the first day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Goal directed isotonic crystalloid resuscitation, analgesia and early oral feeding as tolerated): Early goal directed fluid resuscitation with balanced crystalloid, adequate analgesia and early enteral feeding improve outcomes in acute pancreatitis. Antibiotics, urgent ERCP, parenteral nutrition and octreotide are not routine in the absence of cholangitis or infected necrosis."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 5-day-old boy has a tetanic seizure. Examination shows low-set ears, a small jaw, and a systolic murmur. Serum calcium is 6.2 mg/dL, and chest radiograph shows no thymic shadow. Flow cytometry shows markedly reduced CD3 positive T cells. Which embryologic failure best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Failed fusion of the maxillary and medial nasal prominences from the first pharyngeal arch", "B": "Persistence of the second pharyngeal groove forming a cervical sinus beside the sternocleidomastoid", "C": "Failed descent of the ultimobranchial body from the fifth pouch to the thyroid gland", "D": "Failed differentiation of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches into their normal glandular derivatives", "E": "Defective development of the first pharyngeal pouch forming the tympanic cavity and auditory tube"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Failed differentiation of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches into their normal glandular derivatives): Hypocalcemic seizures from absent parathyroid glands, T cell deficiency from thymic aplasia, a cardiac defect and facial anomalies indicate DiGeorge syndrome from 22q11 deletion. The thymus and inferior parathyroids derive from the third pouch and the superior parathyroids from the fourth. The first pouch forms the middle ear and auditory tube and the second arch is unrelated."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 46-year-old data entry worker has night-time tingling in her right hand that wakes her. Holding both wrists in full flexion for 40 seconds reproduces numbness in the thumb, index and middle fingers. She has no neck pain. Which additional finding would most likely be present on examination?", "options": {"A": "Weak grip with a positive Froment sign and numbness over the ulnar ring and little fingers", "B": "Weak thumb abduction with preserved sensation over the thenar eminence from palmar cutaneous branch sparing", "C": "Wrist drop with numbness over the dorsal first web space and weak triceps on resisted extension", "D": "Forearm pain with weak pronation and numbness including the thenar eminence from proximal median nerve entrapment", "E": "Weak elbow flexion with numbness over the lateral forearm and a diminished biceps reflex"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Weak thumb abduction with preserved sensation over the thenar eminence from palmar cutaneous branch sparing): A positive Phalen test with numbness in the median digits indicates carpal tunnel syndrome. The palmar cutaneous branch leaves the median nerve proximal to the carpal tunnel, so sensation over the thenar eminence is spared while thumb abduction may weaken. Proximal median entrapment would involve the thenar skin and forearm muscles."}, {"id": 352, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 16-year-old tall, thin boy develops sudden left pleuritic chest pain and mild dyspnea while sitting at a desk after a lacrosse game last week. He has no history of trauma or lung disease. Breath sounds are decreased on the left with hyperresonance to percussion. The radiograph shown confirms the diagnosis. Which process most likely caused the condition?", "options": {"A": "Erosion of a tuberculous cavity through the visceral pleura, allowing a bronchopleural fistula to form", "B": "Rupture of a congenital bronchogenic cyst, allowing cyst air to enter the adjacent pleural space", "C": "Rupture of subpleural apical blebs, allowing alveolar air to escape into the pleural space", "D": "Traumatic laceration of visceral pleura by a fractured rib fragment during minor contact sports", "E": "Rupture of emphysematous bullae from alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency with panacinar destruction of lung tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rupture of subpleural apical blebs, allowing alveolar air to escape into the pleural space): Primary spontaneous pneumothorax typically affects tall, thin young males without lung disease, and rupture of apical subpleural blebs is the usual mechanism. Trauma is tempting because of the recent sport, but there was no injury and no rib fracture. Emphysema and tuberculosis require underlying disease that is not described.", "image": "question_images/q_352.png"}, {"id": 353, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 71-year-old man has low back pain and an elevated PSA of 86 ng/mL. Rectal examination reveals a hard, irregular, fixed prostate nodule. Lumbar spine radiographs show multiple sclerotic lesions in vertebral bodies, while chest radiograph and abdominal imaging show no lung or liver metastases. Which route of spread best explains the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Direct extension through the pelvic floor into obturator internus and then into the ischial bones", "B": "Spread through the inferior vena cava to the pulmonary capillary bed before reaching vertebral marrow", "C": "Hematogenous seeding through the portal vein carrying tumor emboli to the liver and bone", "D": "Retrograde flow through the valveless vertebral venous plexus that communicates with the prostatic venous plexus", "E": "Lymphatic drainage through superficial inguinal nodes into the thoracic duct and then to bone marrow"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Retrograde flow through the valveless vertebral venous plexus that communicates with the prostatic venous plexus): Prostatic veins connect with the valveless Batson vertebral plexus, so raised abdominal pressure can carry tumor cells to the lumbar spine and sacrum while sparing lung and liver. This explains osteoblastic lesions with a high PSA. Pelvic lymphatics drain to obturator and iliac nodes, not inguinal nodes."}, {"id": 354, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old man felt a sudden snap in his right posterior ankle while sprinting for a bus. He recently completed a fluoroquinolone course for sinusitis. Examination shows a palpable gap 4 cm above the heel, and the foot does not plantar flex when the calf is squeezed. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Plantaris tendon rupture, causing sudden calf pain from a thin accessory tendon", "B": "Complete Achilles tendon rupture in the watershed zone of poor blood supply", "C": "Gastrocnemius medial head tear, from sudden stretch of the muscle belly in mid-calf", "D": "Deep vein thrombosis of the calf, producing pain, swelling and tenderness over the soleus", "E": "Tibialis posterior tendon rupture, causing medial ankle pain and acquired flatfoot over time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Complete Achilles tendon rupture in the watershed zone of poor blood supply): A sudden snap, a palpable gap about 4 cm above the calcaneus in the hypovascular zone, a positive Thompson test and recent fluoroquinolone exposure indicate complete rupture of the Achilles tendon. A plantaris or gastrocnemius tear causes calf pain but plantar flexion still occurs on the squeeze test."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A study of a common outcome, obesity, with prevalence near 30 percent among 3,000 children reports an odds ratio of 3.0 for daily sugar-sweetened beverage intake. A journalist writes that daily intake triples the risk of obesity. Which statement best explains why this interpretation is flawed?", "options": {"A": "Odds ratios cannot be calculated when prevalence is above ten percent because the denominator becomes unstable", "B": "Confounding by age inflates the odds ratio, so the figure cannot reflect any real association", "C": "Odds ratios understate relative risk for frequent outcomes, so the risk more than triples", "D": "Odds ratios are valid only in case-control studies so a cross-sectional estimate cannot be interpreted", "E": "Odds exceed risks when events are frequent, so the ratio of odds overstates the risk ratio"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Odds exceed risks when events are frequent, so the ratio of odds overstates the risk ratio): When an outcome is frequent, the odds are noticeably larger than the risk, so the odds ratio moves further from 1 than the risk ratio and overstates it. A journalist reading 3.0 as tripled risk therefore exaggerates the effect. Odds ratios can be computed in any design, and the direction of distortion is overstatement, not understatement."}, {"id": 356, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 26-year-old transgender woman begins gender-affirming therapy with oral estradiol and spironolactone. She has no cardiovascular or renal disease and takes no other medications. Her primary physician plans follow-up laboratory monitoring. Which laboratory test is most important to check because of the antiandrogen's mechanism of action?", "options": {"A": "Serum calcium, since the drug inhibits distal tubular calcium reabsorption and can cause symptomatic hypocalcemia", "B": "Serum glucose, since the drug impairs insulin secretion by pancreatic beta cells and produces hyperglycemia", "C": "Serum potassium, since aldosterone receptor antagonism reduces renal potassium excretion in the collecting duct", "D": "Serum uric acid, since the diuretic effect reduces proximal tubular urate secretion and can precipitate gout", "E": "Serum magnesium, since the drug acts at the loop of Henle and causes magnesium wasting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Serum potassium, since aldosterone receptor antagonism reduces renal potassium excretion in the collecting duct): Spironolactone blocks aldosterone receptors in the collecting duct and also lowers androgen action, which is why it is used in this setting. Because it reduces potassium excretion, hyperkalemia is the key risk and potassium should be monitored. Loop and thiazide diuretics produce the other electrolyte changes."}, {"id": 357, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 67-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease and type 2 diabetes has edema, serum potassium 5.7 mEq/L, and phosphate 6.0 mg/dL. She enjoys bananas, potatoes, and cola, and uses a potassium based salt substitute. A dietitian reviews her diet. Which nutrition plan is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "Replace saturated fat with potassium rich plants such as bananas and potatoes for cardiovascular protection", "B": "Limit potassium, phosphorus, and sodium while keeping protein moderate and carbohydrate controlled to slow disease progression", "C": "Restrict dietary fat and encourage dairy products to supply calcium while limiting potassium rich fruits", "D": "Encourage large daily fluid volumes and whole grains to promote renal flushing and supply dietary fiber", "E": "Raise protein intake and use potassium chloride seasoning to offset albumin losses and lower sodium intake"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Limit potassium, phosphorus, and sodium while keeping protein moderate and carbohydrate controlled to slow disease progression): Advanced kidney disease calls for restriction of potassium, phosphorus, and sodium, with moderate protein and glycemic control to reduce the filtration burden. Potassium chloride salt substitutes and high potassium plant foods are harmful with a potassium of 5.7 mEq/L. Dairy and whole grains add phosphorus that she cannot excrete."}, {"id": 358, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 41-year-old woman with untreated Graves disease develops temperature 40.1 C, pulse 164/min, agitation, and vomiting the day after an appendectomy. Propranolol and fluids are started. A potassium iodide solution is planned. Which additional drug should be given before the iodide to prevent iodine from fueling more hormone synthesis?", "options": {"A": "Lithium carbonate, which blocks thyroid hormone release from the gland and adds iodine-independent suppression", "B": "Cholestyramine, which binds enterohepatic thyroid hormone in the gut and lowers circulating hormone levels", "C": "Propylthiouracil, which blocks thyroid peroxidase organification and also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3", "D": "Radioactive iodine, which destroys follicular cells within days and rapidly lowers circulating hormone levels", "E": "Hydrocortisone, which reduces conversion of T4 to T3 and covers relative adrenal insufficiency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Propylthiouracil, which blocks thyroid peroxidase organification and also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3): A thionamide must be given about an hour before iodine so that the iodine load cannot be used as new substrate for hormone synthesis. Propylthiouracil is favored in storm because it also blocks peripheral deiodination of T4. Hydrocortisone is added for the same conversion effect but does not stop synthesis."}, {"id": 359, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 9-month-old boy of Ashkenazi Jewish descent has lost the ability to sit, shows an exaggerated startle to noise, and tracks poorly with his eyes. Examination reveals hypotonia and a cherry-red macular spot, with no hepatosplenomegaly. Which enzyme deficiency most likely explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Glucocerebrosidase, causing glucocerebroside accumulation in macrophages with prominent hepatosplenomegaly and bone pain crises", "B": "Sphingomyelinase, causing sphingomyelin accumulation with hepatosplenomegaly and a cherry-red spot in Niemann-Pick disease", "C": "Hexosaminidase A, causing GM2 ganglioside accumulation within neuronal lysosomes and relentless neurodegeneration in infancy", "D": "Alpha-galactosidase A, causing ceramide trihexoside accumulation with painful neuropathy and renal failure in adults", "E": "Galactocerebrosidase, causing globoid cell accumulation with peripheral neuropathy and irritability but no macular changes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hexosaminidase A, causing GM2 ganglioside accumulation within neuronal lysosomes and relentless neurodegeneration in infancy): Tay-Sachs disease results from hexosaminidase A deficiency and presents with regression, hyperacusis, and a cherry-red macular spot without liver or spleen enlargement. Niemann-Pick disease also shows a cherry-red spot but causes hepatosplenomegaly, which is the key distinguishing detail."}, {"id": 360, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 41-year-old man has epigastric pain that improves after meals and wakes him at night. Endoscopy shows a sharply demarcated ulcer in the duodenal bulb, and stool antigen testing for Helicobacter pylori is positive. Biopsies show gastritis confined to the antrum. Which mechanism most directly explains this patient's high acid output?", "options": {"A": "Gastric outlet narrowing from scarring prolongs antral distension and sustains gastrin release", "B": "Reduced prostaglandin synthesis from cyclooxygenase inhibition removes protective mucus and bicarbonate secretion", "C": "Urease derived ammonia directly stimulates parietal cells to secrete additional hydrochloric acid", "D": "Multifocal atrophic gastritis destroys parietal cells and triggers compensatory gastrin elevation", "E": "Antral inflammation lowers somatostatin release, removing inhibition of gastrin and driving parietal cell stimulation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Antral inflammation lowers somatostatin release, removing inhibition of gastrin and driving parietal cell stimulation): Antral predominant Helicobacter gastritis reduces somatostatin-producing D cells, which releases gastrin from inhibition and increases parietal cell acid output, favoring duodenal ulcer. Atrophic pangastritis lowers acid output and instead raises gastric cancer risk, and the NSAID mechanism has no support in this history."}, {"id": 361, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 19-year-old college student develops mild yellow discoloration of the sclerae during a week of influenza and a restrictive diet. He feels well otherwise. Total bilirubin is 2.8 mg/dL with direct bilirubin 0.4 mg/dL. Hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, haptoglobin, AST, ALT, and alkaline phosphatase are normal. Urinalysis shows no bilirubin. Which condition is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Rotor syndrome, with impaired hepatic reuptake of conjugated bilirubin and a normal appearing liver on biopsy", "B": "Gilbert syndrome, with mildly reduced UGT1A1 activity unmasked by physiologic stress such as fasting", "C": "Hereditary spherocytosis, with erythrocyte membrane defects causing extravascular hemolysis and unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with reticulocytosis", "D": "Dubin Johnson syndrome, with defective canalicular export of conjugated bilirubin causing a dark pigmented liver", "E": "Crigler Najjar syndrome type I, with absent UGT1A1 activity causing severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia in infancy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gilbert syndrome, with mildly reduced UGT1A1 activity unmasked by physiologic stress such as fasting): Mild isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that appears with illness or reduced caloric intake, with normal liver enzymes and no hemolysis, is typical of Gilbert syndrome. Crigler Najjar type I produces severe neonatal disease, and the conjugated disorders would show a raised direct fraction. Normal reticulocytes and haptoglobin exclude hemolysis."}, {"id": 362, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 74-year-old woman presents with newly diagnosed nonvalvular atrial fibrillation. Her history includes hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and a transient ischemic attack two years ago. She has no heart failure or vascular disease. Which CHA2DS2-VASc score and plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Score of 4, so antiplatelet therapy with aspirin alone is adequate for stroke prevention", "B": "Score of 2, so cardioversion followed by observation without long-term anticoagulation is reasonable", "C": "Score of 7, so warfarin is the only acceptable choice for anticoagulation", "D": "Score of 5, so rate control alone is appropriate without any antithrombotic therapy", "E": "Score of 6, so long-term oral anticoagulation with a direct oral anticoagulant is indicated"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Score of 6, so long-term oral anticoagulation with a direct oral anticoagulant is indicated): Points are age 65 to 74 gives 1, female sex 1, hypertension 1, diabetes 1, and prior transient ischemic attack 2, totaling 6. A score this high clearly warrants oral anticoagulation, and a direct oral anticoagulant is preferred over warfarin in nonvalvular disease. Aspirin alone does not adequately prevent cardioembolic stroke."}, {"id": 363, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 24-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a stressful argument, with tingling around her mouth and in both hands and carpopedal spasm. Respiratory rate is 32/min and oxygen saturation is 99%. In the arterial blood gas shown, values have been obtained on room air. Which statement best explains her neuromuscular symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Hypocapnia causes true hypocalcemia through increased urinary calcium excretion within the first hours", "B": "Alkalemia shifts potassium into cells, producing severe hypokalemia and weakness of skeletal muscle", "C": "Reduced cerebral blood flow from hypocapnic vasoconstriction causing cortical ischemia and peripheral paresthesias", "D": "Respiratory muscle fatigue from sustained tachypnea producing lactic acidosis with tetanic muscle cramping", "E": "Alkalemia increases albumin binding of calcium, lowering ionized calcium and raising neuromuscular excitability"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Alkalemia increases albumin binding of calcium, lowering ionized calcium and raising neuromuscular excitability): Acute respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation makes albumin more negatively charged, so more calcium binds albumin and the ionized fraction falls, even though total calcium is normal. The result is perioral paresthesias and carpopedal spasm. Total body calcium is not lost in this setting, and renal calcium wasting would take much longer.", "image": "question_images/q_363.png"}, {"id": 364, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 28-year-old woman taking oral contraceptives develops swelling of the right leg and a proximal deep vein thrombosis. Her mother had a pulmonary embolism at age 45. Baseline PT and aPTT are normal. Which inherited abnormality is the most common cause of thrombophilia in people of European ancestry?", "options": {"A": "Point mutation making factor V resistant to inactivation by activated protein C", "B": "Protein S deficiency reducing the cofactor activity needed for protein C function", "C": "Prothrombin gene mutation increasing plasma prothrombin concentration and thrombin generation capacity", "D": "Antithrombin deficiency reducing inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa by heparin cofactor activity", "E": "Protein C deficiency reducing inactivation of factors Va and VIIIa on endothelial surfaces"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Point mutation making factor V resistant to inactivation by activated protein C): Factor V Leiden, a substitution that makes factor Va resistant to cleavage by activated protein C, is the most common inherited thrombophilia, found in a notable fraction of people of European descent. It typically presents with venous thrombosis, often with a trigger such as estrogen. Prothrombin G20210A is next most common, while the natural anticoagulant deficiencies are much rarer."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old man who plays weekend basketball felt a sudden pop and a blow to the back of his left ankle during a jump. He can walk with a limp but cannot rise onto his toes on that leg. A palpable gap is present above the calcaneus. What result of the calf squeeze test is expected?", "options": {"A": "Absent plantar flexion of the foot, indicating loss of continuity of the Achilles tendon", "B": "Pain with passive dorsiflexion of the foot but normal plantar flexion, indicating tendinopathy rather than rupture", "C": "Brisk plantar flexion of the foot, which is preserved by the intact tibialis posterior tendon", "D": "Anterior translation of the tibia on the femur, indicating an associated cruciate ligament injury", "E": "Plantar flexion that is limited and painful, indicating chronic Achilles tendinopathy with partial thickness degeneration"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Absent plantar flexion of the foot, indicating loss of continuity of the Achilles tendon): In the Thompson test, squeezing the calf of a prone patient normally plantar flexes the foot, and a complete rupture abolishes that movement. A sudden pop, a palpable gap and loss of toe rise support rupture. Weak residual plantar flexion from other flexors can occur, but the squeeze response is absent."}, {"id": 366, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 62-year-old woman, ten years past menopause, sustains a vertebral compression fracture after lifting a grocery bag. Dual energy x-ray absorptiometry shows a T-score of minus 3.1 with normal serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase. Which cellular mechanism best explains the accelerated bone loss that followed estrogen decline?", "options": {"A": "Impaired mineralization of osteoid from vitamin D deficiency, with widened unmineralized seams", "B": "Rising RANKL signaling with falling osteoprotegerin, favoring osteoclast formation and prolonged osteoclast survival", "C": "Defective type I collagen synthesis producing thin cortices and brittle trabeculae from birth", "D": "Loss of osteoclast acidification from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency, producing dense fragile bone", "E": "Excess parathyroid hormone driving osteoclast resorption and subperiosteal bone loss in the hands"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rising RANKL signaling with falling osteoprotegerin, favoring osteoclast formation and prolonged osteoclast survival): Estrogen normally restrains RANKL expression and boosts osteoprotegerin, the decoy receptor for RANKL. With estrogen loss osteoclasts form and live longer, so resorption outpaces formation. Normal calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase argue against osteomalacia or hyperparathyroidism."}, {"id": 367, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A newborn boy weighing 4.6 kg is delivered after a prolonged second stage with shoulder dystocia. Examination shows the right arm hanging at the side, adducted and internally rotated, with the elbow extended and forearm pronated. Grasp is intact and the Moro reflex is asymmetric. Which structure is most likely injured?", "options": {"A": "Lower trunk with C8 and T1 roots, injured by excessive upward traction of the arm", "B": "Lateral cord with musculocutaneous and lateral pectoral branches, injured by lateral neck traction", "C": "Upper trunk with C5 and C6 roots, injured by excessive lateral traction of the neck", "D": "Posterior cord with radial and axillary branches, injured by compression beneath the clavicle", "E": "Long thoracic nerve from C5 to C7, injured by stretch during delivery of the shoulder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Upper trunk with C5 and C6 roots, injured by excessive lateral traction of the neck): Excessive lateral traction of the neck during shoulder dystocia stretches C5 and C6 in the upper trunk, producing the waiter's tip posture with intact grasp, typical of Erb palsy. Lower trunk injury would instead weaken the hand and cause a claw posture, sometimes with Horner syndrome. Cord injuries are unusual in birth trauma."}, {"id": 368, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 69-year-old retired shipyard worker has progressive dyspnea and bibasilar crackles. Chest radiograph shows lower lobe interstitial fibrosis and calcified plaques along the diaphragmatic pleura. A lung biopsy is performed. He quit smoking twenty years ago. Which histologic finding would most strongly support the occupational diagnosis in this man?", "options": {"A": "Golden-brown beaded rods with iron-protein coats within fibrotic alveolar septa, called ferruginous bodies", "B": "Noncaseating granulomas with giant cells and Schaumann bodies distributed along bronchovascular bundles", "C": "Coal macules with focal emphysema formed by carbon-laden macrophages around respiratory bronchioles", "D": "Concentric whorled collagen nodules containing birefringent particles under polarized light in the upper lobes", "E": "Fibroblast foci at the leading edge of subpleural fibrosis with a temporally heterogeneous pattern"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Golden-brown beaded rods with iron-protein coats within fibrotic alveolar septa, called ferruginous bodies): Lower lobe fibrosis with calcified pleural plaques in a shipyard worker indicates asbestosis. Asbestos fibers coated with iron and protein form ferruginous bodies, the characteristic histologic marker. Idiopathic fibrosis also affects the lower lobes but lacks these bodies and the pleural plaques."}, {"id": 369, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 58-year-old man develops a waddling gait three weeks after hip surgery. When he stands on his left leg alone, his pelvis drops on the right side. He rises from a chair and climbs stairs without difficulty, and thigh adduction strength is normal. Which finding best explains this gait?", "options": {"A": "Left superior gluteal nerve injury weakening the left hip abductors that stabilize the pelvis in stance", "B": "Left obturator nerve injury weakening the adductors and producing a circumducting gait with a wide stance", "C": "Left femoral nerve injury weakening quadriceps and causing the knee to buckle during early stance", "D": "Right superior gluteal nerve injury, which causes the pelvis to drop toward the left in stance", "E": "Left inferior gluteal nerve injury weakening the gluteus maximus and impairing rising from a seated position"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left superior gluteal nerve injury weakening the left hip abductors that stabilize the pelvis in stance): A positive Trendelenburg sign means the abductors of the stance leg fail to hold the pelvis level, so the pelvis drops on the opposite side. Dropping on the right while standing on the left localizes the lesion to the left superior gluteal nerve and gluteus medius. Normal stairs and chair rising argue against inferior gluteal nerve injury."}, {"id": 370, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 19-year-old woman has severe nodulocystic acne with early scarring that has not improved after six months of oral doxycycline and topical therapy. She is sexually active and uses condoms inconsistently. Her physician plans a drug that requires enrollment in a risk management program. Which medication is the physician most likely prescribing?", "options": {"A": "Oral doxycycline, a tetracycline that discolors developing teeth and affects bone growth in a fetus", "B": "Oral isotretinoin, a retinoid causing craniofacial, cardiac and central nervous system malformations in exposed fetuses", "C": "Spironolactone, an antiandrogen that can feminize the genitalia of an exposed male fetus", "D": "Oral methotrexate, a folate antagonist causing fetal loss and neural tube malformations", "E": "Topical tretinoin, a retinoid cream applied nightly to normalize follicular keratinization and reduce comedones"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Oral isotretinoin, a retinoid causing craniofacial, cardiac and central nervous system malformations in exposed fetuses): Severe scarring acne unresponsive to antibiotics is treated with isotretinoin, a potent teratogen that requires pregnancy testing, contraception and registry enrollment. Spironolactone raises fetal feminization concerns, but it is not tied to a mandatory risk program, and topical tretinoin has minimal systemic exposure."}, {"id": 371, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with a body mass index of 46 is evaluated preoperatively for bariatric surgery. She reports daytime somnolence and morning headaches, and has no lung disease or cough. Awake arterial blood gas results on room air are shown in the panel. Which mechanism best explains the abnormalities?", "options": {"A": "Ventilation perfusion mismatch from dependent atelectasis with a widened alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient", "B": "Right-to-left shunt from collapsed alveoli with hypoxemia unresponsive to supplemental oxygen and low carbon dioxide", "C": "Alveolar hypoventilation from obesity with chronic CO2 retention and a normal alveolar-arterial gradient", "D": "Diffusion limitation from interstitial fibrosis with exertional desaturation and a widened alveolar-arterial gradient", "E": "Increased dead space from pulmonary embolism with hypocapnia and a widened alveolar-arterial gradient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Alveolar hypoventilation from obesity with chronic CO2 retention and a normal alveolar-arterial gradient): The gas shows chronic respiratory acidosis, with a high PaCO2 of 58 and a bicarbonate raised to compensate. The alveolar-arterial gradient is about 15, which is normal for her age, so the hypoxemia comes from hypoventilation rather than a gas exchange defect. This is obesity hypoventilation. The other mechanisms would widen the gradient or lower the carbon dioxide.", "image": "question_images/q_371.png"}, {"id": 372, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 72-year-old woman has had burning pain along the right chest wall for two days. Today clustered vesicles on an erythematous base appeared in a band that stops at the midline. She has no fever and her only medical problem is hypertension. Which treatment, started now, best shortens the illness and lowers the risk of postherpetic neuralgia?", "options": {"A": "Oral prednisone alone for two weeks to reduce inflammation along the affected dermatome", "B": "Oral valacyclovir started within seventy two hours to limit viral replication and nerve injury", "C": "Topical acyclovir ointment applied to each vesicle cluster to limit local viral replication", "D": "Gabapentin titrated to full dose to prevent sensitization of the dorsal horn neurons", "E": "Oral amoxicillin with clavulanate to cover secondary bacterial infection of the vesicles"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Oral valacyclovir started within seventy two hours to limit viral replication and nerve injury): Herpes zoster is treated with an oral nucleoside antiviral started as early as possible, ideally within 72 hours of rash onset, which shortens the course and reduces neuralgia risk. Topical antivirals penetrate poorly, and gabapentin treats pain but does not address viral replication."}, {"id": 373, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis presents with hematemesis and a heart rate of 118/min. Blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg, hemoglobin is 7.2 g/dL, and he has ascites. After establishing large-bore access and starting octreotide, which additional intervention is associated with reduced infection and mortality in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Short-course intravenous ceftriaxone, because bacterial translocation worsens rebleeding risk and survival in cirrhotic bleeding", "B": "Transfusion of red cells to a target hemoglobin above 10 g/dL to restore oxygen delivery", "C": "Continuous intravenous pantoprazole to reduce rebleeding from the esophageal varices by raising gastric pH", "D": "Fresh frozen plasma infusion to normalize the INR before urgent endoscopic band ligation", "E": "Lactulose therapy to lower ammonia absorption and prevent hepatic encephalopathy during the bleed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short-course intravenous ceftriaxone, because bacterial translocation worsens rebleeding risk and survival in cirrhotic bleeding): Antibiotic prophylaxis, typically ceftriaxone, reduces bacterial infection, rebleeding, and mortality in cirrhotic variceal hemorrhage and is standard with vasoactive therapy and endoscopy. A restrictive transfusion strategy is favored because overtransfusion raises portal pressure and rebleeding. Proton pump inhibitors do not treat varices, and plasma does not reliably correct the coagulopathy of cirrhosis."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 62-year-old man who has smoked two packs daily for decades and drinks whiskey nightly reports four months of progressive difficulty swallowing, now affecting soft foods, with a 9 kg weight loss. Barium study shows an irregular stricture in the mid-esophagus. Biopsy of this lesion is most likely to show which tumor type?", "options": {"A": "Leiomyoma arising from smooth muscle, typically benign with intact overlying mucosa on endoscopy", "B": "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from the stratified epithelium lining the middle esophagus", "C": "Diffuse large B cell lymphoma arising from submucosal lymphoid tissue in the proximal esophagus", "D": "Small cell carcinoma arising from neuroendocrine cells, typically with paraneoplastic hormone secretion", "E": "Adenocarcinoma arising from Barrett metaplasia after chronic reflux, usually in the distal esophagus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Squamous cell carcinoma arising from the stratified epithelium lining the middle esophagus): Heavy smoking and alcohol use are the main risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma, which predominates in the upper and middle esophagus and presents with progressive dysphagia and weight loss. Adenocarcinoma is the tempting answer but arises from Barrett metaplasia in the distal third, in patients with chronic reflux and obesity, which this man lacks."}, {"id": 375, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 46-year-old woman develops a weak, breathy voice the day after total thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy shows the left vocal cord fixed in a paramedian position with incomplete glottic closure, and she has no stridor. Which structure was most likely injured during the operation?", "options": {"A": "External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, which tenses the vocal cords via cricothyroid", "B": "Glossopharyngeal nerve, which supplies stylopharyngeus and sensation from the posterior pharynx", "C": "Internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, which carries sensation above the vocal folds", "D": "Left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid", "E": "Right recurrent laryngeal nerve, which loops around the right subclavian artery"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid): The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies abductor and adductor muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid, and it runs near the inferior thyroid artery, so it is vulnerable in thyroid surgery. Ipsilateral injury leaves the cord paralyzed and breathy voice results. Injury to the external superior laryngeal branch causes loss of pitch range instead."}, {"id": 376, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 4-month-old girl has progressive hypotonia, feeding difficulty, and a large tongue. Chest radiography shows marked cardiomegaly, and echocardiography shows hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Her glucose is normal, with no ketosis and no lactic acidosis. Which enzyme deficiency best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase, causing exercise intolerance with cramps and myoglobinuria usually appearing in adolescents", "B": "Debranching enzyme, causing limit dextrin accumulation with hepatomegaly and fasting hypoglycemia in young children", "C": "Branching enzyme, causing abnormal long-chain glycogen deposits and progressive cirrhosis beginning in the early infant period", "D": "Glucose-6-phosphatase, causing impaired gluconeogenesis with severe fasting hypoglycemia and lactic acidosis in infancy", "E": "Lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase, causing glycogen accumulation inside lysosomes of cardiac and skeletal muscle cells"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase, causing glycogen accumulation inside lysosomes of cardiac and skeletal muscle cells): Pompe disease is a lysosomal glycogen storage disorder in which acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency causes glycogen buildup in the heart and muscle, producing cardiomegaly, hypotonia, and macroglossia with normal glucose. The hypoglycemic forms involve the liver enzymes rather than the lysosome."}, {"id": 377, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 3-year-old boy has fever and irritability. Examination shows perioral crusting and diffuse tender erythema with flaccid blisters that rupture, leaving moist red skin, most prominent in the axillae and groin. The oral mucosa is spared, and gentle rubbing causes the outer skin to slough. Which molecule is the direct target of the causative bacterial toxin?", "options": {"A": "BP180, a hemidesmosomal collagen anchoring basal keratinocytes to the underlying basement membrane", "B": "Keratin 14, an intermediate filament giving structural strength to basal keratinocytes", "C": "Desmoglein 1, a desmosomal cadherin anchoring keratinocytes in the superficial granular layer of the epidermis", "D": "Desmoglein 3, a desmosomal cadherin anchoring basal and suprabasal keratinocytes in mucosal epithelium", "E": "Type VII collagen, forming anchoring fibrils that secure the basement membrane to the dermis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Desmoglein 1, a desmosomal cadherin anchoring keratinocytes in the superficial granular layer of the epidermis): Staphylococcal exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1, which is concentrated in the superficial epidermis, producing a split in the granular layer and sparing the mucosa where desmoglein 3 compensates. Desmoglein 3 and basement membrane proteins are the targets of other blistering diseases."}, {"id": 378, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma is started on a topical drug that lowers intraocular pressure by reducing aqueous humor formation. Gonioscopy shows open angles. Aqueous humor is normally secreted by which of the following structures before it drains through the trabecular meshwork?", "options": {"A": "Nonpigmented epithelium of the ciliary body processes, powered by carbonic anhydrase activity", "B": "Corneal endothelium, pumping fluid out of the stroma to maintain corneal clarity", "C": "Canal of Schlemm, collecting fluid and returning it to episcleral veins", "D": "Retinal pigment epithelium, transporting subretinal fluid toward the choroid and maintaining attachment", "E": "Lens epithelium, secreting fluid from the capsule into the posterior chamber"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nonpigmented epithelium of the ciliary body processes, powered by carbonic anhydrase activity): Aqueous humor is made by the ciliary epithelium of the ciliary processes, with carbonic anhydrase and sodium transport driving secretion, which is why beta blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower pressure. Fluid then flows from the posterior to the anterior chamber and drains at the angle, so the canal of Schlemm is an outflow structure."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 63-year-old man discharged after a heart failure admission says he understands his medications. When asked to explain how he will take furosemide, he says he takes it when ankles swell or maybe daily. He avoids reading forms and asks his wife to sign them. Which teaching approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Ask whether he has questions and take his statement that he understands as adequate confirmation", "B": "Refer him for formal cognitive testing and delay further medication teaching until results are reviewed", "C": "Use plain language with pictorial aids, limit key points, and confirm understanding through teach-back", "D": "Direct teaching to his wife alone, since she signs forms and can manage his medications", "E": "Give a detailed handout covering furosemide dosing, side effects and daily weights to review at home"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use plain language with pictorial aids, limit key points, and confirm understanding through teach-back): Avoiding forms, relying on a spouse and a vague account of dosing suggest limited health literacy, which is not a sign of low intelligence. Plain language, pictures, a few key messages and teach-back verify real understanding. Handouts and a bare statement of understanding do not, and cognitive testing is not indicated by these features alone."}, {"id": 380, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 55-year-old man has widespread painful skin sloughing involving 45 percent of his body surface and mucosal erosions that began 10 days after starting a sulfonamide antibiotic. Nikolsky sign is positive. He is hemodynamically stable. Which action is the most important initial step?", "options": {"A": "Debride all loose epidermis in the clinic and apply topical silver sulfadiazine to the denuded areas", "B": "Start intravenous cyclosporine before any change in medications, because immunosuppression halts epidermal necrosis", "C": "Obtain skin patch testing to confirm the culprit drug before withdrawing any current medication", "D": "Stop the sulfonamide and transfer to a burn or intensive care unit for supportive care", "E": "Begin high dose oral prednisone to suppress the cytotoxic T cell mediated epidermal injury"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the sulfonamide and transfer to a burn or intensive care unit for supportive care): Early withdrawal of the culprit drug and care in a burn or intensive care unit, with fluid, wound and eye management, lowers mortality in toxic epidermal necrolysis. Systemic steroids have no proven benefit and raise infection risk, and silver sulfadiazine is a sulfonamide that should be avoided in this setting."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 67-year-old woman presents with crushing chest pain and dyspnea one day after the sudden death of her husband. Troponin is mildly elevated and the electrocardiogram shows anterior ST elevation. Coronary angiography shows no obstructive lesions. Echocardiography shows apical akinesis with basal hyperkinesis. She takes a statin. Which diagnosis best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Stress cardiomyopathy with catecholamine mediated transient stunning of the left ventricular apical myocardium", "B": "Vasospastic angina with transient epicardial coronary spasm and reversible anterior ST elevation changes", "C": "Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with dynamic apical obliteration and giant inverted precordial T waves", "D": "Viral myocarditis with lymphocytic infiltration producing diffuse global hypokinesis and a markedly raised troponin", "E": "Acute anterior myocardial infarction from spontaneous coronary dissection with spontaneous reperfusion before angiography"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stress cardiomyopathy with catecholamine mediated transient stunning of the left ventricular apical myocardium): Chest pain after severe emotional stress, ST elevation, mild troponin rise, normal coronaries, and apical ballooning with basal hyperkinesis define stress cardiomyopathy, driven by catecholamine surge. Wall motion abnormalities exceed a single coronary territory. Myocarditis, apical hypertrophy, and vasospasm do not produce this pattern."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 38-year-old nonsmoking man has dyspnea, and chest radiography shows basilar predominant hyperlucency. Aminotransferases are mildly elevated. A liver biopsy shows periodic acid Schiff positive, diastase resistant globules in periportal hepatocytes. Which mechanism most directly explains his liver injury, as distinct from his lung disease?", "options": {"A": "Unopposed neutrophil elastase activity degrading hepatic extracellular matrix because of absent circulating protease inhibitor", "B": "Accumulation of misfolded mutant protein within the hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, causing toxic gain of function", "C": "Copper deposition within lysosomes after impaired biliary excretion, producing oxidative damage and cytoplasmic inclusions", "D": "Fatty acid overload with insulin resistance producing macrovesicular steatosis and ballooning of hepatocytes", "E": "Iron overload within periportal hepatocytes from low hepcidin, generating free radicals and progressive fibrosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accumulation of misfolded mutant protein within the hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, causing toxic gain of function): Mutant alpha-1 antitrypsin polymerizes in the endoplasmic reticulum, so the liver is injured by toxic accumulation while the lung is injured by lack of elastase inhibition. The PAS positive diastase resistant globules are the retained protein."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 29-year-old woman at 34 weeks gestation reports worsening low back pain at the end of the day, relieved by lying down. She has no fever, urinary symptoms, or neurologic deficits. Her gait is slowed with the abdomen protruding forward, and palpation shows bilateral lumbar paraspinal hypertonicity. Which postural adaptation best explains her pain?", "options": {"A": "Increased thoracic kyphosis alone from breast enlargement, with a normal lumbar curve and neutral pelvis", "B": "Backward shift of the center of gravity with decreased lumbar lordosis straining paraspinal muscles", "C": "Lateral curve development from unilateral leg length discrepancy caused by progesterone-mediated ligament laxity", "D": "Posterior pelvic tilt flattening the lumbar curve and stretching the hamstrings and paraspinal muscles", "E": "Forward shift of the center of gravity with increased lumbar lordosis straining back extensor muscles"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Forward shift of the center of gravity with increased lumbar lordosis straining back extensor muscles): The enlarging uterus moves the center of gravity anteriorly, so the lumbar spine extends to compensate and the sacral base angle increases. The sustained lordosis loads the lumbar back extensors and facets, producing end-of-day pain that eases when recumbent. A flattened lumbar curve from posterior pelvic tilt would be the opposite of the usual third-trimester adaptation."}, {"id": 384, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 29-year-old graduate student has tension-type headaches with C4 restricted in extension, right sidebending and right rotation, and freer in flexion, left sidebending and left rotation. The physician plans muscle energy treatment. How should the segment be positioned and what is the direction of the patient's effort?", "options": {"A": "Engage the barrier in extension, right sidebending and right rotation, with the patient pushing into restriction", "B": "Place the segment in flexion, left sidebending and left rotation, held passively for ninety seconds", "C": "Place the segment in flexion, left sidebending and left rotation, with the patient pushing toward extension", "D": "Engage the barrier in extension, right sidebending and right rotation, with the patient pushing toward freedom", "E": "Apply a rapid low amplitude thrust at the barrier in extension, right sidebending and right rotation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Engage the barrier in extension, right sidebending and right rotation, with the patient pushing toward freedom): Muscle energy is a direct technique: the physician engages the restrictive barrier in all planes and the patient makes a gentle isometric contraction toward the direction of freedom against a counterforce. After relaxation, the new barrier is engaged. Passive positioning in freedom is counterstrain or functional technique, and a rapid thrust is HVLA."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 24-year-old woman at 31 weeks of gestation presents for a prenatal visit. She received a Tdap vaccine four years ago before college and completed her childhood series. She has no fever and no vaccine allergy. She asks whether another pertussis-containing vaccine is needed now. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Give DTaP now, since its higher pertussis antigen content improves transplacental antibody transfer", "B": "Give Td now and add acellular pertussis later, since combined vaccines are avoided during gestation", "C": "Delay Tdap until immediately after delivery, since vaccination before term carries a risk of fetal harm", "D": "Withhold Tdap because her dose four years ago still provides adequate protection for the newborn", "E": "Give Tdap now, since each pregnancy warrants a dose transferring maternal antibodies to the fetus"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Give Tdap now, since each pregnancy warrants a dose transferring maternal antibodies to the fetus): Tdap is recommended during every pregnancy, ideally between 27 and 36 weeks, regardless of previous doses, because maternal antibodies cross the placenta and protect the newborn before its own vaccinations. The pediatric formulation is not used in adults, and inactivated vaccines are not harmful to the fetus."}, {"id": 386, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a 40 pack-year history reports productive cough for at least three months in each of the past three years. He is cyanotic and has peripheral edema. Hemoglobin is 18.5 g/dL, and arterial PaCO2 is 56 mm Hg. He has a mild childhood eczema history. Which pathologic finding is most characteristic of his airway disease?", "options": {"A": "Hypertrophy of bronchial submucosal mucous glands, with a Reid index greater than 50 percent", "B": "Destruction of alveolar walls distal to terminal bronchioles, producing enlarged airspaces without fibrosis", "C": "Permanent dilation of airways with transmural inflammation and necrotizing destruction of the bronchial wall", "D": "Fibroblast foci and honeycomb change in subpleural regions with patchy interstitial scarring", "E": "Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals within mucus plugs with eosinophil-rich airway inflammation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hypertrophy of bronchial submucosal mucous glands, with a Reid index greater than 50 percent): Cough with sputum on most days for three months in two consecutive years defines chronic bronchitis, and the cyanosis, edema, polycythemia and hypercapnia describe the blue bloater phenotype. The hallmark is mucous gland hypertrophy, measured by a Reid index above 50 percent. Alveolar destruction defines emphysema, and the eczema history does not shift the diagnosis to asthma here."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 34-year-old man is brought in from a house fire with headache and confusion. Pulse oximetry reads 99%, and arterial PaO2 is 98 mm Hg. Co-oximetry shows 35% carboxyhemoglobin. Compared with a healthy person, which combination best describes his arterial oxygen tension, oxygen content, and hemoglobin dissociation curve?", "options": {"A": "Normal oxygen tension, reduced oxygen content, and an unchanged curve position, as in severe anemia", "B": "Preserved PaO2, lowered total oxygen content, and a leftward shift with impaired unloading", "C": "Reduced oxygen tension, reduced oxygen content, and a rightward shift of the dissociation curve", "D": "Normal oxygen tension, normal oxygen content, and a rightward shift improving tissue unloading", "E": "Reduced oxygen tension from competitive displacement, reduced content, and a leftward shifted curve"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Preserved PaO2, lowered total oxygen content, and a leftward shift with impaired unloading): Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with roughly 200 times the affinity of oxygen, lowering the fraction available to carry oxygen without changing dissolved oxygen, so PaO2 stays normal. Remaining binding sites hold oxygen more tightly, shifting the curve left and impairing tissue unloading. Standard pulse oximetry reads carboxyhemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin and is falsely reassuring."}, {"id": 388, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 68-year-old woman is on postoperative day two after hip arthroplasty and develops pneumonia. She is bedridden, has unilateral calf swelling and tenderness on the operated side, and her D-dimer is elevated. The physician wants to add a lymphatic technique. Which technique should be avoided until deep venous thrombosis is excluded?", "options": {"A": "Thoracic pump using rhythmic anterior chest compression with the physician at the head of the table", "B": "Thoracic inlet release using gentle fascial tension over the clavicles and first ribs while supine", "C": "Occipitoatlantal decompression using sustained gentle traction at the occiput with the patient supine", "D": "Pedal pump using rhythmic passive dorsiflexion and plantarflexion of both feet with the patient supine", "E": "Abdominal diaphragm release using gentle pressure beneath the costal margin during the exhalation phase"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pedal pump using rhythmic passive dorsiflexion and plantarflexion of both feet with the patient supine): Repeated passive movement of the feet could dislodge a clot from a suspected deep vein thrombosis, so the pedal pump is held until the thrombosis is excluded. The thoracic, abdominal, inlet, and occipital techniques do not manipulate the leg veins and can still be used to support lymph flow."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 45-year-old mechanic reports tingling in the ring and little fingers that worsens when he holds a phone with the elbow bent. He has weak finger abduction, a positive Froment sign and numbness over the dorsal ulnar side of the hand. Sensation over the medial forearm is spared. Where is the lesion most likely located?", "options": {"A": "Ulnar nerve compressed behind the medial epicondyle, proximal to the dorsal cutaneous branch origin", "B": "Median nerve compressed beneath the transverse carpal ligament, affecting the thenar muscles", "C": "Eighth cervical nerve root compressed at its foramen by a lateral cervical disc herniation", "D": "Ulnar nerve compressed in Guyon canal at the wrist, distal to the dorsal cutaneous branch", "E": "Lower trunk of the brachial plexus compressed by a cervical rib above the clavicle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ulnar nerve compressed behind the medial epicondyle, proximal to the dorsal cutaneous branch origin): Elbow flexion provoking symptoms, ulnar hand weakness and numbness of the dorsal ulnar hand place the lesion at the cubital tunnel, because the dorsal cutaneous branch leaves about 5 cm above the wrist. Guyon canal compression would spare the dorsal hand, and a root or lower trunk lesion would reduce medial forearm sensation."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has bloating and mild lower extremity edema. Palpation shows restricted motion of the thoracoabdominal diaphragm with tight crural attachments at L1 and L2. A student learns that diaphragm motion acts as a lymphatic pump. Which structure, located at this lumbar level, is most directly affected when crural motion is restricted?", "options": {"A": "Cisterna chyli, the dilated lymphatic sac that begins the thoracic duct", "B": "Right lymphatic duct, which drains the right upper body into the venous angle", "C": "Azygos vein, which drains the posterior thoracic wall into the superior vena cava", "D": "Hemiazygos vein, which ascends on the left and crosses to join the azygos", "E": "Inferior vena cava, which pierces the diaphragm at the caval opening near T8"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cisterna chyli, the dilated lymphatic sac that begins the thoracic duct): The cisterna chyli lies near L1 to L2 behind the right crus and collects abdominal lymph before the thoracic duct ascends through the aortic hiatus. Restricted crural motion reduces the pumping action that helps lymph move upward. The azygos systems and vena cava are veins, and the right lymphatic duct drains only the upper right body."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 74-year-old man with chronic venous insufficiency has bilateral pitting leg edema. Abdominal ultrasound for an unrelated reason showed a 5.5 cm infrarenal aortic aneurysm, and a pulsatile midline mass is felt. His physician wants osteopathic treatment to improve fluid return. Which technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Deep abdominal diaphragm release using sustained bimanual compression beneath the xiphoid process", "B": "Mesenteric lifting using deep bimanual elevation of the small bowel toward the umbilicus", "C": "Liver pump using rhythmic compression over the right lower ribs and upper abdomen", "D": "Abdominal lymphatic pump using rapid sustained compression of the anterior abdominal wall", "E": "Gentle pedal pump using slow passive plantar flexion and dorsiflexion of both ankles"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Gentle pedal pump using slow passive plantar flexion and dorsiflexion of both ankles): Techniques that compress the abdomen carry a risk of rupturing a pulsatile aneurysm, so they are avoided here. A pedal pump works at the ankles, away from the aorta, and still moves venous and lymphatic fluid toward the thoracic inlet."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 52-year-old man with low HDL cholesterol and elevated triglycerides is started on extended-release niacin. Within an hour of the first dose he reports intense facial flushing and warmth. He is advised to take aspirin 30 minutes beforehand. Which mediator is responsible for the flushing that aspirin prevents?", "options": {"A": "Serotonin released from enterochromaffin cells, acting on 5-HT receptors to dilate skin vessels", "B": "Prostaglandin D2 released from skin cells, acting on vasodilating receptors in cutaneous vessels", "C": "Histamine released from mast cells, acting on H1 receptors in cutaneous vessels", "D": "Bradykinin generated by kallikrein activation, causing endothelial nitric oxide release and vasodilation in cutaneous vessels", "E": "Substance P released from sensory nerve endings, producing neurogenic vasodilation in cutaneous vessels"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prostaglandin D2 released from skin cells, acting on vasodilating receptors in cutaneous vessels): Niacin activates a receptor on dermal Langerhans cells and keratinocytes, releasing prostaglandin D2 and E2 that dilate cutaneous vessels. Aspirin inhibits cyclooxygenase and so blunts the flush. Histamine, kinins, serotonin and substance P cause flushing in other settings but are not blocked by aspirin."}, {"id": 393, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 74-year-old man in a nursing home is evaluated for decreased oral intake and diarrhea for 4 days. He is orthostatic with dry mucous membranes. Serum creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 2.4 mg/dL. The urine studies shown were obtained before any fluids. Which of the following best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Acute interstitial nephritis from drug hypersensitivity with tubular inflammation and sterile pyuria", "B": "Postrenal obstruction from bladder outlet narrowing causing back pressure on the collecting system", "C": "Prerenal azotemia from volume depletion with preserved tubular function and avid sodium reabsorption", "D": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis from crescent formation with active urinary sediment and red cell casts", "E": "Acute tubular necrosis from ischemic injury with impaired sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prerenal azotemia from volume depletion with preserved tubular function and avid sodium reabsorption): Volume loss lowers renal perfusion, and healthy tubules respond by retaining sodium and water, giving a low urine sodium, low fractional excretion of sodium, concentrated urine and a high BUN to creatinine ratio. Acute tubular necrosis would instead show isosthenuric urine with a high urine sodium and muddy brown casts, because injured tubules cannot reabsorb sodium. Prompt fluid repletion should reverse this injury.", "image": "question_images/q_393.png"}, {"id": 394, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 27-year-old woman describes episodes beginning with a rising sensation in her stomach and an unpleasant burning smell. She then stares, smacks her lips, and fumbles with her clothing for about ninety seconds without responding. Confusion lasts ten minutes afterward, and she has no convulsion. Which seizure type and origin is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Absence seizure with abrupt onset from generalized thalamocortical oscillation and no postictal state", "B": "Generalized myoclonic seizure of juvenile onset occurring shortly after awakening from sleep", "C": "Hypermotor seizure arising from the frontal lobe, typically clustering during sleep with brief duration", "D": "Focal seizure with impaired awareness arising from the mesial temporal lobe structures", "E": "Focal seizure with visual phenomena arising from the occipital cortex and preserved awareness"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Focal seizure with impaired awareness arising from the mesial temporal lobe structures): An epigastric rising sensation, olfactory aura, oral automatisms, impaired awareness lasting more than a minute, and prolonged postictal confusion localize to the mesial temporal lobe, often with hippocampal sclerosis. Absence seizures are shorter, have no aura, and recover immediately. Frontal seizures are brief and often nocturnal."}, {"id": 395, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 16-year-old boy of African ancestry is admitted with severe pain in his back and chest. Hemoglobin electrophoresis confirms homozygous hemoglobin S disease. Which molecular change in the beta globin chain underlies hemoglobin polymer formation under deoxygenated conditions in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Substitution of lysine for glutamic acid at the sixth amino acid position", "B": "Substitution of valine for glutamic acid at the sixth amino acid position", "C": "Deletion of promoter sequences reducing transcription across the beta globin gene cluster", "D": "Frameshift deletion of four nucleotides in codons forty-one and forty-two of the gene", "E": "Point mutation creating a cryptic splice site within the first intron of the gene"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Substitution of valine for glutamic acid at the sixth amino acid position): Hemoglobin S results from a missense mutation replacing glutamic acid with valine at position 6 of the beta chain, creating a hydrophobic patch that promotes polymerization when deoxygenated. Substituting lysine at the same position produces hemoglobin C. Promoter, frameshift, and splice mutations cause thalassemia."}, {"id": 396, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 40-year-old man receives 500 mg of a drug intravenously. Plasma concentration extrapolated to time zero is 5 mg/L. The drug is highly lipophilic and binds extensively to tissue. What is the apparent volume of distribution, and what does it indicate?", "options": {"A": "3 liters, approximating plasma volume as expected for a drug bound to albumin", "B": "14 liters, matching extracellular fluid volume as expected for a large charged molecule", "C": "0.01 liters, reflecting confinement of the drug within the plasma compartment", "D": "100 liters, exceeding total body water and reflecting extensive sequestration outside plasma", "E": "42 liters, matching total body water as expected for a small water-soluble drug"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (100 liters, exceeding total body water and reflecting extensive sequestration outside plasma): The apparent volume equals the dose divided by the initial concentration, so 500 mg divided by 5 mg/L gives 100 L. That exceeds the roughly 42 L of total body water, which means most drug sits in tissue rather than plasma. Drugs confined to plasma show volumes near 3 L."}, {"id": 397, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 35-year-old woman at 33 weeks gestation receives osteopathic treatment for low back pain while lying supine on the table. After several minutes she becomes lightheaded, pale and diaphoretic, with blood pressure 84/50 mm Hg and heart rate 108/min. Symptoms resolve when she rolls onto her left side. Which change in treatment positioning best prevents recurrence?", "options": {"A": "Right lateral recumbent positioning so the uterus shifts away from the descending aorta during treatment", "B": "Supine positioning with the legs elevated on a bolster to increase venous return to the heart", "C": "Prone positioning with pillows under the abdomen to unload the inferior vena cava and aorta", "D": "Left lateral recumbent or seated positioning so the uterus does not compress the inferior vena cava", "E": "Trendelenburg tilt of the table to raise cerebral perfusion pressure during each treatment session"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Left lateral recumbent or seated positioning so the uterus does not compress the inferior vena cava): In late pregnancy the supine position lets the uterus compress the inferior vena cava, lowering venous return, cardiac output and blood pressure. Moving to a left lateral or seated position relieves the compression and can be used for most techniques. Leg elevation while supine leaves the uterine compression in place."}, {"id": 398, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with a 15-year history of type 2 diabetes has early satiety, postprandial bloating and nausea. A gastric emptying study confirms gastroparesis. An osteopathic physician evaluates sympathetic viscerosomatic reflexes. Which spinal levels are most likely to show paraspinal tissue texture changes related to this stomach dysfunction?", "options": {"A": "T1 to T4 paraspinal segments, which carry sympathetic outflow to the head, neck and eyes", "B": "T5 to T9 paraspinal segments, which carry sympathetic outflow to the stomach and upper gastrointestinal tract", "C": "T10 to T11 paraspinal segments, which carry sympathetic outflow to the proximal colon and kidney", "D": "L1 to L2 paraspinal segments, which carry sympathetic outflow to the distal colon and pelvic organs", "E": "Occiput to C2 segments, which carry parasympathetic vagal outflow to the stomach and proximal gut"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (T5 to T9 paraspinal segments, which carry sympathetic outflow to the stomach and upper gastrointestinal tract): Sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the stomach arise from about T5 to T9, so chronic gastric dysfunction produces paraspinal texture change at those levels. The vagus supplies parasympathetic tone but does not create sympathetic paraspinal findings, and the other levels belong to the head, proximal colon, kidney, distal colon and pelvic organs."}, {"id": 399, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with relapsing episodes of numbness is evaluated for double vision on looking to the right. When asked to look right, her left eye fails to adduct while the right eye abducts with nystagmus. Convergence is intact. Which lesion explains the finding?", "options": {"A": "Left medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion interrupting abducens to oculomotor internuclear signals", "B": "Right abducens nerve palsy denervating the lateral rectus of that eye", "C": "Right medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion interrupting abducens to oculomotor internuclear signals", "D": "Left oculomotor nerve palsy denervating the medial rectus muscle of that eye", "E": "Left paramedian pontine reticular formation lesion producing ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion interrupting abducens to oculomotor internuclear signals): A lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus between the abducens interneurons and the contralateral oculomotor nucleus produces internuclear ophthalmoplegia: the eye on the side of the lesion fails to adduct, and the abducting eye shows nystagmus. Convergence bypasses this pathway, so adduction is preserved when convergence is tested. An oculomotor nerve palsy would also impair convergence and cause ptosis or mydriasis. In a young woman, demyelination is the usual cause."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 24-year-old woman has 3 weeks of dry cough and night sweats. Chest imaging shows an anterior mediastinal mass, and a left supraclavicular node is enlarged. Her sister recently had mononucleosis. Excisional biopsy shows scattered large cells with retracted cytoplasm sitting in clear spaces, separated by thick bands of collagen. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Lymphocyte predominant Hodgkin lymphoma, showing popcorn variant cells in nodules of small B lymphocytes", "B": "Precursor T lymphoblastic lymphoma, presenting as a mediastinal mass with TdT positive immature cells", "C": "Primary mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma, with sclerosis and CD20 positive large neoplastic cells", "D": "Classical Hodgkin lymphoma of the nodular sclerosis type, the commonest subtype in young women", "E": "Mixed cellularity Hodgkin lymphoma, with numerous eosinophils and plasma cells around diagnostic binucleate cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Classical Hodgkin lymphoma of the nodular sclerosis type, the commonest subtype in young women): A young woman with an anterior mediastinal mass and lacunar cells within a background of fibrous collagen bands has nodular sclerosis classical Hodgkin lymphoma, the most common subtype. The distinguishing detail is the lacunar morphology with banded fibrosis. Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma also sclerosis-associated but shows CD20 positive cells rather than CD15 and CD30 positive cells."}, {"id": 401, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has neck stiffness. On motion testing of C5, the segment moves freely into extension, right sidebending, and right rotation, but resists flexion, left sidebending, and left rotation. The physician must record the diagnosis using standard osteopathic naming conventions. Which designation is correct?", "options": {"A": "C5 extended, rotated left, sidebent left, named for the side of tissue texture change", "B": "C5 flexed, rotated left, sidebent left, named for the barrier of restricted motion", "C": "C5 flexed, rotated right, sidebent right, named for the position of ease", "D": "C5 extended, rotated right, sidebent left, named for the position of ease", "E": "C5 extended, rotated right, sidebent right, named for the position of ease"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (C5 extended, rotated right, sidebent right, named for the position of ease): A somatic dysfunction is named for the direction of freedom, the position of ease, so a segment moving freely into extension, right sidebending, and right rotation is extended, rotated right, sidebent right. Naming for the restricted directions reverses the diagnosis, and C2 to C7 follow type II mechanics with sidebending and rotation to the same side."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with congestive heart failure presents with dyspnea. An upright chest radiograph shows blunting of the right costophrenic angle with a meniscus-shaped opacity. Her physician wishes to determine whether the fluid is free-flowing before deciding on thoracentesis, and ultrasound is unavailable. Which imaging study is most appropriate at this point?", "options": {"A": "Lordotic apical view to separate the clavicle and first rib shadows from the lung apex", "B": "Decubitus chest radiograph, symptomatic side down, to demonstrate layering along the dependent chest wall", "C": "Barium swallow with fluoroscopy to exclude diaphragmatic hernia as the cause of opacity", "D": "Expiratory chest radiograph taken at residual volume to look for air trapping", "E": "Ventilation-perfusion scan to look for mismatched segmental defects in the lower lobe"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decubitus chest radiograph, symptomatic side down, to demonstrate layering along the dependent chest wall): A lateral decubitus film with the affected side down lets free pleural fluid shift to the dependent chest wall, and a layer thicker than about 1 cm confirms an effusion that is safe to sample. Fluid that does not move suggests loculation, which would call for ultrasound or CT guidance. Expiratory films are used for suspected small pneumothorax, not for effusion."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 30-year-old man with exertional wheeze undergoes a respiratory exam in the clinic. During a deep inhalation the physician palpates the upper chest and feels the anterior chest wall rise as the sternum moves forward and upward. Which rib motion, characteristic of ribs 1 to 5, produces this finding?", "options": {"A": "Bucket-handle motion, with the lateral shafts rising and widening the transverse chest dimension", "B": "Pump-handle motion, with the sternal ends rising and enlarging the front-to-back chest dimension", "C": "Costovertebral gliding, with the rib heads sliding superiorly on the vertebral demifacets alone", "D": "Caliper motion, with the anterior ends spreading apart as the lower ribs move laterally", "E": "Diaphragmatic descent, with the central tendon flattening and lengthening the vertical chest dimension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pump-handle motion, with the sternal ends rising and enlarging the front-to-back chest dimension): Ribs 1 to 5 rotate about an axis through the costovertebral and costotransverse joints, so the sternal end rises like a pump handle and the front-to-back diameter grows. Ribs 6 to 10 swing laterally like a bucket handle, and ribs 11 and 12 move as calipers. Diaphragmatic descent changes the vertical dimension, not the anterior chest wall rise felt here."}, {"id": 404, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A medical student studying a model of fetal circulation traces blood leaving the placenta at 30 weeks' gestation. Gas exchange across the placenta oxygenates fetal blood, and several shunts redirect it away from the unexpanded lungs. Which vessel carries the blood with the highest oxygen saturation in this circuit?", "options": {"A": "Inferior vena cava below the hepatic veins, returning blood from the fetal lower limbs", "B": "Umbilical arteries, carrying blood from the internal iliac arteries to the placenta", "C": "Umbilical vein, returning blood directly from the placental villi toward the liver", "D": "Descending thoracic aorta distal to the ductus, supplying the fetal trunk and legs", "E": "Ductus arteriosus, delivering mixed blood from the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Umbilical vein, returning blood directly from the placental villi toward the liver): The umbilical vein carries blood straight from the placenta and has the highest oxygen saturation, about 80 percent, in the fetal circuit. Every vessel downstream, including the inferior vena cava and descending aorta, carries blood already mixed with deoxygenated venous return. The umbilical arteries carry the least oxygenated blood back to the placenta."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A term newborn delivered after prolonged shoulder dystocia weighs 4.6 kg. The right arm lies adducted, internally rotated and extended at the elbow with the forearm pronated and the wrist flexed. The hand grasp is intact and the Moro reflex is absent on that side. Which injury best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Isolated axillary nerve injury from pressure on the humeral surgical neck during delivery", "B": "Complete avulsion of the whole brachial plexus from the fifth cervical to first thoracic roots", "C": "Lower trunk stretch injury of the brachial plexus at the eighth cervical and first thoracic roots", "D": "Fracture of the clavicle with secondary compression of the subclavian vessels and plexus", "E": "Upper trunk stretch injury of the brachial plexus at the fifth and sixth roots"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Upper trunk stretch injury of the brachial plexus at the fifth and sixth roots): Waiter's tip posture with a preserved grasp indicates an upper trunk (C5 to C6) stretch injury, which paralyzes deltoid, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, biceps and brachialis while sparing the hand. A lower trunk injury would produce a claw hand with absent grasp, and a total plexus injury would also abolish grasp."}, {"id": 406, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 27-year-old woman records basal body temperature daily for one cycle, as shown in the chart. Her next menses begins on day 29 and she is not pregnant. Which hormone most directly explains the sustained temperature rise beginning around day 15?", "options": {"A": "Follicle stimulating hormone from the pituitary, which rises late in the luteal phase and raises heat", "B": "Luteinizing hormone from the anterior pituitary, whose midcycle surge persists and heats the thermoregulatory center", "C": "Human chorionic gonadotropin from the blastocyst, which rescues the corpus luteum and raises the set point", "D": "Estradiol from the dominant follicle, which peaks before ovulation and raises the hypothalamic set point", "E": "Progesterone from the corpus luteum, which resets the hypothalamic thermoregulatory set point upward after ovulation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Progesterone from the corpus luteum, which resets the hypothalamic thermoregulatory set point upward after ovulation): After ovulation the corpus luteum secretes progesterone, which raises the hypothalamic set point and produces a biphasic temperature pattern. The rise ends at menses when the corpus luteum regresses, which fits a cycle without pregnancy. Human chorionic gonadotropin would sustain progesterone beyond day 28 only if implantation had occurred, and the luteinizing hormone surge lasts only about a day.", "image": "question_images/q_406.png"}, {"id": 407, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 4-year-old boy attending day care has honey-colored crusted erosions around the nose and mouth that appeared over three days. He is afebrile and playing normally. Which organism is most commonly responsible for this nonbullous eruption in children in the United States?", "options": {"A": "Corynebacterium minutissimum, a diphtheroid that causes erythrasma with coral red fluorescence in skin folds", "B": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a gram-negative rod that colonizes moist skin and produces green exudate crusts", "C": "Staphylococcus aureus, with Streptococcus pyogenes as a less frequent cause of the same lesions", "D": "Cutibacterium acnes, an anaerobic skin commensal that causes inflammatory lesions in sebaceous follicles", "E": "Streptococcus agalactae, the group B organism that causes neonatal sepsis and occasional skin abscesses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Staphylococcus aureus, with Streptococcus pyogenes as a less frequent cause of the same lesions): Nonbullous impetigo shows honey-colored crusts from superficial infection, most often with Staphylococcus aureus today, though Streptococcus pyogenes remains a recognized cause. Group B streptococcus and Pseudomonas do not produce this pattern in healthy children."}, {"id": 408, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 22-year-old woman has tremor, dysarthria and new depression. Examination shows brown rings at the corneal periphery. Laboratory studies show Coombs-negative hemolytic anemia, a low serum ceruloplasmin, elevated 24-hour urinary copper and mildly elevated aminotransferases. Which defect best explains her disease?", "options": {"A": "Defective ATP7B copper transporting ATPase impairing biliary copper excretion and ceruloplasmin incorporation", "B": "Acquired decrease in ceruloplasmin synthesis from advanced cirrhosis with normal ATP7B function", "C": "Mutation of the HFE gene raising intestinal iron absorption through low hepcidin expression", "D": "Defective MRP2 transporter blocking canalicular excretion of conjugated bilirubin with dark pigment deposition", "E": "Deficiency of the ATP7A transporter impairing intestinal copper absorption in male infants"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Defective ATP7B copper transporting ATPase impairing biliary copper excretion and ceruloplasmin incorporation): Neuropsychiatric symptoms, Kayser-Fleischer rings, low ceruloplasmin and hemolysis point to Wilson disease, an autosomal recessive ATP7B defect that blocks biliary copper excretion. Menkes disease involves ATP7A and causes copper deficiency, the opposite state."}, {"id": 409, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 30-year-old woman presents 24 hours after sudden onset of fever, myalgias, and dry cough during the local influenza season. A rapid antigen test is positive for influenza A. She is started on oseltamivir. Which viral process does this drug directly inhibit to limit the spread of infection?", "options": {"A": "Cap snatching by the polymerase endonuclease that primes viral messenger RNA synthesis in the nucleus", "B": "Cleavage of sialic acid that lets newly formed virions detach from the infected cell", "C": "Reverse transcription of the viral genome into DNA that integrates into the host chromosome", "D": "Hemagglutinin binding to sialic acid receptors on respiratory epithelium that begins viral attachment and entry", "E": "Endosomal acidification through the M2 ion channel that permits uncoating of the viral nucleocapsid"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cleavage of sialic acid that lets newly formed virions detach from the infected cell): Oseltamivir is a neuraminidase inhibitor. Neuraminidase cleaves terminal sialic acid, which frees new virions from the cell surface, so inhibition traps virus and limits spread. Amantadine blocks the M2 channel needed for uncoating, but it is no longer recommended because of resistance. Therapy works best when begun within 48 hours of symptom onset."}, {"id": 410, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 66-year-old man treated for ventricular tachycardia with a long-term antiarrhythmic develops progressive exertional dyspnea and a dry cough over three months. Pulmonary function testing shows restrictive physiology with reduced diffusing capacity. He also has bluish-gray skin discoloration on sun-exposed areas. Which drug most likely caused these findings?", "options": {"A": "Flecainide, a class IC sodium channel blocker avoided after myocardial infarction", "B": "Lidocaine, a class IB agent that causes central nervous system toxicity in high doses", "C": "Amiodarone, a class III agent with a lipophilic iodinated structure that accumulates in lung tissue", "D": "Sotalol, a class III beta blocker causing dose related QT prolongation and torsades", "E": "Procainamide, a class IA agent that can cause a drug induced lupus syndrome"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amiodarone, a class III agent with a lipophilic iodinated structure that accumulates in lung tissue): Amiodarone accumulates in tissues and causes pulmonary fibrosis, thyroid dysfunction, corneal deposits, hepatotoxicity, and blue-gray skin discoloration. Sotalol is associated with torsades, procainamide with lupus, flecainide with excess mortality after infarction, and lidocaine with neurotoxicity."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 68-year-old woman lifting a grocery bag felt sudden mid-back pain. A lateral radiograph shows a wedge compression fracture of T12 with diffusely decreased bone density, and she is otherwise well. Her bone chemistry panel is shown. Which diagnosis best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Paget disease of bone, disordered remodeling with markedly elevated alkaline phosphatase and thickened bone", "B": "Osteomalacia, defective mineralization of osteoid from vitamin D deficiency with low phosphate", "C": "Primary hyperparathyroidism, excess PTH causing hypercalcemia and subperiosteal resorption of cortical bone", "D": "Multiple myeloma, plasma cell marrow infiltration producing lytic lesions and hypercalcemia with anemia", "E": "Postmenopausal osteoporosis, reduced bone mass with normal mineralization and normal serum chemistries"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Postmenopausal osteoporosis, reduced bone mass with normal mineralization and normal serum chemistries): A fragility fracture with diffusely low density and entirely normal calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, PTH and vitamin D fits primary osteoporosis, in which bone mass falls but mineralization is normal. Osteomalacia, hyperparathyroidism and Paget disease each alter at least one of these laboratory values.", "image": "question_images/q_411.png"}, {"id": 412, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 74-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, who takes metformin, is brought from a nursing home with 1 week of polyuria and progressive confusion. Serum glucose is 980 mg/dL, measured osmolality is 345 mOsm/kg, arterial pH is 7.34, and serum bicarbonate is 22 mEq/L. Urine ketones are trace. Which mechanism best explains why he lacks significant ketoacidosis?", "options": {"A": "Metformin induced lactic acidosis from impaired hepatic gluconeogenesis and reduced lactate clearance in older patients", "B": "Absent insulin with unopposed glucagon drives hepatic ketone production, but compensatory hyperventilation prevents acidemia", "C": "Residual insulin secretion suppresses lipolysis and ketogenesis but does not prevent severe hyperglycemia and osmotic diuresis", "D": "Beta cell autoimmunity in elderly patients is slower, leaving a lower ketone burden at presentation", "E": "Hyperosmolality from severe hypernatremia due to diabetes insipidus, with dilute urine and low urinary osmolality"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Residual insulin secretion suppresses lipolysis and ketogenesis but does not prevent severe hyperglycemia and osmotic diuresis): A small amount of insulin is enough to restrain adipose lipolysis and hepatic ketogenesis but not enough to control glucose uptake, so glucose climbs to extreme levels and causes osmotic diuresis, dehydration, and confusion. This is the hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state. Lactic acidosis from metformin would depress bicarbonate and pH."}, {"id": 413, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 62-year-old man is hospitalized after septic shock treated with vasopressors. On day three, urine output falls to 300 mL per day and creatinine rises from 1.0 to 3.4 mg/dL. Fractional excretion of sodium is 3 percent, and output does not improve after two liters of saline. The urine sediment shown is examined. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute tubular necrosis from ischemic injury, with sloughed tubular epithelial cells forming granular casts", "B": "Acute interstitial nephritis from a drug hypersensitivity reaction, with white blood cell casts and eosinophils", "C": "Postrenal obstruction from bilateral ureteral blockage causing hydronephrosis, with crystals or red blood cells in sediment", "D": "Prerenal azotemia from persistent renal hypoperfusion that should reverse promptly with volume resuscitation", "E": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with crescent formation and red blood cell casts in the sediment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acute tubular necrosis from ischemic injury, with sloughed tubular epithelial cells forming granular casts): Prolonged hypotension injures proximal tubular and thick ascending limb cells, which slough and form muddy brown granular casts. A fractional sodium excretion above 2 percent and no response to saline distinguish this from prerenal azotemia, which keeps a bland sediment and reverses with volume.", "image": "question_images/q_413.png"}, {"id": 414, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 28-year-old medical student sustains a needlestick from a patient later found HBsAg positive and HBeAg positive. The student never completed hepatitis B vaccination, and anti-HBs titer drawn today is undetectable. Wounds were washed immediately. What is the most appropriate postexposure management for the student?", "options": {"A": "Reassurance and serial liver enzyme monitoring because prompt washing removes the infectious inoculum", "B": "Vaccine series alone starting today, since immune globulin is reserved for symptomatic exposures", "C": "Zidovudine, lamivudine, and a protease inhibitor for four weeks with baseline HIV testing", "D": "Passive immunization with HBIG given together with starting the active vaccine series today", "E": "Hepatitis B immune globulin alone, with repeat anti-HBs testing in six months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Passive immunization with HBIG given together with starting the active vaccine series today): A nonimmune person exposed to a high risk source needs immediate antibodies from immune globulin plus active vaccination so that protection continues after the passive antibodies wane. Vaccination alone is too slow to protect during the incubation period. Antiretroviral drugs are used for HIV exposure and do nothing for this virus."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 67-year-old woman with recurrent atrial fibrillation is started on a drug that blocks potassium channels to prolong the action potential, with additional sodium channel, calcium channel, and beta-receptor blockade. Months later she develops dry cough and a reticular pattern on chest radiograph. Which drug most likely caused the lung disease?", "options": {"A": "Dofetilide, a pure potassium channel blocker initiated in hospital because of torsades risk", "B": "Sotalol, a class III agent with beta blockade whose main hazard is torsades de pointes", "C": "Amiodarone, an iodinated class III agent with a long half-life and pulmonary fibrosis risk", "D": "Dronedarone, a noniodinated amiodarone analog with a risk of hepatotoxicity and heart failure worsening", "E": "Flecainide, a class IC sodium channel blocker avoided in patients with structural heart disease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amiodarone, an iodinated class III agent with a long half-life and pulmonary fibrosis risk): Amiodarone has class I through IV actions, and its pulmonary toxicity (interstitial pneumonitis and fibrosis) is the classic dose-related adverse effect, so baseline and periodic lung testing is advised. Sotalol shares class III action but its signature toxicity is torsades de pointes, not lung fibrosis."}, {"id": 416, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 38-year-old nonsmoking man has progressive dyspnea and basilar-predominant hyperlucency on chest radiograph. His father died of lung disease in his forties. Liver biopsy done for abnormal aminotransferases shows periportal hepatocytes containing PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules. Which type of emphysema is expected in his lungs?", "options": {"A": "Bullous emphysema, with large subpleural air spaces forming after chronic obstructive scarring of airways", "B": "Centriacinar emphysema, with destruction of respiratory bronchioles in the upper lobes from smoke exposure", "C": "Panacinar emphysema, with uniform destruction of the whole acinus from unopposed neutrophil elastase", "D": "Irregular emphysema, with scarring around old healed inflammation and variable acinar involvement", "E": "Paraseptal emphysema, with subpleural blebs that predispose to spontaneous pneumothorax in young adults"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Panacinar emphysema, with uniform destruction of the whole acinus from unopposed neutrophil elastase): PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in hepatocytes are misfolded alpha-1 antitrypsin, which indicates deficiency. Without this inhibitor, neutrophil elastase destroys the entire acinus evenly, producing panacinar emphysema that favors the lower lobes. Smoking-related centriacinar emphysema favors the upper lobes."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 36-year-old woman has 6 weeks of palpitations and weight loss. TSH is suppressed and free T4 is elevated. The thyroid is small and nontender. Radioactive iodine uptake at 24 hours is 1 percent (normal 10 to 30). Thyroglobulin is undetectable. She recently started a weight loss supplement. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Graves disease with stimulating antibodies causing diffuse gland hyperfunction and increased iodine trapping", "B": "Factitious thyrotoxicosis from exogenous hormone, with TSH suppression and shutdown of intrinsic gland synthesis", "C": "Toxic multinodular goiter with autonomous nodules secreting hormone independent of pituitary regulation", "D": "Painless lymphocytic thyroiditis with follicular injury and leakage of stored hormone during the thyrotoxic phase", "E": "Struma ovarii with ectopic ovarian thyroid tissue producing hormone and suppressing the normal gland"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Factitious thyrotoxicosis from exogenous hormone, with TSH suppression and shutdown of intrinsic gland synthesis): Low uptake with an undetectable thyroglobulin means the gland is not making or releasing its own hormone, so the hormone is coming from outside. This fits a supplement containing thyroid hormone. Thyroiditis also has low uptake but raises thyroglobulin as stored hormone leaks out."}, {"id": 418, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 31-year-old man, 2 days after transsphenoidal resection of a pituitary adenoma, reports constant thirst. He voids 6 liters of urine in 24 hours. Serum sodium is 149 mEq/L, serum osmolality 306 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality 180 mOsm/kg. Urine osmolality rises to 520 mOsm/kg after desmopressin administration. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Primary polydipsia from compulsive water drinking with secondary suppression of antidiuretic hormone release", "B": "Cerebral salt wasting from natriuretic peptide release causing volume depletion and urinary sodium loss", "C": "Osmotic diuresis from postoperative hyperglycemia with glucose-driven urinary water and sodium losses", "D": "Central diabetes insipidus from impaired posterior pituitary release of antidiuretic hormone after surgery", "E": "Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from collecting duct resistance to antidiuretic hormone after perioperative medication exposure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Central diabetes insipidus from impaired posterior pituitary release of antidiuretic hormone after surgery): Polyuria with high serum osmolality and dilute urine that concentrates after desmopressin shows a kidney that works but lacks the hormone. Pituitary surgery can injure the posterior pituitary or stalk and cause transient central diabetes insipidus. Nephrogenic disease would not respond to desmopressin."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 36-year-old woman at 16 weeks gestation, confirmed by early ultrasonography, has a second-trimester quadruple screen reported as follows: low alpha-fetoprotein, high human chorionic gonadotropin, low unconjugated estriol and high inhibin A. She asks what the pattern most suggests about the fetus. Which condition does this pattern most strongly suggest?", "options": {"A": "Down syndrome from nondisjunction, carrying an increased risk of cardiac and duodenal anomalies", "B": "Normal singleton pregnancy, since maternal age alone shifts marker concentrations without a fetal abnormality", "C": "Open neural tube defect, with elevated alpha-fetoprotein from fetal serum leaking across exposed tissue", "D": "Turner syndrome, with a markedly raised nuchal fold and cystic hygroma on ultrasonography", "E": "Trisomy 18, in which all four markers are typically low and clenched hands are common"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Down syndrome from nondisjunction, carrying an increased risk of cardiac and duodenal anomalies): The classic Down syndrome pattern on the quadruple screen is low alpha-fetoprotein and estriol with high hCG and inhibin A. Trisomy 18 lowers all four analytes, while an open neural tube defect raises alpha-fetoprotein. A screen is not diagnostic, so positive results lead to cell-free DNA or diagnostic testing."}, {"id": 420, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 41-year-old woman with a fractured wrist and facial contusions tells the emergency physician her husband pushed her down the stairs. She has full decision-making capacity, refuses police involvement, and her state does not require clinician reporting of this injury. She is afraid to leave right now. Which action is most appropriate for the physician?", "options": {"A": "Document injuries, assess danger, offer safety planning and hotline resources, and respect her stated wishes", "B": "Treat the fracture and discharge her without raising safety, since she asked for no help", "C": "Invite her husband for a joint discussion so the couple can resolve the conflict", "D": "Admit her involuntarily for protection until social work can interview her husband", "E": "Call the police immediately, overriding her refusal because the injuries resulted from a crime"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Document injuries, assess danger, offer safety planning and hotline resources, and respect her stated wishes): A capacitated adult victim retains the right to decide about police involvement when no law compels reporting, so the physician documents injuries, assesses lethality and offers a safety plan and resources. Overriding her wishes, involuntary admission, or contacting the abuser can increase danger and erode trust. Simply treating the fracture misses the chance to intervene."}, {"id": 421, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 58-year-old woman has severe burning pain in her right hand six weeks after a cast was removed for a distal radius fracture. The hand is swollen, shiny and cooler than the left, with sweating and allodynia to light touch. Pain is out of proportion to the injury. Nerve conduction studies are normal. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Complex regional pain syndrome type 2, a limb disorder that follows a defined peripheral nerve injury", "B": "Complex regional pain syndrome type 1, a limb disorder with autonomic changes and intact nerves", "C": "Early septic arthritis of the wrist with warmth, swelling and severe pain on any joint motion", "D": "Disuse atrophy with joint stiffness after immobilization, causing movement related pain and muscle wasting", "E": "Carpal tunnel syndrome from fracture related swelling, with median nerve compression and night paresthesias"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Complex regional pain syndrome type 1, a limb disorder with autonomic changes and intact nerves): Burning pain out of proportion to injury, allodynia, edema, temperature and sweating changes after a fracture define complex regional pain syndrome. Type 1 has no confirmed nerve lesion, as shown by normal conduction studies, whereas type 2 follows a defined nerve injury. Disuse atrophy lacks allodynia and autonomic features."}, {"id": 422, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 50-year-old woman reports burning pain and tingling in the sole and medial heel that worsens at night and after long standing. Tapping behind the medial malleolus reproduces the symptoms, plantar sensation is reduced, and the Achilles reflex is normal. She has well-controlled hypothyroidism. Which condition best explains her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Tibial nerve entrapment beneath the flexor retinaculum on the inner side of the ankle", "B": "Plantar fasciitis from repetitive strain at the calcaneal origin of the plantar aponeurosis", "C": "S1 radiculopathy from a lumbosacral disc herniation compressing the traversing nerve root", "D": "Morton neuroma, perineural fibrosis of the common digital nerve between the metatarsal heads", "E": "Diabetic peripheral polyneuropathy affecting both feet in a stocking pattern of sensory loss"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tibial nerve entrapment beneath the flexor retinaculum on the inner side of the ankle): A reproducible Tinel sign behind the medial malleolus with burning plantar paresthesias localizes the lesion to the tibial nerve beneath the flexor retinaculum, the tarsal tunnel. A normal Achilles reflex argues against S1 radiculopathy, and plantar fasciitis causes pain without sensory loss."}, {"id": 423, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 25-year-old man is observed during an exercise physiology laboratory session. As he begins running, venous return rises and end-diastolic volume increases, so the ventricle ejects a larger stroke volume on the next beat without any neural input. This intrinsic property of the myocardium is best described by which principle?", "options": {"A": "Increased heart rate shortens diastole and enhances calcium loading, known as the Bowditch effect", "B": "Rising arterial pressure triggers the Anrep effect, increasing contractility after sustained afterload elevation", "C": "Lower afterload permits more complete emptying and lowers end-diastolic volume on subsequent beats", "D": "Greater sarcomere stretch before contraction raises calcium sensitivity and increases the force generated", "E": "Sympathetic beta-1 stimulation raises intracellular calcium and shifts contractility upward at the same preload"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Greater sarcomere stretch before contraction raises calcium sensitivity and increases the force generated): Frank-Starling states that greater end-diastolic stretch lengthens sarcomeres toward optimal overlap and increases troponin C calcium sensitivity, raising stroke volume with no neural input. Beta-1 stimulation also raises stroke volume but acts through cAMP and shifts the whole curve, which the stem excludes by stating there is no neural input."}, {"id": 424, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 3-week-old boy has a retained umbilical cord stump with surrounding erythema but no pus. The white blood cell count is 42,000 per microliter with marked neutrophilia. A skin biopsy of the erythema shows almost no neutrophils in the tissue. A skin infection last week also formed no pus. Which defect most likely explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Deficient NADPH oxidase activity, preventing the respiratory burst needed to kill catalase positive organisms", "B": "Defective LYST protein impairing lysosomal trafficking, producing giant granules and failed phagolysosome fusion in neutrophils", "C": "Deficient CD18 beta-2 integrin subunit, preventing firm neutrophil adhesion and transmigration into infected tissue", "D": "Impaired IL-12 and interferon gamma signaling, weakening macrophage killing of intracellular mycobacteria", "E": "Deficient myeloperoxidase, impairing conversion of hydrogen peroxide into hypochlorous acid within phagolysosomes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deficient CD18 beta-2 integrin subunit, preventing firm neutrophil adhesion and transmigration into infected tissue): Neutrophilia with no neutrophils in tissue and delayed cord separation indicate leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1, from defective CD18 so integrin-mediated firm adhesion and transmigration fail. Chronic granulomatous disease would produce tissue neutrophils with abscesses and granulomas, and Chediak-Higashi syndrome shows giant granules with partial albinism."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 40-year-old man weighing 80 kg arrives at the emergency department one hour after a house fire. He has partial-thickness and full-thickness burns covering 30 percent of his total body surface area, with stable airway and vital signs. Using the Parkland formula, which initial resuscitation plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Lactated Ringer 3600 mL in the first 8 hours, counted from the time of injury", "B": "Lactated Ringer 2400 mL in the first 8 hours, counted from the time of injury", "C": "Lactated Ringer 4800 mL in the first 8 hours, counted from the time of injury", "D": "Lactated Ringer 9600 mL in the first 8 hours, counted from the time of injury", "E": "Lactated Ringer 4800 mL in the first 8 hours, counted from hospital arrival time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lactated Ringer 4800 mL in the first 8 hours, counted from the time of injury): The Parkland formula is 4 mL x weight (kg) x percent TBSA, which gives 9600 mL over 24 hours. Half of that, 4800 mL, is given in the first 8 hours measured from the time of the burn, not from hospital arrival, so an hour of the window has already passed. Giving the full volume early or using a smaller per-kilogram factor misapplies the formula."}, {"id": 426, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 10-month-old girl has fallen from the 50th to the 3rd percentile for weight over four months, while length and head circumference remain near the 40th percentile. She is irritable but has no vomiting or diarrhea, and her examination is otherwise unremarkable. Her family recently lost stable housing. What is the most likely explanation for her growth pattern?", "options": {"A": "Inadequate caloric intake, which lowers weight first and spares length and head circumference initially", "B": "Congenital hypothyroidism, which typically delays linear and head growth with lethargy and constipation", "C": "Growth hormone deficiency, which usually stunts length and weight proportionately despite adequate caloric intake", "D": "A genetic syndrome causing symmetric restriction of weight, length, and head circumference from birth", "E": "Celiac disease, causing malabsorption with chronic diarrhea and abdominal distension after gluten exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Inadequate caloric intake, which lowers weight first and spares length and head circumference initially): Caloric deprivation first decreases weight, then length, then head circumference, so isolated weight faltering with preserved length and head size points to insufficient intake, which is the most common cause of failure to thrive. Endocrine and genetic causes usually impair length early. Social stressors in the history support an intake problem rather than organic disease."}, {"id": 427, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 35-year-old woman has 2 weeks of gum bleeding and easy bruising. Platelets are 22,000 per microliter, fibrinogen is low, and D-dimer is elevated. Bone marrow shows abundant abnormal promyelocytes containing bundles of needle-shaped cytoplasmic crystals, and cytogenetics show t(15;17). Which treatment targets the underlying molecular defect?", "options": {"A": "Cytarabine with an anthracycline, which damages DNA of rapidly dividing myeloblasts in the marrow", "B": "Hydroxyurea, which inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and lowers circulating white cell counts in leukemia", "C": "Rituximab, which depletes CD20 expressing malignant cells through complement and antibody dependent cytotoxicity", "D": "All-trans retinoic acid, which overcomes the maturation block by acting on the abnormal fusion receptor", "E": "Imatinib, which inhibits the constitutively active fusion tyrosine kinase in the leukemic clone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (All-trans retinoic acid, which overcomes the maturation block by acting on the abnormal fusion receptor): Auer rod-rich promyelocytes with t(15;17) and coagulopathy define acute promyelocytic leukemia, caused by the PML-RARA fusion that blocks differentiation. All-trans retinoic acid binds the fusion receptor and induces maturation, and it is started urgently because of the associated disseminated intravascular coagulation. Imatinib targets BCR-ABL and rituximab targets CD20."}, {"id": 428, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 27-year-old woman presents after a cast applied for a tibial fracture was found to compress the lateral side of her proximal leg. She now has a high-stepping gait with the toes dragging, and cannot lift or turn out the foot. Sensation is lost over the dorsum of the foot. Which nerve is injured?", "options": {"A": "Superficial fibular nerve alone, passing between the fibularis muscles in the lateral compartment", "B": "Deep fibular nerve alone, running with the anterior tibial artery in the anterior compartment", "C": "Tibial nerve, running deep within the popliteal fossa behind the knee joint", "D": "Sciatic nerve, splitting at the apex of the popliteal fossa above the knee", "E": "Common fibular nerve, wrapping the neck of the fibula just beneath the skin"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Common fibular nerve, wrapping the neck of the fibula just beneath the skin): The common fibular nerve is superficial as it winds around the fibular neck and is easily compressed by casts or straps. Its loss removes dorsiflexion and eversion, causing foot drop, with sensory loss on the dorsum, whereas a single branch lesion would spare either eversion or dorsiflexion."}, {"id": 429, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 35-year-old woman with schizophrenia has gained 14 kg in six months since starting a new antipsychotic. Her fasting glucose is 118 mg/dL, triglycerides are 260 mg/dL, and she reports increased appetite and daytime sedation. Her psychotic symptoms have improved markedly. Which drug is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Olanzapine, whose histamine H1 and serotonin 5-HT2C antagonism promotes appetite and metabolic dysregulation", "B": "Aripiprazole, a partial dopamine agonist whose prominent adverse effect is akathisia and restlessness", "C": "Haloperidol, a high potency dopamine antagonist producing prominent extrapyramidal symptoms and hyperprolactinemia", "D": "Ziprasidone, a serotonin and dopamine antagonist with notable QT prolongation and food dependent absorption", "E": "Lurasidone, a serotonin and dopamine antagonist taken with food for adequate absorption"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Olanzapine, whose histamine H1 and serotonin 5-HT2C antagonism promotes appetite and metabolic dysregulation): Marked weight gain, hypertriglyceridemia, and impaired fasting glucose with sedation fit olanzapine, whose H1 and 5-HT2C blockade drives appetite and insulin resistance. Haloperidol and aripiprazole cause movement side effects more than metabolic ones, and ziprasidone is more associated with QT prolongation."}, {"id": 430, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old man is examined after a diving accident. Elbow flexion and wrist extension are 5 of 5 bilaterally, elbow extension is 0 of 5, and finger flexion and finger abduction are absent. Sensation is absent below the clavicle except over the lateral forearm and thumb. What is the lowest intact neurologic level of motor function?", "options": {"A": "C5, because the biceps give the last working elbow flexion and deltoid abduction", "B": "C6, because wrist extensors are intact while the C7 triceps are no longer functioning", "C": "C7, because elbow extension by the triceps is the key muscle for this level", "D": "C8, because the finger flexors are the key muscle just below the level of injury", "E": "T1, because the interossei that abduct the fingers are the lowest brachial plexus myotome tested"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (C6, because wrist extensors are intact while the C7 triceps are no longer functioning): The neurologic level is the lowest segment with normal motor function, and wrist extension (C6) is the lowest key muscle that is strong here. Triceps weakness shows C7 is not intact, and the intact lateral forearm and thumb sensation also fit C6. Naming the first absent myotome is a common error."}, {"id": 431, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "An 8-month-old girl, exclusively breastfed and never given supplements, is brought for a well visit. She has frontal bossing, widened wrists, beading at the costochondral junctions, and bowed legs. Radiographs show cupped, frayed metaphyses. Serum phosphate and calcium are low-normal, and alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone are elevated. Which intervention would have best prevented this condition?", "options": {"A": "Oral fluoride drops from six months of age to support enamel and bone mineralization", "B": "Intramuscular vitamin K at birth to activate clotting factors and prevent hemorrhagic disease of the newborn", "C": "Daily oral vitamin D from the first days of life to support calcium and phosphate absorption", "D": "Prophylactic cobalamin supplementation to support myelination and red cell maturation in breastfed infants", "E": "Elemental iron drops starting at four months of age to prevent iron deficiency in breastfed infants"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Daily oral vitamin D from the first days of life to support calcium and phosphate absorption): Human milk contains little vitamin D, so exclusively breastfed infants need daily supplementation from early life to avoid rickets. The findings of metaphyseal cupping, widened wrists, and raised alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone fit nutritional rickets. Iron prevents anemia and vitamin K prevents bleeding, but neither prevents rickets."}, {"id": 432, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes starts a once-weekly injectable medication and loses 12 percent of her body weight over six months. She also reports early satiety and mild nausea after meals. Which mechanism most directly accounts for this effect on appetite and food intake?", "options": {"A": "Central reuptake inhibition of norepinephrine and dopamine, raising satiety signaling through the hypothalamus", "B": "Inhibition of renal glucose reabsorption causing urinary calorie loss and secondary reduction in hunger", "C": "Blockade of dipeptidyl peptidase activity that prolongs endogenous incretin half-life and increases insulin secretion", "D": "Blockade of pancreatic lipase reducing dietary fat absorption and increasing fecal fat excretion", "E": "Incretin receptor activation slowing gastric emptying and signaling hypothalamic centers to lower hunger"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Incretin receptor activation slowing gastric emptying and signaling hypothalamic centers to lower hunger): Semaglutide and related agents activate GLP-1 receptors, which slows gastric emptying, increases satiety through hypothalamic and brainstem circuits, and enhances glucose dependent insulin secretion. Nausea and early satiety follow from delayed gastric emptying. SGLT2 inhibitors cause modest weight loss through glycosuria, and orlistat acts on lipase, but neither is a weekly injectable."}, {"id": 433, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 58-year-old woman taking alprazolam for years runs out during a snowstorm and stops it abruptly. Two days later she is brought in with tremor, sweating, heart rate 118, agitation and a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Her only other medication is a statin. Which intervention is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous flumazenil to reverse the receptor adaptation, followed by observation for resolution of tremor", "B": "Intravenous lorazepam, then resume a long-acting benzodiazepine and taper it slowly over weeks", "C": "Oral propranolol to control the tachycardia and tremor, then discharge with outpatient follow-up", "D": "Intravenous phenytoin loading, since benzodiazepine withdrawal seizures respond best to sodium channel blockade", "E": "Intravenous haloperidol for agitation, accepting its effect on the seizure threshold during monitoring"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous lorazepam, then resume a long-acting benzodiazepine and taper it slowly over weeks): Abrupt cessation of a short-acting benzodiazepine causes a withdrawal syndrome with autonomic hyperactivity and seizures that is treated by restoring GABA-A agonism. A benzodiazepine stops the seizure and a long-acting agent allows a gradual taper. Flumazenil would worsen the problem, and phenytoin is ineffective for this seizure mechanism."}, {"id": 434, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 14-year-old boy awakens with sudden severe left scrotal pain and vomiting that began 3 hours ago. He denies dysuria or urethral discharge. The left testis is high riding with a horizontal lie, and the cremasteric reflex is absent on that side. Urinalysis is normal. Which management step is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Manual detorsion at the bedside using an open book maneuver, then discharge with elective repair", "B": "Intravenous ceftriaxone and doxycycline for presumed sexually transmitted epididymitis with urethral culture collection", "C": "Oral antibiotics for epididymitis with scrotal elevation, ice, and outpatient follow up in two days", "D": "Emergent surgical exploration with detorsion and bilateral orchiopexy rather than waiting for confirmatory imaging studies", "E": "Color Doppler ultrasonography of both testes first to confirm absent flow before any operative decision"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Emergent surgical exploration with detorsion and bilateral orchiopexy rather than waiting for confirmatory imaging studies): Abrupt pain with a high riding transverse testis and no cremasteric reflex is testicular torsion, and testicular salvage falls steeply after about six hours. Surgery should not be delayed for imaging when suspicion is high, and the other testis is fixed because of the bell clapper deformity. Epididymitis has a gradual onset and a preserved reflex."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 55-year-old man with exertional fatigue undergoes left heart catheterization. Left ventricular end-diastolic volume is 140 mL and end-systolic volume is 70 mL. His heart rate is 80 beats per minute and body surface area is 2.0 square meters. Which pair of values correctly gives his ejection fraction and cardiac index?", "options": {"A": "Ejection fraction 50 percent with a cardiac index of 11.2 L/min/m2", "B": "Ejection fraction 33 percent with a cardiac index of 2.8 L/min/m2", "C": "Ejection fraction 50 percent with a cardiac index of 2.8 L/min/m2", "D": "Ejection fraction 50 percent with a cardiac index of 5.6 L/min/m2", "E": "Ejection fraction 100 percent with a cardiac index of 2.8 L/min/m2"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ejection fraction 50 percent with a cardiac index of 2.8 L/min/m2): Stroke volume is 140 minus 70, or 70 mL, so ejection fraction is 70 divided by 140, which is 50 percent. Cardiac output is 70 mL times 80 beats per minute, or 5.6 L/min, and dividing by 2.0 square meters gives a cardiac index of 2.8 L/min/m2."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 31-year-old woman underwent resection of 80 cm of terminal ileum for Crohn disease. Two years later she has a megaloblastic anemia and paresthesias. Her stomach and pancreas are intact. Which step in cobalamin absorption has most likely failed in her?", "options": {"A": "Binding of free cobalamin to salivary haptocorrin before entry into the duodenum", "B": "Uptake of the intrinsic factor cobalamin complex by cubilin receptors on distal small bowel enterocytes", "C": "Release of cobalamin from food protein by pepsin and gastric acid in the stomach", "D": "Pancreatic protease degradation of haptocorrin in the duodenum to transfer cobalamin onto intrinsic factor", "E": "Secretion of intrinsic factor by gastric parietal cells in response to histamine stimulation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Uptake of the intrinsic factor cobalamin complex by cubilin receptors on distal small bowel enterocytes): Cobalamin bound to intrinsic factor is absorbed only at receptors in the terminal ileum, so ileal resection or disease causes deficiency even when gastric and pancreatic steps are normal. Pernicious anemia would impair intrinsic factor secretion, but her stomach is intact. Body stores last two to five years, which matches the delayed onset."}, {"id": 437, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A rapid antigen test for streptococcal pharyngitis is evaluated against throat culture in 400 children presenting with sore throat. The results are tabulated in the table shown. A pediatrician wants to know how well the test identifies children who truly have the infection. Which value answers this question?", "options": {"A": "Sensitivity of 90 percent, calculated as true positives divided by all culture-confirmed disease", "B": "Accuracy of 94 percent, calculated as all correct results divided by the total tested", "C": "Negative predictive value of 97 percent, calculated as true negatives divided by all negative tests", "D": "Positive predictive value of 86 percent, calculated as true positives divided by all positive tests", "E": "Specificity of 95 percent, calculated as true negatives divided by all disease-free individuals"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sensitivity of 90 percent, calculated as true positives divided by all culture-confirmed disease): The question asks what fraction of children with true infection the test detects, which is sensitivity: 90 true positives among 100 culture-positive children is 90 percent. Specificity, predictive values and overall accuracy answer different questions and depend on other cells of the table.", "image": "question_images/q_437.png"}, {"id": 438, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 65-year-old man with severe COPD is mechanically ventilated for pneumonia. Parenteral nutrition providing a very high carbohydrate load is started, and over the next several days his PaCO2 rises and he repeatedly fails spontaneous breathing trials. Minute ventilation requirement has increased. Which mechanism best explains the worsening hypercapnia?", "options": {"A": "Fat oxidation yields the highest respiratory quotient, producing excess carbon dioxide per oxygen consumed", "B": "Lipid emulsion infusion deposits in pulmonary capillaries, producing diffusion limitation for carbon dioxide", "C": "Protein catabolism generates nitrogenous waste that depresses central respiratory drive in the medulla", "D": "Glucose oxidation and lipogenesis raise carbon dioxide production relative to oxygen consumption, straining respiratory muscles", "E": "Hyperglycemic osmotic diuresis causes volume contraction that lowers lung compliance and alveolar ventilation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Glucose oxidation and lipogenesis raise carbon dioxide production relative to oxygen consumption, straining respiratory muscles): Respiratory quotient is about 1.0 for carbohydrate, 0.8 for protein, and 0.7 for fat, so overfeeding glucose and converting it to fat (quotient above 1.0) increases carbon dioxide output per oxygen consumed. In a patient with COPD and limited reserve, the extra carbon dioxide raises required minute ventilation and precipitates hypercapnia. Fat has the lowest quotient, so shifting calories toward lipid is the usual adjustment."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 46-year-old man has low back pain for one week and cannot stand fully upright, walking bent slightly forward. He has no fever. Examination shows a positive right Thomas test, deep tenderness lateral to the umbilicus, and non-neutral dysfunction at L2. Pain improves when he lies with hips flexed. Which diagnosis best explains his findings?", "options": {"A": "Acute appendicitis with an inflamed retrocecal appendix irritating the psoas muscle during hip extension", "B": "Sacral torsion on an oblique axis with ipsilateral gluteal guarding limiting trunk extension", "C": "Piriformis syndrome with sciatic nerve irritation producing buttock pain and sacral base rotation", "D": "Psoas syndrome with hypertonic iliopsoas and type II lumbar dysfunction at the upper lumbar segments", "E": "Lumbar facet joint arthropathy with extension-based pain relieved by forward flexion of the trunk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Psoas syndrome with hypertonic iliopsoas and type II lumbar dysfunction at the upper lumbar segments): A forward-bent stance, relief with hips flexed, a positive Thomas test, and deep paraumbilical tenderness with upper lumbar dysfunction describe psoas syndrome. Appendicitis can mimic it but is unlikely without fever or other abdominal findings, and the other options do not explain a hip flexion contracture."}, {"id": 440, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 25-year-old woman, three months postpartum, has sharp right pubic pain when she climbs stairs. Palpation shows the right pubic tubercle higher than the left, with tender ligamentous tissue at the symphysis. Which dysfunction and which treatment, using the patient's own isometric effort, are most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Right inferior pubic shear, treated with muscle energy at the pubic symphysis", "B": "Pubic symphysis compression, treated with articulatory springing of the lumbar spine", "C": "Right superior pubic shear, treated with muscle energy at the pubic symphysis", "D": "Right superior pubic shear, treated with a high velocity thrust across the sacroiliac joint", "E": "Right superior pubic shear, treated with counterstrain using passive positioning of ease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right superior pubic shear, treated with muscle energy at the pubic symphysis): A higher right pubic tubercle indicates a superior pubic shear, and muscle energy uses the patient's isometric effort to correct it. An inferior shear would show a lower tubercle on that side. Counterstrain is passive, and a thrust across the sacroiliac joint does not address the pubic symphysis."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 16-year-old girl attends a routine school physical with her mother waiting outside. She has no complaints and normal vital signs. The physician wants a brief, validated questionnaire that screens for alcohol and drug use during confidential time with adolescents in primary care. Which screening instrument is most appropriate for this visit?", "options": {"A": "CAGE questionnaire, four questions about cutting down, annoyance, guilt and morning eye openers in adults", "B": "AUDIT-C, three consumption questions that quantify frequency and binge drinking in adult populations", "C": "PHQ-9, a nine item inventory that grades depressive symptom severity and screens for suicidal thoughts", "D": "DAST-10, a ten item questionnaire developed to quantify drug problems in adults excluding alcohol", "E": "CRAFFT, a six question tool built for teenagers that asks about riding with impaired drivers"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (CRAFFT, a six question tool built for teenagers that asks about riding with impaired drivers): CRAFFT was designed and validated for adolescents and is recommended for confidential screening in primary care. CAGE and AUDIT-C address alcohol in adult samples, and DAST-10 omits alcohol. PHQ-9 screens mood, not substance use."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 72-year-old woman hospitalized with lobar pneumonia is on antibiotics and oxygen. She has osteoporosis and a compression fracture history. The attending wants a gentle osteopathic adjunct to reduce sympathetic tone to the lungs and support lymphatic drainage. Which technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Rib raising along the thoracic costovertebral region, using gentle passive motion to modulate autonomic tone", "B": "Articulatory technique to the cervical spine, loosening tissue to strengthen vagal supply to the bronchi", "C": "HVLA thrust to the thoracic spine, clearing consolidated lung segments through rapid mobilization", "D": "Counterstrain at anterior tender points, shifting autonomic balance toward vagal predominance in the lungs", "E": "Compression of the fourth ventricle, increasing sympathetic drive to bronchial smooth muscle to open airways"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rib raising along the thoracic costovertebral region, using gentle passive motion to modulate autonomic tone): Rib raising is a gentle, passive technique used in hospitalized pneumonia patients to reduce sympathetic tone and aid lymphatic and respiratory mechanics, and it carries little fracture risk. HVLA thrust is the tempting but inappropriate choice with osteoporosis and a prior compression fracture. The other techniques rest on autonomic claims that do not match lung physiology."}, {"id": 443, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 52-year-old man with long-standing mitral regurgitation develops marked left ventricular dilation, with a larger chamber radius and unchanged wall thickness. At the same intraventricular pressure as before, his myocardial oxygen demand per beat rises. According to the law of Laplace, which change best explains this increased demand?", "options": {"A": "Larger chamber size at fixed pressure raises tension on the myocardium, increasing energy use", "B": "Higher preload stretches sarcomeres further, which raises stroke work and the oxygen cost of ejection", "C": "Greater contractility from sympathetic activation raises the velocity of fiber shortening and energy use", "D": "Increased heart rate shortens diastolic filling time and raises oxygen consumption during each minute", "E": "Higher afterload from raised aortic pressure increases the tension needed to open the valve"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Larger chamber size at fixed pressure raises tension on the myocardium, increasing energy use): Laplace's law gives wall tension proportional to pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so a dilated chamber with unchanged thickness has higher tension per unit myocardium even at the same pressure, and tension is a major determinant of oxygen demand. Higher preload is present in regurgitation but does not address a question framed around Laplace."}, {"id": 444, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 35-year-old man has had a throbbing lower molar toothache for a week. He now has fever, drooling, difficulty swallowing, and muffled voice. Examination shows firm bilateral swelling beneath the jaw, elevated and indurated floor of the mouth, and tongue displacement upward. He is sitting forward and has mild stridor. What is the most appropriate immediate management?", "options": {"A": "Obtain contrast enhanced neck CT before any intervention and begin oral antibiotics while awaiting results", "B": "Secure the airway early, give intravenous broad spectrum antibiotics, and arrange surgical drainage with source control", "C": "Give intravenous corticosteroids and nebulized epinephrine for presumed angioedema and observe in the emergency department overnight", "D": "Extract the offending molar at the bedside under local anesthesia and discharge with oral analgesics", "E": "Prescribe oral amoxicillin clavulanate, advise warm saline rinses, and schedule dental extraction within the following week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Secure the airway early, give intravenous broad spectrum antibiotics, and arrange surgical drainage with source control): Bilateral submandibular and sublingual swelling with floor of mouth elevation after a molar infection is Ludwig angina, a rapidly spreading cellulitis that can obstruct the airway. Airway protection comes first, followed by intravenous antibiotics covering oral flora and surgical drainage. Imaging in a patient with stridor risks catastrophic obstruction."}, {"id": 445, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A case-control study of a congenital heart defect interviews mothers of affected infants and mothers of healthy infants about medication use during pregnancy. Mothers of affected infants report far more drug exposure, but many reviewed their pregnancies repeatedly searching for a cause. Which source of error most plausibly explains the association?", "options": {"A": "Lead-time bias, because affected infants were diagnosed earlier in pregnancy than they would otherwise be", "B": "Observer bias, because the interviewer knew which mothers had delivered infants with a defect", "C": "Recall bias, because parents of ill infants remember and report past drug use more completely", "D": "Confounding, because maternal age differs between groups and independently influences both drug use and defects", "E": "Selection bias, because control mothers came from a source population different from the cases"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recall bias, because parents of ill infants remember and report past drug use more completely): Parents of ill infants search their memories harder, so they report past drug exposures more completely than control parents, producing recall bias that inflates the association. Selection bias concerns how controls were chosen, and confounding requires a third variable, which the stem does not describe. Lead-time bias belongs to screening studies, and interviewer knowledge is not mentioned."}, {"id": 446, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man with end-stage renal disease misses dialysis for a week. He also takes lisinopril and spironolactone. His serum potassium is 7.2 mEq/L, and ECG shows peaked T waves. Which change in resting membrane potential best explains the increased risk of arrhythmia?", "options": {"A": "Depolarization of resting potential that inactivates sodium channels and reduces cell excitability", "B": "Unchanged resting potential because potassium permeability rises to offset the extracellular increase", "C": "Hyperpolarization of resting potential that increases the distance to threshold and reduces excitability", "D": "Shift of the sodium equilibrium potential to a more positive value increasing action potential amplitude", "E": "Depolarization of resting potential that increases the fraction of sodium channels available for opening"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Depolarization of resting potential that inactivates sodium channels and reduces cell excitability): A higher extracellular potassium concentration lowers the potassium gradient, so the potassium equilibrium potential and resting potential become less negative. Sustained depolarization inactivates voltage-gated sodium channels through the inactivation gate, so fewer are available to open and excitability falls, which can lead to conduction block and arrhythmia. Hypokalemia produces hyperpolarization instead."}, {"id": 447, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 17-year-old boy has two episodes of cola-colored urine, each beginning one day after a head cold. He is afebrile with blood pressure of 118/76 mm Hg and no edema. Urinalysis shows red blood cells with dysmorphic forms and mild proteinuria. Serum creatinine and C3 are normal. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Lupus nephritis from immune complex deposition with low complement and positive antinuclear antibodies", "B": "Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis from subepithelial immune complexes with transient complement consumption", "C": "Alport syndrome from a collagen mutation causing familial hematuria and deafness", "D": "Thin basement membrane disease causing benign hematuria with diffuse basement membrane thinning", "E": "IgA nephropathy with mesangial IgA deposition triggered by a mucosal infection"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (IgA nephropathy with mesangial IgA deposition triggered by a mucosal infection): Recurrent gross hematuria beginning within one to two days of a mucosal infection, with normal complement, is typical of IgA nephropathy. Poststreptococcal disease follows an infection by one to three weeks and lowers C3. Thin basement membrane disease causes persistent microscopic hematuria rather than infection linked flares."}, {"id": 448, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 40-year-old man has a chronic nighttime cough, hoarseness, and burning retrosternal discomfort after large meals, with a normal chest radiograph and spirometry. Palpation shows tender, boggy paraspinal tissue at left T5 to T9. Which viscerosomatic relationship best explains the cause of the paraspinal findings and cough?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary reflex from asthma, with sympathetic segmental facilitation at the T1 to T6 levels", "B": "Cardiac reflex from angina, with left-sided facilitation limited to the T1 to T5 levels", "C": "Small bowel reflex from enteritis, with sympathetic segmental facilitation at T8 to T10", "D": "Upper gastrointestinal reflex from esophageal and gastric irritation, with sympathetic segmental facilitation at T5 to T9", "E": "Primary rib dysfunction from posture, with local muscle spasm and no organ involvement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Upper gastrointestinal reflex from esophageal and gastric irritation, with sympathetic segmental facilitation at T5 to T9): Meal-related burning, hoarseness, and nocturnal cough with normal imaging and spirometry suggest reflux, and the stomach and upper gastrointestinal tract refer to T5 to T9. The lungs refer to T1 to T6, the heart to T1 to T5, and the small bowel to T8 to T10, none of which fits both the symptoms and the levels as well."}, {"id": 449, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 35-year-old farmer is brought to the emergency department after a tractor rollover, with an open tibial shaft fracture and a 6 cm soil-contaminated wound. He is hemodynamically stable and neurovascularly intact. Which action should be taken first to reduce his risk of deep infection?", "options": {"A": "Irrigate the wound at the bedside with antibiotic solution then apply a dressing", "B": "Close the wound primarily in the emergency department to seal out contaminants", "C": "Start intravenous antibiotics without delay, then proceed to operative irrigation and surgical debridement", "D": "Obtain wound cultures before any antibiotic is given and await the results", "E": "Apply a splint and arrange elective fixation once the soft tissue swelling resolves"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Start intravenous antibiotics without delay, then proceed to operative irrigation and surgical debridement): Early systemic antibiotics, ideally within the first hour, are the intervention most strongly linked to lower deep infection rates in open fractures, and urgent surgical debridement follows. Contaminated wounds are left open and not closed primarily. Waiting for culture results delays therapy, and routine wound cultures do not guide treatment."}, {"id": 450, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 4-month-old boy born at 29 weeks of gestation develops rhinorrhea, then wheezing, tachypnea and nasal flaring in January. Chest radiograph shows hyperinflation with patchy atelectasis. A nasopharyngeal swab detects an enveloped RNA virus that fuses infected cells into syncytia. Which agent prevents severe disease in at-risk infants by binding the surface glycoprotein responsible for that fusion?", "options": {"A": "Ribavirin, a guanosine analog that inhibits viral RNA synthesis and is given by aerosol", "B": "Nebulized hypertonic saline, an osmotic agent that rehydrates airway surface liquid and aids mucociliary clearance", "C": "Palivizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody directed against the F protein of this virus", "D": "Intravenous immune globulin, pooled polyclonal antibody given to boost passive immunity against many pathogens", "E": "Inactivated influenza vaccine, which stimulates antibodies against hemagglutinin and neuraminidase of seasonal strains"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Palivizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody directed against the F protein of this virus): This is RSV bronchiolitis in a premature infant. Palivizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody against the RSV F protein, which mediates membrane fusion and syncytium formation, and it is given monthly during the season to high-risk infants. Ribavirin is an antiviral with limited use, and immune globulin and influenza vaccine do not provide RSV-specific protection."}, {"id": 451, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 4-month-old boy, born at term, has developed seizures and hypotonia. He has sparse, brittle, steel-colored hair, pale skin and lax joints. Imaging shows tortuous cerebral arteries. His maternal uncle died in infancy. Which enzymatic consequence best explains the connective tissue findings?", "options": {"A": "Reduced cystathionine beta-synthase activity, causing homocysteine to disrupt collagen cross-linking", "B": "Reduced prolyl hydroxylase activity from vitamin C deficiency, impairing collagen triple helix stability", "C": "Reduced lysyl hydroxylase activity from a PLOD1 defect, impairing hydroxylysine cross-link formation", "D": "Reduced lysyl oxidase activity from copper deficiency, impairing collagen and elastin cross-linking", "E": "Reduced procollagen N-peptidase activity, leaving uncleaved propeptides that block fibril assembly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced lysyl oxidase activity from copper deficiency, impairing collagen and elastin cross-linking): Sparse kinky hair, hypotonia, seizures and tortuous vessels in an infant with an affected maternal uncle indicate X-linked Menkes disease, in which defective copper transport lowers lysyl oxidase activity and cross-linking. Vitamin C deficiency and lysyl hydroxylase defects affect hydroxylation instead. Homocystinuria also produces lens dislocation and thrombosis rather than seizures with kinky hair."}, {"id": 452, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A health department analyst reviews household income in a county where a few extremely wealthy families lie far above everyone else. The histogram has a long tail extending to the right. Which relationship among the measures of central tendency would be expected for this distribution?", "options": {"A": "The mean exceeds the median, which exceeds the mode, because outliers drag the average", "B": "The mean falls between the median and the mode, because the tail affects each measure equally", "C": "The median exceeds both the mean and the mode, because it is most sensitive to skew", "D": "The mode exceeds the median, which exceeds the mean, as in a left-skewed distribution", "E": "The mean, median and mode are equal, as in a symmetric normal distribution"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The mean exceeds the median, which exceeds the mode, because outliers drag the average): In a right-skewed distribution the tail of high values pulls the mean the farthest toward the right, the median sits in between, and the mode stays at the peak. A left-skewed pattern would reverse this order, which is the tempting error. The median resists outliers, so it moves much less than the mean."}, {"id": 453, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 72-year-old resident of a nursing home on a course of clindamycin develops profuse watery diarrhea, abdominal cramping, and leukocytosis. Stool toxin assay is positive. The infection control nurse reviews hand hygiene practices for staff caring for the resident. Which hand hygiene method is most appropriate when caring for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Chlorhexidine skin wipe after glove removal to inactivate spores on the skin surface", "B": "Alcohol rub followed by a second alcohol rub to double the antimicrobial contact time", "C": "Gloves alone with hand hygiene deferred until leaving the facility at shift end", "D": "Soap and water washing with friction to physically remove spores from the hands", "E": "Alcohol-based hand rub for thirty seconds because alcohol denatures vegetative cell proteins"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Soap and water washing with friction to physically remove spores from the hands): Clostridioides difficile forms spores that resist alcohol, so mechanical washing with soap and water is needed to lift them off the skin. Alcohol rubs work well against vegetative bacteria but not against spores, and repeating the application does not change that. Gloves are worn in addition to, not instead of, hand hygiene."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A primary care group is paid a fixed monthly amount per enrolled patient regardless of how many visits occur. An administrator reviewing the first year notes fewer specialist referrals and fewer elective tests than before. Which incentive most directly explains this change?", "options": {"A": "Fee for service rewards volume, so the group is now paid more for each additional referral", "B": "Bundled payment sets one price for an episode of care across hospital and rehabilitation services", "C": "Capitation shifts financial risk to the group, rewarding lower utilization and cost containment for each member", "D": "Pay for performance links bonuses to quality metrics, which discourages unnecessary testing through reported benchmarks", "E": "Global budgeting caps total hospital spending annually, so the group sees reduced referrals to specialists"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Capitation shifts financial risk to the group, rewarding lower utilization and cost containment for each member): Capitation pays a fixed amount per enrolled person, so the group keeps the savings when it provides less care, which discourages extra referrals and tests. Fee for service pays for each service and promotes volume. Bundled payment prices an episode, and pay for performance rewards measured quality rather than a fixed per-member payment."}, {"id": 455, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 71-year-old man hospitalized with lobar pneumonia is improving on appropriate antibiotics but still has a productive cough on hospital day three. The attending, an osteopathic physician, adds a thoracic lymphatic pump to his care and explains the rationale to the resident. Which mechanism best explains the expected benefit of this technique?", "options": {"A": "Direct mechanical killing of alveolar bacteria by rhythmic compression transmitted to the consolidated lobe", "B": "Restoration of segmental rib motion that reverses consolidation through improved alveolar ventilation", "C": "Reflex sympathetic stimulation producing bronchodilation and faster resolution of the alveolar exudate", "D": "Pressure changes that enhance lymph flow toward the thoracic duct and mobilize immune cells", "E": "Increased vagal tone that suppresses cytokine release and shortens the inflammatory response"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pressure changes that enhance lymph flow toward the thoracic duct and mobilize immune cells): The thoracic pump alters intrathoracic and abdominal pressures, which increases lymphatic return toward the thoracic duct and is thought to mobilize immune cells and clear inflammatory fluid. It does not kill bacteria directly, and rib motion, sympathetic, or vagal effects are not the primary rationale for this technique."}, {"id": 456, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 56-year-old man reports episodic facial flushing, watery diarrhea, and wheezing. Examination shows a tricuspid regurgitation murmur and an enlarged nodular liver. Urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid is elevated, and CT shows a small ileal mass with multiple liver lesions. Which mechanism best explains why his systemic symptoms developed only after the tumor spread?", "options": {"A": "Tumor cells secrete excess tryptophan hydroxylase that circulates and converts dietary tryptophan to serotonin in plasma", "B": "Ileal tumor cells secrete serotonin into the portal vein, where it escapes metabolism in the liver", "C": "Hepatic metastases release serotonin directly into the hepatic veins, bypassing first-pass degradation by hepatic monoamine oxidase", "D": "Tumor invasion of the mesentery causes lymphatic obstruction that delays serotonin clearance through the thoracic duct", "E": "Liver metastases cause portal hypertension, diverting intestinal serotonin around the liver through gastroesophageal varices"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hepatic metastases release serotonin directly into the hepatic veins, bypassing first-pass degradation by hepatic monoamine oxidase): Serotonin from a midgut tumor drains into the portal vein, where monoamine oxidase in hepatocytes inactivates it to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid, so a small primary tumor is usually silent. Once metastases drain directly into the hepatic veins, serotonin reaches the systemic circulation and causes flushing, diarrhea, bronchospasm, and right-sided valvular fibrosis. Portal delivery alone does not produce the syndrome because the liver clears it."}, {"id": 457, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A new rapid antigen test for streptococcal pharyngitis is compared with throat culture in 1,000 children seen at an urgent care center, and the results are given in the table shown. Which value best represents the probability that a child with a negative rapid test truly does not have the infection?", "options": {"A": "97 percent, the true negatives divided by every child who tested negative", "B": "3 percent, the false negatives divided by every child who tested negative", "C": "90 percent, the true negatives divided by every child who is truly disease free", "D": "90 percent, the true positives divided by every child who truly has disease", "E": "69 percent, the true positives divided by every child who tested positive"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (97 percent, the true negatives divided by every child who tested negative): Negative predictive value is true negatives divided by all negative results: 720 of 740, about 97 percent. Specificity is 720 of 800 (90 percent) because it conditions on children who truly lack infection, which is a different denominator. Positive predictive value (180 of 260, about 69 percent) concerns positive results, not negative ones.", "image": "question_images/q_457.png"}, {"id": 458, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has proximal weakness, new hypokalemia, hypertension, and hyperpigmentation. He has lost weight and has no facial plethora. Hormonal testing is shown in the table. Which is the most likely source of hormone excess?", "options": {"A": "Ectopic ACTH secretion by a neuroendocrine lung tumor, resistant to glucocorticoid feedback suppression", "B": "Adrenal carcinoma secreting cortisol and androgens with suppressed ACTH and rapid virilization", "C": "Primary pigmented nodular adrenal disease producing cortisol independent of ACTH stimulation", "D": "Pituitary corticotroph adenoma retaining partial feedback sensitivity to high-dose glucocorticoid administration overnight", "E": "Autonomous cortisol-secreting adrenal adenoma suppressing pituitary ACTH release through negative feedback"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ectopic ACTH secretion by a neuroendocrine lung tumor, resistant to glucocorticoid feedback suppression): A very high ACTH with pigmentation, hypokalemia, and no suppression after either dose of dexamethasone points to ectopic ACTH, most often small cell lung cancer. Pituitary adenomas usually suppress at least partly with the high dose. Adrenal sources have low ACTH.", "image": "question_images/q_458.png"}, {"id": 459, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 57-year-old man with a body mass index of 38 and a neck circumference of 44 cm is scheduled for elective knee replacement. He snores loudly, feels tired during the day, and has treated hypertension, but no one has witnessed apneas. Applying the STOP-BANG screening tool, what is his risk and best preoperative step?", "options": {"A": "Low risk because no apneas were witnessed; proceed with routine postoperative monitoring only", "B": "High risk with a score of seven; delay surgery until a full CPAP titration is completed", "C": "Intermediate risk with a score of four; cancel surgery until polysomnography confirms the diagnosis", "D": "High risk with a score of seven; manage as presumed sleep apnea with opioid-sparing precautions", "E": "Low risk with a score of three; no additional preoperative precautions or monitoring are required"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (High risk with a score of seven; manage as presumed sleep apnea with opioid-sparing precautions): He scores one point each for snoring, tiredness, hypertension, body mass index over 35, age over 50, neck over 40 cm, and male sex, for a total of seven, which is high risk. Surgery need not be delayed for full testing, and the safe approach is to treat him as having presumed sleep apnea with opioid-sparing analgesia, monitoring, and CPAP available."}, {"id": 460, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 62-year-old man with Parkinson disease has greasy, yellowish scaling and mild erythema along his nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and scalp that wax and wane. Symptoms worsen during winter and stress. He has had no new medications. Which treatment targets the likely contributing organism?", "options": {"A": "Topical permethrin, a pyrethroid that prolongs sodium channel opening in scabies mites", "B": "Topical terbinafine, an allylamine that inhibits squalene epoxidase in dermatophytes of keratinized tissue", "C": "Topical nystatin, a polyene that binds ergosterol and forms pores in Candida membranes", "D": "Topical ketoconazole, an azole that inhibits ergosterol synthesis in Malassezia yeast", "E": "Topical mupirocin, an antibiotic that inhibits bacterial isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase in staphylococci"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Topical ketoconazole, an azole that inhibits ergosterol synthesis in Malassezia yeast): Greasy scale in sebum-rich areas that fluctuates with season and stress, especially in Parkinson disease, is seborrheic dermatitis, in which Malassezia yeast contributes to inflammation. Topical azoles such as ketoconazole inhibit fungal ergosterol synthesis and are first-line. Terbinafine and nystatin target other fungi, and mupirocin and permethrin target bacteria and mites."}, {"id": 461, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 44-year-old woman has resistant hypertension at 168/102 mm Hg despite three medications, with muscle weakness and polyuria. Plasma renin activity is suppressed, and aldosterone is elevated. A basic metabolic panel is obtained. Which combination of findings is most expected in this patient?", "options": {"A": "High potassium and high bicarbonate, from impaired distal hydrogen secretion with potassium retention and volume expansion", "B": "Low sodium and low potassium, from natriuresis and volume depletion due to excess mineralocorticoid action", "C": "Low potassium and high bicarbonate, from collecting duct sodium reabsorption driving potassium and hydrogen secretion", "D": "High potassium and low bicarbonate, from reduced collecting duct sodium reabsorption limiting potassium and hydrogen secretion", "E": "Low potassium and low bicarbonate, from proximal bicarbonate wasting with increased potassium excretion in urine"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low potassium and high bicarbonate, from collecting duct sodium reabsorption driving potassium and hydrogen secretion): Aldosterone increases epithelial sodium channel and sodium potassium ATPase activity in principal cells, so sodium is retained while potassium is secreted, and alpha intercalated cells secrete more hydrogen. The result is hypertension with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. Low renin separates this from secondary causes such as renal artery stenosis."}, {"id": 462, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman has taken tamoxifen for 3 years after resection of an estrogen receptor positive breast carcinoma. She now reports new vaginal spotting without pain or fever. Pelvic examination shows a normal cervix and no adnexal masses. Which pharmacologic mechanism best explains the risk behind this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Aromatase inhibition raising gonadotropin drive and increasing ovarian estrogen secretion after menopause", "B": "Estrogen receptor degradation lowering endometrial thickness while causing breakthrough bleeding from fragile atrophic mucosa", "C": "HER2 receptor blockade on glandular epithelium triggering compensatory growth signaling within the endometrial lining", "D": "Progesterone receptor antagonism leaving the endometrium unopposed and prone to withdrawal bleeding after menopause", "E": "Partial estrogen agonist activity in uterine tissue, stimulating endometrial proliferation despite breast antagonism"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Partial estrogen agonist activity in uterine tissue, stimulating endometrial proliferation despite breast antagonism): Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator that antagonizes the receptor in breast tissue but acts as a partial agonist in the endometrium. This raises the risk of hyperplasia, polyps, and carcinoma, so postmenopausal bleeding on therapy needs evaluation. Aromatase inhibitors lower estrogen and do not stimulate the uterus."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 9-year-old boy is brought by his mother because teachers report he cannot stay seated, loses assignments and blurts out answers. Symptoms appear at home and at school and began before age six. His physician starts methylphenidate. What is the primary mechanism of this drug?", "options": {"A": "Agonism at presynaptic alpha-2A receptors, reducing norepinephrine release from prefrontal neurons", "B": "Inhibition of monoamine oxidase, preventing breakdown of dopamine and serotonin stored in nerve terminals", "C": "Blocking the dopamine and norepinephrine transporters, increasing synaptic catecholamine availability in prefrontal circuits", "D": "Selective inhibition of presynaptic norepinephrine reuptake without any direct action on dopamine transporters", "E": "Direct postsynaptic dopamine D2 receptor blockade, reducing mesolimbic hyperactivity and impulsive behavior"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blocking the dopamine and norepinephrine transporters, increasing synaptic catecholamine availability in prefrontal circuits): Methylphenidate blocks reuptake by the dopamine and norepinephrine transporters, which raises catecholamine signaling in prefrontal circuits and improves attention and impulse control. Atomoxetine acts on the norepinephrine transporter alone, and guanfacine acts through alpha-2A receptors, so they are the closest alternatives."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 43-year-old woman has resistant hypertension despite three medications, with blood pressure 164/102 mm Hg, muscle cramps, and serum potassium 3.1 mEq/L. Serum sodium is 144 mEq/L and bicarbonate is 31 mEq/L. There is no edema. Which finding pattern would best confirm the likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Elevated plasma aldosterone with elevated plasma renin activity, reflecting secondary activation of the renin pathway", "B": "Elevated urinary metanephrines with normal aldosterone, reflecting catecholamine excess from a chromaffin tumor", "C": "Elevated late-night salivary cortisol with normal renin, reflecting autonomous glucocorticoid production by the adrenal", "D": "Elevated plasma aldosterone with suppressed plasma renin activity, giving a high aldosterone to renin ratio", "E": "Low plasma aldosterone with suppressed renin activity, reflecting excess of a different mineralocorticoid agonist"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevated plasma aldosterone with suppressed plasma renin activity, giving a high aldosterone to renin ratio): Hypertension with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis suggests primary aldosteronism. Autonomous aldosterone raises volume and suppresses renin, so the aldosterone to renin ratio is high. High renin with high aldosterone would be secondary, such as from renal artery stenosis."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has seen her family physician for hypertension for 6 years. She recently divorced, mentions that she has always felt very close to him, and invites him to dinner. He finds her attractive and feels flattered. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Decline kindly, explaining that dating a current patient is inappropriate, and continue her care professionally", "B": "Accept only after a mandatory one-year waiting period following the last clinical visit between them", "C": "Decline, then refer her to another physician because the relationship is already too personal", "D": "Accept after transferring her care to a colleague, since the physician-patient relationship then ends immediately", "E": "Accept the dinner, provided the meeting stays social with no physical contact or prescribing involved"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline kindly, explaining that dating a current patient is inappropriate, and continue her care professionally): Romantic involvement with a current patient exploits the power imbalance and is considered unethical, so he should decline kindly and keep treating her. Transferring care in order to date, or waiting a set period, does not remove that imbalance after a six year relationship. Referral is not needed when professional boundaries can simply be maintained."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 24-year-old woman at an outdoor picnic is stung by a bee and within ten minutes develops diffuse hives, wheezing, lip swelling and light-headedness. Her blood pressure is 82/50 mm Hg and heart rate is 128/min. She uses an albuterol inhaler for mild asthma. Which intervention should be given first?", "options": {"A": "Intramuscular epinephrine into the lateral thigh to reverse vasodilation, edema and bronchospasm at once", "B": "Rapid normal saline bolus of two liters to restore intravascular volume before other drugs", "C": "Intravenous diphenhydramine to block histamine receptors and reduce cutaneous and airway swelling", "D": "Nebulized albuterol to relieve bronchospasm while monitoring oxygen saturation and blood pressure", "E": "Intravenous methylprednisolone to suppress late phase inflammation and prevent a biphasic reaction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intramuscular epinephrine into the lateral thigh to reverse vasodilation, edema and bronchospasm at once): Anaphylaxis with hypotension and airway involvement requires immediate intramuscular epinephrine, which acts on alpha and beta receptors to restore vascular tone and relieve bronchospasm. The other measures are adjuncts: antihistamines and steroids act too slowly, and fluids help only after the receptor effects are addressed."}, {"id": 467, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 52-year-old man has two years of stabbing leg pains and unsteady walking that worsens in the dark. Examination shows small pupils that constrict with accommodation but not to light, absent ankle reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense in the feet. The cerebrospinal fluid analysis shown was obtained. Which pathologic change best explains his gait?", "options": {"A": "Vermian cerebellar cortex degeneration producing truncal ataxia from loss of Purkinje cells", "B": "Anterior horn cell degeneration producing lower motor neuron weakness of the legs", "C": "Demyelination of the lateral corticospinal tracts producing spastic gait with hyperreflexia", "D": "Dorsal root and posterior column degeneration abolishing proprioceptive input needed for balance", "E": "Dorsal column demyelination from cobalamin deficiency with associated lateral corticospinal tract involvement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dorsal root and posterior column degeneration abolishing proprioceptive input needed for balance): Tabes dorsalis of tertiary neurosyphilis causes degeneration of the dorsal roots and posterior columns, which removes position and vibration sense, abolishes stretch reflexes, and produces a sensory ataxia worse without visual compensation. Pupils that accommodate but do not react are the Argyll Robertson sign. Cobalamin deficiency also affects dorsal columns but gives hyperreflexia from corticospinal involvement, not absent reflexes.", "image": "question_images/q_467.png"}, {"id": 468, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 7-year-old boy has colicky abdominal pain, swollen painful knees, and a palpable purpuric rash over his buttocks and lower legs, beginning two weeks after an upper respiratory infection. He is afebrile and well appearing. The laboratory panel shown was obtained. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Hemolytic uremic syndrome after diarrheal illness with microangiopathic anemia and acute kidney injury", "B": "Immune thrombocytopenia after viral illness with antibody mediated platelet destruction and mucosal bleeding", "C": "Kawasaki disease with medium vessel vasculitis causing prolonged fever, conjunctivitis, and coronary artery changes", "D": "Meningococcemia with disseminated intravascular coagulation causing petechiae, fever, hypotension, and prolonged clotting times", "E": "IgA vasculitis with small vessel immune complex deposition involving skin, joints, bowel, and kidney"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (IgA vasculitis with small vessel immune complex deposition involving skin, joints, bowel, and kidney): Palpable purpura on the buttocks and legs with abdominal pain, arthritis, and hematuria in a child after an infection is IgA vasculitis, and the normal platelet count and clotting times show the rash is not from a bleeding disorder. Immune thrombocytopenia and hemolytic uremic syndrome both lower the platelet count, which the panel excludes.", "image": "question_images/q_468.png"}, {"id": 469, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 9-year-old boy hospitalized with lobar pneumonia and a productive cough is receiving antibiotics. The osteopathic physician performs rib raising from T2 to T9 using paravertebral soft tissue traction. Based on the sympathetic outflow to the lungs and chest wall mechanics, which effect is the most reasonable goal of this treatment?", "options": {"A": "Increase sympathetic tone to the bronchial tree to widen airways and thin secretions", "B": "Stretch the diaphragm crura to increase intra-abdominal pressure and propel sputum upward", "C": "Mobilize the thoracic curve to treat the underlying consolidation by improving local arterial perfusion", "D": "Improve rib cage excursion and reduce excess sympathetic tone to aid lymphatic and mucus clearance", "E": "Raise parasympathetic tone through vagal facilitation to increase bronchial gland secretion and cough"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Improve rib cage excursion and reduce excess sympathetic tone to aid lymphatic and mucus clearance): Rib raising is an adjunct that improves chest wall motion and lowers sympathetic hyperactivity at the thoracic levels supplying the lungs, which supports lymphatic flow and secretion clearance. It does not treat the infection, and its levels are sympathetic rather than vagal."}, {"id": 470, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 27-year-old man undergoes splenectomy after a motor vehicle collision. Six weeks later, a routine peripheral blood smear is reviewed during a clinic visit. He feels well, has normal vital signs, and his platelet count is mildly elevated. Which finding on the smear is most expected?", "options": {"A": "Schistocytes, representing red cells fragmented by fibrin strands within the small vessels", "B": "Bite cells, representing red cells that had Heinz bodies pitted out by splenic macrophages", "C": "Teardrop cells, representing red cells deformed during marrow fibrosis and extramedullary hematopoiesis", "D": "Howell-Jolly bodies, representing nuclear remnants no longer pitted out by splenic macrophages", "E": "Spherocytes, representing red cells that lost membrane through repeated passage in splenic cords"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Howell-Jolly bodies, representing nuclear remnants no longer pitted out by splenic macrophages): The spleen removes nuclear remnants from red cells, so after splenectomy circulating Howell-Jolly bodies appear, along with mild thrombocytosis and target cells. Spherocytes and bite cells arise from splenic conditioning that no longer occurs. Schistocytes would require a microangiopathic process, and teardrop cells would suggest marrow fibrosis."}, {"id": 471, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 26-year-old nurse has low back pain after a long shift. At L4, motion is free only into flexion, left rotation, and left sidebending, while extension and rightward motions are restricted. Adjacent segments move normally, and no group curve appears in neutral. She also reports a mild recent sore throat. Which diagnosis fits?", "options": {"A": "L4 flexed, rotated left, sidebent right, a type I single segment dysfunction", "B": "L4 flexed, rotated left, sidebent left, a type II single segment dysfunction", "C": "L4 flexed, rotated left, sidebent left, a type III compound dysfunction pattern", "D": "L4 extended, rotated right, sidebent right, a type II single segment dysfunction", "E": "L3 to L5 neutral, rotated left, sidebent right, a type I group dysfunction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (L4 flexed, rotated left, sidebent left, a type II single segment dysfunction): One segment moving freely only into flexion with sidebending and rotation to the same side is a type II single segment dysfunction named for its free directions. A neutral group curve is excluded because neighbors move normally and no group appears in neutral. The sore throat is an unrelated detail."}, {"id": 472, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "During emergency central venous access at the right groin of a hypotensive 66-year-old man, the physician palpates a strong femoral pulse just below the inguinal ligament. To enter the femoral vein, the needle should be directed relative to the pulse at which position?", "options": {"A": "Directly posterior to the pulsation, deep to the artery within the sheath", "B": "Immediately medial to the pulsation, in its own compartment of the sheath", "C": "Immediately lateral to the pulsation, beside the nerve outside the sheath", "D": "Two centimeters above the pulsation, where the vessel lies beneath the inguinal ligament", "E": "Directly over the pulsation, because the vein overlies the artery in the triangle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Immediately medial to the pulsation, in its own compartment of the sheath): From lateral to medial the femoral triangle holds the nerve, then the artery, then the vein, with the nerve outside the femoral sheath. The vein therefore lies immediately medial to the arterial pulse, and puncture above the inguinal ligament risks retroperitoneal bleeding."}, {"id": 473, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 75-year-old woman with no advance directive is unresponsive after a massive stroke and needs a decision about placement of a feeding tube. Her husband died years ago. She has two adult children who disagree, a sibling, and a close friend of 40 years. How should the physician proceed?", "options": {"A": "Her sibling decides because siblings outrank adult children in the usual surrogate hierarchy", "B": "Her close friend decides, because friendship of long duration shows the deepest knowledge of her values", "C": "The attending physician decides alone using best judgment, since the family cannot reach agreement", "D": "Adult children act as surrogates using her known wishes, with ethics consultation if they remain divided", "E": "The feeding tube is withheld by default until a court appoints a guardian for her"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adult children act as surrogates using her known wishes, with ethics consultation if they remain divided): With no spouse or directive, most state hierarchies place adult children next, and they should apply substituted judgment based on what she would have wanted. When they cannot agree, an ethics consultation is the usual next step before any court involvement. The physician does not take over the decision just because the family is divided."}, {"id": 474, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 52-year-old man with end-stage renal disease misses dialysis for two weeks and arrives with generalized weakness. Serum potassium is 7.4 mEq/L. His ECG shows peaked T waves, flattened P waves, and a QRS of 130 ms. Blood glucose is 110 mg/dL. Which intervention should be given first?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous regular insulin with dextrose, shifting potassium into cells by stimulating sodium potassium ATPase activity", "B": "Intravenous sodium bicarbonate, shifting potassium into cells by raising the extracellular pH", "C": "Nebulized albuterol, driving potassium into cells through beta-2 receptor activation of membrane pumps", "D": "Intravenous calcium gluconate, raising the threshold potential and restoring membrane stability within minutes", "E": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate, removing potassium through exchange resin binding in the colon"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intravenous calcium gluconate, raising the threshold potential and restoring membrane stability within minutes): ECG changes with a widened QRS signal imminent conduction failure, so membrane stabilization with intravenous calcium comes first because it acts within minutes but does not lower potassium. Insulin with dextrose and albuterol then shift potassium into cells over the following half hour, and dialysis removes it definitively."}, {"id": 475, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 60-year-old smoker has 2 months of right shoulder pain radiating down the inner forearm to the fourth and fifth fingers. He now has a drooping right eyelid, a smaller right pupil, and absent sweating on the right face. A chest radiograph shows a right apical opacity. Compression of which structure explains the eyelid, pupil, and sweating findings?", "options": {"A": "Right recurrent laryngeal nerve around the subclavian artery, which carries motor fibers to the vocal cords", "B": "Superior vena cava, whose obstruction produces facial plethora and dilated collateral veins over the chest", "C": "Phrenic nerve on the mediastinal surface, which carries C3 to C5 fibers to the diaphragm", "D": "Lower trunk of the brachial plexus, which carries C8 and T1 fibers to the hand", "E": "Cervical sympathetic chain at the stellate ganglion, which carries sympathetic fibers to the eye and face"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cervical sympathetic chain at the stellate ganglion, which carries sympathetic fibers to the eye and face): A right apical tumor in a smoker is a superior sulcus (Pancoast) tumor. Involvement of the lower brachial plexus produces the ulnar distribution pain, while interruption of the stellate ganglion sympathetics causes ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis, which is Horner syndrome. The brachial plexus explains the pain but not the eye findings."}, {"id": 476, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 55-year-old man with long-standing COPD developed fever and productive cough two days ago and is diagnosed with lobar pneumonia. On examination there is warm, moist, edematous tissue with erythema over the right T2 to T6 paraspinal region, and the patient also notes low back stiffness. What is the best interpretation of the T2 to T6 finding?", "options": {"A": "Primary somatic dysfunction from gardening strain, with local muscle spasm independent of visceral input", "B": "Acute viscerosomatic reflex from the pulmonary infection, with sympathetic segmental facilitation at that level", "C": "Somatovisceral reflex in which segmental dysfunction caused the pneumonia by impairing bronchial defenses", "D": "Referred cardiac reflex from myocardial ischemia, with left-sided changes at the T1 to T5 levels", "E": "Chronic viscerosomatic reflex from long-standing COPD, with fibrotic tissue changes from sustained facilitation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acute viscerosomatic reflex from the pulmonary infection, with sympathetic segmental facilitation at that level): Warm, moist, edematous, erythematous tissue indicates an acute process, and its T2 to T6 location matches the segmental supply of the lung, so it reflects viscerosomatic facilitation from the new pneumonia. Chronic COPD would produce cool, fibrotic, ropy tissue, and a cardiac reflex would involve the heart's levels, not a right-sided lung pattern."}, {"id": 477, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 28-year-old woman at 10 weeks of gestation has a first prenatal visit. Laboratory testing shows no rubella IgG antibodies. She works in a daycare and asks to be vaccinated today to protect her pregnancy. She is otherwise healthy with no allergies. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Give the live vaccine today and advise avoiding conception for twelve months afterward", "B": "Give rubella immune globulin today to provide passive protection throughout the remaining pregnancy", "C": "Withhold the live vaccine now and give it postpartum before hospital discharge", "D": "Check a repeat rubella titer in four weeks and vaccinate if the titer remains low", "E": "Offer the vaccine at 28 weeks of gestation, once organogenesis is complete"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold the live vaccine now and give it postpartum before hospital discharge): Measles, mumps and rubella vaccine contains live attenuated virus and is not given during pregnancy because of a theoretical fetal risk. Nonimmune women are vaccinated after delivery, ideally before leaving the hospital. Immune globulin is not recommended for rubella prevention, and waiting for a later trimester does not remove the contraindication."}, {"id": 478, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 68-year-old woman in the emergency department has fever, confusion, and hypotension after two days of dysuria. Her skin is warm and flushed, pulses are bounding, and urine output is low. Blood cultures have been drawn and antibiotics are being prepared. Which hemodynamic profile best matches her early presentation?", "options": {"A": "Decreased systemic vascular resistance with decreased cardiac output and cold extremities from myocardial pump depression", "B": "Normal systemic vascular resistance with decreased cardiac output and elevated right atrial pressure from impaired filling", "C": "Increased systemic vascular resistance with decreased cardiac output and low right atrial and wedge pressures", "D": "Decreased systemic vascular resistance with normal or elevated cardiac output and low or normal wedge pressure", "E": "Increased systemic vascular resistance with decreased cardiac output and elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decreased systemic vascular resistance with normal or elevated cardiac output and low or normal wedge pressure): Early septic shock is distributive: cytokine driven vasodilation lowers resistance, and cardiac output is preserved or raised, producing warm skin and bounding pulses. Filling pressures are low or normal because of venous pooling and capillary leak. Cold extremities with a low output appear later in sepsis, and high resistance profiles belong to cardiogenic and hypovolemic states."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 17-year-old girl with bulimia nervosa binges and purges five times weekly and has failed to improve with a self-help program. She also has a history of a generalized seizure during an earlier illness. Which medication is the most appropriate addition to psychotherapy?", "options": {"A": "Bupropion sustained release, a dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor that reduces binge and purge episodes", "B": "Fluoxetine at a higher dose, an SSRI with evidence for reducing episodes of overeating and vomiting", "C": "Olanzapine daily, an atypical antipsychotic that promotes weight gain and treats the core cognitive distortions", "D": "Lorazepam before meals, a benzodiazepine that lowers anticipatory anxiety and decreases urges to purge", "E": "Lithium carbonate daily, a mood stabilizer that regulates impulsive behavior across eating disorders"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fluoxetine at a higher dose, an SSRI with evidence for reducing episodes of overeating and vomiting): Fluoxetine is the only medication approved for bulimia nervosa and is used at higher doses than for depression. Bupropion is contraindicated because purging and electrolyte disturbance lower the seizure threshold, a risk that is higher here given her seizure history. Lithium is unsafe when vomiting causes volume and sodium loss."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "community_health", "question": "An 80-year-old woman with moderate dementia lives with her son, who is her only caregiver. She arrives from home with a stage 3 sacral pressure ulcer, dehydration, soiled clothing and weight loss. The son declines to leave the room and says he manages her bank accounts. Bruising is absent. Which form of elder mistreatment best fits?", "options": {"A": "Physical abuse, intentional force by the caregiver causing pain, injury or restraint-related harm", "B": "Financial exploitation, improper use of her funds that leaves her unable to buy care", "C": "Neglect, a caregiver's failure to meet needs for food, fluids, hygiene and pressure relief", "D": "Psychological abuse, verbal or emotional mistreatment by the caregiver causing fear, isolation or distress", "E": "Self-neglect, inability of an older adult living alone to attend to her own needs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Neglect, a caregiver's failure to meet needs for food, fluids, hygiene and pressure relief): Pressure ulcers, dehydration, poor hygiene and weight loss in a dependent person whose caregiver is responsible for her care indicate neglect. Control of finances alone could raise concern for exploitation, but the physical findings here are failures of care rather than misuse of money. Self-neglect applies when no caregiver exists, and absence of injuries argues against physical abuse."}, {"id": 481, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 40-year-old man with chronic constipation and a restricted lumbar spine is treated with osteopathic manipulation. The supervising physician explains that restoring segmental motion in the spine can improve motility of the bowel because the nerves serving both are linked. Which tenet of osteopathic medicine does this explanation best illustrate?", "options": {"A": "Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered framework mechanics can change organ behavior", "B": "The body is a unit, and the person is a combination of body, mind, and spirit", "C": "Rational treatment rests on understanding body unity, self-regulation, and the reciprocal link between structure and function", "D": "Somatic dysfunction is recognized by tissue texture change, asymmetry, restriction of motion, and tenderness", "E": "The body has inherent self-regulatory mechanisms that support healing and the maintenance of health"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered framework mechanics can change organ behavior): The stem links a spinal mechanical problem to organ function, which is the reciprocal relationship between structure and function. The unity and self-regulation tenets describe other ideas, and the fourth tenet concerns how treatment is chosen. The diagnostic criteria for somatic dysfunction are a useful fact but are not one of the four tenets."}, {"id": 482, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A newborn girl in the nursery has downward-slanting palpebral fissures, a markedly hypoplastic mandible, malformed external ears, and a conductive hearing deficit on screening. Cardiac examination is normal and serum calcium is normal. Which developmental mechanism best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Persistence of the cervical sinus, leaving a lateral neck cyst lined by pharyngeal remnants", "B": "Defective differentiation of the third pharyngeal pouch, impairing thymic and inferior parathyroid development", "C": "Impaired neural crest migration into the first pharyngeal arch, disrupting jaw and ear development", "D": "Failed fusion of maxillary and medial nasal prominences, producing an upper lip cleft", "E": "Abnormal second arch cartilage formation, impairing the stapes, styloid process and hyoid bone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Impaired neural crest migration into the first pharyngeal arch, disrupting jaw and ear development): Treacher Collins syndrome results from impaired neural crest migration into the first pharyngeal arch, producing mandibular and malar hypoplasia, ear malformation and conductive hearing loss. A third pouch defect would produce hypocalcemia and immune deficiency, which the normal calcium argues against. Second arch derivatives such as the stapes would not explain the small jaw."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 62-year-old man who lifts heavy pallets at work has a groin bulge that appears with straining and reduces when supine. At surgery, the sac exits through the deep inguinal ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and descends within the spermatic cord. Which type of hernia is this, and what is its cause?", "options": {"A": "Spigelian hernia through the semilunar line at the lateral rectus border", "B": "Direct inguinal hernia through weakened transversalis fascia in the inguinal triangle", "C": "Indirect inguinal hernia following a persistent peritoneal outpouching along the path of testicular descent", "D": "Obturator hernia through the obturator canal beside the obturator vessels", "E": "Femoral hernia through the femoral ring medial to the femoral vein"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Indirect inguinal hernia following a persistent peritoneal outpouching along the path of testicular descent): A sac lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels that traverses the deep ring and enters the cord is an indirect hernia, arising from a patent processus vaginalis. A direct hernia lies medial to those vessels and pushes through the posterior wall. Femoral, Spigelian, and obturator hernias have different anatomy."}, {"id": 484, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 17-year-old girl in the emergency department has 18 hours of periumbilical pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, with anorexia and a temperature of 38.2 C. The osteopathic examination finds a small, tender, ropy nodule at the tip of the right twelfth rib. Which interpretation and plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Chapman reflex from appendiceal irritation; arrange urgent surgical evaluation and imaging, not OMT alone", "B": "Rib somatic dysfunction alone; treat with muscle energy and discharge with outpatient follow up", "C": "Viscerosomatic reflex from the ovary; arrange pelvic ultrasound and treat with counterstrain meanwhile", "D": "Referred pain from the gallbladder; obtain right upper quadrant ultrasound and treat the rib with HVLA", "E": "Chapman reflex from renal irritation; obtain urinalysis and defer surgical evaluation pending culture results"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chapman reflex from appendiceal irritation; arrange urgent surgical evaluation and imaging, not OMT alone): A tender ropy nodule at the tip of the right twelfth rib is the classic anterior Chapman reflex for the appendix, and with migrating pain, anorexia, and fever it signals probable appendicitis. Chapman reflexes support diagnosis but never replace surgical evaluation and imaging, since delay risks perforation. Rib dysfunction or renal reflexes do not explain the migration pattern."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 7-year-old boy has intellectual disability, a long narrow face, large everted ears, and a prominent jaw. Examination shows enlarged testes and hyperextensible finger joints. His maternal uncle has similar features. Genetic testing detects an abnormal change at a single locus on the X chromosome. Which molecular mechanism best explains his condition?", "options": {"A": "Untranslated CTG repeat expansion causing toxic RNA that sequesters splicing regulatory proteins in muscle", "B": "Intronic GAA repeat expansion impairing transcription of a mitochondrial iron-handling protein in neurons and cardiomyocytes", "C": "Expanded CAG repeat in a coding exon producing a toxic polyglutamine protein gain of function", "D": "Paternal allele deletion at an imprinted locus leaving only a silenced maternal gene copy", "E": "Hypermethylation and silencing of the FMR1 promoter after an excessive CGG repeat expansion"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Hypermethylation and silencing of the FMR1 promoter after an excessive CGG repeat expansion): Fragile X syndrome results from CGG repeat expansion in the 5 prime untranslated region of FMR1, causing promoter hypermethylation and loss of FMRP. The long face, large ears, macroorchidism and X-linked inheritance fit. The other repeat disorders involve different repeats, locations and mechanisms."}, {"id": 486, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has urinary frequency and urgency with a negative urinalysis. Osteopathic examination finds a sacral somatic dysfunction with tissue texture changes over the sacrum and restricted sacral motion. The physician explains that detrusor contraction depends on autonomic outflow that could be influenced by this region. Which pathway supplies the efferent drive for bladder emptying in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve relayed through the inferior mesenteric plexus to the bladder", "B": "Sympathetic fibers from T10 to T11 relayed through the lesser splanchnic nerves to the kidney", "C": "Somatic motor fibers from S2 to S4 relayed through the pudendal nerve to the sphincter", "D": "Sympathetic fibers from L1 to L2 relayed through the hypogastric nerves to the detrusor", "E": "Parasympathetic fibers from S2 to S4 relayed through the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the bladder"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Parasympathetic fibers from S2 to S4 relayed through the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the bladder): Detrusor contraction for voiding is parasympathetic, arising from S2 to S4 and carried by the pelvic splanchnic nerves. Sacral somatic dysfunction is osteopathically relevant because it lies near this outflow. The hypogastric sympathetic route promotes storage, and the pudendal nerve is somatic to the external sphincter."}, {"id": 487, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 58-year-old man with 20 years of heartburn that is only partly controlled with acid suppression undergoes upper endoscopy. The endoscopic view shown reveals a mucosal change at the gastroesophageal junction, and biopsies are taken. Which histologic finding would carry the greatest risk of progression to esophageal adenocarcinoma?", "options": {"A": "Columnar epithelium with goblet cells replacing the normal distal squamous lining", "B": "Ground glass nuclei and multinucleation with intranuclear inclusions in squamous epithelial cells", "C": "Basal cell hyperplasia with elongated papillae and scattered eosinophils in squamous mucosa", "D": "Gastric type columnar epithelium with parietal cells but no goblet cells in the distal esophagus", "E": "Squamous dysplasia with nuclear enlargement confined to the lower half of the epithelium"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Columnar epithelium with goblet cells replacing the normal distal squamous lining): Chronic reflux produces intestinal metaplasia in which columnar epithelium with goblet cells replaces squamous mucosa (Barrett esophagus), and this is the precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma through dysplasia. Basal zone hyperplasia is an ordinary reflux change without malignant potential, and viral inclusions suggest herpes esophagitis in immunocompromised patients.", "image": "question_images/q_487.png"}, {"id": 488, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 32-year-old woman who delivered twins 20 minutes ago after a prolonged labor has heavy vaginal bleeding. The placenta delivered intact and her uterus is soft, boggy and palpable above the umbilicus. She has well-controlled asthma, and blood pressure is 100/62 mm Hg. Bimanual massage is started. Which medication should be given first?", "options": {"A": "Misoprostol rectally to stimulate contractions as the preferred initial agent in hypotonic bleeding", "B": "Carboprost to contract the uterus through prostaglandin F2 alpha receptor activation", "C": "Methylergonovine to produce sustained uterine tetany through alpha-adrenergic and serotonergic receptor stimulation", "D": "Intravenous oxytocin to stimulate myometrial contraction, the standard first-line uterotonic agent for atony", "E": "Tranexamic acid to inhibit fibrinolysis and reduce bleeding from a hypotonic uterus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intravenous oxytocin to stimulate myometrial contraction, the standard first-line uterotonic agent for atony): Uterine atony after prolonged labor and twin delivery is treated with fundal massage and oxytocin first. Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2 alpha analog that can cause bronchoconstriction, so asthma makes it a poor early choice, and ergots are second-line. Tranexamic acid is an adjunct, not a replacement for a uterotonic."}, {"id": 489, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 16-year-old high school student washes her hands until they are raw, spends three hours daily checking appliances and recognizes that the fears are excessive but cannot resist the urges. Anxiety falls briefly after each ritual. Which combination is the best initial treatment?", "options": {"A": "Habit reversal training combined with buspirone to reduce compulsive urges and washing behavior", "B": "Exposure and response prevention therapy combined with a high-dose SSRI such as fluoxetine", "C": "Cognitive therapy for panic attacks combined with scheduled lorazepam to reduce ritual related anxiety", "D": "Thought stopping techniques combined with a tricyclic such as desipramine for noradrenergic effect", "E": "Supportive psychodynamic psychotherapy focused on childhood conflicts combined with a low-dose SSRI"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Exposure and response prevention therapy combined with a high-dose SSRI such as fluoxetine): Obsessive-compulsive disorder responds best to exposure and response prevention, in which the patient faces the trigger without performing the ritual, plus an SSRI, which often needs higher doses and longer trials than for depression. Clomipramine is a second-line serotonergic drug, but noradrenergic tricyclics like desipramine lack this effect. Benzodiazepines do not treat obsessions."}, {"id": 490, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 62-year-old man of Northern European descent notices a firm painless nodule in his right palm that has progressed to a cord. He can no longer fully straighten his ring finger and cannot lay the hand flat on a table. He has type 2 diabetes and drinks alcohol daily. Which tissue is primarily affected by this disorder?", "options": {"A": "Flexor tendon sheath, where stenosing inflammation at the first annular pulley causes catching of the digit", "B": "Transverse carpal ligament, where thickening compresses the median nerve and produces nocturnal thumb and finger paresthesias", "C": "First dorsal extensor compartment sheath, where stenosis causes radial wrist pain with thumb movement", "D": "Volar plate of the proximal interphalangeal joint, where hyperextension injury leaves a fixed flexion deformity", "E": "Palmar aponeurosis, where myofibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition shorten the fascia and flex the digit"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Palmar aponeurosis, where myofibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition shorten the fascia and flex the digit): Dupuytren contracture is a fibroproliferative disorder of the palmar aponeurosis, with myofibroblasts and thickened collagen forming nodules and cords that flex the ring and little fingers. The flexor tendons themselves are spared, which separates it from trigger finger. Risk factors include Northern European ancestry, diabetes and alcohol use."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 24-year-old woman develops a firm, itchy, raised scar on her earlobe that has extended well beyond the margins of her original piercing over eight months. Biopsy shows thick, glassy, eosinophilic collagen bundles. Compared with a hypertrophic scar, which feature is characteristic of this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Confinement to the original wound edges with frequent regression over one to two years", "B": "Collagen fibers arranged in parallel orientation with nodules containing abundant myofibroblasts", "C": "Predominance of type II collagen replacing the normal dermal matrix architecture", "D": "Epidermal hyperplasia driven by loss of contact inhibition among basal keratinocytes", "E": "Extension into surrounding normal skin with little tendency to regress over time"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Extension into surrounding normal skin with little tendency to regress over time): Keloids grow beyond the original wound boundary, show thick hyalinized collagen bundles, and rarely regress. Hypertrophic scars stay within the wound and often improve over a year or two. The matrix in both scars is type I and III collagen, not type II."}, {"id": 492, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A term neonate develops deepening jaundice on day 2, with total bilirubin rising above 25 mg/dL and nearly all of it unconjugated. Liver enzymes are normal and there is no hemolysis. Phototherapy gives only partial benefit, enzyme inducers do not lower the level, and the infant later develops lethargy and opisthotonos. Which defect best explains this course?", "options": {"A": "Mildly reduced UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity unmasked by fasting, producing benign intermittent unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "B": "Impaired hepatocyte uptake and storage of conjugated bilirubin from sinusoidal blood due to transporter defects", "C": "Absent hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity that blocks bilirubin conjugation and cannot be improved by enzyme induction", "D": "Congenital obliteration of extrahepatic bile ducts causing conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with pale stools and cholestasis", "E": "Defective canalicular MRP2 transport impairing biliary excretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile canaliculus"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Absent hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity that blocks bilirubin conjugation and cannot be improved by enzyme induction): Crigler-Najjar type I is complete loss of UGT1A1, so unconjugated bilirubin accumulates, crosses the immature blood-brain barrier and causes kernicterus, and induction fails because no enzyme exists to induce. Gilbert syndrome is the mild partial form and rarely reaches levels this high. Dubin-Johnson, Rotor syndrome and biliary atresia all raise conjugated bilirubin."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 32-year-old office worker has a bilateral band-like headache that worsens by late afternoon, without nausea or aura. Examination shows tender, hypertonic muscles at the base of the skull with tissue texture change. The physician places finger pads under the occiput and applies steady gentle upward pressure until tissue release. Which technique is being performed?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain, passive positioning of a tender point into its position of ease for about ninety seconds", "B": "High velocity low amplitude thrust, applied to a restrictive barrier to restore segmental motion rapidly", "C": "Occipitoatlantal articulatory technique, repetitive springing of the joint through its restricted range of motion", "D": "Muscle energy, an isometric patient contraction against a counterforce from the physician at the barrier", "E": "Suboccipital inhibition, a soft tissue technique using sustained pressure to relax overactive posterior neck muscles"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Suboccipital inhibition, a soft tissue technique using sustained pressure to relax overactive posterior neck muscles): Sustained gentle pressure over the muscles at the base of the skull until the tissue relaxes is suboccipital inhibition, a soft tissue technique commonly used for tension-type headache. No patient contraction or thrust is involved, which excludes muscle energy and high velocity low amplitude, and the physician is not positioning a tender point as in counterstrain."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 9-year-old girl has recurrent episodes of acute pancreatitis, and her father had the same illness. Genetic testing finds a gain-of-function change that makes trypsinogen resistant to degradation. In normal digestion, which step first activates the pancreatic proenzyme cascade within the duodenal lumen?", "options": {"A": "Brush border enteropeptidase cleaving trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates other pancreatic proenzymes", "B": "Secretin binding acinar cells to convert trypsinogen directly into active trypsin within the lumen", "C": "Gastric pepsin cleaving trypsinogen to trypsin before pancreatic juice reaches the duodenum", "D": "Cholecystokinin activating trypsinogen directly through receptor mediated proteolysis in acinar cell zymogen granules", "E": "Chymotrypsin cleaving trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the remaining pancreatic zymogens"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Brush border enteropeptidase cleaving trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates other pancreatic proenzymes): Enteropeptidase on the duodenal brush border cleaves trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates chymotrypsinogen, proelastase and procarboxypeptidase. In hereditary pancreatitis premature or persistent intrapancreatic trypsin activity overwhelms the protective inhibitors and digests the gland."}, {"id": 495, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 26-year-old man is stabbed in the back at the T10 level. Examination shows weakness and loss of vibration sense in the right leg, and loss of pain and temperature sensation in the left leg beginning about two levels below the injury. Which cord lesion explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Central cord lesion of the thoracic segments with suspended dissociated sensory loss", "B": "Anterior spinal artery infarction sparing the dorsal columns with bilateral motor and pain loss", "C": "Left hemisection of the cord interrupting ipsilateral dorsal column, corticospinal and crossed spinothalamic fibers", "D": "Complete transection of the cord with bilateral loss of all modalities below the level", "E": "Right hemisection of the cord interrupting dorsal column, corticospinal and crossed spinothalamic fibers"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Right hemisection of the cord interrupting dorsal column, corticospinal and crossed spinothalamic fibers): A hemisection produces ipsilateral loss of vibration and proprioception (dorsal columns, crossed in the medulla) and ipsilateral upper motor neuron weakness (corticospinal tract, crossed in the medulla), but contralateral loss of pain and temperature because spinothalamic fibers cross within a few segments of entering. Here the right leg has weakness and dorsal column loss, and the left leg lost pain. Complete transection would affect both sides."}, {"id": 496, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 19-year-old man given intramuscular haloperidol for agitation develops painful sustained contraction of the neck and upward deviation of his eyes within two hours. Vital signs are normal and he is alert. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Oral propranolol to control akathisia and tremor from dopamine blockade", "B": "Intravenous dantrolene to reduce skeletal muscle calcium release and heat production", "C": "Add oral bromocriptine to restore dopamine tone in the nigrostriatal pathway", "D": "Intramuscular benztropine, an antimuscarinic restoring balance between cholinergic and dopaminergic striatal activity", "E": "Intravenous lorazepam as a sedative replacement until the haloperidol effect clears"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intramuscular benztropine, an antimuscarinic restoring balance between cholinergic and dopaminergic striatal activity): Young men given a high potency antipsychotic can develop acute dystonia within hours as dopamine blockade leaves cholinergic activity unopposed in the striatum. An anticholinergic such as benztropine or diphenhydramine rapidly relieves it, while propranolol treats akathisia and dantrolene or bromocriptine are used for hyperthermic syndromes."}, {"id": 497, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 62-year-old woman has chronic ankle edema, and her lower leg fascia is tight, restricting venous and lymphatic return. The physician releases the fascial restriction, reasoning that the tissue's abnormal mechanics are what impair circulation, so normalizing the mechanics should normalize fluid movement. Which osteopathic principle most directly guides this treatment choice?", "options": {"A": "Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered tissue mechanics impair fluid movement", "B": "Somatic dysfunction is the usual root of illness, so treating restrictions takes priority over other care", "C": "The body is a unit, and the person is body, mind and spirit", "D": "The body is capable of self-regulation, self-healing and health maintenance once barriers are removed", "E": "Rational treatment is based on understanding the basic principles of body unity and self-regulation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered tissue mechanics impair fluid movement): The reasoning links an altered tissue structure to impaired function, which is the reciprocal relationship between structure and function. Self-regulation also supports healing, but this plan targets a mechanical cause of the disordered fluid movement directly. The fourth tenet integrates the others rather than identifying the specific link."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 38-year-old woman with chronic reflux and shallow breathing has reduced diaphragmatic excursion on palpation. Examination of the thoracic spine shows no findings. The physician wants to treat the spinal region whose nerve roots provide the motor innervation of the diaphragm. Which spinal levels should be examined and treated?", "options": {"A": "C3 to C5, whose anterior rami merge to form the phrenic nerve", "B": "T1 to T4, whose sympathetic outflow reaches the heart, lungs, and bronchi", "C": "L1 to L3, where the diaphragm crura attach to the vertebral bodies", "D": "C5 to C7, whose anterior rami form the upper and middle trunks of the brachial plexus", "E": "T6 to T12, whose anterior rami form the intercostal and subcostal nerves"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (C3 to C5, whose anterior rami merge to form the phrenic nerve): The phrenic nerve arises from C3, C4, and C5, so cervical somatic dysfunction there can impair diaphragm motion. The saying is that C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive. Lumbar levels mark where the crura attach but do not supply motor fibers."}, {"id": 499, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 74-year-old woman reports a new right temporal headache, jaw pain while chewing, and an episode of transient vision loss in the right eye. Her scalp is tender, the ESR is 96 mm/hr, and she takes a statin. Temporal artery biopsy is scheduled for next week. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Start methotrexate as steroid sparing therapy and recheck inflammatory markers over the next month", "B": "Begin tocilizumab monotherapy to avoid steroid toxicity while inflammatory markers are followed weekly", "C": "Order urgent MRI of the brain and start aspirin pending the imaging result", "D": "Await biopsy confirmation before treatment, because steroids will obscure histology within a few days", "E": "Start high dose glucocorticoids now and continue them while the biopsy is arranged"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Start high dose glucocorticoids now and continue them while the biopsy is arranged): Headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, a very high ESR, and transient vision loss indicate giant cell arteritis, where delayed treatment risks irreversible blindness. High dose glucocorticoids are started immediately because biopsy remains diagnostic for days to weeks after steroids begin. Steroid sparing agents are adjuncts, not initial therapy."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A former 26-week-gestation infant, now 36 weeks postmenstrual age, still requires supplemental oxygen and has tachypnea with diffuse crackles. His course included prolonged mechanical ventilation with high inspired oxygen for respiratory distress syndrome. Which pathologic change best characterizes this condition in current practice?", "options": {"A": "Congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation with multiple air-filled cysts replacing normal lung parenchyma", "B": "Severe bronchiolar epithelial necrosis with obliterative bronchiolitis and dense interstitial fibrosis, the classic pattern", "C": "Arrested alveolar and vascular growth leaving fewer, larger, simplified alveoli with only mild fibrosis", "D": "Persistent hyaline membranes lining terminal airspaces from ongoing surfactant deficiency beyond term", "E": "Pulmonary lymphangiectasia with dilated lymphatics and chylous fluid in interstitium and pleura"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrested alveolar and vascular growth leaving fewer, larger, simplified alveoli with only mild fibrosis): Bronchopulmonary dysplasia in the surfactant era follows injury to an immature lung and is dominated by arrested alveolar septation and abnormal vascular growth, giving fewer, larger alveoli with less fibrosis. The older form with severe airway necrosis and heavy fibrosis is now uncommon. Hyaline membranes belong to the acute phase."}, {"id": 501, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 34-year-old man develops perioral numbness, tingling and rapidly spreading limb weakness 30 minutes after eating pufferfish at a restaurant. Examination shows flaccid weakness, absent reflexes and preserved consciousness. Oxygen saturation is falling steadily. He has no fever. Which molecular action explains his symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels preventing action potential propagation along nerve axons", "B": "Cleavage of SNARE proteins preventing synaptic vesicle fusion in cholinergic terminals", "C": "Inhibition of presynaptic calcium channels preventing acetylcholine vesicle release at the junction", "D": "Irreversible binding of acetylcholine receptors blocking depolarization at the motor end plate", "E": "Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase causing persistent depolarization of the motor end plate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels preventing action potential propagation along nerve axons): Tetrodotoxin in pufferfish binds the outer pore of voltage-gated sodium channels and blocks the rising phase of the action potential, causing paresthesias followed by flaccid paralysis with preserved consciousness. Botulinum toxin and curare act at the neuromuscular junction, and organophosphates would cause cholinergic excess with fasciculations and miosis."}, {"id": 502, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 20-year-old man develops severe cramps and weakness within minutes of starting vigorous exercise but feels better after a brief rest and can then continue. Dark urine followed one episode. During forearm ischemic exercise, venous lactate fails to rise while ammonia rises normally. Which enzyme is deficient?", "options": {"A": "Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II, preventing long chain fatty acid entry into mitochondria", "B": "Phosphofructokinase 1 in muscle, blocking conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate", "C": "Myoadenylate deaminase, impairing purine nucleotide cycling and ammonia release during exertion", "D": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase, preventing release of glucose 1-phosphate from stored glycogen", "E": "Acid alpha-glucosidase, causing lysosomal glycogen accumulation with cardiomegaly and hypotonia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Muscle glycogen phosphorylase, preventing release of glucose 1-phosphate from stored glycogen): Exercise intolerance with a second wind, myoglobinuria, and a flat lactate but normal ammonia rise on forearm exercise indicates McArdle disease, deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase. Muscle phosphofructokinase deficiency also blocks lactate rise, but it more often features hemolysis and no second wind. Normal ammonia rise argues against myoadenylate deaminase deficiency."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 15-year-old girl presents alone to a rural clinic requesting evaluation for possible sexually transmitted infection. She declines to tell her parents and has no emergency, and her parents are unavailable by phone. The physician wants to provide care. Under most state laws, which statement about consent is correct?", "options": {"A": "She may consent, but the physician must notify her parents within twenty four hours", "B": "A court order must be obtained before any minor under sixteen can receive confidential care", "C": "Treatment must wait until a parent or guardian provides written consent by any method", "D": "She may consent to evaluation and treatment herself, and confidentiality applies as for adults", "E": "Care may proceed only under the emergency exception, which requires imminent threat to life"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She may consent to evaluation and treatment herself, and confidentiality applies as for adults): Most states allow minors to consent to diagnosis and treatment of sexually transmitted infections, and the visit remains confidential. This is a separate legal exception from the emergency exception, so no imminent danger is needed. Neither parental permission nor a court order is required."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old woman on postoperative day 4 after hip arthroplasty develops sudden dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. She is tachycardic with clear lungs, and her oxygen saturation is 89% on room air. Imaging confirms a thrombus in a branch of the right pulmonary artery. In the occluded lung zone, how do ventilation, perfusion and the V/Q ratio change?", "options": {"A": "Ventilation is preserved while perfusion falls, so the ratio rises toward infinity as dead space", "B": "Perfusion is preserved while ventilation falls, so the ratio approaches zero as a shunt", "C": "Ventilation rises to compensate while perfusion is unchanged, so the ratio increases modestly toward 1.0", "D": "Both ventilation and perfusion fall, but perfusion falls less, so the ratio drops below normal", "E": "Both fall equally, so the ratio stays normal and gas exchange in that zone continues"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ventilation is preserved while perfusion falls, so the ratio rises toward infinity as dead space): A pulmonary embolus blocks blood flow to a ventilated region, so the airways still receive gas but the capillaries do not receive blood. Perfusion approaches zero and V/Q approaches infinity, which is physiologic dead space. Low-ratio units and shunts arise from airway or alveolar filling problems, not vascular occlusion."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 55-year-old woman, a retired teacher who takes calcium supplements, reports six months of infrequent hard stools. On examination, the paraspinal tissues at L1 to L2 are cool, dry, and ropy with fibrotic texture bilaterally, and the sacrum is restricted. Which interpretation of these findings is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Primary sacral somatic dysfunction producing constipation, with no visceral contribution to the lumbar changes", "B": "Acute viscerosomatic reflex from the proximal colon with edematous tissue at the thoracolumbar junction", "C": "Chronic viscerosomatic reflex from the kidney and ureter sharing sympathetic levels through L1", "D": "Chronic viscerosomatic reflex from the descending colon with persistent sympathetic facilitation at the lumbar level", "E": "Chronic viscerosomatic reflex from the small bowel with fibrotic paraspinal change at midthoracic levels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chronic viscerosomatic reflex from the descending colon with persistent sympathetic facilitation at the lumbar level): Cool, dry, ropy, fibrotic tissue indicates chronicity, and L1 to L2 is the sympathetic level of the descending colon, which fits constipation. Kidney and ureter share L1 but would not explain the bowel complaint. Acute reflexes would be warm and boggy, and sacral dysfunction alone does not account for the lumbar tissue changes."}, {"id": 506, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 62-year-old man has thickened, yellow, crumbling nails on all ten toes for several years. A KOH preparation of subungual debris shows septate hyphae, and culture grows a dermatophyte. He takes simvastatin and amiodarone for cardiac disease, and his liver enzymes are normal. Which oral antifungal is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Fluconazole, an azole with a long half-life that is used weekly off label for nail infection", "B": "Griseofulvin, which disrupts fungal microtubules and is taken daily for twelve to eighteen months", "C": "Terbinafine, which blocks squalene epoxidase and kills dermatophytes with little CYP3A4 drug interaction burden", "D": "Nystatin, a polyene that forms membrane pores and is given as an oral suspension", "E": "Itraconazole, an azole that inhibits fungal ergosterol synthesis and is given in pulsed monthly dosing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Terbinafine, which blocks squalene epoxidase and kills dermatophytes with little CYP3A4 drug interaction burden): Terbinafine is fungicidal against dermatophytes by inhibiting squalene epoxidase and has the highest cure rate for toenail onychomycosis. Itraconazole is effective but inhibits CYP3A4, a risk with simvastatin and amiodarone. Nystatin is poorly absorbed and acts on Candida rather than nail dermatophytes."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease and heart failure was started on spironolactone 2 weeks ago while continuing lisinopril. She reports profound weakness and palpitations. Serum potassium returns at 7.2 mEq/L and the ECG tracing shown is obtained. Which intervention should be given first?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous regular insulin with dextrose to shift potassium into cells and lower the serum level", "B": "Intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the myocyte membrane against potassium induced arrhythmia risk", "C": "Intravenous furosemide to increase urinary potassium excretion through the loop of Henle", "D": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate to remove potassium through exchange in the colonic lumen", "E": "Nebulized albuterol to drive potassium intracellularly through beta two stimulation of the sodium potassium pump"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the myocyte membrane against potassium induced arrhythmia risk): Peaked T waves with a widened QRS show that hyperkalemia is already affecting conduction, so the membrane must be stabilized immediately with intravenous calcium. Insulin with dextrose and albuterol lower the level by shifting potassium, and they follow calcium. Polystyrene and furosemide remove potassium too slowly to be the first step.", "image": "question_images/q_507.png"}, {"id": 508, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with a prior hysterectomy has crampy abdominal pain, repeated bilious vomiting, distension, and no passage of flatus for two days. She is afebrile and has no groin mass. A radiograph shows multiple air-fluid levels in dilated small bowel loops with a collapsed colon. Which cause is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Incarcerated femoral hernia trapping a loop of ileum within the femoral canal", "B": "Gallstone eroding through a cholecystoenteric fistula and lodging near the ileocecal valve", "C": "Sigmoid volvulus with twisting of the sigmoid loop around its mesocolon", "D": "Fibrous adhesions from prior pelvic surgery kinking a loop of small bowel", "E": "Intussusception of the ileum into the cecum led by a mass lesion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fibrous adhesions from prior pelvic surgery kinking a loop of small bowel): Previous abdominal or pelvic surgery is the most common cause of small bowel obstruction in adults, because adhesions kink or trap bowel loops, and a collapsed colon confirms a high grade small bowel process. Femoral hernia is a classic miss in older women but the examination found no groin mass, and gallstone ileus requires air in the biliary tree."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 19-year-old college student falls asleep during lectures despite eight hours of nightly sleep. She reports sudden loss of muscle tone in her knees when laughing and vivid hallucinations as she falls asleep. Overnight polysomnography followed by daytime nap testing is planned. Which finding is expected?", "options": {"A": "Short mean sleep latency with REM sleep appearing within fifteen minutes of sleep onset", "B": "Delayed sleep phase with normal sleep architecture when the patient sleeps at her preferred late hours", "C": "Repeated apneas with oxygen desaturation and arousals, resolving with positive airway pressure therapy", "D": "Normal sleep latency with excessive slow wave sleep and sleepwalking episodes arising from deep sleep", "E": "Prolonged sleep latency with absent REM sleep and frequent arousals from fragmented slow wave sleep"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short mean sleep latency with REM sleep appearing within fifteen minutes of sleep onset): Cataplexy with hypnagogic hallucinations and unrefreshed daytime sleepiness is narcolepsy type 1, caused by loss of hypocretin neurons. Testing shows a mean latency of eight minutes or less and sleep onset REM periods. Circadian delay would show normal architecture when sleep is allowed at the preferred time, and apnea would produce obstructive events."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 66-year-old man with fever, productive cough, and lobar consolidation on chest radiograph has PaO2 of 52 mm Hg on room air. After 15 minutes of 100% oxygen by non-rebreather mask, his PaO2 rises only to 68 mm Hg. Respiratory rate is 28 and PaCO2 is 32 mm Hg. Which mechanism most likely accounts for his persistent hypoxemia?", "options": {"A": "Reduced inspired oxygen pressure from altitude, which a high-fraction oxygen mask would fully reverse", "B": "Intrapulmonary shunt, with perfused alveoli filled by exudate that cannot take up oxygen", "C": "Ventilation-perfusion mismatch with low-ratio units that correct readily with supplemental oxygen", "D": "Diffusion impairment across thickened membranes, which equilibrates with higher inspired oxygen concentration", "E": "Hypoventilation from central depression, which raises carbon dioxide and lowers alveolar oxygen tension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intrapulmonary shunt, with perfused alveoli filled by exudate that cannot take up oxygen): Lobar pneumonia fills alveoli with exudate while their capillaries still carry blood, producing a right-to-left intrapulmonary shunt. Blood from these units cannot be oxygenated no matter how much oxygen is inspired, so the PaO2 barely improves. The low PaCO2 and tachypnea show that ventilation is not the problem."}, {"id": 511, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 69-year-old man with pancreatic head cancer and obstructive jaundice has easy bruising and gum bleeding. Platelet count is normal. His prothrombin time is markedly prolonged and activated partial thromboplastin time is only mildly prolonged. Which explains why the prothrombin time rose before the other test?", "options": {"A": "Factor II has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent proteins and falls first", "B": "Factor VII has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent proteins and falls first", "C": "Protein C falls first of the vitamin K dependent proteins, and its loss prolongs clotting", "D": "Bile salt deficiency impairs platelet function, which prolongs the prothrombin time before other clotting tests", "E": "Fibrinogen is consumed first by low grade disseminated clotting from the tumor, prolonging the test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Factor VII has the shortest half-life of the vitamin K dependent proteins and falls first): Vitamin K carboxylates factors II, VII, IX, and X, and obstructive jaundice prevents bile-dependent absorption of this fat-soluble vitamin. Factor VII has a half-life of only a few hours, so the extrinsic pathway test prolongs before the intrinsic one. Protein C falls early too, but it affects thrombotic tendency rather than the prothrombin time, and a normal platelet count excludes a platelet cause."}, {"id": 512, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 5-month-old boy has recurrent pneumonia, oral thrush and failure to thrive. Chest radiography shows an absent thymic shadow, and flow cytometry reveals profound lymphopenia affecting T, B and natural killer cells. Costochondral cupping of the ribs is noted. Which metabolic abnormality is most likely responsible for the lymphocyte destruction?", "options": {"A": "Orotic acid accumulation depleting pyrimidine nucleotides needed for lymphocyte DNA synthesis and proliferation", "B": "Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency, raising deoxyguanosine triphosphate and mainly impairing T-cell survival", "C": "Excess uric acid crystals depositing in the thymus, causing local inflammation and destruction of developing thymocytes", "D": "Defective purine salvage from absent HGPRT, producing PRPP excess and cytotoxic uric acid within lymphocytes", "E": "Accumulated deoxyadenosine triphosphate inhibiting ribonucleotide reductase, limiting deoxynucleotide supply for lymphocyte DNA synthesis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Accumulated deoxyadenosine triphosphate inhibiting ribonucleotide reductase, limiting deoxynucleotide supply for lymphocyte DNA synthesis): Adenosine deaminase deficiency causes deoxyadenosine to accumulate and be phosphorylated to dATP, which inhibits ribonucleotide reductase so lymphocytes cannot make deoxynucleotides for DNA synthesis. This affects T, B and natural killer cells and can produce rib cupping. Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency also raises a deoxynucleotide but classically spares B cells."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 63-year-old man with a 45 pack-year smoking history presents with weight loss and cough. Chest radiograph shows a central hilar mass. Serum sodium is 123 mEq/L, serum osmolality 255 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg, and he appears euvolemic. Biopsy shows small blue cells with scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, and crush artifact. Which mechanism explains the sodium abnormality?", "options": {"A": "Ectopic secretion of corticotropin by tumor cells, causing cortisol excess with potassium wasting", "B": "Adrenal metastases destroying the cortex, which impairs aldosterone dependent sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct", "C": "Tumor derived parathyroid hormone related peptide, causing hypercalcemia and a secondary nephrogenic concentrating defect", "D": "Hypothalamic metastases that impair thirst regulation and drive excessive free water intake", "E": "Ectopic secretion of antidiuretic hormone by the tumor cells, causing renal retention of free water"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ectopic secretion of antidiuretic hormone by the tumor cells, causing renal retention of free water): Hyponatremia with low serum osmolality, inappropriately concentrated urine, and euvolemia is the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion, which small cell carcinoma produces ectopically. Adrenal failure would cause volume depletion and hyperkalemia. Corticotropin secretion by the same tumor type produces hypokalemic alkalosis, not hyponatremia."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 40-year-old man has a unilateral right-sided headache provoked by neck movement that began after a sports collision. Motion testing shows the occiput flexes and sidebends left more freely and rotates right more freely, with restricted extension, right sidebending and left rotation. How is this somatic dysfunction named?", "options": {"A": "Occipitoatlantal extended, sidebent right and rotated left, named for the directions of restricted motion", "B": "Atlantoaxial rotated right, since C1 on C2 motion is primarily rotation with minimal sidebending", "C": "Occipitoatlantal flexed, sidebent left and rotated right, named for the directions of greatest freedom", "D": "Occipitoatlantal extended, sidebent left and rotated right, with neutral type I mechanics throughout the cervical spine", "E": "Occipitoatlantal flexed, sidebent left and rotated left, following type II mechanics of lower cervical segments"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Occipitoatlantal flexed, sidebent left and rotated right, named for the directions of greatest freedom): Somatic dysfunction is named by the direction of freedom, and the occipitoatlantal joint couples sidebending and rotation to opposite sides, as seen here with left sidebending and right rotation plus flexion. Naming by restriction reverses the label. The atlantoaxial joint is mostly rotation, and type II same-sided coupling applies to C2 through C7."}, {"id": 515, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 52-year-old man with hypercholesterolemia begins a bile acid sequestrant that interrupts enterohepatic recirculation. His liver panel remains normal, and his hepatocytes respond by converting more cholesterol into primary bile acids. Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step of this conversion in the classic pathway?", "options": {"A": "Acyl-CoA cholesterol acyltransferase, which esterifies free cholesterol for storage within hepatocyte lipid droplets", "B": "Lecithin cholesterol acyltransferase, which esterifies cholesterol on circulating high density lipoprotein particles", "C": "Lipoprotein lipase, which hydrolyzes triglycerides carried within chylomicrons and very low density lipoproteins", "D": "HMG-CoA reductase, the committed step controlling hepatic de novo synthesis of cholesterol itself", "E": "Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase, the microsomal cytochrome P450 enzyme initiating the classic bile acid synthesis pathway"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase, the microsomal cytochrome P450 enzyme initiating the classic bile acid synthesis pathway): Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase is feedback inhibited by bile acids returning through the enterohepatic circulation, so sequestrants remove that inhibition and raise its activity. HMG-CoA reductase is tempting because it also rises, but it controls cholesterol formation, not bile acid formation."}, {"id": 516, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 45-year-old woman presents to an outpatient clinic with episodic pounding headaches, sweating and palpitations. Blood pressure reaches 210/120 mm Hg during an episode, and plasma free metanephrines are markedly elevated. Imaging shows a unilateral adrenal mass. From which embryonic tissue do the neoplastic cells of this adrenal mass derive?", "options": {"A": "Endoderm of the primitive gut tube that forms glandular epithelium of the digestive tract", "B": "Neural crest cells that migrated ventrally and differentiated into catecholamine secreting chromaffin cells", "C": "Intermediate mesoderm of the urogenital ridge that forms the steroid producing adrenal cortex", "D": "Neuroectoderm of the neural tube that forms central nervous system neurons and glia", "E": "Surface ectoderm that forms the epidermis, sensory placodes and the anterior pituitary gland"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neural crest cells that migrated ventrally and differentiated into catecholamine secreting chromaffin cells): Adrenal medullary chromaffin cells are modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons derived from neural crest, so pheochromocytoma arises from that lineage. The adrenal cortex, by contrast, comes from intermediate mesoderm and makes steroids, not catecholamines. Neural tube neuroectoderm forms central nervous system cells rather than peripheral crest derivatives."}, {"id": 517, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 62-year-old woman living in a nursing home has diffuse bone pain, difficulty rising from a chair, and a waddling gait. She rarely goes outdoors. Serum calcium is 8.0 mg/dL, phosphate 2.2 mg/dL, alkaline phosphatase elevated, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 8 ng/mL. Radiographs show pseudofractures of the pelvis. Which process explains the bone abnormality?", "options": {"A": "Subperiosteal bone resorption and cystic brown tumors caused by autonomous excess parathyroid hormone secretion", "B": "Defective type I collagen synthesis from a COL1A1 mutation producing thin cortices and recurrent fractures", "C": "Excessive osteoclast resorption of normally mineralized bone driven by estrogen deficiency and increased RANKL signaling", "D": "Disorganized osteoclast and osteoblast turnover producing thickened, mosaic pattern bone that is structurally weak", "E": "Defective mineralization of osteoid matrix because low calcium and phosphate prevent hydroxyapatite deposition on collagen"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Defective mineralization of osteoid matrix because low calcium and phosphate prevent hydroxyapatite deposition on collagen): Low vitamin D with low calcium and phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase, proximal weakness, and pseudofractures indicates adult osteomalacia: osteoid is laid down normally but cannot mineralize. Osteoporosis preserves mineralization, Paget disease is focal with normal calcium, and hyperparathyroidism raises calcium."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 4-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department with vomiting, lethargy and a bulging anterior fontanelle. Parents report no trauma. Examination shows bilateral retinal hemorrhages. Head CT shows subdural hematomas of differing density over both hemispheres. Which mechanism most likely produced the intracranial bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin K deficiency bleeding from absent gut flora, causing spontaneous hemorrhage into several intracranial compartments", "B": "Repetitive acceleration and deceleration with rotation that tears bridging veins crossing from cortex to sinus", "C": "Hypertensive rupture of lenticulostriate vessels producing a deep intraparenchymal hemorrhage in the basal ganglia", "D": "Rupture of a berry aneurysm at the circle of Willis, bleeding into the subarachnoid space", "E": "Tearing of the middle meningeal artery from temporal fracture, forming a lens-shaped epidural collection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repetitive acceleration and deceleration with rotation that tears bridging veins crossing from cortex to sinus): Bilateral retinal hemorrhages with subdural collections of different ages and no matching history indicate abusive head trauma. Violent shaking produces rotational acceleration and deceleration that shears bridging veins. Epidural bleeds arise from arterial tears with skull fracture and are usually single and lens shaped."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 44-year-old man has recurrent ulcers in the duodenum and proximal jejunum despite high dose acid suppression, along with intermittent watery diarrhea that improves with fasting. After acid suppression was held for a week, his laboratory results and secretin response are shown in the table. Which mechanism best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causing achlorhydria and compensatory G-cell stimulation", "B": "Acid suppression therapy raising gastric pH and removing feedback inhibition of gastrin release", "C": "Autonomous gastrin secretion from a neuroendocrine tumor causing sustained parietal cell hyperstimulation", "D": "Antral Helicobacter pylori infection reducing somatostatin cells and modestly increasing gastrin release from G cells", "E": "Antral G-cell hyperplasia with exaggerated gastrin release only after a protein meal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Autonomous gastrin secretion from a neuroendocrine tumor causing sustained parietal cell hyperstimulation): A marked fasting gastrin with very acidic gastric pH and a paradoxical rise after secretin identifies a gastrinoma, since normal G cells and G-cell hyperplasia do not increase gastrin with secretin. Atrophic gastritis and acid suppression also raise gastrin, but only with a high gastric pH.", "image": "question_images/q_519.png"}, {"id": 520, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 72-year-old man living alone who eats mostly tea and toast presents with a leg ulcer that has not healed for two months. Examination shows bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, and corkscrew-shaped hairs. Impairment of which step in collagen synthesis best explains the delayed wound healing?", "options": {"A": "Cleavage of terminal propeptides by procollagen peptidases outside the fibroblast", "B": "Cross-linking of tropocollagen molecules by extracellular lysyl oxidase activity", "C": "Transcription of the collagen gene by activated fibroblasts in granulation tissue", "D": "Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues within the endoplasmic reticulum", "E": "Glycosylation of selected hydroxylysine residues during procollagen processing in the cell"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues within the endoplasmic reticulum): The gum bleeding, perifollicular hemorrhages and corkscrew hairs indicate scurvy. Ascorbic acid is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases, so without it procollagen cannot form a stable triple helix. Lysyl oxidase cross-linking depends on copper, and propeptide cleavage occurs outside the cell without vitamin C."}, {"id": 521, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 45-year-old teacher with chronic low back pain asks why her osteopathic physician examined her gait and breathing pattern before recommending treatment. The physician explains that an altered pelvic posture can impair diaphragm excursion, and that the resulting impaired circulation and respiration in turn perpetuate the posture and her pain. Which tenet does this reasoning most directly illustrate?", "options": {"A": "The body possesses inherent mechanisms for self regulation, self healing, and health maintenance", "B": "Rational treatment rests on understanding body unity, self regulation, and the relationship between form and activity", "C": "The body is a unit, and each person is a combination of body, mind, and spirit", "D": "Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered anatomy changes physiology and the reverse", "E": "The musculoskeletal system, as the largest body system, should be examined before all other systems"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so altered anatomy changes physiology and the reverse): The physician describes posture affecting breathing and circulation, and those functional changes then maintaining the posture, which is the two-way link between structure and function. The body unit tenet is true but would be about the whole person, and the fourth tenet concerns how treatment is chosen."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 25-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, at 28 weeks gestation attends a routine prenatal visit. She is blood type O Rh-negative, her first child is Rh-positive, and her partner is Rh-positive. She received appropriate immune globulin after her first delivery, and today's indirect antiglobulin test is negative. Which management is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Give anti-D antibody now, then again after delivery if the newborn is Rh positive", "B": "Repeat the antiglobulin test at 36 weeks and give immune globulin if it becomes positive", "C": "Begin weekly middle cerebral artery Doppler studies to detect early fetal anemia from alloimmunization", "D": "Withhold immune globulin because a negative antiglobulin test shows she is not at risk", "E": "Give immune globulin only after delivery, since earlier dosing does not reduce antepartum sensitization risk"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give anti-D antibody now, then again after delivery if the newborn is Rh positive): A negative indirect antiglobulin test means she is unsensitized, which is exactly when prophylaxis works. Anti-D given at 28 weeks protects against silent fetomaternal hemorrhage during the third trimester, and a second dose follows delivery when the newborn is Rh positive. Doppler surveillance applies to women who already carry anti-D antibodies, and waiting for a positive screen means sensitization has already occurred."}, {"id": 523, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 19-year-old man with cystic fibrosis has had worsening cough and sputum over several years. Sputum culture grows an oxidase-positive, gram-negative rod that forms mucoid, blue-green colonies. Which virulence factor of this organism best explains persistence in his airways despite repeated antibiotic courses?", "options": {"A": "Alginate exopolysaccharide forming a biofilm matrix that impairs phagocytosis and limits antibiotic penetration", "B": "Pyocyanin pigment generating reactive oxygen species that damage ciliated respiratory epithelium during infection", "C": "Protein A binding the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G to block opsonization by phagocytes", "D": "Type III secretion system injecting exoenzymes that disrupt the cytoskeleton of epithelial cells", "E": "Exotoxin A that inactivates elongation factor 2 and halts host cell protein synthesis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Alginate exopolysaccharide forming a biofilm matrix that impairs phagocytosis and limits antibiotic penetration): Mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa in cystic fibrosis overproduces alginate, which builds a biofilm that shields bacteria from phagocytes and antibiotics. Exotoxin A and the type III secretion system are real virulence factors, but they act mainly in acute infection and tissue injury rather than chronic persistence. Protein A belongs to Staphylococcus aureus."}, {"id": 524, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with osteoporosis falls at home and cannot bear weight on her left leg, which is shortened and externally rotated. The anteroposterior radiograph of the hip is shown. Disruption of which vessel most increases her risk of avascular necrosis of the femoral head?", "options": {"A": "Inferior gluteal artery accompanying the sciatic nerve below the piriformis muscle", "B": "Retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery running along the femoral neck", "C": "Superior gluteal artery supplying the gluteus medius and the posterior hip capsule", "D": "Artery of the ligamentum teres from the obturator artery within the femoral head", "E": "Ascending branch of the lateral femoral circumflex artery supplying the greater trochanter region"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery running along the femoral neck): In adults the femoral head depends mainly on retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery that ascend along the neck, so a displaced subcapital fracture tears them. The artery of the ligamentum teres contributes little after childhood, which makes it a tempting but inadequate collateral source here.", "image": "question_images/q_524.png"}, {"id": 525, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 35-year-old woman sustains a deep partial-thickness scald burn on the thigh from a kitchen accident. The surgeon plans conservative management and explains that the epidermis will regenerate despite loss of the overlying layer. Which structure within the remaining dermis is the main source of keratinocytes for re-epithelialization?", "options": {"A": "Merkel cells located in the stratum basale acting as slowly adapting touch receptors", "B": "Langerhans cells in the stratum spinosum derived from bone marrow monocyte precursors", "C": "Dermal fibroblasts in the papillary layer converting directly into epidermal keratinocytes", "D": "Melanocytes at the dermal-epidermal junction producing pigment transferred to neighboring keratinocytes", "E": "Epithelial stem cells in the bulge region of hair follicles and sweat gland ducts"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Epithelial stem cells in the bulge region of hair follicles and sweat gland ducts): When most of the epidermis is lost, regeneration comes from epithelial stem cells that persist in the bulge of hair follicles and in sweat gland ducts deep in the dermis. Merkel cells, Langerhans cells and melanocytes are specialized epidermal cells, and fibroblasts do not become keratinocytes."}, {"id": 526, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 5-year-old boy has frequent falls and uses his hands to push up on his thighs when rising from the floor. Calves are firm and enlarged, and serum creatine kinase is 18,000 U/L. His maternal uncle died young of heart failure. Which molecular defect most likely explains his presentation?", "options": {"A": "Deficiency of laminin alpha-2 weakening the link between basal lamina and the sarcolemma", "B": "Missense variant in the sarcoglycan complex disrupting the dystrophin associated glycoprotein anchor", "C": "Frameshift deletion causing a premature stop codon and almost no dystrophin protein", "D": "Trinucleotide CTG repeat expansion in a kinase gene causing toxic RNA accumulation in nuclei", "E": "In-frame deletion producing a shortened but partially functional dystrophin protein"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Frameshift deletion causing a premature stop codon and almost no dystrophin protein): Progressive proximal weakness with a Gowers maneuver, calf pseudohypertrophy and a creatine kinase in the tens of thousands in a young boy indicates Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Frameshift deletions truncate dystrophin and leave almost none, whereas in-frame deletions in Becker disease yield partially functional protein and a later, milder course. Myotonic dystrophy has a CTG expansion and shows myotonia."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 55-year-old man on maintenance hemodialysis for diabetic kidney disease has recurrent epistaxis and prolonged bleeding after venipuncture. Platelet count, prothrombin time, and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Bleeding time is prolonged. Von Willebrand factor levels are normal. Which mechanism best explains his bleeding tendency?", "options": {"A": "Accelerated platelet consumption from widespread microvascular thrombosis and fibrin deposition within small vessels", "B": "Reduced hepatic synthesis of vitamin K dependent clotting factors from chronic dialysis associated malnutrition", "C": "Qualitative deficiency of glycoprotein Ib receptors on platelets from an inherited membrane defect", "D": "Retained uremic toxins impair platelet adhesion and aggregation despite a normal circulating platelet number", "E": "Heparin exposure during dialysis inhibits thrombin and factor Xa activation, impairing the coagulation cascade"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Retained uremic toxins impair platelet adhesion and aggregation despite a normal circulating platelet number): Uremia causes a qualitative platelet defect with poor adhesion and aggregation, so bleeding time is prolonged while platelet count and coagulation studies stay normal. Dialysis improves it, and desmopressin can be used for acute bleeding. An inherited receptor defect would have produced bleeding long before kidney disease."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 7-year-old girl has a soft systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border and a wide, fixed split of the second heart sound that does not vary with respiration. Chest radiograph shows increased pulmonary vascularity. Which mechanism best explains the fixed splitting of the second heart sound?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary hypertension from high resistance causing early pulmonic valve closure in affected children", "B": "Left-to-right atrial shunting raising right ventricular volume and delaying pulmonic valve closure in both phases", "C": "Mitral regurgitation shortening left ventricular ejection and advancing aortic valve closure in older adults", "D": "Left bundle branch block delaying aortic closure so the split narrows on inspiration", "E": "Delayed left ventricular emptying from outflow narrowing prolonging aortic valve closure on expiration"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Left-to-right atrial shunting raising right ventricular volume and delaying pulmonic valve closure in both phases): An atrial septal defect equalizes atrial pressures through respiration, so right ventricular volume stays increased and pulmonic closure is delayed in both inspiration and expiration. This produces a fixed split. Bundle branch block and aortic delay cause paradoxical splitting, and mitral regurgitation does not fix the split."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 28-year-old obese woman who smokes has recurrent painful nodules, draining sinus tracts, and rope-like scars in both axillae and the groin. Cultures from the drainage are repeatedly negative or show mixed flora. This chronic condition arises from primary occlusion of which structure?", "options": {"A": "The cutaneous lymphatic vessels, with obstruction producing edema and secondary sinus tract formation", "B": "The terminal hair follicle infundibulum, followed by rupture and inflammation involving adjacent apocrine tissue", "C": "The sebaceous gland duct, with sebum retention and Cutibacterium acnes overgrowth as the initiating event", "D": "The eccrine sweat duct, with primary infection by Staphylococcus aureus spreading into the dermis", "E": "The dermal arterioles, with inflammatory vasculitis leading to ischemic ulceration and tunneling scars"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The terminal hair follicle infundibulum, followed by rupture and inflammation involving adjacent apocrine tissue): Hidradenitis suppurativa begins with follicular occlusion at the terminal hair follicle infundibulum, followed by rupture and a largely sterile inflammatory response in intertriginous skin. Obesity, smoking and female sex are risk factors. Sebaceous duct plugging defines acne, and cultures are usually unrevealing because infection is secondary."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "community_health", "question": "In a randomized trial of 2,000 adults with prior myocardial infarction, 10 percent of the placebo group and 6 percent of the group given a new antiplatelet drug had a recurrent infarction within one year. How many patients must be treated for one year to prevent one recurrent infarction?", "options": {"A": "4 patients, the absolute risk reduction itself read as the number needed to treat", "B": "17 patients, the reciprocal of the 6 percent event rate in the treated group", "C": "10 patients, the reciprocal of the 10 percent event rate in the placebo group", "D": "2.5 patients, calculated as the reciprocal of the 40 percent relative risk reduction", "E": "25 patients, calculated as the reciprocal of the 4 percentage point absolute risk difference"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (25 patients, calculated as the reciprocal of the 4 percentage point absolute risk difference): Absolute risk reduction is 10 percent minus 6 percent, or 4 percentage points (0.04), and number needed to treat is its reciprocal, 25. The relative risk reduction of 40 percent and the single event rates are not the correct denominators. Reporting 25 patients means about one recurrence is prevented for every 25 treated for a year."}, {"id": 531, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 7-year-old boy falls from monkey bars onto an outstretched hand and radiographs show a displaced supracondylar fracture of the humerus. Before reduction he cannot flex the tip of his thumb or index finger when asked to make an O shape, but sensation in his fingertips is normal. Which nerve is most likely injured?", "options": {"A": "Posterior interosseous nerve, a deep motor branch of the radial nerve at the supinator", "B": "Ulnar nerve, running behind the medial epicondyle and supplying the medial flexor digitorum profundus", "C": "Anterior interosseous nerve, a motor branch of the median nerve in the forearm", "D": "Median nerve trunk at the elbow, causing combined sensory loss over the lateral palm", "E": "Recurrent motor branch of the median nerve, supplying the thenar muscles at the palm"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anterior interosseous nerve, a motor branch of the median nerve in the forearm): The anterior interosseous nerve supplies flexor pollicis longus, the lateral half of flexor digitorum profundus and pronator quadratus, so injury abolishes the O sign without sensory loss because it has no cutaneous branch. Injury to the median trunk would add numbness of the lateral palm and weakness of pronation and wrist flexion."}, {"id": 532, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 26-year-old man has 12 hours of periumbilical pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, with anorexia, low-grade fever, and rebound tenderness. Examination also shows ropy paraspinal tissue at T10 to T11 on the right. He is a college student and asks for osteopathic treatment instead of hospital evaluation. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain at right sided tender points with observation at home and reevaluation in one day", "B": "Oral antibiotics with outpatient follow-up in clinic, deferring imaging until symptoms persist for days", "C": "Rib raising to improve visceral drainage and autonomic balance with oral analgesics at home", "D": "Muscle energy to correct the type II thoracic dysfunction followed by pain reassessment after one hour", "E": "Urgent surgical evaluation with imaging, since viscerosomatic findings reflect disease and do not replace care"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Urgent surgical evaluation with imaging, since viscerosomatic findings reflect disease and do not replace care): Migrating pain, anorexia, fever, and rebound tenderness indicate probable appendicitis, a surgical emergency. The paraspinal changes at T10 to T11 are a viscerosomatic reflex of the disease and do not justify manipulative treatment in place of urgent evaluation."}, {"id": 533, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 2-year-old boy has fever, irritability and diffuse tender erythema that began around the mouth. The next day, the superficial skin sloughs with gentle rubbing in sheets, but the oral mucosa is spared. A purulent conjunctivitis was noted earlier. Which mechanism best explains the skin findings?", "options": {"A": "Direct bacterial invasion of the dermis causes lymphatic spread and a purulent subcutaneous infection", "B": "Circulating exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1 in the granular layer of the epidermis", "C": "Drug specific cytotoxic T cells trigger keratinocyte apoptosis through granulysin release at the dermoepidermal junction", "D": "Toxic shock syndrome toxin 1 acts as a superantigen and drives massive polyclonal T cell activation", "E": "IgG autoantibodies bind desmoglein 3 in the suprabasal layer and disrupt keratinocyte adhesion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Circulating exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein 1 in the granular layer of the epidermis): Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome results from exfoliative toxins cleaving desmoglein 1 in the stratum granulosum, so sloughing is superficial and the mucosa is spared because mucosal desmoglein 3 compensates. Drug induced cytotoxic injury would involve the mucosa and full thickness epidermis."}, {"id": 534, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 36-year-old woman who smokes a pack a day says she has no plans to quit within the next six months and believes her cough is only from allergies. She acknowledges no problem with smoking. Which stage of change describes her?", "options": {"A": "Maintenance, with sustained abstinence for over six months and strategies used to prevent relapse", "B": "Preparation, with small steps taken and a firm plan to quit within the coming month", "C": "Action, with overt modification of smoking behavior and environment during the past six months", "D": "Precontemplation, with no intention to change behavior and little recognition that a problem exists", "E": "Contemplation, with ambivalence about quitting and an intention to change within the next six months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Precontemplation, with no intention to change behavior and little recognition that a problem exists): Precontemplation describes a person with no intention to change in the next six months, often with denial of the problem, as here. Contemplation involves recognizing the problem and weighing change. Preparation, action and maintenance all require a plan or active behavior change that she lacks."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 34-year-old woman asks about prevention after her mother and sister were diagnosed with breast cancer before age 45 and a maternal aunt died of ovarian cancer. Genetic testing identifies a pathogenic BRCA1 variant. She has finished childbearing and has a normal breast examination and mammogram. Which management step is most appropriate to lower her ovarian cancer risk?", "options": {"A": "Annual transvaginal ultrasound with CA-125 alone, beginning at age fifty, until natural menopause occurs", "B": "Risk-reducing bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, since surveillance tests cannot reliably detect early tumors in carriers", "C": "Bilateral mastectomy alone, which removes the shared hereditary mechanism driving both breast and ovarian tumors", "D": "Total abdominal hysterectomy with preservation of both ovaries to remove the uterine source of risk", "E": "Daily tamoxifen therapy, which substantially lowers ovarian tumor incidence in carriers of this variant"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Risk-reducing bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, since surveillance tests cannot reliably detect early tumors in carriers): BRCA1 carriers have a high lifetime risk of ovarian and fallopian tube cancer, and no screening strategy has been shown to detect it early enough to save lives. After childbearing is complete, surgical removal of the tubes and ovaries is advised. Mastectomy lowers breast risk only, and tamoxifen does not protect the ovary."}, {"id": 536, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 15-month-old boy is found lethargic after a prolonged illness with poor oral intake. His glucose is 28 mg/dL, with no urinary ketones and normal ammonia. Liver enzymes are mildly elevated. Acylcarnitine analysis shows elevated octanoylcarnitine. Which metabolic process is impaired in this child?", "options": {"A": "Peripheral ketone utilization because succinyl-CoA transferase is deficient, causing severe ketoacidosis in infancy", "B": "Urea cycle flux because ornithine transcarbamylase is deficient, causing hyperammonemia with orotic aciduria after protein intake", "C": "Hepatic glycogenolysis because glucose-6-phosphatase is deficient, causing lactic acidosis with hepatomegaly during fasting", "D": "Gluconeogenesis from alanine because pyruvate carboxylase is deficient, causing lactic acidosis with hyperammonemia", "E": "Mitochondrial beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids, which limits ketone production and gluconeogenic support during fasting"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Mitochondrial beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids, which limits ketone production and gluconeogenic support during fasting): Hypoketotic hypoglycemia with elevated octanoylcarnitine identifies medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency. Without beta-oxidation the liver cannot generate ketones or the energy for gluconeogenesis during fasting. Defects of glycogen or gluconeogenesis cause lactic acidosis, and ketone use defects cause ketosis."}, {"id": 537, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 68-year-old woman had a total knee arthroplasty six months ago. She now reports gradually worsening knee pain at rest and stiffness without fever. Radiographs show a lucent line around the tibial component. Inflammatory markers are modestly elevated, and aspirate cultures grow a slime-producing organism from the implant surface. Which organism best fits this indolent infection?", "options": {"A": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis reactivating near the implant and causing granulomatous synovitis over many months", "B": "Staphylococcus aureus, which produces early postoperative abscess with fever and purulent wound drainage", "C": "Escherichia coli from hematogenous seeding after a urinary tract infection in an older woman", "D": "Coagulase-negative staphylococcus, which forms biofilm on prosthetic material and causes low-virulence delayed infection", "E": "Cutibacterium acnes, which classically infects shoulder prostheses after sebaceous gland contamination during surgery"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Coagulase-negative staphylococcus, which forms biofilm on prosthetic material and causes low-virulence delayed infection): Delayed prosthetic joint infection with a low-grade course and slime production is classically caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci that adhere to hardware in a biofilm. Staphylococcus aureus tends to cause an earlier, more fulminant infection. Cutibacterium acnes is typical of shoulder arthroplasty, and tuberculosis would be unusual and granulomatous."}, {"id": 538, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 45-year-old man in clinic reports burning epigastric pain after meals for three weeks, worse with coffee. Endoscopy is scheduled. On palpation, the physician finds warm, boggy, tender paraspinal tissue with restricted segmental motion at several adjacent levels. Which spinal levels most likely hold these facilitated segments through the sympathetic viscerosomatic reflex?", "options": {"A": "T5 to T9 paraspinal segments, matching the sympathetic supply of stomach and duodenum", "B": "T10 to T11 paraspinal segments, matching the sympathetic supply of the proximal colon", "C": "L1 to L2 paraspinal segments, matching the sympathetic supply of the distal colon", "D": "T12 to L2 paraspinal segments, matching the sympathetic supply of the urinary bladder", "E": "T1 to T4 paraspinal segments, matching the sympathetic supply of head and neck structures"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (T5 to T9 paraspinal segments, matching the sympathetic supply of stomach and duodenum): Stomach and duodenal sympathetic fibers arise from T5 to T9, so visceral irritation facilitates the matching paraspinal segments and produces warm, boggy, tender tissue early in the process. The upper levels of T1 to T4 are tempting because the pain is in the upper abdomen, but they supply the head and neck. Colonic and bladder levels lie lower in the thoracolumbar cord."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A term newborn becomes cyanotic within hours of birth, with no murmur and a single loud second heart sound. Chest radiograph shows an egg-shaped cardiac silhouette with a narrow mediastinum and increased pulmonary markings. Oxygen does not improve saturation. Which intervention should be started first while awaiting definitive surgery?", "options": {"A": "High concentration oxygen by hood to dilate pulmonary vessels and increase saturation quickly", "B": "High concentration oxygen by hood to dilate pulmonary vessels and increase saturation", "C": "Furosemide with fluid restriction to treat pulmonary congestion from left heart failure", "D": "Indomethacin infusion to close the ductus and raise systemic oxygen delivery in the first days", "E": "Prostaglandin E1 infusion to maintain ductal patency and permit mixing between parallel circulations"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Prostaglandin E1 infusion to maintain ductal patency and permit mixing between parallel circulations): Early cyanosis with an egg-on-a-string silhouette indicates transposition of the great arteries, where pulmonary and systemic circuits run in parallel. Survival depends on a patent ductus or septal defect for mixing, so prostaglandin E1 is started. Closing the duct or relying on oxygen would worsen hypoxemia."}, {"id": 540, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 58-year-old woman reports fatigue, constipation, and one episode of nephrolithiasis last year. She takes no medications or supplements. Her outpatient laboratory panel is shown in the table. Radiographs of the hands show subperiosteal resorption of the phalanges. Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Secondary hyperparathyroidism from vitamin D deficiency, with PTH elevation driven by chronically low calcium absorption", "B": "Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy from PTH related peptide secretion, suppressing intact PTH and lowering phosphate", "C": "Primary hyperparathyroidism from autonomous PTH secretion by a parathyroid adenoma, raising calcium and lowering phosphate", "D": "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia from calcium sensing receptor inactivation, with mild hypercalcemia and low urinary calcium excretion", "E": "Vitamin D intoxication from excess supplementation, causing high calcium and phosphate with suppressed intact PTH levels"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Primary hyperparathyroidism from autonomous PTH secretion by a parathyroid adenoma, raising calcium and lowering phosphate): High calcium with low phosphate and an inappropriately elevated intact PTH indicates autonomous parathyroid secretion, and the hypercalciuria, stones, and subperiosteal resorption support it. Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia has low urinary calcium, malignancy suppresses PTH, and secondary disease occurs with low or normal calcium.", "image": "question_images/q_540.png"}, {"id": 541, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old rugby player is seen in the emergency department after a tackle forced his arm into abduction and external rotation, producing an anterior shoulder dislocation. After reduction he cannot abduct the arm beyond the first few degrees and has numbness over the lateral upper arm. Which nerve was most likely injured?", "options": {"A": "Long thoracic nerve, stretched as it descends along the lateral chest wall", "B": "Musculocutaneous nerve, stretched as it pierces the coracobrachialis in the proximal arm", "C": "Axillary nerve, stretched as it wraps the surgical neck of the humerus", "D": "Suprascapular nerve, stretched as it passes through the suprascapular notch under the ligament", "E": "Radial nerve, stretched as it winds around the spiral groove of the humerus"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Axillary nerve, stretched as it wraps the surgical neck of the humerus): The axillary nerve (C5, C6) hugs the surgical neck of the humerus and is stretched by an anterior glenohumeral dislocation, paralyzing deltoid and teres minor and leaving a patch of numbness over the lateral shoulder. Suprascapular injury weakens the start of abduction and external rotation but spares skin sensation, so it cannot explain the numbness."}, {"id": 542, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "An 8-year-old girl develops migratory polyarthritis, involuntary writhing movements, and a new apical holosystolic murmur three weeks after untreated pharyngitis. A myocardial section from a similar fatal case is shown. The lesion in this condition arises through which immunologic mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-reacting with cardiac myosin and valvular glycoproteins", "B": "Direct bacterial invasion of valve leaflets by group A streptococci forming septic vegetations", "C": "Superantigen binding that triggers massive polyclonal T cell activation and systemic cytokine release", "D": "Streptolysin mediated pore formation that directly lyses cardiac myocytes during the pharyngeal infection", "E": "Immune complexes of streptococcal antigen and antibody depositing along glomerular and myocardial basement membranes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-reacting with cardiac myosin and valvular glycoproteins): Acute rheumatic fever is a type II hypersensitivity in which antibodies and T cells directed at streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin and valve proteins through molecular mimicry. The picture shows a granulomatous focus typical of the carditis, not bacterial invasion. Immune complex and superantigen mechanisms explain different streptococcal syndromes.", "image": "question_images/q_542.png"}, {"id": 543, "domain": "community_health", "question": "Investigators want to determine whether a rare childhood tumor is associated with prior parental occupational exposure. They identify 60 affected children and 120 unaffected children, then review parental work histories obtained by interviewing both sets of parents. Which study design is this?", "options": {"A": "Cross-sectional survey, which measures exposure and outcome together at one point in a population", "B": "Randomized controlled trial, which assigns exposure by chance and follows participants for the outcome", "C": "Retrospective cohort study, which starts from exposure status and follows groups forward in recorded time", "D": "Case-control study, which starts from outcome status and then looks backward for earlier exposures", "E": "Ecological study, which compares average exposure and disease rates between whole populations or regions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Case-control study, which starts from outcome status and then looks backward for earlier exposures): Starting with affected and unaffected children and looking back at exposure defines a case-control study, which suits rare outcomes. A cohort design would begin with exposure groups and follow them, and a trial assigns the exposure. A cross-sectional survey would capture too few cases of a rare tumor."}, {"id": 544, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 33-year-old nurse working at a homeless shelter receives a purified protein derivative injection in the forearm. Forty-eight hours later she has 16 mm of firm induration without ulceration. Chest radiograph is normal and she has no symptoms. She received a childhood vaccine abroad. Which immunologic mechanism best explains the skin reaction?", "options": {"A": "Immune complex deposition in dermal vessel walls with complement activation and neutrophil recruitment, type III", "B": "Cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognizing viral peptide on class I molecules and lysing infected keratinocytes", "C": "Preformed IgE on mast cells triggering histamine release within minutes of antigen exposure", "D": "Sensitized CD4 T helper cells releasing interferon gamma, which activates macrophages at the dermal site", "E": "Antibody binding to antigens on cell surfaces causing complement mediated lysis of local cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sensitized CD4 T helper cells releasing interferon gamma, which activates macrophages at the dermal site): The tuberculin reaction is a type IV delayed hypersensitivity driven by memory CD4 T cells that release interferon gamma and recruit and activate macrophages, peaking at 48 to 72 hours. The induration reflects cellular infiltration, not antibody or immune complexes. A positive result in a patient with a normal radiograph indicates latent infection or prior sensitization, not active disease."}, {"id": 545, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 44-year-old woman three months after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass reports that 20 minutes after eating pasta she develops palpitations, flushing, abdominal cramping, and urgent loose stool. Symptoms resolve within an hour. Glucose during an episode is 92 mg/dL. Which mechanism most likely explains these symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Bacterial overgrowth in the blind limb producing carbohydrate fermentation, gas, and bile acid deconjugation", "B": "Marginal ulcer at the gastrojejunal anastomosis causing postprandial epigastric pain and occult bleeding", "C": "Anastomotic stricture at the gastrojejunostomy obstructing outflow and causing postprandial pain with vomiting", "D": "Exaggerated insulin release after rapid glucose absorption causing reactive hypoglycemia two to three hours later", "E": "Rapid jejunal delivery of hyperosmolar chyme drawing fluid into the lumen and releasing vasoactive peptides"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rapid jejunal delivery of hyperosmolar chyme drawing fluid into the lumen and releasing vasoactive peptides): Bypassing the pylorus lets hyperosmolar food enter the jejunum quickly, drawing fluid into the bowel and releasing vasoactive peptides, which causes early dumping with palpitations, flushing, cramps, and diarrhea within 30 minutes. Late dumping occurs one to three hours after meals from reactive insulin surges and low glucose, which does not match her normal glucose. Ulcer and stricture produce pain or vomiting without this systemic pattern."}, {"id": 546, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 14-year-old boy has headaches and leg fatigue during sports. Blood pressure is 160/90 mm Hg in the right arm and 100/60 mm Hg in the leg, with delayed, weak femoral pulses. Chest radiograph shows rib notching. Which additional cardiac lesion is most commonly associated with this condition?", "options": {"A": "Ebstein anomaly with apical displacement of the tricuspid valve leaflets into the ventricle", "B": "Tricuspid atresia with a hypoplastic right ventricle and an obligatory atrial shunt", "C": "Tetralogy of Fallot with pulmonary stenosis and right ventricular hypertrophy causing early cyanosis in infancy", "D": "Total anomalous pulmonary venous return draining into the right atrium with mixing of blood", "E": "Bicuspid aortic valve with possible later stenosis or regurgitation of the aortic outflow"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Bicuspid aortic valve with possible later stenosis or regurgitation of the aortic outflow): Upper extremity hypertension with weak femoral pulses and rib notching from collateral intercostal arteries indicates coarctation of the aorta. A bicuspid aortic valve is the most common coexisting cardiac anomaly. The other lesions are right sided or cyanotic and are not typical companions."}, {"id": 547, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 74-year-old man has exertional dyspnea and an ECG showing a QRS of 150 ms with broad notched R waves in leads I and V6. Auscultation reveals a second heart sound that splits during expiration and merges on inspiration. Which mechanism best explains this finding?", "options": {"A": "Inspiratory fall in intrathoracic pressure increases right-sided filling, delaying pulmonic closure during inhalation", "B": "Late left ventricular depolarization delays aortic valve closure until after pulmonic valve closure", "C": "Fixed delay of pulmonic closure from atrial septal defect, unchanged by the respiratory cycle", "D": "Early aortic valve closure from severe mitral regurgitation shortens left ventricular ejection time", "E": "Delayed right ventricular activation prolongs pulmonic ejection so pulmonic closure follows aortic closure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Late left ventricular depolarization delays aortic valve closure until after pulmonic valve closure): Left bundle branch block delays left ventricular depolarization, so aortic closure occurs after pulmonic closure, producing paradoxical splitting that is widest on expiration and disappears on inspiration when pulmonic closure is delayed. Physiologic splitting is the opposite pattern, widening on inspiration."}, {"id": 548, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 20-year-old college student living in a dormitory presents with fever, severe headache, neck stiffness, and a petechial rash on the trunk and legs. He is lethargic but protecting his airway. A lumbar puncture is performed, and the cerebrospinal fluid analysis is shown. Which organism most likely caused this illness?", "options": {"A": "Enterovirus infection, a frequent summer cause of meningitis in children and young adults", "B": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection producing basilar exudate and cranial nerve palsies over weeks", "C": "Streptococcus pneumoniae infection following otitis, sinusitis, or pneumonia in older adults", "D": "Listeria monocytogenes infection typical of neonates, pregnancy, and immunosuppressed older adults", "E": "Neisseria meningitidis spread by respiratory droplets among crowded young adults"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Neisseria meningitidis spread by respiratory droplets among crowded young adults): Fever, meningismus, and a petechial rash in a dormitory resident indicate meningococcal meningitis with meningococcemia. The fluid shows markedly elevated pressure, neutrophilic pleocytosis, high protein, and low glucose, a bacterial pattern. Enterovirus gives lymphocytes with normal glucose, and tuberculosis is subacute with a lymphocytic picture.", "image": "question_images/q_548.png"}, {"id": 549, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 66-year-old woman receiving her second unit of red cells after hip surgery develops fever, chills, low back pain, and pain at the infusion site 10 minutes into the transfusion. Blood pressure falls to 84/50 mm Hg and urine in the catheter bag turns red-brown. What is the most appropriate immediate step?", "options": {"A": "Give acetaminophen for the fever and continue the unit while monitoring temperature every fifteen minutes", "B": "Halt the unit at once, keep access open with saline, and verify patient and unit labels", "C": "Send the unit for culture and start broad spectrum antibiotics while completing the unit", "D": "Give intravenous furosemide and sit her upright, then continue the unit slowly with diuresis monitoring", "E": "Give diphenhydramine and hydrocortisone, then resume the unit at half the original infusion rate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Halt the unit at once, keep access open with saline, and verify patient and unit labels): Fever, flank pain, hypotension and hemoglobinuria within minutes of starting a unit indicate acute hemolytic transfusion reaction from ABO incompatibility, usually a clerical error. The transfusion must stop immediately, intravenous access maintained with saline, and the labels and patient identity rechecked. Allergy or fever treatments and continuing the unit would allow more hemolysis."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, and an eGFR of 40 mL/min/1.73 m2 needs an additional glucose-lowering agent that also reduces heart failure hospitalizations and slows kidney disease progression. Which drug class and mechanism fits best?", "options": {"A": "Metformin, activating AMP kinase to reduce hepatic gluconeogenesis and modestly improve peripheral insulin sensitivity", "B": "SGLT2 inhibitor, blocking proximal tubular glucose reabsorption and producing glucosuria with osmotic natriuresis", "C": "DPP-4 inhibitor, slowing incretin degradation to increase glucose dependent insulin secretion and suppress glucagon release", "D": "Thiazolidinedione, activating PPAR gamma to improve peripheral insulin sensitivity and raise adiponectin signaling", "E": "Sulfonylurea, closing pancreatic beta cell potassium channels to stimulate insulin release independent of glucose level"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (SGLT2 inhibitor, blocking proximal tubular glucose reabsorption and producing glucosuria with osmotic natriuresis): SGLT2 inhibitors block glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule, lower glucose, and reduce heart failure hospitalization and chronic kidney disease progression through natriuresis and hemodynamic effects. Thiazolidinediones worsen fluid retention, and sulfonylureas and DPP-4 inhibitors lack this cardiorenal benefit. Metformin remains useful for glycemia but does not provide these specific outcomes."}, {"id": 551, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 70-year-old woman taking simvastatin for hyperlipidemia is prescribed clarithromycin for community-acquired pneumonia. Five days later she reports severe diffuse muscle pain and dark urine, and creatine kinase is markedly elevated. She has no history of kidney disease and takes no other new medications. Which mechanism best explains this adverse reaction?", "options": {"A": "Displacement of the statin from albumin binding sites by the macrolide increasing free drug", "B": "Inhibition of CYP2D6 by the macrolide impairing conversion of the statin into its active form", "C": "Induction of CYP3A4 by the macrolide accelerating statin metabolism into a toxic intermediate", "D": "Inhibition of CYP3A4 by the macrolide raising statin plasma concentration and myotoxicity", "E": "Competition for renal tubular secretion by the macrolide reducing urinary excretion of the statin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inhibition of CYP3A4 by the macrolide raising statin plasma concentration and myotoxicity): Clarithromycin is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, and simvastatin depends heavily on that enzyme for clearance, so statin levels rise and cause rhabdomyolysis. An inducer would lower statin exposure and reduce the effect. Hepatic oxidation, not renal secretion, is the main route of elimination for this statin."}, {"id": 552, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 22-year-old woman with asthma presents during an exacerbation. Bronchial smooth muscle constriction has narrowed her airway radius to half of its baseline value, while the driving pressure from her respiratory muscles is unchanged. Assuming laminar flow, how does airflow through these airways compare with baseline?", "options": {"A": "Flow drops to one sixteenth, since resistance rises with the inverse fourth power of radius", "B": "Flow stays near baseline, since turbulent flow depends mainly on airway length rather than radius", "C": "Flow drops to one quarter, since resistance rises with the inverse square of radius", "D": "Flow drops to one eighth, since resistance rises with the inverse cube of radius", "E": "Flow drops to one half, since resistance rises in direct proportion to falling radius"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flow drops to one sixteenth, since resistance rises with the inverse fourth power of radius): Poiseuille's law gives resistance proportional to viscosity times length divided by radius to the fourth power. Halving the radius multiplies resistance by 16, so flow at constant driving pressure falls to one sixteenth. This steep dependence is why modest bronchoconstriction causes severe obstruction."}, {"id": 553, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 24-year-old marathon runner has an irregular pulse at a pre-participation sports physical and reports no dizziness or syncope. She takes no medications. Her blood pressure is normal. A rhythm strip recorded at rest is shown. Which conduction abnormality is most likely, and which site of block accounts for it?", "options": {"A": "Mobitz type I second-degree block with progressive decremental conduction within the atrioventricular node", "B": "Sinoatrial exit block with pauses equal to multiples of the baseline P to P interval", "C": "Mobitz type II second-degree block with intermittent failure of conduction below the bundle of His", "D": "Third-degree block with independent atrial and ventricular rhythms driven by an infranodal escape focus", "E": "First-degree block with constant PR prolongation and no dropped beats from increased vagal tone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mobitz type I second-degree block with progressive decremental conduction within the atrioventricular node): Progressive PR lengthening followed by a nonconducted P wave defines Mobitz type I, which arises in the AV node and is common in athletes with high vagal tone. Mobitz type II shows a constant PR interval before an abrupt dropped beat and signals infranodal disease, often with a wide QRS.", "image": "question_images/q_553.png"}, {"id": 554, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 60-year-old man with cirrhosis is admitted for pneumonia. On day three he is tremulous, tachycardic, diaphoretic and sees insects on the wall, with a blood pressure of 168/98 mm Hg. Liver function is markedly impaired. Which agent is the best choice for controlling his withdrawal?", "options": {"A": "Phenytoin, because it prevents withdrawal seizures and treats autonomic instability from alcohol cessation", "B": "Haloperidol, because it controls hallucinations and agitation and also raises the seizure threshold", "C": "Lorazepam, because conjugation spares oxidative metabolism and prevents active metabolite accumulation in hepatic dysfunction", "D": "Chlordiazepoxide, because oral dosing is convenient and its long-acting metabolites prevent breakthrough symptoms", "E": "Diazepam, because its long half-life provides smooth self-tapering coverage and rapid onset of effect"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lorazepam, because conjugation spares oxidative metabolism and prevents active metabolite accumulation in hepatic dysfunction): Alcohol withdrawal with hallucinations and autonomic hyperactivity is treated with benzodiazepines. In severe liver disease, lorazepam, oxazepam and temazepam are preferred because they are glucuronidated and lack active metabolites. Diazepam and chlordiazepoxide rely on hepatic oxidation and can accumulate into oversedation, and phenytoin does not treat withdrawal."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has episodes of chest pain at rest, usually around 4 a.m., with transient ST elevation on monitoring and angiography showing normal coronary arteries apart from focal spasm. She also has Raynaud phenomenon and smokes occasionally. Which agent is most appropriate for long-term prevention of her episodes?", "options": {"A": "Aspirin, an irreversible cyclooxygenase inhibitor that reduces platelet aggregation in coronary arteries", "B": "Propranolol, a nonselective beta blocker that lowers heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand", "C": "Diltiazem, a nondihydropyridine blocker that relaxes coronary smooth muscle and prevents vasospasm", "D": "Ranolazine, a late sodium current inhibitor that reduces diastolic wall tension in the ventricle", "E": "Clopidogrel, a P2Y12 receptor antagonist that prevents platelet mediated coronary thrombosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Diltiazem, a nondihydropyridine blocker that relaxes coronary smooth muscle and prevents vasospasm): Rest pain at night with transient ST elevation and no fixed stenosis indicates vasospastic Prinzmetal angina. Calcium channel blockers and nitrates relax coronary smooth muscle and are first-line prevention. Nonselective beta blockers can leave alpha mediated vasoconstriction unopposed, and antiplatelet agents do not address spasm."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 9-month-old boy has intermittent episodes of inconsolable crying with legs drawn up, separated by lethargy, after a recent diarrheal illness. He had one stool resembling red jelly. He is hemodynamically stable with a soft abdomen and no peritoneal signs, and his sister has a cold. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Technetium pertechnetate scan to locate ectopic gastric mucosa in the ileum", "B": "Air or contrast enema, serving as both confirmation and nonoperative reduction", "C": "Immediate laparotomy for manual reduction, because bloody stool signals ischemic bowel in all infants", "D": "Observation with oral rehydration and repeat examination, since most episodes pass on their own", "E": "Upper gastrointestinal contrast series to evaluate for duodenal rotation abnormalities"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Air or contrast enema, serving as both confirmation and nonoperative reduction): Colicky pain with lethargy and currant jelly stool is classic for intussusception, usually ileocolic. In a stable child without peritonitis, an enema is diagnostic and therapeutic. Surgery is reserved for failed reduction, perforation, or peritoneal signs."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 34-year-old nurse born in India and vaccinated with BCG as an infant has a 12 mm tuberculin skin test reaction at annual screening. She is asymptomatic, and chest radiograph is normal. Her roommate recently took a cruise. Which next step best clarifies whether she has latent infection?", "options": {"A": "Begin isoniazid and rifampin therapy now because the reaction exceeds ten millimeters", "B": "Interferon-gamma release assay using antigens such as ESAT-6 absent from the vaccine strain", "C": "Sputum acid-fast smear and culture on three consecutive morning specimens for active disease", "D": "Repeat tuberculin skin test in two weeks to detect a boosted reaction", "E": "Repeat chest radiograph with lateral view and CT of the chest to find granulomas"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interferon-gamma release assay using antigens such as ESAT-6 absent from the vaccine strain): BCG vaccination can cause a false positive tuberculin skin test, whereas interferon-gamma release assays use antigens that the vaccine strain lacks. With no symptoms and a normal radiograph, active disease is not suspected, so sputum studies and more imaging add little. Treating on the skin test alone would risk committing a vaccinated person to months of therapy without confirmation."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 50-year-old runner has burning pain and numbness on the sole of the right foot that worsens at night and with prolonged standing. Tapping behind the medial malleolus reproduces tingling. Examination finds a tight flexor retinaculum and restricted subtalar motion. Which nerve is involved, and which osteopathic approach best addresses the cause?", "options": {"A": "Sural nerve, addressed by myofascial release of the lateral ankle and calcaneal counterstrain", "B": "Deep peroneal nerve, addressed by muscle energy of the anterior compartment and subtalar mobilization", "C": "Tibial nerve, addressed by myofascial release of the flexor retinaculum and articulatory subtalar mobilization", "D": "Superficial peroneal nerve, addressed by releasing the peroneal retinaculum and fibular head muscle energy", "E": "Saphenous nerve, addressed by release of the adductor canal and medial knee counterstrain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tibial nerve, addressed by myofascial release of the flexor retinaculum and articulatory subtalar mobilization): Burning plantar pain with tingling reproduced behind the medial malleolus indicates tibial nerve entrapment in the tarsal tunnel. Releasing the flexor retinaculum and mobilizing the subtalar joint addresses the mechanical cause. The peroneal, sural and saphenous nerves do not travel through that tunnel."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 45-year-old hairdresser has nighttime tingling in the thumb, index, middle fingers and radial ring finger, relieved by shaking the hand. Sensation over the thenar eminence skin is normal. Examination shows a palmar lunate somatic dysfunction with restricted wrist extension and tight flexor retinaculum. Which explanation best links these findings to the symptoms and treatment target?", "options": {"A": "Median nerve compression near the pronator teres, treated by releasing the proximal forearm flexor fascia", "B": "C6 root compression at the foramen, treated by cervical traction and muscle energy techniques", "C": "Lower trunk compression at the thoracic outlet, treated by first rib and scalene techniques", "D": "Ulnar nerve compression in Guyon canal, treated by releasing the hypothenar fascia and pisiform", "E": "Median nerve compression in the carpal tunnel, treated by releasing retinaculum and carpal restrictions"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Median nerve compression in the carpal tunnel, treated by releasing retinaculum and carpal restrictions): Sparing of the thenar skin places the lesion distal to the origin of the palmar cutaneous branch, in the carpal tunnel. Pronator syndrome compresses the same nerve more proximally and usually reduces thenar skin sensation. Guyon canal, cervical and thoracic outlet lesions follow different distributions. Releasing the retinaculum and carpal restrictions widens the tunnel."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 30-year-old man tells his psychiatrist during a session that he intends to shoot his former business partner tomorrow and names the man and his location. The patient has the means at home and has refused voluntary admission. He asks the psychiatrist to keep everything confidential. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Maintain confidentiality and document the threat, because psychotherapy communications carry full legal privilege", "B": "Report the threat to the hospital ethics committee and wait for its written recommendation", "C": "Contact only the patient's family and schedule an earlier follow-up visit within one week", "D": "Obtain the patient's written consent before disclosing anything to a third party, then reassess", "E": "Warn the identified person and law enforcement, and arrange emergency involuntary psychiatric evaluation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Warn the identified person and law enforcement, and arrange emergency involuntary psychiatric evaluation): A serious, specific threat against an identifiable person overrides confidentiality, and the duty to protect is met by warning the potential victim and notifying police while arranging safe evaluation. Privilege and consent requirements yield when danger is imminent. Waiting for a committee or scheduling a routine visit would leave the victim exposed."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has taken prednisone 10 mg daily for 3 years. She develops hypotension and vomiting during an emergency appendectomy and does not respond to fluids. Sodium is 131 mEq/L, potassium is 4.2 mEq/L, and glucose is 58 mg/dL. Which explains why potassium remains normal despite her adrenal insufficiency?", "options": {"A": "Angiotensin II-driven aldosterone secretion from an intact zona glomerulosa remains preserved despite ACTH suppression", "B": "Surgical stress raises pituitary ACTH, which directly stimulates aldosterone secretion from the cortex", "C": "Cortisol deficiency alone lowers potassium by reducing distal sodium delivery to the collecting duct", "D": "Prednisone has strong mineralocorticoid activity that replaces the missing aldosterone effect at the distal tubule", "E": "Adrenal medullary catecholamines released during surgical stress directly increase renal potassium excretion in collecting ducts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Angiotensin II-driven aldosterone secretion from an intact zona glomerulosa remains preserved despite ACTH suppression): Long term exogenous steroid suppresses ACTH and atrophies the zona fasciculata, causing secondary adrenal insufficiency with cortisol deficiency. Aldosterone is controlled mainly by angiotensin II and potassium, so the zona glomerulosa keeps working and potassium stays normal. Prednisone has little mineralocorticoid activity."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A medical student reading the history of the profession learns that a Missouri physician became disillusioned with the medical practices of his era after losing family members to meningitis. He began teaching a drugless, structure-focused approach in the early 1890s. Which person founded the first school of osteopathic medicine?", "options": {"A": "William Garner Sutherland, who developed the cranial concept after studying the beveled skull sutures", "B": "Frederick Mitchell Sr., who developed muscle energy methods and taught them at osteopathic colleges", "C": "Andrew Taylor Still, who opened the first college of osteopathy in Kirksville in 1892", "D": "Daniel David Palmer, who founded a spinal manipulation school in Iowa in the late 1890s", "E": "Viola Frymann, who pioneered osteopathic care of children and established a clinic in California"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Andrew Taylor Still, who opened the first college of osteopathy in Kirksville in 1892): Andrew Taylor Still founded osteopathic medicine and opened the American School of Osteopathy in Kirksville in 1892. Palmer began chiropractic in 1895 and Sutherland, Mitchell, and Frymann were later osteopathic physicians who developed specific methods."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 6-year-old boy has had profuse watery diarrhea for four days and appears lethargic with dry mucous membranes. His respirations are deep and rapid at 36 per minute. The arterial blood gas and electrolyte values are shown. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation from accumulation of unmeasured organic acids", "B": "Primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation lowering bicarbonate, from anxiety and hyperventilation in a distressed child", "C": "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with a concurrent primary respiratory alkalosis from excess ventilation", "D": "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with a concurrent primary respiratory acidosis from inadequate ventilation", "E": "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation from bicarbonate loss in stool"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation from bicarbonate loss in stool): The anion gap is 138 minus 116 minus 11, or 11 mEq/L, which is normal, so the acidosis comes from bicarbonate loss in diarrheal stool with chloride retention. The Winter formula predicts a carbon dioxide of about 24.5 plus or minus 2 mm Hg, matching the measured 24, so compensation is appropriate. A lower or higher value would signal a second respiratory disorder.", "image": "question_images/q_563.png"}, {"id": 564, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 51-year-old woman reports 14 months without menstrual periods, nightly hot flashes that disrupt sleep, and vaginal dryness with painful intercourse. Her pelvic examination shows thin vaginal mucosa, and a urine pregnancy test is negative. Which hormonal pattern is expected on laboratory testing?", "options": {"A": "Low follicle-stimulating hormone with low estradiol reflecting hypothalamic suppression from excessive exercise and low body weight", "B": "Elevated estradiol with suppressed follicle-stimulating hormone caused by an ovarian granulosa cell tumor", "C": "Elevated luteinizing hormone with elevated testosterone and normal estradiol typical of polycystic ovary syndrome", "D": "Elevated follicle-stimulating hormone with low estradiol and low inhibin B reflecting depletion of ovarian follicles", "E": "Elevated prolactin with low follicle-stimulating hormone and normal estradiol from a functioning pituitary adenoma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevated follicle-stimulating hormone with low estradiol and low inhibin B reflecting depletion of ovarian follicles): Menopause follows follicular depletion, so estradiol and inhibin B fall and the loss of negative feedback raises follicle-stimulating hormone. Hypothalamic amenorrhea shares low estradiol but with low gonadotropins. Atrophic vaginal tissue also argues against an estrogen-secreting tumor."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A term newborn's blood contains predominantly hemoglobin F. In the placenta, oxygen moves from maternal to fetal blood even though fetal arterial oxygen tension is lower than maternal. At a given oxygen tension, fetal blood binds more oxygen than adult blood. Which property of fetal hemoglobin explains this?", "options": {"A": "Cooperative binding is lost, giving a hyperbolic curve like that of myoglobin and higher affinity", "B": "Gamma chains bind 2,3-BPG poorly, raising oxygen affinity and shifting the curve leftward", "C": "Gamma chains bind 2,3-BPG avidly, lowering oxygen affinity and shifting the curve rightward", "D": "Higher heme iron content per molecule, which increases oxygen carrying capacity at each tension", "E": "Greater sensitivity to the Bohr effect, so placental carbon dioxide release shifts the curve leftward"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gamma chains bind 2,3-BPG poorly, raising oxygen affinity and shifting the curve leftward): Hemoglobin F has gamma chains in place of beta chains, and these bind 2,3-BPG weakly. Less 2,3-BPG binding stabilizes the relaxed form, increasing oxygen affinity and shifting the dissociation curve left, which favors oxygen transfer across the placenta. Hemoglobin F still binds oxygen cooperatively and carries four heme groups."}, {"id": 566, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 4-week-old firstborn boy has had two weeks of forceful nonbilious vomiting immediately after feeds. He remains hungry afterward, is mildly dehydrated, and has a palpable olive-shaped epigastric mass. Before surgical correction, which laboratory pattern would the physician most expect to find?", "options": {"A": "Low chloride, low potassium, and elevated bicarbonate from loss of gastric hydrochloric acid", "B": "Low chloride, high potassium, and low bicarbonate from adrenal insufficiency with salt wasting", "C": "Normal chloride, low potassium, and low bicarbonate from large volume watery stool losses", "D": "Low sodium, low chloride, and high anion gap from starvation ketone accumulation", "E": "High chloride, normal potassium, and low bicarbonate from renal tubular bicarbonate wasting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low chloride, low potassium, and elevated bicarbonate from loss of gastric hydrochloric acid): Repeated loss of gastric acid produces hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis as the kidney retains bicarbonate and wastes potassium and hydrogen ions. Adrenal salt wasting causes hyperkalemia and acidosis, and diarrhea causes a nongap acidosis. Rehydration with saline and potassium should precede pyloromyotomy."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "Passive rotation of her head to the right reproduces brief dizziness, diplopia and unsteadiness that resolve on returning to neutral. A 67-year-old woman with a three-week cervicogenic headache has C2 somatic dysfunction. She takes aspirin and a statin for atherosclerosis. Which technique is most appropriate to treat her cervical dysfunction?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain with the head rotated and extended into the position of ease for ninety seconds", "B": "Articulatory technique with repetitive springing of the upper cervical segments through full rotation and extension", "C": "High velocity low amplitude thrust at the C2 barrier, restoring segmental motion and relieving the headache", "D": "Muscle energy with the head rotated to the restrictive barrier and a firm isometric contraction", "E": "Soft tissue and inhibition work with the head kept neutral, avoiding terminal neck positions"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Soft tissue and inhibition work with the head kept neutral, avoiding terminal neck positions): Dizziness and diplopia with head rotation suggest vertebrobasilar insufficiency, an absolute contraindication to cervical HVLA and a reason to avoid sustained rotation or extension. Techniques that keep the neck neutral, such as soft tissue and inhibition, treat the dysfunction without provoking vertebral artery compromise."}, {"id": 568, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 9-year-old boy has recurrent abdominal pain, eruptive yellow papules on his buttocks, and lipemia retinalis. His serum is milky, and after 12 hours at 4 degrees Celsius a creamy layer floats above clear plasma. Triglycerides are 2,800 mg/dL and LDL cholesterol is low. Which lipoprotein abnormality is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Chylomicron accumulation from deficient lipoprotein lipase activity, preventing hydrolysis of triglycerides carried in chylomicrons", "B": "Hepatic VLDL overproduction from insulin resistance, producing diffuse turbidity with elevated triglycerides and small dense LDL", "C": "LDL particle accumulation from defective receptor uptake, producing tendon xanthomas with normal triglycerides", "D": "Absent HDL particles from ABCA1 mutation, producing orange tonsils, neuropathy and very low plasma cholesterol", "E": "Remnant lipoprotein accumulation from apolipoprotein E2 homozygosity, producing palmar xanthomas and elevated cholesterol with triglycerides"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chylomicron accumulation from deficient lipoprotein lipase activity, preventing hydrolysis of triglycerides carried in chylomicrons): A floating cream layer over clear plasma means chylomicrons are accumulating, and with eruptive xanthomas, lipemia retinalis and pancreatitis-type pain this indicates lipoprotein lipase or apolipoprotein C-II deficiency. VLDL excess produces uniform turbidity, remnant disease raises cholesterol too, and the low LDL excludes familial hypercholesterolemia."}, {"id": 569, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old man presents to the emergency department with three days of fever, productive cough with rust-colored sputum, and pleuritic right-sided chest pain. Temperature is 39.1 C and oxygen saturation is 91% on room air. In the chest radiograph shown, a dense right lower lobe opacity contains tubular lucent branching structures. What do these lucent structures represent?", "options": {"A": "Cavitation with an air-fluid level from central necrosis of the consolidated lung", "B": "Engorged pulmonary vessels seen end-on within an area of interstitial fluid accumulation", "C": "Patent air-filled bronchi outlined by surrounding alveoli filled with inflammatory exudate", "D": "Dilated thick-walled bronchi from chronic airway destruction and repeated infection scarring", "E": "Thin-walled air-filled parenchymal spaces created by destruction of distal alveolar septa"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Patent air-filled bronchi outlined by surrounding alveoli filled with inflammatory exudate): Air bronchograms appear when alveoli fill with exudate, which removes the normal air contrast and silhouettes the still-aerated bronchi. This is the radiographic hallmark of lobar pneumonia, and here it fits the clinical picture of pneumococcal infection. Bronchiectasis produces dilated, thick-walled airways, but those are seen in chronically scarred lung rather than in acute lobar consolidation.", "image": "question_images/q_569.png"}, {"id": 570, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A clinic introduced a hand hygiene checklist and audited compliance weekly, as plotted in the run chart shown. Compliance shifted upward after the change and stayed higher for six consecutive weeks. The team has finished comparing results with its prediction and found no unintended effects. What is the next step in the improvement cycle?", "options": {"A": "Act: adopt the checklist as standard practice and continue monitoring to confirm the gain is sustained", "B": "Study: collect several additional months of baseline data to define the pre-intervention process before acting", "C": "Do: roll the checklist out across the whole hospital with the same measures for comparison", "D": "Do: repeat the same test on a second group for several more cycles before drawing conclusions", "E": "Plan: write a new aim and prediction before deciding whether to keep the checklist"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Act: adopt the checklist as standard practice and continue monitoring to confirm the gain is sustained): In the PDSA cycle, once the Study step shows the predicted, sustained improvement without harm, the Act step follows: adopt the change as standard work and keep measuring. Further Do cycles or rollout are appropriate when results are mixed or the change needs adaptation. Returning to Plan or collecting baseline data repeats completed steps.", "image": "question_images/q_570.png"}, {"id": 571, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 55-year-old man with septic shock from pneumonia needs emergency intubation. Blood pressure is 82/46 mm Hg and he has diffuse wheezing from severe asthma. He has no known drug allergies and takes inhaled albuterol. Which induction agent is the most appropriate choice?", "options": {"A": "Thiopental, which provides rapid onset and lowers intracranial pressure through reduced cerebral metabolic demand", "B": "Ketamine, which preserves sympathetic tone and relaxes bronchial smooth muscle through catecholamine release", "C": "Midazolam, which causes anterograde amnesia with modest hemodynamic effects at induction doses", "D": "Etomidate, which maintains hemodynamic stability with minimal effect on cardiac contractility and airway tone", "E": "Propofol, which provides rapid smooth induction and some bronchodilation through reduced airway reflexes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ketamine, which preserves sympathetic tone and relaxes bronchial smooth muscle through catecholamine release): Ketamine stimulates sympathetic outflow and is a bronchodilator, which suits hypotension with active bronchospasm. Etomidate is also hemodynamically stable and is a defensible alternative in shock, but it gives no airway benefit, while propofol and thiopental would worsen hypotension."}, {"id": 572, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 28-year-old man develops difficulty walking after wearing a tight leg cast for six weeks following a tibial fracture. He catches his toes while walking and cannot walk on his heels. Pain is minimal. Which set of examination findings is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Weak hip abduction with a pelvic drop on single leg stance, and no sensory loss", "B": "Weak plantarflexion and inversion with preserved eversion, plus numbness over the sole of the foot", "C": "Weak knee extension with a diminished patellar reflex, plus numbness over the anterior thigh", "D": "Weak dorsiflexion and eversion with preserved inversion, plus numbness over the dorsal foot", "E": "Weak hip adduction with preserved ankle motion, plus numbness over the medial thigh"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weak dorsiflexion and eversion with preserved inversion, plus numbness over the dorsal foot): The common peroneal nerve is compressed at the fibular neck by tight casts, weakening dorsiflexion and eversion and causing foot drop with dorsal foot numbness, while inversion by the tibial nerve remains. Tibial nerve injury would weaken plantarflexion and inversion with sole numbness. Femoral, obturator, and superior gluteal injuries affect proximal movements."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 17-year-old high school basketball player has a harsh systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border noted at a school physical. His uncle died suddenly during a game at age 30. The murmur is softer when he lies supine. During the examination, which maneuver would most likely decrease the intensity of the murmur?", "options": {"A": "Squatting from standing, which raises venous return and afterload and widens the outflow tract", "B": "Abrupt standing from squatting, which reduces preload and narrows the left ventricular outflow tract", "C": "Sublingual nitroglycerin, which dilates veins and reduces ventricular filling volume and cavity size", "D": "Valsalva strain phase, which decreases venous return and narrows the left ventricular outflow tract", "E": "Amyl nitrite inhalation, which lowers afterload and narrows the left ventricular outflow tract"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Squatting from standing, which raises venous return and afterload and widens the outflow tract): A harsh murmur that softens supine with a family history of sudden death fits dynamic outflow obstruction from hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Squatting increases venous return and afterload, enlarging the ventricle and widening the outflow tract, which softens the murmur. Strain, standing, and vasodilators reduce preload or afterload and increase the obstruction."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with metastatic pancreatic cancer has taken increasing doses of oral morphine for three weeks. Her pain is controlled, and she now needs a lower antiemetic dose and feels less drowsy than during the first days. She takes no other medications. Which adverse effect will most likely persist at unchanged intensity with continued dosing?", "options": {"A": "Euphoria, because limbic reward receptors resist desensitization and keep producing the same pleasure", "B": "Sedation, because cerebral cortex receptors resist desensitization and keep depressing wakefulness over time", "C": "Nausea, because chemoreceptor trigger zone receptors resist desensitization and keep driving emesis", "D": "Constipation, because enteric mu receptors resist desensitization and keep slowing intestinal transit", "E": "Respiratory depression, because brainstem receptors resist desensitization and keep lowering ventilatory drive"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Constipation, because enteric mu receptors resist desensitization and keep slowing intestinal transit): Tolerance develops to nausea, sedation, euphoria, and respiratory depression, but little develops to constipation because enteric mu receptors remain responsive. A scheduled stimulant laxative is therefore started with chronic opioids. Respiratory depression is the tempting choice, but tolerance does diminish it with gradual titration."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 67-year-old retired shipyard worker who smoked one pack daily for 40 years presents with gradually worsening exertional dyspnea. Chest radiograph shows bilateral calcified plaques along the diaphragm and lateral pleura, with lower lobe interstitial fibrosis. Which malignancy is he most likely to develop?", "options": {"A": "Bronchogenic carcinoma, because asbestos and tobacco smoke act synergistically to multiply lung cancer risk", "B": "Laryngeal squamous carcinoma, which carries the highest relative risk among cancers linked to inhaled fibers", "C": "Hepatic angiosarcoma, a tumor associated with heavy occupational exposure to inhaled mineral fibers", "D": "Malignant pleural mesothelioma, since it is the most common cancer among asbestos exposed workers", "E": "Adenocarcinoma of the colon from swallowed fibers carried upward and cleared by the mucociliary escalator"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bronchogenic carcinoma, because asbestos and tobacco smoke act synergistically to multiply lung cancer risk): Lung cancer is the most common malignancy in asbestos-exposed workers, and smoking multiplies the risk many fold, so his risk of bronchogenic carcinoma far exceeds his risk of mesothelioma. Mesothelioma is strongly specific to asbestos but is much less common and shows no interaction with smoking."}, {"id": 576, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 60-year-old man has a recurrent 1.8-cm morpheaform basal cell carcinoma beside the nasal ala that returned within a year of standard surgical excision. He wants the highest cure rate while sparing as much normal facial tissue as possible. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Repeat wide local excision with a four-millimeter margin and routine vertical sectioning", "B": "Topical imiquimod cream applied five days weekly for six weeks over the lesion", "C": "Definitive external beam radiation therapy delivered in small fractions over several weeks", "D": "Curettage and electrodesiccation repeated for three cycles under local anesthesia in clinic, healing by secondary intention", "E": "Mohs micrographic surgery with full horizontal margin assessment during staged tissue removal"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Mohs micrographic surgery with full horizontal margin assessment during staged tissue removal): Recurrent, infiltrative or high-risk facial basal cell carcinoma is best treated by Mohs surgery, which examines the entire margin and conserves tissue while giving the highest cure rate. Curettage and imiquimod suit small superficial lesions, and standard excision has already failed. Radiation is reserved for patients who cannot have surgery."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 66-year-old man has progressive exertional dyspnea, dry cough, bibasilar inspiratory crackles, and digital clubbing over eight months. CT shows subpleural honeycombing with traction bronchiectasis in both lower lobes. Pulmonary function shows FVC 58% of predicted and normal FEV1/FVC. Biopsy was not required. Which medication has been shown to slow his decline in lung function?", "options": {"A": "Nintedanib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that blocks fibroblast proliferation and profibrotic growth factor signaling", "B": "Long-term inhaled bronchodilators, which relieve airflow limitation and slow loss of forced vital capacity", "C": "Azathioprine with prednisone and N-acetylcysteine, targeting presumed lymphocyte-driven inflammation of the interstitium", "D": "High-dose prednisone alone, which suppresses alveolar inflammation and reverses established fibrotic scarring", "E": "Cyclophosphamide pulses, which deplete proliferating lymphocytes and halt progression of usual interstitial pneumonia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nintedanib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that blocks fibroblast proliferation and profibrotic growth factor signaling): Honeycombing at the lung bases with traction bronchiectasis, clubbing and restriction indicate idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis with a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern. Antifibrotic drugs such as nintedanib and pirfenidone slow the loss of forced vital capacity. Immunosuppression with prednisone, azathioprine and N-acetylcysteine increased adverse outcomes in a trial."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 46-year-old man with frequent heartburn undergoes osteopathic evaluation of the thoracoabdominal diaphragm, which is tender and restricted on inhalation. The physician recalls the vertebral attachments of its crura to plan lumbar treatment. Which description of the crural attachments is correct for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Right crus attached to L1 and L2, left crus attached to L1, L2 and L3", "B": "Both crura attach only to L1 and L2 vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs", "C": "Right crus to L1 through L3 and left crus to L1 and L2", "D": "Both crura attach from T10 through T12 along the anterior vertebral bodies", "E": "Right crus to L2 through L4 and left crus to L2 and L3"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right crus to L1 through L3 and left crus to L1 and L2): The right crus is longer and attaches to the anterior bodies of L1 to L3, while the left crus attaches to L1 to L2. The reversed pairing is a common slip. Because the crura reach the upper lumbar spine, dysfunction there can feed back into diaphragm motion and reflux symptoms."}, {"id": 579, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 27-year-old man has had difficulty conceiving for 14 months. He has a left scrotal mass that feels like a bag of worms while standing and decreases when he lies down. Right scrotal examination is normal. Why does this lesion occur on the left side far more often than the right?", "options": {"A": "Left gonadal vein joins the left renal vein at a right angle, transmitting higher pressure", "B": "Left spermatic cord is compressed by the sigmoid colon, which raises pampiniform plexus pressure", "C": "Left testicular lymphatics drain to the external iliac nodes, causing lymphatic engorgement and scrotal swelling", "D": "Left testicular artery crosses the pampiniform plexus and compresses it against the inguinal ring", "E": "Left gonadal vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava, where venous pressure is highest"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left gonadal vein joins the left renal vein at a right angle, transmitting higher pressure): The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein at a nearly right angle, so venous pressure is transmitted back to the pampiniform plexus and valves become incompetent, producing a varicocele. The right gonadal vein enters the inferior vena cava obliquely at lower pressure. Lymphatics drain to the para-aortic nodes and do not form this mass."}, {"id": 580, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has persistent synovitis despite eight months of methotrexate at a maximum tolerated dose. Her rheumatologist plans to add adalimumab. She is a nurse and has no fever or cough. Which pretreatment evaluation is most important for this drug class?", "options": {"A": "Glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase level, because oxidant stress may precipitate hemolysis with the drug", "B": "Serum folate and vitamin B12 concentrations, because the drug depletes tetrahydrofolate pools", "C": "Baseline electrocardiogram with QT interval measurement, because the drug blocks cardiac potassium channels", "D": "Baseline thiopurine methyltransferase genotype, because the drug is inactivated by thiopurine methylation", "E": "Testing for latent tuberculosis, because blocking tumor necrosis factor impairs granuloma maintenance"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Testing for latent tuberculosis, because blocking tumor necrosis factor impairs granuloma maintenance): Tumor necrosis factor inhibitors impair granuloma maintenance, so latent tuberculosis can reactivate and every patient needs screening with an interferon gamma release assay or tuberculin test before treatment. Folate depletion belongs to methotrexate, which she already takes. Thiopurine methyltransferase testing applies to azathioprine rather than to biologics."}, {"id": 581, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 67-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation has sudden onset of word-finding difficulty and cannot see objects in the upper part of the right visual field in both eyes. Strength is normal. Imaging shows a left middle cerebral artery branch infarct. Which pathway was most likely interrupted?", "options": {"A": "Left Meyer loop of the optic radiation, carrying inferior retinal fibers through the temporal lobe", "B": "Left optic tract, carrying crossed and uncrossed fibers from the right visual hemifield", "C": "Left parietal optic radiation, carrying superior retinal fibers through the parietal lobe", "D": "Right Meyer loop of the optic radiation, carrying inferior retinal fibers through the temporal lobe", "E": "Optic chiasm, interrupting crossed nasal retinal fibers from both eyes at the sella"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left Meyer loop of the optic radiation, carrying inferior retinal fibers through the temporal lobe): Inferior retinal fibers see the upper visual field and loop through the temporal lobe as Meyer loop, so a left temporal lesion causes a right superior quadrantanopia in both eyes, called pie in the sky. The parietal radiation carries superior retinal fibers and its lesion causes an inferior defect, while chiasmal lesions give bitemporal defects."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has 2 days of fever, confusion, and dark urine. Examination shows pallor and scattered petechiae. Hemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL, platelets 18,000 per microliter, creatinine 1.7 mg/dL, and smear shows fragmented red cells. PT, aPTT, and fibrinogen are normal. Which mechanism most directly explains this condition?", "options": {"A": "Antibodies against platelet factor 4 and heparin complexes activating platelets through Fc receptors", "B": "Deficient metalloprotease activity leaving ultralarge von Willebrand multimers to form platelet-rich microthrombi", "C": "Autoantibodies against platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa causing splenic macrophage destruction of platelets", "D": "Shiga toxin injury to renal endothelium triggering platelet aggregation after a diarrheal prodrome", "E": "Widespread tissue factor release consuming clotting factors and fibrinogen throughout the microcirculation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Deficient metalloprotease activity leaving ultralarge von Willebrand multimers to form platelet-rich microthrombi): Fever, neurologic change, renal injury, microangiopathic hemolysis and severe thrombocytopenia with normal coagulation studies indicate thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura. The cause is deficient ADAMTS13, which normally cleaves ultralarge von Willebrand multimers. Disseminated intravascular coagulation would prolong the PT and aPTT and lower fibrinogen."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A full-term newborn has not passed meconium by 48 hours and has a distended abdomen with bilious emesis. Digital rectal examination is followed by an explosive release of stool and gas. Contrast enema shows a transition zone. Which finding is expected on rectal suction biopsy?", "options": {"A": "Increased numbers of ganglion cells with giant ganglia in the submucosal plexus", "B": "Mucosal ulceration with transmural inflammation and noncaseating granulomas in the rectal wall", "C": "Absent ganglion cells in submucosal and myenteric plexuses with hypertrophied nerve trunks", "D": "Absent ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus only with a preserved submucosal plexus", "E": "Hypertrophy of the circular muscle layer without change in the nerve plexuses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Absent ganglion cells in submucosal and myenteric plexuses with hypertrophied nerve trunks): Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration, leaving the distal bowel without ganglion cells in both plexuses and with thick extrinsic nerve fibers that stain for acetylcholinesterase. The rectum is always involved and the transition zone is the dilated normal bowel above. Muscle hypertrophy and granulomas describe other diseases."}, {"id": 584, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 38-year-old man presents after a motorcycle crash that fractured the middle of his right humeral shaft. He cannot extend his wrist or fingers, and sensation is reduced over the dorsum of the hand at the first web space. Elbow extension remains strong. Which nerve was injured?", "options": {"A": "Ulnar nerve, passing behind the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel", "B": "Radial nerve, lying directly against bone within the spiral groove of the arm", "C": "Musculocutaneous nerve, running between biceps brachii and brachialis in the anterior arm", "D": "Median nerve, crossing the cubital fossa beneath the bicipital aponeurosis near the elbow", "E": "Posterior interosseous nerve, passing through the supinator muscle in the proximal forearm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Radial nerve, lying directly against bone within the spiral groove of the arm): The radial nerve lies against the humerus in the spiral groove, so a midshaft fracture causes wrist drop and loss of sensation on the dorsal first web space. Triceps is spared because its branches leave the nerve proximal to the groove, and a posterior interosseous lesion would spare skin sensation."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 2-day-old term infant has microcephaly, jaundice, petechiae and hepatosplenomegaly. Cranial ultrasonography shows periventricular calcifications, and the newborn fails the hearing screen. The mother, a daycare worker, had a mild febrile mononucleosis-like illness during the second trimester and owns no cats. Which pathogen most likely caused this infant's condition?", "options": {"A": "Rubella virus, a togavirus causing congenital cataracts, patent ductus arteriosus and sensorineural deafness in infants", "B": "Toxoplasma gondii, a protozoan from cat feces or undercooked meat causing diffuse calcifications and hydrocephalus", "C": "Cytomegalovirus, a herpesvirus shed in child saliva and urine, infecting the fetus across the placenta", "D": "Herpes simplex virus, acquired at delivery and causing vesicles, encephalitis and temporal lobe hemorrhagic necrosis", "E": "Treponema pallidum, a spirochete causing hepatosplenomegaly, snuffles, rash and periosteal bone lesions in newborns"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cytomegalovirus, a herpesvirus shed in child saliva and urine, infecting the fetus across the placenta): Periventricular calcifications with microcephaly, petechiae and sensorineural hearing loss point to congenital cytomegalovirus, the most common congenital infection, often acquired from young children's secretions. Toxoplasmosis typically gives scattered calcifications with hydrocephalus and chorioretinitis, and the absent cat exposure further argues against it. Rubella would add cataracts and a patent ductus."}, {"id": 586, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 52-year-old roofer presents with two days of a swollen, red, painful right elbow after scraping the skin over the olecranon. Temperature is 38.4 C. The bursa is fluctuant and warm, and elbow flexion and extension are nearly full and only mildly painful. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Obtain urgent arthrocentesis of the elbow joint and start empiric broad spectrum intravenous antibiotics", "B": "Aspirate the bursa for cell count, Gram stain and culture, then begin antistaphylococcal antibiotics", "C": "Inject a corticosteroid into the bursa after compressive wrapping to reduce inflammatory swelling quickly", "D": "Perform immediate surgical bursectomy in the operating room to prevent chronic bursal wall thickening", "E": "Prescribe nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, a padded sleeve and rest with follow-up if swelling persists"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aspirate the bursa for cell count, Gram stain and culture, then begin antistaphylococcal antibiotics): A warm, fluctuant bursa with fever after a skin break suggests septic olecranon bursitis, usually from Staphylococcus aureus. Aspiration with culture confirms the diagnosis and antistaphylococcal antibiotics follow. Preserved elbow motion shows the joint is not infected, so joint aspiration is unnecessary, and steroid injection would be harmful."}, {"id": 587, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 68-year-old widower who lives alone eats mostly tea and toast. He presents with bleeding gums, small hemorrhages around hair follicles and coiled hairs on his legs. A laparotomy scar from six weeks ago has partly reopened. Platelet count and coagulation studies are normal. Which biochemical defect best explains the poor wound strength?", "options": {"A": "Excess glucocorticoid state suppressing fibroblast proliferation and collagen synthesis, causing thin skin and poor healing", "B": "Impaired hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues, preventing stable triple helix assembly of procollagen", "C": "Deficient lysyl oxidase activity from low copper, preventing covalent cross-links between mature collagen fibrils", "D": "Defective type III collagen from a COL3A1 mutation, weakening vessel walls and fragile tissues", "E": "Defective propeptide cleavage by procollagen peptidases, leaving soluble procollagen that cannot form fibrils"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Impaired hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues, preventing stable triple helix assembly of procollagen): Perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs and bleeding gums in a man with no fruit or vegetables indicate scurvy. Vitamin C is a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases, so procollagen cannot form a stable triple helix and wounds fail to hold. Copper dependent crosslinking defects occur in a different disorder and would not fit this diet."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 26-year-old woman has 3 days of dysuria and urinary frequency, followed by fever of 39.2 C, right flank pain, and vomiting. She has costovertebral angle tenderness. Urinalysis shows positive leukocyte esterase, positive nitrite, and bacteria. Which additional urine microscopy finding best localizes the infection to the kidney?", "options": {"A": "Broad waxy casts indicating severe tubular dilation from advanced chronic kidney disease", "B": "Dysmorphic red blood cells with acanthocytes indicating bleeding from damaged glomerular capillaries", "C": "Numerous squamous epithelial cells indicating bladder inflammation from a contaminated midstream urine specimen", "D": "Eosinophils in the urine sediment indicating drug induced hypersensitivity of the renal interstitium", "E": "White blood cell casts formed within renal tubules and carried into the urine"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (White blood cell casts formed within renal tubules and carried into the urine): White cell casts take the shape of renal tubules, so they prove the leukocytes came from the kidney rather than the bladder, confirming acute pyelonephritis. Leukocyte esterase, nitrite and bacteria occur in cystitis too. Squamous cells indicate contamination, and eosinophils point to interstitial nephritis."}, {"id": 589, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 17-year-old female distance runner has 3 weeks of worsening pain over the anterior tibia that now occurs at rest and at night. She has had amenorrhea for 5 months and a body mass index of 17 kg/m2. Point tenderness is present over the mid tibia. Radiographs are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Reassurance and return to full training with stretching for medial tibial stress syndrome", "B": "Repeat plain radiographs in two weeks while allowing continued running at reduced mileage", "C": "Compression ultrasound of the calf veins to exclude deep vein thrombosis", "D": "Fasciotomy referral for chronic exertional compartment syndrome of the anterior leg compartment", "E": "Magnetic resonance imaging, with protected weight bearing and evaluation for low energy availability"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Magnetic resonance imaging, with protected weight bearing and evaluation for low energy availability): Rest pain, night pain, focal bony tenderness, and menstrual dysfunction with low body weight suggest a tibial stress fracture in a patient with the female athlete triad. Radiographs are insensitive early, so MRI is the best next test while avoiding loading. Shin splints produce diffuse pain that does not occur at rest."}, {"id": 590, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A newborn boy has a single umbilical artery and mild preauricular anomalies. Ultrasound shows complete absence of the left kidney and left ureter, with a compensatory enlarged right kidney. Which embryonic structure most likely failed to arise from the mesonephric duct on the left side?", "options": {"A": "Mesonephric tubules, which form the efferent ductules and drain the transient embryonic excretory unit", "B": "Pronephros, the first transient excretory structure that regresses and induces the mesonephros to develop", "C": "Ureteric bud, which induces metanephric mesenchyme to form nephrons and gives rise to collecting ducts", "D": "Metanephric mesenchyme, which forms the glomeruli and proximal tubules after induction by the ureteric bud", "E": "Urogenital sinus, which gives rise to the bladder and urethra by cloacal partitioning in early gestation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ureteric bud, which induces metanephric mesenchyme to form nephrons and gives rise to collecting ducts): The ureteric bud sprouts from the mesonephric duct, invades the metanephric mesenchyme and induces nephron formation, so failure of the bud yields absence of the kidney and ureter. The metanephric mesenchyme is a separate intermediate mesoderm derivative and cannot explain a missing ureter."}, {"id": 591, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 57-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C and cirrhosis, who takes metformin for diabetes, has a new 4 cm liver lesion. Multiphase CT shows intense arterial phase enhancement with washout during the portal venous phase. Serum alpha-fetoprotein is 1,200 ng/mL. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Hepatocellular adenoma, a benign tumor linked to oral contraceptive use that carries a bleeding risk", "B": "Metastatic colorectal adenocarcinoma, usually presenting as multiple hypovascular lesions with raised carcinoembryonic antigen", "C": "Focal nodular hyperplasia, a benign lesion with a central stellate scar that usually needs no treatment", "D": "Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma arising from bile duct epithelium, typically with delayed enhancement and raised CA 19-9", "E": "Hepatocellular carcinoma arising from malignant transformation of regenerating hepatocytes after years of viral inflammation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Hepatocellular carcinoma arising from malignant transformation of regenerating hepatocytes after years of viral inflammation): In a cirrhotic liver, a mass with arterial hyperenhancement and portal venous washout, together with a markedly raised alpha-fetoprotein, is diagnostic of hepatocellular carcinoma. Cholangiocarcinoma is the main competing primary tumor but shows delayed, progressive enhancement and rises in CA 19-9 rather than alpha-fetoprotein. Metformin use is incidental."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "An 8-year-old boy has had four episodes of otitis media with effusion this year. Today there is no fever, the tympanic membranes are dull with a middle ear effusion and no bulging, and he is not in pain. The osteopathic physician wants a technique to promote Eustachian tube patency and lymphatic flow. Which is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Frontal lift technique, using gentle upward traction on the frontal bone to drain the sinuses", "B": "Occipitoatlantal decompression, using suboccipital traction to relieve vagal compression and reduce secretions", "C": "Galbreath mandibular drainage technique, using gentle lateral traction on the angle of the mandible", "D": "Venous sinus drainage technique, using sustained pressure at the occipital protuberance to improve venous return", "E": "Compression of the fourth ventricle, using occipital contact to induce a still point"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Galbreath mandibular drainage technique, using gentle lateral traction on the angle of the mandible): The Galbreath technique applies gentle traction at the mandible angle to assist Eustachian tube function and lymphatic drainage in children with otitis media with effusion when no acute infection is present. Frontal lift targets the sinuses, venous sinus technique targets intracranial venous flow, and the other cranial techniques do not directly influence the Eustachian tube."}, {"id": 593, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 39-year-old man has a tender point at the lateral aspect of the knee. During counterstrain, tenderness decreases from ten out of ten to eight out of ten when the leg is placed in the first position. He is comfortable. What should the physician do next to reach the goal of the technique?", "options": {"A": "Ask the patient to push against resistance while holding the leg position", "B": "Apply firm compression to the point while keeping the leg in position", "C": "Hold the current position unchanged and begin the ninety second timer now", "D": "Fine-tune the position with small adjustments until tenderness falls by about seventy percent", "E": "Move the leg into the restrictive barrier to confirm the tenderness returns"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fine-tune the position with small adjustments until tenderness falls by about seventy percent): A reduction of only twenty percent is short of the goal, which is roughly a seventy percent fall in tenderness. The physician fine-tunes with small adjustments and then holds the final position about ninety seconds. Moving to the barrier, compressing or asking for contraction does not meet the criteria of the technique."}, {"id": 594, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 55-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis has ascites, splenomegaly, and a platelet count of 82,000/microL. Dilated, tortuous veins radiate from his umbilicus across the abdominal wall, and no bruit is heard over them. Which pair of venous channels has opened to produce the abdominal wall finding?", "options": {"A": "Left gastric veins communicating with the esophageal tributaries of the azygos system", "B": "Paraumbilical veins of the portal system communicating with the superficial epigastric veins", "C": "Splenic vein tributaries communicating with the left renal vein across the retroperitoneum, forming splenorenal shunts", "D": "Superior rectal veins communicating with the middle and inferior rectal venous plexus", "E": "Retroperitoneal colic veins communicating with the lumbar and renal capsular veins"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Paraumbilical veins of the portal system communicating with the superficial epigastric veins): Portal hypertension reopens the paraumbilical veins within the falciform ligament, which drain to the superficial epigastric veins of the systemic circulation and produce caput medusae. The left gastric to azygos and superior to middle rectal anastomoses cause varices at the esophagus and anorectum instead."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 52-year-old man has had three attacks of podagra this year, each resolving with naproxen. Serum uric acid is 9.8 mg/dL and he takes hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Creatinine is normal and he has no history of kidney stones. His physician starts long term urate lowering therapy. Which mechanism does the drug most commonly chosen first use?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of xanthine oxidase, reducing conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid", "B": "Inhibition of cyclooxygenase enzymes to reduce prostaglandin mediated inflammation within the joint capsule", "C": "Blockade of URAT1 in the proximal tubule, increasing renal excretion of uric acid", "D": "Conversion of uric acid to soluble allantoin by a recombinant uricase enzyme infusion", "E": "Binding of tubulin to impair neutrophil migration and inflammasome activation in the joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Inhibition of xanthine oxidase, reducing conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid): Allopurinol, the usual initial drug for lowering urate, inhibits xanthine oxidase so less hypoxanthine and xanthine is converted to uric acid. Uricosurics act on URAT1 and are second line, colchicine and NSAIDs treat or prevent flares without lowering urate, and pegloticase is reserved for refractory disease. Thiazides raise uric acid by competing for tubular secretion."}, {"id": 596, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old man has exertional chest pressure that resolves with rest and a normal resting ECG. On examination, the physician finds boggy tissue, tenderness, and restricted motion in the paraspinal tissues at T2 to T4 on the left. Which visceral source best fits this viscerosomatic reflex pattern?", "options": {"A": "Stomach affected by gastritis, with sympathetic afferents entering the cord at T5 to T9", "B": "Jejunum affected by enteritis with sympathetic afferents entering the cord at T8 to T10", "C": "Kidney affected by stone disease, with sympathetic afferents entering the cord at T10 to L1", "D": "Myocardium affected by coronary ischemia, with sympathetic afferents entering the cord at T1 to T5", "E": "Distal colon affected by diverticulitis, with sympathetic afferents entering the cord at L1 to L2"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Myocardium affected by coronary ischemia, with sympathetic afferents entering the cord at T1 to T5): The sympathetic supply of the heart arises from T1 to T5, so segmental tissue changes at T2 to T4 on the left point to a cardiac source, in keeping with the exertional pressure. Stomach reflexes occur lower, at T5 to T9, and would not explain upper thoracic findings."}, {"id": 597, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 54-year-old man is hospitalized after hernia repair. On day 3 he becomes agitated and describes insects crawling on the walls. He is disoriented to place, with temperature 38.2 C, pulse 124 per minute, blood pressure 168/98 mm Hg, coarse tremor and drenching sweat. He reports drinking heavily every day until four days ago. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Oral clonidine, reducing central sympathetic outflow to control the tachycardia, hypertension and tremor", "B": "Intravenous phenytoin loading, stabilizing neuronal sodium channels to prevent alcohol withdrawal seizures", "C": "Intravenous lorazepam titrated to symptoms, enhancing GABA-A activity to control delirium and prevent seizures", "D": "Oral naltrexone, blocking opioid receptors to reduce craving and prevent early relapse to drinking", "E": "Intravenous haloperidol alone, blocking dopamine D2 receptors to control the hallucinations and agitation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravenous lorazepam titrated to symptoms, enhancing GABA-A activity to control delirium and prevent seizures): Fever, autonomic instability, hallucinations and disorientation three to four days after the final drink indicate delirium tremens, which carries real mortality if untreated. Benzodiazepines potentiate GABA-A and treat both the delirium and the seizure risk. Haloperidol may calm hallucinations but lowers the seizure threshold and does not treat the underlying withdrawal."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old man falls on his outstretched right hand while skateboarding. He has swelling and tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox and pain with axial compression of the thumb. Wrist radiographs including a scaphoid view appear normal. Neurovascular status is intact. Which next step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply an elastic wrap for a wrist sprain and allow activity as tolerated with routine follow-up", "B": "Inject corticosteroid for suspected de Quervain tenosynovitis and reassess the thumb in two weeks", "C": "Apply a thumb spica splint and obtain MRI or repeat films in about ten days", "D": "Proceed to immediate percutaneous screw fixation because snuffbox tenderness confirms a displaced scaphoid waist fracture", "E": "Order wrist bone densitometry to look for osteoporotic fragility as the explanation for the pain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply a thumb spica splint and obtain MRI or repeat films in about ten days): Snuffbox tenderness after a fall on an outstretched hand suggests a scaphoid fracture even when early radiographs are normal, since fractures are often occult. Empiric thumb spica immobilization protects against nonunion and avascular necrosis while MRI or repeat radiographs at 10 to 14 days confirm the injury. Surgery needs a confirmed displaced fracture."}, {"id": 599, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension presents with orthopnea and ankle swelling. Echocardiography shows a thickened left ventricle and a markedly dilated left atrium. Her serum B-type natriuretic peptide level is elevated. Which stimulus most directly triggers release of this peptide in her condition?", "options": {"A": "Hypoxia sensed by peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the renal cortex and medulla", "B": "Angiotensin II binding in the adrenal cortex after falling renal perfusion pressure", "C": "Beta one receptor stimulation of juxtaglomerular cells by increased sympathetic nerve activity", "D": "Stretch of ventricular myocytes from raised wall tension and elevated filling pressure", "E": "Hyperosmolarity sensed by hypothalamic osmoreceptors in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stretch of ventricular myocytes from raised wall tension and elevated filling pressure): B-type natriuretic peptide is secreted by ventricular myocytes when wall stretch from pressure or volume overload rises, and it promotes natriuresis and vasodilation. The adrenal, juxtaglomerular, hypothalamic, and renal fibroblast stimuli each drive a different hormone and do not release this peptide."}, {"id": 600, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 20-year-old college student has had two weeks of low-grade fever, headache, and a persistent dry cough. Auscultation is nearly normal, but chest radiograph shows patchy interstitial infiltrates worse than expected. Sputum Gram stain shows no organisms, and a mild hemolytic anemia with a positive cold agglutinin titer is found. Which antibiotic is the best treatment?", "options": {"A": "Azithromycin, a 50S ribosomal inhibitor active against an organism that lacks a peptidoglycan wall", "B": "Amoxicillin, a beta-lactam that inhibits transpeptidase and is the first-line drug for lobar pneumonia", "C": "Gentamicin, an aminoglycoside that binds the 30S ribosome and is active against aerobic gram-negative rods", "D": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, which blocks sequential steps in bacterial folate synthesis and treats Pneumocystis pneumonia", "E": "Ceftriaxone, a cephalosporin that disrupts cell wall synthesis and is standard for hospitalized community pneumonia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Azithromycin, a 50S ribosomal inhibitor active against an organism that lacks a peptidoglycan wall): This is walking pneumonia from Mycoplasma pneumoniae, which has no cell wall, so it stains poorly on Gram stain and is resistant to beta-lactams. Macrolides, doxycycline or respiratory fluoroquinolones are active, and azithromycin is the usual choice for a young adult. Cold agglutinins are IgM antibodies against the I antigen on red cells and cause a mild, usually self-limited hemolysis."}, {"id": 601, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 56-year-old man who smoked one pack daily for 25 years quit 8 years ago. He is asymptomatic, with no weight loss, cough, or family history of lung cancer, and he walks 2 miles daily. He asks which test could lower his risk of dying from lung cancer. Which screening plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Annual chest radiograph, because plain films detect early nodules and lower disease specific mortality", "B": "Low-dose chest CT every 5 years, because lung cancer progresses slowly in former smokers", "C": "Annual sputum cytology, because malignant cells shed into sputum before a nodule becomes visible", "D": "No screening, because screening applies only to people who still smoke at the time of evaluation", "E": "Annual low-dose chest CT, because his age, smoking history, and time since cessation meet screening criteria"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Annual low-dose chest CT, because his age, smoking history, and time since cessation meet screening criteria): Annual low-dose CT reduces lung cancer mortality in people aged 50 to 80 with at least a 20 pack-year history who currently smoke or quit within the past 15 years. This man meets each criterion with 25 pack-years and 8 years since quitting. Radiograph and sputum cytology do not reduce mortality, and a 5 year interval is not recommended."}, {"id": 602, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 31-year-old woman with long-standing asthma and recurrent nasal polyps develops wheezing, rhinorrhea, and facial flushing 30 minutes after taking ibuprofen for a headache. Her asthma is otherwise well controlled with an inhaled corticosteroid. Which mechanism most directly explains her reaction to the drug?", "options": {"A": "Complement activation by drug-antibody immune complexes, causing a delayed type III hypersensitivity airway reaction", "B": "Direct beta-2 receptor antagonism by the drug, removing sympathetic bronchodilation and causing airway narrowing", "C": "Cholinergic stimulation of airway smooth muscle by the drug, increasing vagal tone and mucus secretion", "D": "IgE cross-linking on mast cells by an ibuprofen metabolite, triggering immediate histamine release and bronchospasm", "E": "Cyclooxygenase blockade diverts arachidonic acid toward leukotriene synthesis, which drives bronchoconstriction and mucosal edema"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cyclooxygenase blockade diverts arachidonic acid toward leukotriene synthesis, which drives bronchoconstriction and mucosal edema): Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease is triggered by cyclooxygenase inhibition, which pushes arachidonic acid down the lipoxygenase pathway and increases cysteinyl leukotrienes. The reaction is not IgE-mediated, so it occurs on first exposure and cross-reacts among NSAIDs. A leukotriene receptor antagonist can help treat it."}, {"id": 603, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 27-year-old man reports difficulty maintaining erections during intercourse for four months, since starting a high-pressure job and becoming engaged. He reports firm erections on waking and with masturbation. He takes no medications, does not smoke, and has normal blood pressure, genital examination, and glucose. Which cause is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Arteriogenic erectile dysfunction from penile atherosclerosis causing inadequate cavernosal blood inflow during arousal", "B": "Peyronie disease with plaque formation causing penile curvature and secondary difficulty maintaining rigidity during intercourse", "C": "Psychogenic erectile dysfunction related to performance anxiety, with intact vascular, neural, and hormonal pathways", "D": "Hypogonadism with low testosterone reducing libido and the frequency of spontaneous nocturnal and morning erections", "E": "Autonomic neuropathy from early diabetes impairing parasympathetic nitric oxide release in the corpora cavernosa"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Psychogenic erectile dysfunction related to performance anxiety, with intact vascular, neural, and hormonal pathways): Preserved morning and masturbatory erections show that the vascular, neural, and hormonal pathways are working, so situational loss of erections in a young man under stress is psychogenic. Organic causes usually reduce all erections, and hypogonadism would also lower libido and morning erections."}, {"id": 604, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 41-year-old man develops a limp two days after an intramuscular injection into the upper inner part of his right buttock. When he stands on his right leg alone, his left pelvis drops. He has normal sensation in the thigh and leg. Which nerve injury explains this finding?", "options": {"A": "Right sciatic nerve, supplying hamstrings and the leg muscles below the knee", "B": "Right inferior gluteal nerve, supplying gluteus maximus and thigh extension on the stance side", "C": "Right superior gluteal nerve, supplying gluteus medius and minimus on the stance side", "D": "Right obturator nerve, supplying hip adductors in the medial thigh on the stance side", "E": "Left superior gluteal nerve, supplying gluteus medius and minimus on the swing side"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right superior gluteal nerve, supplying gluteus medius and minimus on the stance side): The pelvis drops on the side opposite the weak abductors when standing on one leg, so a dropped left pelvis while standing on the right means right gluteus medius and minimus are weak. Their supply is the superior gluteal nerve, which is endangered by injections placed medial to the upper outer quadrant."}, {"id": 605, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 3-year-old boy recently weaned from breast milk to a diet of mostly cassava porridge is brought in with swollen legs, irritability, and thin, sparse hair. He has a protuberant abdomen with an enlarged liver edge. The serum panel shown was obtained. Which mechanism best explains his hepatomegaly?", "options": {"A": "Excess glycogen storage from deficient debranching enzyme activity within hepatocytes during fasting", "B": "Lysosomal accumulation of sphingolipid from deficient hydrolase activity in Kupffer cells and hepatocytes", "C": "Extramedullary hematopoiesis driven by chronic hemolysis and ineffective erythropoiesis in the liver", "D": "Reduced apolipoprotein synthesis impairing export of triglycerides from hepatocytes as lipoprotein particles", "E": "Passive venous congestion from right heart failure transmitted back to the hepatic sinusoids"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced apolipoprotein synthesis impairing export of triglycerides from hepatocytes as lipoprotein particles): In kwashiorkor, dietary protein is inadequate relative to calories, so the liver cannot make enough apolipoproteins to package and export triglycerides, producing a fatty, enlarged liver. The low albumin on the panel explains the edema through reduced oncotic pressure. Heart failure or glycogen storage disease would not be expected with this diet and laboratory profile.", "image": "question_images/q_605.png"}, {"id": 606, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 27-year-old woman presents to clinic with fatigue, a facial rash that worsens after sun exposure, painless oral ulcers, and symmetric joint pain. Antinuclear antibody is strongly positive. Her physician orders a confirmatory antibody known to be highly specific for her likely diagnosis, though present in only a minority of patients. Which antibody was ordered?", "options": {"A": "Anti-centromere antibody, directed against kinetochore proteins and typical of limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis", "B": "Anti-topoisomerase I antibody, directed against a DNA unwinding enzyme and typical of diffuse systemic sclerosis", "C": "Anti-histone antibody, characteristic of drug induced lupus after exposure to hydralazine or procainamide", "D": "Anti-Smith antibody, directed against core proteins of small nuclear ribonucleoprotein particles", "E": "Anti-Jo-1 antibody, directed against histidyl tRNA synthetase in the antisynthetase syndrome"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anti-Smith antibody, directed against core proteins of small nuclear ribonucleoprotein particles): Anti-Smith antibodies are highly specific for systemic lupus erythematosus even though only about a quarter of patients have them. Anti-histone antibodies point to drug induced lupus, which this patient with a malar rash and oral ulcers without a culprit drug does not suggest. The scleroderma and antisynthetase antibodies belong to different diseases."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 29-year-old woman is unaffected but her brother has cystic fibrosis, an autosomal recessive disease. Her partner has no family history and belongs to a population in which one in 25 people is a carrier. Neither has been tested. Approximately what is the risk that their first child will be affected?", "options": {"A": "1 in 150, from two thirds carrier probability times partner carrier frequency times one fourth", "B": "1 in 300, from one third carrier probability times partner carrier frequency times one fourth", "C": "1 in 75, from two thirds carrier probability times partner carrier frequency times one half", "D": "1 in 100, from certain maternal carrier status times partner carrier frequency times one fourth", "E": "1 in 50, from partner carrier frequency times one half for transmission of the mutant allele"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1 in 150, from two thirds carrier probability times partner carrier frequency times one fourth): An unaffected sibling of a person with a recessive disease has a two thirds chance of being a carrier, since the affected genotype is excluded. The risk is two thirds times one in 25 times one fourth, which is one in 150. Treating her as a certain carrier overstates the risk to one in 100."}, {"id": 608, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 6-year-old boy has palpable purpura over his buttocks and lower legs, colicky abdominal pain and swollen knees. Urinalysis shows red blood cells. He had an upper respiratory infection ten days ago, and his platelet count is normal. Which immune finding underlies the vascular injury in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies directed against proteinase 3 in small vessel walls", "B": "Antibodies against glomerular basement membrane collagen producing linear immunofluorescence in kidney and lung", "C": "Necrotizing inflammation of medium arteries with fibrinoid change and aneurysms, associated with hepatitis B", "D": "Deposition of IgA containing immune complexes in small vessel walls of skin, gut and kidney", "E": "Cryoglobulin immune complexes composed of mixed immunoglobulins precipitating in cool small vessels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Deposition of IgA containing immune complexes in small vessel walls of skin, gut and kidney): The tetrad of palpable purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain and hematuria after an infection describes IgA vasculitis, caused by IgA immune complex deposition in small vessels. Platelets are normal because the purpura reflects vessel inflammation rather than thrombocytopenia."}, {"id": 609, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 50-year-old man with chronic alcohol use has chronic epigastric pain radiating to the back and foul smelling greasy stools that float. He has lost 8 kg. Fecal elastase is low, and a D-xylose absorption test is normal. Fecal fat is markedly increased. Which mechanism best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency reducing lipase delivery while the intestinal mucosa absorbs normally", "B": "Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth deconjugating bile acids and impairing micelle formation", "C": "Tropheryma whipplei infiltration of the lamina propria obstructing nutrient transport into lacteals", "D": "Celiac disease with villous atrophy reducing absorption of sugars and fats across the mucosa", "E": "Terminal ileal disease reducing bile acid reabsorption and depleting the circulating bile pool"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency reducing lipase delivery while the intestinal mucosa absorbs normally): Low fecal elastase with steatorrhea in chronic alcohol use points to pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, and a normal D-xylose test shows the mucosa absorbs the pentose normally. Celiac disease and Whipple disease damage the mucosa and make D-xylose absorption abnormal."}, {"id": 610, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 67-year-old man has weak urinary stream, hesitancy, and nocturia three times nightly. Prostate is symmetrically enlarged, smooth, and firm on rectal examination, and PSA is 3.1 ng/mL. He wants fast symptom relief before a long trip next week. He has well controlled hypertension and no history of syncope. Which drug effect best fits this goal?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of 5-alpha reductase lowering dihydrotestosterone and shrinking the gland over several months", "B": "Gonadotropin releasing hormone agonism suppressing testosterone production and reducing prostate volume after an initial flare", "C": "Cholinergic agonism increasing detrusor contraction to improve bladder emptying and reduce residual volume", "D": "Antimuscarinic blockade reducing detrusor contractions and urgency through inhibition of bladder M3 receptors", "E": "Alpha-1A receptor blockade relaxing prostatic smooth muscle and the bladder neck within days"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Alpha-1A receptor blockade relaxing prostatic smooth muscle and the bladder neck within days): Smooth benign enlargement with obstructive symptoms is benign prostatic hyperplasia, and alpha-1 blockers such as tamsulosin relax the prostate and bladder neck within days, matching his timeline. A 5-alpha reductase inhibitor shrinks the gland but takes months. Antimuscarinic drugs risk retention in obstruction."}, {"id": 611, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 67-year-old retired teacher newly eligible for federal insurance asks which part covers physician office visits, outpatient services and preventive care, and requires a monthly premium. She worked 40 years and paid payroll taxes. Which Medicare component covers these outpatient services?", "options": {"A": "Part B, supplementary medical insurance funded by premiums and general revenue, covering clinic care and screening", "B": "Medicaid, a joint federal and state program covering low-income people including office visits and outpatient services", "C": "Part D, outpatient prescription drug coverage delivered through private plans and funded by premiums and subsidies", "D": "Part A, hospital insurance funded by payroll taxes covering inpatient stays and skilled nursing after admission", "E": "Medigap, supplemental private policies that fill deductibles and copayments left by original Medicare coverage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Part B, supplementary medical insurance funded by premiums and general revenue, covering clinic care and screening): Part B is the supplementary medical insurance that covers physician visits, outpatient care and many preventive services, with a monthly premium. Part A funds inpatient care and is premium free for those with enough work credits. Medigap only reimburses cost sharing and Part D covers drugs."}, {"id": 612, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A researcher wants to study whether a rare industrial solvent exposure, found in only 200 factory workers in a region, increases the risk of a liver tumor. Both exposed workers and a similar group of unexposed workers are available with complete employment records. Which design is most efficient?", "options": {"A": "Randomized trial, because assigning the solvent exposure randomly would remove confounding between workers", "B": "Cross-sectional survey, because exposure and tumor status can both be measured on one occasion", "C": "Case-control study, because the tumor is uncommon and affected patients are easily gathered", "D": "Cohort study, because few workers carry this solvent history and can be enrolled directly", "E": "Ecological study, because regional solvent use and tumor rates are available from existing records"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cohort study, because few workers carry this solvent history and can be enrolled directly): When the exposure is rare, a cohort study that deliberately enrolls exposed workers and a comparison group is efficient, because a case-control sample would contain very few exposed cases. The rarity of the tumor tempts toward a case-control design, but it is the rarity of the solvent history that decides. Randomly assigning a harmful solvent would be unethical."}, {"id": 613, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 36-year-old man is found confused after a house fire in a closed room. He reports headache and nausea. Pulse oximetry shows 99% on room air, respiratory rate is 22/min, and arterial PO2 is normal at 95 mm Hg. Lactate is elevated. Which mechanism explains the normal oximetry reading despite his tissue hypoxia?", "options": {"A": "Dissolved oxygen in plasma contributes most of the signal that a pulse oximeter detects", "B": "Soot on the fingertip scatters light and drives the displayed value toward the maximum", "C": "Methemoglobin absorbs light at both wavelengths equally, which drives the oximeter reading toward ninety percent", "D": "Cyanide binds cytochrome oxidase and raises venous oxygen saturation, which the probe misreads as arterial", "E": "Carboxyhemoglobin absorbs light similarly to oxyhemoglobin, so the two-wavelength device overestimates true oxygen saturation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Carboxyhemoglobin absorbs light similarly to oxyhemoglobin, so the two-wavelength device overestimates true oxygen saturation): Standard pulse oximeters use only two wavelengths and cannot tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin, so saturation looks normal while real oxygen-carrying capacity is reduced. The PO2 is also normal because it reflects dissolved oxygen, not hemoglobin saturation. Co-oximetry on blood confirms the diagnosis and the patient needs 100% oxygen. Cyanide may also cause lactic acidosis in fires, but it does not change the oximeter reading."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 6-month-old boy presents with a protuberant abdomen, doll-like facies and a seizure after a long interval between feedings. Laboratory studies show glucose 34 mg/dL with elevated lactate, uric acid and triglycerides. The liver and kidneys are markedly enlarged, and an injection of glucagon fails to raise his blood glucose. Which enzyme is deficient?", "options": {"A": "Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency blocking the final step of both gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis in liver", "B": "Acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency causing lysosomal glycogen accumulation with cardiomegaly and hypotonia in infants", "C": "Hepatic glycogen phosphorylase deficiency impairing glycogenolysis while leaving gluconeogenesis and lactate handling intact", "D": "Debranching enzyme deficiency leaving abnormal limit dextrin and permitting partial glycogen breakdown in liver", "E": "Branching enzyme deficiency producing abnormal long unbranched glycogen and progressive hepatic cirrhosis in early childhood"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency blocking the final step of both gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis in liver): Without glucose-6-phosphatase, glucose-6-phosphate is trapped in hepatocytes, so neither glycogenolysis nor gluconeogenesis can release free glucose, which explains fasting hypoglycemia unresponsive to glucagon. The trapped intermediate is shunted into glycolysis and the pentose pathway, raising lactate, uric acid and triglycerides. Hers disease also causes hypoglycemia but lacks the marked lactic acidosis and hyperuricemia."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 40-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder stable on long-term lithium reports excessive thirst and urinating large volumes of dilute urine. Fluid restriction does not concentrate her urine, and desmopressin has little effect. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Excess free water intake from primary polydipsia suppressing physiologic antidiuretic hormone release", "B": "Decreased pituitary secretion of antidiuretic hormone from damage to the supraoptic nucleus", "C": "Osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia causing large urine volumes with glucosuria and solute loss", "D": "Lithium induced hypercalcemia from parathyroid hyperplasia impairing urinary concentrating ability in the medulla", "E": "Reduced responsiveness of collecting duct cells to antidiuretic hormone, impairing aquaporin 2 insertion"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Reduced responsiveness of collecting duct cells to antidiuretic hormone, impairing aquaporin 2 insertion): Lithium enters collecting duct cells and interferes with the vasopressin signaling that inserts aquaporin 2, causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus that does not respond to desmopressin. Central diabetes insipidus would correct with desmopressin, and lithium related hypercalcemia is a possible contributor but not the main mechanism."}, {"id": 616, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 30-year-old man brought to the emergency department after a party has a blood ethanol concentration of 200 mg/dL. Serial measurements decline by a roughly constant 15 mg/dL every hour. The emergency physician wants to predict when the level will reach zero and reviews elimination kinetics. Which process best describes ethanol elimination at this concentration?", "options": {"A": "First-order elimination because a fixed fraction of the drug is removed each hour", "B": "Flow-limited hepatic extraction because the liver clears the drug as rapidly as it arrives", "C": "Biexponential redistribution because the drug moves from plasma into peripheral tissue compartments", "D": "Renal tubular secretion because transporters in the proximal tubule eliminate the drug", "E": "Zero-order elimination because enzyme saturation fixes the amount removed per unit time"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Zero-order elimination because enzyme saturation fixes the amount removed per unit time): Alcohol dehydrogenase becomes saturated at ordinary intoxicating concentrations, so a constant amount rather than a constant fraction is metabolized per hour. This gives a straight line decline on a linear plot. A first order process would show a falling hourly amount as the concentration drops."}, {"id": 617, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A newborn has severe respiratory distress, a scaphoid abdomen, and bowel sounds heard over the left hemithorax immediately after delivery. The heart sounds are displaced to the right. Prenatal ultrasound was not performed. The mother had an uncomplicated pregnancy and takes no medications. Failure of which embryologic process best explains the infant's lung findings?", "options": {"A": "Development of the laryngotracheal diverticulum, whose duplication produces a bronchogenic cyst of the mediastinum", "B": "Maturation of type II pneumocytes, whose failure leaves the alveoli stiff and poorly aerated", "C": "Closure of the pleuroperitoneal canal, whose failure lets viscera herniate and restrict lung growth", "D": "Canalization of the lung buds, whose failure produces bronchial atresia and a hyperlucent segment", "E": "Separation of the esophagus from the trachea by the tracheoesophageal septum, with secondary lung compression"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Closure of the pleuroperitoneal canal, whose failure lets viscera herniate and restrict lung growth): A scaphoid abdomen with bowel sounds in the chest and a shifted mediastinum indicates congenital diaphragmatic hernia, usually through the left posterolateral foramen of Bochdalek. Failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to close lets abdominal viscera enter the thorax during lung development, causing pulmonary hypoplasia. A tracheoesophageal fistula would instead cause feeding-related choking with a gas-filled abdomen."}, {"id": 618, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 68-year-old man who smoked 15 cigarettes daily for 20 years and quit at age 50 attends a Medicare wellness visit. He has no abdominal pain, takes a statin for hyperlipidemia, and has a sister with breast cancer. Which screening test, based specifically on his smoking history, is recommended by current USPSTF guidance?", "options": {"A": "Annual low-dose chest CT, because his smoking history places him in the high-risk lung cancer group", "B": "Coronary artery calcium scoring, because statin therapy and smoking history justify routine imaging of asymptomatic adults", "C": "A single abdominal aortic ultrasound, because ever-smokers this age face higher aneurysm rupture risk", "D": "Resting electrocardiography annually, because smoking history indicates routine screening for silent coronary disease", "E": "Carotid duplex ultrasonography every year, because smoking and hyperlipidemia raise the risk of asymptomatic carotid stenosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A single abdominal aortic ultrasound, because ever-smokers this age face higher aneurysm rupture risk): Men aged 65 to 75 who have ever smoked are offered one-time ultrasound screening for abdominal aortic aneurysm. He has about 15 pack-years and quit 18 years ago, so he does not meet lung cancer screening criteria. Screening carotid ultrasound, calcium scoring and resting ECG are not recommended in asymptomatic adults like him."}, {"id": 619, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 31-year-old woman has recurrent throbbing left-sided headaches lasting eight hours with nausea and photophobia, each preceded by twenty minutes of shimmering zigzag visual disturbance. During an attack her physician prescribes sumatriptan, and the pain eases within an hour. Which pharmacologic action best explains how this drug relieves the headache?", "options": {"A": "Antagonism of serotonin 2A receptors reducing cranial vascular smooth muscle contraction and platelet aggregation", "B": "Agonism at serotonin 1B and 1D receptors causing cranial vasoconstriction and reduced neuropeptide release", "C": "Antagonism of dopamine D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone relieving the nausea component", "D": "Inhibition of cyclooxygenase reducing prostaglandin synthesis in meningeal pain fibers and vessels", "E": "Blockade of beta adrenergic receptors reducing cranial vessel dilation through sympathetic withdrawal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Agonism at serotonin 1B and 1D receptors causing cranial vasoconstriction and reduced neuropeptide release): Triptans are agonists at 1B and 1D serotonin receptors, constricting dilated cranial vessels and inhibiting release of calcitonin gene-related peptide and other neuropeptides from trigeminal afferents. Beta blockers are used for prevention, not acute relief. Dopamine blockers treat the nausea but not the headache mechanism itself."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 66-year-old retired farmer has rough, scaly, erythematous patches on his scalp and dorsal hands. A biopsy of one patch shows atypical keratinocytes confined to the lower epidermis with parakeratosis and no dermal invasion. Which statement best describes the natural history of this lesion?", "options": {"A": "It is an early melanoma that frequently metastasizes to regional lymph nodes within months", "B": "It commonly transforms into basal cell carcinoma through loss of hedgehog pathway inhibition", "C": "It carries a small risk of progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma over time", "D": "It is a benign lesion that regresses once cumulative sun exposure stops", "E": "It typically evolves into keratoacanthoma that involutes spontaneously leaving a depressed scar"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It carries a small risk of progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma over time): Actinic keratosis is a premalignant keratinocyte dysplasia caused by cumulative ultraviolet damage. Most lesions do not progress, but a small proportion become invasive squamous cell carcinoma. It does not arise from melanocytes and is not a precursor of basal cell carcinoma."}, {"id": 621, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A new anticonvulsant has a median effective dose of 20 mg and a median toxic dose of 100 mg. A comparison drug has a median effective dose of 10 mg and a median toxic dose of 30 mg. A clinician treating a 12-year-old with epilepsy wants the wider margin of safety. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "The comparison drug has the wider margin because its median effective dose is lower", "B": "The comparison drug is safer because its toxic dose is closer to its effective dose", "C": "Both drugs have equal safety margins because each toxic dose is greater than its effective dose", "D": "The newer agent has a therapeutic index of 5 compared with 3 for the comparator", "E": "The new drug has an index of 0.2, narrower than the comparator's value of 0.33"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The newer agent has a therapeutic index of 5 compared with 3 for the comparator): The therapeutic index is the median toxic dose divided by the median effective dose: 100 divided by 20 gives 5, and 30 divided by 10 gives 3. A higher value means a wider margin between the dose that works and the dose that harms. A lower effective dose reflects potency and says nothing about safety."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 3-week-old boy is diagnosed with classic phenylketonuria after newborn screening, and his parents are counseled about a phenylalanine-restricted diet. His grandmother notes a musty body odor, and he is feeding well with a normal examination. The dietitian explains that a normally nonessential amino acid must now be supplemented. Which amino acid and reason are correct?", "options": {"A": "Cysteine, because its synthesis from methionine requires the same enzymatic step that is blocked here", "B": "Leucine, because branched-chain amino acid transport is competitively inhibited by elevated blood phenylalanine", "C": "Tyrosine, because its usual synthesis from phenylalanine is blocked by the defective hydroxylase enzyme", "D": "Tryptophan, because the same enzyme depends on tetrahydrobiopterin needed for serotonin synthesis", "E": "Arginine, because urea cycle intermediates are depleted by toxic phenylalanine accumulation in hepatic mitochondria"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tyrosine, because its usual synthesis from phenylalanine is blocked by the defective hydroxylase enzyme): Phenylalanine hydroxylase converts phenylalanine to tyrosine, so when it is deficient tyrosine cannot be made and becomes conditionally essential. Tryptophan is tempting because tetrahydrobiopterin is a shared cofactor, but tryptophan is already an essential dietary amino acid and its supply is not affected by this block."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 50-year-old woman takes sertraline for depression. After starting tramadol for back pain, she develops agitation, diarrhea, sweating, shivering, and a temperature of 38.8 C. Examination shows inducible ankle clonus and hyperreflexia. Benzodiazepines are given, but symptoms persist. Which additional agent is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Bromocriptine, a dopamine agonist that reverses central dopamine blockade and rigidity", "B": "Physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor that reverses central anticholinergic toxicity and delirium", "C": "Acetaminophen, an antipyretic that lowers the hypothalamic set point during fever", "D": "Cyproheptadine, a serotonin 5-HT2A receptor antagonist given orally or by nasogastric tube", "E": "Dantrolene, a ryanodine receptor inhibitor that reduces skeletal muscle heat production"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cyproheptadine, a serotonin 5-HT2A receptor antagonist given orally or by nasogastric tube): Hyperreflexia, inducible clonus, diarrhea, and fever after adding tramadol to an SSRI indicate serotonin syndrome. When benzodiazepines are not enough, the serotonin antagonist cyproheptadine is given, and antipyretics do not work because the heat comes from muscle hyperactivity rather than a raised set point."}, {"id": 624, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 40-year-old man has episodic headaches, sweating, and palpitations with blood pressure up to 210/120 mm Hg during spells. Plasma free metanephrines are markedly elevated and CT shows a 4 cm right adrenal mass. He is scheduled for adrenalectomy in two weeks. What is the most appropriate preoperative medical management?", "options": {"A": "Begin high dose spironolactone to correct mineralocorticoid excess before resection of the adrenal mass", "B": "Restrict dietary sodium and start a thiazide to control the pressure before the planned operation", "C": "Start propranolol first to control tachycardia and add an alpha blocker at a later visit", "D": "Start phenoxybenzamine for alpha blockade and add a beta blocker only after adequate alpha blockade", "E": "Proceed to surgery now with intraoperative nitroprusside as the only preparation for pressure swings"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Start phenoxybenzamine for alpha blockade and add a beta blocker only after adequate alpha blockade): Elevated metanephrines with an adrenal mass indicate pheochromocytoma, and the first step is alpha blockade, typically phenoxybenzamine, to prevent hypertensive crisis. A beta blocker is added only afterward, because beta blockade alone leaves unopposed alpha stimulation. Patients also need liberal sodium intake, not restriction or diuretics."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old volleyball player fell on her outstretched right hand with the forearm pronated and now has lateral elbow pain. The radiograph is normal. Palpation shows the radial head sitting posteriorly relative to the capitulum. Which forearm motion is restricted, and why?", "options": {"A": "Supination, since the head cannot translate forward over the humeral capitulum", "B": "Pronation, since the head cannot translate forward over the humeral capitulum", "C": "Pronation, since the head cannot translate backward over the humeral capitulum", "D": "Supination, since the head cannot translate backward over the humeral capitulum", "E": "Elbow flexion, since the olecranon cannot enter the olecranon fossa of the humerus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supination, since the head cannot translate forward over the humeral capitulum): A radial head sitting posteriorly cannot glide forward, and supination moves it anteriorly, so supination is restricted. Pronation moves the head backward and stays free. Elbow flexion involves the humeroulnar joint and is not the motion limited by this finding."}, {"id": 626, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 35-year-old man has severe right flank pain radiating to the groin with hematuria; CT confirms a 5 mm stone in the mid ureter without bladder involvement. He has no voiding symptoms. Osteopathic examination during the acute episode finds warm, edematous, boggy paraspinal tissue on the right. At which levels is this reflex most likely to be palpated?", "options": {"A": "T8 to T10 paraspinal region, corresponding to sympathetic supply of the small intestine and pancreas", "B": "T12 to L2 paraspinal region, corresponding to sympathetic supply of the bladder and urethral neck", "C": "L1 to L2 paraspinal region, corresponding to sympathetic supply of the distal colon and rectum", "D": "T5 to T9 paraspinal region, corresponding to sympathetic supply of the stomach and upper gut", "E": "T10 to L1 paraspinal region, corresponding to sympathetic supply of the kidney and the ureter"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (T10 to L1 paraspinal region, corresponding to sympathetic supply of the kidney and the ureter): The kidney and ureter have sympathetic viscerosomatic reflexes at about T10 to L1, and acute disease produces warm, edematous, boggy tissue there. The bladder reflex sits slightly lower at T12 to L2, which is tempting because the pain radiates toward the groin. The absence of voiding symptoms points away from the bladder levels."}, {"id": 627, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 66-year-old man dies of ventricular fibrillation after an anterior wall myocardial infarction managed without reperfusion. He had hypertension and smoked for decades. At autopsy, a section of the infarcted left ventricular wall is examined and shown. Which description best identifies the stage of infarct healing in this section?", "options": {"A": "Dense collagenous scar with sparse fibroblasts, more than two months old", "B": "Early necrosis with wavy fibers and hypereosinophilia, about four to twelve hours old", "C": "Macrophage infiltrate removing necrotic debris at the border, about seven to ten days old", "D": "Acute inflammation with neutrophil infiltrate and coagulative necrosis, about two to four days old", "E": "Granulation tissue with macrophages and neovascularization, about one to two weeks old"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute inflammation with neutrophil infiltrate and coagulative necrosis, about two to four days old): Neutrophils infiltrating necrotic myocytes with preserved ghost outlines mark acute inflammation, peaking around days 2 to 4 after infarction. Wavy fibers and hypereosinophilia occur in the first hours, whereas macrophages and granulation tissue appear after the first week.", "image": "question_images/q_627.png"}, {"id": 628, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 4-year-old girl meeting criteria for Kawasaki disease is on day 6 of fever with conjunctival injection, a polymorphous rash, and cervical adenopathy. Echocardiography shows normal coronary arteries so far. Her pediatrician wants treatment that lowers the risk of coronary artery damage. Which regimen is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous immune globulin given as a single infusion together with aspirin during the acute illness", "B": "Ibuprofen for fever control with no antiplatelet agent, to avoid the risk of Reye syndrome", "C": "Systemic corticosteroids alone, tapered over several weeks to suppress the vasculitic inflammatory response", "D": "Therapeutic enoxaparin and warfarin starting immediately to prevent coronary thrombosis before aneurysms form", "E": "Oral penicillin for ten days to treat presumed group A streptococcal infection and prevent sequelae"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous immune globulin given as a single infusion together with aspirin during the acute illness): Intravenous immune globulin plus aspirin given in the acute phase, ideally within ten days of fever onset, reduces the incidence of coronary aneurysms, and the diagnosis here is clinical. Penicillin treats streptococcal disease, which can mimic the rash and adenopathy but does not alter the vasculitis."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 63-year-old man with emphysema has a rigid, barrel-shaped chest with reduced rib excursion. A student asks why the physician treats rib motion restriction to improve ventilation instead of only prescribing medications. Which osteopathic principle best explains treating the chest wall to influence lung function?", "options": {"A": "Rational treatment is based on these principles, so manipulation replaces drug therapy for emphysema", "B": "Health maintenance depends on the nervous system alone, so rib treatment acts only through spinal reflexes", "C": "The body is capable of self-regulation, so emphysema resolves once rib motion is restored", "D": "The body is a unit, so lung disease requires treatment of every organ system", "E": "Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so rib cage mechanics shape respiratory performance"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Structure and function are reciprocally interrelated, so rib cage mechanics shape respiratory performance): The tenet that structure and function are reciprocally interrelated links a restricted, rigid rib cage to impaired ventilatory mechanics, so improving rib motion can improve function. The unity and self-regulation tenets are true statements but do not specifically justify treating the chest wall, and osteopathic treatment complements medical therapy rather than replacing it."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old man presents to the emergency department 3 days after lifting a heavy box with severe low back pain radiating down both legs. He has new urinary retention and numbness over the perineum. Examination shows decreased anal sphincter tone and bilateral ankle weakness. Which structure is being compressed?", "options": {"A": "Lumbar plexus within the psoas major muscle, from retroperitoneal hematoma compression", "B": "Lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus medullaris, bundled within the lower dural sac", "C": "Exiting L4 nerve root within the neural foramen on one side, producing unilateral radiculopathy", "D": "Anterior spinal artery supplying the ventral spinal cord, causing infarction of anterior horns", "E": "Sciatic nerve trunk within the greater sciatic foramen beneath the piriformis muscle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus medullaris, bundled within the lower dural sac): Bilateral leg symptoms with urinary retention, saddle anesthesia, and reduced sphincter tone are classic for cauda equina syndrome from a central disc herniation compressing the lower lumbosacral roots. This is a surgical emergency. Unilateral root, peripheral nerve, and plexus lesions do not explain the sphincter and perineal findings."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 64-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has a persistent white plaque on the lateral border of the tongue for four months. It cannot be scraped off with a tongue depressor, is mildly firm, and causes no pain. He uses no inhaled steroids or antibiotics. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe oral nystatin suspension for presumed candidiasis and reassess the plaque after two weeks of therapy", "B": "Obtain an incisional biopsy including the firm area to assess for epithelial dysplasia or invasive carcinoma", "C": "Reassure the patient and recheck in six months because most white plaques are benign keratosis", "D": "Order HIV testing and treat presumed oral hairy leukoplakia with antiviral therapy against Epstein Barr virus", "E": "Start topical corticosteroid therapy for presumed oral lichen planus and review the lesion in one month"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Obtain an incisional biopsy including the firm area to assess for epithelial dysplasia or invasive carcinoma): A persistent, nonscrapable white plaque on the lateral tongue in a heavy smoker is leukoplakia until proven otherwise, and the firm area raises concern for dysplasia or squamous cell carcinoma. Tissue diagnosis by biopsy is required. Candidiasis scrapes off, and watchful waiting would delay diagnosis of a premalignant or malignant lesion."}, {"id": 632, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man who spent decades cleaning polymerization reactors at a plastics plant presents with abdominal pain and weight loss. CT shows multiple vascular hepatic masses, and biopsy shows malignant endothelial cells forming irregular channels. Which occupational exposure is most likely responsible for this tumor?", "options": {"A": "Aromatic amine dye intermediates, causing urothelial carcinoma after prolonged skin and inhalational contact", "B": "Benzene solvent vapors, causing acute myeloid leukemia through toxic injury to bone marrow progenitors", "C": "Chlorinated vinyl compound exposure linked to malignant transformation of hepatic sinusoidal endothelium", "D": "Inhaled mineral fibers from insulation work, causing pleural malignancy after a very long latency period", "E": "Fungal grain toxin contamination, causing hepatocellular carcinoma through mutation of the p53 gene"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chlorinated vinyl compound exposure linked to malignant transformation of hepatic sinusoidal endothelium): Hepatic angiosarcoma is the classic tumor linked to vinyl chloride exposure in polymer plant workers. Aflatoxin also targets the liver but causes hepatocellular carcinoma from hepatocytes, whereas this biopsy shows malignant endothelial cells."}, {"id": 633, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 67-year-old man with a long smoking history has a three-week history of mild fatigue. He is alert and euvolemic. Serum sodium is 124 mEq/L, serum osmolality is 258 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality is 480 mOsm/kg, and urine sodium is 52 mEq/L. Thyroid and cortisol studies are normal. Which initial management is best?", "options": {"A": "Fluid restriction while the cause is evaluated, because retained free water is diluting the sodium", "B": "Intranasal desmopressin to enhance water reabsorption in the collecting duct and stabilize the serum sodium", "C": "Demeclocycline started immediately to antagonize vasopressin at the collecting duct and raise the serum sodium", "D": "Three percent hypertonic saline bolus to raise the serum sodium and prevent seizures from cerebral edema", "E": "Isotonic saline infusion to restore volume and correct the serum sodium through renal water excretion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fluid restriction while the cause is evaluated, because retained free water is diluting the sodium): A low serum osmolality with inappropriately concentrated urine, high urine sodium and euvolemia with normal thyroid and adrenal function indicates SIADH, here likely from small cell lung cancer. Mild chronic hyponatremia without neurologic symptoms is treated with fluid restriction. Isotonic saline can worsen the sodium because the excess solute is excreted in a small urine volume while free water is retained."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 14-year-old wrestler has an annular, scaly plaque with a raised erythematous border and central clearing on his shoulder. A potassium hydroxide preparation shows branching septate hyphae. He has no other lesions. Examination of his scalp is normal. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Topical hydrocortisone cream applied twice daily to calm the inflammatory border of the plaque", "B": "Oral ketoconazole daily for six weeks to treat the infection systemically through the epidermis", "C": "Oral griseofulvin daily for twelve weeks, which accumulates in keratin and prevents hyphal growth", "D": "Topical terbinafine applied daily to the lesion and a margin of surrounding normal skin", "E": "Topical nystatin cream applied twice daily, which binds ergosterol in yeast cell membranes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Topical terbinafine applied daily to the lesion and a margin of surrounding normal skin): Localized tinea corporis with confirmed hyphae responds to a topical allylamine or azole applied beyond the border. Nystatin is active against yeast but not dermatophytes, and steroid alone worsens the infection; oral therapy is reserved for extensive, scalp or nail disease."}, {"id": 635, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 27-year-old man has acute low back pain after lifting a box. A discrete tender point is found over the posterior spinous process of L3, and tissue is warm and guarded. The physician chooses counterstrain to reduce proprioceptive input from the shortened muscle. Which positioning and hold best fit this posterior tender point?", "options": {"A": "Flex the trunk to the end range and hold for about ten seconds", "B": "Extend the trunk to the barrier and request a gentle isometric push for several seconds", "C": "Flex the trunk and fine-tune toward comfort until tenderness drops, then hold about ninety seconds", "D": "Extend the trunk and fine-tune toward comfort until tenderness drops, then hold about ninety seconds", "E": "Extend the trunk with a quick repetitive springing motion through the segment for several minutes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extend the trunk and fine-tune toward comfort until tenderness drops, then hold about ninety seconds): Posterior tender points are generally treated with extension, and counterstrain positions the tissue toward ease, fine-tuning until tenderness falls by most of its starting level, and holds that position about ninety seconds. Anterior points use flexion, and active contractions or springing belong to other techniques."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 45-year-old man is newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes after a routine screening panel. He has no visual symptoms. His hemoglobin A1c is 8.1 percent, blood pressure is 128/78, and he has never had an eye examination. Which approach to retinopathy screening is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Annual examination beginning once microalbuminuria appears, since retinopathy tracks closely with the onset of nephropathy", "B": "Dilated eye examination within five years of diagnosis, since retinopathy develops only after prolonged disease duration", "C": "Referral to ophthalmology only after visual acuity falls below 20/40 on a Snellen chart", "D": "Screening only after hemoglobin A1c stays above 9 percent for a full year of follow up", "E": "Dilated eye examination at diagnosis, then every one to two years if the first is normal"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Dilated eye examination at diagnosis, then every one to two years if the first is normal): Type 2 diabetes may be present for years before diagnosis, so retinopathy can already exist and a dilated examination is done at diagnosis. If it is normal and glycemia is controlled, repeat examinations can be spaced to every one to two years. The five year delay applies to type 1 diabetes, and waiting for visual symptoms misses early disease."}, {"id": 637, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A research subject undergoes lung volume testing. Tidal volume is 500 mL, inspiratory reserve volume is 3000 mL, expiratory reserve volume is 1100 mL, and residual volume is 1200 mL. The subject is a healthy 30-year-old man. Which value represents the volume remaining in his lungs at the end of a normal quiet expiration?", "options": {"A": "4600 mL, calculated as inspiratory reserve plus tidal plus expiratory reserve volumes combined", "B": "5800 mL, calculated as vital capacity plus residual volume at maximal inspiration", "C": "1700 mL, calculated as tidal volume plus residual volume at the end of expiration", "D": "3500 mL, calculated as tidal volume plus inspiratory reserve volume for inspiratory capacity", "E": "2300 mL, calculated as expiratory reserve volume plus residual volume of the lungs"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (2300 mL, calculated as expiratory reserve volume plus residual volume of the lungs): The volume left after a quiet breath out is the functional residual capacity, equal to expiratory reserve volume plus residual volume, or 1100 plus 1200 mL. Vital capacity is 4600 mL and total lung capacity is 5800 mL. Inspiratory capacity of 3500 mL describes air that can be inhaled, not air that remains."}, {"id": 638, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 3-year-old girl born at 28 weeks gestation walks on her toes with a scissoring gait. Examination shows increased tone and hyperreflexia in both legs, with normal arm strength and normal cognition. Neurologic status has not worsened since infancy. Which of the following best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Progressive spinal muscular atrophy from loss of anterior horn motor neurons in the cord", "B": "Germinal matrix hemorrhage extending into the lateral ventricles with obstruction of CSF flow", "C": "Periventricular white matter injury from hypoxic ischemia in the watershed zone of the immature brain", "D": "Tethered spinal cord with traction injury to the conus medullaris during growth", "E": "Kernicterus from bilirubin deposition in the basal ganglia causing choreoathetoid movements and deafness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Periventricular white matter injury from hypoxic ischemia in the watershed zone of the immature brain): Prematurity with nonprogressive spastic diplegia and preserved cognition points to periventricular leukomalacia, ischemic injury to the white matter fibers that carry leg motor tracts near the ventricles. Kernicterus produces athetoid movements and hearing loss, and spinal muscular atrophy would give flaccid weakness with hyporeflexia."}, {"id": 639, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 2-week-old genetically female infant has ambiguous genitalia, vomiting, and lethargy. She was born at term and the pregnancy was uncomplicated. Blood pressure is low. Serum sodium is 124 mEq/L, potassium is 6.4 mEq/L, and 17-hydroxyprogesterone is markedly elevated. Which enzyme deficiency is most likely?", "options": {"A": "3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency blocking all steroid classes with mild virilization and salt wasting", "B": "17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency blocking sex steroid synthesis with hypertension and absent virilization in females", "C": "21-hydroxylase deficiency blocking cortisol and aldosterone synthesis while diverting precursors into androgen production", "D": "11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causing deoxycorticosterone excess with hypertension and virilization of the female infant", "E": "Cholesterol side-chain cleavage deficiency, lipoid hyperplasia, with absent adrenal steroids and 46,XY sex reversal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (21-hydroxylase deficiency blocking cortisol and aldosterone synthesis while diverting precursors into androgen production): A markedly raised 17-hydroxyprogesterone with virilization and salt wasting is classic for 21-hydroxylase deficiency. Cortisol and aldosterone are low, and precursors flow to androgens. Deficiency of 11-beta-hydroxylase also virilizes but retains salt and raises blood pressure."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 5-year-old boy adopted at age 2 has short stature, microcephaly, short palpebral fissures, a smooth philtrum and a thin upper lip. His teacher reports hyperactivity and poor impulse control, and an echocardiogram shows a small ventricular septal defect. Birth records are unavailable. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Fragile X syndrome from a CGG repeat expansion with long face, large ears and macroorchidism", "B": "Williams syndrome from a chromosome 7q11.23 deletion with elfin facies and supravalvular aortic stenosis", "C": "Down syndrome from trisomy 21 with upslanting fissures, flat nasal bridge and atrioventricular septal defect", "D": "Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder from prenatal ethanol exposure with characteristic facial and growth findings", "E": "Fetal hydantoin syndrome from phenytoin exposure with hypoplastic nails, distal phalanges and coarse facies"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder from prenatal ethanol exposure with characteristic facial and growth findings): Short palpebral fissures, a smooth philtrum, a thin upper lip, growth restriction, microcephaly and behavioral problems define the fetal alcohol spectrum, and a septal defect fits the associated cardiac anomalies. Ethanol is a leading preventable cause of intellectual disability and has no known safe threshold in pregnancy. Williams and Fragile X have different facial features and genetic mechanisms."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 52-year-old man with chronic alcohol use is brought to the emergency department with confusion, horizontal nystagmus, and a wide-based gait. He has eaten poorly for weeks. An intravenous glucose infusion is started before other treatment, and his symptoms worsen. Which enzymatic function is most directly impaired in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Pyruvate carboxylase requiring biotin for gluconeogenic formation of oxaloacetate in liver mitochondria", "B": "Glutathione reductase requiring a flavin cofactor for regeneration of reduced glutathione in red cells", "C": "Glycogen phosphorylase requiring pyridoxal phosphate for release of glucose from stored liver glycogen", "D": "Pentose phosphate pathway transferase requiring a thiamine derived cofactor for carbon shuffling", "E": "Methionine synthase requiring cobalamin for remethylation of homocysteine to methionine in cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pentose phosphate pathway transferase requiring a thiamine derived cofactor for carbon shuffling): Confusion, nystagmus, and ataxia in a malnourished drinker indicate Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency, and a glucose load increases thiamine demand. Transketolase of the pentose phosphate pathway needs thiamine pyrophosphate, as do pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 47-year-old woman with a body mass index of 33 reports heel pain that is sharpest with her first steps each morning and eases after several minutes. She is tender at the medial plantar heel, and passive toe extension reproduces the pain. Tinel sign at the tarsal tunnel is negative. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Tarsal tunnel syndrome, entrapment of the tibial nerve causing burning paresthesias that worsen with prolonged standing", "B": "Plantar fasciitis, a degenerative overuse injury of the fibrous band attaching at the calcaneal tuberosity", "C": "Insertional Achilles tendinopathy, degeneration at the posterior calcaneus with pain on resisted plantar flexion", "D": "Calcaneal stress fracture, a repetitive loading injury with pain on medial and lateral heel squeeze", "E": "Heel fat pad atrophy, loss of cushioning that causes central heel pain with every heel strike"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Plantar fasciitis, a degenerative overuse injury of the fibrous band attaching at the calcaneal tuberosity): Start-up heel pain that improves with walking, medial calcaneal tenderness and pain with toe extension (windlass stretch) are typical of plantar fasciitis, which is more common with obesity. Tarsal tunnel syndrome would give burning paresthesias and a positive Tinel sign. A stress fracture is tender with lateral squeeze and tends to worsen with continued loading."}, {"id": 643, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A team plans a trial of a new inhaled corticosteroid to reduce emergency visits in children with asthma. The initial design has insufficient power. The sample size cannot be increased because of funding, and the outcome is already measured with a highly standardized protocol. Which modification would most directly increase power while keeping alpha at 0.05?", "options": {"A": "Adding a third active comparison arm that divides the same participants into smaller groups", "B": "Redefining a smaller difference as clinically meaningful so the expected effect is smaller", "C": "Blinding the outcome assessors to reduce observer bias in the emergency visit counts", "D": "Switching to a two-sided test with alpha divided across several additional secondary outcomes", "E": "Enrolling patients with more severe disease so the expected treatment effect is larger"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Enrolling patients with more severe disease so the expected treatment effect is larger): Power rises with a larger expected effect, and enrolling patients with more severe disease can increase the treatment effect that can be detected. Splitting alpha across secondary outcomes or adding a third arm lowers power, and a smaller target difference reduces it. Blinding reduces bias but does not change the ability to detect a true difference."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 3-year-old child living in a house built in 1950 has constipation, irritability, and developmental regression. The blood count shows microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling of red cells, and the blood lead level is markedly elevated. Which pair of heme synthesis enzymes is most directly inhibited by this exposure?", "options": {"A": "Uroporphyrinogen synthase and protoporphyrinogen oxidase, causing deposition of protoporphyrin in the epidermis", "B": "Porphobilinogen deaminase and coproporphyrinogen oxidase, leading to accumulation of porphobilinogen and urinary porphyrins", "C": "Delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase, causing accumulation of porphyrin precursors and protoporphyrin", "D": "Heme oxygenase and biliverdin reductase, preventing the breakdown of heme into bilirubin", "E": "Aminolevulinic acid synthase and uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, blocking the rate limiting step of porphyrin production"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase, causing accumulation of porphyrin precursors and protoporphyrin): Lead inhibits delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase, so aminolevulinic acid and protoporphyrin accumulate and heme production falls. It also inhibits rRNA degradation, producing basophilic stippling. The other pairs belong to different porphyrias or to heme catabolism."}, {"id": 645, "domain": "community_health", "question": "Serum cholesterol in a large screening program is approximately normally distributed with a mean of 200 mg/dL and a standard deviation of 20 mg/dL. A school-based nurse wants to estimate the percentage of participants whose values fall between 160 and 240 mg/dL. What is the closest estimate?", "options": {"A": "About 95 percent, since the interval extends two SDs above and below the average", "B": "About 34 percent, since the interval covers only the area between the mean and one SD", "C": "About 99.7 percent, since the interval extends three SDs above and below the mean", "D": "About 68 percent, since the interval extends one SD above and below the mean", "E": "About 50 percent, since the interval is centered on the mean value of the distribution"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 95 percent, since the interval extends two SDs above and below the average): The limits of 160 and 240 are each two standard deviations from the mean of 200, and roughly 95 percent of a normal distribution lies within two standard deviations. One standard deviation would enclose about 68 percent and three would enclose about 99.7 percent."}, {"id": 646, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 2-year-old boy is brought for a well visit. His mother says he runs, climbs stairs holding the rail, and kicks a ball. He uses about 10 single words, points to request items, and follows one-step commands, but does not combine words. His hearing screen and general examination are normal. Which developmental domain is most appropriately flagged as delayed?", "options": {"A": "Social-emotional skills, because pointing to request items normally emerges well after this age", "B": "Gross motor skills, because kicking a ball and climbing stairs usually appear only after age three", "C": "Receptive language, because following one-step commands is expected only after the third birthday", "D": "Cognitive skills, because lack of word combinations reflects delayed symbolic thinking expected by this age", "E": "Expressive language, since about 50 words and two-word combinations are expected by two years"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Expressive language, since about 50 words and two-word combinations are expected by two years): By two years a child typically has about 50 words and begins combining two words, so about 10 isolated words is an expressive language delay. Comprehension, pointing, and motor skills are age-appropriate here, which makes a global or motor delay unlikely. Normal hearing screening points toward an isolated speech delay that merits early language evaluation."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A laboratory serving a pediatric clinic lowers the positivity cutoff of a screening antibody assay for celiac disease from 20 to 10 units per milliliter, so that more samples now read as positive. The disease and the patients tested are unchanged. Which pair of changes best describes the effect on test performance?", "options": {"A": "Positive predictive value rises while sensitivity falls, because more samples are called positive", "B": "Sensitivity rises and specificity falls, producing more false positives and fewer false negatives", "C": "Sensitivity and specificity both rise, producing fewer false positives and fewer false negatives", "D": "Sensitivity falls and specificity rises, producing fewer false positives and more false negatives", "E": "Sensitivity is unchanged and specificity falls, because the diseased population remains the same"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sensitivity rises and specificity falls, producing more false positives and fewer false negatives): Lowering the cutoff calls more samples positive, which catches more truly diseased children (higher sensitivity) but also labels more healthy children positive (lower specificity). The number of false positives therefore rises and false negatives fall. Positive predictive value would, if anything, fall rather than rise."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 10-day-old term boy is brought with poor feeding, vomiting, jaundice, and lethargy that began after breastfeeding was established. He has hepatomegaly. Blood cultures grow Escherichia coli, and urine is positive for reducing substances but negative on glucose oxidase testing. Which enzyme is most likely deficient?", "options": {"A": "Aldolase B, causing fructose-1-phosphate accumulation after ingestion of fructose-containing foods during weaning", "B": "Galactokinase, causing galactitol accumulation that usually produces isolated cataracts without liver injury or sepsis", "C": "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase, causing toxic galactose-1-phosphate accumulation in the liver, kidney, lens, and brain tissue", "D": "Fructokinase, causing benign fructosuria without liver disease or hypoglycemia after fructose intake in affected children", "E": "Glucose-6-phosphatase, causing fasting hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis and massive hepatomegaly in early infancy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase, causing toxic galactose-1-phosphate accumulation in the liver, kidney, lens, and brain tissue): Classic galactosemia from uridyltransferase deficiency presents after lactose feeding with jaundice, hepatomegaly, and a predisposition to E. coli sepsis. Urine reducing substances are positive while glucose oxidase testing is negative because galactose is not detected by it. Galactokinase deficiency causes cataracts alone and lacks liver failure."}, {"id": 649, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 35-year-old woman with a cervicogenic headache is tested with the neck fully flexed to lock the lower cervical segments, then rotated passively. Rotation to the left reaches about 20 degrees less than to the right. Which segment is most likely dysfunctional and how is it named?", "options": {"A": "C1 on C2 sidebent right, named for the side of the free coupled motion", "B": "C1 on C2 rotated right, named for the direction in which the segment moves freely", "C": "C2 on C3 rotated right, with sidebending coupled to the same side as typical segments", "D": "Occiput on atlas rotated right, named for the direction in which the occiput slides", "E": "C1 on C2 rotated left, named for the direction in which motion is limited"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (C1 on C2 rotated right, named for the direction in which the segment moves freely): Full flexion locks the lower cervical segments, so a rotation deficit during this test points to C1 on C2, whose primary motion is rotation. Restricted left rotation means free right rotation, and the dysfunction is named for freedom, so it is rotated right."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 5-week-old girl, born at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy, has persistent jaundice, dark urine, and pale stools. She is feeding well and gaining weight. The liver is firm and the gallbladder was not visualized on fasting ultrasound. Based on the hepatic panel shown, which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Biliary atresia with progressive obliteration of the extrahepatic bile ducts", "B": "Neonatal hepatitis with giant cell transformation of hepatocytes and a normally filled gallbladder", "C": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency with periodic acid Schiff positive globules in periportal hepatocytes", "D": "Choledochal cyst with cystic dilation of the common bile duct and an abdominal mass", "E": "Alagille syndrome with paucity of interlobular bile ducts and a characteristic vertebral anomaly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Biliary atresia with progressive obliteration of the extrahepatic bile ducts): Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with acholic stools, a high GGT, and a nonvisualized gallbladder in a well-appearing infant points to biliary atresia, which needs surgical portoenterostomy early. Neonatal hepatitis usually allows a visible gallbladder, and a choledochal cyst would show cystic dilation on imaging. Delay beyond about two months worsens outcome.", "image": "question_images/q_650.png"}, {"id": 651, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 70-year-old man, two days after open abdominal surgery, develops fever, dyspnea and low oxygen saturation. He has taken shallow breaths because of incision pain and has not used his incentive spirometer. A chest radiograph shows increased opacity of the left lower lobe with the trachea and heart shifted toward the opacity. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary embolism with infarction producing a wedge-shaped pleural-based opacity and ipsilateral diaphragm elevation", "B": "Hospital-acquired lobar pneumonia, which classically pulls the mediastinum toward the side of consolidation", "C": "Postoperative atelectasis from hypoventilation and mucus plugging causing collapse with lobar volume loss", "D": "Tension pneumothorax with air trapped under pressure pushing the trachea toward the opposite hemithorax", "E": "Large pleural effusion compressing the lung and pushing the mediastinum away from the opacity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Postoperative atelectasis from hypoventilation and mucus plugging causing collapse with lobar volume loss): Atelectasis is a volume-loss process, so the trachea and heart are pulled toward the opaque side, and postoperative splinting with retained secretions is the classic setting. Effusion and tension pneumothorax both push the mediastinum away from the lesion. Pneumonia alone consolidates without volume loss and does not shift the mediastinum."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 64-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 30 percent has exertional dyspnea and leg edema despite taking an ACE inhibitor, a beta blocker, and furosemide. Potassium is 4.2 mEq/L and creatinine is normal. Which added drug has been shown to reduce mortality in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Hydralazine alone, an arteriolar vasodilator that lowers afterload and improves cardiac output at rest", "B": "Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that lowers afterload and improves exercise tolerance", "C": "Spironolactone, an aldosterone receptor antagonist that limits myocardial fibrosis and adverse ventricular remodeling", "D": "Digoxin, a sodium potassium ATPase inhibitor that strengthens contraction and reduces heart failure admissions", "E": "Ivabradine, a funny current inhibitor that slows sinus node firing and lowers resting heart rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Spironolactone, an aldosterone receptor antagonist that limits myocardial fibrosis and adverse ventricular remodeling): Aldosterone antagonists block mineralocorticoid driven fibrosis and sodium retention and lower mortality when added to an ACE inhibitor and beta blocker in reduced ejection fraction. Digoxin reduces admissions but does not lower death. Amlodipine is neutral at best, and ivabradine lowers hospitalization without a mortality benefit."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis has mild fatigue and takes methotrexate, folic acid, and ibuprofen. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL with a mean corpuscular volume of 82 fL. Ferritin is elevated, transferrin saturation is low, and total iron-binding capacity is decreased. Which mechanism best explains the anemia?", "options": {"A": "Methotrexate induced inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase impairing thymidylate synthesis in erythroblasts", "B": "Interleukin-6 driven hepcidin release trapping iron within macrophages and blocking intestinal absorption", "C": "Chronic gastrointestinal blood loss from nonsteroidal drug use depleting total body iron stores", "D": "Reduced erythropoietin production from tubulointerstitial injury in an inflamed kidney", "E": "Autoantibody mediated destruction of red cells through extravascular hemolysis in the spleen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interleukin-6 driven hepcidin release trapping iron within macrophages and blocking intestinal absorption): Inflammation raises interleukin-6, which stimulates hepcidin; hepcidin degrades ferroportin, trapping iron in macrophages and limiting absorption. This yields high ferritin, low iron, and low binding capacity, typical of anemia of chronic disease. Blood loss would lower ferritin, and folate supplementation argues against methotrexate related macrocytosis."}, {"id": 654, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 43-year-old man is brought by his wife for irritability, clumsiness and uncontrolled twitching movements that have progressed over three years. His father died in a psychiatric hospital at age 55. Examination shows brief, irregular, flowing movements of the face and arms. The axial head CT shown was obtained. Which neurotransmitter change is expected?", "options": {"A": "Reduced glutamate in the subthalamic nucleus with relative excess of GABA activity", "B": "Reduced serotonin in the raphe nuclei with relative excess of norepinephrine activity", "C": "Reduced dopamine in the striatum with relative excess of acetylcholine activity", "D": "Reduced GABA and acetylcholine in the striatum with relative excess of dopamine activity", "E": "Reduced acetylcholine in the basal forebrain with preserved GABA and dopamine levels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced GABA and acetylcholine in the striatum with relative excess of dopamine activity): Huntington disease, an autosomal dominant CAG repeat disorder, destroys striatal medium spiny neurons, reducing GABA and acetylcholine while dopaminergic input is relatively overactive, which produces chorea. Imaging typically shows caudate atrophy with enlarged frontal horns. Parkinson disease shows the opposite dopamine and acetylcholine balance.", "image": "question_images/q_654.png"}, {"id": 655, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 32-year-old woman visits a clinic for the fourth time this year with headaches and abdominal pain. Bruises of different ages are on her upper arms. Her partner answers most questions and stays in the room. She looks at him before replying and denies any problems. How should the physician proceed?", "options": {"A": "Confront the partner about the bruises in the room and request an explanation from him", "B": "Refer the couple together to marital counseling to address the conflict behind her recurrent symptoms", "C": "Order brain imaging and abdominal CT to exclude organic disease before raising any social concerns", "D": "Prescribe a tricyclic antidepressant for presumed somatization and schedule a visit in one month", "E": "Arrange time alone with her, then ask direct, nonjudgmental questions about safety at home"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Arrange time alone with her, then ask direct, nonjudgmental questions about safety at home): Patients are usually safest when screened alone, so the physician should create a routine reason to separate them and then ask directly. Confronting the partner or offering couples therapy can raise the risk of retaliation. Repeated vague complaints with bruises of different ages should prompt screening, not repeated testing or medication."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 45-year-old man with chronic heavy alcohol use is brought to the emergency department after a 2-day binge during which he ate nothing. He is confused and diaphoretic, and the panel shown reveals marked hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis and ketoacidosis. Ethanol oxidation has shifted the hepatic redox state. Which process is most directly impaired, producing the hypoglycemia?", "options": {"A": "Glycogenolysis, because ethanol metabolites inhibit phosphorylase although hepatic glycogen stores remain abundant after fasting", "B": "Hepatic glycolysis, because ethanol metabolites activate phosphofructokinase-1 and trap glucose as lactate rather than releasing it", "C": "Fatty acid oxidation, because NADH inhibits beta-oxidation and prevents acetyl-CoA generation needed to activate pyruvate carboxylase", "D": "Glucose-6-phosphatase activity, because ethanol metabolites directly inhibit the enzyme in the endoplasmic reticulum of hepatocytes", "E": "Gluconeogenesis, since pyruvate and oxaloacetate are diverted to lactate and malate by excess NADH"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Gluconeogenesis, since pyruvate and oxaloacetate are diverted to lactate and malate by excess NADH): Ethanol oxidation by alcohol and acetaldehyde dehydrogenase floods hepatocytes with NADH, driving pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate, so substrates for gluconeogenesis are unavailable. Glycogen is already depleted after two days of fasting, so glycogenolysis cannot rescue glucose. The lactate and ketone elevations in the panel fit this redox shift.", "image": "question_images/q_656.png"}, {"id": 657, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 55-year-old man has three months of postprandial epigastric burning, and a urea breath test is positive. The physician finds palpable tissue texture changes in the paraspinal muscles between the scapulae, with tender adjacent ribs. Which interpretation best explains these paraspinal findings?", "options": {"A": "Cardiac viscerosomatic reflex through sympathetic input to facilitated cord segments at roughly T1 to T5", "B": "Small bowel viscerosomatic reflex through sympathetic input to facilitated cord segments at roughly T8 to T10", "C": "Stomach viscerosomatic reflex through sympathetic input to facilitated cord segments at roughly T5 to T9", "D": "Stomach viscerosomatic reflex through vagal input to sacral cord segments at S2 to S4", "E": "Renal viscerosomatic reflex through sympathetic input to facilitated cord segments at roughly T10 to L1"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stomach viscerosomatic reflex through sympathetic input to facilitated cord segments at roughly T5 to T9): Postprandial epigastric burning with a positive urea breath test indicates gastric disease, and stomach sympathetic afferents are linked to cord segments at roughly T5 to T9, which matches the paraspinal findings. The vagus is a cranial parasympathetic supply to the stomach and does not act through sacral segments."}, {"id": 658, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 19-year-old man with sickle cell disease has recurrent right upper quadrant colic. He has had multiple hemolytic crises since childhood, takes folic acid and is not obese, with no history of biliary infection or parenteral nutrition. Ultrasound shows several small shadowing gallbladder stones. Which composition is most likely for these stones?", "options": {"A": "Black pigment stones of calcium bilirubinate, formed from excess unconjugated bilirubin in chronic hemolysis", "B": "Cholesterol stones formed in bile supersaturated with cholesterol relative to bile salts and lecithin", "C": "Brown pigment stones formed in bile ducts from bacterial beta glucuronidase deconjugating bilirubin", "D": "Calcium carbonate stones composed of mineral precipitated from a porcelain gallbladder wall", "E": "Mixed stones arising from gallbladder stasis and prolonged fasting during total parenteral nutrition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Black pigment stones of calcium bilirubinate, formed from excess unconjugated bilirubin in chronic hemolysis): Chronic hemolysis loads bile with unconjugated bilirubin, which precipitates with calcium into black pigment stones in the gallbladder. Cholesterol stones follow cholesterol supersaturation, as in obesity. Brown stones need bacterial beta-glucuronidase from infection, usually in bile ducts."}, {"id": 659, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man has eight months of progressive hand weakness and wasting, frequent muscle twitching, and mild slurred speech. Examination shows tongue fasciculations, brisk reflexes in the legs with extensor plantar responses, and a spastic gait. Sensation, eye movements, and bladder function are normal. Which pathologic process best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Degeneration of cortical upper motor neurons together with spinal anterior horn lower motor neurons", "B": "Cavitation of the central spinal cord by a syrinx interrupting crossing spinothalamic fibers", "C": "Degeneration of dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts from cobalamin deficiency", "D": "Immune-mediated demyelination of multiple central white matter tracts separated in time and space by oligodendrocyte injury", "E": "Viral destruction restricted to anterior horn cells and sparing the corticospinal pathways"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Degeneration of cortical upper motor neurons together with spinal anterior horn lower motor neurons): Combined lower motor neuron signs (wasting, fasciculations) and upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia, extensor plantars, spasticity) with no sensory or sphincter involvement define amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. Both cortical and anterior horn motor neurons degenerate. Poliovirus affects only anterior horn cells, and syringomyelia produces sensory loss."}, {"id": 660, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 42-year-old woman has early satisfaction and a 7-kg weight loss over four months. Her mother died of gastric cancer at age 45. Endoscopy shows a thickened, rigid stomach wall without a discrete mass, and biopsy shows infiltrating cells with intracytoplasmic mucin that pushes the nucleus to the edge. Which genetic abnormality most likely contributed?", "options": {"A": "Germline mismatch repair mutation producing microsatellite instability and intestinal type tumors", "B": "The t(11;18) translocation creating an API2-MALT1 fusion in marginal zone lymphoma after chronic infection", "C": "Germline CDH1 mutation causing loss of E-cadherin and impaired adhesion between epithelial cells", "D": "Activating KIT mutation in interstitial cells of Cajal producing a spindle cell tumor", "E": "Acquired p53 loss on a background of intestinal metaplasia in chronic Helicobacter gastritis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Germline CDH1 mutation causing loss of E-cadherin and impaired adhesion between epithelial cells): Diffuse gastric cancer with signet ring cells and linitis plastica, especially with a young affected relative, suggests hereditary diffuse gastric cancer from germline CDH1 mutation. Intestinal type tumors arise through metaplasia with p53 changes or mismatch repair defects and form masses or ulcers rather than a rigid thickened wall."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 34-year-old woman returning from a trip abroad has a fever of 39 degrees Celsius, abdominal cramps, and ten bloody stools over two days. She asks the clinic physician to recommend loperamide to stop the diarrhea before her return flight. What is the best counsel regarding this drug?", "options": {"A": "Use it, because mu opioid effects stay in the gut and do not worsen infection", "B": "Use it only at half the usual dose, because lower doses spare bowel motility", "C": "Use diphenoxylate with atropine instead, because it acts centrally and is safer in dysentery", "D": "Use bismuth subsalicylate instead, because it binds toxins without affecting colonic motility", "E": "Avoid it, because slowed transit may prolong invasive infection and risk toxic megacolon"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Avoid it, because slowed transit may prolong invasive infection and risk toxic megacolon): Fever and bloody stools suggest invasive bacterial enteritis, where reduced motility can prolong exposure to the pathogen and promote toxic megacolon. Diphenoxylate shares the same risk, and dose reduction does not remove it. The patient needs evaluation and targeted treatment rather than antimotility therapy."}, {"id": 662, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 47-year-old man with erosive reflux esophagitis is started on a medication taken before breakfast. The drug is a prodrug activated in acidic environments, and after a single dose it suppresses acid secretion for more than 24 hours because its binding is irreversible. Which target does this drug act on?", "options": {"A": "Histamine H2 receptors on the basolateral membrane of gastric parietal cells", "B": "Prostaglandin E2 receptors on parietal cells that normally inhibit adenylate cyclase", "C": "Muscarinic M3 receptors on parietal cells mediating vagally driven acid secretion", "D": "Hydrogen potassium ATPase on the secretory canalicular membrane of gastric parietal cells", "E": "Gastrin receptors on enterochromaffin-like cells that release paracrine histamine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hydrogen potassium ATPase on the secretory canalicular membrane of gastric parietal cells): Proton pump inhibitors are activated in acid and covalently and irreversibly bind the H+/K+ ATPase of the parietal cell canaliculus, so a single dose acts for more than a day until new pumps are synthesized. H2 blockers act reversibly at the histamine receptor and give shorter, less complete suppression."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 45-year-old man with latent tuberculosis begins daily isoniazid. Two months later he reports burning pain and numbness in both feet. Examination shows reduced ankle reflexes and decreased vibration sense at the toes. He drinks several beers each week. Which mechanism most likely explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of thiamine pyrophosphate dependent enzymes, which impairs neuronal glucose oxidation and energy supply", "B": "Depletion of pyridoxine through drug-induced impairment of its conversion to pyridoxal phosphate", "C": "Depletion of cobalamin through blocked binding of intrinsic factor in the terminal ileum", "D": "Direct inhibition of bacterial RNA polymerase homologs in Schwann cells, impairing myelin protein synthesis", "E": "Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis in neuronal myelin, producing demyelination of peripheral axons"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Depletion of pyridoxine through drug-induced impairment of its conversion to pyridoxal phosphate): Isoniazid interferes with the activation of pyridoxine, so patients develop a sensory peripheral neuropathy that is prevented by giving vitamin B6. Alcohol use and malnutrition raise the risk, which makes thiamine deficiency tempting, but the temporal link to isoniazid and the pyridoxine pathway make it the best answer. The drug inhibits mycolic acid in mycobacteria, not human myelin."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 14-month-old boy is found lethargic and vomiting the morning after a viral illness with poor oral intake. Glucose is 28 mg/dL, urine ketones are negative, and serum free fatty acids are elevated. Urine organic acid analysis shows dicarboxylic acids, and ammonia is mildly elevated. Which defect best explains the failure to mount a ketotic response during fasting?", "options": {"A": "Deficient glucose-6-phosphatase, trapping glucose-6-phosphate in hepatocytes and raising lactate and uric acid during fasting", "B": "Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase deficiency, blocking gluconeogenesis from lactate and glycerol and producing ketotic hypoglycemia during fasting", "C": "Insulin excess suppressing hepatic HMG-CoA synthase, blocking acetoacetate formation despite abundant fatty acid delivery from adipose", "D": "Deficient pyruvate carboxylase activity, limiting oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis and producing lactic acidosis with fasting hypoglycemia", "E": "Impaired mitochondrial beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids, limiting acetyl-CoA supply for hepatic ketone body synthesis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Impaired mitochondrial beta-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids, limiting acetyl-CoA supply for hepatic ketone body synthesis): In medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency, fatty acids cannot be oxidized to acetyl-CoA, so ketone bodies are not made and the fasting child becomes hypoglycemic without ketosis. Dicarboxylic aciduria results from omega-oxidation of unoxidized fatty acids. Hyperinsulinism also gives hypoketosis, but it suppresses lipolysis, so free fatty acids would be low rather than high."}, {"id": 665, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 24-year-old man has had voices commenting on his actions and a belief that neighbors broadcast his thoughts for eight months, with declining work and self-care. Toxicology is negative and no mood episode occurred. Which neurochemical abnormality best explains his hallucinations?", "options": {"A": "Reduced dopaminergic transmission in the nigrostriatal pathway causing bradykinesia and rigidity", "B": "Reduced dopaminergic activity in the mesocortical pathway projecting to the prefrontal cortex", "C": "Excess dopamine in the tuberoinfundibular pathway suppressing prolactin secretion from the pituitary", "D": "Excess dopamine in the nigrostriatal pathway producing choreiform movements and tics", "E": "Excess dopaminergic transmission in the mesolimbic pathway from the ventral tegmentum to the nucleus accumbens"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Excess dopaminergic transmission in the mesolimbic pathway from the ventral tegmentum to the nucleus accumbens): Hallucinations and delusions, the positive symptoms of schizophrenia, are attributed to overactivity of dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway, which is why D2 blockers relieve them. Reduced mesocortical dopamine is linked to negative and cognitive symptoms, and nigrostriatal and tuberoinfundibular effects explain drug side effects rather than the psychosis."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 7-year-old girl who swims regularly has multiple 3 to 4 mm flesh-colored, dome-shaped papules with central umbilication on the trunk and axillae. The lesions are painless and mildly pruritic, and she has no fever or systemic symptoms. Biopsy would show eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion bodies. Which virus is responsible?", "options": {"A": "A coxsackievirus enterovirus that infects mucosa and produces vesicles on the palms and soles", "B": "A parvovirus that targets erythroid precursors and causes a slapped cheek exanthem in children", "C": "A herpesvirus that establishes latency in sensory ganglia and produces multinucleated giant cells", "D": "A poxvirus that replicates in the cytoplasm of keratinocytes and forms Henderson-Patterson bodies", "E": "A human papillomavirus type that infects basal keratinocytes and causes koilocytic change in the epidermis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A poxvirus that replicates in the cytoplasm of keratinocytes and forms Henderson-Patterson bodies): Molluscum contagiosum is caused by a poxvirus, which replicates in the cytoplasm and produces eosinophilic Henderson-Patterson bodies in the epidermis. Papillomavirus causes warts with koilocytes, and herpesviruses produce vesicles with multinucleated cells."}, {"id": 667, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 9-year-old boy with known sickle cell disease presents to the emergency department with three days of fever and severe pain over the right tibia. The area is warm and tender. Blood cultures are drawn and MRI confirms marrow edema with a subperiosteal collection. Which organism is most characteristically associated with osteomyelitis in this underlying condition?", "options": {"A": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which causes vertebral infection with insidious onset and weight loss", "B": "Staphylococcus aureus, the most common cause of hematogenous bone infection in all children", "C": "Salmonella species, which invade infarcted bone in patients with functional asplenia", "D": "Pasteurella multocida, which infects bone after cat bites in young children", "E": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which infects bone after puncture wounds through athletic footwear"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Salmonella species, which invade infarcted bone in patients with functional asplenia): Bone infarcts and functional asplenia in sickle cell disease predispose to Salmonella osteomyelitis, although Staphylococcus aureus remains the single most common cause overall. Pseudomonas follows puncture wounds through the foot and Pasteurella follows animal bites. Tuberculosis would present far more indolently and usually in the spine."}, {"id": 668, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 5-year-old boy develops periorbital swelling and weight gain over 10 days, a week after an upper respiratory infection. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL, cholesterol is elevated, and complement levels are normal. Light microscopy of a kidney biopsy appears normal. Which electron microscopy finding is expected?", "options": {"A": "Large subepithelial humps with mesangial proliferation and neutrophil infiltration of glomeruli", "B": "Subendothelial electron dense deposits with mesangial interposition producing double contour basement membranes", "C": "Lamellated splitting of the glomerular basement membrane producing a basket weave appearance", "D": "Diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes with no discrete immune deposits", "E": "Subepithelial spike and dome projections with granular IgG and C3 deposition"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes with no discrete immune deposits): Heavy proteinuria without blood and with normal light microscopy in a young child is minimal change disease. Podocyte injury flattens the foot processes and loses the charge barrier, which responds well to corticosteroids. The other patterns involve visible deposits or basement membrane changes."}, {"id": 669, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 61-year-old woman has episodic substernal chest pain and difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids that comes and goes, with partial relief from sublingual nitroglycerin. Cardiac evaluation is normal. Barium swallow shows a corkscrew appearance, and manometry shows simultaneous high-amplitude contractions with normal lower esophageal sphincter relaxation. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Peptic stricture from chronic reflux producing progressive dysphagia mainly for solid food", "B": "Diffuse esophageal spasm from impaired inhibitory innervation causing uncoordinated simultaneous contractions", "C": "Zenker diverticulum from posterior pharyngeal herniation causing regurgitation of undigested food and halitosis", "D": "Achalasia from myenteric neuron loss with incomplete sphincter relaxation and absent peristalsis", "E": "Systemic sclerosis esophagus from smooth muscle fibrosis with a weak sphincter and absent peristalsis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diffuse esophageal spasm from impaired inhibitory innervation causing uncoordinated simultaneous contractions): Intermittent chest pain and dysphagia to both solids and liquids with a corkscrew barium appearance and simultaneous high-amplitude contractions with normal sphincter relaxation define diffuse esophageal spasm. Achalasia would show incomplete relaxation and absent peristalsis, and scleroderma would show a weak sphincter with no contractions."}, {"id": 670, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with a BMI of 43 reports exertional dyspnea and snoring. Spirometry shows FVC 62% of predicted, FEV1/FVC 0.85, and reduced expiratory reserve volume. Diffusing capacity is normal when corrected for hemoglobin. She has no cough, wheeze, or exposure history. Which process best explains this pulmonary function pattern?", "options": {"A": "Respiratory muscle weakness from neuromuscular disease, as inspiratory force fails to expand the lungs", "B": "Pulmonary vascular disease, as obliteration of capillaries lowers volumes and reduces diffusing capacity", "C": "Intrinsic parenchymal fibrosis, as alveolar septal thickening reduces compliance and impairs gas transfer", "D": "Extraparenchymal restriction, as chest wall and abdominal mass load limits lung expansion without alveolar damage", "E": "Obstructive airway disease, as dynamic collapse of small airways traps gas and lowers the ratio"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Extraparenchymal restriction, as chest wall and abdominal mass load limits lung expansion without alveolar damage): A reduced FVC with a normal or high FEV1/FVC ratio indicates restriction. A normal corrected diffusing capacity places the problem outside the lung parenchyma, and severe obesity limits chest wall and diaphragmatic excursion, lowering expiratory reserve volume. Intrinsic interstitial disease would reduce diffusing capacity."}, {"id": 671, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 58-year-old man in a primary care clinic reports three episodes of sudden lip and tongue swelling over two months, without itching or hives. He takes lisinopril for hypertension, started two years ago, and has no family history of similar episodes. Antihistamines gave no relief. Which mechanism best explains the swelling?", "options": {"A": "Allergen cross-linking of IgE on mast cells, releasing histamine within minutes of exposure", "B": "Inherited deficiency of C1 esterase inhibitor, allowing uncontrolled activation of the complement and contact cascades", "C": "Direct nonimmunologic degranulation of mast cells triggered by a drug acting on opioid receptors", "D": "Impaired enzymatic breakdown of bradykinin, increasing vascular permeability in the deep dermis and submucosa", "E": "Immune complex deposition in small vessels, activating complement and recruiting neutrophils"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Impaired enzymatic breakdown of bradykinin, increasing vascular permeability in the deep dermis and submucosa): Angiotensin converting enzyme normally degrades bradykinin, so an inhibitor lets it accumulate and leak fluid into deep tissue without itching or hives. This is why antihistamines fail. Hereditary C1 inhibitor deficiency is also bradykinin mediated but would not first appear in middle age after starting a new drug."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 24-year-old man after a motorcycle crash with head trauma develops polyuria of 6 liters per day. After 8 hours of water deprivation his urine osmolality is 180 mOsm/kg and serum osmolality is 305 mOsm/kg. After subcutaneous desmopressin, urine osmolality rises to 540 mOsm/kg. Which process explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Compulsive water drinking with medullary washout that blunts urine concentration during early water restriction", "B": "Impaired hypothalamic hormone secretion from the posterior pituitary, with a preserved collecting duct response", "C": "Osmotic diuresis from uncontrolled hyperglycemia, producing large volumes of urine with high solute content and osmolality", "D": "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion after head injury, with concentrated urine and dilutional hyponatremia", "E": "Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from collecting duct resistance to antidiuretic hormone, as seen with chronic lithium therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Impaired hypothalamic hormone secretion from the posterior pituitary, with a preserved collecting duct response): Dilute urine after water deprivation with a rising serum osmolality shows the kidney is not conserving water, and a rise of more than 50 percent in urine osmolality after desmopressin places the defect in hormone release, as in central diabetes insipidus after head trauma. Nephrogenic disease would show little response to desmopressin."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 28-year-old man with asthma and eczema has recurrent episodes of solid food sticking in the chest, with one impaction needing endoscopic removal. Symptoms persisted after eight weeks of a proton pump inhibitor, and he drinks two coffees daily. Endoscopy shows concentric rings and linear furrows. Which diagnosis and mechanism best fit?", "options": {"A": "Reflux esophagitis from acid and pepsin injury that should respond to proton pump therapy", "B": "Eosinophilic esophagitis from antigen driven type 2 helper T cell inflammation of the squamous mucosa", "C": "Achalasia from degeneration of inhibitory neurons with a tight lower esophageal sphincter and no peristalsis", "D": "Pill esophagitis from direct mucosal injury by a retained medication tablet at the aortic arch", "E": "Candida esophagitis from fungal invasion producing white plaques in an immunosuppressed host"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Eosinophilic esophagitis from antigen driven type 2 helper T cell inflammation of the squamous mucosa): Atopy, food impaction, rings and furrows, and failure to respond to a proton pump inhibitor point to eosinophilic esophagitis, a type 2 helper T cell mediated response to food antigens confirmed by eosinophils on biopsy. Reflux esophagitis is the main mimic, but it would be expected to improve with acid suppression."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 62-year-old man has 2 months of early satiety and weight loss. Examination shows a firm, nontender, 2 cm lymph node in the left supraclavicular fossa. He denies fever, sore throat, or recent infection. Abdominal examination is unremarkable. Which source is the most likely origin of this finding?", "options": {"A": "Carcinoma of the right breast, spreading through axillary nodes draining into the right lymphatic duct", "B": "Viral pharyngitis with reactive hyperplasia of tonsillar and upper deep cervical lymph nodes", "C": "Squamous carcinoma of the scalp, spreading through occipital and posterior auricular nodes to the neck", "D": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma, spreading through deep cervical chain nodes along the internal jugular vein", "E": "Abdominal malignancy such as gastric carcinoma, spreading through the thoracic duct to the venous angle"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Abdominal malignancy such as gastric carcinoma, spreading through the thoracic duct to the venous angle): The thoracic duct drains the left side of the body and the abdomen and empties at the left venous angle, so a firm left supraclavicular node (Virchow node) points to an intra-abdominal cancer such as gastric carcinoma. The early satiety and weight loss fit. Head and neck malignancies would drain to higher cervical nodes, and breast cancer on the right would not reach the left side."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 6-year-old boy has morning headaches, vomiting and unsteady walking for six weeks. He sways when sitting unsupported, walks on a wide base, and falls backward. Limb coordination, tested with finger-to-nose, is normal. MRI shows a midline posterior fossa mass. Which cerebellar structure is primarily involved?", "options": {"A": "Lateral cerebellar hemisphere regulating planning and coordination of ipsilateral limb movements", "B": "Vermis and flocculonodular lobe regulating axial posture and truncal balance during standing and walking", "C": "Intermediate zone of the paravermis regulating distal limb movements through the interposed nuclei", "D": "Dentate nucleus outflow through the superior cerebellar peduncle to the contralateral thalamus", "E": "Cerebellar tonsils herniating through the foramen magnum and compressing the medulla"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vermis and flocculonodular lobe regulating axial posture and truncal balance during standing and walking): Midline cerebellar lesions, such as a medulloblastoma arising in the vermis, disrupt the vestibulocerebellar and spinocerebellar circuits that control posture, causing truncal ataxia with a wide-based gait and falls while limb coordination is spared. Hemispheric lesions cause ipsilateral limb dysmetria. Obstructive hydrocephalus from fourth ventricle compression accounts for the morning headaches and vomiting."}, {"id": 676, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 38-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis has persistent back pain despite maximal NSAID therapy and is about to start infliximab. He emigrated from a country with high tuberculosis prevalence 10 years ago, has no cough or fever, and his chest radiograph is normal. Which step is most appropriate before starting the drug?", "options": {"A": "Screening with an interferon gamma release assay, since TNF blockade impairs granuloma maintenance", "B": "Echocardiography for ejection fraction, since TNF blockade worsens heart failure in some patients", "C": "Repeat chest radiograph in three months, since the normal film excludes latent disease reliably", "D": "Baseline antinuclear antibody titer, since TNF inhibitors commonly induce a lupus like autoimmune syndrome", "E": "Sputum acid fast smear on three mornings, since asymptomatic patients may still shed organisms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Screening with an interferon gamma release assay, since TNF blockade impairs granuloma maintenance): TNF alpha is required to maintain granulomas, so blockade can reactivate latent tuberculosis, and testing for latent infection is mandatory before treatment even with a normal radiograph. Drug induced lupus is rare and does not require a baseline titer. Sputum studies and echocardiography target different problems."}, {"id": 677, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 2-year-old child squats after playing and has episodic blue spells that resolve with knee-to-chest positioning. Examination shows a harsh systolic murmur at the left upper sternal border and the chest radiograph shows a boot-shaped heart. Which feature of the lesion most directly determines the severity of cyanosis?", "options": {"A": "Extent of aortic override over the septum mixing venous blood into systemic outflow", "B": "Size of the patent ductus arteriosus allowing left-to-right flow into the pulmonary artery", "C": "Degree of right ventricular outflow obstruction driving right-to-left shunting across the septal defect", "D": "Size of the ventricular septal defect restricting pressure equalization between the ventricles", "E": "Severity of right ventricular hypertrophy limiting stroke volume delivered to the lungs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Degree of right ventricular outflow obstruction driving right-to-left shunting across the septal defect): In tetralogy of Fallot the ventricular septal defect is large and nonrestrictive, so ventricular pressures equalize and the direction of flow depends on relative resistance. The more severe the pulmonary outflow obstruction, the more blood shunts right to left and the deeper the cyanosis. Squatting raises systemic resistance and reduces that shunt."}, {"id": 678, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 6-year-old boy has three weeks of morning headaches, vomiting, and clumsy gait with frequent falls. Examination shows truncal ataxia and papilledema. MRI shows a midline cerebellar mass compressing the fourth ventricle, with drop metastases on spinal imaging. Biopsy would most likely show which of the following findings?", "options": {"A": "Rosenthal fibers within bipolar cells in a biphasic pattern, typical of pilocytic astrocytoma", "B": "Whorled meningothelial cells with psammoma bodies, typical of a slow growing dural tumor", "C": "Fried-egg cells with perinuclear halos and chicken-wire capillaries, typical of a calcified frontal oligodendroglioma", "D": "Small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes, a primitive neuroectodermal tumor pattern", "E": "Perivascular pseudorosettes of GFAP-positive cells around vessels, typical of ependymoma in the fourth ventricle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes, a primitive neuroectodermal tumor pattern): A midline cerebellar mass in a young child that seeds the subarachnoid space is classic for medulloblastoma, a small round blue cell tumor with Homer Wright rosettes. Pilocytic astrocytoma is cystic with a mural nodule and does not typically spread along the neuraxis, and ependymoma shows perivascular pseudorosettes rather than neuroblastic rosettes."}, {"id": 679, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with chronic pelvic floor tension and bilateral leg swelling has a physician treat the pelvic diaphragm to improve venous and lymphatic return from the legs. Before treating, the physician reviews which muscles form this transverse restriction and contribute to the circulatory-respiratory model of osteopathic care.", "options": {"A": "Obturator internus and piriformis muscles lining the lateral wall of the pelvis", "B": "Psoas major and iliacus muscles crossing the pelvic brim toward the lesser trochanter", "C": "Transversus abdominis and internal oblique muscles forming the deep abdominal wall layers", "D": "Levator ani and coccygeus muscles stretching across the inferior opening of the pelvis", "E": "Superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles spanning the urogenital triangle within the perineum"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Levator ani and coccygeus muscles stretching across the inferior opening of the pelvis): The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the levator ani and coccygeus, and it is the caudal transverse restriction through which venous and lymphatic return from the legs must pass. The perineal muscles belong to the urogenital triangle and lie inferior to this layer, which makes them a tempting but incorrect answer."}, {"id": 680, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 25-year-old woman on an inpatient unit idolizes one resident as perfect and caring, then abruptly calls him cruel and incompetent after he declines an extra pass. She has unstable relationships, chronic emptiness and prior superficial cuts on her forearms. Which defense mechanism best describes her view of the resident?", "options": {"A": "Reaction formation, in which an unacceptable impulse is replaced by its exact opposite behavior", "B": "Displacement, in which anger toward one person is redirected onto a safer, less threatening target", "C": "Splitting, in which people are seen as all good or all bad without integration", "D": "Projection, in which one's own unacceptable feelings are attributed to the resident instead", "E": "Rationalization, in which a plausible explanation is created to justify an unacceptable behavior"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Splitting, in which people are seen as all good or all bad without integration): Splitting is the characteristic defense of borderline personality disorder: others are idealized or devalued and the two views are not integrated, so opinions flip with perceived slights. Projection and displacement involve a different target or attribution, and the same person is the object of both extremes here."}, {"id": 681, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 45-year-old woman asks for manipulative treatment for a bilateral pressing headache lasting months, with tender paraspinal muscles and restricted upper thoracic motion. Today she also reports a new sudden severe headache that peaked within seconds, with neck stiffness and vomiting. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Defer osteopathic manipulation and obtain urgent noncontrast head CT, because subarachnoid hemorrhage must be excluded first", "B": "Treat with suboccipital inhibition and counterstrain, then reassess in one week for persistent pain", "C": "Apply muscle energy to the thoracic segment and give analgesics, since tension-type headache is likely", "D": "Obtain cervical spine radiographs and then proceed with articulatory technique if no fracture is seen", "E": "Perform cervical HVLA to the restricted segment, since sudden headache with neck stiffness reflects somatic dysfunction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Defer osteopathic manipulation and obtain urgent noncontrast head CT, because subarachnoid hemorrhage must be excluded first): A sudden worst headache with neck stiffness and vomiting is subarachnoid hemorrhage until proven otherwise, and manipulation is contraindicated before intracranial bleeding is excluded. Her chronic tension-type headache and thoracic somatic dysfunction can be treated later. Treating the pattern as somatic or imaging the cervical spine alone delays the necessary head CT."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 22-year-old runner undergoes cardiopulmonary testing on a treadmill. During moderate steady-state exercise, minute ventilation rises about tenfold over resting values. Arterial blood sampled at that workload shows PaO2 of 98 mm Hg, PaCO2 of 40 mm Hg and pH of 7.40. Which mechanism best accounts for the rise in ventilation at this workload?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary stretch receptor reflex that terminates inspiration as tidal volume expands during exertion", "B": "Metabolic acidemia from lactate release stimulating the carotid bodies through falling arterial pH", "C": "Motor cortex and joint proprioceptor input driving medullary centers in proportion to muscular effort", "D": "Rising arterial PCO2 stimulating central chemoreceptors as carbon dioxide production exceeds its elimination", "E": "Falling arterial PO2 stimulating carotid bodies as oxygen delivery fails to match muscle demand"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Motor cortex and joint proprioceptor input driving medullary centers in proportion to muscular effort): During moderate exercise the arterial blood gas values stay normal, so ventilation is not being driven by changes in PO2, PCO2 or pH. The rise is matched to metabolic rate by feed-forward signals from the motor cortex and from muscle and joint receptors. Acidemia from lactate stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors only at heavy workloads above the anaerobic threshold."}, {"id": 683, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 52-year-old woman completed adjuvant chemotherapy for breast cancer two years ago. She now has progressive dyspnea and edema. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with global hypokinesis and an ejection fraction of 30 percent. Coronary angiography is normal. Which agent most likely caused this cumulative, dose dependent cardiomyopathy?", "options": {"A": "Trastuzumab, which blocks HER2 signaling and produces myocardial dysfunction that often improves after stopping", "B": "Fluorouracil, which provokes coronary vasospasm and ischemic chest pain during the infusion", "C": "Doxorubicin, which generates iron dependent free radicals that injure cardiac myocytes", "D": "Paclitaxel, which stabilizes microtubules and causes sinus bradycardia and conduction delay", "E": "Cyclophosphamide, which causes acute hemorrhagic myocardial necrosis when given at very high doses"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Doxorubicin, which generates iron dependent free radicals that injure cardiac myocytes): Anthracyclines cause cumulative, dose dependent myocyte injury through iron mediated free radical formation, leading to dilated cardiomyopathy even years later. Trastuzumab injury is not dose related and often reverses. Cyclophosphamide, fluorouracil, and paclitaxel cause acute necrosis, vasospasm, and conduction effects respectively."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 74-year-old woman is admitted with an inferior wall myocardial infarction managed medically. On day 4 she develops abrupt dyspnea and hypotension. Examination reveals a new loud holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla and bilateral crackles. Echocardiography shows a flail mitral leaflet. Which structure's ischemic rupture best explains this deterioration, and why is it vulnerable?", "options": {"A": "Left ventricular free wall, which tears when necrotic muscle cannot withstand systolic wall tension", "B": "Posteromedial papillary muscle, which has a single blood supply from the posterior descending artery", "C": "Interventricular septum, which ruptures where ischemic necrosis weakens the muscular septal wall", "D": "Anterolateral papillary muscle, which relies on a lone left anterior descending branch for perfusion", "E": "Mitral annulus, which dilates when the posterior wall of the left ventricle becomes ischemic"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Posteromedial papillary muscle, which has a single blood supply from the posterior descending artery): The posteromedial papillary muscle is supplied only by the posterior descending artery, whereas the anterolateral muscle has dual supply from the left anterior descending and circumflex branches, so inferior infarction preferentially ruptures the posteromedial muscle, giving acute severe mitral regurgitation. Septal rupture also causes a holosystolic murmur but at the left sternal border with a step-up in oxygen saturation."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 6-week-old girl born breech is brought for a routine visit. Examination shows asymmetric thigh skin folds and limited abduction of the left hip. The Barlow maneuver produces a palpable posterior dislocation, and the Ortolani maneuver reduces it with a clunk. Which is the most appropriate initial management at this age?", "options": {"A": "Open reduction with femoral shortening osteotomy to restore stable concentric hip alignment", "B": "Pavlik harness maintaining the hips flexed and abducted to keep the femoral head reduced", "C": "Pelvic osteotomy to improve acetabular coverage of the femoral head at this infant's age", "D": "Closed reduction under general anesthesia followed by application of a hip spica cast", "E": "Triple diapers with abduction positioning and reexamination at six months for spontaneous maturation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pavlik harness maintaining the hips flexed and abducted to keep the femoral head reduced): In infants under six months with a dislocatable or reducible hip, the Pavlik harness holds the hips in flexion and abduction and stimulates normal acetabular development. Spica casting and surgery are reserved for failure of harness treatment or later presentation. Triple diapers are not reliable treatment."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 38-year-old woman presents with episodic headaches, palpitations, and drenching sweats. Blood pressure is 190/110 mm Hg, and plasma free metanephrines are markedly elevated. CT shows a 4 cm right adrenal mass. Before surgical resection, which initial medication is most appropriate to start?", "options": {"A": "Clonidine, a central alpha-2 agonist, started to reduce sympathetic outflow from the brainstem", "B": "Hydrochlorothiazide, a thiazide diuretic, started to lower blood pressure by reducing circulating plasma volume", "C": "Propranolol, a nonselective beta blocker, started first to control tachycardia and palpitations before other agents", "D": "Phenoxybenzamine, a nonselective alpha blocker, started days before surgery to control vasoconstriction and volume contraction", "E": "Octreotide, a somatostatin analog, started to suppress catecholamine release from the chromaffin cell tumor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Phenoxybenzamine, a nonselective alpha blocker, started days before surgery to control vasoconstriction and volume contraction): Catecholamine excess causes intense alpha-mediated vasoconstriction and chronic volume contraction, so an irreversible alpha blocker is started days before surgery and followed by liberal salt and fluid intake. A beta blocker may be added only after alpha blockade is established, because beta blockade alone leaves alpha stimulation unopposed and can precipitate a hypertensive crisis. Diuretics would deepen the volume depletion."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 31-year-old man with schizophrenia was started on haloperidol decanoate three days ago. He now has lead pipe rigidity, temperature 40.1 C, heart rate 124, fluctuating blood pressure, confusion, and creatine kinase 12,000 U/L. Reflexes are normal. He takes no other medications. Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Serotonin syndrome from excess serotonergic stimulation with tremor and rapid onset", "B": "Malignant hyperthermia from abnormal skeletal muscle calcium release triggered by exposure to certain agents", "C": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome from central dopamine receptor blockade with hypothalamic and muscle dysfunction", "D": "Anticholinergic toxicity with dry skin, mydriasis, urinary retention, and agitation after dopamine blockade", "E": "Acute dystonic reaction with sustained involuntary contraction of muscles after dopamine antagonist exposure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Neuroleptic malignant syndrome from central dopamine receptor blockade with hypothalamic and muscle dysfunction): Lead pipe rigidity, high fever, autonomic instability, altered mental status, and a markedly elevated creatine kinase after starting a high potency antipsychotic define neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Serotonin syndrome gives hyperreflexia and clonus, and a simple dystonic reaction lacks systemic features."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 26-year-old man has recurrent perianal abscesses and a draining fistula, along with eight months of crampy right lower quadrant pain, intermittent diarrhea, and a 6 kg weight loss. He smokes cigarettes. Colonoscopy would most likely demonstrate which underlying process?", "options": {"A": "Continuous mucosal inflammation starting at the rectum with crypt abscesses", "B": "Ileocecal caseating granulomas with Langhans giant cells from mycobacterial infection", "C": "Segmental mucosal ulceration of the watershed colon from low flow ischemia", "D": "Discontinuous transmural inflammation with noncaseating granulomas involving the terminal ileum", "E": "Pseudomembranes of adherent fibrin and neutrophils on a background of recent antibiotic use"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discontinuous transmural inflammation with noncaseating granulomas involving the terminal ileum): Perianal fistulas with right lower quadrant pain, weight loss, and smoking suggest Crohn disease, which causes skip lesions with transmural inflammation and noncaseating granulomas, often in the terminal ileum. Ulcerative colitis is continuous from the rectum and seldom forms fistulas. Tuberculosis has caseation, and ischemic and pseudomembranous colitis do not fit the history."}, {"id": 689, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 41-year-old nurse working night shifts has left buttock and groin discomfort. On examination the standing flexion test is positive on the left, the left ASIS is inferior to the right, and the left PSIS is superior to the right. Which innominate dysfunction explains these landmarks, and how is apparent leg length affected?", "options": {"A": "Left superior innominate shear, producing a true shortening of the left leg", "B": "Left anterior innominate rotation, producing an apparent shortening of the left leg", "C": "Left anterior innominate rotation, producing an apparent lengthening of the left leg", "D": "Left posterior innominate rotation, producing an apparent lengthening of the left leg", "E": "Left posterior innominate rotation, producing an apparent shortening of the left leg"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Left anterior innominate rotation, producing an apparent lengthening of the left leg): Anterior rotation of the left innominate brings the ASIS down and the PSIS up, and the acetabulum moves inferiorly, so the left leg appears longer. Posterior rotation would raise the ASIS and lower the PSIS with an apparent shortening. A superior shear moves both landmarks upward rather than in opposite directions."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has right low back pain after prolonged driving. The seated flexion test is positive on the right, the right sacral sulcus is deeper than the left, and the right inferior lateral angle is posterior and inferior. Lumbar lordosis is normal, and neurologic examination is normal. Which sacral diagnosis is correct?", "options": {"A": "Bilateral sacral extension, in which both bases move posteriorly together about the middle transverse axis", "B": "Left on left forward sacral torsion, in which rotation occurs about a left oblique axis", "C": "Right unilateral sacral flexion, in which the sacral base moves anteriorly on that side", "D": "Left unilateral sacral flexion, in which the sacral base moves anteriorly on that side", "E": "Right unilateral sacral extension, in which the sacral base moves posteriorly on that side"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right unilateral sacral flexion, in which the sacral base moves anteriorly on that side): A positive seated flexion test with a deep sulcus and a posterior, inferior inferior lateral angle on the same side indicates a unilateral sacral flexion on that side. Extension would produce a shallow sulcus, and torsions or bilateral patterns do not fit findings confined to one side."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old man has had eight months of low back pain with morning stiffness lasting over an hour that improves with exercise and worsens with rest. Ibuprofen gives partial relief. He has no rash, nail changes, diarrhea, urethritis, or recent infection. Chest expansion is reduced, and pelvic radiographs show bilateral sacroiliac erosions with sclerosis. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Psoriatic arthritis, an inflammatory spondyloarthropathy that can involve the sacroiliac joints with asymmetric patterns", "B": "Reactive arthritis, a sterile asymmetric joint inflammation following enteric or genitourinary infection", "C": "Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis, a flowing ossification of the anterior spine in older adults", "D": "Enteropathic arthritis, a spondyloarthropathy accompanying inflammatory bowel disease and often tracking its flares", "E": "Ankylosing spondylitis, an HLA-B27 associated enthesitis centered inflammation of the axial skeleton"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ankylosing spondylitis, an HLA-B27 associated enthesitis centered inflammation of the axial skeleton): Inflammatory back pain that improves with exercise, reduced chest expansion and bilateral sacroiliitis in a young man indicate ankylosing spondylitis. Psoriatic, reactive and enteropathic arthritis can cause sacroiliitis but the skin, nail, infectious and bowel clues are absent here. Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis affects older adults and spares the sacroiliac joints."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 38-year-old woman had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass three years ago and stopped taking her multivitamin. She now has fatigue and brittle nails. Hemoglobin is 9.4 g/dL, MCV is 72 fL, and ferritin is low. Vitamin B12 is normal. Which mechanism best explains her anemia?", "options": {"A": "Loss of intrinsic factor secretion by the excluded stomach limiting ileal cobalamin absorption and erythropoiesis", "B": "Copper deficiency from impaired absorption in the duodenum, reducing ceruloplasmin dependent iron mobilization", "C": "Exclusion of duodenum and proximal jejunum from food flow, plus low acid, reduces iron uptake", "D": "Chronic inflammation raising hepcidin and trapping iron within macrophages despite adequate dietary absorption", "E": "Impaired folate absorption in the proximal jejunum producing ineffective DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Exclusion of duodenum and proximal jejunum from food flow, plus low acid, reduces iron uptake): Dietary nonheme iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, in an acidic environment that keeps it in the ferrous form, and both conditions are reduced after Roux-en-Y bypass. Low ferritin with microcytosis indicates true iron deficiency, not anemia of inflammation. Vitamin B12 deficiency would cause macrocytosis and is excluded by the normal level."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 3-year-old boy has had four long-bone fractures after minor falls. Examination shows blue-gray sclerae, dental discoloration, and joint hypermobility. Child protective evaluation is unrevealing. Serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase are normal, and the father had early hearing loss. Which molecular defect best accounts for his findings?", "options": {"A": "Mutation in FGFR3 causing constitutive activation that inhibits growth plate chondrocyte proliferation", "B": "Mutation in fibrillin-1 weakening elastic microfibrils in the aorta and suspensory ligament", "C": "Mutation in COL3A1 impairing type III collagen of vessel walls and hollow organs", "D": "Mutation in COL1A1 or COL1A2 reducing the quantity or quality of type I collagen", "E": "Mutation in COL2A1 disrupting type II collagen of cartilage and the vitreous"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mutation in COL1A1 or COL1A2 reducing the quantity or quality of type I collagen): Recurrent low-impact fractures with blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta and early hearing loss define osteogenesis imperfecta, most often from COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations. Normal mineral chemistry rules out rickets or hypophosphatasia. Fibrillin and type III collagen defects cause Marfan and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, which do not feature recurrent fractures."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 19-year-old college student is admitted with fever, petechial rash and meningococcal bacteremia. He had a similar admission for meningococcal meningitis two years ago. Immunizations are up to date, white cell count is normal and immunoglobulin levels are normal. Which immune defect most likely explains the recurrent infections?", "options": {"A": "Deficiency of terminal complement components, which prevents assembly of the membrane attack complex on Neisseria", "B": "Deficiency of C3, causing severe recurrent pyogenic infections from failed opsonization and alternative pathway activation", "C": "Deficiency of decay accelerating factor, causing complement mediated lysis of red cells and nocturnal hemoglobinuria", "D": "Deficiency of C1 esterase inhibitor, causing unchecked bradykinin generation and recurrent episodes of angioedema", "E": "Deficiency of early classical components such as C4, producing immune complex disease and lupus-like illness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Deficiency of terminal complement components, which prevents assembly of the membrane attack complex on Neisseria): Recurrent Neisseria infections with normal antibody levels and white cell counts point to a terminal complement defect, since the membrane attack complex made of C5 through C9 is the main defense against these organisms. Early component deficiencies cause lupus-like immune complex disease, C1 inhibitor deficiency causes angioedema, and a broad C3 deficiency produces many pyogenic infections."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension has abrupt tearing chest pain radiating to the back. Blood pressure is 190/105 mm Hg in the right arm and 150/80 mm Hg in the left arm, and heart rate is 104/min. Chest radiography shows a widened mediastinum. CT angiography is being arranged. Which is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic therapy?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous sodium nitroprusside alone to rapidly lower systemic vascular resistance and arterial pressure", "B": "Intravenous heparin infusion to prevent thrombosis within the false lumen of the aortic wall", "C": "Intravenous esmolol to lower heart rate and the rate of rise of aortic pressure", "D": "Intravenous alteplase to dissolve possible coronary thrombus and restore myocardial perfusion pressure", "E": "Intravenous hydralazine to relax arteriolar smooth muscle and reduce the afterload on the ventricle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravenous esmolol to lower heart rate and the rate of rise of aortic pressure): Tearing pain, an arm pressure difference, and a widened mediastinum suggest aortic dissection, where the goal is to reduce shear stress by lowering heart rate and the rate of pressure rise. A beta blocker such as esmolol comes first, and a vasodilator is added only after rate control. Thrombolytics and heparin risk catastrophic bleeding."}, {"id": 696, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 5-day-old exclusively breastfed infant born at home has bloody oozing from the umbilical stump and melena. Prothrombin time is prolonged, partial thromboplastin time is prolonged, and platelet count and fibrinogen are normal. No injection was given after delivery. Which post-translational process is deficient in this infant?", "options": {"A": "Gamma carboxylation of glutamate residues in clotting factors using a reduced vitamin cofactor form", "B": "Cross linking of fibrin strands by factor XIII using calcium as a necessary cofactor", "C": "Glycosylation of von Willebrand factor multimers inside endothelial cell Golgi compartments before storage granules form", "D": "Cleavage of fibrinogen to fibrin monomers by thrombin within the common pathway of coagulation", "E": "Hydroxylation of proline residues in collagen requiring ascorbic acid as an essential enzyme cofactor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gamma carboxylation of glutamate residues in clotting factors using a reduced vitamin cofactor form): Newborns have sterile guts and little stored vitamin K, so factors II, VII, IX, and X are not gamma carboxylated, prolonging both PT and PTT. Defective factor XIII cross linking gives normal clotting times, and von Willebrand defects spare the PT."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 47-year-old man receives amphotericin B for disseminated candidiasis. By day 10 he has weakness and muscle cramps. Laboratory studies show potassium 2.9 mEq/L, magnesium 1.2 mg/dL, bicarbonate 17 mEq/L, and a normal anion gap. Urine pH is persistently above 6. Which mechanism best explains these electrolyte abnormalities?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of the loop cotransporter causing renal loss of calcium and magnesium with alkalosis", "B": "Osmotic diuresis from nonabsorbed tubular solute producing potassium loss and a hyperchloremic acidosis", "C": "Aldosterone deficiency impairing sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in the collecting duct with acidosis", "D": "Proximal tubular bicarbonate wasting from carbonic anhydrase inhibition producing hypokalemia and a hyperchloremic acidosis", "E": "Pore formation in distal tubular membranes causing proton back leak with potassium and magnesium wasting"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Pore formation in distal tubular membranes causing proton back leak with potassium and magnesium wasting): Amphotericin B forms pores in tubular membranes, which allows protons to leak back and causes a distal renal tubular acidosis with an inappropriately high urine pH. The same membrane leak wastes potassium and magnesium, giving a normal anion gap acidosis. Aldosterone deficiency would raise potassium instead of lowering it."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 44-year-old man with type 2 diabetes says he knows he should lose weight but nothing ever works for me. He agrees his A1c is high yet has missed two visits. The physician wants to use motivational interviewing. Which response best applies the technique?", "options": {"A": "Many patients succeed on a low carbohydrate diet, so I will refer you to a dietitian", "B": "I will list weight loss methods that worked for other patients so you can pick one", "C": "Missing two visits suggests low motivation, so I will schedule weekly appointments to monitor your progress", "D": "You want better control, yet past attempts felt discouraging; what would make a change worth trying?", "E": "You must lose weight now, because uncontrolled diabetes will damage your kidneys, eyes and nerves soon"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (You want better control, yet past attempts felt discouraging; what would make a change worth trying?): Motivational interviewing uses reflective listening and open questions to draw out the patient's own reasons for change and explore ambivalence, here by naming both sides and inviting him to consider what would help. Directive warnings, advice lists and labeling his motivation as low confront or bypass his ambivalence and tend to increase resistance."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 64-year-old man who fishes for a living has a 7-mm pearly papule with rolled borders, fine telangiectasias, and a central crusted ulcer on the side of his nose that has enlarged slowly over a year. Biopsy shows palisading basaloid cells. Which molecular alteration most likely underlies this tumor?", "options": {"A": "Loss of CDKN2A function removing inhibition of cyclin-dependent kinases four and six", "B": "Gain-of-function BRAF V600E mutation producing constitutive activation of the MAPK pathway", "C": "Loss of PTCH1 function causing unchecked signaling through the hedgehog pathway", "D": "Inactivating mutation of TP53 impairing cell cycle arrest after ultraviolet induced DNA damage", "E": "Amplification of the KIT receptor tyrosine kinase driving mucosal and acral proliferation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Loss of PTCH1 function causing unchecked signaling through the hedgehog pathway): A pearly papule with rolled borders and telangiectasia with palisading basaloid cells is basal cell carcinoma. These tumors arise from loss of PTCH1 (or activating SMOH mutations), leading to unrestrained hedgehog signaling. BRAF, CDKN2A and KIT alterations belong mainly to melanoma, and TP53 mutations are more typical of squamous cell carcinoma."}, {"id": 700, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 52-year-old man with severe disseminated coccidioidomycosis receives intravenous amphotericin B. By the fifth day his creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 1.9 mg/dL, potassium is 2.9 mEq/L, and magnesium is 1.2 mg/dL. He also had chills during the first infusion. Which mechanism best explains the electrolyte findings?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of the sodium potassium chloride cotransporter in the thick ascending limb of the loop", "B": "Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, causing bicarbonate and potassium loss in the urine", "C": "Pore formation in distal tubular cell membranes, which leaks potassium and magnesium into the urine", "D": "Excess aldosterone effect in the collecting duct, which promotes sodium retention and potassium secretion", "E": "Inhibition of the sodium chloride cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule, causing urinary potassium wasting"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pore formation in distal tubular cell membranes, which leaks potassium and magnesium into the urine): Amphotericin B binds sterols and forms pores, and in the distal nephron this causes tubular leak of potassium and magnesium along with renal vasoconstriction and a rise in creatinine. The findings mimic a diuretic effect, but the drug has no transporter target. Chills at the first infusion reflect cytokine release, a separate infusion reaction."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 28-year-old woman with recurrent nocturnal cough and wheezing undergoes spirometry. Her FEV1 improves from 68 to 84 percent of predicted after inhaled albuterol, as shown in the flow-volume curves. The albuterol relaxes airway smooth muscle through a signaling pathway beginning at a specific membrane receptor. Which receptor-mediated effect accounts for this bronchodilation?", "options": {"A": "Muscarinic M3 receptor blockade preventing acetylcholine-driven phospholipase C activation and calcium release", "B": "Beta-1 adrenergic receptor stimulation increasing heart rate and relaxing bronchial smooth muscle", "C": "Adenosine receptor antagonism and phosphodiesterase inhibition raising cyclic AMP in airway smooth muscle", "D": "Cysteinyl leukotriene receptor blockade preventing leukotriene D4 induced bronchoconstriction and mucus secretion", "E": "Beta-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation raising cyclic AMP and inactivating myosin light chain kinase"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Beta-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation raising cyclic AMP and inactivating myosin light chain kinase): Albuterol is a selective beta-2 agonist whose Gs-coupled receptor raises cyclic AMP, activating protein kinase A and inhibiting myosin light chain kinase, so airway smooth muscle relaxes. Anticholinergics, methylxanthines and leukotriene antagonists act through different targets. The reversible obstruction on spirometry fits asthma.", "image": "question_images/q_701.png"}, {"id": 702, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 3-year-old boy is brought to the clinic after two episodes of painless, brick-red stools. He is afebrile with a soft, nontender abdomen and normal bowel sounds. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL. A technetium pertechnetate scan shows focal uptake in the right lower quadrant. Which developmental abnormality most likely accounts for this bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Juvenile hamartomatous polyp of the rectosigmoid colon with surface ulceration and granulation tissue", "B": "Ileal outpouching from persistent vitelline duct tissue containing ectopic gastric mucosa that secretes acid", "C": "Lead point in the terminal ileum causing intussusception with vascular compromise of the bowel wall", "D": "Angiodysplasia of the cecum with dilated submucosal vessels and fragile overlying mucosa", "E": "Aganglionosis of the distal colon causing enterocolitis with mucosal sloughing and bleeding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ileal outpouching from persistent vitelline duct tissue containing ectopic gastric mucosa that secretes acid): Painless brick-red hematochezia in a toddler with a technetium-avid focus in the right lower quadrant points to a Meckel diverticulum, a vitelline duct remnant whose ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid and ulcerates the adjacent ileum. Intussusception presents with colicky pain and currant jelly stool, and juvenile polyps would not take up the isotope."}, {"id": 703, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 19-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after falling from his bicycle without a helmet. He lost consciousness briefly, then felt well for two hours before developing severe headache and vomiting. He is now drowsy, and the right pupil is dilated and sluggish. A noncontrast head CT is shown. Which diagnosis best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Epidural hematoma from a torn middle meningeal artery beneath a temporal bone fracture", "B": "Subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured saccular aneurysm at the circle of Willis base", "C": "Subdural hematoma from torn bridging veins crossing the subdural space after acceleration injury", "D": "Diffuse axonal injury from rotational shearing of white matter tracts at the gray-white junction", "E": "Intraparenchymal hemorrhage from rupture of hypertensive lenticulostriate arteries in the basal ganglia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Epidural hematoma from a torn middle meningeal artery beneath a temporal bone fracture): A brief loss of consciousness, a lucid interval, and then rapid deterioration with a blown pupil point to arterial bleeding between the skull and dura, classically from the middle meningeal artery under the thin pterion. A subdural bleed is venous, usually develops more slowly, and crosses suture lines. Subarachnoid and basal ganglia hemorrhages do not fit a young man with head trauma and a lucid interval.", "image": "question_images/q_703.png"}, {"id": 704, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with acute neck pain after a car accident cannot tolerate forceful treatment. The physician finds a small tender area in the anterior cervical muscles, positions her head into flexion toward the greatest comfort, holds it for roughly ninety seconds until tenderness fades, then slowly returns her to neutral. Which technique was used?", "options": {"A": "Myofascial release, a method that engages fascial barriers with sustained load until a release occurs", "B": "Facilitated positional release, an indirect method that adds compression to the tissue while positioned in ease", "C": "Counterstrain, an indirect method that places tissue in ease to quiet abnormal muscle spindle input", "D": "Functional technique, an indirect method that follows palpated tissue motion toward ease without a fixed hold", "E": "Muscle energy, a direct method using a patient activated isometric contraction against physician resistance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Counterstrain, an indirect method that places tissue in ease to quiet abnormal muscle spindle input): Locating a tender point and holding the positioned tissue in ease for about ninety seconds describes counterstrain. Functional technique and facilitated positional release are also indirect but use dynamic monitoring or compression. Muscle energy depends on patient effort against resistance, which this patient was not asked to give."}, {"id": 705, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A drug has a volume of distribution of 40 L and a clearance of 4 L/hr. A physician plans a continuous infusion in a 68-year-old hospitalized man. About how long will it take to reach approximately 94 percent of the final steady-state concentration?", "options": {"A": "About 7 hours, since one half-life brings the concentration to the plateau", "B": "About 28 hours, which equals four half-lives of roughly seven hours each", "C": "About 55 hours, which equals eight half-lives of roughly seven hours each", "D": "About 10 hours, calculated as the volume of distribution divided by the clearance", "E": "About 14 hours, which equals two half-lives of roughly seven hours each"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 28 hours, which equals four half-lives of roughly seven hours each): The half-life equals 0.693 times volume divided by clearance, or about 7 hours. Each half-life closes half of the remaining gap to steady state, so four half-lives reach about 94 percent. One half-life gives only 50 percent, and the ratio of volume to clearance is the time constant, not the half-life."}, {"id": 706, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with a history of recurrent urinary tract infections and a neurogenic bladder presents with flank pain and fever. Urine pH is 8.2. Abdominal radiograph shows a large radiopaque branching stone filling the renal pelvis and calyces. Cultures grow a gram-negative rod that swarms on agar. Which process explains the stone composition?", "options": {"A": "Absorptive hypercalciuria raises urinary calcium, forming calcium phosphate stones in the setting of hyperparathyroidism", "B": "Acidic urine with high purine load promotes uric acid crystals that appear radiolucent on radiographs", "C": "Enteric hyperoxaluria increases urinary oxalate, forming calcium oxalate crystals in acidic urine", "D": "Bacterial urease hydrolyzes urea to ammonia, alkalinizing urine and precipitating magnesium ammonium phosphate", "E": "Excess urinary cystine from defective proximal tubular reabsorption forms hexagonal crystals and faintly radiopaque stones"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bacterial urease hydrolyzes urea to ammonia, alkalinizing urine and precipitating magnesium ammonium phosphate): A urea splitting organism such as Proteus, which swarms on agar, produces ammonia, raising urine pH and precipitating struvite, which grows into staghorn calculi. Uric acid stones need acidic urine and are radiolucent. Calcium oxalate and cystine stones are not driven by infection."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 44-year-old woman develops severe burning pain in her right hand six weeks after a nondisplaced distal radius fracture treated in a cast. Light touch of the skin is painful, and the hand is swollen, cooler, and mottled compared with the left. Nerve conduction studies and radiographs are unremarkable. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Raynaud phenomenon, a vasospastic disorder with episodic color change triggered by cold exposure", "B": "Carpal tunnel syndrome, a median nerve compression from swelling inside the cast after fracture", "C": "Fibromyalgia, a central sensitization disorder with diffuse tenderness and fatigue after physical trauma", "D": "Postherpetic neuralgia, a persistent dermatomal burning pain following a prior varicella zoster outbreak", "E": "Complex regional pain syndrome type I, a sympathetically maintained disorder following minor limb trauma"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Complex regional pain syndrome type I, a sympathetically maintained disorder following minor limb trauma): Disproportionate burning pain, allodynia, swelling, and temperature and color change after minor limb trauma, with normal nerve studies and imaging, define complex regional pain syndrome type I. Carpal tunnel syndrome follows median nerve distribution with abnormal studies. Fibromyalgia is diffuse, and Raynaud phenomenon is episodic and cold triggered."}, {"id": 708, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 24-year-old primigravida at 40 weeks gestation has regular contractions every two minutes. Examination shows the cervix completely dilated and effaced, the fetal head at plus two station, and an involuntary urge to push. Fetal heart tones are reassuring. Which stage of labor is she in, and what defines its endpoint?", "options": {"A": "First stage, which ends when the cervix reaches full dilation and the head descends", "B": "Latent phase of the first stage, which ends when contractions become regular and painful", "C": "Second stage, which ends with delivery of the infant after full cervical dilation", "D": "Fourth stage, which ends about one hour after placental delivery once bleeding is controlled", "E": "Third stage, which ends with expulsion of the placenta and fetal membranes after delivery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Second stage, which ends with delivery of the infant after full cervical dilation): The second stage begins at complete cervical dilation and ends with delivery of the infant, and pushing and descent occur during it. The first stage ended at full dilation, the third stage covers delivery of the placenta, and the immediate postpartum recovery period follows."}, {"id": 709, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 40-year-old man has a single thoracic vertebra, T7, that is held in a flexed position, with the segments above and below moving normally. A medical student asks which Fryette type applies and how sidebending and rotation are coupled at that segment. Which answer is correct?", "options": {"A": "Type II, a non-neutral vertebra whose sidebending and rotation couple toward the same side", "B": "Type II, a neutral group curve with sidebending and rotation toward the same side", "C": "Type III, motion introduced in one plane that then modifies motion in other planes", "D": "Type I, a flexed single segment with sidebending and rotation toward opposite sides", "E": "Type I, a neutral group curve with sidebending and rotation toward opposite sides of the spine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Type II, a non-neutral vertebra whose sidebending and rotation couple toward the same side): A single vertebra held in flexion or extension follows type II mechanics, with sidebending and rotation to the same side. Type I applies to neutral group curves in which sidebending and rotation occur to opposite sides, and type III describes motion introduced in one plane modifying the others."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 52-year-old man in the intensive care unit has a functioning gut but is being considered for either enteral or parenteral feeding after major trauma. His team favors early enteral feeding. Which physiologic effect of luminal nutrients supports this choice over prolonged parenteral nutrition?", "options": {"A": "Gut contents suppress insulin release, lowering the risk of hyperglycemia and hepatic fat accumulation", "B": "Gut contents lower serum triglycerides by increasing hepatic lipoprotein lipase activity during critical illness", "C": "Gut contents eliminate the need for vitamin and trace element supplementation during critical illness", "D": "Gut contents sustain epithelial growth and barrier function, limiting bacterial translocation across the mucosa", "E": "Gut contents bypass hepatic first pass metabolism, delivering amino acids directly to the systemic circulation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gut contents sustain epithelial growth and barrier function, limiting bacterial translocation across the mucosa): Enteral nutrition keeps enterocytes supplied with luminal fuel such as glutamine and stimulates trophic hormones, so villi and tight junctions are preserved. Prolonged parenteral feeding is associated with villous atrophy and greater bacterial translocation. Enteral feeding still requires attention to micronutrients, and the parenteral route, not the enteral one, bypasses the liver."}, {"id": 711, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with a large goiter has noisy breathing and inspiratory stridor. Spirometry shows reduced peak inspiratory flow, with a flattened inspiratory limb on the flow-volume loop, while the expiratory limb is largely preserved. Her smoking history is minimal. At which site and type of obstruction does this pattern most closely point?", "options": {"A": "Fixed central obstruction, where a rigid narrowing flattens both inspiratory and expiratory limbs equally", "B": "Variable intrathoracic obstruction, where positive pleural pressure on expiration narrows the airway", "C": "Restrictive lung disease, where reduced volumes narrow the loop but preserve its normal shape", "D": "Peripheral small airway obstruction, where early airway collapse scoops the expiratory limb of the loop", "E": "Variable extrathoracic obstruction, where negative airway pressure on inspiration collapses the narrowed airway segment"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Variable extrathoracic obstruction, where negative airway pressure on inspiration collapses the narrowed airway segment): Above the thoracic inlet, airway pressure on inspiration falls below the surrounding atmospheric pressure, so a compressible narrowed segment collapses during inhalation. This flattens the inspiratory limb only. Intrathoracic variable lesions flatten the expiratory limb, and fixed lesions flatten both."}, {"id": 712, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 26-year-old woman of Southeast Asian ancestry has mild asymptomatic anemia found on routine screening. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL, mean corpuscular volume is 62 fL, and the red cell count is 5.8 million per microliter. Ferritin is normal, and hemoglobin electrophoresis shows normal hemoglobin A2 and F. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Sideroblastic anemia with ring sideroblasts and marrow iron overload", "B": "Beta thalassemia minor with elevated hemoglobin A2 from reduced beta chain synthesis", "C": "Hemoglobin C trait with target cells and a distinct electrophoretic band", "D": "Iron deficiency anemia with depleted marrow stores and a low serum ferritin level", "E": "Alpha thalassemia trait from deletion of two alpha globin genes"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Alpha thalassemia trait from deletion of two alpha globin genes): Microcytosis with a high red cell count, a Mentzer index under 13 (62 divided by 5.8 is about 10.7), normal ferritin, and normal electrophoresis suggests alpha thalassemia trait. Beta thalassemia minor raises hemoglobin A2, and iron deficiency lowers ferritin. Hemoglobin C would show a variant band."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 27-year-old woman at 7 weeks of gestation has newly diagnosed Graves disease with a free T4 twice the upper limit of normal and a resting pulse of 108/min. She has no eye disease and no history of liver disease. Which antithyroid drug strategy is most appropriate at this stage of pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Beta blocker alone with no antithyroid drug, since fetal exposure to thionamides is considered unsafe", "B": "Immediate total thyroidectomy in the first trimester, with lifelong levothyroxine started after the operation", "C": "Radioactive iodine ablation now, followed by levothyroxine replacement once the thyroid becomes underactive", "D": "Propylthiouracil, chosen to avoid first-trimester methimazole embryopathy, with a planned switch to methimazole afterward", "E": "Methimazole at standard dose, because it has greater efficacy and lower hepatotoxicity than the alternative"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Propylthiouracil, chosen to avoid first-trimester methimazole embryopathy, with a planned switch to methimazole afterward): Methimazole is associated with a recognizable embryopathy in early pregnancy, so propylthiouracil is preferred in the first trimester. Because propylthiouracil carries a risk of severe liver injury, therapy is usually changed to methimazole after organogenesis. Radioiodine is absolutely avoided in pregnancy."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 27-year-old woman is found unresponsive in a restroom with a needle nearby. Respirations are 5 per minute, pupils are 1 mm and oxygen saturation is 82 percent on room air. After bag-mask ventilation, naloxone is given and she awakens, then becomes sleepy again 50 minutes later. What explains the relapse in sedation?", "options": {"A": "Naloxone is a partial agonist whose own sedating effect emerges as the antagonist effect wanes", "B": "Hypoxic brain injury from the earlier respiratory depression produces delayed and progressive encephalopathy", "C": "Naloxone has a shorter duration of action than the opioid, allowing renewed receptor activation", "D": "Precipitated withdrawal from naloxone causes a rebound hypersomnolent state once autonomic symptoms resolve", "E": "Co-ingested benzodiazepine unmasked after naloxone reversal, since naloxone also antagonizes gamma-aminobutyric acid receptors"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Naloxone has a shorter duration of action than the opioid, allowing renewed receptor activation): Naloxone is a competitive mu receptor antagonist with a duration of roughly one hour, shorter than many opioids, so sedation and respiratory depression can return and the patient needs observation or an infusion. Withdrawal would produce agitation, vomiting and diarrhea instead, and naloxone does not act at GABA receptors."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 17-year-old girl taking an oral contraceptive reports sudden pleuritic chest pain after a long drive. Heart rate is 104/min, oxygen saturation is 94 percent on room air, and she has no leg swelling, hemoptysis, or prior venous thromboembolism. Clinical assessment gives a low pretest probability for pulmonary embolism. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Order CT pulmonary angiography immediately, since imaging is the first test for every suspected embolism", "B": "Order a D-dimer assay, since a negative result safely rules out clot at low probability", "C": "Begin therapeutic anticoagulation empirically and defer testing until the pleuritic pain has resolved", "D": "Obtain leg compression ultrasonography first, since most emboli arise from the deep veins of the legs", "E": "Order ventilation perfusion scintigraphy first, since contrast studies are avoided in adolescents and women"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Order a D-dimer assay, since a negative result safely rules out clot at low probability): In a low pretest probability patient, a highly sensitive D-dimer is the right first step because a negative result effectively excludes embolism and spares imaging. A positive result would then lead to CT pulmonary angiography. Starting heparin or obtaining a leg ultrasound first reverses the sensible order for this patient."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 44-year-old woman describes a sudden explosive headache, the worst of her life, that began three days ago while she was lifting groceries. It has partly eased, but she now has mild neck stiffness and vomiting. She is alert with a nonfocal neurologic examination. A noncontrast head CT performed today is read as normal. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the noncontrast head CT in twenty-four hours because the first scan was negative", "B": "Lumbar puncture with spectrophotometric analysis of centrifuged cerebrospinal fluid for bilirubin pigment", "C": "Empiric triptan therapy for presumed migraine with discharge and outpatient neurology follow-up", "D": "Carotid duplex ultrasonography to evaluate for cervical artery dissection as the source of headache", "E": "MRI of the brain with diffusion weighting to identify early ischemic injury in the cortex"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lumbar puncture with spectrophotometric analysis of centrifuged cerebrospinal fluid for bilirubin pigment): Sensitivity of CT for subarachnoid blood falls over days as blood is cleared, so a normal scan three days after a thunderclap onset does not exclude hemorrhage. Lumbar puncture detects xanthochromia, which appears hours after bleeding and persists for weeks. Triptans are contraindicated in this setting, and repeating the same scan adds little."}, {"id": 717, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 26-year-old man has sharp substernal chest pain for two days that improves when he leans forward and worsens when he lies flat. He had an upper respiratory infection last week. Temperature is 37.9 C. His electrocardiogram is shown. Troponin is normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Brugada pattern, a sodium channel disorder with coved ST elevation in the right precordial leads", "B": "Acute anterior myocardial infarction from proximal left anterior descending occlusion with reciprocal changes", "C": "Early repolarization, a benign variant with notched J point elevation in the lateral leads", "D": "Acute pericarditis with diffuse epicardial inflammation producing concave ST elevation and PR depression", "E": "Viral myocarditis with myocyte necrosis causing focal ST elevation and a markedly raised troponin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute pericarditis with diffuse epicardial inflammation producing concave ST elevation and PR depression): Positional pleuritic pain after a viral illness with diffuse concave ST elevation and PR depression is acute pericarditis. Early repolarization lacks PR depression and the typical pain. A myocardial infarction would be territorial with reciprocal changes, and myocarditis would raise troponin.", "image": "question_images/q_717.png"}, {"id": 718, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 61-year-old man is resuscitated after cardiac arrest during a coronary bypass operation with prolonged hypotension. Two days later he has weakness of both shoulders and thighs with relatively preserved hands and feet, and difficulty judging distances. Carotid duplex shows no stenosis. Which mechanism best explains the weakness?", "options": {"A": "Hypoperfusion infarcts in border zones between anterior, middle and posterior cerebral artery territories", "B": "Lacunar infarcts of bilateral internal capsules from hypertensive small vessel disease", "C": "Basilar artery occlusion causing bilateral pontine infarction with quadriparesis and facial weakness", "D": "Anterior spinal artery infarction at the cervical level sparing posterior column function", "E": "Bilateral emboli to the distal middle cerebral arteries from the bypass circuit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hypoperfusion infarcts in border zones between anterior, middle and posterior cerebral artery territories): Global hypoperfusion injures end-zone cortex where distal branches of adjacent arteries meet. The anterior to middle junction produces proximal arm and leg weakness (man in a barrel), and the middle to posterior junction produces visuospatial and distance judgment problems. Embolic strokes follow a single territory and are rarely bilateral and symmetric."}, {"id": 719, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 26-year-old woman has sudden palpitations and lightheadedness that began 40 minutes ago. Blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. The monitor shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 190 beats per minute without visible P waves. A modified Valsalva maneuver fails to terminate it. Which treatment is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Synchronized cardioversion, because a rate near 190 per minute requires immediate electrical termination", "B": "Rapid intravenous adenosine, transiently blocking atrioventricular nodal conduction to interrupt the reentrant circuit", "C": "Intravenous digoxin loading, slowing atrioventricular conduction through increased vagal tone over several hours", "D": "Intravenous amiodarone, blocking potassium channels to terminate the polymorphic ventricular arrhythmia", "E": "Intravenous procainamide, slowing conduction in the accessory pathway before attempting atrioventricular nodal blockade"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rapid intravenous adenosine, transiently blocking atrioventricular nodal conduction to interrupt the reentrant circuit): A regular narrow-complex tachycardia without P waves in a stable patient is most consistent with AV nodal reentry, and when vagal maneuvers fail the next step is rapid IV adenosine, which briefly blocks the AV node and breaks the circuit. Cardioversion is reserved for instability, and digoxin acts too slowly to terminate the rhythm."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 19-year-old tall man with arachnodactyly and an upward dislocated lens has chest discomfort. Echocardiography shows an aortic root diameter of 4.6 cm. His father died of aortic rupture at age 38. Which underlying defect most likely explains the dilation of his aortic root?", "options": {"A": "Cystathionine synthase deficiency causing homocysteine accumulation with downward lens dislocation and thrombosis", "B": "Type III collagen defect weakening arterial walls, with thin translucent skin and easy bruising", "C": "Elastin gene deletion producing diffuse vascular stenosis and supravalvular narrowing of the aorta", "D": "Fibrillin 1 mutation causing excess transforming growth factor beta signaling and medial degeneration", "E": "Type I collagen mutation causing brittle bones and blue sclerae with aortic root involvement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fibrillin 1 mutation causing excess transforming growth factor beta signaling and medial degeneration): Tall habitus, arachnodactyly, upward lens dislocation, and aortic root dilation define Marfan syndrome, caused by a fibrillin 1 mutation with excess transforming growth factor beta signaling. Vascular Ehlers Danlos involves type III collagen. Homocystinuria dislocates the lens downward and favors thrombosis, and the other defects do not produce this combination."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 45-year-old sandblaster with 20 years of exposure presents with exertional dyspnea. Chest radiograph shows multiple small nodules in the upper lobes and thin calcification around the rims of enlarged hilar lymph nodes. Alveolar macrophages ingesting the inhaled particles are damaged. Which infection is this patient at greatest increased risk of developing?", "options": {"A": "Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, because silica deposition depletes CD4 lymphocytes throughout the lung tissue", "B": "Aspergillus fumigatus invasive disease, because silica causes neutropenia and defective neutrophil oxidative killing", "C": "Histoplasma capsulatum infection, because dusty work environments expose workers to heavy inhalation of fungal spores", "D": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis, because damaged phagocytes fail to contain latent organisms and permit reactivation", "E": "Legionella pneumophila, because dust-laden airways favor survival of waterborne organisms inhaled from cooling systems"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mycobacterium tuberculosis, because damaged phagocytes fail to contain latent organisms and permit reactivation): Silica is toxic to alveolar macrophages, which die and release fibrogenic mediators, and the loss of effective phagocyte function raises the risk of tuberculosis several fold, including reactivation. Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes and upper lobe nodules are classic findings. Silicosis does not cause CD4 depletion, so Pneumocystis is not the expected opportunist."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 63-year-old woman with a history of breast cancer treated 6 years ago presents with 6 weeks of progressive thoracic back pain worse at night and unrelieved by rest. She has lost 4 kg. Examination shows percussion tenderness over T8. Neurologic examination is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and blood cultures, since vertebral osteomyelitis is suspected in older women", "B": "Six weeks of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and physical therapy before any imaging study", "C": "Bone density scan, since postmenopausal osteoporotic compression fracture is the most common cause", "D": "Plain radiographs of the thoracic spine alone, since they reliably exclude early marrow infiltration", "E": "Magnetic resonance imaging of the whole spine, since vertebral metastasis is the leading concern"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Magnetic resonance imaging of the whole spine, since vertebral metastasis is the leading concern): Night pain, weight loss, age, focal bony tenderness, and a prior malignancy are red flags for spinal metastasis. MRI of the entire spine is the best study because it shows marrow infiltration and other levels of involvement. Plain films miss early disease, and delaying imaging for conservative care is inappropriate."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with severe osteoporosis and a compression deformity of the lower thoracic spine has chronic mid back pain. She takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. Examination shows ropy, cool paraspinal tissue and a T8 type II dysfunction. She enjoys gardening. Which approach is the most appropriate initial treatment for her somatic dysfunction?", "options": {"A": "Muscle energy using maximal patient effort against strong physician counterforce while the trunk is rotated", "B": "Thrust technique at the restrictive barrier using a prone crossed pisiform contact over the transverse process", "C": "Articulatory technique using repetitive springing of the thoracic cage through the full restricted range of motion", "D": "Deep sustained prone pressure over the paraspinal muscles using a firm direct stretch against the barrier", "E": "Counterstrain using gentle passive positioning toward ease with no thrust or sustained leverage on the spine"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Counterstrain using gentle passive positioning toward ease with no thrust or sustained leverage on the spine): Severe osteoporosis and warfarin make thrust and strong direct forces unsafe, so a gentle indirect method such as counterstrain is the best starting choice. The strongest tempting wrong choice is forceful muscle energy, since it needs maximal effort and torque on fragile bone."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history has cough, constipation, and confusion. Serum calcium is 13.4 mg/dL, intact PTH is suppressed, and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is low. Chest imaging shows a central cavitating mass; biopsy shows keratin pearls. A bone scan shows no skeletal lesions. Which mechanism best explains the hypercalcemia?", "options": {"A": "Ectopic secretion of parathyroid hormone from the tumor, mimicking primary hyperparathyroidism physiology in bone", "B": "Tumor secretion of a peptide that activates the parathyroid hormone receptor in bone and kidney", "C": "Excess calcium and vitamin D intake with appropriately suppressed parathyroid gland secretion", "D": "Local osteolysis from widespread skeletal metastases releasing calcium directly from the bone matrix", "E": "Tumor expression of 1-alpha hydroxylase raising calcitriol levels and intestinal calcium absorption"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tumor secretion of a peptide that activates the parathyroid hormone receptor in bone and kidney): Squamous cell lung cancer commonly makes parathyroid hormone related peptide, which binds the PTH receptor and raises calcium while suppressing true PTH and calcitriol. A negative bone scan argues against osteolytic metastases, and a low calcitriol level argues against a calcitriol driven mechanism."}, {"id": 725, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 29-year-old athlete has a resting heart rate of 48 beats per minute. His physician reviews the parasympathetic supply to the heart while planning occipitomastoid suture decompression for a patient with a restricted cranial base. Which cranial nerve supplies the sinoatrial node and passes through the jugular foramen?", "options": {"A": "Facial nerve, which carries parasympathetic fibers to salivary glands and exits at the stylomastoid foramen", "B": "Accessory nerve, which supplies sternocleidomastoid and trapezius and passes through the jugular foramen", "C": "Glossopharyngeal nerve, which carries carotid sinus afferent signals and exits through the same foramen", "D": "Vagus nerve, which slows pacemaker firing and exits with cranial nerves IX and XI", "E": "Oculomotor nerve, which carries parasympathetic fibers to the pupil and exits the superior orbital fissure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vagus nerve, which slows pacemaker firing and exits with cranial nerves IX and XI): The vagus nerve provides parasympathetic input to the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes and leaves the skull at the jugular foramen with the glossopharyngeal and accessory nerves. The glossopharyngeal nerve shares the foramen and senses carotid sinus pressure but does not slow the pacemaker directly."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 58-year-old man with hypertension and obesity develops a sudden, severe, swollen red first metatarsophalangeal joint after starting a new antihypertensive three weeks ago. Joint aspiration shows needle-shaped crystals with negative birefringence. Serum uric acid is 9.8 mg/dL. Which drug most likely contributed by raising serum uric acid?", "options": {"A": "Hydrochlorothiazide, which competes for tubular organic anion secretion and promotes volume depletion, reducing renal urate excretion", "B": "Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker with mild uricosuric effect through increased renal perfusion", "C": "Losartan, an angiotensin receptor blocker that lowers uric acid by inhibiting URAT1 in proximal tubule", "D": "Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor that lowers angiotensin II and raises bradykinin levels in plasma", "E": "Spironolactone, an aldosterone antagonist that spares potassium and increases urinary sodium loss from the collecting duct"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hydrochlorothiazide, which competes for tubular organic anion secretion and promotes volume depletion, reducing renal urate excretion): Thiazide diuretics reduce renal urate excretion by competing for secretory transporters and by volume contraction, which raises serum urate and can precipitate gout. Needle-shaped negatively birefringent crystals are monosodium urate. Losartan is mildly uricosuric, amlodipine has a small urate-lowering effect, and ACE inhibitors and spironolactone do not meaningfully raise urate."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A rural practice compares length of hospital stay after hip fracture surgery between two groups of eight patients each. Stay lengths are strongly right-skewed, with one patient staying 70 days while most stayed under 10. The outcome is continuous, and the groups are independent. Which test is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Chi-square test, which compares proportions in categories after dichotomizing length of stay", "B": "Unpaired t test, which compares the means of two independent groups on a continuous scale", "C": "Wilcoxon signed-rank test, which compares ranks of paired differences within the same patients", "D": "Wilcoxon rank-sum (Mann-Whitney) test, which compares ranks and does not rely on normality", "E": "Paired t test, which compares two measurements on matched or repeated subjects"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wilcoxon rank-sum (Mann-Whitney) test, which compares ranks and does not rely on normality): With only eight patients per group and one extreme outlier, the normality assumption of the t test is not defensible. The rank-sum test uses ranks and is the nonparametric counterpart for two independent samples. The signed-rank test is the paired equivalent, and dichotomizing length of stay would throw away information."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 42-year-old woman with a bleeding gastric ulcer has hemoglobin of 5.8 g/dL and is hypotensive. She is alert and states that her faith forbids transfusion, and she carries a signed refusal card. Her husband begs the physician to transfuse her. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Seek a court order to transfuse, because the potential loss of life outweighs religious belief", "B": "Transfuse under the emergency exception, because hypotension makes her capacity to refuse uncertain", "C": "Respect her refusal and pursue other measures, such as endoscopic hemostasis, volume expanders, and iron", "D": "Follow her husband's wishes as surrogate, since a spouse's decision overrides a patient's earlier refusal", "E": "Obtain a psychiatric consultation to override her refusal, since refusing lifesaving treatment shows impaired judgment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Respect her refusal and pursue other measures, such as endoscopic hemostasis, volume expanders, and iron): A capable adult may refuse any treatment, including transfusion, and her alert state and documented refusal show a clear informed choice. The emergency exception applies only when the patient's wishes are unknown. Surrogates cannot override a capable patient, and the physician should maximize every acceptable alternative to control bleeding."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with heavy menses has fatigue and pallor. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL with an MCV of 71 fL and a high red cell distribution width. Her C-reactive protein is normal. Which set of serum results is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Low serum iron, low total iron binding capacity, high ferritin, and low transferrin saturation", "B": "Low serum iron, high total iron binding capacity, low ferritin, and low transferrin saturation", "C": "High serum iron, low total iron binding capacity, high ferritin, and high transferrin saturation", "D": "Low serum iron, normal total iron binding capacity, normal ferritin, and low transferrin saturation", "E": "High serum iron, normal total iron binding capacity, normal ferritin, and high transferrin saturation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low serum iron, high total iron binding capacity, low ferritin, and low transferrin saturation): Iron deficiency depletes stores, so ferritin falls, while the liver makes more transferrin, raising total iron binding capacity and lowering saturation. Anemia of chronic disease shows low iron and low binding capacity with high ferritin because hepcidin traps iron in macrophages, which a normal C-reactive protein argues against. Iron overload and sideroblastic states raise iron and saturation."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 7-year-old boy has persistent headaches after a minor fall from a bicycle, with tenderness at the suboccipital region. The physician tests the occipitoatlantal joint by translating the occiput and nodding the head. Which description correctly states the primary motion and coupled sidebending and rotation at this joint?", "options": {"A": "Flexion and extension as main motion, with sidebending and rotation coupled toward the same side", "B": "Flexion and extension as main motion, with sidebending and rotation coupled toward opposite sides", "C": "Rotation as the main motion, with sidebending and rotation coupled toward opposite sides", "D": "Rotation as the main motion, with sidebending and rotation coupled toward the same side", "E": "Sidebending as the main motion, with rotation and flexion coupled toward the same side"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Flexion and extension as main motion, with sidebending and rotation coupled toward opposite sides): The occipitoatlantal joint is the nodding joint, with flexion and extension as its primary motion and sidebending and rotation coupled to opposite sides. Rotation is the primary motion of C1 on C2, and the same side coupling belongs to typical cervical segments C2 to C7."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 29-year-old woman presents to her gynecologist with 8 months of amenorrhea and bilateral milky nipple discharge. Pregnancy test is negative and TSH is normal. Serum prolactin is markedly elevated, and MRI shows a 7 mm sellar lesion without visual field changes. Which pharmacologic mechanism is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this tumor?", "options": {"A": "D2 receptor agonism on lactotrophs, restoring dopamine-mediated inhibition and shrinking the adenoma", "B": "Somatostatin receptor agonism on pituitary cells, suppressing hormone release and limiting tumor growth", "C": "Dopamine receptor blockade, increasing hypothalamic inhibition of lactotroph secretion and shrinking the adenoma", "D": "Growth hormone receptor antagonism, blocking peripheral signaling through the JAK-STAT pathway in the liver", "E": "Estrogen receptor antagonism, reducing estrogen-stimulated lactotroph proliferation and hormone gene transcription in the tumor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (D2 receptor agonism on lactotrophs, restoring dopamine-mediated inhibition and shrinking the adenoma): Lactotrophs are under tonic dopamine inhibition through D2 receptors, so cabergoline or bromocriptine lowers hormone output and usually shrinks a prolactinoma. Somatostatin analogs are reserved for growth hormone or TSH secreting tumors and work poorly here. Blocking dopamine receptors, as antipsychotics do, would raise the level further."}, {"id": 732, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "An 82-year-old woman with known osteoporosis and a prior vertebral compression fracture has acute thoracic pain with tissue texture changes at T7 to T9. She cannot lie prone for long and is anxious about manipulation. Which technique is the most appropriate first choice for this segment?", "options": {"A": "Muscle energy using a strong isometric contraction against a fixed counterforce", "B": "Direct myofascial release applying firm sustained load into the restrictive barrier", "C": "High velocity low amplitude thrust applied at the restrictive barrier", "D": "Articulatory technique with repetitive springing through the restrictive barrier", "E": "Functional technique, a gentle indirect method following palpated tissue response"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Functional technique, a gentle indirect method following palpated tissue response): In an elderly patient with osteoporosis and a prior vertebral fracture, a gentle indirect method guided by palpated tissue response avoids forceful loading of the spine. Thrust, repetitive springing, strong contraction and firm direct myofascial loading all risk further fracture. Functional technique can be done with the patient in a comfortable position."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 31-year-old woman, three months postpartum, reports radial-sided wrist pain that worsens when lifting her infant. There is tenderness and mild swelling over the radial styloid. When she grasps her thumb in a fist and the examiner deviates the wrist toward the ulnar side, she has sharp pain. Which tendons are involved?", "options": {"A": "Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, which run together in the second dorsal compartment", "B": "Extensor carpi ulnaris, which occupies the sixth dorsal compartment and subluxes during forearm supination", "C": "Flexor carpi radialis and flexor pollicis longus, which travel adjacent to the scaphoid tubercle volarly", "D": "Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis, which share the fourth dorsal compartment beneath the extensor retinaculum", "E": "Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis, which share the tight first dorsal extensor compartment"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis, which share the tight first dorsal extensor compartment): A positive Finkelstein test with radial styloid tenderness in a new mother points to De Quervain tenosynovitis of the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis. These tendons run in the first dorsal compartment. The second compartment tendons are involved in intersection syndrome, which causes pain proximal to the wrist."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 34-year-old sexually active woman has two days of fever, migratory joint pain, and a painful swollen right knee. Examination shows pustular skin lesions on the distal limbs and tenosynovitis of the wrist. Synovial fluid shows 30,000 white cells per microliter with no crystals and a negative Gram stain. Which organism is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Neisseria meningitidis, a gram negative diplococcus causing petechial rash and joint effusions after bacteremia", "B": "Streptococcus pyogenes, which causes a migratory polyarthritis a few weeks after pharyngitis", "C": "Staphylococcus aureus, the most common cause of nongonococcal septic arthritis in adults", "D": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a gram negative diplococcus that disseminates hematogenously from a mucosal site", "E": "Borrelia burgdorferi, a spirochete causing intermittent knee arthritis weeks after a tick bite"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a gram negative diplococcus that disseminates hematogenously from a mucosal site): Young sexually active adults with migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, pustular dermatitis and a negative Gram stain have disseminated gonococcal infection, because Neisseria gonorrhoeae is hard to see in joint fluid. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause of septic arthritis overall but rarely produces this triad. Rheumatic fever causes a migratory arthritis with a sterile, much less cellular effusion."}, {"id": 735, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 68-year-old man with a 40 pack-year history has cough, constipation, and polyuria. Serum calcium is 13.2 mg/dL, phosphorus is low, and intact parathyroid hormone is suppressed. Chest CT shows a central cavitating mass, and biopsy shows keratin pearls with intercellular bridges. He takes an over the counter antacid. Which mediator best explains the calcium abnormality?", "options": {"A": "Osteolytic skeletal metastases, which release calcium directly from bone through local cytokine driven resorption", "B": "Ectopic secretion of intact parathyroid hormone by the tumor, which raises serum calcium and lowers phosphorus", "C": "Milk alkali syndrome from excess calcium carbonate antacid use, which raises the serum calcium level", "D": "Tumor production of parathyroid hormone related peptide, which activates receptors in bone and kidney", "E": "Tumor expression of 1-alpha hydroxylase, which converts calcidiol to calcitriol and increases intestinal absorption"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tumor production of parathyroid hormone related peptide, which activates receptors in bone and kidney): Keratinizing squamous carcinoma commonly causes humoral hypercalcemia through parathyroid hormone related peptide, which mimics the hormone at its receptor and lowers phosphorus. The suppressed hormone level excludes ectopic hormone and primary hyperparathyroidism. Antacid use can raise calcium, but the mass and low phosphorus point to the tumor mediator."}, {"id": 736, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 58-year-old man with neck stiffness has C3 somatic dysfunction. He reports transient dizziness, diplopia and slurred speech when he turns his head to the right, which began last week. The physician is considering several techniques for this segment. Which approach should be avoided?", "options": {"A": "Cervical high velocity low amplitude thrust with rotation, given suspected vertebrobasilar insufficiency", "B": "Cervical counterstrain held for ninety seconds in a comfortable position of ease", "C": "Suboccipital myofascial release using gentle sustained inhibitory pressure at the occiput", "D": "Cervical functional technique guided by palpated tissue response toward ease", "E": "Cervical muscle energy using a light isometric contraction from a neutral position"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cervical high velocity low amplitude thrust with rotation, given suspected vertebrobasilar insufficiency): Transient dizziness, diplopia and slurred speech with head turning suggest vertebrobasilar insufficiency, an absolute contraindication to cervical HVLA. Gentle indirect or low-force techniques such as counterstrain, functional and suboccipital release do not stress the vertebral arteries in this way. Light muscle energy kept in neutral is also lower risk."}, {"id": 737, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 66-year-old man has right lower lobe pneumonia with fever, productive cough, and dullness to percussion. He also has focal tenderness and crepitus over left ribs 8 and 9 after a fall two days ago, confirmed as nondisplaced fractures. His physician wants to assist immune function and recovery. Which treatment should be avoided?", "options": {"A": "Myofascial release of the thoracic inlet with light traction while the patient lies supine", "B": "Seated muscle energy to the cervical spine using a light isometric contraction of the neck", "C": "Pedal pump with gentle oscillation of both feet to enhance lymphatic return from the legs", "D": "Thoracic lymphatic pump with rhythmic compression across the lower rib cage on both sides", "E": "Suboccipital inhibition with gentle sustained pressure at the base of the skull to promote relaxation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thoracic lymphatic pump with rhythmic compression across the lower rib cage on both sides): Rhythmic compression of the lower rib cage is unsafe over fresh rib fractures because it can displace the fragments and injure the underlying lung. The other techniques apply no pressure to the injured ribs, and a pedal pump in particular can still assist lymphatic return in pneumonia."}, {"id": 738, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A county health department tracks a chronic condition that is sometimes fatal. Over the past decade, a new medication allowed patients to survive much longer without curing the disease, although the number of new diagnoses each year did not change. Which change in disease frequency measures would be expected?", "options": {"A": "Both prevalence and incidence fall, because effective treatment reduces the number of patients with disease", "B": "Prevalence rises while incidence remains stable, because patients remain in the diseased pool for longer", "C": "Incidence rises while prevalence remains stable, because more new diagnoses accumulate among longer-lived patients", "D": "Prevalence falls while incidence remains stable, because the medication shortens the average duration of illness", "E": "Incidence and prevalence both remain stable, because prevalence depends only on the number of new cases"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prevalence rises while incidence remains stable, because patients remain in the diseased pool for longer): Prevalence is approximately incidence multiplied by average disease duration. Longer survival without cure extends duration, so existing cases pile up and prevalence rises. Incidence counts only new diagnoses and therefore does not move."}, {"id": 739, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 34-year-old man presents to the emergency department with three days of malaise, anorexia, dark urine and right upper quadrant discomfort after a camping trip. He drinks little alcohol and takes no medications. Based on the hepatic function panel shown, which process best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Extrahepatic biliary obstruction with disproportionate rise of alkaline phosphatase and conjugated bilirubin", "B": "Intravascular hemolysis with predominantly unconjugated bilirubin and near normal aminotransferase values", "C": "Acute hepatocellular injury with preserved synthetic function and mild secondary conjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "D": "Decompensated cirrhosis with falling albumin, prolonged clotting time and only modest aminotransferase elevations", "E": "Alcohol-related hepatitis with an AST predominant pattern and clearly impaired hepatic synthetic function"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acute hepatocellular injury with preserved synthetic function and mild secondary conjugated hyperbilirubinemia): Aminotransferases above 1,000 with ALT greater than AST, a nearly normal alkaline phosphatase and normal albumin and INR indicate acute hepatocellular necrosis, as in acute viral hepatitis. The direct fraction predominates because injured hepatocytes cannot excrete conjugated bilirubin efficiently. Alcoholic hepatitis rarely pushes AST past a few hundred and reverses the ratio.", "image": "question_images/q_739.png"}, {"id": 740, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 44-year-old accountant has acute right neck pain. The physician finds a tender point in the anterior cervical region, passively flexes and sidebends the neck until the patient reports tenderness drops from nine to three out of ten, holds the position for ninety seconds without applying compression, then slowly returns the neck to neutral. Which technique was used?", "options": {"A": "Myofascial release, a method engaging fascial tension until a palpable softening occurs", "B": "Counterstrain, an indirect technique held in the position of greatest comfort", "C": "Facilitated positional release, which adds a compressive or twisting facilitating force", "D": "Muscle energy, a direct technique using patient effort against the neck's restriction", "E": "Functional technique, an indirect method following continuous tissue motion feedback"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Counterstrain, an indirect technique held in the position of greatest comfort): A discrete tender point treated by passively positioning the tissue until tenderness drops, then holding about ninety seconds, is counterstrain. Facilitated positional release adds a facilitating force that was not used here. Functional technique has no tender point endpoint, and muscle energy is active and direct."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 68-year-old man with diabetes for 20 years has calf pain when walking two blocks that resolves with rest. He smokes and takes metformin. Pedal pulses are not palpable. Ankle brachial index is 1.6 bilaterally, and the vessels are not compressible with the cuff. Which next test best assesses his limb perfusion?", "options": {"A": "Toe brachial index, because digital arteries rarely develop the medial calcification of ankle vessels", "B": "Serum D-dimer testing to evaluate for acute arterial thromboembolism of the lower extremity", "C": "Lumbar spine MRI to evaluate neurogenic claudication from spinal canal stenosis", "D": "Venous duplex of the legs to evaluate for chronic venous insufficiency and reflux", "E": "Repeat ankle brachial index with a higher cuff inflation pressure to compress the vessels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Toe brachial index, because digital arteries rarely develop the medial calcification of ankle vessels): An ankle brachial index above 1.4 reflects medial calcification that prevents compression, which is common in diabetes, so the value cannot be trusted. Digital arteries are usually spared, so the toe brachial index measures perfusion reliably. Inflating the cuff higher does not help, and the other studies address different problems."}, {"id": 742, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 29-year-old woman develops an intensely itchy, erythematous, vesicular rash on both earlobes, the skin under her watchband, and below her jeans button, 48 hours after wearing new costume jewelry. Patch testing is positive for nickel. Which immune mechanism most likely accounts for this reaction?", "options": {"A": "Deposition of circulating antigen-antibody complexes in dermal vessels activating complement and neutrophils", "B": "Antibody binding to antigen on the cell surface triggering complement-mediated keratinocyte lysis", "C": "Delayed hypersensitivity in which sensitized T lymphocytes recognize hapten-modified self proteins", "D": "Direct chemical damage to keratinocytes by the metal through nonimmune cytotoxic injury", "E": "Immediate IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation after crosslinking of surface-bound antibody by antigen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delayed hypersensitivity in which sensitized T lymphocytes recognize hapten-modified self proteins): Allergic contact dermatitis from nickel is a type IV, T cell mediated delayed hypersensitivity in which nickel acts as a hapten that binds self proteins. The 24 to 72 hour onset and positive patch test distinguish it from IgE-mediated urticaria. Irritant dermatitis occurs without sensitization and does not give a positive patch test."}, {"id": 743, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 28-year-old woman at 17 weeks gestation has an elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein. Ultrasonography shows a lumbosacral cystic mass containing neural tissue, a banana-shaped cerebellum and bilateral talipes. She took no supplements before conception. Which embryologic event most directly explains the spinal lesion?", "options": {"A": "Persistence of the primitive streak caudally, producing a lumbosacral mass of mixed germ layers", "B": "Abnormal canalization of the caudal cell mass, leaving a tethered cord and sacral defects", "C": "Incomplete closure of the anterior neuropore during the fourth week, leaving brain tissue exposed", "D": "Failure of the vertebral arches to fuse with an intact, normally closed neural tube", "E": "Incomplete closure of the posterior neuropore near the end of the fourth embryonic week"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Incomplete closure of the posterior neuropore near the end of the fourth embryonic week): The caudal opening of the neural tube, the posterior neuropore, normally closes by about day 28, and failure produces myelomeningocele with the elevated alpha-fetoprotein seen here. The banana-shaped cerebellum reflects the associated Chiari II malformation. Anterior neuropore failure causes anencephaly rather than a lumbosacral lesion."}, {"id": 744, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 28-year-old man twisted his left knee while skiing 2 days ago. He reports medial joint line pain, intermittent locking, and a clicking sensation with squatting. Flexing the knee fully, externally rotating the tibia, and extending the knee produces a palpable click and pain. Which injury is most likely present?", "options": {"A": "Osteochondritis dissecans lesion of the lateral femoral condyle with a loose cartilage fragment", "B": "Tear of the posterior horn of the medial meniscus with a displaced fragment", "C": "Patellar subluxation with medial retinaculum disruption causing catching and sensation of giving way", "D": "Grade II sprain of the medial collateral ligament with partial fiber disruption", "E": "Tear of the posterior horn of the lateral meniscus with a displaced fragment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tear of the posterior horn of the medial meniscus with a displaced fragment): Medial joint line pain, locking, and a click on the McMurray maneuver with external rotation indicate a medial meniscal tear. Internal rotation during the maneuver tests the lateral meniscus. A medial collateral sprain causes valgus laxity without mechanical locking."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 3-week-old boy born by forceps delivery feeds poorly, has a weak suck, and is irritable. The osteopathic physician finds compression across the left occipitomastoid suture and suspects it is compromising structures that exit nearby. Which structures pass through the foramen formed at this junction?", "options": {"A": "Hypoglossal nerve alone, together with a small emissary vein from the condylar region", "B": "Glossopharyngeal, vagus, and accessory nerves, along with the origin of the internal jugular vein", "C": "Mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, along with the accessory meningeal artery to the dura", "D": "Oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves, along with the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve", "E": "Facial and vestibulocochlear nerves, along with the labyrinthine artery to the inner ear"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Glossopharyngeal, vagus, and accessory nerves, along with the origin of the internal jugular vein): The jugular foramen lies between the occiput and temporal bone near the occipitomastoid suture and transmits cranial nerves IX, X, and XI plus the beginning of the internal jugular vein. Compression there can impair vagal function, producing poor suck and feeding difficulty in infants. The hypoglossal nerve exits through its own canal, and the other groups exit elsewhere."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 72-year-old man with carotid atherosclerosis reports sudden painless loss of vision in his right eye that began an hour ago. Fundoscopy shows a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula and a relative afferent pupillary defect. Which finding best explains why the macular spot stays red?", "options": {"A": "Neovascularization at the macula produces a red fibrovascular patch over pale retina", "B": "Increased perfusion of the macula through collateral retinal arterioles from the ciliary circulation", "C": "The fovea is thin and receives choroidal blood supply, so it shows through ischemic retina", "D": "Hemorrhage collects in the foveal pit from rupture of new vessels after retinal ischemia", "E": "Ganglion cell lipid accumulation spares the foveola where few ganglion cells exist"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The fovea is thin and receives choroidal blood supply, so it shows through ischemic retina): In central retinal artery occlusion the inner retina turns pale from ischemia, while the foveola is very thin and nourished by the choroid, so the underlying red color shows through. A storage disease such as Tay-Sachs produces a similar spot for a different reason, and this older vascular patient with sudden painless loss fits occlusion."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 61-year-old man in a rural hospital has 90 minutes of crushing chest pain with 3 mm ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. He has no bleeding history, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. The nearest percutaneous coronary intervention center is three hours away by ground transport. Which management is best?", "options": {"A": "Start heparin and aspirin, and wait for troponin results before choosing a reperfusion strategy", "B": "Give intravenous fibrinolytic therapy now, then transfer for angiography once he is stabilized", "C": "Transfer immediately for primary percutaneous coronary intervention without giving any reperfusion medication", "D": "Give intravenous nitroglycerin and metoprolol, and schedule elective coronary angiography the following week", "E": "Perform emergency coronary artery bypass grafting at the local hospital after a surgical consultation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give intravenous fibrinolytic therapy now, then transfer for angiography once he is stabilized): In STEMI presenting within 12 hours, reperfusion should not wait for biomarkers. When primary PCI cannot be performed within about 120 minutes of first contact, fibrinolysis is preferred, followed by transfer for angiography. Waiting for troponin or relying on medical therapy alone leaves the artery occluded."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with hypertension is evaluated for sudden speech difficulty. She speaks fluently in long sentences with invented words, cannot follow commands, and appears unaware of her errors. Strength and sensation are normal. Confrontation testing reveals a right superior visual field loss in both eyes. Which lesion best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Infarction of the left angular gyrus from watershed hypoperfusion between middle and posterior territories", "B": "Occlusion of the left posterior cerebral artery producing infarction of the occipital pole", "C": "Infarction of the left arcuate fasciculus causing impaired repetition with intact comprehension", "D": "Occlusion of the superior division of the left middle cerebral artery", "E": "Embolic occlusion of the inferior division of the left middle cerebral artery"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Embolic occlusion of the inferior division of the left middle cerebral artery): Fluent speech with poor comprehension is Wernicke aphasia from the posterior superior temporal gyrus, supplied by the inferior division of the middle cerebral artery. Meyer loop fibers in the temporal lobe pass through the same region, producing a contralateral superior quadrantanopia. Superior division lesions cause nonfluent speech with weakness, and an isolated arcuate lesion spares comprehension."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 28-year-old woman at 29 weeks gestation reports pubic and buttock pain with a waddling gait. Examination shows increased sacroiliac and symphyseal mobility and multiple tender paraspinal segments. Which hormone most directly accounts for the ligament laxity, and what consequence does it have for choosing osteopathic technique?", "options": {"A": "Prolactin stiffens the pubic symphysis, so articulatory technique is preferred to muscle energy at that joint", "B": "Human placental lactogen loosens pelvic ligaments, so gentle indirect techniques are unsafe during gestation", "C": "Relaxin loosens pelvic ligaments, so large-amplitude thrust techniques at these joints warrant caution", "D": "Oxytocin softens pelvic ligaments, so direct thrust techniques at these joints become the preferred approach", "E": "Estrogen tightens pelvic ligaments, so prolonged stretching of the sacroiliac region is the safest option"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Relaxin loosens pelvic ligaments, so large-amplitude thrust techniques at these joints warrant caution): Relaxin from the corpus luteum and placenta softens the pubic symphysis and pelvic ligaments in preparation for delivery. Hypermobile joints are vulnerable to overstretching, so gentle techniques such as muscle energy or counterstrain are favored over large-amplitude thrust. Gentle indirect methods remain safe in pregnancy."}, {"id": 750, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 66-year-old man is on postoperative day four after total hip arthroplasty. He develops sudden dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain with a pulse of 112, temperature 37.2 C, and oxygen saturation 89 percent. Lungs are clear, the left calf is swollen, and prophylactic anticoagulation was held for bleeding. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Postoperative atelectasis from shallow breathing, typically peaking on the first postoperative days", "B": "Hospital-acquired pneumonia from aspiration, presenting with fever and focal crackles after several days", "C": "Cardiogenic pulmonary edema from fluid overload, with bibasilar crackles and a third heart sound", "D": "Pulmonary embolism from deep venous thrombosis after orthopedic surgery and interrupted prophylaxis", "E": "Fat embolism syndrome from long bone fracture, with petechiae and neurologic changes within days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pulmonary embolism from deep venous thrombosis after orthopedic surgery and interrupted prophylaxis): Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, tachycardia, hypoxemia, and clear lungs with a swollen calf several days after hip surgery and interrupted anticoagulation point to pulmonary embolism from deep venous thrombosis. Atelectasis and pneumonia would produce crackles or fever, and fat embolism and edema lack the skin, neurologic, or cardiac features."}, {"id": 751, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 58-year-old man with erectile dysfunction is prescribed an oral medication that increases penile blood flow during sexual stimulation. His medical history includes stable angina treated with sublingual nitroglycerin as needed. Which mechanism explains the drug's benefit and the reason this combination is dangerous?", "options": {"A": "Phosphodiesterase 5 inhibition prolongs cGMP action, and added nitric oxide donation risks profound hypotension", "B": "Adenylyl cyclase activation raises cAMP in cavernosal tissue, and nitrates compete for the same receptor", "C": "Phosphodiesterase 3 inhibition raises cAMP in platelets and vessels, and nitrates then precipitate bleeding", "D": "Alpha-1 receptor blockade relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle, and combined venodilation risks bradyarrhythmia and syncope", "E": "Dopamine D2 receptor agonism enhances central arousal, and nitrates then trigger hypertensive crisis through vasoconstriction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Phosphodiesterase 5 inhibition prolongs cGMP action, and added nitric oxide donation risks profound hypotension): Phosphodiesterase 5 inhibitors block breakdown of cGMP, so nitric oxide released during stimulation causes sustained cavernosal vasodilation. Nitrates also raise cGMP by donating nitric oxide, so the combination can cause profound, life-threatening hypotension. The two must not be used together."}, {"id": 752, "domain": "community_health", "question": "Measles has a basic reproduction number of about 15 in an unvaccinated community. Public health officials want to estimate the minimum proportion of the population that must be immune to interrupt sustained transmission. Vaccine effectiveness is assumed to be perfect for this calculation. Which threshold is closest?", "options": {"A": "About 15 percent, derived from taking the reproduction number directly as the percentage immune", "B": "About 7 percent, derived from the inverse of R0 alone without subtracting from one", "C": "About 50 percent, derived from the proportion needed when each case infects two other people", "D": "About 80 percent, derived from the threshold used for diseases with R0 near five", "E": "About 93 percent, derived from one minus the inverse of R0, the transmission multiplier"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (About 93 percent, derived from one minus the inverse of R0, the transmission multiplier): The herd immunity threshold equals one minus one divided by R0. With R0 of 15 this is 1 minus 0.067, or about 93 percent. Using the inverse alone gives 7 percent, and the lower thresholds belong to diseases with a smaller reproduction number."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with heart failure and atrial fibrillation takes digoxin and furosemide. She presents with nausea, yellow-green visual halos, and palpitations. The ECG tracing shown demonstrates a regular atrial rhythm with variable atrioventricular block and scooped ST depression. Which laboratory abnormality most likely precipitated this toxicity?", "options": {"A": "Hypernatremia from dehydration, reducing sodium potassium pump activity within myocytes", "B": "Hypokalemia from loop diuretic use, increasing digoxin binding to the sodium potassium pump", "C": "Hypocalcemia from reduced intake, lowering intracellular calcium and cardiac excitability", "D": "Hypermagnesemia from antacid use, antagonizing digoxin binding at the membrane pump in cardiac cells", "E": "Hypophosphatemia from poor intake, depleting cellular energy needed for pump function"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hypokalemia from loop diuretic use, increasing digoxin binding to the sodium potassium pump): Digoxin binds the potassium site of the sodium potassium ATPase, so low potassium increases binding and toxicity. Loop diuretics commonly cause hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia and hypercalcemia also potentiate toxicity. Atrial tachycardia with block and scooped ST segments is characteristic.", "image": "question_images/q_753.png"}, {"id": 754, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 17-year-old raised as a girl presents with new clitoral enlargement, deepening voice, and increased muscle mass during puberty. Genitalia were ambiguous at birth. Examination shows no uterus, testes in the inguinal canals, and minimal breast development. Serum testosterone is normal for a male and dihydrotestosterone is low. Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Klinefelter syndrome with small firm testes, gynecomastia and elevated gonadotropins from seminiferous tubule dysgenesis", "B": "17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase 3 deficiency with impaired testicular testosterone synthesis and virilization at puberty", "C": "Complete androgen insensitivity from androgen receptor mutations, with female genitalia and no virilization at puberty", "D": "Steroid 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency, sparing wolffian structures but impairing external genital masculinization before puberty", "E": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia from 21-hydroxylase deficiency causing androgen excess in a 46,XX infant"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Steroid 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency, sparing wolffian structures but impairing external genital masculinization before puberty): Ambiguous genitalia at birth, normal male testosterone, low dihydrotestosterone and virilization at puberty indicate 5-alpha-reductase 2 deficiency. Dihydrotestosterone is needed for external genital masculinization in utero, while testosterone from the testes later drives pubertal changes. Complete androgen insensitivity would not virilize."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A term newborn has a translucent membrane-covered sac at the umbilicus containing loops of bowel and part of the liver. Examination also shows clenched fists with overlapping fingers and rocker-bottom feet. Which embryologic event best explains the abdominal wall finding in this infant?", "options": {"A": "Failure of the urachus to obliterate, leaving a bladder communication with the umbilicus", "B": "Persistent vitelline duct forming a fistula between the terminal ileum and the umbilicus", "C": "Failure of the herniated midgut loops to return to the abdominal cavity after physiologic herniation", "D": "Vascular compromise near the right umbilical ring causing a full-thickness defect lateral to the cord", "E": "Failure of rotation of the midgut around the superior mesenteric artery leaving duodenal bands"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Failure of the herniated midgut loops to return to the abdominal cavity after physiologic herniation): Omphalocele is a membrane-covered midline herniation that results when midgut loops that normally herniate into the umbilical cord fail to return by about week 12, and the trisomy 18 features fit. Gastroschisis, by contrast, is a full-thickness defect lateral to the cord with no covering sac and few associated syndromes."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 45-year-old accountant was passed over for promotion and says nothing to his supervisor at work. That evening he shouts at his wife about a misplaced coffee mug, which he usually ignores, and later admits he feels guilty. Which defense mechanism best explains his behavior?", "options": {"A": "Displacement, redirecting anger from the threatening source onto a safer and less powerful target", "B": "Reaction formation, adopting exaggerated behavior opposite to his true feelings of anger toward others", "C": "Isolation of affect, describing the event factually while separating it from the emotion it produces", "D": "Projection, attributing his own unacceptable feelings of resentment to his supervisor or other people", "E": "Passive aggression, indirectly expressing hostility toward the supervisor through delay and subtle obstruction at work"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Displacement, redirecting anger from the threatening source onto a safer and less powerful target): Displacement redirects an impulse from a threatening target, the supervisor, to a safer substitute, his wife. Projection assigns one's own feelings to others, and reaction formation turns a feeling into its opposite. Passive aggression would be directed at the supervisor himself rather than at a bystander."}, {"id": 757, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 31-year-old runner is seen two days after a lumbar strain. Palpation of the right paraspinal tissues at L3 reveals warm, moist, boggy texture with erythema and tenderness, and the segment resists motion testing. Which interpretation of these findings best fits the history?", "options": {"A": "Chronic somatic dysfunction with fibrotic, ropy tissue and reduced local blood flow persisting for months", "B": "Viscerosomatic reflex from kidney disease with sympathetic facilitation across the lower thoracic segments", "C": "Chapman reflex point from visceral disease appearing as a small tender anterior gangliform contraction", "D": "Latent myofascial trigger point producing referred pain only when firmly compressed by the examiner", "E": "Acute somatic dysfunction with local inflammatory vasodilation and edema from recent tissue injury at that segment"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Acute somatic dysfunction with local inflammatory vasodilation and edema from recent tissue injury at that segment): Acute somatic dysfunction presents with warm, moist, edematous, erythematous tissue, reflecting recent injury and local vasodilation. Chronic dysfunction becomes cool, dry and ropy with fibrosis. A viscerosomatic reflex would need a plausible organ source, which the history does not offer."}, {"id": 758, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 49-year-old man with end-stage kidney disease misses two dialysis sessions and presents with generalized weakness and palpitations. Serum potassium is 7.4 mEq/L. His ECG shows peaked T waves and a widened QRS complex in the tracing shown. Blood pressure is 150/90 mm Hg. Which intervention should be given first?", "options": {"A": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate to bind potassium in the colon and increase fecal elimination", "B": "Intravenous regular insulin with glucose to drive potassium into skeletal muscle and hepatic cells quickly", "C": "Intravenous furosemide to increase urinary potassium loss by enhancing distal sodium delivery and flow", "D": "Intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac myocyte membrane and antagonize potassium induced depolarization", "E": "Nebulized albuterol to stimulate beta-2 receptors and shift potassium into cells within minutes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac myocyte membrane and antagonize potassium induced depolarization): Conduction changes on the ECG signal imminent arrhythmia, so the first step is to raise the threshold potential with calcium, which acts within minutes but does not lower potassium. Insulin with glucose, albuterol, and later dialysis then remove or shift the potassium. A resin or loop diuretic is too slow or ineffective in anuric kidney failure.", "image": "question_images/q_758.png"}, {"id": 759, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 68-year-old man with advanced heart failure refuses hospital admission for worsening dyspnea. He is alert, states he understands he may die without treatment, and explains his wish to spend remaining time at home. His daughter insists he is confused. Which assessment best establishes decision-making capacity?", "options": {"A": "Score on a mini-mental status examination above the established cutoff for cognitive impairment", "B": "Whether the physician considers the choice reasonable and consistent with standard medical recommendations", "C": "Formal psychiatric evaluation confirming absence of any depressive disorder before honoring the refusal", "D": "Agreement of his daughter, as the closest relative, that the decision matches his values", "E": "Ability to understand relevant information, appreciate consequences, reason about options, and express a consistent choice"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ability to understand relevant information, appreciate consequences, reason about options, and express a consistent choice): Capacity is decision specific and rests on understanding, appreciation, reasoning, and a stable expressed choice. It does not depend on whether the physician or family agrees with the decision, and a screening score or a psychiatric diagnosis alone does not settle it. Here the stated reasoning meets each element."}, {"id": 760, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 58-year-old man with diabetes has a swollen, dusky left calf after minor trauma two days ago. He is febrile and tachycardic, and reports pain far out of proportion to the visible skin findings. Crepitus is palpated and bullae with gray fluid are forming. Which next step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Duplex ultrasonography of the leg veins to exclude deep venous thrombosis as the cause", "B": "Emergent surgical exploration and debridement alongside broad spectrum intravenous antibiotics and aggressive fluid resuscitation", "C": "Magnetic resonance imaging of the calf to confirm fascial involvement before any operative decision", "D": "Needle aspiration of the bullae with Gram stain to select targeted oral antibiotic therapy", "E": "Admission for intravenous cefazolin with limb elevation and repeat examination in twenty four hours"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Emergent surgical exploration and debridement alongside broad spectrum intravenous antibiotics and aggressive fluid resuscitation): Pain out of proportion, crepitus, bullae with gray fluid and systemic toxicity indicate necrotizing fasciitis, a surgical emergency in which delay raises mortality. Imaging and observation waste time, and antibiotics alone cannot reach necrotic tissue."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 34-year-old hiker from Connecticut returns from a camping trip. Ten days later he notes a slowly expanding annular red lesion on the thigh, as shown in the photograph, with partial central clearing. He has mild fatigue and low-grade fever but no joint or neurologic symptoms. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Oral azithromycin for seven days as first line therapy because it covers Borrelia and rickettsiae", "B": "Oral doxycycline for ten days, started on clinical diagnosis without waiting for serologic testing", "C": "Intravenous ceftriaxone for fourteen days to prevent cardiac and neurologic involvement of disseminated disease", "D": "Reassurance with observation, since erythema migrans resolves spontaneously and rarely progresses to systemic disease", "E": "Serum Borrelia antibody testing with ELISA, treating only if the confirmatory immunoblot is positive"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Oral doxycycline for ten days, started on clinical diagnosis without waiting for serologic testing): Erythema migrans in an endemic area is diagnosed clinically, and serology is often negative this early, so doxycycline for ten days begins immediately. Intravenous therapy is reserved for carditis or meningitis, and observation risks dissemination.", "image": "question_images/q_761.png"}, {"id": 762, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with hypertension and atrial fibrillation takes warfarin, and her INR today is 3.4. She has tender, restricted segments at T4 to T6 after lifting. The physician wants to treat the somatic dysfunction safely at this visit. Which technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Direct myofascial release with deep sustained force into the restrictive barrier of the paraspinal tissue", "B": "High velocity low amplitude thrust to the restricted segments through the restrictive barrier", "C": "Muscle energy with the patient contracting maximally against a rigid counterforce from the physician", "D": "Counterstrain, passively positioning the patient into ease and holding about ninety seconds without a thrust", "E": "Articulatory technique with repetitive vigorous springing of the thoracic segments through the barrier"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Counterstrain, passively positioning the patient into ease and holding about ninety seconds without a thrust): An INR above the usual target makes forceful or thrust techniques risky because of bleeding into soft tissue, so a gentle indirect technique such as counterstrain is the safest way to treat the tender segments. Muscle energy can be gentle in some forms, but the maximal contraction against rigid resistance described here adds avoidable strain."}, {"id": 763, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 71-year-old man with a known large paraesophageal hernia presents with sudden severe chest and epigastric pain, repeated retching without vomiting, and inability to swallow. He is anxious, afebrile, and diaphoretic, and asks whether osteopathic treatment of his diaphragm could help. Which is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Urgent imaging and surgical consultation for suspected gastric volvulus, deferring visceral osteopathic treatment", "B": "Visceral manipulation to reduce the herniated stomach and relieve the obstruction at the hiatus", "C": "High velocity low amplitude thrust to T10 to T12 to release diaphragm crural attachments", "D": "Trial of a proton pump inhibitor and counterstrain at the epigastrium, with outpatient follow-up", "E": "Diaphragm doming with myofascial release, followed by repeat examination in two hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Urgent imaging and surgical consultation for suspected gastric volvulus, deferring visceral osteopathic treatment): Sudden pain, retching without vomiting, and inability to swallow suggest an obstructed or volvulized paraesophageal hernia, a surgical emergency. Visceral manipulation is contraindicated when strangulation or obstruction is suspected. Imaging and surgical consultation come first, and gentle diaphragm techniques do not replace them."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 55-year-old house painter has a painful arc between 70 and 120 degrees of shoulder abduction. Pain is reproduced when the examiner passively flexes the fully pronated arm forward and again when the arm is flexed to 90 degrees and internally rotated. Strength of external rotation is normal. Which structure is being compressed?", "options": {"A": "Supraspinatus tendon with its subacromial bursa, pinched under the coracoacromial arch as the humerus elevates", "B": "Subscapularis tendon at the lesser tuberosity, which causes weakness with the internal rotation lift-off test", "C": "Infraspinatus tendon at its greater tuberosity insertion, which causes weakness of resisted external rotation", "D": "Anterior glenoid labrum, which produces apprehension when the abducted arm is passively externally rotated", "E": "Long head of biceps tendon within the bicipital groove, which causes pain on resisted forearm supination"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supraspinatus tendon with its subacromial bursa, pinched under the coracoacromial arch as the humerus elevates): The painful arc with positive Neer and Hawkins signs reflects subacromial impingement, in which the supraspinatus tendon and bursa are compressed beneath the coracoacromial arch. Normal external rotation strength argues against an infraspinatus problem. Labral pathology produces apprehension rather than pain on these maneuvers."}, {"id": 765, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 70-year-old man has multiple brown, sharply demarcated plaques on his back that have accumulated slowly over ten years, as in the photograph shown. Lesions have a greasy, warty surface, appear pasted onto the skin, and are painless. There has been no recent change in color or size. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Pigmented basal cell carcinoma, a slow malignant tumor of basal layer keratinocytes", "B": "Seborrheic keratosis, a benign epidermal proliferation of basaloid keratinocytes with horn cysts", "C": "Dermatofibroma, a benign dermal proliferation of fibroblasts producing a firm button-like nodule", "D": "Superficial spreading melanoma, a malignant proliferation of atypical melanocytes with radial growth", "E": "Actinic keratosis, a premalignant proliferation of atypical keratinocytes in sun-exposed skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Seborrheic keratosis, a benign epidermal proliferation of basaloid keratinocytes with horn cysts): Numerous stable, waxy, stuck-on brown plaques with a verrucous surface in an older adult are typical of seborrheic keratosis, a benign epidermal tumor with keratin-filled horn cysts. Melanoma would show change, asymmetry and color variation. Actinic keratoses are rough, scaly and erythematous rather than greasy and pasted on.", "image": "question_images/q_765.png"}, {"id": 766, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 28-year-old woman receives succinylcholine for rapid sequence intubation and remains paralyzed for 3 hours afterward, requiring ventilator support. Her family history is notable for a brother with similar prolonged paralysis. Liver and renal function tests are normal. Which of the following best explains this?", "options": {"A": "Malignant hyperthermia susceptibility with sustained muscle rigidity after exposure to a triggering agent", "B": "Phase II block from receptor desensitization after repeated or large succinylcholine doses", "C": "Reduced plasma butyrylcholinesterase activity from an inherited variant, slowing hydrolysis of the depolarizing blocker", "D": "Myasthenia gravis with reduced acetylcholine receptors increasing sensitivity to nondepolarizing agents", "E": "Hypermagnesemia potentiating neuromuscular blockade through reduced presynaptic acetylcholine release"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduced plasma butyrylcholinesterase activity from an inherited variant, slowing hydrolysis of the depolarizing blocker): Succinylcholine is cleared by plasma butyrylcholinesterase, so an inherited atypical enzyme variant prolongs the block for hours and can run in families. A single standard dose does not usually produce phase II block, and malignant hyperthermia would present with rigidity, hypercarbia, and fever rather than isolated paralysis."}, {"id": 767, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 52-year-old man arrives with severe headache, confusion, and blurred vision. Blood pressure is 224/132 mm Hg. Fundoscopy shows bilateral papilledema and flame hemorrhages. CT of the head is normal and urinalysis shows mild proteinuria. He is admitted to the intensive care unit for an intravenous titratable agent. What is the most appropriate blood pressure goal for the first hour?", "options": {"A": "Reduce mean arterial pressure by half over the first hour with intravenous nitroprusside infusion", "B": "Reduce mean arterial pressure by no more than about 25 percent over the first hour", "C": "Delay lowering pressure and begin oral agents to reach the target gradually over several days", "D": "Normalize pressure below 130 over 80 mm Hg within thirty minutes using a rapid infusion", "E": "Lower systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg immediately to limit further injury to retina and kidney"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce mean arterial pressure by no more than about 25 percent over the first hour): Encephalopathy with papilledema is a hypertensive emergency, and cerebral autoregulation is reset to higher pressures. Lowering mean pressure by more than about 25 percent in the first hour risks watershed ischemia, so reduction is gradual toward the target over hours to days. Aortic dissection is an exception that needs faster control."}, {"id": 768, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 19-year-old man has absent facial hair, small testes, and no deepening of the voice. He reports he has never been able to smell coffee or food aromas. He denies medication or supplement use. Serum testosterone, LH and FSH are all low, and prolactin and MRI of the pituitary are normal. Which defect best explains his findings?", "options": {"A": "Failed migration of GnRH-secreting neurons from the olfactory placode into the hypothalamus during fetal development", "B": "Mumps orchitis in childhood destroying Leydig and seminiferous tissue with compensatory gonadotropin elevation", "C": "Exogenous anabolic androgen use suppressing gonadotropin secretion and shrinking the testes through negative feedback", "D": "Prolactin-secreting pituitary microadenoma suppressing pulsatile GnRH release from the hypothalamus and delaying pubertal development", "E": "Testicular seminiferous tubule failure from an extra X chromosome with feedback loss on the pituitary"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Failed migration of GnRH-secreting neurons from the olfactory placode into the hypothalamus during fetal development): Low testosterone with low LH and FSH indicates hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, and anosmia identifies Kallmann syndrome. GnRH neurons arise in the olfactory placode and fail to migrate. Primary testicular failure would raise gonadotropins, and normal prolactin and imaging argue against a pituitary lesion."}, {"id": 769, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 24-year-old man has a firm thyroid nodule and chronic watery diarrhea. His father died of a thyroid tumor, and his brother had a pheochromocytoma. Fine needle aspiration shows spindle-shaped cells in sheets, and Congo red stains the stroma pink with apple-green birefringence. Which tumor is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Follicular carcinoma showing capsular and vascular invasion with hematogenous spread to bone and lung", "B": "Papillary carcinoma from follicular cells with RET rearrangement and lymphatic spread to cervical nodes", "C": "Thyroid lymphoma arising from chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis with rapid enlargement and B cell clones", "D": "Medullary carcinoma arising from parafollicular C cells with amyloid deposits derived from calcitonin", "E": "Anaplastic carcinoma with rapidly growing pleomorphic giant cells and local invasion of neck structures"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Medullary carcinoma arising from parafollicular C cells with amyloid deposits derived from calcitonin): Amyloid derived from calcitonin, with sheets of spindle cells and a family history of pheochromocytoma, indicates medullary carcinoma from C cells, part of MEN 2 with RET mutation. Diarrhea comes from secreted peptides. Papillary and follicular cancers arise from follicular cells and do not form amyloid."}, {"id": 770, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man with hypertension taking lisinopril has two months of progressive personality change, morning headaches, and new mild left arm weakness. He has no history of cancer or fever. In the contrast-enhanced MRI shown, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Metastatic adenocarcinoma, a solitary deposit at the gray white junction from an occult primary", "B": "Meningioma, an extra-axial dural based mass with a dural tail and slow growth", "C": "Primary CNS lymphoma, a periventricular homogeneously enhancing mass common in immunocompromised patients", "D": "Cerebral abscess, a ring-enhancing collection from hematogenous bacterial seeding of the frontal lobe", "E": "Glioblastoma, an IDH-wildtype astrocytic tumor with central necrosis and rapid growth across white matter"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Glioblastoma, an IDH-wildtype astrocytic tumor with central necrosis and rapid growth across white matter): An irregular ring-enhancing mass with central necrosis that infiltrates across the corpus callosum in a butterfly pattern in an older adult is glioblastoma. Metastasis is the main competitor but is usually multiple and well circumscribed at the gray white junction, and an abscess is unlikely without fever or an infectious source.", "image": "question_images/q_770.png"}, {"id": 771, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has recurrent duodenal and jejunal ulcers with diarrhea. Laboratory studies show elevated serum calcium and PTH, elevated prolactin, and markedly elevated fasting gastrin. MRI reveals a pituitary microadenoma. Her mother had hypercalcemia and a pancreatic tumor. Which genetic abnormality most likely explains this family syndrome?", "options": {"A": "MEN1 gene loss of function, removing menin mediated tumor suppression in multiple endocrine glands", "B": "SDHB loss of function impairing succinate dehydrogenase in familial paraganglioma and pheochromocytoma syndromes", "C": "VHL loss of function, stabilizing hypoxia inducible factor in renal, cerebellar, and adrenal medullary tumors", "D": "NF1 neurofibromin loss of function, releasing RAS activity in neural crest tumors and pheochromocytoma", "E": "RET gain of function causing constitutive tyrosine kinase signaling in thyroid C cells and chromaffin cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (MEN1 gene loss of function, removing menin mediated tumor suppression in multiple endocrine glands): The triad of parathyroid hyperplasia, gastrin producing pancreatic islet tumor (Zollinger-Ellison physiology), and pituitary adenoma defines multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1, caused by loss of the menin tumor suppressor. The RET proto-oncogene produces the type 2 syndromes, which feature medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma rather than this combination."}, {"id": 772, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 52-year-old man with obesity and hypertension has no polyuria, polydipsia, or weight loss. At a routine visit, he reports that his father has type 2 diabetes. The physician wants to establish or exclude diabetes with a single blood draw. Which set of results would establish the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Fasting glucose of 131 mg/dL and hemoglobin A1c of 6.8 percent drawn from the same sample", "B": "Hemoglobin A1c of 6.2 percent with fasting plasma glucose of 118 mg/dL on repeat testing", "C": "A two-hour glucose of 168 mg/dL after a 75 gram oral glucose load", "D": "A random glucose of 180 mg/dL measured after a large meal in a patient without symptoms", "E": "A single hemoglobin A1c of 6.8 percent measured in a patient without hyperglycemic symptoms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fasting glucose of 131 mg/dL and hemoglobin A1c of 6.8 percent drawn from the same sample): Without unequivocal hyperglycemia, diagnosis needs two abnormal results, which can come from two different tests in the same sample. A fasting glucose of 126 mg/dL or higher and hemoglobin A1c of 6.5 percent or higher together satisfy this. A single test, a two-hour value under 200, prediabetic values, or a random glucose under 200 do not."}, {"id": 773, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 55-year-old man with long-standing alcohol use has progressive abdominal distension. Paracentesis yields straw-colored fluid with 90 neutrophils per microliter, albumin 1.0 g/dL and total protein 1.2 g/dL, while serum albumin is 2.4 g/dL. Which condition best explains the ascites in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Tuberculous peritonitis with exudative fluid, lymphocyte predominance and a low serum ascites albumin gradient", "B": "Peritoneal carcinomatosis with exudative fluid from tumor seeding and a low serum ascites albumin gradient", "C": "Sinusoidal portal hypertension from cirrhosis, producing high gradient fluid with low protein content", "D": "Congestive heart failure with elevated hepatic venous pressure producing high gradient, protein rich fluid", "E": "Nephrotic syndrome with low oncotic pressure producing transudate and a low gradient between serum and fluid"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sinusoidal portal hypertension from cirrhosis, producing high gradient fluid with low protein content): The serum ascites albumin gradient is 2.4 minus 1.0, or 1.4 g/dL, which exceeds 1.1 and indicates portal hypertension. A total protein of 1.2 g/dL separates cirrhosis from cardiac ascites, where protein is usually above 2.5. The neutrophil count is below 250, so spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is not present."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 78-year-old thin woman with chronic constipation presents with two days of vomiting, abdominal distension, and a tender, firm, irreducible bulge just below the inguinal ligament. She has had a mild cough for a week and takes no regular medications. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Spigelian hernia with obstruction through the aponeurosis lateral to the rectus muscle at the arcuate line", "B": "Incarcerated direct inguinal hernia with obstruction through the inguinal triangle", "C": "Incarcerated indirect inguinal hernia with obstruction at the deep inguinal ring", "D": "Incarcerated femoral hernia with small bowel obstruction through the narrow femoral ring", "E": "Incarcerated obturator hernia with obstruction at the obturator canal and thigh pain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Incarcerated femoral hernia with small bowel obstruction through the narrow femoral ring): A firm, irreducible bulge below the inguinal ligament in an older woman is typical of a femoral hernia, which has a narrow, rigid ring and a high strangulation rate. Inguinal hernias present above the ligament, and obturator hernias seldom produce a palpable groin mass. Early surgical repair is needed."}, {"id": 775, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 63-year-old woman with hypertension reports transient dizziness and visual blurring when she turns her head and extends her neck. Examination shows C5 somatic dysfunction with posterior paraspinal tenderness, and imaging shows mild degenerative change. She asks for a quick high-velocity manipulation. Which treatment plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Defer cervical thrust techniques and use gentle muscle energy or counterstrain after vascular assessment", "B": "Perform cervical high-velocity thrust with rotation held in extension, since the degenerative change is mild", "C": "Treat only the upper thoracic segments with thrust technique and avoid any cervical treatment", "D": "Perform cervical high-velocity thrust in neutral position without rotation to lower risk of vessel injury", "E": "Treat with articulatory technique using repetitive springing through full cervical rotation and extension"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Defer cervical thrust techniques and use gentle muscle energy or counterstrain after vascular assessment): Dizziness and visual blurring with head rotation and extension suggest vertebrobasilar insufficiency, a contraindication to cervical thrust, so gentle indirect or muscle energy options are preferred after vascular assessment. Moving the neck through the provocative positions or thrusting in neutral does not remove the risk."}, {"id": 776, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 14-month-old boy develops sudden coughing and choking while playing with toys at a family picnic, then has persistent right-sided wheeze. He is afebrile with oxygen saturation of 94%. Right-sided breath sounds are decreased. An expiratory chest radiograph is obtained. Which finding is most expected?", "options": {"A": "Hyperinflated right lung with mediastinal shift away from the obstructed side on expiration", "B": "Subglottic narrowing of the tracheal air column producing a steeple appearance on frontal view", "C": "Lobar consolidation of the right lower lobe with air bronchograms and a small effusion", "D": "Bilateral perihilar peribronchial cuffing with symmetric hyperinflation and flattened diaphragms on both views", "E": "Complete right lung collapse with ipsilateral mediastinal shift and elevation of the hemidiaphragm"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hyperinflated right lung with mediastinal shift away from the obstructed side on expiration): A witnessed choking episode followed by unilateral wheeze and decreased breath sounds suggests a bronchial foreign body. A ball-valve obstruction allows air in but not out, so the affected lung stays hyperinflated on expiratory films and the mediastinum shifts away from it. Complete obstruction would instead cause collapse."}, {"id": 777, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A second-year resident realizes that she ordered double the intended enoxaparin dose for a 70-year-old woman with a deep venous thrombosis, and the dose has been given. The patient has no bleeding and is unaware of the error. The attending is in the operating room for two hours. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Tell the patient only if bleeding or a laboratory abnormality later develops from the extra dose", "B": "Wait until the attending returns and decide together whether disclosure is needed since no harm occurred", "C": "Correct the order silently and document only a routine dose change to avoid causing unnecessary alarm", "D": "Report the error to the state medical board before informing the patient or the hospital", "E": "Disclose the error promptly, with expected effects and a monitoring plan, and file an incident report"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Disclose the error promptly, with expected effects and a monitoring plan, and file an incident report): Ethical practice and institutional policy call for prompt disclosure of an error that reached the patient, even when no harm has occurred, along with a plan to monitor and mitigate effects and an internal report. Concealment or waiting for harm to appear undermines trust. Notifying a licensing board first skips the system and the patient."}, {"id": 778, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 42-year-old woman reports spontaneous bloody discharge from the right nipple for 3 weeks. There is no palpable mass or skin change. Compression of the breast near the areolar edge at the 3 o'clock position expresses blood from a single duct opening. Mammography is normal and ultrasound shows a small mass inside a dilated duct. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Fibrocystic change with apocrine cysts and sclerosing adenosis producing diffuse nodular tenderness", "B": "Mammary duct ectasia with periductal inflammation producing thick green discharge from several ducts", "C": "Intraductal papilloma, a benign epithelial growth on a fibrovascular stalk within a lactiferous duct", "D": "Paget disease of the nipple with intraepidermal malignant cells and an eczematous scaly nipple", "E": "Ductal carcinoma in situ with comedo necrosis and extensive calcifications across the breast"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intraductal papilloma, a benign epithelial growth on a fibrovascular stalk within a lactiferous duct): Unilateral, single duct bloody discharge with a small intraductal mass and a normal mammogram points to an intraductal papilloma. Ductal carcinoma in situ can bleed, but it usually brings calcifications and rarely a discrete intraluminal mass at this age. Duct ectasia typically involves several ducts with thick, colored discharge."}, {"id": 779, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 27-year-old man notices a painless, firm right testicular mass that does not transilluminate. Ultrasonography shows a heterogeneous intratesticular mass. Serum alpha-fetoprotein is markedly elevated and beta-hCG is normal. Radical orchiectomy is performed. Which histologic feature is most likely in the specimen?", "options": {"A": "Large anaplastic cells forming papillary and glandular patterns with prominent necrosis and invasion", "B": "Mixed tissues derived from all three germ layers, including cartilage, neural tissue, and gut epithelium", "C": "Sheets of uniform cells with clear cytoplasm, fried egg appearance, and lymphocytic infiltrate", "D": "Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast cells with extensive hemorrhage and necrosis within the tumor", "E": "Perivascular glomeruloid structures around a central capillary, called Schiller-Duval bodies, lined by tumor cells"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Perivascular glomeruloid structures around a central capillary, called Schiller-Duval bodies, lined by tumor cells): A markedly raised alpha-fetoprotein with a normal hCG identifies a yolk sac tumor, which forms Schiller-Duval bodies. Seminoma produces fried egg cells without alpha-fetoprotein, and choriocarcinoma makes hCG. Mixed or embryonal tumors usually show a more varied marker pattern."}, {"id": 780, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with severe COPD on long-term oral prednisone has dyspnea, kyphosis, and thoracic tenderness. A recent radiograph shows diffuse osteopenia with a mild T8 compression deformity. Rib restriction is found bilaterally. Which technique is least appropriate for the rib and thoracic dysfunction?", "options": {"A": "Myofascial release applied to the thoracic paraspinal and rib cage tissues with light tension", "B": "Muscle energy using gentle isometric contraction of muscles attached to the restricted ribs", "C": "Functional technique guiding the rib cage toward the direction of freedom with minimal force", "D": "Counterstrain positioning of the thorax to the position of ease held for about ninety seconds", "E": "High-velocity low-amplitude thrust applied through the thoracic spine at the restrictive barrier"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (High-velocity low-amplitude thrust applied through the thoracic spine at the restrictive barrier): Steroid-induced osteopenia with an existing compression deformity makes a direct thrust at the restrictive barrier risky for fracture, so high-velocity low-amplitude treatment is the technique to avoid. Muscle energy, myofascial release, counterstrain, and functional methods all use low force and remain safe choices for the rib restriction."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 26-year-old man has hemoptysis, hematuria, and anti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies. He is a lifelong nonsmoker and takes no medications. Spirometry is normal. A single-breath carbon monoxide diffusing capacity is measured during active alveolar hemorrhage. Which result is expected, and why?", "options": {"A": "Unchanged, because carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin far more avidly than oxygen and saturates quickly", "B": "Increased, because hemoglobin from extravasated red cells in the alveoli binds the tracer gas", "C": "Unchanged, because the tracer gas diffusion depends only on ventilation, which spirometry shows is normal", "D": "Decreased, because lost capillary surface area from vasculitis reduces the membrane area available", "E": "Decreased, because basement membrane damage thickens the alveolar wall and slows gas transfer"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increased, because hemoglobin from extravasated red cells in the alveoli binds the tracer gas): Blood within the alveolar spaces contains hemoglobin that takes up the inhaled carbon monoxide, so the measured uptake rises. This is a recognized artifact in alveolar hemorrhage syndromes such as anti-GBM disease. Most parenchymal and vascular lung diseases lower the value instead."}, {"id": 782, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 63-year-old man who smokes has two weeks of constipation, polyuria and confusion. Serum calcium is 14.2 mg/dL, intact parathyroid hormone is suppressed, and parathyroid hormone related peptide is elevated. A chest radiograph shows a hilar mass. He is clinically dehydrated. Which treatment should be started first?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous furosemide to promote calcium excretion in the thick ascending limb and relieve volume overload", "B": "Urgent hemodialysis with low calcium dialysate to remove calcium directly from the circulation in symptomatic patients", "C": "Intravenous zoledronic acid to inhibit osteoclast bone resorption and lower the serum calcium for several weeks", "D": "Subcutaneous calcitonin to inhibit osteoclast bone resorption and increase renal calcium excretion within hours", "E": "Isotonic saline infusion to restore volume and increase urinary calcium excretion through reduced proximal reabsorption"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Isotonic saline infusion to restore volume and increase urinary calcium excretion through reduced proximal reabsorption): Hypercalcemia causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and vomiting, so patients are volume depleted, and saline restores filtration and reduces proximal calcium reabsorption with sodium. Zoledronic acid should follow because it needs days to work, and loop diuretics are added only after euvolemia. Suppressed PTH with elevated PTHrP identifies humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy."}, {"id": 783, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 29-year-old woman climbs rapidly to 4,300 m and develops headache, nausea and insomnia the first night. Her breathing is rapid and deep. Before a planned ascent next year, her physician recommends acetazolamide prophylaxis. Which effect of this drug best explains its benefit in preventing acute mountain sickness?", "options": {"A": "Blockade of peripheral chemoreceptors that blunts the hypoxic drive and reduces hyperventilation", "B": "Increased renal erythropoietin release that raises red cell mass and oxygen carrying capacity", "C": "Inhibition of aldosterone that promotes sodium loss and limits fluid shift into brain tissue", "D": "Selective pulmonary vasodilation that lowers pulmonary artery pressure and prevents hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction", "E": "Bicarbonate diuresis producing metabolic acidosis that offsets respiratory alkalosis and sustains hypoxic ventilation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Bicarbonate diuresis producing metabolic acidosis that offsets respiratory alkalosis and sustains hypoxic ventilation): Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, causing bicarbonate loss and a mild metabolic acidosis. This offsets the respiratory alkalosis that otherwise limits the hypoxic drive at high elevation, so ventilation and oxygenation improve. It does not block peripheral chemoreceptors, which are the sensors that drive the hyperventilation."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 19-year-old football player is struck on the outer side of his planted right knee by a helmet and immediately falls. The knee is swollen and unstable to stress, and the tibia translates forward on the femur with the knee flexed. Which combination of structures was most likely torn?", "options": {"A": "Medial collateral ligament, anterior cruciate ligament and the medial meniscus tethered to it", "B": "Lateral collateral ligament, posterior cruciate ligament and the lateral meniscus beside it", "C": "Medial collateral ligament, posterior cruciate ligament and the medial meniscus attached to the capsule", "D": "Lateral collateral ligament, anterior cruciate ligament and the lateral meniscus beside it", "E": "Medial collateral ligament, anterior cruciate ligament and the lateral meniscus beside it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Medial collateral ligament, anterior cruciate ligament and the medial meniscus tethered to it): A lateral blow to a planted knee produces valgus stress, tearing the medial collateral ligament and then the anterior cruciate ligament, and the medial meniscus fails because it is attached to the medial ligament. Anterior tibial translation confirms the anterior cruciate, whereas the lateral meniscus is not tethered to the lateral collateral."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with metastatic pancreatic cancer on a hospice unit has escalating pain despite current dosing. Her husband fears that raising the morphine dose will shorten her life. She is comfortable accepting any risk to obtain relief. The physician increases the opioid dose. Which ethical principle most directly justifies this action?", "options": {"A": "Nonmaleficence, because any intervention that could shorten life violates the duty to avoid causing harm", "B": "Autonomy, because patient consent to risk makes the physician's intent irrelevant to the outcome", "C": "Double effect: the aim is pain relief, and earlier death is only a foreseen side effect", "D": "Justice, because hospice resources should be distributed so that every dying patient receives an equal dose", "E": "Fidelity, because the physician earlier promised the family that the patient would stay comfortable at death"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Double effect: the aim is pain relief, and earlier death is only a foreseen side effect): Double effect permits a treatment with a good intended effect even when a harmful effect is foreseen, if the harm is not the means to the good and the good outweighs it. Opioid titration for pain fits these conditions. Nonmaleficence is respected by proportionate titration, so it does not forbid the dose increase."}, {"id": 786, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 4-year-old unvaccinated child presents to the emergency department with fever, cough, conjunctivitis, and a maculopapular rash that began on the face and spread downward. Koplik spots are noted on the buccal mucosa. The department must place the child in isolation immediately. Which isolation precaution is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Droplet precautions with a surgical mask worn within three feet of the patient", "B": "Airborne precautions in a negative pressure room with an N95 respirator for staff", "C": "Contact precautions with gown and gloves for every entry into the room", "D": "Standard precautions alone with hand hygiene and no private room placement", "E": "Contact plus droplet precautions with a private room and gown and mask"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Airborne precautions in a negative pressure room with an N95 respirator for staff): This presentation is classic measles, which spreads as small aerosolized particles that remain suspended for hours, so the child needs a negative pressure room and staff wearing N95 respirators. Droplet precautions suit organisms such as meningococcus or influenza, whose larger particles travel only short distances. Gown and gloves would not prevent inhalation of the virus."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 36-year-old man with a normal examination, laboratory studies and abdominal imaging reports daily abdominal pain for two years. He visits the clinic monthly, devotes hours to researching causes, and is convinced the pain signals a serious disease despite repeated reassurance. Symptoms are real to him and not feigned. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Malingering, with intentional symptom production to obtain a clear external incentive such as compensation", "B": "Somatic symptom disorder, with excessive thoughts and behaviors devoted to a persistent physical symptom", "C": "Conversion disorder, with a neurologic deficit that is incompatible with recognized neurologic disease patterns", "D": "Illness anxiety disorder, with preoccupation about having a serious disease despite minimal or absent symptoms", "E": "Factitious disorder, with deliberate symptom production to assume the sick role without external reward"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Somatic symptom disorder, with excessive thoughts and behaviors devoted to a persistent physical symptom): Somatic symptom disorder requires one or more distressing somatic symptoms plus disproportionate thoughts, anxiety or time devoted to them, regardless of a medical explanation. Illness anxiety disorder is the near miss, but it applies when symptoms are absent or mild. Factitious disorder and malingering involve conscious falsification, which the stem rules out."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 55-year-old man is admitted after a motor vehicle collision with a lacerated spleen. Heart rate is 128 per minute. Pulmonary artery catheter readings show central venous pressure 2 mm Hg, pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 4 mm Hg, cardiac index 1.8 L/min/m2, and elevated systemic vascular resistance. Which intervention best addresses this hemodynamic state?", "options": {"A": "Norepinephrine infusion to raise vascular tone before any volume replacement is given", "B": "Dobutamine infusion to increase myocardial contractility and raise the depressed cardiac index in this patient", "C": "Furosemide to lower filling pressures and reduce myocardial oxygen demand during tachycardia", "D": "Intravenous nitroglycerin to dilate veins and reduce the elevated afterload against the ventricle", "E": "Rapid infusion of crystalloid and blood products to restore intravascular volume and ventricular preload"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rapid infusion of crystalloid and blood products to restore intravascular volume and ventricular preload): Low filling pressures with a low cardiac index and high resistance indicate hypovolemic shock from hemorrhage. Restoring preload with volume and blood increases stroke volume and cardiac output. Inotropes and vasopressors do not replace lost blood, and diuretics or nitrates would worsen the preload deficit."}, {"id": 789, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 46-year-old teacher has chronic low back pain. The seated flexion test is positive on the left, the standing flexion test is negative, the left sacral sulcus is deep, and the right inferior lateral angle is posterior. The lumbar spring test is negative. She drives two hours daily. Which diagnosis is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Left unilateral sacral flexion with anterior base and posterior apex on the left", "B": "Left posterior innominate rotation with restricted motion at the left iliosacral joint", "C": "Left-on-left forward sacral torsion around a left oblique axis of the sacrum", "D": "Left-on-right backward sacral torsion around a right oblique axis of the sacrum", "E": "Right-on-right forward sacral torsion around a right oblique axis of the sacrum"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Right-on-right forward sacral torsion around a right oblique axis of the sacrum): A positive seated flexion test with a deep left sulcus and a posterior right inferior lateral angle fits a right-on-right forward torsion, and the negative spring test supports a forward rather than backward pattern. A unilateral flexed sacrum would put the posterior angle on the same side as the deep sulcus. A negative standing test argues against a primary innominate problem."}, {"id": 790, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 39-year-old woman who wears narrow high-heeled shoes reports burning pain radiating into the third and fourth toes, relieved by removing her shoes. Squeezing the forefoot from the sides produces a palpable click and reproduces the pain. Which structure is most likely irritated?", "options": {"A": "Flexor hallucis longus tendon, inflamed within its groove behind the talus and sustentaculum tali", "B": "Common plantar digital nerve, pinched beneath the deep transverse metatarsal ligament in the forefoot", "C": "Medial plantar nerve, compressed at the master knot where the tendons cross", "D": "Sesamoid bones of the great toe, fractured beneath the first metatarsal head", "E": "Second metatarsal shaft, stressed by repetitive impact in a runner or dancer"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Common plantar digital nerve, pinched beneath the deep transverse metatarsal ligament in the forefoot): Burning pain into adjacent toes with a Mulder click is Morton neuroma, a perineural fibrosis of the common plantar digital nerve beneath the deep transverse metatarsal ligament. Narrow shoes squeeze the forefoot, which explains the relief when they are removed."}, {"id": 791, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 6-month-old boy has chronic cough, frequent greasy foul-smelling stools, and poor weight gain despite a good appetite. His skin tastes salty to his mother. A sweat chloride level is 85 mEq/L on two occasions. Which molecular defect is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Loss of function of the epithelial sodium channel causing salt wasting in sweat", "B": "Deficiency of alpha-1 antitrypsin allowing unopposed neutrophil elastase injury to lung and liver", "C": "Defective SBDS ribosome maturation causing pancreatic insufficiency with neutropenia and short stature", "D": "Defective dynein arm assembly in ciliary axonemes impairing mucociliary clearance and sperm motility", "E": "Misfolded CFTR protein degraded before reaching the apical membrane of epithelial cells"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Misfolded CFTR protein degraded before reaching the apical membrane of epithelial cells): Chronic cough, pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea, failure to thrive and sweat chloride above 60 mEq/L establish cystic fibrosis. The most common mutation, delta F508, causes misfolding of the chloride channel so it is degraded and never reaches the apical membrane. Primary ciliary dyskinesia has a normal sweat test."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 66-year-old woman hospitalized for pneumonia develops profuse watery diarrhea, lower abdominal cramping, and a temperature of 38.6 C on day 8 of ceftriaxone. White blood cell count is 19,000/mm3. Colonoscopy was performed, and the mucosal view is shown. Which pathogenic mechanism most likely accounts for these findings?", "options": {"A": "Transmural lymphocytic inflammation with granulomas from dysregulated immunity to commensal gut bacteria in the colon", "B": "Shiga toxin removal of adenine from ribosomal RNA, damaging endothelium and colonic epithelium", "C": "Trophozoite invasion of colonic mucosa producing flask-shaped ulcers with a risk of liver abscess", "D": "Colonic ischemia from low flow in the watershed area near the splenic flexure", "E": "Glucosylation of Rho GTPases by secreted toxins, disrupting the actin cytoskeleton and causing epithelial necrosis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Glucosylation of Rho GTPases by secreted toxins, disrupting the actin cytoskeleton and causing epithelial necrosis): Antibiotic exposure disrupts colonic flora, allowing Clostridioides difficile to release toxins A and B that glucosylate Rho GTPases, collapsing the cytoskeleton and producing the adherent yellow-white pseudomembranes seen at colonoscopy. Shiga toxin acts on ribosomes and typically causes bloody diarrhea after beef or produce exposure. Crohn colitis is granulomatous but is not tied to recent cephalosporin therapy.", "image": "question_images/q_792.png"}, {"id": 793, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 52-year-old woman has postprandial burning, regurgitation, and a sliding hiatal hernia confirmed on barium swallow. She prefers a nonpharmacologic option. Examination shows a restricted thoracoabdominal diaphragm and tenderness over the epigastrium. Which osteopathic treatment is most directly aimed at the anatomical problem?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain of anterior costal tender points held about ninety seconds in a position of ease", "B": "Visceral technique with diaphragm doming that draws the herniated stomach inferiorly through the hiatus", "C": "High velocity low amplitude thrust to the cervical spine to modulate vagal tone", "D": "Rib raising over the lower thoracic region to reduce sympathetic tone to the stomach", "E": "Muscle energy for an exhalation dysfunction of the sixth through tenth ribs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Visceral technique with diaphragm doming that draws the herniated stomach inferiorly through the hiatus): A sliding hiatal hernia is a displacement of the stomach through the esophageal hiatus, so a visceral technique with diaphragm doming targets the structure at fault. Rib raising and cervical work may help autonomic tone but do not address the herniation itself. Counterstrain and rib muscle energy treat musculoskeletal tender points, not the stomach position."}, {"id": 794, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 63-year-old man who smoked for forty years reports aching pain along the medial arm and forearm with weakness of grip. Examination shows wasting of the small hand muscles, numbness of the ring and little fingers, and a drooping right eyelid with a smaller pupil. Which structure is most likely being compressed?", "options": {"A": "Median nerve alone, entrapped beneath the transverse carpal ligament at the wrist", "B": "Upper trunk of the brachial plexus together with the phrenic nerve near the scalene", "C": "Posterior cord of the brachial plexus together with the axillary artery in the axilla", "D": "Ulnar nerve alone, entrapped behind the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel", "E": "Lower trunk of the brachial plexus together with the adjacent cervical sympathetic chain"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Lower trunk of the brachial plexus together with the adjacent cervical sympathetic chain): Pain along the medial arm, ulnar hand wasting and Horner syndrome point to an apical lung tumor invading the lower trunk (C8, T1) and the cervical sympathetic chain. A peripheral ulnar or median entrapment cannot produce ptosis and miosis, and an upper trunk lesion would weaken the shoulder rather than the hand."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with diabetic kidney disease and eGFR 18 mL/min/1.73 m2 has bone pain and pruritus. She takes no vitamin supplements. Labs show calcium 7.9 mg/dL, phosphate 6.8 mg/dL, and markedly elevated PTH. Which process is the principal cause of her hypocalcemia?", "options": {"A": "Reduced renal conversion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol, impairing intestinal calcium absorption and mineralization", "B": "Increased calcitonin secretion from medullary thyroid hyperplasia, inhibiting osteoclasts and lowering serum calcium", "C": "Reduced hepatic 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D from cirrhosis, impairing intestinal calcium absorption", "D": "Intestinal malabsorption from celiac disease with villous atrophy reducing calcium and vitamin uptake", "E": "Hypoalbuminemia from nephrotic losses lowering total calcium while ionized calcium remains normal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced renal conversion of 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol, impairing intestinal calcium absorption and mineralization): Failing nephrons lose 1-alpha hydroxylase activity, so calcitriol falls, gut calcium absorption drops and serum calcium decreases. Phosphate retention and low calcitriol then drive secondary hyperparathyroidism and renal osteodystrophy. A raised PTH shows the hypocalcemia is real, which argues against a simple albumin effect."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 6-year-old boy has fulminant appendicitis with peritonitis and needs emergency laparotomy tonight. His parents refuse surgery because of a religious belief that illness should be treated by prayer alone. Surgery is the only reasonable treatment, and delay risks death. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Delay operating until the ethics committee reaches consensus with the parents, even if this takes days", "B": "Honor the refusal and document it, because parents hold authority over medical decisions for their child", "C": "Operate under emergency doctrine or court order, because parental religious refusal cannot override lifesaving care", "D": "Give antibiotics and observe overnight, deferring surgery until the parents change their minds voluntarily", "E": "Obtain the boy's own assent as sufficient consent, since minors may decide about emergency surgery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Operate under emergency doctrine or court order, because parental religious refusal cannot override lifesaving care): When a child faces imminent death from a treatable condition, the state's interest in protecting the child overrides parental refusal, and physicians may treat under emergency doctrine or seek a court order. Respecting refusal fits a competent adult, not a young child. A minor's assent cannot substitute for lawful consent here."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 55-year-old man with frequent postprandial burning and a normal upper endoscopy is started on daily omeprazole before breakfast. His physician explains how the drug lowers gastric acid output through its action on the parietal cell. Which mechanism best describes the action of this drug class?", "options": {"A": "Irreversible inactivation of the hydrogen potassium ATPase on the canalicular membrane", "B": "Synthetic prostaglandin E1 analog binding receptors to decrease cyclic AMP in parietal cells", "C": "Competitive blockade of histamine receptors on the basolateral membrane of parietal cells", "D": "Binding to ulcer base proteins forming a protective viscous barrier over exposed mucosa", "E": "Blockade of muscarinic M3 receptors on parietal cells reducing vagal stimulation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Irreversible inactivation of the hydrogen potassium ATPase on the canalicular membrane): Proton pump inhibitors are activated in the acidic canaliculus and bind covalently to the hydrogen potassium ATPase, blocking the final step of acid secretion. H2 blockers, antimuscarinics, and misoprostol act upstream and are less complete. Sucralfate protects ulcer bases and does not reduce acid output."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 40-year-old woman three months after kidney transplantation has gingival overgrowth, new hirsutism and blood pressure of 158/96 mm Hg. She takes an immunosuppressant that binds a cytosolic immunophilin to block T cell activation. Which drug is most likely responsible for these adverse effects?", "options": {"A": "Mycophenolate mofetil, which inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase and blocks de novo purine synthesis in lymphocytes", "B": "Sirolimus, which binds FKBP-12 and inhibits mTOR, blocking T cell response to interleukin-2 signaling", "C": "Azathioprine, which is converted to 6-mercaptopurine and impairs purine synthesis, causing bone marrow suppression", "D": "Cyclosporine, which binds cyclophilin and inhibits calcineurin, reducing interleukin-2 transcription in T cells", "E": "Tacrolimus, which binds FKBP-12 and inhibits calcineurin, reducing interleukin-2 transcription in T cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cyclosporine, which binds cyclophilin and inhibits calcineurin, reducing interleukin-2 transcription in T cells): Gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism and hypertension are characteristic of cyclosporine, which complexes with cyclophilin to inhibit calcineurin. Tacrolimus shares the calcineurin mechanism but binds FKBP-12 and more often causes tremor, hyperglycemia and alopecia. Sirolimus acts on mTOR, and mycophenolate and azathioprine act on nucleotide synthesis."}, {"id": 799, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 70-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden weakness of the right arm and leg sparing the face. His tongue deviates to the left on protrusion, and vibration and position sense are reduced on the right side of the body. Pain and temperature sensation is normal. Which vessel supplies the infarcted region?", "options": {"A": "Left posterior cerebral artery branches feeding the thalamus and midbrain tegmentum", "B": "Posterior inferior cerebellar artery branches feeding the lateral medulla on the left", "C": "Anterior spinal artery branches from the right vertebral artery feeding the medial medulla", "D": "Anterior spinal artery branches from the left vertebral artery feeding the medial medulla", "E": "Paramedian basilar artery branches feeding the right ventral pons causing contralateral hemiparesis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anterior spinal artery branches from the left vertebral artery feeding the medial medulla): Medial medullary syndrome involves the pyramid (contralateral hemiparesis sparing the face), the medial lemniscus (contralateral loss of vibration and position sense) and the hypoglossal fibers (tongue deviation toward the side of the lesion). The left medulla is infarcted, supplied by anterior spinal artery branches. Spinothalamic fibers lie laterally, which explains the normal pain and temperature sensation."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 66-year-old man has three months of painless jaundice, pruritus, 8 kg weight loss and new onset diabetes. Examination reveals a palpable nontender gallbladder. Total bilirubin is 9 mg/dL with a direct fraction of 7.8 mg/dL and alkaline phosphatase is elevated. Ultrasound shows a dilated common bile duct. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Serum CA 19-9 measurement alone to establish the diagnosis and guide surgical resection decisions", "B": "Multiphase contrast enhanced pancreatic protocol CT to define the mass and vascular involvement", "C": "Hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid scan to evaluate for cystic duct obstruction and gallbladder ejection fraction", "D": "Immediate percutaneous cholecystostomy tube placement to relieve the distended gallbladder before further imaging", "E": "Repeat ultrasound in six weeks to monitor ductal size before pursuing more invasive evaluation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Multiphase contrast enhanced pancreatic protocol CT to define the mass and vascular involvement): Painless jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign), weight loss and new diabetes strongly suggest pancreatic head adenocarcinoma. Pancreas protocol CT defines the mass and vascular involvement, which determines resectability. CA 19-9 supports monitoring but cannot establish the diagnosis."}, {"id": 801, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 63-year-old man with hypertension has sudden double vision. Examination shows right ptosis, a dilated right pupil, and a right eye resting down and out. He also has weakness of the left arm and leg with left lower facial droop. Which structure pair is injured?", "options": {"A": "Left oculomotor fascicles and the adjacent left cerebral peduncle corticospinal fibers", "B": "Right oculomotor nucleus and the adjacent medial longitudinal fasciculus in the tegmentum", "C": "Right oculomotor fascicles and the right red nucleus with cerebellar outflow fibers", "D": "Right abducens fascicles and the adjacent right ventral pontine corticospinal fibers", "E": "Right oculomotor fascicles and the adjacent right cerebral peduncle corticospinal fibers"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Right oculomotor fascicles and the adjacent right cerebral peduncle corticospinal fibers): In Weber syndrome a midbrain infarct involves the exiting oculomotor fascicles, causing ipsilateral ptosis, a dilated pupil and a down and out eye, and the cerebral peduncle, whose corticospinal fibers have not yet crossed, causing contralateral hemiparesis. Corticobulbar fibers explain the contralateral lower face droop. A pontine abducens lesion would instead cause failure of abduction."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 45-year-old man with episodic hypertension and tachycardia has a 3 cm adrenal mass, and plasma metanephrine is elevated more than normetanephrine. A woman with a retroperitoneal paraganglioma near the aortic bifurcation instead has predominantly elevated normetanephrine. Which explains the difference in the catecholamine secreted?", "options": {"A": "Adrenal tumors express more catechol-O-methyltransferase, which methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine before release into the blood", "B": "Paragangliomas are located near sympathetic ganglia where angiotensin II suppresses methylation of norepinephrine to epinephrine", "C": "Adrenal tumors express more monoamine oxidase, which converts norepinephrine into epinephrine within the chromaffin cell", "D": "Extra-adrenal tumors have faster vesicular reuptake of epinephrine, so only norepinephrine accumulates in the plasma pool", "E": "Cortisol draining from the adrenal cortex induces phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase, which methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cortisol draining from the adrenal cortex induces phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase, which methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine): Blood draining from the adrenal cortex bathes the medulla in high cortisol, which induces the enzyme that converts norepinephrine to epinephrine. Extra-adrenal paragangliomas lack this exposure and therefore secrete mainly norepinephrine, producing a normetanephrine predominant pattern. Catechol-O-methyltransferase forms the metanephrine metabolites afterward and does not make epinephrine."}, {"id": 803, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 5-month-old girl has two days of rhinorrhea followed by tachypnea, diffuse wheezes and crackles, and nasal flaring. Temperature is 38.1 C, and oxygen saturation is 95% on room air. She is feeding adequately and attends daycare, where several children have had similar illnesses. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Nebulized hypertonic saline in the office followed by chest physiotherapy to clear secretions", "B": "Oral prednisolone for five days to reduce airway edema and the need for admission", "C": "Scheduled nebulized albuterol every four hours to relieve wheezing and reduce hospital stay", "D": "Supportive care with nasal suctioning and fluids, adding oxygen if saturation falls below 90 percent", "E": "Intravenous azithromycin to eradicate atypical bacterial coinfection such as Mycoplasma and shorten the illness course"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Supportive care with nasal suctioning and fluids, adding oxygen if saturation falls below 90 percent): Bronchiolitis in an infant, most often from respiratory syncytial virus, is managed supportively with hydration, nasal clearing and oxygen only when saturation is persistently low. Bronchodilators, steroids, antibiotics, routine hypertonic saline and chest physiotherapy have not shown consistent benefit."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man with chronic bilateral ankle swelling has normal cardiac, renal, and hepatic evaluations. He is a retired accountant. Examination shows restricted first rib and thoracic inlet motion and reduced excursion of the primary breathing muscle. His osteopathic physician treats these areas to improve fluid return. Which osteopathic model best explains this rationale?", "options": {"A": "Metabolic energy model describing balanced energy expenditure and conservation by the tissues of the body", "B": "Respiratory circulatory model, where thoracic and diaphragm mechanics drive fluid return and tissue nutrition", "C": "Behavioral model addressing psychosocial and lifestyle contributors to health and disease in the patient", "D": "Neurological model describing facilitation and reflex arcs that alter organ and tissue function in segments", "E": "Biomechanical model focusing on postural balance and efficient load transfer through the spine and pelvis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Respiratory circulatory model, where thoracic and diaphragm mechanics drive fluid return and tissue nutrition): Treating the thoracic inlet and the breathing muscle to improve venous and lymphatic return is the respiratory circulatory model. The biomechanical model also involves structure, but its goal is posture and load transfer and not fluid movement."}, {"id": 805, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 30-year-old woman who has been fasting for a week to lose weight is started on a barbiturate for seizures. She develops abdominal pain and dark urine. Heme is normally the end product of a pathway whose first and rate-limiting step is inhibited by heme itself. Which enzyme catalyzes that first step?", "options": {"A": "ALA dehydratase, which condenses two molecules of aminolevulinic acid into porphobilinogen in the cytosol", "B": "Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, which removes carboxyl groups from uroporphyrinogen III in the cytosol of hepatocytes", "C": "Porphobilinogen deaminase, which assembles four porphobilinogen units into the linear tetrapyrrole hydroxymethylbilane", "D": "ALA synthase, which condenses glycine and succinyl-CoA in mitochondria and requires pyridoxal phosphate", "E": "Ferrochelatase, which inserts ferrous iron into protoporphyrin in the mitochondrial matrix as the final step"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (ALA synthase, which condenses glycine and succinyl-CoA in mitochondria and requires pyridoxal phosphate): Aminolevulinate synthase is the rate-limiting, heme-inhibited first step, condensing glycine and succinyl-CoA in mitochondria with pyridoxal phosphate as cofactor. Barbiturates and fasting induce it, which is why they precipitate attacks in porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency. The later enzymes are not subject to this regulation."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with a prior kidney transplant takes azathioprine for immunosuppression. After developing her first gout flares, she is started on a urate lowering drug by her primary care physician. Two weeks later she has fever and mouth ulcers, and the complete blood count shows pancytopenia. Which mechanism best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Uricosuric blockade of renal organic anion transporters raising azathioprine levels through reduced tubular secretion", "B": "Immune complex deposition from drug antibody binding, producing serum sickness with secondary cytopenias", "C": "Xanthine oxidase inhibition blocking breakdown of a thiopurine metabolite, causing toxic accumulation in marrow", "D": "Hypersensitivity reaction to the sulfonamide moiety causing immune destruction of marrow progenitors", "E": "Inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase producing folate deficiency and megaloblastic marrow suppression"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Xanthine oxidase inhibition blocking breakdown of a thiopurine metabolite, causing toxic accumulation in marrow): Azathioprine is converted to 6-mercaptopurine, which xanthine oxidase inactivates. Allopurinol blocks that enzyme, so the active thiopurine metabolites accumulate and cause marrow suppression, and the dose of azathioprine must be reduced by about two thirds. The other mechanisms do not describe this interaction."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 27-year-old woman has ptosis and double vision that worsen late in the day and improve after rest. Sustained upgaze for one minute increases her ptosis. Intravenous edrophonium produces brief improvement of the lid position within seconds. Which mechanism accounts for the improvement?", "options": {"A": "Removal of autoantibodies bound to the acetylcholine receptor through rapid complement inactivation", "B": "Direct stimulation of postsynaptic nicotinic receptors independent of acetylcholine release from the nerve terminal", "C": "Increased acetylcholine persistence in the synaptic cleft allowing more binding to remaining receptors", "D": "Enhanced presynaptic calcium entry increasing the number of acetylcholine vesicles released per impulse", "E": "Restoration of muscarinic receptor signaling in the levator palpebrae superioris muscle fibers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increased acetylcholine persistence in the synaptic cleft allowing more binding to remaining receptors): Edrophonium is a short-acting acetylcholinesterase inhibitor. In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies reduce functional nicotinic receptors, so blocking acetylcholine breakdown lets the transmitter remain in the cleft longer and bind the remaining receptors, briefly restoring end plate potentials. It does not stimulate receptors directly or remove antibodies."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 48-year-old man with chronic bronchitis has increased bronchial secretions and wheeze. Osteopathic structural examination finds a compressed left occipitomastoid suture with a restricted occipital condyle. The physician reasons that the dysfunction could alter parasympathetic input to the airways. Which mechanism most directly links this finding to the respiratory symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Phrenic nerve irritation at the cervical level altering diaphragmatic contraction and airway caliber", "B": "Compression of the pelvic splanchnic nerves changing parasympathetic tone to the bronchial tree", "C": "Altered sympathetic outflow from the T1 to T6 lateral horn cells to the bronchi", "D": "Altered tension at the jugular foramen affecting vagus nerve outflow to bronchial smooth muscle and glands", "E": "Facial nerve compression at the stylomastoid foramen altering secretomotor input to bronchial glands"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Altered tension at the jugular foramen affecting vagus nerve outflow to bronchial smooth muscle and glands): The vagus nerve passes through the jugular foramen, which lies between the occiput and the temporal bone near the occipitomastoid suture, so restriction there can alter its outflow to the bronchi. The thoracic sympathetic supply is a different pathway, and the pelvic splanchnic, facial, and phrenic nerves do not carry the vagal parasympathetic supply to the lungs."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with extensive sun damage has dozens of rough, erythematous macules across her forehead and scalp, clinically consistent with actinic keratoses. Her dermatologist prescribes topical 5-fluorouracil for field treatment. Which mechanism explains the selective destruction of the abnormal rapidly dividing keratinocytes?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase, depleting tetrahydrofolate needed for purine synthesis", "B": "Cross-linking of guanine residues by alkylation, causing strand breaks during replication", "C": "Intercalation between DNA base pairs, blocking topoisomerase II and strand resealing", "D": "Stabilization of microtubules, arresting dividing cells in metaphase of the cell cycle", "E": "Inhibition of thymidylate synthase, depleting thymidine nucleotides needed for DNA synthesis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Inhibition of thymidylate synthase, depleting thymidine nucleotides needed for DNA synthesis): 5-Fluorouracil is a pyrimidine analog whose metabolite forms a stable complex with thymidylate synthase, blocking thymidine production and DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells. Methotrexate targets dihydrofolate reductase, taxanes act on microtubules, anthracyclines intercalate DNA, and alkylators cross-link guanine."}, {"id": 810, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department after ingesting an unknown number of pills during an argument. She has tinnitus, vomiting and rapid deep breathing, and her temperature is 38.4 C. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.47, PaCO2 20 mm Hg and bicarbonate 14 mEq/L, with sodium 140 and chloride 104 mEq/L. What is the acid-base disturbance?", "options": {"A": "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation, from bicarbonate loss caused by vomiting and fever", "B": "Anion gap metabolic acidosis plus separate primary respiratory alkalosis, with carbon dioxide under expected compensation", "C": "Pure primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation, because alkalemia and low carbon dioxide dominate the picture", "D": "Metabolic alkalosis from vomiting with respiratory compensation, and an elevated gap from concurrent volume contraction", "E": "Anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation, and carbon dioxide close to the predicted value"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Anion gap metabolic acidosis plus separate primary respiratory alkalosis, with carbon dioxide under expected compensation): The anion gap is 140 minus 104 minus 14, or 22 mEq/L, so a gap metabolic acidosis is present. The Winter formula predicts a carbon dioxide near 29 mm Hg, but the measured value is 20, so a second primary respiratory alkalosis from direct respiratory center stimulation coexists, the classic salicylate pattern. Simple compensation would leave the pH acidemic."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 70-year-old man, weight 70 kg, is brought from a nursing home with fever and decreased oral intake for five days. He is lethargic with dry mucous membranes. Serum sodium is 160 mEq/L. Assuming total body water is 50 percent of body weight in older men, what free water deficit would bring his sodium to 140 mEq/L?", "options": {"A": "About 4.4 liters, from total body water times sodium excess divided by the measured sodium", "B": "About 5 liters, from total body water times the fractional excess of sodium above 140", "C": "About 40 liters, from total body water multiplied by the ratio of 160 to 140", "D": "About 2.5 liters, from half of the total body water multiplied by the fractional sodium excess", "E": "About 6 liters, from total body water of 42 liters times the fractional sodium excess"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 5 liters, from total body water times the fractional excess of sodium above 140): Free water deficit equals total body water multiplied by the quantity of measured sodium over 140, minus one. With 35 liters of total body water, 35 times 160 over 140 minus 35 gives about 5 liters. A factor of 0.6 would give 6 liters, which ignores the stated lower water fraction in older men. Correction should be slow, lowering sodium by no more than about 10 mEq/L per day."}, {"id": 812, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 66-year-old man with hypertension has low back pain and L4 somatic dysfunction on examination. Over the past 12 hours he has developed urinary retention, bilateral leg weakness, and numbness of the perineum. He asks for a lumbar manipulation to relieve the pain. Which next step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply counterstrain to the tender points and schedule outpatient lumbar radiographs within the week", "B": "Start oral anti-inflammatory therapy with soft tissue treatment and follow up in one week", "C": "Arrange emergency MRI and surgical consultation for suspected cauda equina compression before any spinal treatment", "D": "Perform gentle muscle energy at L4 and reassess neurologic function after the treatment session", "E": "Perform lumbar roll manipulation with high-velocity thrust once pain is controlled with oral analgesics"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Arrange emergency MRI and surgical consultation for suspected cauda equina compression before any spinal treatment): Urinary retention, saddle anesthesia, and bilateral leg weakness are red flags for cauda equina syndrome, which needs emergency imaging and surgical evaluation. Any manipulative treatment, even gentle, delays definitive care, and the somatic dysfunction at L4 does not explain the deficits."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has been unable to conceive for 14 months. She has regular 28-day cycles with premenstrual breast tenderness and a history of untreated chlamydial cervicitis at age 21. Her partner's semen analysis is normal, and her pelvic examination and thyroid studies are normal. Which test is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Diagnostic laparoscopy with chromotubation as the first evaluation before any less invasive tubal imaging", "B": "Hysterosalpingography to assess tubal patency and the uterine cavity contour after her prior pelvic infection", "C": "Late luteal endometrial biopsy to document secretory transformation of the endometrium before any imaging", "D": "Day 3 follicle-stimulating hormone and estradiol to assess ovarian reserve despite regular ovulatory cycles", "E": "Serum prolactin and pituitary imaging to exclude hyperprolactinemic anovulation in a woman with regular menstrual cycles"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hysterosalpingography to assess tubal patency and the uterine cavity contour after her prior pelvic infection): Regular cycles with premenstrual symptoms suggest ovulation, and the semen analysis is normal, so prior chlamydial infection makes tubal scarring the leading cause. Hysterosalpingography is the standard first test of tubal patency and the cavity. Laparoscopy is reserved for cases where imaging suggests pathology or other evaluation fails."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 19-year-old soccer player felt a pop in her right knee when she planted and pivoted. She has immediate swelling and cannot continue playing. On examination, the tibia translates anteriorly relative to the femur when the knee is flexed 20 degrees and pulled forward. Which structure is most likely injured?", "options": {"A": "Posterior cruciate ligament, the primary restraint against the shin sliding behind the thigh", "B": "Medial collateral ligament, the primary restraint against valgus stress across the knee joint", "C": "Anterior cruciate ligament, the primary restraint against the shin sliding ahead of the thigh", "D": "Patellar tendon, the structure that transmits quadriceps force to the tibial tubercle", "E": "Lateral meniscus, the structure that deepens the tibial plateau and absorbs compressive load"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anterior cruciate ligament, the primary restraint against the shin sliding ahead of the thigh): A noncontact pivoting injury with an audible pop, rapid hemarthrosis, and increased anterior tibial translation on Lachman testing indicates an anterior cruciate ligament tear. The posterior cruciate ligament resists posterior translation. Collateral ligament and meniscal injuries produce different instability and examination findings."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 34-year-old man returns from a trip abroad with sudden onset of profuse watery stools resembling rice water, vomiting, and muscle cramps. He has no fever or blood in the stool. Stool microscopy shows no leukocytes, and he is markedly dehydrated. Which toxin mechanism best explains the secretory diarrhea in this infection?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of the inhibitory Gi protein by ADP-ribosylation, persistently raising cyclic AMP in enterocytes", "B": "Glucosylation of Rho GTPases, disrupting the actin cytoskeleton of enterocytes", "C": "Activation of guanylate cyclase by a heat-stable peptide, raising cyclic GMP in enterocytes", "D": "Activation of the stimulatory G protein by ADP-ribosylation, raising cyclic AMP in enterocytes", "E": "Removal of an adenine from ribosomal RNA, halting protein synthesis in enterocytes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Activation of the stimulatory G protein by ADP-ribosylation, raising cyclic AMP in enterocytes): The A subunit of cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the alpha subunit of Gs, locking it active so adenylate cyclase drives cyclic AMP and chloride and water secretion. Inhibiting the inhibitory protein is the pertussis pattern and does not apply here. Heat-stable E. coli toxin acts through cyclic GMP and rarely produces output of this volume."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 45-year-old man treated for latent tuberculosis reports numbness and tingling in both feet after two months of therapy. Examination shows reduced vibration sense distally. His sister took the same medication for a year with no symptoms. Which mechanism best explains his neuropathy, and which supplement would have prevented it?", "options": {"A": "Rapid acetylator status generating hepatotoxic acetyl metabolites, preventable with N-acetylcysteine supplementation given daily", "B": "Slow acetylator status prolonging drug exposure and causing demyelination, preventable with thiamine supplementation", "C": "Slow acetylator status prolonging drug exposure and depleting pyridoxine, preventable with vitamin B6 supplementation", "D": "CYP2D6 poor metabolizer status with parent drug accumulation, preventable with cobalamin supplementation", "E": "Rapid acetylator status producing toxic metabolites, preventable with folic acid supplementation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Slow acetylator status prolonging drug exposure and depleting pyridoxine, preventable with vitamin B6 supplementation): Isoniazid is inactivated by N-acetyltransferase, and slow acetylators accumulate the drug, which binds pyridoxine and increases its urinary loss, causing peripheral neuropathy. Vitamin B6 prevents this. Rapid acetylators are more prone to a different concern and do not develop this neuropathy more readily."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 48-year-old man is hospitalized with cellulitis and starts intravenous nafcillin. Ten days later he develops fever, a diffuse maculopapular rash, and a rise in creatinine from 0.9 to 2.6 mg/dL. Urinalysis shows white blood cells, white blood cell casts, and eosinophils. Which mechanism best explains his kidney injury?", "options": {"A": "Circulating immune complex deposition in glomerular capillaries causing complement activation and hematuria", "B": "Renal vasoconstriction from afferent arteriolar prostaglandin inhibition reducing glomerular perfusion pressure", "C": "Precipitation of drug crystals within distal tubules causing intratubular obstruction and oliguria", "D": "Delayed type hypersensitivity with T cell infiltration of the interstitium after drug exposure", "E": "Direct dose dependent toxicity to proximal tubular cells causing sloughing and muddy brown casts"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Delayed type hypersensitivity with T cell infiltration of the interstitium after drug exposure): Fever, rash, eosinophiluria and a rising creatinine one to two weeks after a beta-lactam define acute interstitial nephritis, a T cell mediated delayed hypersensitivity reaction. Stopping the drug is the key treatment. Acute tubular necrosis would show muddy brown casts and no allergic signs."}, {"id": 818, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis has taken prednisone daily for 10 years and has a DXA T-score of minus 3.2 with a prior vertebral compression fracture. She has painful thoracic somatic dysfunction at T6 to T8 with tender, restricted segments. Which osteopathic approach should the physician avoid at these segments?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain, an indirect technique holding the tender point in a position of ease", "B": "High-velocity low-amplitude thrust, a direct technique that risks fracture in osteoporotic vertebrae and ribs", "C": "Functional technique, an indirect method following palpated tissue motion toward the position of ease", "D": "Facilitated positional release, an indirect method placing the segment in neutral and ease with light compression", "E": "Muscle energy, a direct technique using gentle patient-activated isometric contraction against a counterforce from the physician"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (High-velocity low-amplitude thrust, a direct technique that risks fracture in osteoporotic vertebrae and ribs): Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis with a prior vertebral fracture makes a thrust technique hazardous because direct force at the restrictive barrier can fracture weakened vertebrae or ribs. Counterstrain, functional technique and facilitated positional release apply little force, and low-intensity muscle energy is also acceptable. The somatic dysfunction can still be treated safely."}, {"id": 819, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 31-year-old man with type 1 diabetes takes bedtime NPH insulin and premeal insulin lispro. His overnight continuous glucose profile is shown in the chart, and he reports sweating and anxiety at 3 AM. Dinner and exercise habits are unchanged. Which regimen change best addresses the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Substitute insulin glargine, a basal analog with flat absorption, for the evening intermediate acting insulin", "B": "Increase the bedtime NPH dose to suppress hepatic glucose output responsible for the morning hyperglycemia", "C": "Switch to bedtime regular insulin, whose rapid onset and short duration should prevent overnight lows", "D": "Add rapid acting lispro at bedtime to cover the dawn rise in counterregulatory hormones overnight", "E": "Move the NPH dose to the morning so its peak coincides with the dawn phenomenon"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Substitute insulin glargine, a basal analog with flat absorption, for the evening intermediate acting insulin): NPH peaks roughly 4 to 10 hours after injection, so a bedtime dose peaks in the early morning and causes the nadir near 3 AM, followed by a rebound rise from counterregulatory hormones. A peakless basal analog such as glargine removes the peak. Raising NPH or adding rapid insulin at bedtime would deepen the overnight low.", "image": "question_images/q_819.png"}, {"id": 820, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 52-year-old woman reports difficulty swallowing dry crackers, gritty eyes, and bilateral cheek swelling for a year. Examination shows enlarged parotid glands bilaterally and dental caries at the gum line. Anti-Ro antibodies are positive. Chest radiograph is normal, and she has no uveitis or facial weakness. Lip biopsy shows focal mononuclear infiltrates around ducts. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "HIV associated salivary gland disease, with cystic lymphoepithelial lesions and diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis in the parotids", "B": "Sarcoidosis presenting as uveoparotid fever, with noncaseating granulomas, hilar adenopathy, and cranial nerve VII palsy", "C": "Mumps parotitis, a paramyxovirus infection causing acute painful parotid swelling with fever and possible orchitis", "D": "IgG4 related disease, with storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis, and abundant IgG4 positive plasma cells in glands", "E": "Sjogren syndrome, an autoimmune destruction of salivary and lacrimal glands that also raises lymphoma risk"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Sjogren syndrome, an autoimmune destruction of salivary and lacrimal glands that also raises lymphoma risk): Dry mouth, dry eyes, bilateral parotid enlargement, anti-Ro positivity, and periductal lymphocytic infiltrates on lip biopsy establish Sjogren syndrome. Sarcoidosis can enlarge the parotids but typically shows hilar adenopathy, uveitis, or facial palsy, all absent here. Mumps would be acute and painful rather than chronic."}, {"id": 821, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old man with chronic alcohol use disorder is brought to the emergency department with fever, productive cough of thick, blood-tinged sputum, and confusion. Chest radiograph shows right upper lobe consolidation with a bulging minor fissure. He takes no regular medications and has not been hospitalized recently. Which organism is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Staphylococcus aureus, a gram-positive coccus producing necrotizing pneumonia with cavities after viral infection", "B": "Legionella pneumophila, an intracellular gram-negative rod acquired from contaminated water aerosols in older smokers", "C": "Klebsiella pneumoniae, an encapsulated gram-negative rod producing thick mucoid exudate in debilitated hosts", "D": "Streptococcus pneumoniae, an encapsulated gram-positive diplococcus causing typical lobar pneumonia in older adults", "E": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae, a cell wall deficient organism causing atypical pneumonia in otherwise healthy young adults"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Klebsiella pneumoniae, an encapsulated gram-negative rod producing thick mucoid exudate in debilitated hosts): Chronic alcohol use predisposes to aspiration and to pneumonia from Klebsiella, whose thick capsule produces heavy mucoid, blood-tinged sputum and a lobe so full of exudate that the fissure bulges. Streptococcus pneumoniae is the tempting common cause, but it does not typically give this sticky currant jelly sputum or fissure bulging, and Klebsiella tends to cause abscesses and necrosis."}, {"id": 822, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 45-year-old man smoking one pack daily asks for medication to quit. He had a generalized tonic-clonic seizure after a head injury two years ago and takes no antiseizure medication. He also has binge eating with purging. He relapsed within weeks on nicotine patches alone last year. Which pharmacotherapy is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Bupropion, a dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor that reduces craving and withdrawal irritability", "B": "Nicotine patch monotherapy at a higher dose, delivering steady nicotine to reduce withdrawal and craving", "C": "Nortriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant used as second line to reduce withdrawal symptoms and craving", "D": "Clonidine, an alpha-2 agonist that dampens autonomic withdrawal symptoms, reserved for later lines of therapy", "E": "Varenicline, a partial nicotinic receptor agonist that eases craving and blunts the reward from smoking"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Varenicline, a partial nicotinic receptor agonist that eases craving and blunts the reward from smoking): Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated in seizure disorders and in bulimia, which removes it despite its efficacy. Varenicline is among the most effective single agents, carries no seizure-threshold concern here, and suits a patient whose patch monotherapy failed. Nortriptyline also lowers the threshold and is a later line."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 22-year-old woman with severe nodular acne is about to start isotretinoin. She is sexually active and uses condoms inconsistently. Her menses are regular. Baseline liver tests and lipids are normal. Which step must occur before the first prescription is written?", "options": {"A": "Baseline echocardiography and slit lamp eye examination because of expected cardiac and ocular toxicity", "B": "A trial of oral doxycycline for eight weeks to confirm resistant disease before retinoid use", "C": "Bone density scan and vitamin D level because retinoids rapidly cause osteoporosis in young women", "D": "Negative pregnancy testing and iPLEDGE enrollment with counseling on using two forms of contraception", "E": "A skin biopsy of a nodule to exclude lymphoma before any systemic retinoid is started"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Negative pregnancy testing and iPLEDGE enrollment with counseling on using two forms of contraception): Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen, so US prescribing requires registration in the iPLEDGE program, negative pregnancy testing, and counseling on two forms of contraception. Cardiac and bone imaging and biopsy are not baseline requirements, and a longer antibiotic trial is not mandated when severe nodular disease has already prompted referral."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 52-year-old woman who has had three vaginal deliveries reports urine leakage when she coughs, laughs, or lifts groceries. She has no urgency and no leakage at night. Postvoid residual volume is 30 mL and urinalysis is normal. Examination shows urethral hypermobility with straining. Which mechanism best explains her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Denervated detrusor muscle from diabetic neuropathy retains large residual volumes and leaks when overfilled", "B": "Vesicovaginal fistula after childbirth allows constant urine leakage into the vagina without relation to activity", "C": "Chronic bladder outlet obstruction causes overdistension and continuous dribbling from raised intravesical pressure", "D": "Involuntary detrusor contractions from central inhibitory loss produce sudden urgency with leakage before reaching toilet", "E": "Weak pelvic floor support lets the bladder neck descend, so rising abdominal pressure exceeds closure pressure"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Weak pelvic floor support lets the bladder neck descend, so rising abdominal pressure exceeds closure pressure): Childbirth injury weakens pelvic floor and urethral support, so effort raises bladder pressure above the pressure that keeps the outlet closed, producing stress incontinence. A small residual volume argues against overflow, and the lack of urgency argues against detrusor overactivity. A fistula leaks continuously."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 72-year-old man in septic shock remains hypotensive, with a mean arterial pressure of 55 mm Hg, after 30 mL/kg of crystalloid. His heart rate is 110 per minute and lactate is 4.8 mmol/L. A guideline recommended first-line vasopressor is started. Which drug and primary mechanism describe this choice?", "options": {"A": "Norepinephrine, which constricts arterioles through alpha-1 receptors with modest cardiac stimulation", "B": "Vasopressin, which acts on V1 receptors and is chosen as the initial single agent", "C": "Dobutamine, which stimulates beta-1 receptors to raise contractility and mildly lower vascular resistance", "D": "Isoproterenol, which activates beta receptors to increase heart rate and dilate peripheral arterioles", "E": "Low dose dopamine, which stimulates D1 receptors to dilate renal and mesenteric vessels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Norepinephrine, which constricts arterioles through alpha-1 receptors with modest cardiac stimulation): Norepinephrine is the recommended first-line pressor in septic shock because alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction restores mean arterial pressure while beta-1 effects maintain output. Dobutamine and isoproterenol can lower resistance and worsen hypotension. Vasopressin is typically added to norepinephrine rather than used first."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 32-year-old woman has bloating, iron deficiency anemia unresponsive to oral iron, and an intensely itchy vesicular rash on her elbows. Serum IgA antibodies against tissue transglutaminase are elevated. She recently returned from a hiking trip. Duodenal biopsy would most likely show which finding?", "options": {"A": "Villous blunting with crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes in the duodenal mucosa", "B": "Villous flattening with pear shaped trophozoites adherent to the surface of epithelial cells", "C": "Normal villous height with dilated lacteals and lymphatic obstruction of the lamina propria", "D": "Transmural inflammation with deep fissuring ulcers and noncaseating granulomas in the submucosa", "E": "Lamina propria foamy macrophages filled with periodic acid Schiff positive rod shaped bacilli"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Villous blunting with crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes in the duodenal mucosa): Elevated anti tissue transglutaminase antibody with dermatitis herpetiformis and refractory iron deficiency indicates celiac disease, in which gluten driven T cell injury blunts the villi and expands the crypts. Giardia can also flatten villi but would be identified by trophozoites and would not explain the antibody or the rash."}, {"id": 827, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 34-year-old woman, eight days after a long-haul flight, develops pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea. Heart rate is 104/min and chest radiograph is normal. She has stage 4 chronic kidney disease with a GFR of 22 mL/min/1.73 m2. Clinical probability of pulmonary embolism is high. Which study is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Conventional pulmonary arteriography via catheter, the reference standard for confirming suspected embolism", "B": "Lower extremity compression ultrasound alone, since a negative result excludes embolism in high probability patients", "C": "CT pulmonary angiography with iodinated contrast, the standard first test for stable patients", "D": "D-dimer assay to exclude thromboembolism before any decision about further vascular imaging is made", "E": "Ventilation-perfusion scintigraphy, which avoids iodinated contrast and works best when lung fields are clear"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ventilation-perfusion scintigraphy, which avoids iodinated contrast and works best when lung fields are clear): With a normal chest radiograph, a ventilation-perfusion scan is interpretable, and it spares a patient with advanced kidney disease an iodinated contrast load. D-dimer is only used to exclude embolism when pretest probability is low or intermediate, so it is not appropriate here. Compression ultrasound can support treatment if positive, but a negative study does not exclude embolism."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with trigeminal neuralgia starts a new medication. Three weeks later she is lethargic and mildly confused. Serum sodium is 123 mEq/L, serum osmolality is low, and urine osmolality is inappropriately concentrated. She is euvolemic and takes no diuretics. Which drug most likely caused these findings?", "options": {"A": "Carbamazepine, which enhances renal collecting duct responsiveness to antidiuretic hormone", "B": "Topiramate, which inhibits carbonic anhydrase and produces hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis in the plasma", "C": "Valproate, which raises serum ammonia and produces encephalopathy with preserved serum sodium", "D": "Lamotrigine, which causes dose-related dizziness and rash through sodium channel blockade", "E": "Gabapentin, which accumulates in renal failure and causes sedation without electrolyte disturbance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Carbamazepine, which enhances renal collecting duct responsiveness to antidiuretic hormone): Carbamazepine is first-line for trigeminal neuralgia and is a well-known cause of the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion. The patient is euvolemic with low serum osmolality and concentrated urine. Valproate causes hyperammonemia, and topiramate causes metabolic acidosis rather than hyponatremia."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man has an episode of hypotension during surgery. A liver biopsy taken afterward shows hepatocytes that are enlarged and pale with small clear cytoplasmic vacuoles. Nuclei look normal and there is no inflammation. Which underlying biochemical change most directly explains the swelling of these cells?", "options": {"A": "Calcium influx activating phospholipases and proteases that degrade membrane and cytoskeletal components", "B": "Mitochondrial release of cytochrome c activating caspases that fragment nuclear chromatin in an orderly fashion", "C": "Lipid peroxidation of membranes by hydroxyl radicals generated through the Fenton reaction in the cytosol", "D": "Accelerated anaerobic glycolysis producing lactate and low pH that clumps nuclear chromatin", "E": "Failed Na-K ATPase pump function from ATP depletion, letting sodium and water accumulate"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Failed Na-K ATPase pump function from ATP depletion, letting sodium and water accumulate): Early ischemic injury lowers ATP, so the Na-K ATPase fails and sodium and water enter the cell, producing swelling and clear vacuoles that are still reversible. Calcium influx with membrane degradation belongs to irreversible injury, and caspase activation is apoptosis, not hypoxic swelling."}, {"id": 830, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 52-year-old woman returns for follow-up six weeks after axillary lymph node dissection for breast cancer. When she pushes forward against a wall, the medial border of her left scapula lifts away from the chest wall. Shoulder abduction is intact. Which muscle is most likely paralyzed?", "options": {"A": "Pectoralis minor, supplied by the medial pectoral nerve piercing the clavipectoral fascia", "B": "Latissimus dorsi, supplied by the thoracodorsal nerve crossing the posterior axillary wall to the humerus", "C": "Serratus anterior, supplied by a nerve running superficially along the lateral thoracic wall", "D": "Trapezius, supplied by the spinal accessory nerve crossing the posterior cervical triangle", "E": "Rhomboid major, supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve beneath the levator scapulae muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Serratus anterior, supplied by a nerve running superficially along the lateral thoracic wall): Pushing against resistance makes serratus anterior hold the scapula against the thorax, and its long thoracic nerve is vulnerable on the lateral chest wall during axillary dissection. Latissimus dorsi also lies in the operative field, but its loss weakens adduction and internal rotation rather than producing scapular winging."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with hypertension is brought in 90 minutes after sudden right arm weakness, facial droop, and difficulty finding words, witnessed by her daughter. Blood pressure is 172/94 mm Hg, glucose is 108 mg/dL, and she takes no anticoagulant. Noncontrast head CT shows no hemorrhage or early large infarct. Which intervention is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous thrombolytic therapy to restore flow through the occluded intracranial vessel within the treatment window", "B": "Aggressive intravenous antihypertensive therapy to bring systolic pressure below one hundred twenty within the hour", "C": "Oral aspirin alone with admission for observation until confirmatory diffusion imaging can be completed", "D": "Emergent decompressive craniectomy to relieve pressure from the expanding infarcted hemisphere in the left side", "E": "Intravenous unfractionated heparin infusion to halt clot propagation in the symptomatic cerebral artery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous thrombolytic therapy to restore flow through the occluded intracranial vessel within the treatment window): She is within the window, has a disabling deficit, normal glucose, no anticoagulant, and CT excluding hemorrhage, so thrombolysis is indicated. Her pressure of 172/94 is below the threshold that must be lowered before treatment, and permissive hypertension is otherwise tolerated. Heparin and aspirin alone do not substitute for reperfusion, and craniectomy is reserved for later malignant edema."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 17-year-old boy undergoes colonoscopy because his father had a colectomy at age 32 for numerous polyps and colon cancer. The examination shows more than 100 adenomatous polyps carpeting the colon. Prophylactic colectomy is advised. Which gene is most likely mutated in this family?", "options": {"A": "MLH1 mismatch repair gene, producing microsatellite instability and predominantly right sided tumors", "B": "APC tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q, which regulates beta catenin degradation", "C": "SMAD4 signaling gene, associated with juvenile polyps and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia", "D": "STK11 kinase gene, associated with hamartomatous polyps and mucocutaneous pigmentation of the lips", "E": "MUTYH base excision repair gene, producing a milder attenuated polyposis with biallelic inheritance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (APC tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q, which regulates beta catenin degradation): Familial adenomatous polyposis is autosomal dominant from mutation of the APC gene on chromosome 5q, which allows beta catenin accumulation and produces hundreds of adenomas with near certain cancer without colectomy. Lynch syndrome produces few polyps, and hamartomatous syndromes involve STK11 or SMAD4."}, {"id": 833, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 36-year-old woman with endometriosis receives a long-acting depot injection of a gonadotropin-releasing hormone agonist. After an initial flare of symptoms, she develops hot flashes and amenorrhea. Her serum estradiol is low. Which mechanism best explains the sustained hypoestrogenic state?", "options": {"A": "Increased hepatic clearance of estradiol through induction of cytochrome enzymes, lowering circulating hormone concentrations", "B": "Direct blockade of ovarian estrogen receptors, which stops granulosa cells from producing estradiol", "C": "Competitive antagonism at gonadotropin releasing hormone receptors, preventing the early flare in gonadotropin release", "D": "Inhibition of aromatase in granulosa cells, which blocks conversion of androgens to estrogens", "E": "Continuous receptor occupation downregulates pituitary gonadotrophs, lowering luteinizing and follicle stimulating hormone secretion"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Continuous receptor occupation downregulates pituitary gonadotrophs, lowering luteinizing and follicle stimulating hormone secretion): Pulsatile GnRH stimulates gonadotrophs, but continuous agonist exposure first causes a flare and then receptor downregulation and desensitization. Luteinizing and follicle stimulating hormone fall, ovarian estradiol production drops, and menopausal symptoms follow. Antagonists block the receptor from the start with no flare, which distinguishes them from the agonist mechanism here."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 46-year-old woman twisted her left knee rising from a deep squat and felt a pop. The knee swelled the next day and now intermittently locks short of full extension. There is medial joint line tenderness and a click on McMurray testing, while Lachman test is negative. Which structure is injured?", "options": {"A": "Medial collateral ligament, with a sprain from valgus stress along the medial joint", "B": "Patellar cartilage, with chondromalacia causing crepitus and pain on climbing daily stairs", "C": "Lateral meniscus, with a radial tear at the popliteal hiatus on the outer side", "D": "Anterior cruciate ligament, with a rupture causing anterior tibial translation under stress", "E": "Medial meniscus, with a displaced bucket-handle fragment wedged within the femorotibial compartment"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Medial meniscus, with a displaced bucket-handle fragment wedged within the femorotibial compartment): A twisting injury with joint line tenderness, a McMurray click and true mechanical locking indicates a meniscal tear, and the medial side is shown by the tenderness. A negative Lachman test argues against anterior cruciate rupture, and a collateral sprain would give laxity to valgus stress rather than locking."}, {"id": 835, "domain": "community_health", "question": "In a ten-year cohort study of hospital nurses, 50 of 1,000 night-shift nurses and 100 of 4,000 day-shift nurses developed type 2 diabetes mellitus. The investigators use day-shift nurses as the reference group and report results for the full follow-up period. Which value best describes the association?", "options": {"A": "Attributable risk of 2.0 percent, indicating the excess incidence among nurses with the exposure", "B": "Relative risk of 2.0, indicating twice the cumulative incidence among nurses with the exposure", "C": "Odds ratio of 2.0, required because cohort studies cannot yield direct risk estimates", "D": "Relative risk of 2.5, indicating the absolute excess incidence among nurses with the exposure", "E": "Relative risk of 0.5, indicating half the cumulative incidence among nurses with the exposure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Relative risk of 2.0, indicating twice the cumulative incidence among nurses with the exposure): Cumulative incidence is 5 percent in night-shift nurses (50 of 1,000) and 2.5 percent in day-shift nurses (100 of 4,000), so relative risk is 5 divided by 2.5, or 2.0. The absolute excess is 2.5 percentage points, which is a separate measure and not the relative risk. Cohort designs yield risks directly, so reporting an odds ratio is unnecessary."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 48-year-old man with cirrhosis and medium sized esophageal varices with red wale signs, and no prior bleeding, is started on nadolol to lower his risk of first hemorrhage. He has no asthma or heart block and his resting heart rate is 82 per minute. Which effect accounts for the fall in portal pressure?", "options": {"A": "Reduced cardiac output through beta-1 blockade plus splanchnic vasoconstriction from unopposed alpha activity after beta-2 blockade", "B": "Direct dilation of intrahepatic sinusoids through nitric oxide release, reducing intrahepatic vascular resistance", "C": "Splanchnic vasoconstriction through vasopressin V1 receptor stimulation, as with terlipressin, lowering portal venous inflow", "D": "Diuresis lowering plasma volume through aldosterone antagonism, reducing hepatic venous pressure gradient directly", "E": "Direct relaxation of esophageal smooth muscle, decreasing varix wall tension without altering portal blood flow"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced cardiac output through beta-1 blockade plus splanchnic vasoconstriction from unopposed alpha activity after beta-2 blockade): Nonselective beta blockers lower portal inflow by reducing cardiac output through beta-1 blockade and by blocking splanchnic beta-2 mediated vasodilation, leaving alpha tone to constrict mesenteric vessels. Terlipressin also constricts splanchnic vessels but through vasopressin receptors, so it is not the mechanism of nadolol."}, {"id": 837, "domain": "human_development", "question": "An 82-year-old woman in a nursing home, previously conversant and ambulatory with mild memory loss, becomes inattentive, drowsy by day, and agitated at night over two days. She has a temperature of 38.2 C, and urinalysis shows pyuria and nitrites. Which diagnosis best explains her mental status change?", "options": {"A": "Dementia with Lewy bodies with well-formed visual hallucinations and parkinsonism alongside fluctuating cognition", "B": "Delirium due to acute infection superimposed on mild underlying cognitive impairment in an older resident", "C": "Alzheimer disease progression, which characteristically causes gradual decline over years with preserved arousal until late", "D": "Major depressive disorder with pseudodementia causing apathy and effortful answers of I do not know", "E": "Vascular dementia with stepwise decline following small cerebral infarcts and focal neurologic findings on examination"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Delirium due to acute infection superimposed on mild underlying cognitive impairment in an older resident): Acute onset over days, inattention, fluctuating arousal, and an infectious trigger define delirium, which is common in older patients with baseline cognitive impairment. Alzheimer disease progresses over years and preserves attention until late. Treatment targets the infection and precipitating factors."}, {"id": 838, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 16-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department 5 hours after ingesting an over-the-counter analgesic. She is nauseated but alert, with a respiratory rate of 14 and a normal arterial pH. Serum drug concentration plotted on a treatment nomogram falls above the treatment line. Liver enzymes are still normal. Which antidote is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Sodium bicarbonate, alkalinizing the urine to enhance renal excretion of the ingested analgesic", "B": "Intravenous N-acetylcysteine, replenishing glutathione stores to neutralize the reactive toxic metabolite of CYP oxidation", "C": "Activated charcoal alone, adsorbing residual drug in the gut before hepatic metabolism occurs", "D": "Naloxone, reversing opioid receptor activation that follows ingestion of an analgesic overdose", "E": "Fomepizole, inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase to block formation of the toxic metabolite in the liver"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous N-acetylcysteine, replenishing glutathione stores to neutralize the reactive toxic metabolite of CYP oxidation): The nomogram and normal acid-base status indicate acetaminophen, whose toxic metabolite NAPQI overwhelms glutathione and injures the liver. N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione and works best when started early, even before enzymes rise. Charcoal helps only within a short window after ingestion and does not replace the antidote."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 34-year-old uninsured woman at term arrives at a small emergency department with regular contractions every three minutes and a cervix dilated to 7 cm. A clerk suggests sending her to the county hospital because the department cannot bill for the delivery. Which statement best reflects the department's legal obligation?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a payment guarantee first, then begin the obstetric screening once clearance is documented", "B": "Treat her only if she is a citizen or lawful resident, as the law limits duties", "C": "Transfer her promptly to the county hospital, since emergency departments may decline nonpaying uninsured patients", "D": "Treat her only if the delivery is judged an emergency by the hospital administrator on duty", "E": "Perform screening and stabilizing care, including delivery, before any transfer, with payment status playing no role"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Perform screening and stabilizing care, including delivery, before any transfer, with payment status playing no role): EMTALA requires a hospital emergency department to screen anyone who presents and to stabilize an emergency condition, including active labor, before transfer, whatever the person's ability to pay or immigration status. Payment checks or citizenship checks cannot delay screening. A transfer before stabilization is allowed only under narrow conditions that do not include inability to bill."}, {"id": 840, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 27-year-old woman delivers a 3.1 kg infant vaginally after a rapid second stage. The placenta delivers intact. Thirty minutes later she has persistent bright red bleeding despite a firm, contracted uterus palpated at the umbilicus. Vital signs show heart rate 112/min and blood pressure 98/60 mm Hg. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Retained placental fragments preventing effective uterine contraction and sustaining bleeding from the implantation site", "B": "Coagulopathy from consumption of clotting factors after an undetected concealed placental abruption before labor", "C": "Cervical or vaginal laceration causing ongoing venous or arterial bleeding from the birth canal", "D": "Uterine atony from failure of the myometrium to contract and compress spiral arteries", "E": "Uterine inversion with the fundus prolapsing through the cervix after traction on the cord"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cervical or vaginal laceration causing ongoing venous or arterial bleeding from the birth canal): Bleeding that continues with a firm, contracted uterus and an intact placenta points to trauma of the cervix or vaginal wall, especially after a rapid delivery. Atony causes a soft, boggy fundus, and inversion removes the palpable fundus. Careful inspection and repair of the birth canal controls this bleeding."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 19-year-old with sickle cell disease has scleral icterus during a hemolytic crisis. Stools are normal brown. A urine dipstick shows no bilirubin despite a total serum bilirubin of 5 mg/dL, and urobilinogen is increased. Which explanation accounts for the absent urine bilirubin in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Albumin binding of unconjugated bilirubin prevents glomerular filtration, so none reaches the urine", "B": "Renal tubules reabsorb all filtered conjugated bilirubin before it can appear in voided urine", "C": "Intestinal bacteria convert bilirubin to stercobilin before it can enter the portal circulation", "D": "Biliary obstruction prevents conjugated bilirubin from reaching the intestine and therefore the kidney", "E": "Hepatic conjugation is deficient, so bilirubin leaves only as urobilinogen in stool"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Albumin binding of unconjugated bilirubin prevents glomerular filtration, so none reaches the urine): In hemolysis the excess is unconjugated bilirubin, which is lipid soluble and tightly albumin bound, so it is not filtered and the urine stays acholuric. Increased urobilinogen reflects the larger conjugated load handled by gut bacteria and enterohepatic recycling. Conjugated bilirubin, being water soluble, would appear in urine in cholestasis."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 52-year-old woman who has never smoked has a peripheral lung nodule. Biopsy shows adenocarcinoma, and molecular testing reveals an exon 19 deletion in the EGFR gene. Staging imaging shows a single brain metastasis. She asks about oral targeted therapy rather than chemotherapy. Which agent is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Osimertinib, a third generation inhibitor of the mutant epidermal growth factor receptor tyrosine kinase", "B": "Bevacizumab, a monoclonal antibody against vascular endothelial growth factor, avoided in squamous histology", "C": "Imatinib, an inhibitor of BCR-ABL and KIT tyrosine kinases used in chronic myeloid leukemia", "D": "Pembrolizumab, a programmed death 1 antibody most useful in tumors lacking targetable driver mutations", "E": "Crizotinib, an inhibitor of the fusion kinase created by anaplastic lymphoma kinase gene rearrangements"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Osimertinib, a third generation inhibitor of the mutant epidermal growth factor receptor tyrosine kinase): An exon 19 deletion in the epidermal growth factor receptor gene is a sensitizing mutation, and a tyrosine kinase inhibitor such as osimertinib is preferred first line, with good central nervous system penetration. Crizotinib is tempting because ALK rearrangement also occurs in never smokers, but this tumor carries a different driver. Immunotherapy has little benefit in tumors with this mutation."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A physician must tell a 52-year-old woman that her biopsy shows pancreatic adenocarcinoma. Using a structured six-step protocol for delivering bad news, she prepares before entering the room by arranging privacy, sitting down and silencing her pager. Which step of the protocol is she performing?", "options": {"A": "Knowledge, giving the diagnosis in plain language after a brief warning shot that bad news follows", "B": "Empathy, responding to the patient's emotions by acknowledging and naming what she appears to feel", "C": "Setting, creating a calm environment with seating and few interruptions before the discussion starts", "D": "Invitation, asking how much information the patient wants to receive about the diagnosis and prognosis", "E": "Perception, asking the patient what she already understands about her illness before sharing results"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Setting, creating a calm environment with seating and few interruptions before the discussion starts): The first step of the SPIKES protocol is Setting up the interview, which includes privacy, sitting down, involving relevant others and managing interruptions such as a pager. The Perception and Invitation steps occur after this preparation inside the conversation. Knowledge and Empathy follow later still."}, {"id": 844, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 28-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has been on sulfasalazine for four months. His diarrhea has improved, but he now reports fatigue, pallor, and difficulty conceiving with his partner. Complete blood count shows macrocytic anemia, and semen analysis shows a reduced sperm count. Which feature of the drug best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Purine analog toxicity causing marrow suppression with low thiopurine methyltransferase activity", "B": "Oxidative hemolysis from glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency triggered by the sulfonamide component", "C": "Mesalamine moiety causing tubulointerstitial nephritis with secondary erythropoietin deficiency and anemia", "D": "Tumor necrosis factor blockade causing hemolytic anemia and testicular inflammation in susceptible patients", "E": "Sulfapyridine moiety impairing folate absorption, with reversible reduction in sperm number"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Sulfapyridine moiety impairing folate absorption, with reversible reduction in sperm number): Sulfasalazine is split into mesalamine and sulfapyridine, and the sulfapyridine part impairs folate absorption and reversibly lowers sperm count. This yields macrocytic anemia that responds to folate. Mesalamine nephritis would not cause macrocytosis, and the other options describe drugs the patient is not receiving."}, {"id": 845, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 68-year-old woman reports exertional syncope and has a harsh systolic ejection murmur radiating to the carotid arteries. Her left ventricular pressure-volume loop, recorded at catheterization, is shown with a normal loop for comparison. Which hemodynamic abnormality best explains the changes seen in her loop?", "options": {"A": "Increased contractility from inotrope exposure, shifting the end-systolic point upward and leftward", "B": "Increased preload from a regurgitant valve, widening the loop and raising end-diastolic volume", "C": "Reduced contractility from dilated cardiomyopathy, shifting the end-systolic point downward and rightward", "D": "Reduced preload from hypovolemia, shifting the loop leftward with lower end-diastolic volume", "E": "Increased afterload from a stenotic outflow valve, raising peak pressure and reducing stroke volume"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Increased afterload from a stenotic outflow valve, raising peak pressure and reducing stroke volume): In aortic stenosis the ventricle must generate a higher peak pressure to open a stenotic valve, so the loop is tall; ejection is limited, so stroke volume falls and end-systolic volume rises, making the loop narrower. Regurgitation would instead widen the loop and raise end-diastolic volume, the opposite of the tall, narrowed loop shown.", "image": "question_images/q_845.png"}, {"id": 846, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 30-year-old woman develops right upper quadrant pain 30 minutes after eating a cheeseburger. Fatty acids and amino acids entering the duodenum cause release of a hormone from mucosal I cells. Which hormone and action best explain the gallbladder contraction that triggers her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Somatostatin, causing gallbladder contraction while inhibiting insulin, glucagon and gastric acid release", "B": "Cholecystokinin, causing gallbladder contraction, sphincter of Oddi relaxation and pancreatic enzyme secretion", "C": "Gastrin, causing gallbladder contraction through direct parietal cell stimulation and sphincter of Oddi relaxation", "D": "Motilin, causing gallbladder contraction during fasting as part of the migrating motor complex", "E": "Secretin, causing gallbladder contraction and release of bicarbonate rich fluid from pancreatic ductal cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cholecystokinin, causing gallbladder contraction, sphincter of Oddi relaxation and pancreatic enzyme secretion): Cholecystokinin is released by I cells in response to fat and protein, contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi and stimulates acinar enzyme secretion. Secretin is tempting since it is also a duodenal hormone, but it is released by acid and drives bicarbonate secretion."}, {"id": 847, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 66-year-old retired shipyard insulation worker has progressive dyspnea, chest wall pain, and weight loss. CT shows unilateral pleural thickening with a large effusion. Biopsy shows spindle and epithelioid cells that stain positive for calretinin. He smoked for 30 years. Which statement about the cause of this tumor is correct?", "options": {"A": "Risk arises from beryllium sensitization, which produces noncaseating granulomas that undergo malignant change in the pleura", "B": "Risk is multiplied by tobacco smoke in asbestos workers, as it is for bronchogenic carcinoma", "C": "Risk is greatest with silica exposure, since silicosis fibrosis transforms pleural mesothelial cells into malignant cells", "D": "Risk follows asbestos exposure after decades of latency and is not increased by cigarette smoking", "E": "Risk appears within about five years of exposure and declines once the worker leaves the job"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Risk follows asbestos exposure after decades of latency and is not increased by cigarette smoking): Malignant mesothelioma is tied to asbestos exposure with a latency of 20 to 40 years, and unlike bronchogenic carcinoma, smoking does not add to the risk. Calretinin positivity supports mesothelial origin. Silica and beryllium cause other pneumoconioses but are not recognized causes of this tumor."}, {"id": 848, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 30-year-old man with multiple sclerosis has painful leg spasticity that interferes with walking. His physician prescribes a medication that acts at spinal cord synapses and may cause sedation and, if stopped abruptly, seizures and hallucinations. Which mechanism of action explains this drug's effect?", "options": {"A": "Agonism of presynaptic GABA B receptors reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release in the dorsal horn", "B": "Alpha 2 adrenergic agonism reducing spinal motor neuron firing through presynaptic inhibition", "C": "Antagonism of central muscarinic receptors with tricyclic structure causing anticholinergic muscle relaxation", "D": "Blockade of ryanodine receptors in skeletal muscle sarcoplasmic reticulum preventing calcium release", "E": "Positive allosteric modulation of GABA A chloride channels increasing frequency of channel opening"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Agonism of presynaptic GABA B receptors reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release in the dorsal horn): Baclofen is a GABA B agonist that hyperpolarizes presynaptic terminals and reduces excitatory transmission in the spinal cord, treating spasticity. Abrupt cessation, particularly of intrathecal therapy, can cause seizures, hallucinations, and rebound spasticity. Dantrolene, tizanidine, and benzodiazepines act through different targets."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 31-year-old woman has episodes of optic neuritis and leg weakness. MRI shows periventricular white matter lesions. Visual evoked potentials show prolonged P100 latency with preserved amplitude in the left eye. Which change in axonal electrical properties best explains the delay?", "options": {"A": "Widened nodal gaps allowing saltatory conduction to skip several nodes at increased speed", "B": "Reduced sodium channel density at the nodes of Ranvier raising the threshold of each node", "C": "Failure of sodium potassium ATPase activity causing progressive depolarization and loss of the resting gradient", "D": "Increased axon diameter raising internal resistance so current travels more slowly along the axon", "E": "Demyelination raising capacitance and lowering resistance so current dissipates before reaching the next node"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Demyelination raising capacitance and lowering resistance so current dissipates before reaching the next node): Myelin raises membrane resistance and lowers capacitance, letting current flow along the axon to the next node of Ranvier with minimal loss. Demyelination in multiple sclerosis reverses both properties, so more current leaks and the length constant shortens, delaying or blocking saltatory conduction. A larger diameter lowers internal resistance and would speed conduction, not slow it."}, {"id": 850, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 24-year-old woman develops bloating, flatulence, and watery diarrhea about an hour after drinking a milkshake, and symptoms stop when she fasts. Stool pH is 5.0 and the stool osmotic gap is increased. Small bowel biopsy would show normal villi. Which mechanism best accounts for the diarrhea?", "options": {"A": "Excess bile acids reaching the colon and stimulating electrolyte and water secretion", "B": "Toxin mediated activation of adenylate cyclase driving chloride and water secretion into the lumen", "C": "Unabsorbed lactose drawing water into the lumen and fermenting into gas and organic acids", "D": "Pancreatic lipase deficiency leaving dietary triglycerides undigested and increasing fecal fat content", "E": "Gluten triggered T cell injury flattening villi and reducing all brush border enzyme activity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Unabsorbed lactose drawing water into the lumen and fermenting into gas and organic acids): Lactase deficiency leaves lactose in the lumen, where it is osmotically active and fermented by colonic bacteria to gas and acids, lowering stool pH and creating an osmotic gap that resolves with fasting. Secretory diarrhea has a normal gap and continues during fasting, and celiac disease would show villous blunting."}, {"id": 851, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A newborn boy has a left foot held in equinus with heel varus, forefoot adduction, and a high arch. The foot cannot be passively corrected to neutral and the calf is smaller than the right. Spine and hip examinations are normal. Which is the best initial treatment?", "options": {"A": "Serial manipulation and casting by the Ponseti method, usually followed by percutaneous Achilles tenotomy", "B": "Corrective shoes joined by a Denis Browne bar worn full time from the first week", "C": "Observation with parental stretching until walking age, since most rigid deformities correct spontaneously", "D": "Triple arthrodesis to fuse the subtalar, talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints in infancy", "E": "Posteromedial soft tissue release surgery performed at three months for early deformity correction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Serial manipulation and casting by the Ponseti method, usually followed by percutaneous Achilles tenotomy): Congenital talipes equinovarus is treated first with weekly serial manipulation and casting by the Ponseti method, often with percutaneous Achilles tenotomy, then abduction bracing to prevent relapse. Observation is appropriate only for positional deformities that correct easily. Surgery and fusion are reserved for resistant or neglected cases."}, {"id": 852, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 45-year-old mail sorter develops fever, dry cough, and chest tightness that briefly improve, then return with dyspnea and shock. Chest radiograph shows a widened mediastinum without infiltrates. Blood cultures grow large gram-positive rods in chains. Public health is notified. Which organism and virulence mechanism best explain this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Clostridium perfringens releasing alpha toxin lecithinase that lyses cell membranes and causes hemolysis", "B": "Bacillus cereus producing a heat-stable emetic toxin that acts within hours of ingestion", "C": "Corynebacterium diphtheriae secreting an ADP-ribosylating toxin that inhibits elongation factor 2", "D": "Bacillus anthracis producing edema factor and lethal factor with an antiphagocytic poly-D-glutamate capsule", "E": "Yersinia pestis delivering Yop proteins and F1 capsule antigen to evade phagocytosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bacillus anthracis producing edema factor and lethal factor with an antiphagocytic poly-D-glutamate capsule): Inhalational anthrax produces biphasic illness with hemorrhagic mediastinitis and large encapsulated gram-positive rods on culture. Edema factor is an adenylate cyclase and lethal factor is a protease, both delivered by protective antigen. Yersinia pestis can also cause a bioterrorism pneumonia, but it is a gram-negative bipolar staining rod and does not match the culture finding."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 30-year-old man has an abscess incised and drained in the emergency department. The physician injects lidocaine into the surrounding inflamed tissue but the patient still feels significant pain. Local tissue pH is acidic at about 6.8. Which of the following best explains the poor anesthesia?", "options": {"A": "Lidocaine binds more tightly to plasma proteins in acidic fluid, lowering the free fraction available", "B": "Infected tissue contains esterases that rapidly hydrolyze lidocaine before it reaches the nerve", "C": "Bacterial toxins upregulate voltage-gated sodium channels, requiring higher concentration for blockade", "D": "Acidity raises the ionized fraction of the weak base, limiting penetration into the axon", "E": "Inflammation increases blood flow, causing rapid systemic absorption and removal of lidocaine from the site"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acidity raises the ionized fraction of the weak base, limiting penetration into the axon): Lidocaine is a weak base, and in acidic inflamed tissue more of it is protonated, but only the uncharged form crosses the nerve membrane to reach the sodium channel from inside. Amides are cleared by the liver rather than tissue esterases, and increased perfusion plays a smaller role than the pH effect."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 45-year-old man with lung cancer develops dyspnea, hypotension, and distended neck veins with muffled heart sounds. During quiet inspiration his systolic blood pressure falls from 118 to 94 mm Hg. Echocardiography shows a large pericardial effusion with right atrial collapse. Which mechanism best explains the exaggerated inspiratory fall in systolic pressure?", "options": {"A": "Direct compression of the aortic root by the effusion lowers systolic ejection during inspiration", "B": "Inspiratory rise in right ventricular filling bows the septum leftward and lowers left ventricular stroke volume", "C": "Loss of parasympathetic tone during inspiration slows heart rate and reduces overall cardiac output", "D": "Inspiratory fall in intrathoracic pressure pools blood in the pulmonary veins and traps left atrial return", "E": "Inspiratory decrease in systemic venous return empties the right ventricle and lowers left ventricular filling"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inspiratory rise in right ventricular filling bows the septum leftward and lowers left ventricular stroke volume): With a fixed pericardial volume, inspiration increases right ventricular filling and the septum shifts toward the left ventricle. Left ventricular filling and stroke volume fall, producing pulsus paradoxus greater than 10 mm Hg. Pulmonary vein pooling explains only a small normal fall, and venous return actually increases with inspiration."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 62-year-old man has tense bullae on the flexor surfaces of the arms and inner thighs that have persisted for two months. Oral mucosa is unaffected. Nikolsky sign is negative, and direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin shows linear deposits along the basement membrane zone. Which antigen is targeted by the autoantibodies?", "options": {"A": "Desmoglein 3 in desmosomes of the suprabasal epidermal layer linking adjacent keratinocytes", "B": "Epidermal transglutaminase complexed with IgA deposits in the tips of the dermal papillae", "C": "Hemidesmosomal proteins BP180 and BP230 anchoring basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane", "D": "Desmoglein 1 in desmosomes of the superficial granular layer linking adjacent keratinocytes", "E": "Type VII collagen of the anchoring fibrils securing the dermis to the lamina densa"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hemidesmosomal proteins BP180 and BP230 anchoring basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane): Bullous pemphigoid features tense subepidermal bullae in older adults, a negative Nikolsky sign and linear basement membrane IgG and C3 caused by antibodies to BP180 and BP230. Desmogleins are the targets in pemphigus, which gives flaccid intraepidermal blisters with oral erosions."}, {"id": 856, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old man with COPD has marked dyspnea. Palpation during deep breathing shows right ribs 5 through 7 fail to rise fully on inhalation, with tenderness over all three ribs. The physician plans muscle energy treatment. Which diagnosis and key rib should be treated?", "options": {"A": "Exhalation dysfunction of ribs 5 to 7, treating rib 6 as the key rib", "B": "Exhalation dysfunction of ribs 5 to 7, treating rib 7 as the key rib", "C": "Inhalation dysfunction of ribs 5 to 7, treating rib 5 as the key rib", "D": "Inhalation dysfunction of ribs 5 to 7, treating rib 7 as the key rib", "E": "Exhalation dysfunction of ribs 5 to 7, treating rib 5 as the key rib"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Exhalation dysfunction of ribs 5 to 7, treating rib 5 as the key rib): Ribs that fail to rise on inhalation are stuck in exhalation, so this is an exhalation dysfunction. In exhalation dysfunction the uppermost rib of the group is the key rib because it restricts upward motion of the ribs below it, whereas in inhalation dysfunction the lowest rib is key. Treating the lowest or middle rib applies the wrong rule."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for primary amenorrhea. She is 143 cm tall with a broad chest, a low posterior hairline, and no breast development. Blood pressure is 152/84 mm Hg in the right arm, and femoral pulses are delayed compared with brachial pulses. Serum FSH is markedly elevated. Which karyotype is most likely?", "options": {"A": "45,X/46,XX mosaicism with spontaneous puberty, menarche and preserved fertility in many cases", "B": "45,X with streak gonads from accelerated oocyte loss, short stature and aortic coarctation", "C": "47,XXX with tall stature, normal fertility and mild learning difficulties in most affected girls", "D": "46,XY complete androgen insensitivity with undescended testes and a blind-ending vagina in a phenotypic female", "E": "46,XX pure gonadal dysgenesis with streak gonads, normal stature and no cardiovascular abnormalities"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (45,X with streak gonads from accelerated oocyte loss, short stature and aortic coarctation): Short stature, a webbed or low hairline, a broad chest, hypertension with delayed femoral pulses from coarctation, and hypergonadotropic primary amenorrhea indicate Turner syndrome, 45,X. Gonadal dysgenesis alone would not cause short stature or coarctation, and mosaic forms often show some puberty. Echocardiography and renal imaging follow the diagnosis."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 40-year-old man has three days of fever, personality change, and an olfactory hallucination, followed by a generalized seizure. Temperature is 38.9 C. MRI shows hyperintensity of the right medial temporal lobe. Cerebrospinal fluid has 90 lymphocytes per microliter, 120 red cells per microliter, and mildly elevated protein with normal glucose. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Start intravenous acyclovir immediately while polymerase chain reaction testing of cerebrospinal fluid is pending", "B": "Give intravenous dexamethasone alone to reduce temporal lobe edema and lower seizure recurrence risk", "C": "Withhold antiviral therapy until herpes simplex polymerase chain reaction results return positive", "D": "Begin intravenous ceftriaxone and vancomycin alone and add antivirals if no improvement occurs", "E": "Begin intravenous amphotericin B for presumed fungal encephalitis pending culture results"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start intravenous acyclovir immediately while polymerase chain reaction testing of cerebrospinal fluid is pending): Fever, behavioral change, olfactory hallucination, seizure, temporal lobe MRI changes, and lymphocytic fluid with red cells indicate herpes simplex encephalitis. Mortality is high without early therapy, so acyclovir is started empirically and not delayed for polymerase chain reaction results. Antibacterial therapy alone or steroids alone do not treat the virus."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A case-control study of birth defects interviews mothers of affected infants and mothers of healthy infants about medication use during pregnancy. Mothers of affected infants report far more exposures. Records review later shows true exposure rates were nearly equal in both groups. Blinded interviewers used one structured questionnaire for everyone. Which bias best explains the discrepancy?", "options": {"A": "Interviewer bias, because investigators questioned mothers of affected infants more intensively than controls", "B": "Selection bias, because hospitalized mothers differ from the general population in exposure patterns", "C": "Recall bias, because parents of a sick child scrutinize their past exposures more intensely", "D": "Non-response bias, because mothers of healthy infants declined interviews more often than other mothers", "E": "Non-differential misclassification, because exposure was measured with error equally in both groups"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recall bias, because parents of a sick child scrutinize their past exposures more intensely): True exposure was similar, yet the mothers of affected infants reported more of it, which is the signature of differential recall driven by the search for a cause. Blinded interviewers and a structured instrument make interviewer bias unlikely. Non-differential misclassification would push results toward the null rather than create a spurious excess."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 63-year-old man is hospitalized with an acute myocardial infarction and is doing well. Four days later he develops new chest pain at rest. Troponin I remains elevated from the index event. His physician wants a laboratory marker to help detect recurrent myocardial injury during this time window. Which marker is most useful?", "options": {"A": "Aspartate aminotransferase, which rises in myocardial necrosis and is specific to cardiac muscle", "B": "Troponin T, because it rises early and remains elevated for roughly one to two weeks", "C": "Creatine kinase MB, which returns to baseline within about three days after one infarct", "D": "LDH isoenzyme 1, which peaks around three days and stays elevated for about one week", "E": "B-type natriuretic peptide, which is released from necrotic myocytes and normalizes within about three days"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Creatine kinase MB, which returns to baseline within about three days after one infarct): CK-MB rises within hours and normalizes in 48 to 72 hours, so a new rise after several days points to reinfarction. Troponin stays elevated for up to two weeks after a single event and so cannot separate old injury from new. Aspartate aminotransferase and LDH are neither cardiac specific nor timed usefully."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 58-year-old painter reports night pain and difficulty starting arm abduction. On examination, with both arms abducted 90 degrees, internally rotated and angled forward with thumbs pointing down, resisting downward pressure reproduces pain and weakness on the right. Which structure is most likely damaged?", "options": {"A": "Subscapularis tendon, which internally rotates the arm and is tested by lift-off posteriorly", "B": "Infraspinatus tendon, which externally rotates the arm and is tested with the elbow flexed", "C": "Long head of biceps tendon, which sits in the bicipital groove anteriorly", "D": "Supraspinatus tendon, compressed beneath the acromion during early elevation of the arm", "E": "Teres minor tendon, which externally rotates the arm and shares axillary nerve supply"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Supraspinatus tendon, compressed beneath the acromion during early elevation of the arm): The empty can position isolates the supraspinatus, whose tendon passes under the acromion and is the most commonly torn cuff tendon in older manual workers. Infraspinatus and teres minor would instead weaken external rotation with the elbow at the side, which is not the maneuver reproducing the pain here."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history and chronic hypoxemia presents with ankle edema and abdominal fullness. He has a barrel chest, jugular venous distension, an enlarged tender liver, and a right ventricular heave. Echocardiography shows right ventricular hypertrophy with normal left ventricular ejection fraction and normal valves. Which mechanism most directly raised his pulmonary artery pressure?", "options": {"A": "Mechanical obstruction of the central pulmonary arteries by organized thromboemboli from recurrent clots", "B": "Increased pulmonary blood flow from a left to right shunt, causing progressive vascular remodeling", "C": "Germline BMPR2 mutation causing plexiform proliferation of endothelial cells in small pulmonary arteries", "D": "Chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction with arteriolar remodeling that increases pulmonary vascular resistance", "E": "Elevated left atrial pressure from mitral valve stenosis transmitted backward through the pulmonary veins"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction with arteriolar remodeling that increases pulmonary vascular resistance): Right heart failure from chronic lung disease is cor pulmonale, and the driver is alveolar hypoxia, which constricts pulmonary arterioles and eventually remodels them. Normal left heart function and valves rule out postcapillary causes. Thromboembolic obstruction and heritable arterial disease are possible causes of right heart strain but are not suggested here."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 57-year-old man with hypertension and hyperlipidemia presents with 2 hours of substernal chest pressure radiating to his jaw. He is hemodynamically stable. His ECG shows 1 mm ST depression in leads V4 to V6 without ST elevation, and the serial cardiac marker panel is shown. Which diagnosis best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Prinzmetal angina, since transient coronary vasospasm causes ST changes without elevation of cardiac markers", "B": "ST-elevation myocardial infarction, since transmural injury from total coronary occlusion raises cardiac enzymes markedly", "C": "Stable angina, since fixed stenosis produces ischemia resolving with rest and no marker release", "D": "Unstable angina, since subendocardial ischemia with ST depression occurs without detectable myocyte necrosis", "E": "Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction, since the rising troponin confirms myocyte necrosis in the subendocardium"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction, since the rising troponin confirms myocyte necrosis in the subendocardium): ST depression with chest pain and rising troponin indicates infarction limited to the subendocardium, which is a non-ST-elevation MI. Unstable angina has the same ECG pattern but no troponin rise, which is the detail the panel supplies. ST elevation would signal transmural injury.", "image": "question_images/q_863.png"}, {"id": 864, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 26-year-old woman has had recurrent sinus and lung infections since her late teens and chronic diarrhea with Giardia found on stool testing. Her father and brother are healthy and she has no history of severe childhood infection. The immunoglobulin and B cell panel shown is reported. Which diagnosis best fits?", "options": {"A": "Severe combined immunodeficiency, with absent T cell function from birth and fatal infections in infancy", "B": "Selective IgA deficiency, a common primary immunodeficiency causing mucosal infections and sometimes anaphylaxis to transfused plasma", "C": "Common variable immunodeficiency, with impaired B cell differentiation into plasma cells and low serum antibodies", "D": "X-linked agammaglobulinemia, a BTK defect that blocks pre-B cell maturation and leaves B cells absent", "E": "Hyper IgM syndrome from CD40 ligand mutation, causing failed class switching with low IgG"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Common variable immunodeficiency, with impaired B cell differentiation into plasma cells and low serum antibodies): Low IgG, IgA and IgM with normal B cell numbers and onset of infections and giardiasis in young adulthood indicate common variable immunodeficiency, where B cells fail to become plasma cells. Agammaglobulinemia would show absent B cells and presents in infancy in males, while isolated IgA deficiency keeps normal IgG.", "image": "question_images/q_864.png"}, {"id": 865, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 76-year-old man has cognitive decline over one year with marked day-to-day fluctuations in alertness, vivid well-formed visual hallucinations of children in the house, and mild rigidity and bradykinesia. After a dose of haloperidol for agitation, he became severely rigid and unresponsive. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Dementia with Lewy bodies, in which dopamine-blocking antipsychotic drugs cause severe sensitivity reactions", "B": "Delirium from anticholinergic medication with acute fluctuating inattention resolving after stopping the offending drug", "C": "Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease with rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus evolving over weeks to a few months", "D": "Parkinson disease dementia, in which cognitive decline appears more than one year after established motor symptoms", "E": "Frontotemporal dementia with early personality change, disinhibition, and relatively preserved memory in the early course"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dementia with Lewy bodies, in which dopamine-blocking antipsychotic drugs cause severe sensitivity reactions): Fluctuating cognition, well-formed visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism with cognitive decline beginning before or within a year of motor signs define dementia with Lewy bodies. Severe reactions to neuroleptics are characteristic. In Parkinson disease dementia, motor symptoms precede dementia by more than a year."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 41-year-old woman has six months of symmetric swelling and tenderness of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints with 90 minutes of morning stiffness that improves with use. Radiographs show periarticular osteopenia and C-reactive protein is elevated. Which laboratory antibody test is most specific for her likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "IgM antibody directed against the Fc portion of IgG, found in many chronic inflammatory states", "B": "Antibodies against topoisomerase I, a nuclear enzyme that relaxes supercoiled DNA", "C": "Antinuclear antibodies with a homogeneous pattern, directed against histones and double stranded DNA", "D": "Antibodies against Smith antigen, a component of the small nuclear ribonucleoprotein complex", "E": "Antibodies against cyclic citrullinated peptide, formed against proteins modified by peptidylarginine deiminase"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Antibodies against cyclic citrullinated peptide, formed against proteins modified by peptidylarginine deiminase): Anti-CCP antibodies recognize citrullinated proteins and are highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis, and they may precede symptoms. Rheumatoid factor is frequently positive in other chronic inflammatory conditions and infections, which lowers its specificity. Anti-Smith and anti-topoisomerase antibodies mark lupus and systemic sclerosis."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "community_health", "question": "On January 1, a town of 10,000 residents has 2,000 people with type 2 diabetes. During the following year, 400 additional residents are newly diagnosed, and nobody dies, moves away or recovers. What is the cumulative incidence of diabetes in this town during the year?", "options": {"A": "20 percent, from new cases divided by the number of existing cases at baseline", "B": "5.0 percent, from new cases divided by the disease-free population at baseline", "C": "24 percent, from all existing and new cases divided by the whole town population", "D": "5.3 percent, from new cases divided by the population still disease-free at year end", "E": "4.0 percent, from new cases divided by the entire town population at baseline"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (5.0 percent, from new cases divided by the disease-free population at baseline): Only the 8,000 residents without diabetes on January 1 were at risk, so cumulative incidence is 400 divided by 8,000, or 5.0 percent. Including the 2,000 prevalent cases in the denominator understates the risk, and the combined 2,400 cases over 10,000 is end-of-year prevalence rather than incidence."}, {"id": 868, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 58-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia has refractory hypoxemia on high-flow oxygen. Chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral infiltrates. The intensivist intubates him and applies positive end-expiratory pressure of 12 cmH2O, after which his arterial oxygen saturation improves from 84% to 94% without a change in the oxygen fraction. Which mechanism best explains this improvement?", "options": {"A": "Higher cardiac output delivering more blood to ventilated alveoli at each breath", "B": "Decreased anatomic dead space that improves the fraction of each breath reaching alveoli", "C": "Improved diffusion across a thinner alveolar membrane from reduced interstitial fluid", "D": "Recruitment of collapsed alveoli, increasing functional residual capacity and reducing intrapulmonary shunt fraction", "E": "Left shift of the hemoglobin oxygen dissociation curve increasing oxygen binding in pulmonary capillaries"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Recruitment of collapsed alveoli, increasing functional residual capacity and reducing intrapulmonary shunt fraction): End-expiratory pressure keeps injured, fluid-filled alveoli open, which raises functional residual capacity and converts shunted blood into ventilated, oxygenated blood. PEEP typically lowers cardiac output rather than raising it, and it does not change hemoglobin affinity or anatomic dead space in any useful way."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 10-year-old boy with sickle cell disease has fever of 39.4 degrees Celsius and pain over the right tibia for three days, after a diarrheal illness two weeks ago. Examination shows warmth and tenderness over the tibial shaft. Blood cultures are pending. Which organism is the most likely cause of the bone infection in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Streptococcus pneumoniae, an encapsulated organism responsible for sepsis in functionally asplenic patients", "B": "Staphylococcus aureus, the most frequent cause of acute osteomyelitis in children without hemoglobinopathies", "C": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa, typically seen after puncture wounds through the soles of footwear", "D": "Haemophilus influenzae type b, an encapsulated organism preventable by conjugate immunization in infancy", "E": "Salmonella species, favored by intestinal translocation and impaired reticuloendothelial clearance in sickle cell disease"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Salmonella species, favored by intestinal translocation and impaired reticuloendothelial clearance in sickle cell disease): Patients with sickle cell disease have repeated bowel and bone infarcts that allow enteric bacteria to seed bone, and they have poor clearance of organisms. Salmonella is the classic cause of osteomyelitis in this group, although Staphylococcus aureus remains common overall. Encapsulated organisms cause sepsis rather than typical bone infection."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 17-year-old boy with beta thalassemia major receives red cell transfusions every 3 weeks. Ferritin is 3,400 ng/mL, and cardiac T2 star imaging suggests early myocardial iron loading. He has refused nightly subcutaneous pump infusions because of skin reactions and needs a once daily oral agent. Which drug is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Succimer, an oral dimercaptosuccinic acid analog used to chelate lead in children", "B": "Deferoxamine, a hexadentate chelator given by prolonged parenteral infusion to promote urinary excretion", "C": "Deferasirox, a tridentate chelator that binds ferric iron and promotes fecal excretion", "D": "Dimercaprol, a dithiol chelator given by deep intramuscular injection for heavy metal intoxication", "E": "Edetate calcium disodium, an intravenous chelator that mobilizes lead and zinc into urine"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deferasirox, a tridentate chelator that binds ferric iron and promotes fecal excretion): Chronic transfusion causes iron overload that the body cannot excrete, and chelation is needed. Deferasirox is an oral, once daily iron chelator that excretes iron in stool, which fits a patient unable to use subcutaneous pump infusions. Succimer, dimercaprol and edetate target lead or other heavy metals rather than iron."}, {"id": 871, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has 3 months of drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen late in the day and improve with rest. She has no dysphagia or dyspnea. Chest CT shows a well-circumscribed anterior mediastinal mass. She recently started a new running program. Which mass is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Neurogenic tumor arising from intercostal nerve sheaths in the posterior mediastinum", "B": "Hodgkin lymphoma, a nodal malignancy with Reed-Sternberg cells commonly involving mediastinal lymph nodes", "C": "Substernal goiter, an enlarged thyroid extending below the thoracic inlet in older patients", "D": "Thymoma, an epithelial neoplasm of the thymus linked to antibodies against postsynaptic receptors", "E": "Mature teratoma, a germ cell tumor containing tissues from several embryonic layers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thymoma, an epithelial neoplasm of the thymus linked to antibodies against postsynaptic receptors): Fatigable ptosis and diplopia suggest myasthenia gravis, and about a third of these patients have a thymoma in the anterior mediastinum. Teratoma and goiter also arise anteriorly but are not associated with the syndrome, and neurogenic tumors are posterior. The new running program only explains why symptoms appeared late in the day."}, {"id": 872, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 62-year-old man with orthopnea, bilateral ankle edema, and an elevated B-type natriuretic peptide has bilateral pleural effusions. Serum albumin is normal at 3.9 g/dL. Diagnostic thoracentesis results are shown in the table. He takes a calcium channel blocker for hypertension. Which mechanism best explains the effusion?", "options": {"A": "Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure from left ventricular failure with an intact pleural surface", "B": "Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from protein loss in nephrotic syndrome, favoring fluid filtration into the pleura", "C": "Impaired lymphatic drainage from tumor obstruction of mediastinal nodes, trapping protein rich fluid in the pleura", "D": "Transdiaphragmatic movement of ascitic fluid through diaphragmatic defects from cirrhosis with portal hypertension", "E": "Increased pleural capillary permeability from inflammation, allowing protein rich fluid to leak into the space"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure from left ventricular failure with an intact pleural surface): The pleural to serum protein ratio is about 0.33 and the pleural lactate dehydrogenase is far below two thirds of the upper limit of normal, so the fluid meets neither of Light criteria and is a transudate. With orthopnea and a high natriuretic peptide, left heart failure raising capillary hydrostatic pressure is the cause. Inflammation and lymphatic blockage would give an exudate.", "image": "question_images/q_872.png"}, {"id": 873, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with hypertension and atrial fibrillation suddenly develops violent, flinging movements of the right arm and leg. She takes warfarin and lisinopril. Strength is normal and there is no sensory loss. Imaging was ordered. Which lesion most likely explains these movements?", "options": {"A": "Infarct of the left ventral posterolateral thalamus interrupting sensory relay to the cortex", "B": "Loss of the left substantia nigra pars compacta reducing dopamine input to the putamen", "C": "Infarct of the right subthalamic nucleus removing excitatory drive to the globus pallidus internus", "D": "Infarct of the left subthalamic nucleus removing excitatory drive to the globus pallidus internus", "E": "Degeneration of the left caudate nucleus removing inhibitory drive to the globus pallidus externus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Infarct of the left subthalamic nucleus removing excitatory drive to the globus pallidus internus): Hemiballismus follows a lesion of the contralateral subthalamic nucleus, most often a lacunar infarct in a hypertensive or embolic patient. Loss of excitatory glutamate drive to the globus pallidus internus reduces its inhibition of the thalamus, so cortical motor output is excessive. The left lesion explains right-sided movements. Chorea from caudate loss is slower and develops gradually."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 46-year-old man with chronic heavy alcohol use is brought in after not eating for two days. He is confused and diaphoretic with a glucose of 38 mg/dL. His liver is metabolizing ethanol using alcohol dehydrogenase. Which biochemical change best explains the hypoglycemia?", "options": {"A": "Elevated NADH to NAD+ ratio diverting pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate, inhibiting gluconeogenesis", "B": "Cortisol deficiency from adrenal suppression reducing stimulation of hepatic gluconeogenic enzyme expression", "C": "Increased insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells stimulated by ethanol, driving glucose into peripheral tissues", "D": "Direct inhibition of glycogen phosphorylase by acetaldehyde, preventing mobilization of stored hepatic glycogen", "E": "Impaired glucose absorption from alcohol induced mucosal injury of the small intestine, lowering circulating glucose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Elevated NADH to NAD+ ratio diverting pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate, inhibiting gluconeogenesis): Ethanol oxidation generates large amounts of NADH, which drives pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate and so removes the substrates for gluconeogenesis. After glycogen runs out in fasting, glucose falls. Glycogenolysis is not the issue because stores are already depleted."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 65-year-old hyperopic woman develops severe left eye pain, halos around lights, nausea, and blurred vision after sitting in a dark movie theater. Examination shows a red eye, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, and a firm globe. She takes an over-the-counter antihistamine. Which mechanism best explains her presentation?", "options": {"A": "Elevated episcleral venous pressure from an arteriovenous fistula impeding venous outflow", "B": "Ciliary body overproduction of aqueous humor despite a normally wide drainage angle", "C": "Iris bowing forward against the trabecular meshwork, obstructing aqueous drainage at a narrow angle", "D": "Gradual sclerosis of the trabecular meshwork impairing outflow through a wide open angle", "E": "Posterior vitreous detachment pulling on the retina and producing traction at the periphery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Iris bowing forward against the trabecular meshwork, obstructing aqueous drainage at a narrow angle): A hyperopic eye has a shallow anterior chamber, and dim light plus an anticholinergic antihistamine dilates the pupil so the peripheral iris blocks the angle, causing acute angle-closure glaucoma. Chronic open-angle glaucoma is painless and gradual, so it does not produce an acute red eye with a fixed mid-dilated pupil."}, {"id": 876, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 34-year-old woman in clinic reports weeks of excessive worry, restlessness, muscle tension and poor sleep about work and finances, on most days for eight months. Her physician prescribes a short course of lorazepam while a longer-term agent takes effect. Through which mechanism does lorazepam produce its anxiolytic effect?", "options": {"A": "Blocking presynaptic reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine, raising synaptic neurotransmitter concentrations over several weeks", "B": "Increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening at the GABA-A receptor, enhancing neuronal hyperpolarization", "C": "Increasing the duration of chloride channel opening at the GABA-A receptor, as barbiturates do", "D": "Partial agonism at presynaptic serotonin 5-HT1A receptors, reducing serotonergic neuronal firing in the raphe", "E": "Antagonizing histamine H1 receptors in the cortex, producing sedation that reduces perceived anxiety"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening at the GABA-A receptor, enhancing neuronal hyperpolarization): Benzodiazepines bind an allosteric site on the GABA-A receptor and increase how often the chloride channel opens when GABA is bound, hyperpolarizing neurons and reducing anxiety within minutes to hours. Barbiturates are the tempting alternative because they also act at this receptor, but they lengthen channel opening and can open it without GABA, which explains their narrower safety margin."}, {"id": 877, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 76-year-old woman is brought by her daughter because of three years of gradually worsening memory loss. She repeats questions, misplaces objects, and recently became lost driving to a familiar store. Gait and strength are normal, and there are no hallucinations. MRI shows bilateral hippocampal atrophy. Which microscopic finding is most likely at autopsy?", "options": {"A": "Multiple small cavitary infarcts in deep white matter with arteriolar wall thickening", "B": "Extracellular beta-amyloid plaques with intraneuronal neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau", "C": "Intraneuronal cytoplasmic inclusions composed of aggregated alpha-synuclein in cortical and brainstem neurons", "D": "Spongiform vacuolation of cortical neuropil caused by misfolded protease-resistant prion protein", "E": "Round tau-positive intraneuronal inclusions in atrophic frontal and anterior temporal cortex"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Extracellular beta-amyloid plaques with intraneuronal neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau): Slowly progressive amnestic dementia with hippocampal atrophy is typical of Alzheimer disease. Neuropathology shows extracellular amyloid plaques and intraneuronal tau tangles. Lewy bodies, Pick bodies, spongiform change, and lacunar infarcts belong to other dementias with different clinical profiles."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with hypertension has recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones and osteopenia. Her serum calcium is high-normal and her sodium is 138 mEq/L. She wishes to avoid adding a separate stone prevention medication. Which antihypertensive would best address both her blood pressure and the risk of stone recurrence?", "options": {"A": "Hydrochlorothiazide, which increases distal tubule calcium reabsorption and lowers urinary calcium excretion", "B": "Amiloride, which blocks epithelial sodium channels and reduces potassium secretion in the collecting duct", "C": "Acetazolamide, which inhibits carbonic anhydrase and alkalinizes urine in the proximal tubule", "D": "Spironolactone, which blocks aldosterone receptors and retains potassium in the collecting duct", "E": "Furosemide, which inhibits the loop cotransporter and enhances urinary calcium excretion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hydrochlorothiazide, which increases distal tubule calcium reabsorption and lowers urinary calcium excretion): Thiazides inhibit the sodium chloride cotransporter in the distal tubule, which increases calcium reabsorption and reduces urinary calcium, lowering stone formation and aiding bone density. Loop diuretics increase calcium loss and worsen stones and osteopenia. The other agents do not lower urinary calcium meaningfully."}, {"id": 879, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department after telling her counselor she wants to die. She endorses a plan to overdose on her mother's pills and has already collected them at home. She denies intoxication. She is calm and cooperative. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor and arrange parental supervision of all medications", "B": "Provide a crisis hotline number and schedule outpatient cognitive behavioral therapy within two weeks", "C": "Discharge home with a written no-harm contract and outpatient follow-up within one week", "D": "Start observation in the emergency department for six hours and discharge if she remains calm", "E": "Admit for inpatient psychiatric stabilization with continuous safety observation until risk is reassessed"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Admit for inpatient psychiatric stabilization with continuous safety observation until risk is reassessed): A specific plan with the means already gathered marks high imminent risk, so she needs a protected setting with continuous observation and a full psychiatric evaluation. Calm behavior does not lower risk, and a safety contract or outpatient follow-up does not provide enough protection."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "An 82-year-old nursing home resident has been bedbound for three weeks after a hip fracture and now has bilateral pitting foot edema, with normal albumin, a normal creatinine, and a normal echocardiogram. The osteopathic physician plans a pedal pump. Which mechanism best explains why immobility worsened her edema?", "options": {"A": "Renal sodium retention from reduced glomerular filtration expands total extracellular fluid volume diffusely", "B": "Elevated right atrial pressure from pump failure impairs venous drainage of the lower extremities", "C": "Loss of plasma oncotic pressure from low albumin allows fluid to leak into interstitial tissue", "D": "Lymphatic vessel obstruction by filarial worms blocks flow through the regional inguinal lymph nodes", "E": "Loss of skeletal muscle contraction removes the external squeeze that propels lymph past one-way valves"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Loss of skeletal muscle contraction removes the external squeeze that propels lymph past one-way valves): Lymph moves largely through the squeezing action of working skeletal muscle on vessels that contain one-way valves, so three weeks of bed rest leaves fluid pooled in the feet. The normal albumin, creatinine, and echocardiogram argue against low oncotic pressure, renal retention, and cardiac causes, which is why a pedal pump is reasonable."}, {"id": 881, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 10-year-old boy with Burkitt lymphoma is admitted before chemotherapy. Laboratory studies show uric acid 11 mg/dL, creatinine 1.0 mg/dL and a markedly elevated LDH. He is given intravenous fluids and rasburicase, and a G6PD level is confirmed normal beforehand. By which mechanism does rasburicase lower the serum uric acid?", "options": {"A": "Inhibition of interleukin-1 signaling, reducing neutrophil recruitment to urate crystals in inflamed joints and tissues", "B": "Recombinant urate oxidase converts uric acid to the more soluble allantoin for renal excretion", "C": "Inhibition of amidophosphoribosyl transferase, reducing de novo purine synthesis and the supply of uric acid precursors", "D": "Inhibition of xanthine oxidase, blocking conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid", "E": "Blockade of URAT1 in the proximal tubule, increasing urinary uric acid excretion by preventing its reabsorption"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recombinant urate oxidase converts uric acid to the more soluble allantoin for renal excretion): Rasburicase is recombinant urate oxidase, an enzyme humans lack, and it converts uric acid to allantoin, which is far more soluble. It acts quickly on existing urate in tumor lysis syndrome. Allopurinol only prevents new formation, uricosurics are unsafe with heavy urate loads, and interleukin-1 blockade treats inflammation rather than urate."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 29-year-old soccer player has lateral knee pain and numbness over the dorsum of the right foot after a hamstring strain. The fibular head is posterior, and gait is normal with intact dorsiflexion. Which muscle most likely maintains this fibular head position, and which nerve is irritated?", "options": {"A": "Biceps femoris tethering the head backward, with irritation of the deep peroneal nerve", "B": "Peroneus longus tethering the head backward, with irritation of the superficial peroneal nerve", "C": "Tibialis anterior tethering the head forward, with irritation of the sural nerve", "D": "Semimembranosus tethering the head backward, with irritation of the tibial nerve", "E": "Biceps femoris tethering the head backward, with irritation of the superficial peroneal nerve"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Biceps femoris tethering the head backward, with irritation of the superficial peroneal nerve): The biceps femoris inserts on the fibular head, and a tight or strained muscle pulls it posteriorly and can irritate the superficial peroneal branch crossing the lateral leg. Intact dorsiflexion argues against deep peroneal involvement. Peroneus longus, semimembranosus and tibialis anterior do not fit the posterior position or the dorsal foot numbness."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 3-year-old girl is brought to the clinic after pulling a cup of hot tea onto her forearm four hours ago. The skin shows intact fluid-filled blisters over a moist, pink base that blanches with pressure and is exquisitely painful. Capillary refill is brisk. Which burn depth best describes this injury?", "options": {"A": "Superficial epidermal injury confined to the stratum corneum without blister formation", "B": "Full-thickness injury destroying epidermis and dermis with painless leathery eschar formation", "C": "Deep partial-thickness injury destroying most dermis with diminished sensation and delayed healing", "D": "Superficial partial-thickness injury sparing deep dermal structures and healing without scarring", "E": "Fourth-degree injury extending through subcutaneous fat into underlying muscle or bone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Superficial partial-thickness injury sparing deep dermal structures and healing without scarring): Blisters, a moist pink blanching base, brisk capillary refill and severe pain indicate that dermal appendages and papillary dermis are intact, so this is a superficial partial-thickness (superficial second-degree) burn. Deep partial-thickness burns are drier, mottled, less painful and slower to heal, and an epidermal-only burn would not blister."}, {"id": 884, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 42-year-old man felt a sudden snap in his left posterior ankle while sprinting for a bus and can still plantar flex weakly. A palpable gap is present 4 cm above the calcaneus, and squeezing the calf with him prone produces no foot motion. He recently completed ciprofloxacin for sinusitis. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Complete Achilles tendon rupture, with weak residual motion from the deep posterior compartment flexors", "B": "Posterior tibial tendon rupture with acquired flatfoot deformity and inability to perform heel rise", "C": "Achilles tendinopathy with intratendinous degeneration, mucoid change and preserved tendon continuity on examination", "D": "Gastrocnemius medial head tear, the tennis leg, with a calf hematoma and intact tendon", "E": "Retrocalcaneal bursitis with inflammation of the bursa between the tendon and posterior calcaneus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Complete Achilles tendon rupture, with weak residual motion from the deep posterior compartment flexors): A sudden snap, a palpable gap, and an absent plantar flexion response on the Thompson test indicate complete Achilles rupture, and fluoroquinolone exposure increases that risk. Residual weak plantar flexion comes from the flexor hallucis longus and tibialis posterior, which should not mislead the examiner. Tendinopathy and bursitis do not cause a gap."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 29-year-old woman reports fatigue, exertional dyspnea, and brittle nails over several months. She follows a mixed diet and takes no medications. Examination shows pale conjunctivae and a normal spleen. Which diagnosis best fits the complete blood count and iron studies shown?", "options": {"A": "Anemia of chronic disease with iron sequestered in macrophages by elevated hepcidin", "B": "Sideroblastic anemia with impaired iron incorporation into protoporphyrin inside mitochondria", "C": "Beta thalassemia trait with microcytosis from reduced beta globin chain synthesis", "D": "Iron deficiency with depleted storage iron and raised transferrin availability in the circulation", "E": "Lead poisoning with inhibition of heme synthetic enzymes and basophilic stippling of red cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Iron deficiency with depleted storage iron and raised transferrin availability in the circulation): Low ferritin, low iron, high total iron-binding capacity, and low transferrin saturation with a microcytic anemia and wide red cell distribution width indicate iron deficiency. The Mentzer index above 13 also favors iron deficiency over thalassemia trait. Chronic disease raises ferritin and lowers binding capacity.", "image": "question_images/q_885.png"}, {"id": 886, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 3-year-old boy is brought to clinic with a firm, nontender abdominal mass that crosses the midline, along with intermittent diarrhea and irritability. Urine testing shows elevated vanillylmandelic acid and homovanillic acid. Biopsy shows small round blue cells forming rosettes. Which genetic alteration in this tumor is most strongly associated with an aggressive clinical course?", "options": {"A": "Amplification of a transcription factor gene that drives unchecked proliferation of neural crest derived cells", "B": "Deletion of a transcription factor gene required for normal nephron development in the embryo", "C": "Fusion gene producing constitutive tyrosine kinase signaling in developing hematopoietic progenitor cells of marrow", "D": "Biallelic loss of a tumor suppressor controlling the restriction point between G1 and S phase", "E": "Overexpression of a growth factor receptor tyrosine kinase signaling through the PI3K pathway"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Amplification of a transcription factor gene that drives unchecked proliferation of neural crest derived cells): Elevated urinary catecholamine metabolites with a midline crossing mass and small round blue cell rosettes indicate neuroblastoma, and amplification of the N-myc transcription factor gene marks the aggressive form. Wilms tumor usually stays unilateral and does not raise catecholamine metabolites, and retinoblastoma arises in the retina."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 49-year-old man in a rural clinic reports six weeks of epigastric burning that worsens after meals, and endoscopy confirmed gastritis. The physician palpates the paraspinal muscles for sympathetic viscerosomatic reflex changes, expecting skin drag and ropy texture. At which spinal levels are these findings most likely located?", "options": {"A": "T5 to T9 paraspinal region, matching the sympathetic outflow to the stomach and upper gut", "B": "T10 to L1 paraspinal region, matching the sympathetic outflow to the kidney and ureter", "C": "L1 to L2 paraspinal region, matching the sympathetic outflow to the distal colon", "D": "S2 to S4 paraspinal region, matching the parasympathetic outflow to the pelvic viscera", "E": "T1 to T4 paraspinal region, matching the sympathetic outflow to the head and neck"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (T5 to T9 paraspinal region, matching the sympathetic outflow to the stomach and upper gut): Gastric disease facilitates sympathetic segments from T5 to T9, so tissue texture changes appear in those paraspinal levels. Lower levels correspond to the kidney, colon and pelvic organs, and sacral findings relate to parasympathetic supply rather than a paraspinal sympathetic reflex."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 63-year-old man with long-standing hypertension, claudication, and an abdominal bruit begins lisinopril. One week later creatinine rises from 1.0 to 1.9 mg/dL, and renal ultrasound shows bilateral small kidneys of unequal size. Urinalysis is bland and potassium is 5.4 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains the rise in creatinine?", "options": {"A": "Loss of prostaglandin mediated afferent arteriolar dilation reducing glomerular blood flow during inhibitor therapy", "B": "Loss of angiotensin II constriction of efferent arterioles reducing filtration pressure in stenotic kidneys", "C": "Immune complex deposition in glomerular basement membranes producing nephritic sediment and red cell casts", "D": "Crystal precipitation within distal tubular lumens causing intratubular obstruction and a rising creatinine", "E": "Direct proximal tubular cell toxicity producing acute tubular necrosis with muddy brown granular casts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loss of angiotensin II constriction of efferent arterioles reducing filtration pressure in stenotic kidneys): When renal perfusion is reduced by artery stenosis, efferent constriction from angiotensin II maintains filtration pressure. An ACE inhibitor removes that constriction, so filtration falls and creatinine and potassium rise, with a bland urinalysis. The prostaglandin mechanism describes nonsteroidal drugs, not this drug class."}, {"id": 889, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 68-year-old man reports two months of painless jaundice, dark urine, pale stools, and 7 kg weight loss. He was diagnosed with diabetes mellitus three months ago and recently had a leg vein thrombosis. CT shows a 3 cm hypodense mass in the uncinate process with upstream biliary and pancreatic dilation. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor secreting insulin or gastrin, usually small and presenting with hormonal symptoms", "B": "Ductal adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic head obstructing the distal common bile duct and main pancreatic duct", "C": "Distal cholangiocarcinoma arising from bile duct epithelium and causing obstruction without involvement of the pancreatic parenchyma", "D": "Chronic pancreatitis with a fibrotic inflammatory head mass, usually seen after years of heavy alcohol intake", "E": "Choledocholithiasis with a gallstone impacted in the ampulla, typically causing fever and pain with intermittent jaundice"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ductal adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic head obstructing the distal common bile duct and main pancreatic duct): Painless obstructive jaundice, weight loss, new onset diabetes, migratory venous thrombosis, and a hypodense head mass with dilation of both ducts is classic for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. A distal cholangiocarcinoma would not produce a parenchymal mass with pancreatic duct dilation, and a stone would more likely cause pain and fever."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 45-year-old man with recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones has persistent idiopathic hypercalciuria despite high fluid intake. His blood pressure is 148/92 mm Hg, and serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL. His physician wants one drug that lowers both urinary calcium excretion and blood pressure. Which drug and nephron site of action fit best?", "options": {"A": "Mannitol acting as an osmotic agent in proximal tubule and descending limb, raising urinary calcium excretion", "B": "Acetazolamide inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, reducing bicarbonate and sodium reabsorption there", "C": "Furosemide blocking the sodium potassium chloride cotransporter in the thick ascending limb, increasing urinary calcium loss", "D": "Hydrochlorothiazide blocking the sodium chloride cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule, which enhances calcium reabsorption", "E": "Spironolactone blocking aldosterone receptors in the collecting duct, reducing sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion there"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hydrochlorothiazide blocking the sodium chloride cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule, which enhances calcium reabsorption): Thiazides block the sodium chloride cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule, which lowers intracellular sodium and drives calcium reabsorption through the basolateral sodium calcium exchanger, so urinary calcium falls and blood pressure improves. Loop diuretics have the opposite effect on calcium and would worsen stone risk."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 13-year-old premenarchal girl is noted at a school physical to have uneven shoulders. On forward bending, a right thoracic rib prominence is visible, and it persists when she is seated. Neurologic examination and skin are normal, and leg lengths are equal. Standing films show a 25 degree curve with vertebral rotation. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Functional scoliosis from a leg length discrepancy that corrects when the patient sits", "B": "Scheuermann kyphosis with anterior wedging of three adjacent thoracic vertebrae in an adolescent", "C": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 scoliosis with dystrophic curves and cafe-au-lait macules on the skin", "D": "Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis, a structural lateral spinal curve of unknown cause in teenagers", "E": "Congenital scoliosis from a hemivertebra that is usually apparent in early infancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis, a structural lateral spinal curve of unknown cause in teenagers): A persistent rib hump on forward bending that remains when seated shows a structural curve with vertebral rotation, and a normal neurologic and skin examination with equal leg lengths points to adolescent idiopathic scoliosis. Functional curves from leg length discrepancy correct on sitting, and congenital curves are typically recognized earlier."}, {"id": 892, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 48-year-old man with a waist circumference of 46 inches, blood pressure 142/90 mm Hg, fasting glucose 118 mg/dL, triglycerides 260 mg/dL and HDL cholesterol 32 mg/dL asks why his triglycerides are high. Which mechanism in insulin-resistant adipose tissue and liver best explains the hypertriglyceridemia?", "options": {"A": "Unrestrained adipose lipolysis delivers fatty acids to liver, driving triglyceride synthesis and increased VLDL secretion", "B": "Defective hepatic LDL receptor binding, preventing uptake of apolipoprotein B-100 particles and raising triglyceride-rich VLDL remnants", "C": "Impaired lipoprotein lipase activity from low apolipoprotein C-II, preventing chylomicron clearance and raising triglyceride-rich particle levels", "D": "Excess dietary cholesterol overwhelming hepatic LDL receptors, raising LDL and triglyceride levels through downregulated receptor clearance", "E": "Enhanced fatty acid oxidation in skeletal muscle, increasing acetyl-CoA availability and driving hepatic triglyceride synthesis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Unrestrained adipose lipolysis delivers fatty acids to liver, driving triglyceride synthesis and increased VLDL secretion): In insulin resistance, adipose tissue fails to suppress hormone-sensitive lipase, so free fatty acids flood the liver and drive triglyceride synthesis and VLDL secretion. This also lowers HDL through lipid exchange. Familial LDL receptor and apolipoprotein C-II defects are monogenic and do not fit a patient with central obesity and elevated glucose."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 41-year-old woman reports widespread aching for eight months, nonrestorative sleep, fatigue and poor concentration. Examination reveals multiple tender areas without joint swelling. ESR, CRP, TSH and creatine kinase are normal. She has started graded walking and sleep hygiene. Which medication is the most appropriate addition to her management?", "options": {"A": "Methotrexate, a folate antagonist that suppresses autoimmune synovitis and slows progressive joint damage in inflammatory arthritis", "B": "Oxycodone, an opioid agonist that binds mu receptors to relieve widespread pain over prolonged use", "C": "Prednisone, a corticosteroid that suppresses systemic inflammation and relieves diffuse musculoskeletal aching during long courses", "D": "Duloxetine, a serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor that augments descending inhibition of pain pathways", "E": "Ibuprofen, a nonsteroidal drug that inhibits cyclooxygenase to reduce peripheral prostaglandin driven pain and stiffness"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Duloxetine, a serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor that augments descending inhibition of pain pathways): Fibromyalgia reflects central sensitization with reduced descending pain inhibition, so drugs that raise serotonin and norepinephrine, such as duloxetine, have the best supporting evidence alongside exercise. Opioids and corticosteroids are not recommended, and NSAIDs add little. Methotrexate treats inflammatory arthritis, which is not present with normal inflammatory markers."}, {"id": 894, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old motorcyclist with a tibial shaft fracture reports escalating leg pain despite opioids. Diastolic blood pressure is 70 mm Hg and the measured anterior compartment pressure is 48 mm Hg. Based on the difference between diastolic and compartment pressure, which conclusion is best?", "options": {"A": "Perfusion pressure of 22 mm Hg indicates arterial injury requiring urgent angiography first", "B": "Perfusion pressure of 118 mm Hg is adequate, so observation with elevation is appropriate", "C": "Perfusion pressure of 22 mm Hg is below the usual threshold and supports fasciotomy", "D": "Compartment pressure of 48 mm Hg is below the usual pressure that mandates intervention", "E": "Perfusion pressure of 22 mm Hg is acceptable because only systolic pressure matters for muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Perfusion pressure of 22 mm Hg is below the usual threshold and supports fasciotomy): Perfusion pressure equals diastolic pressure minus compartment pressure, here 22 mm Hg, and a value under about 30 mm Hg indicates inadequate muscle perfusion. Adding the two pressures is a common arithmetic error. Delay risks irreversible necrosis, and arterial injury is not required, since distal pulses are often preserved."}, {"id": 895, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 45-year-old man has burning upper abdominal discomfort after meals, and endoscopy confirms gastritis. His osteopathic physician palpates the paraspinal region for a viscerosomatic reflex. At which spinal levels is the physician most likely to find acute warm, boggy tissue texture change related to this organ?", "options": {"A": "L1 to L2 paraspinal changes reflecting sympathetic outflow to the distal colon and pelvic structures", "B": "T1 to T4 paraspinal changes reflecting sympathetic outflow to the head, neck, and upper cardiac structures", "C": "T12 to L2 paraspinal changes reflecting sympathetic outflow to the bladder and the pelvic organs", "D": "T5 to T9 paraspinal changes reflecting sympathetic outflow to the stomach and upper gastrointestinal tract", "E": "T10 to T11 paraspinal changes reflecting sympathetic outflow to the small intestine and proximal colon"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (T5 to T9 paraspinal changes reflecting sympathetic outflow to the stomach and upper gastrointestinal tract): Sympathetic input to the stomach and upper gut arises from about T5 to T9, so an acute viscerosomatic reflex from gastritis produces warm, boggy paraspinal tissue there. The proximal colon maps lower, around T10 to T11."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 68-year-old man is on postoperative day 2 after sigmoid colectomy for a diverticular stricture. He has abdominal distension, absent bowel sounds, and no flatus. Palpation of the thoracolumbar paraspinal region reveals warm, boggy tissue. The physician plans osteopathic treatment to reduce sympathetic inhibition of gut motility near the surgical site. Which paraspinal level should be evaluated first?", "options": {"A": "T10 to T11, supplying the proximal large bowel through the lesser splanchnic nerves", "B": "S2 to S4, supplying the sigmoid colon and rectum through the pelvic splanchnic nerves", "C": "L1 to L2, supplying the distal large bowel through the lumbar splanchnic nerves", "D": "T5 to T9, supplying the stomach and foregut through the greater splanchnic nerves", "E": "T1 to T4, supplying the head and neck viscera through the cervical sympathetic ganglia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (L1 to L2, supplying the distal large bowel through the lumbar splanchnic nerves): The distal colon, including the sigmoid segment, receives sympathetic input from L1 to L2, so tissue texture change there marks the relevant viscerosomatic reflex. The T10 to T11 range is tempting but serves the colon proximal to the resection. Sacral outflow is parasympathetic, whereas this treatment targets sympathetic tone."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 71-year-old man reports bilateral buttock and thigh pain with numbness after walking about 100 meters. Sitting or leaning forward over a shopping cart relieves symptoms within minutes. Pedal pulses are normal, and he can cycle 30 minutes without symptoms. Which mechanism best explains his symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Reduced canal diameter during lumbar extension compressing nerve roots, relieved when the spine flexes", "B": "Piriformis muscle spasm entrapping the sciatic nerve during repetitive hip external rotation", "C": "Atherosclerotic narrowing of the iliac arteries limiting blood flow to working leg muscles", "D": "Lumbar facet joint arthropathy producing axial pain aggravated by spinal extension and rotation", "E": "Lumbar disc herniation compressing a single root, aggravated by sitting and forward flexion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced canal diameter during lumbar extension compressing nerve roots, relieved when the spine flexes): Neurogenic claudication from lumbar spinal stenosis worsens with extension and walking and improves with flexion, such as leaning on a cart or cycling. Vascular claudication improves with simple standing and is accompanied by diminished pulses. A herniated disc typically worsens with sitting."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes for 12 years and hypertension has a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio of 180 mg/g on two of three samples over 6 months and an eGFR of 62 mL/min/1.73 m2. She has background retinopathy and no hematuria or red cell casts. Which finding would a renal biopsy most likely show?", "options": {"A": "Subepithelial immune complex deposits with spike and dome pattern and diffuse capillary wall thickening", "B": "Nodular mesangial expansion with eosinophilic hyaline material and diffusely thickened glomerular basement membranes", "C": "Diffuse crescent formation with fibrin deposition and linear immunofluorescence staining along the glomerular basement membrane", "D": "Hypercellular glomeruli with neutrophil infiltration and subepithelial humps on electron microscopy after a recent infection", "E": "Effacement of podocyte foot processes with otherwise normal glomeruli on light microscopy and no deposits"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Nodular mesangial expansion with eosinophilic hyaline material and diffusely thickened glomerular basement membranes): Long standing diabetes with retinopathy and persistent albuminuria without active urine sediment indicates diabetic nephropathy. Hyperglycemia causes basement membrane thickening and mesangial expansion that forms the classic nodules of Kimmelstiel-Wilson disease. The other patterns belong to nephritic or nephrotic syndromes with different clinical clues."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 45-year-old woman in a primary care clinic has gradually progressive, velvety, hyperpigmented thickening of the posterior neck skin, shown in the photograph, along with multiple skin tags. Her BMI is 36 kg/m2 and her fasting glucose is 118 mg/dL. Which mechanism most directly produces the skin change?", "options": {"A": "Hyperinsulinemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor receptors in the skin", "B": "Chronic venous stasis depositing hemosiderin from extravasated red cells into the dermis", "C": "Androgen driven sebaceous gland hypertrophy and follicular plugging producing thick oily plaques", "D": "Excess cortisol suppressing collagen synthesis and thinning the dermis with wide violaceous striae", "E": "Excess ACTH activating melanocortin receptors on melanocytes and darkening skin in friction areas"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hyperinsulinemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor receptors in the skin): High circulating insulin binds insulin-like growth factor receptors on keratinocytes and dermal fibroblasts, causing papillomatosis and hyperkeratosis in flexural skin. Obesity with elevated fasting glucose points to insulin resistance. ACTH excess darkens skin diffusely and does not produce a velvety texture.", "image": "question_images/q_899.png"}, {"id": 900, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 68-year-old obese woman with hypertension has exertional dyspnea and bilateral leg edema. Echocardiography shows a left ventricular ejection fraction of 62 percent, concentric hypertrophy, and an elevated E to e prime ratio. She takes a loop diuretic. Which added drug class has trial evidence of reducing heart failure hospitalization or cardiovascular death?", "options": {"A": "Digoxin, which increases contractility and slows atrioventricular nodal conduction in the failing ventricle", "B": "Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate, which lowers afterload and venous preload in the failing ventricle", "C": "Carvedilol, which blocks beta and alpha receptors and lowers heart rate and blood pressure", "D": "Nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, which slows heart rate and lengthens ventricular filling time", "E": "Sodium glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor, which promotes natriuresis and lowers cardiac filling pressures"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Sodium glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor, which promotes natriuresis and lowers cardiac filling pressures): This is heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, where sodium glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors lower hospitalization and cardiovascular death in addition to diuretics for congestion. Rate slowing drugs and inotropes have no outcome benefit when systolic function is normal. The nitrate and hydralazine combination is validated in reduced ejection fraction."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 70-year-old woman who speaks only Vietnamese presents with chest pain. Her 15-year-old grandson, who accompanied her, offers to translate, and the clinic's telephone interpreter line is available. The physician needs a detailed history and must discuss a possible cardiac catheterization. What is the best approach?", "options": {"A": "Ask a nurse with some Vietnamese to interpret, since she is nearby and knows the clinic", "B": "Use the grandson for the history and the interpreter line only for the catheterization consent discussion", "C": "Use a qualified professional interpreter and permit the grandson to stay as support if she agrees", "D": "Rely on the grandson to translate, since relatives know the patient and speed the interview", "E": "Proceed in English with simple words, gestures and written handouts, and confirm understanding by nodding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a qualified professional interpreter and permit the grandson to stay as support if she agrees): Qualified interpreters improve accuracy and safety and protect confidentiality, and a minor child should not interpret for sensitive or complex medical discussions. Bilingual staff without interpreter training can omit or distort content. The grandson can remain for support only if the patient wishes."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 36-year-old man with opioid use disorder is started on buprenorphine. His physician explains that increasing the dose beyond a certain point produces no greater respiratory depression than a lower dose. Which pharmacodynamic property of the drug at the mu receptor best explains this ceiling effect?", "options": {"A": "Competitive antagonism, shifting the dose response curve rightward while preserving the maximal effect", "B": "Full agonism with low potency, requiring higher doses to reach the same maximal response", "C": "Inverse agonism, stabilizing the inactive receptor conformation and lowering basal signaling activity", "D": "Noncompetitive antagonism, reducing the maximal response by removing receptors from the available pool", "E": "Partial agonism, producing a submaximal effect even when every receptor is occupied"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Partial agonism, producing a submaximal effect even when every receptor is occupied): A partial agonist has lower intrinsic activity, so even with full receptor occupancy the response stays below that of a full agonist, which creates a ceiling on respiratory depression. A competitive antagonist would shift the curve but not lower the maximum. Low potency changes only the dose needed, not the maximal effect."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "community_health", "question": "Twenty-two attendees of a church luncheon develop vomiting and diarrhea. The epidemic curve shown plots cases by hours after the meal. The diagnosis is confirmed, all cases are counted, and a food history shows many ate potato salad. All leftover food was discarded, so no one remains exposed. What is the next best step in the investigation?", "options": {"A": "Calculate food-specific attack rates among exposed and unexposed diners to test the leading hypothesis", "B": "Verify the diagnosis by sending stool specimens from ill attendees for culture and toxin testing", "C": "Confirm that an outbreak exists by comparing the case count with expected baseline numbers", "D": "Define a case by symptoms, time and place so that counting can begin", "E": "Communicate findings to the public recommending removal of potato salad from all church luncheons"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Calculate food-specific attack rates among exposed and unexposed diners to test the leading hypothesis): The diagnosis is verified, cases are defined and counted, and the curve shows a point-source pattern, so the investigation now moves to testing the hypothesis. Comparing attack rates in those who ate each item with those who did not identifies the likely vehicle. Public communication follows, since discarded food leaves no ongoing exposure to interrupt.", "image": "question_images/q_903.png"}, {"id": 904, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 62-year-old man of Han Chinese descent started a daily medication to lower serum uric acid three weeks ago. He now has fever, painful oral and conjunctival erosions, and a dusky erythematous rash with flaccid bullae over 20 percent of his body surface. Nikolsky sign is positive. Which drug most likely triggered this reaction?", "options": {"A": "Febuxostat, a nonpurine xanthine oxidase inhibitor linked mainly to hepatotoxicity and cardiovascular death", "B": "Colchicine, a tubulin binding agent whose toxicity is gastrointestinal upset and bone marrow suppression", "C": "Allopurinol, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor linked to HLA-B*58:01 hypersensitivity in this ancestry", "D": "Naproxen, a nonsteroidal agent whose cutaneous reactions are usually mild photosensitive or urticarial eruptions", "E": "Probenecid, a uricosuric organic anion transport inhibitor that can precipitate uric acid kidney stones"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Allopurinol, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor linked to HLA-B*58:01 hypersensitivity in this ancestry): Mucosal erosions with epidermal detachment over 10 to 30 percent of the body surface describe Stevens-Johnson and toxic epidermal necrolysis overlap, and allopurinol in patients carrying HLA-B*58:01, especially of Han Chinese ancestry, is a classic cause. Febuxostat belongs to the same class but lacks this strong HLA association."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 28-year-old woman taking oral contraceptives begins a four-drug regimen for pulmonary tuberculosis. Three weeks later her tears and urine are orange, and she is found to be pregnant despite taking every pill on schedule. Her liver enzymes are normal. Which drug best explains both observations?", "options": {"A": "Streptomycin, an aminoglycoside that alters intestinal flora and reduces enterohepatic recycling of estrogen", "B": "Pyrazinamide, which inhibits renal urate secretion and colors urine while displacing hormones from carrier proteins", "C": "Ethambutol, which accumulates in lacrimal and renal tissue and reduces enterohepatic recirculation of estrogen", "D": "Isoniazid, a drug cleared by acetylation whose metabolites pigment body fluids and displace estrogen", "E": "Rifampin, which induces hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes, accelerating estrogen metabolism and coloring secretions orange"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rifampin, which induces hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes, accelerating estrogen metabolism and coloring secretions orange): Rifampin turns tears, urine, and sweat orange and is a potent inducer of cytochrome P450, which speeds clearance of estrogen and progestin and causes contraceptive failure. Streptomycin is tempting because of the long standing flora theory, but evidence supports enzyme induction by rifampin. Isoniazid, in contrast, inhibits these enzymes."}, {"id": 906, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 55-year-old man develops sudden vertigo, hoarseness and trouble swallowing. He has smoked for 30 years. Examination shows loss of pain and temperature on the left face and the right body, left Horner syndrome, and left limb ataxia. Which artery is most likely occluded?", "options": {"A": "Left paramedian basilar branches supplying the basis pontis", "B": "Left anterior spinal artery supplying the medial medulla and pyramid", "C": "Left anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplying the lateral caudal pons", "D": "Left superior cerebellar artery supplying the dorsolateral rostral pons", "E": "Left posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplying the lateral medulla"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Left posterior inferior cerebellar artery supplying the lateral medulla): Wallenberg lateral medullary syndrome results from occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery or the vertebral artery. Ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss (spinal trigeminal nucleus), contralateral body loss (spinothalamic tract), Horner syndrome, dysphagia and hoarseness (nucleus ambiguus) and ataxia all fit. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery lesions add facial paralysis and deafness."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A healthy 25-year-old volunteer in a sleep laboratory is awakened from a period showing low-voltage fast EEG activity, bursts of rapid eye movements and almost absent chin muscle tone. She reports a vivid, story-like dream. Heart rate and respiration were irregular during the period. Which stage is she in, and what is its mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Stage N1 sleep, a transition stage with slow rolling eye movements and theta waves", "B": "Stage N2 sleep, defined by sleep spindles and K complexes generated by thalamic and cortical circuits", "C": "REM sleep, a paradoxical stage with wake-like cortical activity driven by pontine cholinergic neurons", "D": "Stage N3 sleep, slow wave activity with delta waves, with growth hormone release and sleepwalking events", "E": "Quiet wakefulness with eyes closed, showing alpha rhythm and normal skeletal muscle tone during relaxation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (REM sleep, a paradoxical stage with wake-like cortical activity driven by pontine cholinergic neurons): REM sleep combines low-voltage fast EEG activity, rapid eye movements, skeletal muscle atonia, vivid dreaming and irregular autonomic activity, and it is generated by cholinergic neurons of the pontine reticular formation. N3 shows delta waves with sleepwalking and growth hormone release, N2 shows spindles and K complexes, and relaxed wakefulness shows alpha rhythm with intact muscle tone."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 32-year-old woman reports sharp pain during and after defecation lasting hours, with streaks of bright red blood on the tissue. She has avoided bowel movements for several days. Examination shows a linear tear at the posterior midline with a sentinel tag and spasm of the sphincter. What is the best initial management?", "options": {"A": "Anal dilation under anesthesia to disrupt the hypertonic internal sphincter", "B": "Rubber band ligation of the sentinel tag and underlying vascular cushion", "C": "Incision and drainage of the perianal collection followed by a course of antibiotics", "D": "Lateral internal sphincterotomy at the earliest opportunity to relieve sphincter hypertonia", "E": "Fiber, fluids, and sitz baths with a topical agent that relaxes the internal sphincter"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Fiber, fluids, and sitz baths with a topical agent that relaxes the internal sphincter): Acute fissures usually heal with stool softening, bulk, warm baths, and a topical calcium channel blocker or nitroglycerin that lowers sphincter pressure. Sphincterotomy is for chronic fissures that fail medical therapy. Banding and drainage address other anorectal problems."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 19-year-old primigravida with no prenatal care delivers a term infant. In the delivery room, the newborn has loops of bowel protruding through a small abdominal wall defect just right of the umbilical cord, with no covering membrane. The cord inserts normally, and the infant is otherwise well-appearing. Which feature is the most likely associated finding with this defect?", "options": {"A": "Intestinal atresia from vascular compromise of the exposed bowel, with no increased chromosomal risk", "B": "Lateral fold closure defect of the thorax, producing ectopia cordis and sternal clefting", "C": "Trisomy 18 with associated cardiac defects, typical of a midline herniation covered by peritoneum", "D": "Persistent urachus draining urine from the bladder dome through the umbilicus after birth", "E": "Failure of the gut to return to the abdomen, with Beckwith-Wiedemann macroglossia and hypoglycemia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intestinal atresia from vascular compromise of the exposed bowel, with no increased chromosomal risk): A paraumbilical defect beside a normally inserted cord with uncovered eviscerated bowel is gastroschisis, usually seen in young mothers. The exposed loops can twist or lose blood supply in utero, producing intestinal atresia. Chromosomal and cardiac associations belong to omphalocele, in which the midline herniated gut is covered by a membrane."}, {"id": 910, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 22-year-old college swimmer presents 2 days after a fall with sudden left-sided low back pain. The paraspinal tissues at L2 to L4 are warm, moist, boggy, and erythematous; the patient also reports a long history of mild right shoulder aching. Which interpretation best describes the lumbar findings?", "options": {"A": "Subacute dysfunction with mixed findings, as seen when acute injury overlies an old chronic restriction", "B": "Chronic myofascial trigger point syndrome with taut band formation and local twitch response in the paraspinals", "C": "Chronic somatic dysfunction reflecting fibrosis, ropy tissue, and reduced local blood flow with cool, dry skin", "D": "Primary visceral dysfunction with viscerosomatic reflex producing acute paraspinal edema at the same levels", "E": "Acute somatic dysfunction reflecting local edema, vasodilation, and early inflammatory congestion of the segmental soft tissues"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Acute somatic dysfunction reflecting local edema, vasodilation, and early inflammatory congestion of the segmental soft tissues): Warm, moist, boggy, erythematous tissue after sudden injury is the classic texture of acute somatic dysfunction. Chronic dysfunction would feel cool, dry, and ropy, and the long-standing shoulder aching is a separate complaint that does not make the lumbar findings subacute."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 9-year-old boy has had a sore throat for two days with fever of 38.9 C, tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and tonsillar exudates. He has no cough, rhinorrhea, or splenomegaly. A rapid antigen test performed in the clinic is negative. Which next step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Order a heterophile antibody test to evaluate for Epstein-Barr virus mononucleosis first", "B": "Reassure that the illness is viral and recommend only analgesics and fluids", "C": "Start penicillin empirically because the clinical score predicts streptococcal disease despite the negative test", "D": "Send a throat culture to confirm or exclude group A streptococcal infection before treating", "E": "Obtain a lateral neck radiograph to exclude a retropharyngeal abscess before discharge"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Send a throat culture to confirm or exclude group A streptococcal infection before treating): Fever, exudates, tender nodes and no cough give a high pretest probability of group A streptococcus. In children a negative rapid antigen test should be followed by a throat culture because the rapid test misses some cases, and treatment prevents rheumatic fever. Mononucleosis testing and imaging are not indicated by this presentation."}, {"id": 912, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 38-year-old warehouse worker has had low back pain for ten days radiating down the posterior thigh and calf to the lateral foot. He cannot rise on his toes, his ankle jerk is reduced, and the patellar reflex is normal. Which nerve root is most likely compressed, and by what lesion?", "options": {"A": "L4 root, compressed by a posterolateral herniation of the L3 to L4 disc", "B": "L3 root, compressed by a posterolateral herniation of the L2 to L3 disc", "C": "L5 root, compressed by a posterolateral herniation of the L4 to L5 disc", "D": "S1 root, compressed by a posterolateral herniation of the L5 to S1 disc", "E": "L5 root, compressed by a central herniation of the L5 to S1 disc"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (S1 root, compressed by a posterolateral herniation of the L5 to S1 disc): A reduced ankle jerk, weak plantarflexion, and lateral foot numbness indicate S1 root compression, typically from a posterolateral L5 to S1 herniation that catches the traversing root. A normal patellar reflex argues against L3 or L4 involvement. L5 root compression would weaken dorsiflexion and great toe extension with a preserved ankle jerk."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 68-year-old man with diabetes and hypertension has creatinine 1.1 mg/dL at baseline. Two days after an outpatient contrast-enhanced CT, he is lethargic with reduced urine output. He had no hypotension and takes no new drugs. The laboratory panel shown includes urine studies. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Acute tubular necrosis from medullary hypoxia and direct toxicity to renal tubular epithelium", "B": "Postrenal obstruction from bladder outlet narrowing causing bilateral hydronephrosis and a variable urine sodium", "C": "Prerenal azotemia from effective volume depletion with avid sodium and water retention by tubules", "D": "Acute interstitial nephritis from a hypersensitivity reaction with white cell casts and urine eosinophils", "E": "Atheroembolic renal disease from cholesterol crystals shed during vascular manipulation, with eosinophilia and livedo"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acute tubular necrosis from medullary hypoxia and direct toxicity to renal tubular epithelium): A rise in creatinine 24 to 72 hours after contrast with muddy brown granular casts, a fractional excretion of sodium above 2 percent, and urine osmolality near that of plasma indicates acute tubular necrosis. Prerenal disease would give a low urine sodium and concentrated urine. Atheroembolism follows arterial catheterization, not intravenous contrast, and brings eosinophilia.", "image": "question_images/q_913.png"}, {"id": 914, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 24-year-old woman notices a painless breast lump during a routine clinic visit. Examination shows a firm, rubbery, freely mobile 2 cm mass in the upper outer quadrant that has not changed in size across her menstrual cycles. Ultrasound shows a smooth, oval, well-circumscribed hypoechoic lesion. Which histologic finding is expected on core biopsy?", "options": {"A": "Proliferation of both stromal and epithelial elements compressing ducts into slit-like spaces within loose myxoid stroma", "B": "Fibrous stroma with cysts, apocrine metaplasia, and mild ductal hyperplasia typical of fibrocystic change", "C": "Sclerosing adenosis with distorted lobular architecture and microcalcifications that can mimic carcinoma", "D": "Dilated ducts with periductal inflammation, plasma cells, and nipple retraction from duct ectasia", "E": "Leaf-like projections with hypercellular stroma and frequent mitoses growing rapidly over several months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Proliferation of both stromal and epithelial elements compressing ducts into slit-like spaces within loose myxoid stroma): A fibroadenoma is a benign biphasic lesion in which proliferating stroma and epithelium compress ducts into slit-like spaces. Its mobility, smooth margins, and lack of cyclic change separate it from fibrocystic change. A phyllodes tumor would show leaf-like architecture with hypercellular stroma and would usually grow quickly."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 66-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has two days of severe dyspnea and orthopnea. Blood pressure is 152/94 mm Hg and heart rate is 108/min, with crackles to the scapulae, jugular venous distension, and warm extremities. Oxygen saturation is 88 percent on room air. Which is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic therapy?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous milrinone to raise cardiac output through phosphodiesterase three inhibition, inotropy and peripheral vasodilation", "B": "Oral digoxin loading to strengthen contraction and slow the ventricular rate over several hours", "C": "Rapid normal saline bolus to restore effective circulating volume and left ventricular filling pressure", "D": "Intravenous metoprolol to slow the sinus rate and reduce myocardial oxygen demand during congestion", "E": "Intravenous loop diuretic given early to relieve pulmonary congestion through venodilation and natriuresis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Intravenous loop diuretic given early to relieve pulmonary congestion through venodilation and natriuresis): He is warm and wet with preserved perfusion, so rapid decongestion with an intravenous loop diuretic is first. Milrinone is reserved for low output states with poor perfusion. Starting a beta blocker during acute pulmonary edema can worsen failure, and a saline bolus adds to the congestion."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "An 80-year-old woman with postoperative ileus after bowel resection has known metastatic breast cancer with a lytic lesion at T11 on recent imaging. Examination shows tender, restricted T10 to T12 segments. A resident proposes osteopathic treatment to support gut motility. Which approach is most appropriate for this patient?", "options": {"A": "High velocity low amplitude thrust at T11 to restore segmental motion and sympathetic balance", "B": "Articulatory technique with repetitive direct springing of T10 to T12 through the restrictive barrier", "C": "Indirect positioning of ease at adjacent segments, with no direct force applied through the involved vertebra", "D": "Direct muscle energy at T11 using isometric contraction against a counterforce at the barrier", "E": "Direct myofascial release with sustained deep pressure over the T11 spinous process and paraspinal muscles"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Indirect positioning of ease at adjacent segments, with no direct force applied through the involved vertebra): A lytic metastasis makes the vertebra vulnerable to fracture, so thrust and forceful direct techniques are contraindicated at that segment. Gentle indirect positioning of adjacent regions can still influence sympathetic tone without loading the lesion. Muscle energy and articulatory methods are direct and still apply load across the involved segment."}, {"id": 917, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 19-year-old Army recruit has two weeks of worsening pain in the left shin during marching that now persists at rest. There is focal tenderness over the posteromedial tibia and pain with single-leg hopping. Radiographs of the tibia are normal. He wants to avoid radiation. Which study is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Ultrasound of the posterior calf to exclude deep venous thrombosis as the cause of leg pain", "B": "Repeat tibial radiographs within twenty four hours to look for a newly developing fracture line", "C": "Magnetic resonance imaging, which detects early marrow edema and cortical stress reaction before plain films change", "D": "Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry of the lumbar spine and hip to measure bone density loss", "E": "Computed tomography of the tibia, performed first because it outlines cortical disruption in fine detail"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Magnetic resonance imaging, which detects early marrow edema and cortical stress reaction before plain films change): Focal bony tenderness with pain on hopping in a recruit suggests a tibial stress injury, and plain films are often normal for the first weeks. MRI detects marrow edema and periosteal reaction early without ionizing radiation. Repeating films within a day adds nothing, and densitometry does not evaluate a focal injury."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 22-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with an acute asthma exacerbation. The osteopathic physician palpates the paraspinal tissues and expects viscerosomatic reflex changes reflecting sympathetic input to the affected organ. At which spinal levels are the segmental tissue texture changes most likely to be found?", "options": {"A": "L1 to L2, the sympathetic outflow levels supplying the distal colon", "B": "T5 to T9, the sympathetic outflow levels supplying the stomach and upper gastrointestinal tract", "C": "T10 to L1, the sympathetic outflow levels supplying the kidney and ureter", "D": "S2 to S4, the parasympathetic outflow levels supplying the pelvic viscera", "E": "T1 to T6, the sympathetic outflow levels supplying the bronchi and pulmonary vasculature"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (T1 to T6, the sympathetic outflow levels supplying the bronchi and pulmonary vasculature): Sympathetic fibers to the bronchi and lungs leave the cord at about T1 to T6, so acute asthma produces reflex changes in the paraspinal tissues at those levels. The T5 to T9, T10 to L1, and L1 to L2 ranges belong to the stomach, kidney, and distal colon, and S2 to S4 is a parasympathetic pelvic outflow."}, {"id": 919, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 68-year-old man with known gallstones presents with fever to 39.4 C, right upper quadrant pain and jaundice for two days. He is now confused with a blood pressure of 82/50 mmHg despite an initial fluid bolus. Ultrasound shows a dilated common bile duct of 12 mm. Which is the best next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Elective magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography after a seven day course of oral antibiotics", "B": "Hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid scan to confirm cystic duct obstruction before choosing antibiotics", "C": "Laparoscopic cholecystectomy within the next twenty four hours, followed by antibiotics for postoperative infection", "D": "Broad spectrum intravenous antibiotics with urgent endoscopic biliary decompression by ERCP", "E": "Intravenous antibiotics alone for forty eight hours, reserving drainage for failure of medical therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Broad spectrum intravenous antibiotics with urgent endoscopic biliary decompression by ERCP): Fever, jaundice, pain, hypotension and confusion form Reynolds pentad, indicating severe suppurative cholangitis that needs prompt biliary drainage along with antibiotics. Antibiotics alone may suffice in mild disease, but shock demands decompression. Cholecystectomy treats the gallbladder, not the infected duct."}, {"id": 920, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 24-year-old college student has intense itching that is worse at night for three weeks. Her roommate has similar symptoms. Examination shows excoriated papules and short linear burrows in the finger webs, wrists, and waistline, with sparing of the face. Which treatment is most appropriate for her and close contacts?", "options": {"A": "Oral doxycycline for ten days, since the organism is a bacterial pathogen of the skin", "B": "Topical hydrocortisone cream twice daily for a week to suppress the allergic reaction", "C": "Oral fluconazole daily for two weeks to eradicate the burrowing organism from the epidermis", "D": "Topical lindane applied once and washed off after eight hours as first line therapy", "E": "Topical permethrin applied from the neck down and repeated after one week, with contacts treated"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Topical permethrin applied from the neck down and repeated after one week, with contacts treated): Scabies from Sarcoptes scabiei causes nocturnal pruritus and burrows in web spaces, and household contacts need simultaneous treatment even if asymptomatic. Permethrin 5 percent cream is first line, and lindane is reserved for failures because of neurotoxicity."}, {"id": 921, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 52-year-old man with a BMI of 38 kg/m2 reports loud snoring and daytime sleepiness. His wife observes nightly pauses in breathing followed by gasping. Blood pressure is 158/96 mmHg despite two antihypertensive agents. Polysomnography shows an apnea-hypopnea index of 32 events per hour. Which first-line therapy is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Continuous positive airway pressure applied nightly to splint the collapsing pharyngeal airway", "B": "Modafinil alone to treat sleepiness while deferring any airway-directed therapy", "C": "Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty as the initial treatment to remove redundant soft palate tissue", "D": "Supplemental oxygen alone by nasal cannula overnight to correct the recurrent desaturations", "E": "Nightly zolpidem to consolidate sleep and reduce the arousals that follow each apneic event"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continuous positive airway pressure applied nightly to splint the collapsing pharyngeal airway): Moderate to severe obstructive sleep apnea with resistant hypertension is treated first with CPAP, which prevents pharyngeal collapse and can improve blood pressure. Sedative-hypnotics can worsen obstruction, and surgery is a later option. Oxygen alone or a stimulant leaves apneas and arousals untreated."}, {"id": 922, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with Alzheimer disease starts a new medication, and its dose is increased after two weeks. She then has a syncopal episode. Heart rate is 42 beats per minute, and she reports nausea and loose stools. ECG shows sinus bradycardia. Which mechanism most likely explains these adverse effects?", "options": {"A": "Noncompetitive NMDA receptor blockade lowering calcium entry and reducing cardiac conduction velocity", "B": "Antagonism of histamine receptors producing sedation and an orthostatic fall in blood pressure", "C": "Increased synaptic acetylcholine from cholinesterase inhibition enhancing vagal and gastrointestinal muscarinic activity", "D": "Monoamine oxidase B inhibition raising catecholamine levels and causing reflex vagal slowing", "E": "Dopamine D2 receptor blockade prolonging ventricular repolarization and slowing sinus node firing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increased synaptic acetylcholine from cholinesterase inhibition enhancing vagal and gastrointestinal muscarinic activity): Cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil raise acetylcholine and increase vagal tone, producing bradycardia, syncope, nausea, and diarrhea through muscarinic stimulation. Memantine blocks NMDA receptors and rarely slows the heart. Catecholamine elevation would raise heart rate, not lower it."}, {"id": 923, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 58-year-old man with newly diagnosed hypertension is started on hydrochlorothiazide. He has no kidney disease, takes no other medications, and reports mild fatigue. Six weeks later he undergoes laboratory testing. Which pattern of electrolyte findings is most expected from the drug's action on the distal convoluted tubule?", "options": {"A": "Low potassium, high chloride, and nonanion gap acidosis from proximal bicarbonate wasting in the kidney", "B": "Low potassium, low sodium, and low calcium with metabolic alkalosis from thick ascending limb blockade", "C": "High potassium and normal calcium from reduced angiotensin II mediated aldosterone release in the adrenal", "D": "Low potassium, low sodium, and high calcium with a mild metabolic alkalosis", "E": "High potassium, low sodium, and metabolic acidosis from reduced principal cell sodium reabsorption"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low potassium, low sodium, and high calcium with a mild metabolic alkalosis): Thiazides block the sodium chloride cotransporter in the distal tubule, increasing distal sodium delivery and potassium loss, causing hypokalemia, hyponatremia, and alkalosis. They enhance calcium reabsorption, so calcium rises, unlike loop diuretics. The other patterns belong to loop, potassium sparing, carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, and renin angiotensin drugs."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 17-year-old high school student has had 2 weeks of low-grade fever, headache, and dry hacking cough. Examination reveals few crackles, yet the chest radiograph shows patchy bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Gram stain of sputum shows neutrophils without organisms. Amoxicillin produced no improvement. Which property of the causative organism best explains the treatment failure?", "options": {"A": "Absence of a peptidoglycan cell wall, which removes the target of beta-lactam antibiotics", "B": "Production of beta-lactamase that hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring before the drug reaches its target", "C": "Active efflux pumps that export beta-lactam drugs out of the periplasmic space", "D": "Replication within the host cell cytoplasm, which limits penetration of penicillin derivatives into the organism", "E": "Thick polysaccharide capsule that prevents drug binding to penicillin-binding proteins on the cell surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Absence of a peptidoglycan cell wall, which removes the target of beta-lactam antibiotics): Gradual onset, dry cough, interstitial infiltrates out of proportion to examination, and a Gram stain showing no organisms fit Mycoplasma pneumoniae. It lacks a cell wall, so amoxicillin has no peptidoglycan to disrupt, and it does not stain with the Gram method. A macrolide or doxycycline acts on the ribosome and is effective."}, {"id": 925, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 44-year-old man reports increasing ring and shoe size over several years, coarse facial features, and new snoring. Examination shows prognathism, macroglossia, and wide spacing of teeth. Blood pressure is 152/94 mm Hg. Random glucose is mildly elevated. Which test is the best initial biochemical study to establish the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Random serum growth hormone level obtained in the morning before breakfast and exercise", "B": "Low-dose dexamethasone suppression test measuring morning cortisol the following day to exclude hypercortisolism", "C": "Serum insulin-like growth factor 1 level, reflecting integrated growth hormone secretion over the preceding day", "D": "Serum prolactin level, since many growth hormone secreting adenomas cosecrete prolactin", "E": "Pituitary MRI with gadolinium to localize an adenoma before any biochemical confirmation is obtained"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Serum insulin-like growth factor 1 level, reflecting integrated growth hormone secretion over the preceding day): Growth hormone is released in pulses, so IGF-1, produced by the liver in response to sustained exposure, is the best screening test for acromegaly. A random growth hormone value can be normal or high by chance. Confirmation uses failure of growth hormone to suppress after a glucose load, and MRI comes after biochemical diagnosis."}, {"id": 926, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 23-year-old man with known Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome presents with palpitations. He is hemodynamically stable. His ECG shows an irregularly irregular, wide-complex tachycardia at 240 beats per minute with varying QRS morphology. A resident proposes several drugs. Which drug is the most appropriate pharmacologic treatment?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous digoxin, enhancing vagal tone to slow atrioventricular conduction during atrial fibrillation", "B": "Intravenous verapamil, slowing atrioventricular nodal conduction to control the ventricular response rate during tachycardia", "C": "Intravenous procainamide, slowing conduction in the accessory pathway and prolonging its refractory period", "D": "Intravenous adenosine, briefly blocking atrioventricular nodal conduction to terminate the reentrant tachycardia", "E": "Intravenous esmolol, reducing sympathetic drive to slow atrioventricular conduction and heart rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravenous procainamide, slowing conduction in the accessory pathway and prolonging its refractory period): Irregular wide-complex tachycardia with varying morphology in a patient with WPW indicates preexcited atrial fibrillation, in which AV nodal blockers can increase conduction down the accessory pathway and precipitate ventricular fibrillation. Procainamide slows accessory pathway conduction, so it is the appropriate drug in a stable patient. Digoxin even shortens accessory pathway refractoriness."}, {"id": 927, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 44-year-old woman with a chronic cough has thoracic segments T3 to T5 that, in neutral position, rotate freely to the right and sidebend freely to the left, with restriction of the opposite motions. Motion testing in flexion and extension does not change the pattern. Which dysfunction best describes this finding?", "options": {"A": "Type I group dysfunction in neutral, sidebent left and rotated right (NSLRR)", "B": "Type III dysfunction, with motion introduced in one plane modifying motion in the others", "C": "Type II single segment dysfunction, extended, sidebent left and rotated left (ESLRL)", "D": "Type II single segment dysfunction, flexed, sidebent right and rotated left (FSRRL)", "E": "Type I group dysfunction in neutral, sidebent right and rotated right (NSRRR)"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Type I group dysfunction in neutral, sidebent left and rotated right (NSLRR)): A multisegment group curve with sidebending and rotation to opposite sides that does not change with flexion or extension is a type I neutral dysfunction. Because dysfunctions are named for the direction of freedom, it is sidebent left and rotated right. Type II is a single non-neutral segment with sidebending and rotation to the same side, and type III involves motion introduced in one plane modifying the others."}, {"id": 928, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 79-year-old woman who has taken metformin and omeprazole for many years reports numb feet and unsteady gait. Examination shows reduced vibration sense and positive Romberg sign. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL with MCV 112 fL. Both serum homocysteine and methylmalonic acid are elevated. Which nutritional deficiency is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Cobalamin deficiency from drug related malabsorption, which blocks both methionine synthase and methylmalonyl CoA mutase", "B": "Vitamin E deficiency from fat malabsorption, causing spinocerebellar degeneration with hemolytic anemia and ataxia", "C": "Thiamine deficiency from poor intake, causing dry beriberi with symmetric distal neuropathy and cardiac involvement", "D": "Folate deficiency from poor intake, which impairs thymidylate synthesis and causes megaloblastic anemia with elevated homocysteine", "E": "Copper deficiency from zinc excess or malabsorption, causing myeloneuropathy and cytopenias with low ceruloplasmin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cobalamin deficiency from drug related malabsorption, which blocks both methionine synthase and methylmalonyl CoA mutase): Metformin and acid suppression both reduce cobalamin absorption. Macrocytic anemia, dorsal column signs, and elevation of both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid indicate cobalamin deficiency, since methylmalonyl CoA mutase needs it as well as methionine synthase. Folate deficiency raises homocysteine alone and does not cause the neurologic findings."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 62-year-old man has three months of back pain, fatigue, normocytic anemia, serum calcium of 11.8 mg/dL and elevated creatinine. A skeletal survey shows multiple punched-out lytic lesions of the skull and spine. A technetium-99m bone scan is ordered. Which result is most likely, and why?", "options": {"A": "Superscan pattern with diffuse hot uptake, because plasma cells stimulate bone formation like prostate metastases", "B": "Normal or low uptake at many lytic sites, since tracer needs osteoblastic activity that is minimal", "C": "Uptake mainly in soft tissue and kidneys, because circulating light chains bind the radiotracer avidly", "D": "Intense uniform uptake at all lytic sites, since tracer binds the exposed hydroxyapatite of destroyed bone", "E": "Diffuse increased uptake across the skeleton, because hypercalcemia deposits calcium in normal bone and lung"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal or low uptake at many lytic sites, since tracer needs osteoblastic activity that is minimal): Technetium-99m diphosphonate localizes where osteoblasts are forming new bone. Multiple myeloma lesions are purely lytic with suppressed osteoblast activity, so the bone scan is often falsely negative, which is why skeletal survey, MRI or PET is preferred. Prostate metastases, by contrast, are osteoblastic and produce hot uptake."}, {"id": 930, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A new serum marker for a rapidly progressive but curable lymphoma yields a curve allowing several possible cutoffs. Confirmatory biopsy is safe, inexpensive and widely available, while missed disease leads to death within months. An oncology committee reviews a cutoff yielding 98 percent sensitivity and 60 percent specificity. Which statement best supports adopting it?", "options": {"A": "The cutoff should favor specificity so fewer healthy patients are sent for biopsy", "B": "The cutoff should maximize positive predictive value because treating only confirmed cases avoids overtreatment", "C": "False positives are tolerable because biopsy resolves them, and missed cases cost lives", "D": "The cutoff nearest the diagonal line is preferable because it balances both error types evenly", "E": "Sixty percent specificity makes the marker unusable because specificity should match the sensitivity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (False positives are tolerable because biopsy resolves them, and missed cases cost lives): When missed disease is fatal and curable while confirmatory biopsy is safe and cheap, the cost of a false positive is small and the cost of a false negative is large. A cutoff favoring sensitivity is therefore appropriate, and biopsy removes the false positives. Favoring specificity would sacrifice the sensitivity that protects patients, and positive predictive value is secondary here."}, {"id": 931, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 31-year-old nurse brings her 2-year-old to the emergency department repeatedly with unexplained vomiting and lethargy. Hospital testing shows hypoglycemia with high insulin and suppressed C-peptide while she is the only caregiver present. She appears devoted and shows no financial or legal motive. What is the most likely diagnosis in the mother?", "options": {"A": "Somatic symptom disorder, with excessive worry about the child's symptoms and repeated reassurance seeking", "B": "Malingering by proxy, with illness induced in the child to obtain housing or compensation", "C": "Delusional disorder, somatic type, with a fixed false belief that the child is gravely ill", "D": "Factitious disorder imposed on another, with fabricated illness in a dependent to gain attention", "E": "Illness anxiety disorder, with preoccupation that the child has a serious undiagnosed endocrine disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Factitious disorder imposed on another, with fabricated illness in a dependent to gain attention): High insulin with suppressed C-peptide indicates exogenous insulin, so the illness is being produced deliberately in the child. When the motive is the caregiver's psychological need for the sick role by proxy, rather than a tangible reward, the diagnosis is factitious disorder imposed on another. Malingering requires an external incentive, which the stem excludes."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 24-year-old woman has left chest wall pain after coughing for a week. Ribs 5 through 7 on the left stay elevated and move poorly during exhalation, and the group is diagnosed as an inhalation rib dysfunction. The physician must choose the rib to treat first. Which rib is the key rib for this group, and why?", "options": {"A": "Rib 7, the lowest rib, because its restriction prevents the entire group from exhaling", "B": "Rib 5, the highest rib, because its restriction prevents the entire group from inhaling", "C": "Rib 5, the highest rib, because its restriction prevents the entire group from exhaling", "D": "Rib 7, the lowest rib, because its restriction prevents the entire group from inhaling", "E": "Rib 6, the middle rib, because its attachments coordinate motion of the entire group"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rib 7, the lowest rib, because its restriction prevents the entire group from exhaling): In an inhalation group the ribs are stuck in inhalation and cannot exhale, and the lowest rib is the key rib because it locks the group in that position. For an exhalation group the key rib is the highest one, which reverses this pattern."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 31-year-old woman at 26 weeks gestation has right buttock pain with walking. Standing flexion test is positive on the right, seated flexion test is negative, and the right posterior superior iliac spine is more superior. She has no vaginal bleeding or contractions. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Rib raising to the lower thoracic cage alone, since pelvic treatment must wait until delivery", "B": "Deep visceral manipulation over the uterine fundus to release round ligament tension on the pelvis", "C": "High-velocity low-amplitude thrust across the right sacroiliac joint with the patient lying prone", "D": "Sustained lumbar roll with strong extension while prone to open the right sacroiliac joint", "E": "Muscle energy to the right innominate with the patient side-lying, avoiding prolonged prone positioning"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Muscle energy to the right innominate with the patient side-lying, avoiding prolonged prone positioning): A positive standing flexion test with a negative seated test places the restriction at the iliosacral joint, so an innominate somatic dysfunction is treated. Muscle energy is a gentle, patient-activated direct technique that works side-lying, avoiding the discomfort of prone positioning late in pregnancy. Thrust techniques add strain to ligaments loosened by relaxin, and uterine visceral work does not address this finding."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 54-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis reports suboccipital pain and difficulty turning her head. Motion testing finds restricted rotation at C1 on C2 with tender muscles. A lateral flexion-extension radiograph shows an increased atlantodental interval. She requests the treatment that worked for her husband. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use long-lever cervical traction with thrust to restore symmetric motion before imaging is repeated", "B": "Use muscle energy with minimal patient effort at the C1 on C2 restrictive barrier", "C": "Use articulatory technique with repeated springing through full rotation to widen the restricted joint", "D": "Avoid direct techniques at the unstable segments and use gentle indirect or soft tissue methods", "E": "Apply a high-velocity low-amplitude rotational thrust at C1 on C2 because restriction and tenderness are documented"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid direct techniques at the unstable segments and use gentle indirect or soft tissue methods): An increased atlantodental interval indicates atlantoaxial instability, so direct techniques that load the barrier, including thrust, muscle energy, and articulation, are contraindicated at that level. Gentle indirect or soft tissue treatment addresses the muscle tenderness without stressing the unstable joint."}, {"id": 935, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who stopped taking her anticoagulant presents with sudden severe abdominal pain. Her abdomen is soft with mild tenderness despite the intensity of her pain. She reports no previous pain with meals. Lactate is 5.2 mmol/L and she has leukocytosis. Stool is guaiac positive. Which is the most likely cause of her condition?", "options": {"A": "Embolus lodging in the superior mesenteric artery just beyond the middle colic branch", "B": "Nonocclusive hypoperfusion from sustained splanchnic vasoconstriction during a severe low cardiac output state", "C": "Thrombosis of the superior mesenteric vein from hypercoagulability causing venous congestion of bowel", "D": "Thrombosis at the origin of the superior mesenteric artery on a background of chronic atherosclerosis", "E": "Compression of the celiac trunk by the median arcuate ligament producing foregut ischemia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Embolus lodging in the superior mesenteric artery just beyond the middle colic branch): Atrial fibrillation without anticoagulation predisposes to emboli, which typically lodge in the superior mesenteric artery just distal to the middle colic branch. Pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen reflects early ischemia before peritonitis develops. Thrombosis at the origin usually follows months of postprandial pain and weight loss, which she denies."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A researcher measures the reaction velocity of an enzyme at several substrate concentrations, then repeats the assay after adding a new drug. At very high substrate concentrations the maximal velocity is identical to the first assay, but the substrate concentration giving half-maximal velocity has doubled. Which inhibitor type best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Uncompetitive inhibitor that binds only the enzyme substrate complex and lowers both kinetic parameters together", "B": "Irreversible inhibitor that covalently modifies a catalytic residue and removes functional enzyme molecules from the assay", "C": "Noncompetitive inhibitor that binds an allosteric site and lowers maximal velocity while leaving substrate affinity unchanged", "D": "Competitive inhibitor that occupies the active site and is overcome by excess substrate concentration", "E": "Allosteric activator that increases substrate affinity and shifts the saturation curve toward lower concentrations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Competitive inhibitor that occupies the active site and is overcome by excess substrate concentration): A competitive inhibitor raises the apparent Km but does not change Vmax, because high substrate concentration outcompetes the inhibitor. Noncompetitive and irreversible inhibitors lower Vmax, and an activator would lower Km."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 48-year-old woman with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis of 2 years' duration presents to the emergency department with headache and blurred vision. Blood pressure is 210/120 mm Hg, creatinine has risen from 0.9 to 2.4 mg/dL, and smear shows schistocytes. She takes low dose prednisone. Which therapy is the most appropriate for her condition?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous loop diuretic therapy to relieve volume overload and lower the blood pressure", "B": "Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor to suppress renin driven hypertension and protect renal perfusion", "C": "Plasma exchange to remove circulating antibody as in thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura with schistocytes", "D": "High dose glucocorticoids to suppress the inflammatory vasculopathy damaging the renal arterioles", "E": "Intravenous cyclophosphamide to halt progressive skin fibrosis and interstitial lung involvement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor to suppress renin driven hypertension and protect renal perfusion): Scleroderma renal crisis results from intimal proliferation of renal arterioles with renin release, producing malignant hypertension and microangiopathic hemolysis. Rapid titration of an ACE inhibitor is lifesaving, and glucocorticoids should be avoided because they are a known precipitant. Plasma exchange and diuretics do not address the renin mediated mechanism."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with chronic bronchitis has an exhalation dysfunction involving right ribs 6 through 9, with bucket-handle restriction. The physician chooses a muscle energy technique and asks the patient to push against resistance using the muscle that attaches to the lateral shafts of these ribs. Which muscle provides the activating force?", "options": {"A": "Pectoralis minor, whose attachments pull ribs 3 to 5 upward during inhalation", "B": "Serratus anterior, whose lateral attachments pull ribs 6 to 9 upward during inhalation", "C": "Latissimus dorsi, whose lower attachments act on ribs 10 and 11 during inhalation", "D": "Quadratus lumborum, whose attachment fixes rib 12 and acts during forced inhalation", "E": "Scalene muscles, whose attachments elevate ribs 1 and 2 during inhalation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Serratus anterior, whose lateral attachments pull ribs 6 to 9 upward during inhalation): Serratus anterior attaches to the lateral aspects of the middle ribs and lifts ribs 6 to 9 when the patient contracts it against the physician's counterforce. Pectoralis minor serves ribs 3 to 5, latissimus dorsi serves ribs 10 and 11, quadratus lumborum serves rib 12, and the scalenes serve ribs 1 and 2, so each is matched to a different rib group."}, {"id": 939, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "During a research protocol, a healthy adult volunteer receives a constant infusion of inulin. Plasma inulin concentration is 2 mg/mL, urine inulin concentration is 120 mg/mL, and urine flow rate is 1.5 mL/min. Plasma creatinine is 1 mg/dL. Using these data, what is the glomerular filtration rate?", "options": {"A": "Clearance of 120 mL/min, calculated as urine concentration divided by plasma creatinine concentration", "B": "Clearance of 60 mL/min, calculated as the ratio of urine concentration to plasma concentration", "C": "Clearance of 3 mL/min, calculated as plasma concentration multiplied by the urine flow rate", "D": "Clearance of 180 mL/min, calculated as urine concentration multiplied by the urine flow rate", "E": "Clearance of 90 mL/min, calculated as urine concentration times flow divided by plasma concentration"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Clearance of 90 mL/min, calculated as urine concentration times flow divided by plasma concentration): Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow divided by plasma concentration, so 120 times 1.5 divided by 2 gives 90 mL/min. Inulin is freely filtered and neither secreted nor reabsorbed, so its clearance equals filtration rate; the plasma creatinine value is irrelevant to this calculation."}, {"id": 940, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 28-year-old man of East Asian descent develops facial flushing, palpitations, and nausea within minutes of drinking one beer at a wedding. He has had the same reaction with every alcoholic drink since adolescence. Which metabolic abnormality best explains these symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Variant mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase with reduced activity, allowing acetaldehyde to accumulate after ethanol oxidation", "B": "Deficient cytosolic alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing conversion of ethanol to acetaldehyde and prolonging ethanol toxicity", "C": "Induced microsomal ethanol oxidizing system activity, depleting NADPH and generating reactive oxygen species with chronic drinking", "D": "Peroxisomal catalase deficiency, leaving hydrogen peroxide unmetabolized and causing a histamine like vascular reaction", "E": "Excess NADH from ethanol oxidation, inhibiting gluconeogenesis and producing fasting hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Variant mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase with reduced activity, allowing acetaldehyde to accumulate after ethanol oxidation): A common variant of mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase in people of East Asian ancestry slows conversion of acetaldehyde to acetate. Acetaldehyde accumulates and causes flushing, tachycardia, and nausea. Lack of the first enzyme would instead leave ethanol unmetabolized and would not produce this acetaldehyde reaction."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A premature infant born at 30 weeks has tachypnea, bounding pulses, and a continuous machinery-like murmur at the left infraclavicular area on day four. Echocardiography confirms a patent ductus arteriosus with left-to-right flow. Which medication is most appropriate to promote closure of the vessel?", "options": {"A": "Indomethacin, which inhibits cyclooxygenase and lowers the prostaglandin level that keeps the ductus open", "B": "Alprostadil, which supplies prostaglandin E1 to hold the ductus open and preserve pulmonary blood flow", "C": "Furosemide, which lowers preload and pulmonary congestion but leaves ductal tone unchanged", "D": "Dexamethasone, which accelerates lung maturity but does not constrict the ductal smooth muscle", "E": "Digoxin, which raises contractility and closes the shunt by increasing aortic pressure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Indomethacin, which inhibits cyclooxygenase and lowers the prostaglandin level that keeps the ductus open): Prostaglandin E2 keeps the ductus open after birth, and cyclooxygenase inhibitors such as indomethacin or ibuprofen reduce its synthesis and induce closure. Alprostadil does the opposite and is used in ductal dependent lesions. Diuretics may ease symptoms but do not close the vessel."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min is admitted with pneumonia. Her physician plans to prescribe a drug that is eliminated almost entirely unchanged by the kidneys, with a narrow therapeutic window. She also has well-controlled hypertension and takes lisinopril. Which dosing adjustment best maintains the same average steady-state concentration?", "options": {"A": "Reduce both the loading dose and maintenance dose by half because the half-life is doubled", "B": "Keep both doses unchanged and monitor serum creatinine weekly because the window is narrow", "C": "Increase the maintenance dose because slower elimination lowers the fraction of drug absorbed", "D": "Reduce the maintenance dose in proportion to the reduced clearance, leaving the loading dose unchanged", "E": "Reduce the loading dose in proportion to the reduced clearance, leaving the maintenance dose unchanged"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce the maintenance dose in proportion to the reduced clearance, leaving the loading dose unchanged): Average steady-state concentration equals dosing rate divided by clearance, so lower clearance requires a proportionally lower maintenance rate. The loading dose depends on volume of distribution, which is largely unchanged by renal impairment. Monitoring alone would let the drug accumulate toward toxicity."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 35-year-old man with persistent asthma takes high-dose inhaled fluticasone twice daily by metered-dose inhaler without a spacer. He reports hoarseness and a sore mouth. Examination shows white plaques on the buccal mucosa that scrape off, leaving an erythematous base. Which preventive measure would most likely have reduced this adverse effect?", "options": {"A": "Halving the dose and inhaling more slowly to reduce absorption of swallowed drug", "B": "Taking each dose with a large meal to dilute the steroid in the oropharynx", "C": "Replacing the inhaled steroid with a long-acting beta agonist monotherapy to avoid mucosal immunosuppression", "D": "Using a valved holding chamber and rinsing the mouth with water after each dose", "E": "Adding daily oral nystatin prophylaxis before each steroid dose to suppress yeast colonization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Using a valved holding chamber and rinsing the mouth with water after each dose): Inhaled corticosteroids deposited in the oropharynx cause candidiasis and dysphonia. A spacer cuts deposition, and rinsing and spitting removes residual drug, which lowers the risk. Long-acting beta agonist monotherapy is unsafe in asthma, and prophylactic antifungals are not standard."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 62-year-old man with severe pneumonia has been mechanically ventilated with an inspired oxygen fraction of 1.0 for five days. His sepsis has improved, yet he develops worsening compliance and new diffuse bilateral infiltrates. Bronchoalveolar lavage shows no organisms. Which mechanism best explains this deterioration?", "options": {"A": "Reactive oxygen species damaging alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium, causing diffuse alveolar damage and edema", "B": "Absorption atelectasis from nitrogen washout producing collapse of poorly ventilated alveolar units", "C": "Barotrauma from excessive plateau pressures producing pneumothorax and subcutaneous emphysema in stiff lungs", "D": "Cardiogenic pulmonary edema from volume overload and rising left atrial pressure after resuscitation", "E": "Hypercapnic narcosis from loss of hypoxic drive producing hypoventilation and respiratory acidosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reactive oxygen species damaging alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium, causing diffuse alveolar damage and edema): Prolonged exposure to a high oxygen fraction generates superoxide and hydroxyl radicals that injure alveolar epithelium and endothelium, causing diffuse alveolar damage and noncardiogenic edema after about 24 to 72 hours or more. Absorption atelectasis happens earlier, and the sterile lavage and improving sepsis argue against infection."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 70-year-old woman who follows a strict vegan diet and has seen no physician in years reports fatigue, tingling in both feet, and unsteady gait. Examination shows decreased vibration sense in the lower extremities. Her laboratory panel is shown. Which metabolic derangement best explains her neurologic findings?", "options": {"A": "Accumulation of an odd chain fatty acid intermediate that disrupts myelin lipid synthesis in nerves", "B": "Failure of folate dependent thymidylate synthesis producing megaloblastic changes without spinal cord injury", "C": "Impaired transketolase activity causing lactate accumulation and axonal energy failure in peripheral nerves", "D": "Impaired tryptophan conversion to niacin producing demyelination of the dorsal columns and skin changes", "E": "Reduced neurotransmitter synthesis from decreased pyridoxal phosphate dependent decarboxylation of amino acids in neurons"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Accumulation of an odd chain fatty acid intermediate that disrupts myelin lipid synthesis in nerves): Macrocytic anemia, low cobalamin, and a raised methylmalonic acid level indicate vitamin B12 deficiency from a vegan diet. Methylmalonyl CoA accumulation yields abnormal fatty acids that damage myelin in the dorsal columns. Folate deficiency raises homocysteine but leaves methylmalonic acid normal and spares the cord.", "image": "question_images/q_945.png"}, {"id": 946, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 71-year-old man with severe cervical spondylosis falls forward and strikes his chin, causing neck hyperextension. He has no fracture on imaging. Examination shows marked weakness and burning dysesthesia in both hands, mildly reduced leg strength, and urinary retention. Which injury pattern best explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Brown-Sequard syndrome from lateral hemisection with ipsilateral motor and proprioceptive loss", "B": "Posterior cord syndrome from dorsal column contusion with isolated loss of position sense", "C": "Central cord syndrome with injury to medially placed corticospinal fibers serving the arms", "D": "Conus medullaris syndrome from lumbar compression with early bladder and saddle involvement", "E": "Anterior cord syndrome from compression of the anterior spinal artery territory"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Central cord syndrome with injury to medially placed corticospinal fibers serving the arms): Hyperextension in a spondylotic canal contuses the central cervical cord. Corticospinal fibers to the arms lie medial to those for the legs, so upper limbs are disproportionately weak, with burning hand pain from central gray damage and variable bladder dysfunction. Anterior cord injury gives worse leg and pain deficits with preserved vibration, and Brown-Sequard syndrome is unilateral."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with a urinary tract infection is hospitalized after syncope. She started azithromycin and has taken a loop diuretic for edema. Telemetry shows polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with twisting QRS axis, which self-terminates. QTc is 540 ms and potassium is 3.1 mEq/L. She is currently stable. Which therapy should be started now?", "options": {"A": "Oral potassium chloride alone, since correcting hypokalemia removes the risk of recurrence", "B": "Intravenous amiodarone, which prolongs repolarization further to suppress the polymorphic ventricular arrhythmia", "C": "Intravenous magnesium sulfate, which suppresses early afterdepolarizations and prevents recurrence of torsades", "D": "Intravenous procainamide, which blocks sodium channels to terminate the recurrent ventricular rhythm", "E": "Synchronized cardioversion at 100 joules, which resets the circuit in a stable patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravenous magnesium sulfate, which suppresses early afterdepolarizations and prevents recurrence of torsades): Magnesium sulfate is first-line for torsades de pointes even when serum magnesium is normal, because it suppresses the early afterdepolarizations that trigger the rhythm. Class IA and III drugs such as procainamide and amiodarone prolong the QT interval further and can worsen it. Potassium should also be repleted, but that alone is not the best immediate step."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 17-year-old basketball player lands on another player's foot, rolling the left ankle into plantar flexion and inversion. There is swelling and tenderness anterior and inferior to the lateral malleolus, and the anterior drawer test shows increased forward talar translation. Which ligament is injured first and most often in this mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Anterior talofibular ligament, the weakest lateral band when the foot is pointed downward", "B": "Anterior tibiofibular ligament, the syndesmotic band injured by forced external rotation and dorsiflexion", "C": "Posterior talofibular ligament, the deep band injured only in severe inversion with dislocation", "D": "Deltoid ligament, the strong medial complex torn by forced eversion of the foot", "E": "Calcaneofibular ligament, the extra-articular band that tightens when the foot is dorsiflexed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Anterior talofibular ligament, the weakest lateral band when the foot is pointed downward): In plantar flexion and inversion the anterior talofibular ligament is the most taut and weakest of the lateral ligaments, so it tears first, and the anterior drawer test examines it. The calcaneofibular tears with more severe inversion, and the deltoid is injured by eversion instead."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 34-year-old man in a rural practice has right low back pain after lifting. The standing flexion test shows the right posterior superior iliac spine traveling farther cephalad than the left, and the seated flexion test is symmetric. Which region is the most likely site of the somatic dysfunction?", "options": {"A": "The right hip joint, where the femoral head is restricted within the acetabulum", "B": "The right iliosacral joint, where the innominate moves independently on the sacrum", "C": "The right sacroiliac articulation, where the sacrum itself is restricted between the ilia", "D": "The pubic symphysis, where the superior pubic rami are shifted on one another", "E": "The lumbosacral junction, where the fifth lumbar vertebra is restricted on the sacral base"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The right iliosacral joint, where the innominate moves independently on the sacrum): Standing flexion loads the innominates through the legs, so asymmetric PSIS excursion with a symmetric seated test places the dysfunction at the iliosacral joint. A positive seated flexion test would instead implicate the sacrum between the ilia. The lumbosacral junction and hip do not produce this asymmetric PSIS movement in standing flexion."}, {"id": 950, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 38-year-old woman, G2P1 at 16 weeks gestation, undergoes second-trimester maternal serum screening, with results in the table shown. Ultrasonography confirms dating, and she has no diabetes or twin gestation. She has no vaginal bleeding or pain. Which fetal condition is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Open neural tube defect, in which AFP leaks into amniotic fluid and raises maternal serum levels", "B": "Fetal growth restriction from placental insufficiency, in which AFP and hCG are both raised", "C": "Trisomy 21, in which low AFP and estriol accompany raised hCG and inhibin A levels", "D": "X-linked steroid sulfatase deficiency, in which estriol is very low but the other analytes are normal", "E": "Trisomy 18, in which AFP, hCG and estriol are all low with normal inhibin A levels"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Trisomy 21, in which low AFP and estriol accompany raised hCG and inhibin A levels): Down syndrome shows low AFP, low unconjugated estriol, high hCG and high inhibin A, and maternal age of 38 raises the prior risk. Trisomy 18 lowers all analytes, and neural tube defects raise AFP. Diagnostic confirmation would use amniocentesis or cell-free DNA with karyotype.", "image": "question_images/q_950.png"}, {"id": 951, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 45-year-old mechanic who often rests his elbow on a hard armrest has numbness of the little finger and medial half of the ring finger. He cannot grip paper between his fingers and flexes his thumb tip when trying to hold a sheet. Sensation over the medial forearm is normal. Which nerve problem is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Radial nerve compression in the spiral groove, weakening wrist extension and finger extension", "B": "Ulnar nerve compression behind the medial epicondyle, weakening thumb adduction and finger abduction", "C": "C6 nerve root compression at the foramen, weakening elbow flexion and wrist extension", "D": "Musculocutaneous nerve compression near the coracobrachialis, weakening elbow flexion and forearm supination", "E": "Median nerve compression beneath the flexor retinaculum, weakening thumb opposition and the lateral lumbricals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ulnar nerve compression behind the medial epicondyle, weakening thumb adduction and finger abduction): Ulnar nerve compression at the elbow behind the medial epicondyle causes numbness of the fifth and medial fourth digits, weak finger abduction, and a positive Froment sign from thumb adductor weakness. Median compression spares these digits, and radial injury affects extension. A cervical root lesion would involve the forearm or thumb instead."}, {"id": 952, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "During a cranial osteopathic skills laboratory, a student palpates a volunteer's head and feels the flexion phase of the cranial rhythmic impulse. The instructor asks the class to describe the expected motion at the sphenobasilar synchondrosis and the skull's overall shape change during this phase. Which description is correct?", "options": {"A": "Midline bones extend and paired bones externally rotate, widening the head and increasing anteroposterior diameter", "B": "Midline bones extend and paired bones internally rotate, narrowing the head and increasing anteroposterior diameter", "C": "The sphenoid flexes while the occiput extends, widening the head and decreasing anteroposterior diameter", "D": "Midline bones flex and paired bones internally rotate, narrowing the head and decreasing anteroposterior diameter", "E": "Midline bones flex and paired bones externally rotate, widening the head and decreasing anteroposterior diameter"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Midline bones flex and paired bones externally rotate, widening the head and decreasing anteroposterior diameter): In the flexion phase the midline bones, sphenoid and occiput, flex at the sphenobasilar synchondrosis while paired bones externally rotate, so the head widens and its anteroposterior diameter decreases. Extension reverses this pattern with internal rotation of paired bones and a narrower, longer head. The sphenoid and occiput move in the same phase together."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 20-year-old college athlete has an ECG at a pre-participation physical. The tracing shows a normal sinus rhythm with a PR interval of 0.16 seconds. During this interval the impulse is delayed before ventricular activation begins. Which structure contributes the greatest share of this conduction delay?", "options": {"A": "Sinoatrial node, because impulse exit from pacemaker cells to atrial muscle requires prolonged transit", "B": "Purkinje network, because it spreads the impulse through the subendocardium at the slowest velocity", "C": "Atrioventricular node, whose slow calcium-dependent depolarization provides the pause between atrial and ventricular systole", "D": "Bundle of His, because its narrow fibers conduct slowly before reaching the bundle branches", "E": "Internodal atrial pathways, because atrial myocardium conducts more slowly than any other cardiac tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Atrioventricular node, whose slow calcium-dependent depolarization provides the pause between atrial and ventricular systole): AV nodal cells are small, have few gap junctions and depend on slow calcium channel upstrokes, so the AV node produces most of the PR interval and allows atrial emptying before ventricular systole. Purkinje fibers conduct the fastest in the heart, so they cannot account for the delay."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 34-year-old hiker ascending to 4,500 meters develops headache, nausea and poor sleep with periodic breathing. A drug is started that causes bicarbonate loss in the urine and produces a mild metabolic acidosis, thereby stimulating ventilation. Which mechanism of action best describes this drug?", "options": {"A": "Blockade of the thick ascending limb cotransporter, causing chloride loss, volume contraction and bicarbonate gain", "B": "Inhibition of the sodium chloride cotransporter in the distal tubule, with calcium retention and metabolic alkalosis", "C": "Antagonism of aldosterone at mineralocorticoid receptors in the collecting duct, reducing hydrogen secretion and causing hyperkalemia", "D": "Osmotic retention of water in proximal tubule and descending limb, increasing urine volume and bicarbonate delivery", "E": "Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in proximal tubule cells, which reduces hydrogen secretion and bicarbonate reabsorption"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in proximal tubule cells, which reduces hydrogen secretion and bicarbonate reabsorption): Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in proximal tubule cells, so less hydrogen is secreted and less bicarbonate is reabsorbed. The resulting mild metabolic acidosis drives ventilation and offsets the respiratory alkalosis of altitude. Loop and thiazide diuretics instead tend to cause contraction alkalosis."}, {"id": 955, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 63-year-old man with chronic kidney disease taking hydrochlorothiazide develops sudden severe pain, warmth and swelling of the right knee overnight. Temperature is 37.9 C. Synovial fluid shows 28,000 leukocytes per microliter, 85 percent neutrophils, a negative Gram stain and needle-shaped crystals that appear yellow when parallel to the compensator axis. Which diagnosis is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Osteoarthritis flare with secondary synovitis, causing a noninflammatory effusion with fewer than 2,000 leukocytes per microliter", "B": "Reactive arthritis after enteric infection, an asymmetric oligoarthritis with an inflammatory sterile effusion and sometimes conjunctivitis", "C": "Gout from monosodium urate deposition, promoted by diuretic therapy and impaired renal urate excretion", "D": "Pseudogout from calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition, favored by hemochromatosis and hyperparathyroidism in older adults", "E": "Septic arthritis from Staphylococcus aureus, requiring urgent drainage because purulent synovial fluid destroys cartilage quickly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gout from monosodium urate deposition, promoted by diuretic therapy and impaired renal urate excretion): Needle-shaped crystals that are yellow when parallel to the compensator axis are negatively birefringent, which identifies monosodium urate and therefore gout. Thiazide diuretics and kidney disease raise urate. Calcium pyrophosphate crystals are rhomboid and positively birefringent, and an osteoarthritic effusion would have a far lower cell count."}, {"id": 956, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 4-month-old girl is brought to an osteopathic clinic because her head persistently tilts to the left. The pediatrician found tight left sternocleidomastoid tissue and mild flattening of the right occiput. She is feeding well and has normal development. Which technique is the most appropriate first choice for her neck?", "options": {"A": "Articulatory technique using repetitive rotational springing of the cervical spine", "B": "Counterstrain, identifying tender points by the patient's graded verbal tenderness reports", "C": "Gentle myofascial release of the left sternocleidomastoid and surrounding fascia", "D": "Muscle energy with the infant pushing against the physician's hand resistance", "E": "High velocity low amplitude thrust to the upper cervical segments"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gentle myofascial release of the left sternocleidomastoid and surrounding fascia): An infant cannot contract on command or report tenderness, which excludes muscle energy and counterstrain, and a thrust or springing is unsuitable for her neck. Gentle myofascial release of the tight sternocleidomastoid fits the findings and the age. This is a typical approach to congenital muscular torticollis."}, {"id": 957, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 35-year-old man has fatigue and mild jaundice. His hematocrit is 22 percent and his reticulocyte count is 10 percent. The reticulocyte production index is calculated using a maturation correction of 2.0 and a normal hematocrit of 45 percent. Which process best explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Impaired hemoglobin synthesis from iron deficiency with a blunted reticulocyte response and low production index", "B": "Peripheral red cell destruction with an appropriate marrow response, as in warm autoimmune hemolysis", "C": "Decreased erythropoietin from advanced chronic kidney disease with a low production index", "D": "Ineffective erythropoiesis from vitamin B12 deficiency with intramedullary destruction of precursors", "E": "Impaired hemoglobin synthesis from iron deficiency with a blunted reticulocyte response"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Peripheral red cell destruction with an appropriate marrow response, as in warm autoimmune hemolysis): The corrected reticulocyte count is 10 times 22 over 45, about 4.9 percent, and dividing by the maturation factor of 2.0 gives an index of roughly 2.4. An index above 2 means the marrow is responding appropriately, so the anemia reflects red cell loss or destruction. Hypoproliferative causes give an index below 2."}, {"id": 958, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 66-year-old retired carpenter has morning stiffness lasting 15 minutes, bony enlargements at the distal interphalangeal joints, and knee pain that worsens late in the day. Radiographs show asymmetric joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis and marginal osteophytes. Which process is the primary driver of cartilage loss in this disease?", "options": {"A": "Chondrocyte release of metalloproteinases and aggrecanases that degrade proteoglycan and type II collagen", "B": "Autoantibody mediated synovial pannus formation that erodes cartilage and marginal bone surfaces", "C": "Repeated bacterial seeding of the joint producing chronic suppurative destruction of cartilage", "D": "Neutrophil phagocytosis of monosodium urate crystals releasing lysosomal enzymes into joint fluid", "E": "Ischemic death of subchondral bone segments producing collapse of the overlying articular surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chondrocyte release of metalloproteinases and aggrecanases that degrade proteoglycan and type II collagen): Osteoarthritis is driven by chondrocytes that, under mechanical and cytokine stress, release metalloproteinases and aggrecanases that break down proteoglycan and type II collagen. The bony changes are reactive. Pannus and autoantibodies characterize rheumatoid arthritis, which spares the distal interphalangeal joints and causes prolonged morning stiffness."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 38-year-old woman reports progressive difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids over a year, with regurgitation of undigested food at night and a 5-kg weight loss. Barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus tapering to a narrow distal segment. Manometry shows absent peristalsis and incomplete lower esophageal sphincter relaxation. Which pathologic change underlies this disorder?", "options": {"A": "Infiltrating distal adenocarcinoma producing a rigid stricture that mimics a motility disorder", "B": "Antibodies blocking acetylcholine receptors on striated muscle fibers of the upper esophagus", "C": "Smooth muscle atrophy with submucosal collagen deposition producing a patulous, hypotensive lower esophageal sphincter", "D": "Excess excitatory cholinergic output from vagal neurons causing uncoordinated simultaneous contractions", "E": "Loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide): Achalasia results from degeneration of inhibitory myenteric neurons, so the sphincter cannot relax and peristalsis is lost. Scleroderma produces a weak, patulous sphincter rather than a tight one, and a tumor causing pseudoachalasia typically presents in older patients with a shorter, more rapid course."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 2-year-old boy has several episodes of painless, large-volume rectal bleeding with brick-red stool. He is pale but hemodynamically stable, with a soft nontender abdomen. Colonoscopy was nondiagnostic. A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. What explains the finding on this scan?", "options": {"A": "Uptake by inflamed mucosa in a duplication cyst lined by ileal epithelium", "B": "Uptake by ectopic pancreatic tissue in a rest within the ileal wall", "C": "Uptake by hyperplastic lymphoid follicles in the terminal ileum after a viral illness", "D": "Uptake by ectopic gastric mucosa within a persistent remnant of the vitelline duct", "E": "Uptake by neoplastic cells in a juvenile polyp of the rectosigmoid colon"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Uptake by ectopic gastric mucosa within a persistent remnant of the vitelline duct): Meckel diverticulum is a vitelline duct remnant, and acid from heterotopic gastric mucosa ulcerates adjacent ileum, giving painless bleeding. Pertechnetate concentrates in gastric mucosal cells, which makes the scan useful. Pancreatic rests and lymphoid tissue do not take up the tracer."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 29-year-old violinist has right arm tingling and neck pain. Palpation shows the right first rib elevated, tender, and resistant to exhalation, with a taut muscle in the anterior neck. The radial pulse weakens when she inhales deeply and turns her head to the right. Which diagnosis best explains her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Pectoralis minor syndrome with subcoracoid compression of the axillary vessels during overhead shoulder abduction", "B": "First rib inhalation dysfunction with scalene hypertonicity compressing the neurovascular bundle in the interscalene triangle", "C": "Carpal tunnel syndrome with median nerve entrapment producing thenar weakness and nocturnal hand paresthesia", "D": "C6 radiculopathy from foraminal narrowing producing dermatomal paresthesia and a normal radial pulse on provocation", "E": "Costoclavicular compression from a depressed first rib narrowing the space between clavicle and rib"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (First rib inhalation dysfunction with scalene hypertonicity compressing the neurovascular bundle in the interscalene triangle): An elevated rib 1 resistant to exhalation with a tight anterior neck muscle and a pulse that weakens on deep inhalation and head turning points to scalene compression within the interscalene triangle. A depressed rib would be needed for the costoclavicular explanation, and radiculopathy or carpal tunnel would not change the pulse."}, {"id": 962, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 44-year-old woman has a thick, tender, adherent horizontal scar from a cesarean delivery eight months ago. Skin glide over the lower abdomen is restricted in one direction, and she describes pulling sensations with forward bending. Which osteopathic technique is most appropriate to improve tissue mobility at the scar?", "options": {"A": "High velocity low amplitude thrust applied across the scar to break adhesions at the barrier", "B": "Thoracic lymphatic pump with rhythmic compression to enhance fluid drainage from abdominal tissues", "C": "Muscle energy using patient isometric contraction of the rectus abdominis against physician counterforce", "D": "Counterstrain positioning of the scar into ease for about ninety seconds without stretching tissue", "E": "Direct myofascial release engaging the restrictive barrier with sustained gentle load until the tissue softens"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Direct myofascial release engaging the restrictive barrier with sustained gentle load until the tissue softens): Fascial scarring with directional restriction responds to myofascial release, in which the physician takes up slack and holds a sustained load against the restrictive barrier until the tissue lengthens. Thrust and isometric techniques address joint restrictions rather than adherent soft tissue."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 12-year-old girl has six weeks of right thigh pain and swelling with fever of 38.2 C and a raised ESR. Radiograph of the femoral diaphysis shows a permeative lytic lesion with lamellated periosteal reaction and a soft tissue mass. Biopsy shows sheets of small round blue cells that are CD99 positive and PAS positive. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Primary lymphoma of bone, composed of atypical lymphoid cells that stain for leukocyte common antigen", "B": "Osteosarcoma, a malignant osteoid-producing tumor of the metaphysis with sunburst periosteal reaction and Codman triangle", "C": "Ewing sarcoma, a tumor of primitive neuroectodermal origin carrying the characteristic EWS gene translocation", "D": "Subacute osteomyelitis, a bacterial infection of marrow with fever, periosteal new bone and raised inflammatory markers", "E": "Langerhans cell histiocytosis, a lytic bone lesion of clonal dendritic cells with fever and periosteal reaction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ewing sarcoma, a tumor of primitive neuroectodermal origin carrying the characteristic EWS gene translocation): A diaphyseal permeative lesion with onion-skin periosteal reaction, systemic features and CD99-positive, PAS-positive small round blue cells is Ewing sarcoma. Osteosarcoma arises in the metaphysis and makes osteoid. Langerhans cell histiocytosis would stain for CD1a and show Langerhans cells with grooved nuclei, and osteomyelitis would show inflammatory cells rather than a monotonous tumor."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "An 11-year-old boy has had 3 weeks of polyuria, polydipsia, and a 5 kg weight loss despite increased appetite. His body mass index is at the 30th percentile. Serum glucose is 420 mg/dL, and urine ketones are positive. His maternal aunt has celiac disease. Which mechanism most likely underlies his disease?", "options": {"A": "T cell mediated autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, associated with HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 haplotypes", "B": "Peripheral insulin resistance from adipose derived free fatty acids, with progressive beta cell exhaustion over years", "C": "Amyloid deposition within pancreatic islets from islet amyloid polypeptide, gradually replacing beta cells across decades", "D": "Autoantibodies against the insulin receptor producing severe insulin resistance with acanthosis nigricans and ovarian hyperandrogenism", "E": "Pancreatic exocrine destruction by recurrent alcohol induced pancreatitis causing calcification and secondary insulin deficiency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (T cell mediated autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, associated with HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 haplotypes): Abrupt onset, weight loss, ketosis, normal body weight, and a family history of another autoimmune disease indicate type 1 diabetes. Autoreactive T cells destroy beta cells, with a strong HLA-DR3 and DR4 association. Insulin resistance and islet amyloid belong to type 2 disease."}, {"id": 965, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 62-year-old man taking daily ibuprofen for arthritis develops sudden, severe epigastric pain that spreads across the abdomen over one hour. He lies motionless, with a rigid abdomen and diffuse rebound tenderness. Heart rate is 112/min and blood pressure is 98/62 mm Hg. An upright chest radiograph shows free subdiaphragmatic air. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Urgent upper endoscopy to locate the ulcer and place a hemostatic clip through the defect", "B": "Barium swallow study to confirm the site of leakage before deciding on therapy", "C": "Abdominal ultrasound to look for gallstones, followed by laparoscopic cholecystectomy if stones are found", "D": "Fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics, acid suppression, and emergency surgical consultation for operative repair", "E": "Nasogastric decompression, intravenous antibiotics, and observation with serial examinations over 48 hours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics, acid suppression, and emergency surgical consultation for operative repair): Free subdiaphragmatic air with peritonitis and hemodynamic instability indicates a perforated viscus, most often a peptic ulcer in an NSAID user, and needs resuscitation, antibiotics, and prompt operative repair. Endoscopy insufflates air into an already open peritoneal cavity and delays source control. Nonoperative management is reserved for carefully selected stable patients without diffuse peritonitis."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 54-year-old strict vegan reports numbness in both feet and difficulty walking in the dark. Examination shows reduced vibration sense and a positive Romberg sign. The blood count shows macrocytic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils. Which pair of serum metabolites would be elevated in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Both metabolites reduced because dietary intake of precursor amino acids is low", "B": "Formiminoglutamic acid and orotic acid, reflecting disrupted histidine and pyrimidine metabolism", "C": "Methylmalonic acid elevated with a normal homocysteine, reflecting impaired odd chain fatty acid breakdown", "D": "Homocysteine elevated with a normal methylmalonic acid, as seen in isolated folate deficiency", "E": "Both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid, because cobalamin serves two separate enzymatic reactions"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid, because cobalamin serves two separate enzymatic reactions): Vitamin B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase, which converts homocysteine to methionine, and for methylmalonyl CoA mutase. Deficiency therefore raises both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid, and the latter explains the neuropathy. Folate deficiency raises homocysteine alone."}, {"id": 967, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 58-year-old man has a 9-mm pigmented lesion on his upper back that his wife says has enlarged and darkened over six months, as in the photograph shown. It has irregular borders, multiple shades of brown and black, and occasional bleeding. Which next step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Wide local excision with two-centimeter margins before any tissue diagnosis has been established", "B": "Dermoscopic photography with repeat examination in three months to document interval change in size", "C": "Punch biopsy of only the darkest nodular area with observation of the remainder", "D": "Full-thickness excisional biopsy with narrow margins to preserve accurate measurement of tumor depth", "E": "Shallow shave biopsy of the lesion to obtain tissue quickly with minimal scarring"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Full-thickness excisional biopsy with narrow margins to preserve accurate measurement of tumor depth): A changing, asymmetric, multicolored lesion with bleeding is suspicious for melanoma and needs tissue diagnosis. A narrow-margin full-thickness excisional biopsy keeps the whole lesion intact so Breslow thickness can be measured accurately, which guides later wide excision. Shave biopsy risks transecting the deep margin, and observation delays diagnosis.", "image": "question_images/q_967.png"}, {"id": 968, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 63-year-old woman, three days after total hip arthroplasty, has new left calf swelling, warmth, and tenderness with pitting edema to the knee. She is on prophylactic enoxaparin. The osteopathic physician considers treating edema with a lymphatic technique. Which technique is most clearly contraindicated at this point?", "options": {"A": "Counterstrain to the tender thoracic points, because positioning of ease relaxes the paraspinal muscles", "B": "Thoracic inlet myofascial release with light traction, because it opens the terminal lymphatic outflow", "C": "Suboccipital inhibition with gentle sustained pressure, because it reduces cervical tension and sympathetic tone", "D": "Respiratory diaphragm doming with gentle exhalation resistance, because it moves fluid up through the chest", "E": "Pedal pump with rhythmic ankle dorsiflexion, because calf compression could embolize a deep vein clot"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Pedal pump with rhythmic ankle dorsiflexion, because calf compression could embolize a deep vein clot): Unilateral calf swelling, warmth, and tenderness after hip surgery suggests a deep vein thrombosis, and rhythmic ankle and calf motion could dislodge the clot and cause pulmonary embolism. The diaphragm, inlet, cervical, and counterstrain techniques do not load the leg veins, so they remain acceptable."}, {"id": 969, "domain": "community_health", "question": "In a case-control study of 100 patients with lung cancer and 200 controls, investigators calculate that cases were four times more likely to have smoked. A student asks which measure of association can be calculated directly from this study design without additional information.", "options": {"A": "Number needed to treat, because it gives the persons treated to prevent one additional outcome", "B": "Relative risk, because it compares the probability of disease between exposed and unexposed groups", "C": "Absolute risk reduction, because it gives the difference in event rates between treated and untreated", "D": "Attributable risk, because it subtracts the disease rate in unexposed persons from that in exposed", "E": "Odds ratio, because sampling by outcome status prevents direct calculation of disease incidence among exposed"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Odds ratio, because sampling by outcome status prevents direct calculation of disease incidence among exposed): In a case-control study, investigators fix the numbers of cases and controls, so true incidence among exposed and unexposed persons is unknown and relative risk cannot be computed directly. The odds ratio can be calculated from the exposure odds and approximates relative risk for rare diseases. Attributable risk, absolute risk reduction and number needed to treat all require incidence."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 33-year-old woman, G3P2 with a prior low transverse cesarean delivery, is in labor augmented with oxytocin. She suddenly reports severe tearing abdominal pain. Fetal heart rate falls to 70/min, vaginal bleeding is moderate, and the fetal presenting part, previously at plus one station, can no longer be palpated on vaginal examination. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Placenta previa with bleeding from a low-lying placenta that covers the internal cervical os", "B": "Placental abruption with retroplacental hemorrhage causing a rigid, tender uterus and fetal distress", "C": "Uterine rupture at the old scar with the fetus escaping into the peritoneal cavity", "D": "Vasa previa with rupture of fetal vessels running across the membranes over the cervix", "E": "Amniotic fluid embolism with sudden cardiovascular collapse and consumptive coagulopathy during active labor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Uterine rupture at the old scar with the fetus escaping into the peritoneal cavity): A prior cesarean scar, oxytocin augmentation, sudden tearing pain, fetal bradycardia and loss of the presenting part indicate uterine rupture. Abruption gives a rigid uterus but does not make the fetus recede, and previa bleeding is painless. Emergency laparotomy is required."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 78-year-old frail woman weighing 45 kg has a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL, which is within the laboratory reference range. A pharmacist is concerned about dosing of a renally cleared antibiotic. Which explanation best accounts for her normal creatinine despite age-related reduction in renal function?", "options": {"A": "Reduced muscle mass lowers creatinine production, so the estimated glomerular filtration rate may be overestimated", "B": "Age-related increase in renal blood flow maintains filtration despite loss of functioning nephrons in the elderly", "C": "Increased tubular secretion of creatinine with age offsets the decline in filtration across glomerular capillaries", "D": "Decreased dietary protein intake lowers blood urea nitrogen but does not change creatinine production or clearance", "E": "Compensatory glomerular hypertrophy in remaining nephrons restores filtration rate to young-adult values in healthy aging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced muscle mass lowers creatinine production, so the estimated glomerular filtration rate may be overestimated): Glomerular filtration falls with age, but creatinine production also falls because muscle mass shrinks, so the serum value stays normal while true clearance is lower. Renal blood flow declines with age, and drug doses should be based on estimated clearance instead of creatinine alone."}, {"id": 972, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 41-year-old woman who smokes a pack daily wants to quit. She has a history of generalized tonic-clonic seizures from a prior head injury and takes no antiepileptic drug. She has failed nicotine patches alone. Which pharmacotherapy for smoking cessation should be avoided in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Nicotine polacrilex gum, a rapidly absorbed buccal formulation used to treat breakthrough withdrawal cravings", "B": "Nicotine nasal spray, the fastest-acting replacement product, causing local nasal irritation and watering eyes", "C": "Bupropion, an aminoketone antidepressant that increases cortical excitability and lowers the convulsive threshold", "D": "Nicotine lozenges, which deliver short-acting nicotine to relieve cravings between scheduled doses", "E": "Varenicline, a partial agonist at alpha-4 beta-2 nicotinic receptors that blunts nicotine reward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bupropion, an aminoketone antidepressant that increases cortical excitability and lowers the convulsive threshold): Bupropion is contraindicated in seizure disorders because it lowers the convulsive threshold in a dose-dependent way. Varenicline and every nicotine replacement form are acceptable choices, and a patch combined with a short-acting product is more effective than a patch alone. Varenicline works by partial agonism that reduces both craving and reward."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 3-week-old girl born at term in a US hospital has prolonged jaundice, poor feeding, constipation, a large tongue, a hoarse cry, and an umbilical hernia. The pregnancy was uncomplicated. Serum TSH is markedly elevated with low free T4. Radionuclide scan shows uptake only at the base of the tongue. Which is the most likely underlying cause?", "options": {"A": "Dyshormonogenesis from inherited peroxidase deficiency, producing a goiter with brisk uptake in the neck", "B": "Resistance to TSH from an inactivating receptor mutation, with a normally located but poorly responsive gland", "C": "Dietary iodine deficiency during pregnancy limiting substrate for hormone synthesis in the fetal gland", "D": "Maternal blocking antibodies crossing the placenta and transiently inhibiting the infant's TSH receptor", "E": "Ectopic gland from failed descent of the thyroid anlage along the thyroglossal duct"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ectopic gland from failed descent of the thyroid anlage along the thyroglossal duct): Uptake only at the base of the tongue shows thyroid tissue that did not descend, a form of thyroid dysgenesis and the most common cause of congenital hypothyroidism in the United States. The ectopic tissue cannot make adequate hormone, so TSH rises. Dyshormonogenesis would show an enlarged gland in the normal location."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 19-year-old college basketball player is 205 cm tall with arm span exceeding height, pectus excavatum and arachnodactyly. Slit-lamp examination shows superotemporal lens displacement. He has a family history of sudden death. Which cardiac lesion is most likely to threaten him, and what mechanism explains it?", "options": {"A": "Coarctation of the aorta with rib notching from abnormal neural crest migration", "B": "Infective endocarditis of the tricuspid valve from defective collagen type III synthesis and vascular fragility", "C": "Aortic root dilation and dissection from excess TGF-beta signaling in a fibrillin-1 defect", "D": "Mitral valve prolapse with severe regurgitation from myxomatous leaflet degeneration", "E": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with outflow obstruction from a sarcomere protein mutation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Aortic root dilation and dissection from excess TGF-beta signaling in a fibrillin-1 defect): Tall stature, long fingers, pectus excavatum and superotemporal lens displacement indicate Marfan syndrome, from fibrillin-1 loss and excess TGF-beta signaling that weakens the aortic media. Aortic dissection causes the early sudden death. Mitral prolapse also occurs but is not the usual cause of death in young patients. Coarctation and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy have different causes."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 69-year-old man taking daily ibuprofen for osteoarthritis presents with epigastric pain and a gastric ulcer on endoscopy. He must continue the NSAID for now. The physician wants an additional drug that restores the mucosal protective mediator that the NSAID depletes. Which drug fits this goal?", "options": {"A": "Aluminum hydroxide, which neutralizes luminal acid and raises intragastric pH briefly", "B": "Sucralfate, an aluminum salt that binds ulcer crater proteins and forms a physical barrier", "C": "Bismuth subsalicylate, which coats the ulcer base and exerts direct antibacterial activity", "D": "Misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 analog that increases mucus and bicarbonate secretion", "E": "Ranitidine, a competitive histamine H2 blocker that lowers basal and nocturnal acid secretion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 analog that increases mucus and bicarbonate secretion): NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower mucosal prostaglandin E2, which normally stimulates mucus and bicarbonate secretion and maintains blood flow. Misoprostol supplies a prostaglandin E1 analog, whereas sucralfate and antacids protect or neutralize but do not replace the lost mediator. Misoprostol is contraindicated in pregnancy."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has episodic vertigo lasting under one minute, triggered by rolling over in bed or looking up. No hearing loss, tinnitus, or aural fullness. Neurologic exam is normal. Dix-Hallpike reproduces vertigo with torsional upbeating nystagmus after a short delay that fatigues. Which is the most likely mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve causing sustained unilateral loss of peripheral vestibular input", "B": "Vertebrobasilar ischemia reducing perfusion of the brainstem and cerebellum during neck extension", "C": "Calcium carbonate debris displaced into a semicircular canal, producing abnormal endolymph movement with head position", "D": "Schwannoma of the vestibular nerve compressing it within the internal auditory canal", "E": "Endolymphatic hydrops from excess fluid accumulation, distending the membranous labyrinth of the cochlea and vestibule"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Calcium carbonate debris displaced into a semicircular canal, producing abnormal endolymph movement with head position): Brief positional vertigo with a latent, fatigable torsional nystagmus on Dix-Hallpike is benign paroxysmal positional vertigo from otoconia in a semicircular canal. Meniere disease causes episodes of hours with hearing loss and fullness, and central causes give nonfatigable nystagmus and other neurologic signs."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 66-year-old man with diabetes wakes with trouble reading. He can write a full sentence correctly and speak normally but cannot read what he has just written. He names objects normally, and strength is intact. Visual field testing shows loss of the right half of vision in both eyes. Which pair of structures was infarcted?", "options": {"A": "Left angular gyrus and the adjacent inferior parietal lobule cortex", "B": "Right occipital cortex and the anterior commissure of the temporal lobes", "C": "Left posterior superior temporal gyrus and the underlying arcuate fasciculus fibers", "D": "Left inferior frontal gyrus and the underlying anterior limb of internal capsule", "E": "Left occipital cortex and the splenium of the posterior corpus callosum"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Left occipital cortex and the splenium of the posterior corpus callosum): A left posterior cerebral artery infarct destroys the left visual cortex, causing a right homonymous hemianopia, and the splenium, which carries visual information from the right occipital cortex. Visual input then reaches only the right hemisphere and cannot cross to the left language areas, so reading fails while writing and speech remain intact. Angular gyrus lesions would impair writing as well."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 35-year-old man presents with a tender 4-cm fluctuant erythematous nodule on the thigh that developed over three days. He is afebrile with normal vital signs and no systemic illness. Examination shows a fluctuant center with a 2-cm rim of surrounding erythema. What is the most appropriate initial management?", "options": {"A": "Oral cephalexin for ten days, which covers the usual streptococcal and methicillin-sensitive staphylococcal organisms", "B": "Warm compresses with observation, because most small skin abscesses resolve through spontaneous rupture and drainage", "C": "Topical mupirocin applied three times daily, which eradicates staphylococci from the surface and underlying collection", "D": "Hospital admission for intravenous vancomycin, which provides reliable coverage of methicillin-resistant staphylococcal infection", "E": "Incision and drainage of the collection, with culture of the pus when feasible"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Incision and drainage of the collection, with culture of the pus when feasible): A fluctuant abscess is treated primarily by incision and drainage, which achieves cure in most immunocompetent patients with mild disease. Antibiotics alone do not penetrate a pus-filled cavity, and admission for vancomycin is reserved for systemic toxicity or failure of drainage."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "musculoskeletal", "question": "A 66-year-old woman who smokes and has a mother with a hip fracture undergoes dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry. The lumbar spine T-score is negative 2.7 and the femoral neck T-score is negative 2.1. She has never had a fracture. Laboratory evaluation for secondary causes is unrevealing. Which statement correctly interprets her bone density result?", "options": {"A": "Osteoporosis, because the lowest site is at least 2.5 standard deviations under young adult mean", "B": "Osteopenia, because only the femoral neck value is used for classification and it exceeds negative 2.5", "C": "Osteomalacia, because a low T-score reflects defective mineralization that requires vitamin D and bone biopsy", "D": "Osteopenia, because T-scores between negative 1 and negative 2.5 define low bone mass without osteoporosis", "E": "Normal for age, because the Z-score rather than the T-score is used to diagnose postmenopausal women"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Osteoporosis, because the lowest site is at least 2.5 standard deviations under young adult mean): In postmenopausal women the World Health Organization classification uses the T-score at the lowest of the spine, total hip and femoral neck, and a value of negative 2.5 or lower defines osteoporosis. Her spine value meets that threshold even though the femoral neck falls in the osteopenic range. Z-scores are reserved for younger patients and children."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 71-year-old man has exertional syncope and angina. He has hypertension and takes a statin. His carotid upstroke is slow and delayed. Simultaneous left ventricular and aortic pressure tracings obtained during cardiac catheterization are shown. Which auscultatory finding is most likely on his examination?", "options": {"A": "Holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the left axilla with a soft first sound", "B": "Early diastolic decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border with a widened pulse pressure", "C": "Crescendo decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids", "D": "Mid diastolic rumble at the apex following a low pitched opening snap after the second sound", "E": "Midsystolic click followed by a late systolic murmur at the apex that moves earlier on standing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Crescendo decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids): The tracings show left ventricular systolic pressure far above aortic pressure, a gradient across the aortic valve. Aortic stenosis produces a crescendo decrescendo murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids, with a late peak as obstruction worsens. The other murmurs arise from regurgitant, prolapsing, or diastolic mitral lesions.", "image": "question_images/q_980.png"}, {"id": 981, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 45-year-old man on sertraline for depression is given linezolid for a skin infection. Within hours he has agitation, diaphoresis, diarrhea and temperature of 39.2 degrees Celsius. Examination shows inducible clonus, hyperreflexia and mydriasis. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Which diagnosis and treatment are most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Thyroid storm, treated by propylthiouracil, beta blockade and glucocorticoids to block hormone synthesis and conversion", "B": "Anticholinergic toxicity, treated by supportive care and physostigmine to reverse the peripheral and central effects", "C": "Malignant hyperthermia, treated by stopping the triggering agent and giving dantrolene with active cooling", "D": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, treated by stopping antipsychotics and giving dantrolene or bromocriptine", "E": "Serotonin syndrome, treated by stopping serotonergic drugs and giving benzodiazepines, with cyproheptadine if needed"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Serotonin syndrome, treated by stopping serotonergic drugs and giving benzodiazepines, with cyproheptadine if needed): Linezolid inhibits monoamine oxidase, and with an SSRI it produces excess serotonin signaling: neuromuscular hyperactivity with clonus, autonomic instability and diarrhea. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome has bradyreflexia, rigidity and slow onset, and anticholinergic toxicity causes dry skin with decreased bowel sounds. Treatment is withdrawal of the agents, benzodiazepines and cyproheptadine."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 48-year-old woman has intermittent facial flushing, watery diarrhea, and wheezing for months. She now develops a scaly, pigmented rash on sun-exposed skin of her neck and forearms, along with irritability and poor concentration. Her diet is adequate and she takes no medications. Which mechanism most likely accounts for the rash?", "options": {"A": "Defective neutral amino acid transport in renal and intestinal epithelium causing excessive tryptophan loss in urine", "B": "Tumor diversion of tryptophan toward serotonin synthesis, depleting substrate available for niacin production", "C": "Excess vitamin A intake causing exfoliative dermatitis, headaches, and hepatic injury over several months", "D": "Malabsorption of fat soluble retinoids from bile salt loss, causing follicular hyperkeratosis of skin", "E": "Impaired intestinal zinc absorption causing perioral and acral dermatitis from reduced metalloenzyme activity in skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tumor diversion of tryptophan toward serotonin synthesis, depleting substrate available for niacin production): Flushing, diarrhea, and wheezing suggest carcinoid syndrome, in which a tumor consumes tryptophan to make serotonin and leaves too little for niacin. The result is pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Hartnup disease also causes pellagra-like findings but starts in childhood without flushing."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 26-year-old woman has episodes of severe colicky abdominal pain, constipation, and anxiety after starting a sulfonamide antibiotic during a low-calorie diet. Her urine darkens to port-wine color on standing in light. Examination shows tachycardia but no skin blistering or photosensitivity. Which enzyme deficiency most likely underlies this disorder?", "options": {"A": "Porphobilinogen deaminase, causing accumulation of porphobilinogen and aminolevulinic acid in neurovisceral attacks", "B": "ALA dehydratase, causing accumulation of aminolevulinic acid with severe neurovisceral symptoms in childhood", "C": "Coproporphyrinogen oxidase, causing accumulation of coproporphyrinogen with both neurovisceral attacks and photosensitivity", "D": "Ferrochelatase, causing accumulation of protoporphyrin in red cells with painful phototoxic skin reactions", "E": "Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, causing accumulation of uroporphyrins and blistering lesions on sun exposed skin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Porphobilinogen deaminase, causing accumulation of porphobilinogen and aminolevulinic acid in neurovisceral attacks): Acute attacks of abdominal pain and neuropsychiatric symptoms triggered by fasting and drugs, with urine that darkens on standing and no photosensitivity, indicate acute intermittent porphyria from porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency. The trigger induces ALA synthase and the precursors accumulate. Hereditary coproporphyria is similar but typically adds skin photosensitivity."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 30-year-old man with known epilepsy is found by his roommate having generalized tonic-clonic activity that has now lasted six minutes. Paramedics have placed an intravenous line, fingerstick glucose is 96 mg/dL, and oxygen is running. He recently moved and missed several doses of his medication. Which drug should be given first?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous propofol infusion with intubation as the opening therapy for prolonged convulsions", "B": "Intravenous lorazepam to enhance GABA-A mediated chloride conductance for immediate seizure termination", "C": "Intravenous phenobarbital loading dose as the first agent ahead of benzodiazepine attempts", "D": "Intravenous phenytoin loading dose to stabilize inactivated sodium channels before any sedative is given", "E": "Intravenous levetiracetam bolus as the preferred initial agent for ongoing generalized convulsions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous lorazepam to enhance GABA-A mediated chloride conductance for immediate seizure termination): A seizure lasting beyond five minutes is status epilepticus, and the first-line treatment is a parenteral benzodiazepine such as lorazepam. Fosphenytoin, levetiracetam, valproate, or phenobarbital serve as second-line agents if seizures continue. Glucose is already normal, so correction of hypoglycemia is not the issue."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 76-year-old man has had three episodes of painless maroon stools over a year and now has iron deficiency anemia. He takes no NSAIDs. Examination reveals a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur radiating to the carotids. Colonoscopy shows several colonic diverticula and no mass. Which is the most likely source of his bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Right colon angiodysplasia with acquired loss of large von Willebrand multimers from valve shear stress", "B": "Occult sigmoid adenocarcinoma with chronic blood loss causing intermittent maroon stools and anemia", "C": "Diverticular hemorrhage from a vasa recta vessel at the neck of a colonic diverticulum", "D": "Ischemic colitis in the splenic flexure watershed region after episodes of low cardiac output", "E": "Internal hemorrhoids with prolapse and mucosal erosion causing painless bleeding over many months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Right colon angiodysplasia with acquired loss of large von Willebrand multimers from valve shear stress): Aortic stenosis can produce an acquired von Willebrand syndrome because high shear destroys large multimers, predisposing to bleeding from right-sided colonic angiodysplasias (Heyde syndrome). Diverticula are common and can bleed, but they typically cause abrupt, large-volume bleeding rather than recurrent chronic loss. Carcinoma is not seen and hemorrhoids bleed bright red blood."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "nervous_mental_health", "question": "A 67-year-old retired teacher has a slowly progressive pill-rolling tremor of the right hand at rest, shuffling gait, and cogwheel rigidity. His handwriting has become small and cramped over two years, and his sense of smell has declined. Which pathologic finding is expected in his brain at autopsy?", "options": {"A": "Loss of serotonergic neurons in the dorsal raphe nuclei with cytoplasmic tau inclusions", "B": "Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta with alpha-synuclein inclusions", "C": "Loss of GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the caudate with intranuclear huntingtin aggregates", "D": "Infarction of the subthalamic nucleus with loss of excitatory glutamatergic neurons", "E": "Loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert with neurofibrillary tangles"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta with alpha-synuclein inclusions): Parkinson disease is characterized by degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, with Lewy bodies composed of alpha-synuclein. Reduced dopamine to the striatum shifts basal ganglia output toward inhibition of movement, causing bradykinesia and rigidity. Loss of cholinergic basal forebrain neurons marks Alzheimer disease."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 62-year-old man visits a rural clinic for a routine physical. He smoked one pack daily from age 22 to age 50, then quit. He has well-controlled hypertension and reports no cough, weight loss or hemoptysis. His father died of colon cancer. Which screening test addresses his smoking history according to current USPSTF recommendations?", "options": {"A": "Colonoscopy at once, because his father's colon cancer history outweighs his own smoking exposure", "B": "Annual low-dose chest CT, since age, pack-year history and recent quit date meet criteria", "C": "Chest radiograph every year, because plain films reduce lung cancer mortality in former smokers", "D": "Sputum cytology every year, because it detects central squamous tumors before symptoms appear in smokers", "E": "No lung cancer screening, because he has no symptoms and quit over ten years ago"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Annual low-dose chest CT, since age, pack-year history and recent quit date meet criteria): He is between 50 and 80 years old with 28 pack-years and quit 12 years ago, which falls within the fifteen-year window, so he meets criteria for annual low-dose CT. Radiography and sputum cytology are not recommended, and eligibility does not depend on symptoms. His family history is relevant to colorectal screening but does not answer this question."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "gastrointestinal_nutrition", "question": "A 24-year-old woman has six months of nonbloody diarrhea, right lower quadrant pain, and a 6-kg weight loss. She has aphthous ulcers and a perianal fistula. CT enterography shows a thickened terminal ileum with skip areas. She smokes cigarettes daily. Which histologic finding is most likely on biopsy?", "options": {"A": "Caseating granulomas with Langhans giant cells in the ileocecal region", "B": "Dense neutrophilic infiltrate with pseudomembranes of fibrin and necrotic debris on the mucosa", "C": "Transmural lymphoid inflammation with noncaseating granulomas distributed in a discontinuous segmental pattern", "D": "Subepithelial collagen band thickening with lymphocytic infiltration of the surface epithelium", "E": "Continuous mucosal inflammation with crypt abscesses and loss of goblet cell mucin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transmural lymphoid inflammation with noncaseating granulomas distributed in a discontinuous segmental pattern): Skip lesions, perianal fistula, aphthous ulcers, smoking, and a thickened terminal ileum indicate Crohn disease, which shows transmural inflammation with noncaseating granulomas. Ulcerative colitis is continuous and mucosal, and ileocecal tuberculosis would show caseating granulomas."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 28-year-old man from a shelter has three months of cough, night sweats and weight loss. Chest imaging shows an upper lobe cavity. Biopsy of a hilar lymph node reveals granulomas with epithelioid macrophages and Langhans giant cells surrounding amorphous, pink, granular debris without any preserved tissue architecture. What pattern of necrosis is present in the center?", "options": {"A": "Caseous necrosis, a blend of coagulative and liquefactive change seen with tuberculous and fungal infections", "B": "Fat necrosis, with lipase release saponifying adipose tissue into chalky white calcium soaps in pancreatitis", "C": "Liquefactive necrosis, with enzymatic digestion of tissue into a viscous liquid mass typical of abscesses", "D": "Fibrinoid necrosis, with immune complexes and fibrin deposited in vessel walls giving a bright pink appearance", "E": "Coagulative necrosis, with preserved cellular outlines for days from denaturation of structural proteins and enzymes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Caseous necrosis, a blend of coagulative and liquefactive change seen with tuberculous and fungal infections): Tuberculous granulomas have a necrotic center of amorphous granular pink material with no preserved architecture, called caseous necrosis, a blend of coagulative and liquefactive features. Coagulative necrosis keeps tissue outlines for some days, while liquefactive necrosis yields a liquid abscess cavity. Fibrinoid and fat necrosis occur in vessel walls and around pancreatic inflammation."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease who had an ileal resection presents with severe left flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a 6 mm ureteral stone. Urine pH is 5.8 and urine shows octahedral crystals. Her serum calcium is normal. Which mechanism best explains stone formation?", "options": {"A": "Defective renal tubular transport of cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine causing hexagonal cystine crystals", "B": "Urease producing bacteria split urea, raising urine pH and precipitating magnesium ammonium phosphate crystals", "C": "Increased purine turnover with acidic urine promoting uric acid precipitation and radiolucent stones", "D": "Fat malabsorption binds luminal calcium, leaving free oxalate to be absorbed and excreted in urine", "E": "Hypercalciuria from absorptive intestinal calcium hyperabsorption causing calcium phosphate precipitation in alkaline urine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fat malabsorption binds luminal calcium, leaving free oxalate to be absorbed and excreted in urine): After ileal resection, unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium in the gut, so oxalate stays free, is absorbed in the colon, and raises urinary oxalate. This produces calcium oxalate stones, which appear as octahedral or envelope shaped crystals. Struvite and cystine stones form under different urine conditions."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "genitourinary_renal", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes develops gastroenteritis with vomiting and poor oral intake for 3 days. Her estimated glomerular filtration rate falls from 62 to 24 mL/min/1.73 m2 and she continues taking her usual medications, including metformin. The physician advises holding metformin. Which consequence of continued use most justifies this advice?", "options": {"A": "Fluid retention and edema from renal sodium reabsorption worsening heart failure in susceptible older patients", "B": "Direct stimulation of pancreatic insulin release producing prolonged hypoglycemia as clearance of the agent falls", "C": "Delayed gastric emptying with severe constipation and pancreatitis risk when exposure to the drug rises", "D": "Volume contraction from glucosuria precipitating euglycemic ketoacidosis in a patient with reduced oral intake", "E": "Drug accumulation impairing mitochondrial respiration and raising lactate, risking an anion gap metabolic acidosis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Drug accumulation impairing mitochondrial respiration and raising lactate, risking an anion gap metabolic acidosis): Metformin is cleared by the kidneys, so a sharp fall in filtration lets it accumulate and inhibit mitochondrial respiration, raising lactate and the risk of lactic acidosis. Hypoglycemia is a sulfonylurea problem, and euglycemic ketoacidosis belongs to the SGLT2 inhibitors. Fluid retention is associated with thiazolidinediones."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has fatigue, constipation, cold intolerance, and a firm, diffusely enlarged, nontender thyroid. Her sister has type 1 diabetes. Examination is otherwise unremarkable. Laboratory results are shown in the table. Which histologic finding would a biopsy of the gland most likely show?", "options": {"A": "Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells surrounding fragments of disrupted colloid", "B": "Lymphocytic infiltrate with germinal centers and Hurthle cells replacing normal follicles", "C": "Dense fibrosis extending through the capsule into neck structures with a sparse inflammatory infiltrate", "D": "Orphan Annie nuclei within papillae containing psammoma bodies and fibrovascular cores", "E": "Tall columnar hyperplastic follicles with scalloped colloid and papillary infoldings lined by crowded cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lymphocytic infiltrate with germinal centers and Hurthle cells replacing normal follicles): High TSH, low free T4, and high peroxidase antibodies in a woman with a family autoimmune history indicate Hashimoto thyroiditis. Biopsy shows lymphocytes with germinal centers and oxyphilic Hurthle cells. Granulomas indicate the painful subacute form.", "image": "question_images/q_992.png"}, {"id": 993, "domain": "respiratory", "question": "A 70-year-old man has been mechanically ventilated in the intensive care unit for 6 days after abdominal surgery. He develops fever, purulent tracheal secretions, and a new right lower lobe infiltrate. Sputum culture grows an oxidase-positive, lactose-nonfermenting gram-negative rod producing a blue-green pigment. Which virulence factor of this organism is most responsible for local tissue necrosis and systemic toxicity?", "options": {"A": "Capsular polysaccharide, which blocks complement deposition and resists phagocytosis by alveolar macrophages", "B": "Pneumolysin, which binds membrane cholesterol and lyses ciliated respiratory epithelial cells", "C": "Panton-Valentine leukocidin, which forms pores in the membranes of neutrophils and macrophages", "D": "Exotoxin A, which ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 and halts host cell protein synthesis", "E": "Lethal factor, which cleaves mitogen-activated protein kinase kinases inside infected macrophages"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Exotoxin A, which ADP-ribosylates elongation factor 2 and halts host cell protein synthesis): A blue-green pigmented, oxidase-positive, lactose-nonfermenting rod is Pseudomonas aeruginosa, the classic ventilator associated pathogen. Its exotoxin A uses the same mechanism as diphtheria toxin, blocking elongation factor 2 and killing host cells. Capsule or antiphagocytic factors contribute to survival but do not produce the toxin mediated necrosis."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "community_health", "question": "A 71-year-old widow whose husband died 4 months ago reports intense sadness, yearning, and occasionally hearing his voice calling her name. She cries daily but enjoys time with grandchildren, sleeps adequately, eats normally and denies guilt or suicidal thoughts. Which is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Brief psychotic disorder, since hearing a voice calling her name represents a hallucination requiring treatment", "B": "Normal grief, since waves of longing and brief perceptions of the deceased fall within expected bereavement", "C": "Major depressive disorder, since persistent sadness and crying daily for several months meet duration criteria", "D": "Adjustment disorder with depressed mood, since the sadness is an excessive reaction to a stressor", "E": "Prolonged grief disorder, since yearning for the deceased continues beyond several months after the death"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Normal grief, since waves of longing and brief perceptions of the deceased fall within expected bereavement): Yearning, waves of grief and transient perceptions of the deceased are common in uncomplicated grief when functioning, sleep, appetite, self-worth and enjoyment are preserved. Major depression would add pervasive anhedonia, guilt or worthlessness, and prolonged grief disorder requires a longer period after the loss with persistent impairment. Her features do not suggest psychosis."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "community_health", "question": "An emergency physician wants a test for a dangerous but treatable condition in patients with low pretest probability, so that a negative result reliably excludes disease. The laboratory notes that the candidate assay costs more and takes 40 minutes to run. Which test characteristic should be prioritized when choosing the assay?", "options": {"A": "High specificity, since few healthy patients are labeled positive when the assay is abnormal", "B": "Short turnaround time, since a faster assay changes management more than accuracy does", "C": "Low cost per assay, since affordability determines how widely a screening assay can be used", "D": "High positive predictive value, since it rises when disease is rare in the tested group", "E": "High sensitivity, since few diseased patients are missed when the assay returns normal"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (High sensitivity, since few diseased patients are missed when the assay returns normal): A test used to exclude a dangerous disease must rarely miss it, which requires high sensitivity so that a normal result carries strong rule-out value. High specificity helps confirm disease rather than exclude it, and positive predictive value actually falls when disease is rare. Cost and speed matter practically but do not determine how reliably a result excludes disease."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 3-year-old boy has episodic vomiting, ataxia and lethargy after protein-rich meals, and a maternal uncle died in infancy of unexplained encephalopathy. Plasma ammonia is markedly elevated, blood urea nitrogen is low, and urine orotic acid is high. Liver function tests are normal. Which enzyme deficiency is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Orotate phosphoribosyltransferase, which converts orotic acid to orotidine monophosphate during de novo pyrimidine synthesis", "B": "Ornithine transcarbamylase, which combines carbamoyl phosphate with ornithine to form citrulline in the mitochondrial matrix", "C": "Argininosuccinate synthetase, which condenses citrulline with aspartate to form argininosuccinate in the cytosol of hepatocytes", "D": "Arginase, which cleaves arginine into ornithine and urea in the cytosol and causes progressive spastic diplegia", "E": "Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I, which forms carbamoyl phosphate from ammonia and bicarbonate in the mitochondrial matrix"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ornithine transcarbamylase, which combines carbamoyl phosphate with ornithine to form citrulline in the mitochondrial matrix): Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency is X-linked, so affected boys with a maternal uncle history present with hyperammonemia after protein loads. Carbamoyl phosphate accumulates and spills into pyrimidine synthesis, raising orotic acid, while low urea production gives a low BUN. Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase deficiency has no orotic acid rise, and orotic aciduria alone has normal ammonia."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "circulatory_hematologic", "question": "A 7-year-old girl has fever, sore throat, and tender cervical lymphadenopathy for 10 days. Blood smear shows many large atypical lymphocytes, and heterophile antibody testing is positive. A node biopsy is performed for diagnostic uncertainty. Which node region would show the most prominent expansion?", "options": {"A": "Medullary cords, the plasma cell rich region that secretes antibody toward efferent lymph", "B": "Secondary follicle germinal centers, the site of B cell class switching in bacterial infection", "C": "Primary follicles, the B cell aggregates present before antigen exposure without germinal centers", "D": "Subcapsular sinus, the macrophage lined channel where afferent lymph first enters the node", "E": "Paracortex, the diffuse T cell region that proliferates in response to viral antigen"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Paracortex, the diffuse T cell region that proliferates in response to viral antigen): Infectious mononucleosis from Epstein-Barr virus causes reactive T cell proliferation, so the paracortex expands in the node. Atypical lymphocytes on smear are activated cytotoxic T cells. B cell germinal centers expand mainly in humoral responses such as bacterial infection, and medullary cords hold plasma cells."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "human_development", "question": "A 30-year-old woman at 30 weeks gestation has no symptoms at a routine prenatal visit. Hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL with a normal mean corpuscular volume, normal serum ferritin and no evidence of hemolysis. Blood pressure is 106/62 mm Hg. Which physiologic change best explains the hemoglobin concentration?", "options": {"A": "Fetal iron demand depletes maternal stores, causing a microcytic anemia by the third trimester", "B": "Shortened red cell survival from placental mechanical stress causes a compensated mild hemolytic anemia", "C": "Suppressed erythropoietin release from the kidney reduces red cell production during the second trimester", "D": "Plasma volume expands proportionally more than red cell mass, producing hemodilution without true anemia", "E": "Folate deficiency from increased fetal demand impairs DNA synthesis and produces a mild macrocytic anemia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Plasma volume expands proportionally more than red cell mass, producing hemodilution without true anemia): Plasma volume rises by roughly 40 to 50 percent while red cell mass rises by about 20 to 30 percent, so hemoglobin concentration falls even though total red cell mass is increased. A normal ferritin and indices argue against iron or folate deficiency, and erythropoietin actually rises in pregnancy. The slightly lower blood pressure reflects falling systemic vascular resistance."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "endocrine_metabolism", "question": "A 14-month-old boy has developmental delay, writhing movements of the limbs, and has begun biting his lips and fingers until they bleed. His diapers contain orange crystalline sand. Serum uric acid is markedly elevated, and megaloblastic anemia is absent. Which enzyme deficiency explains these findings?", "options": {"A": "Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase, degrading uracil and thymine, with deficiency causing neurologic signs and severe fluorouracil toxicity", "B": "Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase, which salvages hypoxanthine and guanine into nucleotides using PRPP in purine recycling", "C": "Adenosine deaminase, converting adenosine and deoxyadenosine to inosine and preventing toxic dATP accumulation in lymphocytes", "D": "Glucose-6-phosphatase, hydrolyzing glucose-6-phosphate in liver, with deficiency causing hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis and secondary hyperuricemia", "E": "Phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate synthetase overactivity, increasing PRPP and de novo purine production with hyperuricemia and sensorineural deafness"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase, which salvages hypoxanthine and guanine into nucleotides using PRPP in purine recycling): Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase deficiency prevents purine salvage, so PRPP accumulates and de novo purine synthesis and uric acid rise, producing orange urate crystals. The X-linked disorder also causes dystonia and compulsive self-mutilation. PRPP synthetase overactivity gives hyperuricemia without self-injury, and the other enzymes do not fit."}, {"id": 1000, "domain": "integumentary", "question": "A 26-year-old librarian presents with five days of tender red nodules on both shins, shown in the photograph, with ankle pain and low-grade malaise. She has no fever or cough. A chest radiograph shows bilateral symmetric hilar lymphadenopathy. A skin biopsy shows septal panniculitis. Which underlying disease is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Hodgkin lymphoma with mediastinal nodal enlargement and a paraneoplastic skin eruption", "B": "Crohn disease with an extraintestinal inflammatory skin manifestation that tracks bowel activity", "C": "Sarcoidosis presenting as acute Lofgren syndrome with noncaseating granulomas within the enlarged hilar lymph nodes", "D": "Primary pulmonary tuberculosis with a hypersensitivity skin reaction to mycobacterial antigens", "E": "Group A streptococcal infection with delayed immune mediated panniculitis after pharyngitis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sarcoidosis presenting as acute Lofgren syndrome with noncaseating granulomas within the enlarged hilar lymph nodes): Erythema nodosum with bilateral hilar adenopathy and ankle periarthritis is the classic Lofgren triad of acute sarcoidosis, which carries a good prognosis. Tuberculosis typically causes unilateral adenopathy with systemic features, and lymphoma would be expected to bring constitutional symptoms.", "image": "question_images/q_1000.png"}];
